window.CATALOG=[{"id":"J-01","ia":"summation-non-military-activities-japan-korea-no-01-1945-09","s":"J","no":1,"d":"1945-09","d2":"1945-10","t":"Summation of Non-Military Activities in Japan and Korea, No. 1 (September-October 1945)","v":"","pg":203,"o":"Covering the foundational two months of the Allied occupation (September–October 1945), this initial summation records the establishment of General Headquarters, Supreme Commander for the Allied Powers (GHQ/SCAP) in Tokyo under General Douglas MacArthur and the simultaneous installation of U.S. Army Military Government in South Korea under the XXIV Corps. In Japan, SCAP instituted indirect governance through the Japanese cabinet and Diet while enforcing rapid demilitarization, dissolving the imperial military, and issuing the historic 4 October 'Bill of Rights' directive. That directive abolished thought-control police, repealed repressive statutes like the Peace Preservation Act, freed political prisoners, precipitated the collapse of the Higashikuni Cabinet, and initiated constitutional revision. The report portrays widespread economic paralysis across both territories: catastrophic coal deficits following the liberation of Korean and Chinese forced laborers, acute food shortages worsened by devastating September typhoons, uncontrolled black markets, and fiscal turmoil. In Korea, authorities confronted the crippling economic and political division along the 38th parallel, the rise of dozens of competing political factions, and popular resistance to allied trusteeship. For historians, the report provides invaluable baseline statistical surveys, structural blueprints of occupation administration, and candid accounts of early policy improvisation.","h":["General MacArthur established GHQ SCAP in Tokyo on 2 October 1945 and activated nine Special Staff Sections to supervise non-military occupation policy, governing indirectly through the Japanese Government.","SCAP's 4 October 'Bill of Rights' directive repealed the Peace Preservation Law, abolished the thought-control police (4,800 officials dismissed), and freed 507 political prisoners.","Prime Minister Prince Higashikuni's Cabinet resigned en bloc on 5 October after the civil liberties directive and was replaced on 9 October by Baron Kijuro Shidehara's Cabinet.","The four major Zaibatsu combines, Mitsui, Mitsubishi, Yasuda and Sumitomo, agreed to voluntary dissolution plans transferring holding company securities to a liquidation commission; 29 wartime colonial banks were closed.","SCAP ordered the arrest of former Premier Hideki Tojo, his Pearl Harbor Cabinet and about 300 suspected war criminals.","Coal production fell from 3.4 million metric tons in June 1945 to under 600,000 tons in October after forced laborers were liberated, while typhoons worsened food shortages and the staple ration fell to 294 grams.","General MacArthur proclaimed U.S. Military Government in Korea on 7 September 1945, refusing recognition to domestic provisional regimes amid partition at the 38th parallel and 54 rival political parties.","Active Japanese merchant shipping shrank to 908,000 gross tons, mostly allocated to coastal coal and repatriation, while black-market prices reached up to 260 times official ceilings."],"k":["SCAP","USAMGIK","Bill of Rights Directive","Zaibatsu dissolution","demilitarization","38th parallel","repatriation","Peace Preservation Law","Hideki Tojo","Shidehara Cabinet","coal shortage","price control"],"toc":[{"t":"Front Matter","lv":1,"p":[1,5],"pp":"","s":"Title page, document documentation sheet, War Department transmittal memorandum and general table of contents for the first monthly summation."},{"t":"Part I: General","lv":1,"p":[6,27],"pp":"1","s":"Executive overview of SCAP organization, early political and social reforms, economic demilitarization and stabilization in Japan, and U.S. Military Government civil administration in Korea."},{"t":"Section 1. Organization under SCAP","lv":2,"p":[8,11],"pp":"3","s":"GHQ SCAP was established in Tokyo with nine Special Staff Sections, governing indirectly through the Japanese Government's Central Liaison Office; the section also outlines the Military Government framework in Korea."},{"t":"Section 2. Political and Social Activities in Japan","lv":2,"p":[12,17],"pp":"7","s":"The 4 October 'Bill of Rights' directive toppled the Higashikuni Cabinet in favor of Shidehara's, 4,800 thought-police officials were dismissed, political prisoners freed, new parties emerged, and war crimes arrests began."},{"t":"Section 3. Economic Activities in Japan","lv":2,"p":[18,23],"pp":"13","s":"War industries were demilitarized and Zaibatsu dissolution planned; trade unions were legalized, coal and food were scarce, trade and exchange were tightly controlled, and 29 wartime colonial banks were closed."},{"t":"Section 4. Civil Administration in Korea","lv":2,"p":[24,27],"pp":"19","s":"U.S. Military Government in South Korea began operating under the 38th parallel division, replaced Japanese personnel, faced dozens of political factions and coal shortages, and moved to prevent winter typhus."},{"t":"Part II: Political","lv":1,"p":[28,45],"pp":"23","s":"Government administration, political reorganization, legal affairs, public safety and intelligence in Japan, including the Cabinet change, demobilization, release of political prisoners, and arrest orders for Tojo and 300 suspected war criminals."},{"t":"Section 1. Government and Civil Administration","lv":2,"p":[30,37],"pp":"25","s":"Indirect administration worked through the Diet and Ministries; Higashikuni's Cabinet fell and Shidehara's formed, governors were shaken up, constitutional revision committees began, woman suffrage was proposed, and rival parties emerged."},{"t":"Section 2. Legal, Public Safety and Intelligence","lv":2,"p":[38,45],"pp":"33","s":"Armed forces were fully demobilized, peace preservation laws abrogated, political prisoners released, 4,800 secret police purged, arrest orders issued for Tojo and 300 war criminals, and press censorship implemented."},{"t":"Part III: Economic","lv":1,"p":[46,135],"pp":"41","s":"Economic conditions in Japan: food and coal collapse, stagnant industry, crippled transport, controlled foreign trade, unemployment, black markets, and finance including closure of 29 colonial banks and Zaibatsu dissolution plans."},{"t":"Section 1. Natural Resources","lv":2,"p":[48,59],"pp":"43","s":"September typhoon damage to rice worsened food deficits; fishing zones were authorized despite fuel and net shortages, urban charcoal ran short, and coal output collapsed after forced laborers were liberated."},{"t":"Section 2. Industry","lv":2,"p":[60,77],"pp":"55","s":"Industry stagnated: monthly truck production was authorized, shipyards repaired around the clock, iron and steel fell with coal shortages, wartime control associations were slated for dissolution, and cyclotron research was restricted."},{"t":"Section 3. Transportation","lv":2,"p":[78,83],"pp":"73","s":"Railroads proved relatively resilient despite overcrowding while motor vehicles deteriorated severely; active merchant shipping shrank to 908,000 gross tons, allocated mainly to coastal coal and repatriation."},{"t":"Section 4. Imports and Exports","lv":2,"p":[84,89],"pp":"79","s":"Imports were limited to vital necessities paid for by approved exports; export surpluses such as raw silk were identified, emergency coal went to Korea and Hong Kong, and wartime military trading companies were reviewed."},{"t":"Section 5. Labor","lv":2,"p":[90,95],"pp":"85","s":"Labor was procured for Allied forces and unemployment projected above four million; prewar labor protections returned, independent unions re-formed under a unified federation, and Korean and Chinese coal miners struck violently."},{"t":"Section 6. Communications","lv":2,"p":[96,111],"pp":"91","s":"War-damaged telephone and telegraph networks were rehabilitated for Allied use, international radio circuits censored, seized military vacuum tubes redistributed, equipment manufacturing recovered slowly, and electronics research restricted."},{"t":"Section 7. Rationing and Price Control","lv":2,"p":[112,119],"pp":"107","s":"Commodity distribution broke down; the staple food ration fell to 294 grams, newsprint and textiles ran short, black-market prices reached 260 times official ceilings, and price control reform was pursued."},{"t":"Section 8. Finance","lv":2,"p":[120,135],"pp":"115","s":"Fiscal distress persisted with the currency stabilized at 42 billion yen; 29 wartime colonial banks were liquidated, gold and silver impounded, war damage insurance left unsettled, and Zaibatsu dissolution begun."},{"t":"Part IV: Social Services","lv":1,"p":[136,177],"pp":"131","s":"Public health, welfare, medical supplies, education reform, religious freedom, cultural preservation and media liberalization in Japan, including relief for over 83,000 persons and the dissolution of the Domei news monopoly."},{"t":"Section 1. Public Health and Welfare","lv":2,"p":[138,151],"pp":"133","s":"Hospital facilities were damaged; relief reached over 83,000 persons, venereal disease directives issued, typhus prevention applied to repatriates, military medical stockpiles transferred, and relief supplies delivered to Hiroshima."},{"t":"Section 2. Education, Religion and Media of Expression","lv":2,"p":[152,177],"pp":"147","s":"Education was demilitarized and teachers screened, the Religious Bodies Law abolished, State Shinto separation planned, the Domei news monopoly dissolved, and democratic radio, film and theatrical programs introduced."},{"t":"Part V: Korea","lv":1,"p":[178,203],"pp":"173","s":"U.S. Army Military Government in South Korea: political developments, economic conditions and social affairs, including the partition at the 38th parallel, rice market, typhus immunization and refugee relief."},{"t":"Section 1. Political","lv":2,"p":[180,191],"pp":"175","s":"Direct U.S. Military Government was established under the 38th parallel partition; Japanese officials and police were purged, 54 parties rivaled one another, Syngman Rhee returned, and trusteeship drew wide hostility."},{"t":"Section 2. Economic","lv":2,"p":[192,197],"pp":"187","s":"A free rice market was established; fertilizer and fuel were severely short after supplies from North Korea were cut, emergency coal came from Japan, railroads and communications were reorganized, and finances tightly controlled."},{"t":"Section 3. Social","lv":2,"p":[198,203],"pp":"193","s":"Secondary schools reopened with Korean-language instruction, Military Government press and radio services expanded, the Bureau of Public Health was created, mass typhus immunization ran, and refugees were relieved at Fusan and Seoul."}],"by":"gemini-3.8-flash-high"},{"id":"J-01-phw","ia":"summation-non-military-activities-japan-korea-no-01-1945-09-public-health-welfare","s":"J","no":1,"d":"1945-09","d2":"1945-10","t":"Summation of Non-Military Activities in Japan and Korea, No. 1 (September-October 1945): Public Health and Welfare","v":"Public Health and Welfare section","pg":14,"o":"This initial monthly summation covers non-military activities in public health and welfare across Japan during September and October 1945, representing the earliest comprehensive SCAP assessment of Japan's domestic medical and social conditions following surrender. The report documents the acute deterioration caused by wartime neglect and air raids, including damage to over 1,000 civilian hospitals, paralyzed medical manufacturing, substandard nursing and dental training, and the disruption of municipal sanitation and water treatment. Occupation authorities moved quickly to establish administrative control by issuing directives for communicable disease reporting, narcotics prohibition, weekly hospital bed censuses, and the demilitarization of the Japanese Red Cross. Key public health concerns included widespread outbreaks of diphtheria and dysentery, epidemic typhus among Korean coal miners and repatriates, and severe shortages of food, clothing, and housing, though per capita nutrition was targeted at 1,800 calories daily. For historians, this report is invaluable as a baseline inventory of Japan's devastated postwar welfare infrastructure, the early mechanics of SCAP indirect rule, the repatriation of roughly 150,000 Koreans, and emergency medical relief delivered to atomic bomb victims in Hiroshima.","h":["On 6 September, about 12 tons of emergency medical supplies were dispatched through the International Red Cross to relieve victims in Hiroshima.","A SCAP directive of 12 October prohibited cultivation of narcotic plants, banned narcotics export, and froze all existing stocks of opium, cocaine, heroin and marijuana.","SCAP ordered the Japanese government to inventory all military medical and dental stockpiles and transfer them to the Home Ministry for distribution to civilians.","By 31 October, about 150,000 of an estimated 2,000,000 Koreans in Japan had been repatriated through Fusan, using returning Japanese shipping.","Allied air raids destroyed or damaged 1,083 civilian hospitals with 53,007 beds in 46 prefectures, about 25 percent of Japan's civilian hospitals.","Communicable disease reports through 13 October recorded 57,729 dysentery, 50,877 diphtheria, 28,787 typhoid and 1,900 typhus cases, with epidemic typhus noted among Korean laborers.","A standard prefectural Venereal Disease Control Ordinance was mandated, instituting compulsory weekly examinations and introducing penicillin therapy for high-risk groups.","Public welfare administration was disorganized: 83,502 persons received relief, and the Japanese Red Cross was to be demilitarized."],"k":["Public Health and Welfare","Hiroshima relief","narcotics control","repatriation of Koreans","communicable diseases","typhus control","hospital administration","Japanese Red Cross","social insurance","medical supplies","venereal disease control","sanitary engineering"],"toc":[{"t":"General","lv":1,"p":[1,1],"pp":"133","s":"Notes wartime neglect of health and welfare, return of military medical stockpiles to civilian use, narcotics control, and new directives for disease reporting and hospital bed tracking."},{"t":"Public Welfare","lv":1,"p":[2,3],"pp":"134","s":"Describes disorganized welfare administration, 83,502 persons on relief, five social insurance programs, demilitarization of the Japanese Red Cross, and repatriation of about 150,000 Koreans."},{"t":"Administration of Hospitals","lv":1,"p":[4,4],"pp":"136","s":"Air raids damaged 1,083 civilian hospitals; 78,000 veterans were treated in military facilities. Japan has 3,335 hospitals with 356,143 beds, operating at two-thirds capacity with sufficient personnel."},{"t":"Veterinary Affairs","lv":1,"p":[5,6],"pp":"137","s":"Veterinary services have collapsed. The report gives 1944 livestock census figures and 1945 animal disease outbreaks; commercial slaughter runs at 10 percent of normal and dairy sanitation is substandard."},{"t":"Dental Affairs","lv":1,"p":[7,7],"pp":"139","s":"War devastated dental practice and equipment manufacture: 75 percent of pre-adolescent children have severe dental problems, and air raids displaced 7,000 of Japan's 23,000 dentists."},{"t":"Nursing Affairs","lv":1,"p":[8,8],"pp":"140","s":"Nursing education and midwife licensing are substandard; 34,000 nurses were absorbed into wartime military service, and women have no representation in nursing associations or licensing boards."},{"t":"Medical Supply","lv":1,"p":[9,9],"pp":"141","s":"Medical supply manufacturing is paralyzed. About 12 tons of emergency supplies went to Hiroshima, military medical stockpiles moved to civilian channels, and a 12 October directive froze and controlled narcotics."},{"t":"Legal","lv":1,"p":[10,10],"pp":"142","s":"Reviews public health laws; a standard prefectural venereal disease ordinance brings compulsory weekly examinations and penicillin therapy. Civil service reform to elevate technical specialists over administrators is under study."},{"t":"Preventive Medicine","lv":1,"p":[11,14],"pp":"143","s":"Reports over 50,000 cases each of diphtheria and dysentery, epidemic typhus among Korean laborers, inadequate water chlorination, port quarantine protocols, and a nutritional target of 1,800 calories per day."}],"by":"gemini-3.8-flash-high"},{"id":"J-03","ia":"summation-non-military-activities-japan-korea-no-03-1945-12","s":"J","no":3,"d":"1945-12","d2":"","t":"Summation of Non-Military Activities in Japan and Korea, No. 3 (December 1945)","v":"","pg":134,"o":"Monthly Summation No. 3 covers non-military activities of GHQ/SCAP in Japan and USAMGIK in southern Korea during December 1945. In Japan, decisive structural reforms reshaped political and social life. The extraordinary Diet session enacted landmark democratic legislation, including the Trade Union Law, the Agricultural Lands Adjustment Law, and an Election Law granting women suffrage. Concurrently, SCAP directives disestablished State Shinto, banned militaristic textbooks, ordered dissolution of eighteen Zaibatsu holding companies, and established the International Prosecution Section. War crimes proceedings intensified with the apprehension of 205 high-ranking suspects—precipitating Prince Fumimaro Konoye's suicide—and the first Yokohama military trial convicting Tatsuo Tsuchiya. Acute coal shortages, currency expansion, and low urban food reserves hindered industrial recovery. In Korea, the Moscow Conference agreement proposing a four-power five-year trusteeship triggered nationwide strikes and mass protests led by Kim Koo, while General Hodge repudiated the authority of the Korean People's Republic. For historians, this report provides crucial statistical baselines from the November 1945 census, primary documentation of demilitarization policies, and essential evidence of early Cold War tensions across Northeast Asia.","h":["The 89th Imperial Diet passed the Trade Union Law, the Agricultural Lands Adjustment Law and an Election Law establishing women's suffrage before it was dissolved.","SCAP issued the Shinto Directive on 15 December, separating State Shinto from government administration, public finance and school curricula; on 21 December it banned militaristic textbooks in morals, geography and history.","Seven directives ordered the arrest of 205 war criminal suspects, bringing Sugamo Prison internments to 445; former Prime Minister Prince Fumimaro Konoye committed suicide on 16 December rather than be arrested.","At Japan's first war crimes trial, at Yokohama, a military commission convicted Tatsuo Tsuchiya of prisoner abuse and sentenced him to life imprisonment.","Eighteen holding companies, including Mitsui, Mitsubishi, Yasuda and Sumitomo, and 325 subsidiaries were designated restricted concerns for dissolution; the Big Four Zaibatsu reported 3.15 billion yen in assets.","Coal production rose 53 percent to 840,000 metric tons while steel stayed at five percent of capacity; Bank of Japan note circulation reached 51.09 billion yen and urban food reserves fell to two-to-eight days.","Announcement of the Moscow Conference's four-power trusteeship plan set off nationwide Korean strikes and mass demonstrations coordinated by Kim Koo's Anti-Trusteeship Committee; free rice marketing was suspended from 1 January 1946.","Lieutenant General John R. Hodge denounced the Korean People's Republic, declaring Military Government the sole lawful governing authority in South Korea."],"k":["SCAP","USAMGIK","Trade Union Law","Shinto Directive","textbook screening","agrarian reform","Zaibatsu dissolution","war crimes trials","repatriation","Moscow Conference","Korean trusteeship","Korean People's Republic"],"toc":[{"t":"Front Matter","lv":1,"p":[1,2],"pp":"","s":"Title page, overall table of contents, and list of charts, graphs, maps and tables for the December 1945 report."},{"t":"Part I: General","lv":1,"p":[3,12],"pp":"1","s":"Executive summary for December 1945: the 89th Diet's election, land and trade union laws, Konoye's suicide, the Shinto Directive, Zaibatsu restrictions, and Korean protests over Moscow Conference trusteeship."},{"t":"Organization Under SCAP","lv":2,"p":[4,4],"pp":"3","s":"General Orders No. 20 established the International Prosecution Section for war crimes trials; in Korea, Civil Administration created the Provincial Affairs and Korean Civil Service Sections."},{"t":"Political Activities in Japan","lv":2,"p":[4,6],"pp":"3","s":"The 89th Diet passed election, agrarian land and trade union laws before dissolving; Prince Konoye committed suicide to avoid arrest; Tatsuo Tsuchiya received a life sentence in the first Yokohama war crimes trial."},{"t":"Economic Activities in Japan","lv":2,"p":[7,8],"pp":"9","s":"Coal production rose 53 percent to 840,000 metric tons; the Trade Union Law was enacted; eighteen holding companies and 325 subsidiaries were designated restricted concerns; Bank of Japan note issues expanded."},{"t":"Social Activities in Japan","lv":2,"p":[9,10],"pp":"13","s":"State Shinto was separated from government support; militaristic textbooks in morals, geography and history were banned; census showed sharp city population declines; over 654,000 repatriates left Japan."},{"t":"Civil Administration in Korea","lv":2,"p":[11,12],"pp":"17","s":"The Moscow Conference's four-power trusteeship announcement provoked nationwide Korean resentment; coalition efforts continued under Kim Koo; free rice marketing was ordered suspended from 1 January 1946."},{"t":"Part II: Political - Japan","lv":1,"p":[13,23],"pp":"21","s":"Japanese politics, public safety and war crimes: the 89th Diet's laws, new parties, Tokyo crime statistics, arrest of 205 war criminal suspects, and the Tsuchiya trial at Yokohama."},{"t":"Government and Civil Administration","lv":2,"p":[14,16],"pp":"23","s":"The 89th Diet passed women's suffrage, land reform and trade union laws before dissolution; many parties formed, including the Japan Cooperative and New Japan Women's parties; the Cabinet abolished the Board of Information."},{"t":"Public Safety and Intelligence","lv":2,"p":[17,18],"pp":"29","s":"Tokyo crime statistics showed no postwar crime wave; the Economic Police Section was eliminated in police demilitarization; political prisoners regained voting rights; hidden military aircraft engines were seized in Shizuoka."},{"t":"Legal and War Crimes","lv":2,"p":[19,23],"pp":"33","s":"Seven directives ordered arrest of 205 war criminal suspects, bringing Sugamo Prison internments to 445; Tatsuo Tsuchiya received a life sentence at Yokohama; trials of camp commanders Yuri and Furushima began."},{"t":"Part III: Economic - Japan","lv":1,"p":[24,76],"pp":"42","s":"Japanese economy: rice collection, coal output of 840,000 tons, steel at five percent of capacity, Labor Union Law, 3.19 million unemployed, 51.09 billion yen note circulation, and eighteen restricted holding companies."},{"t":"Agriculture and Fisheries","lv":2,"p":[25,32],"pp":"45","s":"Government rice collection lagged at 30 percent of quota, prompting higher purchase prices and subsidies; soil surveys assessed reclamation; naval fuel oil releases boosted fish landings and curbed black-market sales."},{"t":"Forestry and Mining","lv":2,"p":[33,39],"pp":"61","s":"Operations at Japan's largest modern sawmill and the Amagi Imperial Forest were surveyed; coal production rebounded 53 percent to 840,000 tons; declassified wartime data showed overexpansion in subsidized mining."},{"t":"Industry","lv":2,"p":[40,52],"pp":"75","s":"Steel ran at five percent of capacity; 228 vessels were repaired in shipyards; the textile industry reorganized into a democratic association; the Diet repealed sixteen major wartime industrial control laws."},{"t":"Transportation and Public Utilities","lv":2,"p":[53,54],"pp":"99","s":"Coastal tankers carried vital petroleum products; seventeen radio navigation beacons were restored; electric heater use overloaded urban distribution systems; coal shortages limited Tokyo gas service to three hours daily."},{"t":"Imports and Exports","lv":2,"p":[55,55],"pp":"105","s":"An Imperial Ordinance created the Board of Trade to manage foreign commerce; imports were limited to salt and kerosene, coal and silkworm eggs were exported, and an inventory of 50,000 silk bales was ordered."},{"t":"Labor","lv":2,"p":[56,60],"pp":"106","s":"The Labor Union Law spurred rapid unionization in mining and transport; Yomiuri and Keisei Railway disputes ended with wage increases; occupational registration recorded 3.19 million unemployed."},{"t":"Communications","lv":2,"p":[61,64],"pp":"116","s":"Trunk cable reconstruction advanced; Sender Composition Message service to the United States handled record holiday traffic; 1946 radio set production was projected at 3.1 million; captured military equipment was stripped for civilian parts."},{"t":"Rationing and Price Control","lv":2,"p":[65,70],"pp":"125","s":"Urban food reserves fell to two-to-eight days; rice purchase prices and producer subsidies rose; fish ceiling prices were reestablished; petroleum prices surged 280 to 400 percent after subsidy abolition."},{"t":"Money and Banking","lv":2,"p":[71,73],"pp":"136","s":"Bank of Japan note circulation reached 51.09 billion yen on government advances and commercial borrowing; monthly bank condition reporting began; repatriates could bring in yen savings books and bonds."},{"t":"Public Finance","lv":2,"p":[74,76],"pp":"143","s":"Diet supplementary budgets funded food and salt production; military pensions were suspended; the Big Four Zaibatsu reported 3.15 billion yen in assets; SCAP restricted eighteen holding companies financially."},{"t":"Part IV: Social - Japan","lv":1,"p":[77,93],"pp":"148","s":"Japanese public health, welfare, education, religion and media: DDT against typhus, the 71,996,477 census count, the Shinto Directive, textbook bans, and the 'Now It Can Be Told' broadcasts."},{"t":"Public Health and Welfare","lv":2,"p":[78,86],"pp":"151","s":"DDT dusting checked typhus among Hokkaido coal miners; military hospitals went civilian, doubling bed capacity; the November census counted 71,996,477 residents; a 30,000-person nutrition survey was run in Tokyo."},{"t":"Education, Religion and Media of Expression","lv":2,"p":[87,93],"pp":"169","s":"SCAP issued the Shinto Directive abolishing state sponsorship and banned militaristic textbooks in morals, geography and history; an American educational mission was requested; 'Now It Can Be Told' war history was broadcast."},{"t":"Part V: Korea","lv":1,"p":[94,104],"pp":"182","s":"South Korea under USAMGIK: anti-trusteeship protests led by Kim Koo, Hodge's repudiation of the Korean People's Republic, rice price ceilings restored, bank mergers, and education and health appointments."},{"t":"Political Activities in Korea","lv":2,"p":[95,98],"pp":"185","s":"The Moscow Conference trusteeship announcement ignited Korean strikes and mass demonstrations led by Kim Koo; General Hodge repudiated the Korean People's Republic; a National Bar Association was established."},{"t":"Economic Activities in Korea","lv":2,"p":[99,102],"pp":"193","s":"Despite a 15.97-million-koku rice harvest, market withholding forced suspension of free trading and restored price ceilings; Japanese-owned farmland went to the New Korea Company; Japanese branch banks merged into Korean ones."},{"t":"Social Activities in Korea","lv":2,"p":[103,104],"pp":"200","s":"A Korean was appointed Director of Education; Korean Pacific Press began independent operations with Associated Press; Seoul University graduated its first public health class; diphtheria antitoxin was flown in from Japan."},{"t":"Charts, Graphs, Maps and Tables","lv":1,"p":[105,134],"pp":"Following page 206","s":"Full-page charts, graphs, maps and tables on Japanese and Korean economic, social and demographic statistics, following printed page 206."}],"by":"gemini-3.8-flash-high"},{"id":"J-03-phw","ia":"summation-non-military-activities-japan-korea-no-03-1945-12-public-health-welfare","s":"J","no":3,"d":"1945-12","d2":"","t":"Summation of Non-Military Activities in Japan and Korea, No. 3 (December 1945): Public Health and Welfare","v":"Public Health and Welfare section","pg":18,"o":"This December 1945 monthly report details early U.S. Occupation efforts to stabilize public health, welfare, and sanitation across Japan amidst severe post-surrender dislocation. Major developments include the containment of typhus outbreaks in Hokkaido using DDT dusting, the introduction of quarantine protocols at repatriation ports, and the directed transfer of 292 Imperial Army and Navy hospitals to the Ministry of Health and Welfare to nearly double civilian bed capacity. SCAP established civilian relief responsibilities, initiated comprehensive urban nutrition surveys in Tokyo to evaluate starvation rumors, and distributed surrendered military medical reserves while combating severe shortages of penicillin, vaccines, and raw materials. Legally, SCAP enacted venereal disease control measures, protected technical health personnel from indiscriminate government layoffs, and received petitions for self-governance from the national nurses’ association. For historians, the report provides invaluable baseline data on early postwar conditions, including the complete November 1945 population census showing extensive wartime urban-to-rural displacement, detailed livestock and disease statistics, repatriation volumes of Koreans and Japanese nationals, and the initial institutional rebuilding of Japan’s public welfare administration.","h":["SCAP ordered 292 Japanese Army and Navy hospitals with 156,725 beds transferred to the Ministry of Health and Welfare for civilian use, against existing civilian capacity of 142,926 beds.","By 30 December 1945, 924,584 Japanese nationals had returned to Japan, while 654,523 Koreans, Chinese, Formosans and Ryukyuans were repatriated from the Home Islands.","The 1 November 1945 census put Japan's population at 71,996,477, with major cities such as Tokyo falling by up to 60 percent because of wartime dispersal.","A Tokyo nutrition survey examined 33,000 residents and 2,777 households and, with post-mortem findings, found no evidence for rumors of widespread civilian starvation deaths.","Emergency DDT dusting, an airlift of DDT and quarantine procedures cut weekly typhus cases among Hokkaido coal miners from 151 in early November to 44 in December.","SCAP intervened in the Japanese Cabinet's civil service retrenchment plans to prevent indiscriminate 50 percent personnel cuts among technical public health staff.","The Japanese government was directed to administer destitution relief and distribute military reserve stocks, while SCAP issued a new venereal disease control ordinance.","A national refresher course trained 250 public health nurses from 46 prefectures, and the Nurses Association petitioned for self-governing status."],"k":["public health","repatriation","typhus control","DDT delousing","hospital administration","medical supplies","nutrition survey","1945 population census","venereal disease control","veterinary hygiene","public welfare relief","nursing education"],"toc":[{"t":"Front matter","lv":1,"p":[1,1],"pp":"","s":"Report title, table of contents and organizational overview of the Public Health and Welfare Section."},{"t":"General","lv":1,"p":[1,1],"pp":"151","s":"Summarizes typhus and smallpox control, post-mortem findings showing no starvation deaths, relief directives, and the ordered transfer of military hospitals to civilian use."},{"t":"Public Welfare","lv":1,"p":[2,2],"pp":"152","s":"The Japanese government was directed to administer destitution relief and distribute military reserve stocks. Repatriation totaled 654,523 non-Japanese leaving and 924,584 Japanese nationals arriving."},{"t":"Administration of Hospitals","lv":1,"p":[3,3],"pp":"153","s":"Civilian hospital capacity reached 142,926 beds while in-patient numbers declined. SCAP ordered 292 military hospitals with 156,725 beds transferred to the Health Ministry for civilian use."},{"t":"Veterinary Affairs","lv":1,"p":[4,7],"pp":"154","s":"The 1945 livestock census showed declines in cattle and poultry. Also covers horse breeding, animal disease outbreaks including swine cholera in Osaka, and slaughterhouse and dairy inspection statistics."},{"t":"Dental Affairs","lv":1,"p":[8,8],"pp":"158","s":"Shortages of equipment, supplies and housing hampered dental practice, though manufacturing facilities were judged adequate if fuel and materials are supplied. 295 dentists resumed practice in November."},{"t":"Nursing Affairs","lv":1,"p":[8,8],"pp":"158","s":"Standardization of nursing education and prefectural licensing began. A national refresher course trained 250 public health nurses from 46 prefectures, and the Nurses Association petitioned for self-governing status."},{"t":"Supply","lv":1,"p":[8,10],"pp":"158","s":"Covers distribution of returned military medical stocks and controlled drugs, emergency airlift of DDT for typhus control, bottlenecks in biologicals and penicillin production, and impounding of raw opium stocks."},{"t":"Legal","lv":1,"p":[11,11],"pp":"161","s":"A new venereal disease control ordinance was issued. SCAP intervened to prevent indiscriminate 50 percent personnel cuts among Health Ministry technical staff; drug standards legislation was found deficient."},{"t":"Preventive Medicine","lv":1,"p":[12,18],"pp":"162","s":"Covers DDT dusting, water chlorination, Tokyo nutrition surveys refuting starvation rumors, port quarantine, and the November 1945 census showing dramatic wartime urban-to-rural population shifts."}],"by":"gemini-3.8-flash-high"},{"id":"J-04","ia":"summation-non-military-activities-japan-korea-no-04-1946-01","s":"J","no":4,"d":"1946-01","d2":"","t":"Summation of Non-Military Activities in Japan and Korea, No. 4 (January 1946)","v":"","pg":160,"o":"Summation No. 4 documents non-military activities in Japan and American-occupied Korea during January 1946, marking a pivotal phase of early postwar transformation. In Japan, the Occupation accelerated institutional democratization through the landmark 4 January purge directives, the Emperor’s New Year rescript renouncing divinity, the abolition of licensed prostitution, and preparations for national elections. Allied authorities promulgated the Charter for the International Military Tribunal for the Far East, completed initial war crimes trials in Yokohama, and impounded 394 industrial plants for reparations. Concurrently, Japan confronted severe socioeconomic distress: acute food shortages, rampant black markets, and an expanding Bank of Japan note issue, counterbalanced by surging labor unionization and recovering coal production. In Southern Korea, the formal establishment of USAMGIK was overshadowed by massive public unrest following the Moscow Conference trusteeship announcement, while severe coal shortages and rice hoarding paralyzed the economy. For historians, this summation provides extraordinary empirical baselines: complete tabulations of Mitsui, Mitsubishi, Yasuda, and Sumitomo corporate subsidiaries, 20-year trends in agricultural output, national employment census data, nationwide disease maps, and the structural reorganization of Japanese education and police administration.","h":["The Emperor's New Year Rescript reaffirmed the Meiji Charter Oath and explicitly renounced imperial divinity, while SCAP's 4 January directives ordered militarists removed from office and ultra-nationalist organizations abolished, forcing a Shidehara Cabinet reshuffle.","The International Military Tribunal for the Far East was established by proclamation and its Charter approved; SCAP ordered 171 war crimes suspects arrested, including Koki Hirota and Yosuke Matsuoka.","The first Yokohama war atrocity trials concluded with two death sentences and one life imprisonment sentence.","SCAP directed Japan to abrogate laws permitting licensed prostitution and annul contracts binding women to it.","Allied authorities impounded 394 industrial plants, including 294 aircraft and arsenal facilities, as the first step in interim reparations; 41 companies including Oji Paper were added to the Zaibatsu schedule.","Bank of Japan note issue reached a record ¥56 billion as direct government overdraft borrowing was halted; coal output rose 41 percent, though government rice collections stood at only 20 percent of quotas.","Labor unions expanded at two per day and planned a national federation, while a Tokyo survey found half of food came from black markets and Hokkaido cities held as little as two days of staples.","USAMGIK was formally established in Southern Korea, where nationwide protests and strikes opposed the Moscow Conference trusteeship agreement, and rice hoarding and coal shortages strained the economy."],"k":["SCAP purge directives","Imperial Rescript","humanity declaration","International Military Tribunal for the Far East","USAMGIK","Moscow Agreement","trusteeship protests","licensed prostitution","reparations","zaibatsu dissolution","labor unions","rice collection","black markets","repatriation"],"toc":[{"t":"Front matter","lv":1,"p":[1,4],"pp":"","s":"Cover, table of contents, and list of 50 charts, graphs, maps and tables for the January 1946 report."},{"t":"Part I: General","lv":1,"p":[5,12],"pp":"1","s":"Overview of January 1946: the 4 January purge directives, the Emperor's rescript, 394 plants held for reparations, ¥56 billion note issue, USAMGIK's establishment and Korean anti-trusteeship protests."},{"t":"Organization under SCAP","lv":2,"p":[6,6],"pp":"1","s":"SCAP established the International Military Tribunal for the Far East; Sixth Army's General Accounting Section was deactivated with Eighth Army taking control; USAMGIK was formally established in Korea."},{"t":"Political Activities in Japan","lv":2,"p":[6,7],"pp":"2","s":"The 4 January purge directives forced reorganization of the Shidehara Cabinet; the Emperor renounced divinity, licensed prostitution was abolished, elections were scheduled, and Yokohama war crimes trials concluded."},{"t":"Economic Activities in Japan","lv":2,"p":[8,9],"pp":"7","s":"Allied custody covered 394 industrial plants for reparations; coal output rose 41 percent; Bank of Japan note issue reached ¥56 billion; capital levy and war-profit tax bills were drafted."},{"t":"Social Activities in Japan","lv":2,"p":[10,11],"pp":"11","s":"A Tokyo survey found half of food came from black markets; typhus was checked while smallpox spread; 143 textbooks were cleared of militarism; professional educators were appointed to the Education Ministry."},{"t":"Civil Administration in Korea","lv":2,"p":[12,12],"pp":"14","s":"USAMGIK took direct control of local military government units; anti-trusteeship demonstrations spread; a Korean constabulary was formed; emergency rice collection struggled against hoarding."},{"t":"Part II: Political - Japan","lv":1,"p":[13,30],"pp":"17","s":"Japan's political affairs: the purge and Cabinet reshuffle, the Imperial Rescript, police and censorship measures, the Charter of the International Military Tribunal, 171 war crimes arrests and Yokohama death sentences."},{"t":"Government and Civil Administration","lv":2,"p":[14,20],"pp":"19","s":"The purge directives prompted a major Shidehara Cabinet and local government reshuffle; the Emperor's rescript denied divinity; Sanzo Nozaka returned from exile; Japan's administrative authority was limited to the four main islands."},{"t":"Public Safety and Intelligence","lv":2,"p":[21,24],"pp":"33","s":"Authorities fought black markets and lethal bootleg whiskey, abolished the Keihei-tai auxiliary police while arming civil police with revolvers, interned ex-Ambassador Stahmer, and required censorship screening of all motion pictures."},{"t":"Legal and War Crimes","lv":2,"p":[25,30],"pp":"41","s":"The Supreme Commander approved the Far East Tribunal's Charter; SCAP ordered 171 war crimes suspects arrested, including Koki Hirota and Yosuke Matsuoka; Yokohama commissions imposed two death sentences."},{"t":"Part III: Economic - Japan","lv":1,"p":[31,128],"pp":"53","s":"Japan's economy: rice collection at 20 percent of quotas, coal up 41 percent, 394 plants held for reparations, unions growing, ¥56 billion note issue, and 41 firms added to the Zaibatsu schedule."},{"t":"Agriculture and Fisheries","lv":2,"p":[32,48],"pp":"55","s":"Government rice collections reached only 20 percent of quotas amid black markets; the Agricultural Association Law was amended toward democratization; a fuel-oil link system was set up to stimulate fish deliveries."},{"t":"Forestry and Mining","lv":2,"p":[49,69],"pp":"82","s":"Charcoal and lumber shortages hampered heating and reconstruction; coal production rebounded 41 percent after increased miner rations; geological surveys assessed nonferrous metal, cement and quarrying capacity."},{"t":"Industry","lv":2,"p":[70,85],"pp":"119","s":"Allied authorities took custody of 394 aircraft plants, arsenals and laboratories for reparations; coal shortages suppressed iron, steel, chemical, textile and motor vehicle output despite new housing construction targets."},{"t":"Transportation and Public Utilities","lv":2,"p":[86,95],"pp":"151","s":"Railroads suffered neglected rolling stock while electrification and fare increases were planned; shipping stood at a quarter of prewar capacity; electric power consumption rose sharply from residential heating."},{"t":"Imports and Exports","lv":2,"p":[96,97],"pp":"169","s":"Foreign trade ran strictly under SCAP direction; U.S. Army surplus flour, diesel oil and medicines were imported; raw silk, coal, pitch and mining timbers went to Korea, China and Hong Kong."},{"t":"Labor","lv":2,"p":[98,102],"pp":"175","s":"Unions grew at two per day and planned a national federation; substantial wage increases offset rising living costs; an official registration counted 32.7 million employed and 3.3 million unemployed."},{"t":"Communications","lv":2,"p":[103,106],"pp":"185","s":"International radio and telephone circuits to the United States and Britain were restored; signal facilities supported occupation forces; a goal of 2.1 million radio receivers was set despite vacuum tube shortages."},{"t":"Rationing and Price Control","lv":2,"p":[107,117],"pp":"191","s":"Staple food collections lagged, leaving Hokkaido cities with as little as two days of reserves; the government abandoned vegetable auctions and reimposed official ceiling prices on perishable foods."},{"t":"Money and Banking","lv":2,"p":[118,122],"pp":"211","s":"Bank of Japan note issue hit a record ¥56 billion; direct government overdraft borrowing was halted; commercial banks limited credit to essential producers; closed colonial and Axis financial institutions were liquidated."},{"t":"Public Finance","lv":2,"p":[123,128],"pp":"221","s":"SCAP ordered fiscal retrenchment and abolition of the Special Account for War Expenses; capital levy and war profit tax drafts were prepared; 41 companies including Oji Paper joined the Zaibatsu schedule."},{"t":"Part IV: Social - Japan","lv":1,"p":[129,152],"pp":"233","s":"Japan's social affairs: relief and migration controls, smallpox vaccination, repatriation figures, appointment of educators to the Education Ministry, textbook screening, severing Shinto from the state, and media campaigns."},{"t":"Public Health and Welfare","lv":2,"p":[130,142],"pp":"235","s":"SCAP curbed rural-to-urban migration; the Welfare Ministry estimated 8,000,000 people needed relief; smallpox prompted nationwide vaccination; repatriation processed over 1.2 million returnees and 720,000 departing foreign nationals."},{"t":"Education, Religion and Media of Expression","lv":2,"p":[143,152],"pp":"261","s":"Professional educators were appointed to head the Ministry of Education; English translations were required for textbook screening; Shinto was severed from state control; media explained SCAP's political purge."},{"t":"Part V: Korea","lv":1,"p":[153,160],"pp":"279","s":"Southern Korea under USAMGIK: Moscow Agreement protests and strikes, stalled coalition talks, Special Judicial Officers, constabulary recruitment, rice hoarding, trade licensing, coal shortages and refugee relief."},{"t":"Political Activities in Korea","lv":2,"p":[154,156],"pp":"281","s":"Strikes and turmoil followed the Moscow Agreement's trusteeship announcement; Right-Left coalition talks stalled; USAMGIK created Special Judicial Officers; recruitment began for the Korean Constabulary."},{"t":"Economic Activities in Korea","lv":2,"p":[157,158],"pp":"287","s":"Rice hoarding persisted despite ceiling prices and incentives; foreign trade came under rigid licensing; the Chosen Stock Exchange was dissolved; coal shortages from Japan crippled southern Korean manufacturing."},{"t":"Social Activities in Korea","lv":2,"p":[159,160],"pp":"291","s":"Committees drafted democratic school programs; free civic newspapers passed one million circulation; procedures were set to recover cultural property taken by Japan; relief and disease control aided northern refugees."}],"by":"gemini-3.8-flash-high"},{"id":"J-04-nlm","ia":"summation-non-military-activities-japan-korea-no-04-1946-01-nlm","s":"J","no":4,"d":"1946-01","d2":"","t":"Summation of Non-Military Activities in Japan and Korea, No. 4 (January 1946)","v":"NLM copy","pg":306,"o":"Summation No. 4 documents non-military activities of the Allied occupation in Japan and southern Korea during January 1946, a pivotal month of institutional overhaul and democratic reform. In Japan, SCAP issued the sweeping 4 January purge directives banning militaristic organizations and disqualifying wartime leaders from public office, triggering a crisis and reorganization of the Shidehara Cabinet. Emperor Hirohito issued his historic New Year Rescript renouncing imperial divinity, while SCAP abolished licensed prostitution, promulgated the Charter for the International Military Tribunal for the Far East, and placed 394 industrial plants under custody for reparations. Severe economic strains persisted, marked by lagging food collections, runaway inflation, a ¥56 billion banknote issue, and critical coal and charcoal shortages, which catalyzed rapid labor unionization. In Korea, General Orders No. 1 formally integrated the United States Army Military Government in Korea (USAMGIK). Nationwide civil unrest erupted following the Moscow Conference trusteeship announcement, prompting US-Soviet talks in Seoul, while severe rice hoarding forced emergency economic controls. For historians, the report provides invaluable contemporary statistical data, organograms, and candid accounts of early demilitarization, institutional democratization, and the divergent political trajectories of occupied Japan and Korea.","h":["SCAP's 4 January purge directives banned ultra-nationalistic societies and disqualified wartime leaders from public office, triggering a crisis and reorganization of the Shidehara Cabinet.","Emperor Hirohito's 1 January New Year Rescript reaffirmed the Meiji Charter Oath and renounced the doctrine of imperial divinity.","The Charter of the International Military Tribunal for the Far East was promulgated on 19 January, and 171 war crimes suspects, including Koki Hirota and Yosuke Matsuoka, were apprehended.","SCAP ordered the abolition of licensed prostitution on 21 January, nullifying all contracts that bound women to involuntary servitude.","Allied authorities took 394 Japanese aircraft factories, arsenals and laboratories into custody as an initial step toward interim reparations.","Bank of Japan note circulation reached ¥56 billion, SCAP limited monthly General Account expenditures to ¥2 billion, and coal output rose 41 percent to 1.18 million tons.","General Orders No. 1 established USAMGIK, and violent Korean demonstrations against the Moscow Conference trusteeship decision spread across southern Korea, prompting US-Soviet talks in Seoul.","Union formation in Japan expanded by two a day, with preparatory meetings for a National Federation of Labor, while rice hoarding in Japan and Korea forced emergency food controls."],"k":["SCAP purge directives","Imperial Rescript","humanity declaration","Shidehara Cabinet","International Military Tribunal for the Far East","licensed prostitution abolition","interim reparations","USAMGIK","Moscow Conference trusteeship","rice hoarding","labor unionization","Bank of Japan note issue"],"toc":[{"t":"Front matter","lv":1,"p":[1,10],"pp":"","s":"Title pages, printed table of contents for the whole report, and list of charts, graphs, maps and tables."},{"t":"Part I: General","lv":1,"p":[11,26],"pp":"1","s":"General summary of January 1946: the IMTFE and USAMGIK established, the 4 January purge directives, 394 plants taken into custody, currency at ¥56 billion, rapid unionization, and Korean protests over trusteeship."},{"t":"Section 1: Organization Under SCAP","lv":2,"p":[13,13],"pp":"3","s":"Reports establishment of the International Military Tribunal for the Far East, creation of the General Accounting Section, transfer of Sixth Army territory to Eighth Army, and establishment of USAMGIK in Korea."},{"t":"Section 2: Political Activities in Japan","lv":2,"p":[13,16],"pp":"3","s":"Covers the 4 January purge directives, the Emperor's New Year Rescript denying divinity, Shidehara Cabinet reorganization, party preparations for elections, abolition of licensed prostitution, and war crimes apprehensions."},{"t":"Section 3: Economic Activities in Japan","lv":2,"p":[17,20],"pp":"7","s":"Coal production gains, Allied custody of 394 industrial plants for reparations, currency expansion to ¥56 billion, lagging food collections, and rapid labor union formation."},{"t":"Section 4: Social Activities in Japan","lv":2,"p":[21,24],"pp":"11","s":"Communicable disease control, Tokyo nutrition surveys, abolition of licensed prostitution, textbook screening, Shinto directive compliance, and appointment of professional educators to the Ministry of Education."},{"t":"Section 5: Civil Administration in Korea","lv":2,"p":[25,26],"pp":"15","s":"Integration of USAMGIK, Korean demonstrations against the Moscow Conference trusteeship decision, judicial and constabulary reorganization, rice hoarding countermeasures, and educational planning."},{"t":"Part II: Political - Japan","lv":1,"p":[27,62],"pp":"17","s":"Japanese politics in January 1946: the 4 January purge and cabinet reorganization, public safety and censorship, 171 war crimes suspects apprehended, and promulgation of the IMTFE Charter."},{"t":"Section 1: Government and Civil Administration","lv":2,"p":[29,42],"pp":"19","s":"Impact of SCAP's 4 January purge directives, Shidehara Cabinet reorganization, the Emperor's renunciation of divinity, party maneuvers after Sanzo Nozaka's return, and severance of Japanese administrative ties with overseas areas."},{"t":"Section 2: Public Safety and Intelligence","lv":2,"p":[43,50],"pp":"33","s":"Black-market rings, poisonous bootleg liquor, disbanding of the auxiliary Keibei-tai, rearming of civil police with revolvers, internment of former German Ambassador Stahmer, and media censorship."},{"t":"Section 3: Legal and War Crimes","lv":2,"p":[51,62],"pp":"41","s":"Legal opinions on occupation law, apprehension of 171 war crimes suspects including Koki Hirota and Yosuke Matsuoka, three Yokohama atrocity convictions, and promulgation of the IMTFE Charter."},{"t":"Part III: Economic - Japan","lv":1,"p":[63,242],"pp":"53","s":"Japan's economy: lagging rice collections, a 41 percent coal increase to 1.18 million tons, custody of 394 plants, labor union growth, ¥56 billion note circulation, and a ¥2 billion monthly spending limit."},{"t":"Section 1: Agriculture and Fisheries","lv":2,"p":[65,92],"pp":"55","s":"Lagging government rice collections caused by hoarding, Diet amendments democratizing the Agricultural Cooperative Association, critical vegetable seed shortages, and fishing fleet rehabilitation."},{"t":"Section 2: Forestry and Mining","lv":2,"p":[93,128],"pp":"83","s":"Severe urban charcoal shortages, a 41 percent increase in coal production to 1.18 million tons, mineral mining operations, timber zones, and surveys of hydroelectric resources."},{"t":"Section 3: Industry","lv":2,"p":[129,160],"pp":"119","s":"Custody of 394 plants for interim reparations, production declines from the New Year holiday and fuel shortages, rolling mill outputs, and national housing construction allotment plans."},{"t":"Section 4: Transportation and Public Utilities","lv":2,"p":[161,178],"pp":"151","s":"Railroad rehabilitation with increased coal allocations, acute coastal shipping shortages, electric power production and labor settlements, and strict daily gas ration limits."},{"t":"Section 5: Imports and Exports","lv":2,"p":[179,182],"pp":"169","s":"Arrival of U.S. Army flour and fuel imports, raw silk, silkworm eggs and seedlings readied for export, coal exports to Hong Kong and Korea, and restrictions on pearl transactions."},{"t":"Section 6: Labor","lv":2,"p":[183,192],"pp":"173","s":"Union formation expanding by two a day, preparatory meetings for a National Federation of Labor, wage-price disparities, worker 'production control' disputes, and seamen recruitment for repatriation."},{"t":"Section 7: Communications","lv":2,"p":[193,200],"pp":"183","s":"Rehabilitation of war-damaged wire facilities, reopened radiotelephone service to the U.S. and radiotelegraph to London, an Advisory Committee for Broadcasting, and postal service surveys."},{"t":"Section 8: Rationing and Price Control","lv":2,"p":[201,220],"pp":"191","s":"Widespread staple food withholding, critical shortages in Hokkaido, a 16,020-family Tokyo nutrition survey, re-established vegetable price ceilings, and rampant black-market activity."},{"t":"Section 9: Money and Banking","lv":2,"p":[221,230],"pp":"211","s":"Bank of Japan note circulation reaching ¥56 billion, commercial bank credit restrictions, prohibition of government overdrafts with the central bank, and liquidation of colonial banks."},{"t":"Section 10: Public Finance","lv":2,"p":[231,242],"pp":"221","s":"SCAP fiscal directives limiting monthly General Account expenditures to ¥2 billion, the submitted 1946-47 budget, draft capital levy and war profit tax laws, national debt figures, and Zaibatsu restriction lists."},{"t":"Part IV: Social - Japan","lv":1,"p":[243,288],"pp":"233","s":"Social conditions in Japan: relief for eight million needy citizens, typhus and smallpox control, abolition of licensed prostitution, textbook screening, Shinto separation from the state, and censorship of militaristic films."},{"t":"Section 1: Public Health and Welfare","lv":2,"p":[245,270],"pp":"235","s":"Directives restricting rural-to-urban migration, relief for eight million needy citizens, repatriation movements, control of typhus and smallpox outbreaks, Tokyo nutrition surveys, and abolition of licensed prostitution."},{"t":"Section 2: Education, Religion and Media of Expression","lv":2,"p":[271,288],"pp":"261","s":"Appointment of professional educators to the Education Ministry, textbook screening and deletions, separation of Shinto from the state, broadcasting reorganization, and censorship of militaristic films."},{"t":"Part V: Korea","lv":1,"p":[289,306],"pp":"279","s":"Southern Korea under USAMGIK: General Orders No. 1, protests against the Moscow trusteeship decision, Korean Constabulary, rice hoarding controls, JODK radio expansion, and dissolution of the Chosen Stock Exchange."},{"t":"Section 1: Political Activities in Korea","lv":2,"p":[291,296],"pp":"281","s":"Establishment of USAMGIK under General Orders No. 1, Korean protests against the Moscow Agreement trusteeship, US-Soviet joint conferences, creation of the Korean Constabulary, and repatriation."},{"t":"Section 2: Economic Activities in Korea","lv":2,"p":[297,300],"pp":"287","s":"Severe rice hoarding and emergency distribution countermeasures, formation of the Korean Agriculture Association, silk industry reorganization, mineral and electric power coordination, and dissolution of the Chosen Stock Exchange."},{"t":"Section 3: Social Activities in Korea","lv":2,"p":[301,306],"pp":"291","s":"Educational planning conferences, teacher institutes, expansion of JODK radio broadcasting and weekly news publications, restitution of looted cultural assets, refugee disease control, and dissolution of the Japanese medical monopoly."}],"by":"gemini-3.8-flash-high"},{"id":"J-04-phw","ia":"summation-non-military-activities-japan-korea-no-04-1946-01-public-health-welfare","s":"J","no":4,"d":"1946-01","d2":"","t":"Summation of Non-Military Activities in Japan and Korea, No. 4 (January 1946): Public Health and Welfare","v":"Public Health and Welfare section","pg":26,"o":"This monthly report covers public health and welfare activities in Japan for January 1946 under the early Allied Occupation. Major developments include SCAP directives restricting civilian migration into devastated major cities, the Japanese Ministry of Health and Welfare’s estimate of 8,000,000 persons requiring relief, and the logistics of massive bilateral repatriation movements. The report documents major public health initiatives: addressing widespread smallpox outbreaks and regional typhus cases, expanding civilian hospital capacity by 73,502 beds through military facility transfers, standardizing biological products like penicillin and vaccines, and establishing rigorous controls over narcotics and returned Japanese military medical stocks. Notably, on 21 January SCAP issued a landmark directive abolishing licensed prostitution and voiding all contracts binding women to the sex trade. For historians, this summation provides critical statistical benchmarks, including nationwide 1945 communicable disease rates across 46 prefectures, comprehensive veterinary and social insurance figures, and detailed dietary data from a survey of 30,000 Tokyo residents revealing an average daily intake of 1,970 calories and extensive reliance on the black market.","h":["On 21 January, SCAP directed the Japanese Government to abolish licensed prostitution and nullify all contracts binding women to the practice.","The Ministry of Health and Welfare estimated that 8,000,000 persons in Japan needed relief as of 1 January 1946, while SCAP directed restrictions on migration into large cities.","By 27 January 1946, 730,458 foreign nationals had been repatriated from Japan, while 1,245,930 Japanese ex-servicemen and civilians had returned.","Since October 1945, civilian hospital capacity expanded by 413 facilities and 73,502 beds, largely through transfers of former military hospitals, leaving bed occupancy below 40 percent.","On 27 January, SCAP ordered comprehensive legislation establishing strict annual registration, licensing and transaction accounting for all narcotic dealers; returned military medical stocks were also inventoried and controlled.","Smallpox spread widely across Japan in January, with major epidemic foci in Hyogo, Aichi, Nagasaki and Hokkaido prompting nationwide vaccination planning; regional typhus cases were also reported.","A December nutrition survey of 30,000 Tokyo residents showed an average intake of 1,970 calories, with nearly half obtained from black-market sources.","A warehouse fire destroyed a three-month supply of one-third of essential dental materials, even as precious metals were released for dental use."],"k":["Public Health and Welfare","repatriation","licensed prostitution","relief","communicable diseases","smallpox","typhus","narcotic control","nutrition survey","medical supplies","social insurance","hospital administration"],"toc":[{"t":"Front matter","lv":1,"p":[1,1],"pp":"","s":"Report header and printed table of contents for Section 1, Public Health and Welfare."},{"t":"Public Welfare","lv":1,"p":[1,3],"pp":"235","s":"SCAP restricted migration to large cities; Japan estimated 8,000,000 persons needing relief. Repatriation continued, with 730,458 foreign nationals leaving Japan and 1,245,930 Japanese returning."},{"t":"Hospital Administration","lv":1,"p":[4,4],"pp":"238","s":"Reconstruction and transfers of former military hospitals added 73,502 civilian beds since October 1945, leaving bed occupancy below 40 percent although patient totals stayed stable."},{"t":"Veterinary Affairs","lv":1,"p":[4,6],"pp":"238","s":"Japanese dairy (1933 Code) and animal disease laws translated; the 11-track racing association idle since 1943; December 1945 outbreaks led by 2,791 cases of white diarrhea in chickens; 1944-46 antiserum production figures."},{"t":"Dental Affairs","lv":1,"p":[7,7],"pp":"241","s":"Precious metals were released for dental use and dental goods got transport priority, but a warehouse fire destroyed a three-month supply of one-third of essential dental materials."},{"t":"Nursing Affairs","lv":1,"p":[7,7],"pp":"241","s":"Japanese public health nursing regulations were evaluated, Red Cross first-aid manuals approved for reprinting, and the Ministry of Health and Welfare organized three new graded public health nursing courses."},{"t":"Medical Supply","lv":1,"p":[8,13],"pp":"242","s":"Priority allocations and price increases stimulated medicine production; returned military medical stocks and narcotics were concentrated and inventoried. On 27 January SCAP ordered strict registration, licensing and accounting for narcotic dealers."},{"t":"Legal Affairs","lv":1,"p":[14,14],"pp":"248","s":"SCAP directed abolition of licensed prostitution and nullification of binding contracts on 21 January; the Ministry of Health and Welfare submitted a ministry-wide reorganization and personnel reduction plan."},{"t":"Preventive Medicine","lv":1,"p":[15,26],"pp":"249","s":"Surveys 1945 communicable disease incidence and smallpox and typhus outbreaks (foci in Hyogo, Aichi, Nagasaki, Hokkaido), plus water rehabilitation, a 30,000-person Tokyo nutrition survey (1,970 calories average) and venereal disease control."}],"by":"gemini-3.8-flash-high"},{"id":"J-05","ia":"summation-non-military-activities-japan-korea-no-05-1946-02","s":"J","no":5,"d":"1946-02","d2":"","t":"Summation of Non-Military Activities in Japan and Korea, No. 5 (February 1946)","v":"","pg":158,"o":"Summation No. 5 covers non-military activities of the Allied occupation in Japan and the U.S. Army Military Government in Korea (USAMGIK) during February 1946. In Japan, SCAP and the Shidehara Cabinet vigorously enforced the 4 January purge directives, disqualifying roughly 90 percent of former Diet members and dissolving 120 ultranationalist organizations, while postponing national elections to 10 April. Confronting rampant inflation and critical food shortages, the Japanese government promulgated emergency ordinances blocking bank deposits, mandating currency conversion, expropriating hoarded staple crops, and establishing price controls. Meanwhile, labor unions gained legal recognition under the new Labor Union Law, with membership approaching one million, as workers increasingly deployed 'production control' strikes. In social affairs, SCAP directed the abolition of licensed prostitution, implemented mass typhus control in Osaka, and expanded democratic media dissemination. In southern Korea, USAMGIK established the Representative Democratic Council headed by Syngman Rhee, enacted strict regulations on political parties, instituted forced rice collections, and concluded initial interzone administrative agreements with Soviet authorities. For historians, this report provides invaluable contemporary statistical data and policy documentation detailing the structural democratization, economic crises, and institutional transitions across postwar Japan and Korea.","h":["Japan enforced the 4 January purge directives, disqualifying roughly 90 percent of former Diet members and dissolving 120 ultranationalist organizations, while general elections were postponed from 21 March to 10 April 1946 for candidate screening.","The Supreme Commander appointed nine judges to the International Military Tribunal for the Far East, with Sir William Flood Webb of Australia as President; 81 war crime suspects were apprehended and Yokohama trials began.","Emergency Imperial ordinances blocked bank deposits, converted old yen currency, and authorized compulsory expropriation of hoarded staple foods (Ordinance No. 66) amid rampant inflation and critical food shortages.","The 1945-46 rice collection reached only 50 percent of quota, while February coal production reached 1,364,000 metric tons and railway deficits prompted rate increases of 250 to 300 percent.","Procedural regulations for the Labor Union Law were promulgated as union membership approached one million, and the cabinet debated workers' 'production control' strikes.","Japan abolished licensed prostitution and nullified contracts between prostitutes and managers; mass DDT dusting was used against a major typhus outbreak in Osaka.","USAMGIK established the Representative Democratic Council under Syngman Rhee, regulated political parties under Ordinance No. 55, and began compulsory rice collection under Ordinance No. 45.","U.S. and Soviet commanders concluded initial interzone agreements on transportation and communication, while Korea faced fertilizer shortages after the cutoff from North Korea."],"k":["Political Purge","Constitutional Revision","International Military Tribunal for the Far East","Currency Conversion","Deposit Freeze","Rice Expropriation","Labor Union Law","Production Control","Zaibatsu Dissolution","Abolition of Licensed Prostitution","USAMGIK","Representative Democratic Council"],"toc":[{"t":"Front Matter","lv":1,"p":[1,2],"pp":"","s":"Cover, table of contents, and list of charts, graphs, and maps for the February 1946 summation."},{"t":"Part I: General","lv":1,"p":[3,14],"pp":"1","s":"Executive summaries for Japan and Korea: purge enforcement, elections postponed to 10 April, currency conversion and deposit freeze, relief and repatriation, and USAMGIK's Representative Democratic Council."},{"t":"Section 1: Organization under SCAP","lv":2,"p":[6,6],"pp":"5","s":"No changes in GHQ SCAP organization; USAMGIK redesignated several bureaus and created the Korean Economic Advisory Board."},{"t":"Section 2: Political Activities in Japan","lv":2,"p":[6,8],"pp":"5","s":"Purge enforcement affected 90 percent of former Diet members; elections postponed to 10 April; political party maneuvers, civil service reform, and war crimes tribunal preparations."},{"t":"Section 3: Economic Activities in Japan","lv":2,"p":[9,10],"pp":"11","s":"Rice collections lagged while coal and steel output rose modestly; emergency ordinances froze bank deposits and converted currency; import-export planning for China and Korea."},{"t":"Section 4: Social Activities in Japan","lv":2,"p":[11,12],"pp":"18","s":"Relief programs consolidated; repatriation milestones; textbook screening; Shinto separated from state control; democratic media programs expanded."},{"t":"Section 5: Civil Administration in Korea","lv":2,"p":[13,14],"pp":"19","s":"USAMGIK reorganized; Representative Democratic Council established under Syngman Rhee; US-Soviet conference held; compulsory rice collection under Ordinance No. 45; currency controls imposed."},{"t":"Part II: Political - Japan","lv":1,"p":[15,33],"pp":"33","s":"Political and legal reports for Japan: the 4 January purge directives and election postponement, food-shortage unrest and police surveys, and war crimes arrests, trials and tribunal judges."},{"t":"Section 1: Government and Civil Administration","lv":2,"p":[16,24],"pp":"33","s":"Enforcement of the 4 January purge directives dissolved 120 militarist organizations; general election postponed to 10 April; party platforms, civil service restructuring, and the Emperor's inspection tours."},{"t":"Section 2: Public Safety and Intelligence","lv":2,"p":[25,29],"pp":"43","s":"Civil unrest over food shortages, police raids on hoarded goods, police-system surveys by Americans Lewis J. Valentine and Oscar Olander, and censorship operations."},{"t":"Section 3: Legal and War Crimes","lv":2,"p":[30,33],"pp":"53","s":"Japanese court jurisdiction over UN nationals restricted; 81 war crime suspects apprehended; first military commission trials at Yokohama; nine judges appointed to the International Military Tribunal."},{"t":"Part III: Economic - Japan","lv":1,"p":[34,131],"pp":"61","s":"Economic reports for Japan covering agriculture, fisheries, forestry, mining, industry, transport, communications, labor, trade, price control, banking and public finance."},{"t":"Section 1: Agriculture and Fisheries","lv":2,"p":[35,42],"pp":"63","s":"1945-46 rice collection reached only 50 percent of quota; sweet potato trends, fuel allotments for Hokkaido herring fleets, Miyagi seed oyster culture, and democratized fishermen's cooperatives."},{"t":"Section 2: Forestry and Mining","lv":2,"p":[43,59],"pp":"79","s":"Sawmill capacity and critical lumber shortages; February coal output of 1,364,000 metric tons; coal operator subsidies; petroleum exploration plans; sub-marginal nickeliferous iron ore."},{"t":"Section 3: Industry and Industrial Reparations","lv":2,"p":[60,61],"pp":"111","s":"Industrial production limited by coal and labor shortages; SCAP Reparations Branch established; Japanese government maintains aircraft factories and arsenals designated for reparations."},{"t":"Section 4: Heavy Industries","lv":2,"p":[62,68],"pp":"115","s":"Production and stock statistics for aluminum, copper, zinc, lead and ferroalloys; petroleum output; emergency construction plans; ship repair; heavy chemicals."},{"t":"Section 5: Manufacturing","lv":2,"p":[69,76],"pp":"129","s":"Flour milling and sake brewing; declining pulp and paper output; sheet glass and refractories; electrical manufacturing associations reorganized; motor vehicle production and demand."},{"t":"Section 6: Textile Industries","lv":2,"p":[77,84],"pp":"145","s":"Textiles declined after New Year shutdowns; preparations to receive 20,000 short tons of American raw cotton; raw silk ceiling prices raised; rayon, wool, knitting and leather output."},{"t":"Section 7: Transportation and Public Utilities","lv":2,"p":[85,94],"pp":"161","s":"Motor transport shortages; railway deficits prompting 250 to 300 percent rate increases; coastal shipping; hydro-electric load increases requiring supplemental steam plants; gas utility restrictions."},{"t":"Section 8: Communications","lv":2,"p":[95,101],"pp":"177","s":"Board of Communications reorganized; long-distance carrier cables rehabilitated; trans-Pacific radiotelephone service expanded; communication equipment manufacturing; financial statements of major communication companies."},{"t":"Section 9: Labor","lv":2,"p":[102,105],"pp":"191","s":"Procedural regulations for the Labor Union Law promulgated; union membership approaching one million; cabinet debates over 'production control' strikes; wage adjustments; coal miner recruitment."},{"t":"Section 10: Imports and Exports","lv":2,"p":[106,107],"pp":"199","s":"Foreign trade runs through the revolving Foreign Trade Fund; 1946 export plan; raw cotton shipments from the United States; interim coal, timber and seed trade with Korea and China."},{"t":"Section 11: Rationing and Price Control","lv":2,"p":[108,119],"pp":"203","s":"Imperial Ordinance No. 66 authorizes compulsory expropriation of hoarded food; revised consumer ceiling prices; coal allocation schedules; emergency urban housing; economic police controls on street vendors."},{"t":"Section 12: Money and Banking","lv":2,"p":[120,123],"pp":"225","s":"Emergency ordinances block bank deposits and enforce currency conversion; Bank of Japan note issues rise; financial mergers restricted; colonial banks liquidated."},{"t":"Section 13: Public Finance","lv":2,"p":[124,131],"pp":"233","s":"Budget adjusted under inflation; Special Account for War Expenses abolished; local government borrowing; Imperial Household assets appraised; anti-monopoly measures against the Big Four zaibatsu."},{"t":"Part IV: Social - Japan","lv":1,"p":[132,147],"pp":"247","s":"Social reports for Japan: public health, relief, typhus control and abolition of licensed prostitution, plus education, Shinto, cultural properties and information media."},{"t":"Section 1: Public Health and Welfare","lv":2,"p":[133,141],"pp":"249","s":"Unified public relief program; nationwide smallpox vaccination; mass DDT dusting against a major Osaka typhus outbreak; veterinary hygiene surveys; abolition of licensed prostitution."},{"t":"Section 2: Education, Religion and Media of Expression","lv":2,"p":[142,147],"pp":"267","s":"Library censorship abrogated; textbook screening; Home Ministry's Shrine Board abolished; cultural properties protected; information campaigns on democracy, labor unions and women's suffrage."},{"t":"Part V: Korea","lv":1,"p":[148,158],"pp":"279","s":"Korea under USAMGIK: political reorganization, Ordinance No. 55, the Representative Democratic Council, US-Soviet agreements, rice collection, fertilizer shortage, schools and smallpox control."},{"t":"Section 1: Political Activities in Korea","lv":2,"p":[149,152],"pp":"281","s":"USAMGIK reorganized; political parties regulated by Ordinance No. 55; Representative Democratic Council formed; US-Soviet joint conference agreements; Korean Constabulary and Coast Guard recruitment."},{"t":"Section 2: Economic Activities in Korea","lv":2,"p":[153,156],"pp":"289","s":"Compulsory rice collections under Ordinance No. 45; acute fertilizer shortage after cutoff from North Korea; Seoul factories reopened; 75 percent railway curtailment; Japanese currency deposited."},{"t":"Section 3: Social Activities in Korea","lv":2,"p":[157,158],"pp":"297","s":"School attendance exceeded pre-war levels; Ewha Girls College and Seoul College of Law reopened; smallpox controlled with domestic vaccines; Seoul incorporated refugee relief societies."}],"by":"gemini-3.8-flash-high"},{"id":"J-05-phw","ia":"summation-non-military-activities-japan-korea-no-05-1946-02-public-health-welfare","s":"J","no":5,"d":"1946-02","d2":"","t":"Summation of Non-Military Activities in Japan and Korea, No. 5 (February 1946): Public Health and Welfare","v":"Public Health and Welfare section","pg":19,"o":"This February 1946 monthly report details public health, welfare administration, and disease control across occupied Japan during early post-surrender reconstruction. Key developments center on SCAP directives reforming Japanese relief administration into a centralized, non-discriminatory public assistance program that explicitly abolished military pensions and preferential treatment for ex-servicemen. To avert humanitarian crisis, Occupation authorities released former military stocks—including over 111,000 metric tons of cereals and 430,000 blankets—and supervised relief for foreign nationals. In public health, the report highlights an aggressive response to an escalating Osaka typhus outbreak through a mass DDT dusting campaign reaching over 90 percent of the city's populace, alongside rapid expansion of smallpox vaccine production exceeding 10 million doses monthly. The report also documents the rehabilitation of 2,579 bombing-displaced dentists, a new national public health nursing curriculum, and strict narcotics controls including nationwide heroin confiscation. Historians will find valuable quantitative baselines on social insurance funds, civilian hospital capacity, communicable disease curves, and repatriation flows tracking 829,000 repatriated foreign nationals and 1.46 million returning Japanese, illustrating the institutional transition from Imperial wartime systems to demilitarized civilian governance.","h":["A SCAP directive overhauled public assistance into a centralized relief system based strictly on need, abolishing preferential pensions and aid for military ex-servicemen.","An emergency mass DDT dusting campaign in Osaka reached over 90 percent of the population to suppress a typhus outbreak that exceeded 400 cases.","Relief reserves from returned military stocks began distribution, including 430,000 blankets, 150,000 overcoats, 200,000 uniforms and 111,457 metric tons of cereals.","Smallpox vaccine production rose sharply, surpassing the February goal of 10,250,000 doses, to combat smallpox outbreaks across Japan.","Repatriation through 24 February 1946 totaled 768,209 Koreans, 30,695 Chinese, 18,532 Formosans, 12,187 Ryukyuans and 1,465,980 returning Japanese.","Japanese authorities ordered nationwide confiscation of heroin and set deadlines for prosecuting unauthorized possession of the narcotic.","Of 5,932 dentists displaced by wartime bombing, 2,579 were rehabilitated and re-established in practice, though equipment shortages persisted.","A new Ministry of Health public health nursing curriculum takes effect in April 1946, with plans assisted for a Kyushu training institute."],"k":["public assistance","typhus control","DDT dusting","smallpox vaccine","repatriation","social insurance","military pensions","narcotics control","public health nursing","veterinary inspection","medical supplies","displaced dentists"],"toc":[{"t":"Front matter","lv":1,"p":[1,1],"pp":"","s":"Cover title and printed table of contents for Section I (Public Health and Welfare) of Monthly Summary No. 5."},{"t":"General","lv":1,"p":[1,2],"pp":"1","s":"Japan plans to restrict rural-to-urban migration; veterinary inspections in southern Japan; smallpox vaccine output rises; an emergency mass DDT dusting in Osaka reached over 90 percent of residents to suppress typhus."},{"t":"Public Welfare","lv":1,"p":[3,5],"pp":"3","s":"A directive creates unified, non-discriminatory public assistance, ending veteran preferences. Military blanket and clothing reserves are released, social insurance fund balances reviewed, foreign-national relief organized, and repatriation movements overseen."},{"t":"Administration of Hospitals","lv":1,"p":[6,6],"pp":"6","s":"Inspections of medical supply storage and narcotic safeguarding at Tachikawa National Hospital; civilian hospital bed capacity and patient statistics are referenced across prefectures."},{"t":"Veterinary Affairs","lv":1,"p":[6,7],"pp":"6","s":"Surveys former Japanese army veterinary institutions and Tokyo Veterinary College, reviews November-December 1945 meat and milk inspections, and compiles animal disease tables including the 1945 equine encephalitis outbreak."},{"t":"Dental Affairs","lv":1,"p":[8,8],"pp":"8","s":"Sales of controlled dental items rose in January 1946; 2,579 bombing-displaced dentists were rehabilitated and re-established in practice despite equipment shortages."},{"t":"Nursing Affairs","lv":1,"p":[9,9],"pp":"9","s":"The Ministry of Health's new public health nursing curriculum takes effect April 1946; plans for a Kyushu training institute are assisted, and articles on Japanese nursing are prepared for publication."},{"t":"Supply","lv":1,"p":[9,12],"pp":"9","s":"Covers distribution of new pharmaceuticals and returned military supplies, mandated heroin confiscation, smallpox vaccine output past 10 million monthly doses, and Korean pyrophyllite rock explored for regional DDT powder production."},{"t":"Charts","lv":1,"p":[13,19],"pp":"","s":"Statistical tables and charts on school expenditures, communicable disease incidence, medical supply production values, nurse training curricula, civilian hospital capacities, and repatriation flows through late February 1946."}],"by":"gemini-3.8-flash-high"},{"id":"J-06","ia":"summation-non-military-activities-japan-no-06-1946-03","s":"J","no":6,"d":"1946-03","d2":"","t":"Summation of Non-Military Activities in Japan, No. 6 (March 1946)","v":"","pg":131,"o":"Covering March 1946, this summation documents a critical transitional month in the early Allied occupation of Japan, marked by profound constitutional, political, and economic initiatives. Most notable is the publication of the SCAP-approved draft Constitution of Japan, featuring popular sovereignty, imperial demotion to a state symbol, and the Article 9 renunciation of war. In politics, intense campaigning and voter education unfolded ahead of the 10 April general election—the first featuring female suffrage—alongside rigorous surveillance by Occupation Forces and broadened purges of wartime elites. Economically, SCAP authorized the first post-war raw silk shipments to the United States to generate foreign credits, while Japanese authorities tackled soaring inflation through an emergency monetary conversion that drastically contracted Bank of Japan note circulation. The report also highlights the enforcement of the Labor Union Law, the investigation of zaibatsu war profits and indemnity claims, and aggressive public health measures deploying DDT dusting teams against typhus epidemics. For historians, this report provides exceptional primary documentation, including the full text of the draft constitution, pre-election campaign finance data, zaibatsu asset structures, and initial administrative preparations for the International Military Tribunal for the Far East.","h":["The draft Japanese Constitution, approved by SCAP and published on 6 March, vests sovereignty in the people, reduces the Emperor to a state symbol, and renounces war in Article 9.","Preparations for the 10 April general election, the first with women's suffrage, included party campaigning, voter education, Occupation Forces surveillance and non-interference directives, and broadened purge directives against wartime elites.","General MacArthur confirmed the Manila military commission's death sentence on General Tomoyuki Yamashita, while the courtroom and preparations for the International Military Tribunal for the Far East advanced in Tokyo.","The U.S. Education Mission headed by Dr. George D. Stoddard arrived to assist in reforming Japanese schooling, alongside compliance with the directive separating Shinto from the state.","Emergency currency conversion cut Bank of Japan note circulation from 61.8 billion yen to 15.2 billion yen, as authorities fought soaring inflation.","SCAP authorized the first post-war raw silk export, 3,000 bales shipped to New York to establish commodity credits for Japan.","The Labor Union Law took effect on 1 March and the tripartite Central Labor Relations Committee was inaugurated; mining labor standards were reinstated.","Japanese DDT dusting teams and mass smallpox vaccination fought typhus epidemics, and 180 companies were added to the Schedule of Restricted Concerns amid investigation of Zaibatsu war profits."],"k":["Constitution of Japan","General Election","Women's Suffrage","Purge Directive","International Military Tribunal for the Far East","War Crimes Trials","Currency Conversion","Zaibatsu Dissolution","Labor Union Law","Raw Silk Exports","Typhus Control","United States Education Mission"],"toc":[{"t":"Front matter","lv":1,"p":[1,3],"pp":"","s":"Cover, title pages, general table of contents, and list of charts, graphs, maps and tables for the March 1946 report."},{"t":"Part I: General","lv":1,"p":[4,10],"pp":"1","s":"Monthly overview: draft constitution published, 10 April election preparations, first raw silk export to New York, emergency currency conversion, typhus and smallpox control, and arrival of the U.S. Education Mission."},{"t":"Organization under SCAP","lv":2,"p":[5,5],"pp":"2","s":"Establishes the Office of the Civil Property Custodian under SCAP and amends directives to place the Izu Islands north of and including Sofu Gan within Japan."},{"t":"Political Activities","lv":2,"p":[5,8],"pp":"3","s":"Summarizes publication of the draft constitution, party campaigns for the 10 April general election, extension of the purge directive, and establishment of occupation provost courts."},{"t":"Economic Activities","lv":2,"p":[9,9],"pp":"9","s":"Notes relaxed industrial controls, rail transport gains, the first raw silk export shipment to New York, emergency currency conversion, and the wartime asset growth of the Zaibatsu."},{"t":"Social Activities","lv":2,"p":[10,10],"pp":"12","s":"Outlines smallpox and typhus control, repatriation progress, arrival of the U.S. Education Mission, separation of Shinto from the state, and women's campaign activities."},{"t":"Part II: Political","lv":1,"p":[11,36],"pp":"15","s":"Covers the draft constitution and election preparations, black-market raids and the Valentine police mission, and Eighth Army provost courts, Yamashita's death sentence and Tokyo tribunal preparations."},{"t":"Government and Civil Administration","lv":2,"p":[12,27],"pp":"17","s":"Presents the full draft constitution renouncing war and vesting sovereignty in the people, party campaigns and pre-campaign spending, election surveillance rules, and new purge directives."},{"t":"Public Safety and Intelligence","lv":2,"p":[28,29],"pp":"47","s":"Details black-market raids including seized raw silk and contraband opium, traffic safety drives, arrival of Lewis J. Valentine's police advisory mission, and military censorship operations."},{"t":"Legal and War Crimes","lv":2,"p":[30,36],"pp":"53","s":"Reports the legal status of Korean residents, Eighth Army provost courts, trials of military war criminals, confirmation of Tomoyuki Yamashita's death sentence, and preparations for the Tokyo tribunal."},{"t":"Part III: Economic","lv":1,"p":[37,114],"pp":"65","s":"Economic review spanning the agrarian reform proposal, coal and timber, steel and textiles, Labor Union Law enforcement, raw silk exports, flour and price ceilings, note contraction, and 180 firms added to restricted list."},{"t":"Agriculture and Fisheries","lv":2,"p":[38,41],"pp":"67","s":"Details the Japanese Government's proposed agrarian reform program, Kanto Plain soil surveys, Hokkaido herring season preparations, Bonin Islands whaling, and pearl culture at Mikimoto."},{"t":"Forestry and Mining","lv":2,"p":[42,49],"pp":"75","s":"Covers timber shortages, Kyushu pulp and paper production, coal mine strikes and output, dismissal of female and child miners, and Pacific phosphate rock surveys for fertilizer."},{"t":"Industry and Industrial Reparations","lv":2,"p":[50,51],"pp":"89","s":"Reports slight industrial production gains, relaxed government business restrictions, creation of the Japanese Reparation Council, and SCAP inspections of 152 university and government scientific institutions."},{"t":"Heavy Industries","lv":2,"p":[52,59],"pp":"93","s":"Details output of steel, aluminum, copper and petroleum, merchant shipyard repairs, chemical and fertilizer production, and machinery inspections for potential reparations."},{"t":"Manufacturing","lv":2,"p":[60,71],"pp":"108","s":"Covers production and prices in food processing, pulp and paper, glass, electrical equipment, truck chassis, rubber and leather goods, all facing fuel and raw material shortages."},{"t":"Textile Industries","lv":2,"p":[72,77],"pp":"122","s":"Reports production and mill inventories in cotton, silk, rayon, wool and hard fibers, including preparations to receive 50,000 short tons of American raw cotton."},{"t":"Transportation and Public Utilities","lv":2,"p":[78,86],"pp":"145","s":"Covers rising railway passenger and freight traffic, severe automotive fuel shortages, expanding electric power generation, and a proposal to unify Japan on 60-cycle frequency."},{"t":"Communications","lv":2,"p":[87,91],"pp":"161","s":"Details rehabilitation of domestic wire lines, overseas radiotelephone traffic, communications equipment manufacture, and the Board of Communications' request for cabinet ministry status."},{"t":"Labor","lv":2,"p":[92,96],"pp":"171","s":"Reports enforcement of the Labor Union Law, creation of the Central Labor Relations Committee, reinstated protective mining labor standards, and elimination of cut-rate convict labor."},{"t":"Imports and Exports","lv":2,"p":[97,98],"pp":"179","s":"Details shipment of 3,000 bales of raw silk to New York to establish commodity credits, salt imports from China, and the visit of the U.S. Food Survey Board."},{"t":"Rationing and Price Control","lv":2,"p":[99,106],"pp":"182","s":"Covers revised staple food collection quotas and price increases, release of imported American flour for Tokyo bread rations, and price ceilings on fertilizers, rubber and leather."},{"t":"Money and Banking","lv":2,"p":[107,110],"pp":"201","s":"Reports a sharp fall in Bank of Japan note circulation after emergency currency conversion, external asset declarations, and liquidation procedures for closed colonial banks."},{"t":"Public Finance","lv":2,"p":[111,114],"pp":"207","s":"Analyzes the national debt, 180 companies added to the Schedule of Restricted Concerns, wartime expansion of the Big Four Zaibatsu, and pending industrial war damage indemnity claims."},{"t":"Part IV: Social","lv":1,"p":[115,131],"pp":"217","s":"Covers typhus DDT dusting and smallpox vaccination, repatriation statistics, the U.S. Education Mission, State Shinto separation, women's political participation, and theatrical labor unions."},{"t":"Public Health and Welfare","lv":2,"p":[116,124],"pp":"219","s":"Details typhus control by Japanese DDT dusting teams, widespread smallpox vaccination, urban population entry controls, repatriation statistics, and organization of the Japanese Council on Medical Education."},{"t":"Education, Religion and Media of Expression","lv":2,"p":[125,131],"pp":"227","s":"Covers arrival of the U.S. Education Mission, compliance with the State Shinto separation directive, women's political participation, political broadcast regulations, and theatrical labor unions."}],"by":"gemini-3.8-flash-high"},{"id":"K-06","ia":"summation-usamg-activities-korea-no-06-1946-03","s":"K","no":6,"d":"1946-03","d2":"","t":"Summation of United States Army Military Government Activities in Korea, No. 6 (March 1946)","v":"","pg":28,"o":"Covering March 1946, this monthly summation documents the administration of the United States Army Military Government in Korea (USAMGIK) during a crucial phase of post-liberation occupation. Major political developments include the opening of the first formal sessions of the United States–Soviet Joint Commission at Duk Soo Palace on 20 March to discuss Korean independence, the dissolution of Japanese-era local councils under Ordinance No. 60, the reorganization of the Oriental Development Company into the New Korea Company Limited (Ordinance No. 52), and massive, orderly rightist celebrations on Korean Independence Day (Sam Il Day). Economically, the occupation government struggled with critical shortages of food, fertilizer, and timber. It enforced a controversial rice seizure and urban rationing program in Seoul, banned opium poppy cultivation, received emergency shipments of industrial chemicals and coal from Japan, and restarted damaged textile and industrial mills. In social affairs, extensive smallpox and typhus vaccination programs were executed, educational planning was instituted alongside bans on student political activism, and relief supplies were distributed. For historians, this report offers vital empirical data on repatriation flows, vested Japanese property management, interzone relations, and the administrative mechanics of early American military rule in South Korea.","h":["The US-Soviet Joint Commission convened on 20 March at Duk Soo Palace to discuss establishing a unified provisional Korean democratic government and Korean independence.","Ordinance No. 60 dissolved all Japanese-era provincial and local government councils and impounded their official records and funds.","The New Korea Company Limited was created under Ordinance No. 52, reorganizing the former Japanese Oriental Development Company and taking over its extensive assets.","Military Government announced plans to sell formerly Japanese-owned agricultural lands to landless Korean tenant farmers on long-term credit.","USAMGIK enforced a strict rice collection program and prepared urban rationing in Seoul amid severe food shortages and public criticism.","Korean civil police were granted authority to arrest US military personnel caught committing serious felonies, while third-degree interrogation methods were prohibited.","Repatriation of Koreans from Japan reached 831,387 cumulative returnees, while Japanese nationals remaining in South Korea dropped below 1,200.","Extensive smallpox and typhus vaccination programs were carried out, and emergency chemicals and coal arrived from Japan while damaged textile mills restarted."],"k":["USAMGIK","US-Soviet Joint Commission","New Korea Company","Oriental Development Company","Repatriation","Rice rationing","Land reform","Ordinance No. 60","Representative Democratic Council","Syngman Rhee","Kim Kyuh Sik","38th parallel"],"toc":[{"t":"Front Matter","lv":1,"p":[1,6],"pp":"","s":"Title page, publication details, printed table of contents and list of charts."},{"t":"Section 1: General","lv":1,"p":[7,10],"pp":"1","s":"March 1946: Ordinance 60 dissolved provincial councils; National Economic Board created; 134 parties registered; 831,387 Koreans repatriated from Japan by 25 March; Seoul rice stocks 7,123 tons; smallpox vaccination spread."},{"t":"Section 2: Political Activities","lv":1,"p":[11,16],"pp":"5","s":"Ordinance No. 60 dissolves local councils; the New Korea Company is created; political parties register; the US-Soviet Joint Commission convenes; repatriation continues."},{"t":"Section 3: Economic Activities","lv":1,"p":[17,24],"pp":"11","s":"Critical food shortages and rice rationing, timber scarcity, chemical imports from Japan, currency controls, reopened textile mills, and railway and shipping operations."},{"t":"Section 4: Social Activities","lv":1,"p":[25,28],"pp":"19","s":"Educational reforms and a ban on politicking in schools, smallpox and typhus inoculation campaigns, distribution of US medical and food relief supplies, and public information."}],"by":"gemini-3.8-flash-high"},{"id":"J-07","ia":"summation-non-military-activities-japan-no-07-1946-04","s":"J","no":7,"d":"1946-04","d2":"","t":"Summation of Non-Military Activities in Japan, No. 7 (April 1946)","v":"","pg":142,"o":"This seventh monthly report covers non-military activities of the Allied occupation in Japan during April 1946, a pivotal month marked by the nation's first post-war general election on 10 April. The election witnessed widespread popular participation, the debut of female suffrage with thirty-nine women elected to the Diet, and a moderate conservative victory that precipitated the en bloc resignation of the Shidehara Cabinet on 22 April. The report documents major political and legal milestones, notably the publication of the simplified draft constitution, the expansion and amendment of the International Military Tribunal for the Far East charter, and the filing of formal indictments against twenty-eight major war criminals. Economically, SCAP monitored persistent urban food deficits, post-conversion currency circulation, the establishment of the Economic Stabilization Board, and the dissolution of wartime control companies alongside rapid trade union growth. In social affairs, the report highlights the United States Education Mission recommendations, public health campaigns containing typhus and cholera, and the repatriation of over two million Japanese nationals. For historians, this summation provides invaluable statistical data, government directives, and institutional records detailing Japan’s early post-war democratization and economic restructuring.","h":["Japan held its first post-war general election on 10 April with 72.1% turnout, electing thirty-nine women to the Diet under newly granted suffrage.","Premier Kijuro Shidehara and his Cabinet resigned en bloc on 22 April amid inter-party negotiations following the general election, and a revised popular-style draft constitution was published.","An indictment of 55 counts against 28 major Japanese war criminals was filed with the International Military Tribunal for the Far East on 29 April, after amendments expanded the tribunal's membership.","The United States Education Mission submitted its report recommending administrative decentralization, curriculum democratization, language simplification, and tuition-free coeducational schooling.","General Orders No. 18 established the Diplomatic Section as a Special Staff Section under SCAP on 18 April 1946.","An Imperial Ordinance establishing the Holding Company Liquidation Commission was approved by SCAP and promulgated by the Japanese Government on 20 April, and 65 subsidiaries were added to the restricted list.","The first restitution of identifiable looted property occurred on 12 April, when two Hong Kong fire engines were returned to British authorities.","Union membership reached 877,014, government rice collections stood at 80.7% of quota, and over 2.3 million Japanese had been repatriated."],"k":["General Election","Women's Suffrage","Shidehara Cabinet","Constitution Revision","International Military Tribunal for the Far East","War Crimes","United States Education Mission","Holding Company Liquidation Commission","Repatriation","Rationing","Labor Unions","Black Markets"],"toc":[{"t":"Front matter","lv":1,"p":[1,4],"pp":"","s":"Cover page, errata sheet, general table of contents, and lists of charts, graphs and maps for the April 1946 report."},{"t":"Part I: General","lv":1,"p":[5,13],"pp":"1","s":"Overview of April 1946: the Diplomatic Section's creation, the 10 April general election and Shidehara Cabinet resignation, rising industrial output, currency controls, the US Education Mission report, and public assistance spending."},{"t":"Organization under SCAP","lv":2,"p":[5,5],"pp":"2","s":"The Diplomatic Section was established as a Special Staff Section under SCAP, and amendments expanded the membership of the International Military Tribunal for the Far East."},{"t":"Political Activities","lv":2,"p":[5,7],"pp":"3","s":"Covers the 10 April general election, party campaigns, the Shidehara Cabinet's resignation, constitutional draft revisions, civil service reforms, and war crime trials."},{"t":"Economic Activities","lv":2,"p":[8,10],"pp":"9","s":"Industrial production rose gradually; also covers food collection, fishery developments, emergency financial controls, fluctuating currency circulation, and restrictions on holding company subsidiaries."},{"t":"Social Activities","lv":2,"p":[11,13],"pp":"15","s":"Covers national public assistance expenditures, epidemic disease control, urban nutrition surveys, the US Education Mission report, and information programs teaching voters democratic election procedures."},{"t":"Part II: Political","lv":1,"p":[14,44],"pp":"21","s":"Covers the 10 April general election, Shidehara Cabinet resignation, public safety, legal developments, and war crimes, including the 55-count indictment of 28 major war criminals."},{"t":"Government and Civil Administration","lv":2,"p":[15,28],"pp":"22","s":"Covers the 10 April election, inter-party negotiations after the Shidehara Cabinet resigned, the revised popular-style draft constitution, enforcement of the purge directive, and civil service reforms."},{"t":"Public Safety and Intelligence","lv":2,"p":[29,32],"pp":"21","s":"Reports black-market investigations, poisonous alcohol deaths, occupation court convictions, police and prison inspections, demobilization intelligence, and press and radio censorship."},{"t":"Legal and War Crimes","lv":2,"p":[33,44],"pp":"29","s":"Covers judicial reorganization, arrest of 72 war crime suspects, trials in Yokohama and the Philippines, and the 55-count indictment of 28 major war criminals."},{"t":"Part III: Economic","lv":1,"p":[45,119],"pp":"83","s":"Covers agriculture, forestry, mining, heavy industry, textiles, utilities, labor, trade, rationing, finance, and reparations, including rice collections at 80.7% of quota and union membership of 877,014."},{"t":"Agriculture and Fisheries","lv":2,"p":[46,50],"pp":"85","s":"Reports tea crop deterioration, pyrethrum output, government rice collections reaching 80.7% of quota, Bonin Island whaling, and heavy Hokkaido herring catches."},{"t":"Forestry and Mining","lv":2,"p":[51,54],"pp":"95","s":"Covers veneer and plywood stockpiles, charcoal production goals, pulp shortages caused by the loss of Karafuto, coal production trends, and lead and zinc mining."},{"t":"Heavy Industries","lv":2,"p":[55,63],"pp":"102","s":"Reports pig iron and steel output, aluminum reduction gains, petroleum production, cement manufacturing, merchant ship repairs, chemical fertilizer supplies, and industrial machinery surveys."},{"t":"Manufacturing","lv":2,"p":[64,74],"pp":"121","s":"Covers seasonal peak sake and beer output, pulp and paper production, doubled truck chassis assembly, tractor manufacturing, rubber goods, and raw hide and leather shortages."},{"t":"Textile Industries","lv":2,"p":[75,81],"pp":"143","s":"Reviews yarn spinning and cloth weaving, cotton spindle expansion ahead of expected US raw cotton shipments, raw silk exports, wool weaving gains, and sundry goods production."},{"t":"Transportation and Public Utilities","lv":2,"p":[82,86],"pp":"157","s":"Covers government railway passenger growth and operating deficits, rolling stock repairs, coastwise shipping, electric power demand at 99% of 1944 levels, and gas utility coal shortages."},{"t":"Communications","lv":2,"p":[87,94],"pp":"165","s":"Covers occupation wire communications installations, delays in long-distance telephone service, overseas radiotelegraph traffic, postal operations, radio station inventories, and equipment manufacturing shortages."},{"t":"Labor","lv":2,"p":[95,99],"pp":"181","s":"Reports drafting of the Labor Disputes Adjustment Bill, union membership reaching 877,014, new national industrial federations, Central Labor Relations Committee rulings, and a multi-billion yen public works program."},{"t":"Imports and Exports","lv":2,"p":[100,103],"pp":"191","s":"Reports customs centralized under the Ministry of Finance, Boeki Cho designated sole foreign trade agency, raw silk exports of 8,300 bales, and imports of US surplus food and Chinese salt."},{"t":"Rationing and Price Control","lv":2,"p":[104,111],"pp":"199","s":"Covers staple food stocks and reduced household rations, distribution of imported foodstuffs, fresh vegetable and fish rationing in major cities, expiring textile coupons, and official coal prices."},{"t":"Finance","lv":2,"p":[112,115],"pp":"215","s":"Reports end of compulsory bank lending, deposit withdrawal limits to curb currency circulation, Bank of Japan balance sheet changes, capital levy and tax revision proposals, and local budget subsidies."},{"t":"Property Controls and Reparations","lv":2,"p":[116,119],"pp":"222","s":"Covers custody of 6,146 United Nations properties, German assets, first restitution of looted Hong Kong fire engines to British authorities, 65 subsidiaries added to the restricted list, and the Holding Company Liquidation Commission."},{"t":"Part IV: Social","lv":1,"p":[120,142],"pp":"281","s":"Covers public health, welfare, education, religion, cultural monuments, and media of expression, including repatriation of over 2.3 million Japanese and the US Education Mission report."},{"t":"Public Health and Welfare","lv":2,"p":[121,133],"pp":"233","s":"Covers public assistance disbursements, child protection committees, repatriation of over 2.3 million Japanese, typhus control by DDT dusting in Osaka, smallpox vaccination, port cholera quarantine, and urban nutrition surveys."},{"t":"Education, Religion and Media of Expression","lv":2,"p":[134,142],"pp":"257","s":"Reports US Education Mission recommendations, textbook purges, procedures for returning Christian missionaries, civic education around the general election, BCJ radio reorganization, and theatrical and library activities."}],"by":"gemini-3.8-flash-high"},{"id":"K-07","ia":"summation-usamg-activities-korea-no-07-1946-04","s":"K","no":7,"d":"1946-04","d2":"","t":"Summation of United States Army Military Government Activities in Korea, No. 7 (April 1946)","v":"","pg":54,"o":"Number 7 of the Summation of United States Army Military Government Activities in Korea covers non-military operations during April 1946. Significant administrative reorganizations took place under Ordinance No. 64, renaming bureaus into departments, establishing the Police Department as an independent agency, devolving fire protection to municipalities, and completing the removal of Japanese personnel from civil service. Politically, the report details fifth and sixth communiqués of the U.S.-Soviet Joint Commission regarding Korean party consultations under the Moscow Agreement, right-wing party unification under Kim Koo, and an assassination plot against Syngman Rhee. Economically, military authorities addressed severe food distress by legalizing open-market rice purchases in Seoul, appointing a National Food Administrator, and importing vital bituminous coal and raw materials from Japan, while grappling with critical fertilizer shortages. Social developments included record school enrollments, planned transitions to compulsory schooling and horizontal Korean script, and extensive DDT dusting and vaccination campaigns that curbed typhus and smallpox. For historians, this issue offers invaluable documentation on early American state-building, bureaucratic decolonization, interzone logistical frictions, food policy interventions, and the emerging Cold War diplomacy of the joint commission.","h":["Ordinance No. 64 redesignated all government bureaus as departments and sections as offices, made the Police Department an independent agency, and devolved fire protection to municipalities, expanding responsibilities for Korean personnel.","All remaining Japanese personnel, who numbered 70,000 in September 1945, were completely removed from the South Korean civil service.","The U.S.-Soviet Joint Commission issued Communiqués 5 and 6, agreeing to consult democratic parties that sign declarations upholding the Moscow Decision on Korea.","A plot to assassinate right-wing leader Dr. Syngman Rhee was foiled when Korean police arrested seven armed members of a rival political party.","Open-market rice purchasing was legalized in Seoul, and market prices dropped from 450 yen to between 200 and 225 yen per small marl; a National Food Administrator was also appointed.","In the first espionage case against Military Government, Kim Kei Cho was convicted, fined 3,100,000 yen, and sentenced to five years at hard labor.","Educational authorities scheduled compulsory schooling for September 1946 and began adopting the horizontal Korean alphabet for newly printed textbooks, amid record school attendance.","Extensive DDT dusting and vaccination campaigns curbed typhus and smallpox, while critical fertilizer shortages persisted and bituminous coal and raw materials arrived from Japan."],"k":["USAMGIK","U.S.-Soviet Joint Commission","Ordinance No. 64","Korean Civil Service","Rice rationing","Repatriation","Korean Constabulary","Korean Coast Guard","Syngman Rhee","Kim Koo","New Korea Company","Typhus control"],"toc":[{"t":"Front matter","lv":1,"p":[1,12],"pp":"","s":"Title pages, publication details, table of contents and list of charts for Summation No. 7."},{"t":"Section 1. General","lv":1,"p":[13,18],"pp":"1","s":"Overview of April 1946 in South Korea: military government reorganization, legalized open-market rice purchasing in Seoul, coal received from Japan, and disease control efforts."},{"t":"Section 2. Political Activities","lv":1,"p":[19,30],"pp":"7","s":"Ordinance No. 64 reorganization, civil service localization, the 1946-47 budget, an espionage trial, police work, right-hand traffic rules, Joint Commission Communiqués 5 and 6, and Korean and Japanese repatriation."},{"t":"Section 3. Economic Activities","lv":1,"p":[31,42],"pp":"19","s":"Rice distribution adjustments, land reclamation contracts, critical fertilizer shortages, commodity shipments from Japan, appointment of a National Food Administrator, and improved rail and coastal shipping."},{"t":"Section 4. Social Activities","lv":1,"p":[43,54],"pp":"29","s":"Record school attendance, planned educational reforms and adoption of the Korean alphabet, adult leadership courses, typhus vaccination and DDT dusting, public health clinics, and refugee and orphanage welfare."}],"by":"gemini-3.8-flash-high"},{"id":"J-08","ia":"summation-non-military-activities-japan-no-08-1946-05","s":"J","no":8,"d":"1946-05","d2":"","t":"Summation of Non-Military Activities in Japan, No. 8 (May 1946)","v":"","pg":128,"o":"Summation No. 8 covers non-military activities across occupied Japan during May 1946, documenting a pivotal month dominated by political transition and an acute food crisis. Following the resignation of the Shidehara Cabinet and SCAP's dramatic 4 May directive barring Liberal Party leader Ichiro Hatoyama from public office, protracted inter-party negotiations culminated in the installation of Shigeru Yoshida's coalition cabinet on 22 May. Widespread popular restlessness culminated in historic May Day demonstrations drawing 1,000,000 participants and the massive 'Food May Day' rally in Tokyo, prompting a stern warning from General MacArthur against political violence and an unprecedented radio appeal by the Emperor. Critical staple food shortages in metropolitan centers caused ration delivery delays of up to 11 days, requiring emergency releases of imported American flour. Meanwhile, the International Military Tribunal for the Far East commenced proceedings with the formal arraignment of 28 major war criminals. Other notable developments include the registration of 1.7 million trade unionists, the 26 April census adjusting Japan's population to 74 million, and comprehensive screening regulations to purge militarists from the educational system.","h":["SCAP barred Liberal Party leader Ichiro Hatoyama from public office on 4 May, and after the Shidehara Cabinet resigned and inter-party talks dragged on, Shigeru Yoshida's coalition cabinet was installed on 22 May.","Arraignment proceedings opened on 3 May before the International Military Tribunal for the Far East against 28 major Japanese war criminals, including Hideki Tojo.","About 1,000,000 citizens joined nationwide May Day rallies, followed by the massive 'Food May Day' demonstration outside Tokyo's Imperial Palace on 19 May.","General MacArthur warned on 20 May against undisciplined physical violence and mass intimidation by disorderly minority groups.","Severe urban food shortages delayed Tokyo-Yokohama rice rationing by 10 to 11 days, prompting SCAP to release 8,705 tons of imported wheat flour in an emergency.","General Orders No. 20 of 3 May discontinued the Civil Intelligence Section as a Special Staff Section and transferred its duties to G-2.","Imperial Ordinance 262 established screening committees to remove militarists and ultranationalists from teaching posts and education administration.","The 26 April census recorded 73,110,995 persons in Japan Proper, adjusted to 74,000,000 for uncounted foreign nationals and transients; 3,789 unions with 1,690,985 members registered."],"k":["Yoshida Cabinet","Ichiro Hatoyama","Purge Directive","Food May Day","International Military Tribunal for the Far East","food shortage","repatriation","rice rationing","labor unions","population census","textile inventory","educational reform"],"toc":[{"t":"Front matter","lv":1,"p":[1,4],"pp":"","s":"Report title pages, general table of contents across four parts, and list of 68 charts, graphs and maps."},{"t":"Part I: General","lv":1,"p":[5,12],"pp":"1","s":"Overview of May 1946: General Orders No. 20 on the Civil Intelligence Section, Hatoyama's purge and the Yoshida Cabinet, labor registrations, food shortages, the 26 April census and educational screening."},{"t":"Organization Under SCAP","lv":2,"p":[5,5],"pp":"2","s":"General Orders No. 20 of 3 May discontinued the Civil Intelligence Section as a Special Staff Section and transferred its duties to G-2."},{"t":"Political Activities","lv":2,"p":[5,7],"pp":"3","s":"Summarizes the purge of Ichiro Hatoyama, installation of the Yoshida Cabinet, May Day rallies, and the war crimes trials."},{"t":"Economic Activities","lv":2,"p":[8,10],"pp":"9","s":"Covers natural resources, industrial production, labor union registrations, critical food shortages, note circulation of ¥29.5 billion, and additions to restricted concerns."},{"t":"Social Activities","lv":2,"p":[11,12],"pp":"15","s":"Notes declines in typhus and smallpox, progress in repatriation, the 26 April census adjusted to 74,000,000 persons, and educational screening."},{"t":"Part II: Political","lv":1,"p":[13,32],"pp":"19","s":"Covers the Yoshida coalition cabinet and Hatoyama purge, food demonstrations and police matters, and legal affairs including orders apprehending 96 war crime suspects and IMTFE arraignments."},{"t":"Government and Civil Administration","lv":2,"p":[14,21],"pp":"21","s":"Details the cabinet crisis leading to Shigeru Yoshida's coalition cabinet, SCAP's directive purging Ichiro Hatoyama, May Day mass rallies, and Diet convocation."},{"t":"Public Safety and Intelligence","lv":2,"p":[22,24],"pp":"27","s":"Reports mass food demonstrations, crimes against occupation forces, warnings on methyl alcohol in liquor, metropolitan police reorganization surveys, and media censorship."},{"t":"Legal and War Crimes","lv":2,"p":[25,32],"pp":"42","s":"Covers SCAP legal opinions, district court convictions, orders apprehending 96 war crime suspects, trials in Japan and the Philippines, and IMTFE arraignments."},{"t":"Part III: Economic","lv":1,"p":[33,110],"pp":"57","s":"Economic review: rice collections at 83.4 percent of quota, coal stockpiles down to five days, 258,000,000 square yards of textiles inventoried, 3,789 registered unions, and note circulation of ¥33.4 billion."},{"t":"Agriculture and Fisheries","lv":2,"p":[33,37],"pp":"59","s":"Rice collections fell to 83.4 percent of quota; emergency food and fertilizer measures, experimental aerial DDT spraying, and 416 steel fishing vessels authorized."},{"t":"Forestry and Mining","lv":2,"p":[38,42],"pp":"69","s":"Covers timber and pulp output, coal mining accidents under 'production control', coal stockpiles falling to a five-day supply, and petroleum output."},{"t":"Heavy Industries","lv":2,"p":[43,52],"pp":"79","s":"Pig iron furnaces ran at only 3.5 percent capacity; coal shortages limited nonferrous refining; also cement, petroleum production and shipyard repairs."},{"t":"Manufacturing","lv":2,"p":[53,64],"pp":"90","s":"Cabinet order halted beer and sake brewing for two months to conserve grain; also food processing trends and production statistics for consumer goods."},{"t":"Textile Industries","lv":2,"p":[65,76],"pp":"123","s":"Nationwide textile inventory completed, showing 258,000,000 square yards of fabric; also cotton yarn price revisions, preparations for American cotton, and silk reeling."},{"t":"Transportation and Public Utilities","lv":2,"p":[77,82],"pp":"147","s":"Reviews registered motor vehicles, rising railroad traffic, coastal shipping, adequate hydroelectric power, and gas system repairs cutting leakage from 80 to 40 percent."},{"t":"Communications","lv":2,"p":[83,88],"pp":"155","s":"Domestic postal and telephone rates rose; overseas radiotelegraph links expanded to Hawaii and France; also telecommunications equipment manufacturing and overseas assets."},{"t":"Labor","lv":2,"p":[89,93],"pp":"167","s":"3,789 unions registered with 1,690,985 members; May Day demonstrations involved 1,000,000 workers; draft Labor Relations Adjustment legislation and wage adjustments."},{"t":"Imports and Exports","lv":2,"p":[94,95],"pp":"177","s":"Exports included 10,986 bales of raw silk to the United States and coal to Korea; imports of wheat and salt; Hoover's relief mission."},{"t":"Rationing and Price Control","lv":2,"p":[96,103],"pp":"181","s":"Critical urban food shortages delayed Tokyo-Yokohama rations by 10 to 11 days; 8,705 tons of imported flour released in emergency; price control enforcement."},{"t":"Finance","lv":2,"p":[104,106],"pp":"197","s":"Bank of Japan note circulation rose to ¥33.4 billion; deposit and loan trends under currency conversion and deposit freeze; war damage claims interest terminated."},{"t":"Property Control and Reparations","lv":2,"p":[107,110],"pp":"203","s":"Administration of 1,057 seized United Nations properties, custody of $255 million in precious metals, additions to restricted concerns, and reparations inventory plans."},{"t":"Part IV: Social","lv":1,"p":[111,128],"pp":"211","s":"Covers public assistance, repatriation of over 3,000,000 Japanese, cholera control at Uraga, the 26 April census, Imperial Ordinance 262 teacher screening, textbook review, and Shinto symbol directives."},{"t":"Public Health and Welfare","lv":2,"p":[112,121],"pp":"213","s":"Covers public assistance expenditures, repatriation of over 3,000,000 Japanese, cholera control at Uraga, DDT dusting, vaccine production, and the 26 April census."},{"t":"Education, Religion and Media of Expression","lv":2,"p":[122,128],"pp":"223","s":"Imperial Ordinance 262 set up teacher screening committees; revised textbooks approved; Shinto symbols prohibited on stamps and currency; new radio programming."}],"by":"gemini-3.8-flash-high"},{"id":"K-08","ia":"summation-usamg-activities-korea-no-08-1946-05","s":"K","no":8,"d":"1946-05","d2":"","t":"Summation of United States Army Military Government Activities in Korea, No. 8 (May 1946)","v":"","pg":112,"o":"Summation No. 8 covers May 1946 in South Korea under USAMGIK, with Lt. Gen. Hodge commanding USAFIK and Maj. Gen. Archer Lerch as Military Governor. It opens with a three-section digest (Part I) and then gives fuller Political, Economic and Social parts. The central political event is the 6 May adjournment sine die of the U.S.-Soviet Joint Commission over whether anti-trusteeship groups could be consulted. The report prints the American statements in full and surveys Korean press and public reaction. Other developments include the abolition of the Offices of Planning and Provincial Affairs, decentralized property custody, railway consolidation, and the FY 1946-47 budget (¥11.8 billion expenditure against ¥8.0 billion income). The economic sections stress the worsening rice shortage, summer grain collection and barter plans, and a late-May cholera outbreak at Pusan. Other topics are repatriation and the refugee flow across the 38th parallel, police reform and public-order incidents, and a long background study of Korean postal, savings and insurance systems. Historians will find ordinance lists, judicial appointments, statistical tables and press summaries useful.","h":["On 6 May the U.S.-Soviet Joint Commission adjourned sine die after the Soviets sought to exclude parties that had criticized trusteeship; General Shtikov left for North Korea on 8 May.","The FY 1946-47 national budget was approved: expenditure ¥11,800,212,360 against income ¥8,013,393,996, leaving a deficit of about ¥3.79 billion.","Cholera broke out at Pusan at the end of May (24 deaths in 74 cases by the 31st); the city was quarantined and its 400,000 residents were inoculated.","Controlled staple foods fell to 30,000 metric tons on 1 May and the daily ration was cut to 150 grams; a 1946 rice crop 23 percent below 1945's is forecast.","By 2 June, 736,527 Japanese had been repatriated and 973,923 Koreans had returned to South Korea; refugees crossing the 38th parallel rose to 8,314 in the week ending 18 May.","Ordinances 70-90 were promulgated, including the ban on sale of females (No. 70), abolition of the Offices of Planning (No. 81) and Provincial Affairs (No. 74), and the railway merger (No. 75).","A rightist anti-trusteeship rally of about 50,000 in Seoul on 12 May ended in an unauthorized parade and attacks on Communist headquarters and three left-wing newspapers.","Counterfeiters who circulated ¥9,000,000 in bogus notes were arrested; their equipment was found in the building housing Korean Communist Party headquarters."],"k":["USAMGIK","Joint Commission","trusteeship","Moscow Decision","rice shortage","repatriation","38th parallel","cholera","national budget","property custody","police","political parties","Korean Broadcasting Corporation","postal savings","Hodge"],"toc":[{"t":"Front Matter","lv":1,"p":[1,14],"pp":"","s":"Library flyleaves, cover and title pages of Summation No. 8 (May 1946), the printed contents (p. 11) and the list of 17 charts, graphs and maps (p. 13)."},{"t":"Part I: General","lv":1,"p":[15,22],"pp":"1","s":"Digest of the month in three sections (Political, Economic, Social), with numbered paragraphs summarizing the detailed Parts II-IV."},{"t":"Section 1: Political Activities","lv":2,"p":[15,18],"pp":"3","s":"Digest of the abolition of two offices, provincial property custody offices, the budget, new parties, the 6 May Joint Commission breakdown, Chinese refugee repatriation, May Day and anti-trusteeship demonstrations, police and prisons."},{"t":"Section 2: Economic Activities","lv":2,"p":[19,20],"pp":"7","s":"Notes the acute rice shortage and poor crop outlook, Samchok coal output, railway consolidation and the Korean Liberator train, 30,000 tons of controlled staples, and Materials Control Corporation sales of ¥76 million."},{"t":"Section 3: Social Activities","lv":2,"p":[21,22],"pp":"9","s":"Covers dissolution of Japanese labor relief associations, enlargement of the Seoul refugee camp, the Pusan cholera epidemic, education committees and teacher shortages, the folk dance revival, and press reaction to the Commission's adjournment."},{"t":"Part II: Political","lv":1,"p":[23,42],"pp":"11","s":"Covers administration, new parties, the 6 May Joint Commission breakdown, Chinese repatriation, ordinances 70-90, demonstrations, counterfeiting arrests, police training, prisons (12,150 inmates) and the Coast Guard move to Chinhae."},{"t":"Section 1: Government and Politics","lv":2,"p":[25,34],"pp":"13","s":"Covers administrative reorganization, new ordinances, the FY 1946-47 budget, new parties and leaders, the Joint Commission breakdown with American statements and Korean reaction, and Chinese repatriation."},{"t":"Administration","lv":3,"p":[25,27],"pp":"13","s":"Provincial governors met on food (2 May); Offices of Planning and Provincial Affairs abolished; provincial property custody offices created; customs, trade and railway ordinances; ¥11.8 billion budget; Chonju and Chunchon made cities."},{"t":"Political Parties","lv":3,"p":[27,28],"pp":"15","s":"New groups (Pak Ei Won, Independent Labor-Farmers Party, youth alliances), Lyuh Woon Hong's split from the People's Party, Chosen Democratic Party's move to Seoul, 160 registered parties, and profiles of Rhee and Kim Koo."},{"t":"Interzone Activities","lv":3,"p":[28,33],"pp":"16","s":"The 6 May Joint Commission adjournment over consulting anti-trusteeship groups, the full American statements of 27 April, 11 March and the breakdown, and Korean disappointment and rising support for continued Military Government."},{"t":"Foreign Affairs","lv":3,"p":[34,34],"pp":"22","s":"228 Chinese refugees left Inchon on 1 May, completely clearing the Seoul-Inchon area; a vessel was sent to Tangku to repatriate 2,000 Koreans."},{"t":"Section 2: Legal and Public Safety","lv":2,"p":[35,42],"pp":"23","s":"Covers Justice Department reorganization and judicial appointments, ordinances 70-90, May Day and anti-trusteeship demonstrations, counterfeiting and black-market arrests, police training and discipline, prisons, and Coast Guard and Constabulary matters."},{"t":"Legal","lv":3,"p":[35,37],"pp":"23","s":"Justice Department took over the Legal Division, with Koreans in all posts but Director; appointments and resignations; ordinances on limitations, sale of females and 82 offenses; 66 Japanese criminals repatriated."},{"t":"Public Safety","lv":3,"p":[38,42],"pp":"26","s":"Demonstrations including the 12 May rally of 50,000, Pusan civilian guards tried, counterfeiting, police academy graduates, women's police, prisons (12,150 inmates), and Coast Guard HQ move to Chinhae."},{"t":"Part III: Economic","lv":1,"p":[43,82],"pp":"31","s":"Covers natural resources, commerce and industry, finance and property control: rice shortage, Samchok coal, railway merger, communications, rationing at 150 grams, the budget, and Materials Control Corporation sales."},{"t":"Section 1: Natural Resources","lv":2,"p":[45,56],"pp":"33","s":"Surveys agriculture, fisheries, forestry and mining, with charts of staple food, fishing areas and lumber output; rice shortage, Samchok anthracite and a ten-year reforestation program."},{"t":"Agriculture","lv":3,"p":[45,47],"pp":"33","s":"Rice shortage after the 1945 crop of 2,167,000 tons; 1946 rice forecast 23 percent lower; cotton acreage cut; 6,000 Army horses sold to farmers; new agricultural and sericulture schools."},{"t":"Fisheries","lv":3,"p":[47,51],"pp":"35","s":"Fishing gear is one third of need; 3,500 tons of salt allocated; licensing system to 1948; plan to store 15,000 tons of dried fish; fishing area map and table."},{"t":"Forestry","lv":3,"p":[51,53],"pp":"39","s":"6,420,000 hectares of forest; timber output far above sustainable cut; ten-year reforestation program; curbs on firewood cutting; first LST of Kangwon logs reached Inchon on 3 May."},{"t":"Mining and Geology","lv":3,"p":[53,55],"pp":"41","s":"Samchok anthracite reached 53,000 tons in April; ¥100 million credit for coal; table of 1944 mineral production; talc mill, mining law revision and survey of hidden Japanese ownership."},{"t":"Section 2: Commerce and Industry","lv":2,"p":[57,78],"pp":"45","s":"Covers industry and textiles, rail and utilities, communications (postal, savings, insurance), labor, imports and exports, and rationing and price control, including the 150-gram ration and Ordinance 75."},{"t":"Industry and Textiles","lv":3,"p":[57,59],"pp":"45","s":"Inchon factories at 46 percent capacity; paper output of 550,000 pounds; flour, noodle and salt regulations; fertilizer plants; textile technician shortage; silk industry at 15 percent."},{"t":"Transportation and Public Utilities","lv":3,"p":[59,60],"pp":"47","s":"Ordinance 75 merged three private railways into the national system with a one-third pay rise; Korean Liberator train debuted 20 May; Chung Pyung plant produced 40,000 kW per day."},{"t":"Communications","lv":3,"p":[61,71],"pp":"49","s":"Background on the Department of Communications, telephone and telegraph shortages, postal history and operations, postal savings plans and insurance, with 1945 and 1946 insurance statistics."},{"t":"Labor, Public Works and Imports and Exports","lv":3,"p":[71,75],"pp":"59","s":"Provincial labor chiefs' conference and a printing strike; highway equipment schools; tables of SCAP-authorized imports, coal shipments and talc and graphite exports; Ordinance 82 on foreign trade licensing."},{"t":"Rationing and Price Control","lv":3,"p":[75,78],"pp":"63","s":"Food survey given to Herbert Hoover on 4 May; ration cut to 150 grams; summer grain collection plan; barter of consumer goods for rice; fish, feed, silkworm egg and cotton price controls."},{"t":"Section 3: Finance and Property Control","lv":2,"p":[79,82],"pp":"67","s":"Covers impounded coins, tax training, the FY 1946-47 budget table, provincial property custody offices under Ordinance 73, zaibatsu property, and Materials Control Corporation sales and warehouse thefts."},{"t":"Part IV: Social","lv":1,"p":[83,104],"pp":"71","s":"Covers public health and welfare (repatriation, refugees, Pusan cholera), education and culture, and public information (radio, press, Joint Commission opinion)."},{"t":"Section 1: Public Health and Welfare","lv":2,"p":[85,94],"pp":"73","s":"Covers welfare and relief, repatriation figures, northern refugees, hospitals, nursing, veterinary and medical supply, the Pusan cholera epidemic (24 deaths in 74 cases), and disease and vital statistics."},{"t":"Public Welfare","lv":3,"p":[85,89],"pp":"73","s":"Liquidation of Japanese labor relief societies, food relief of 11,000 tons, repatriation totals, 702 Japanese remaining, northern refugee flow, enlarged refugee camps, and a planned Korean Red Cross."},{"t":"Public Health, Hospitals and Nursing","lv":3,"p":[90,91],"pp":"78","s":"Health motto contest, hospital payment for emergency cases, runaway lepers returned to Sorokdo, Masan sanitarium, 40 public health nurses, nursing registry and textbooks."},{"t":"Medical Education, Veterinary Affairs and Medical Supply","lv":3,"p":[91,92],"pp":"79","s":"Fact-finding board on merging Seoul medical schools; veterinary reporting forms cut from 18 to 7; first commercial ether; cholera vaccine; 610 tons of medical supplies distributed; drug price schedule."},{"t":"Preventive Medicine and Vital Statistics","lv":3,"p":[92,93],"pp":"80","s":"Pusan cholera epidemic (24 deaths in 74 cases) with quarantine and inoculation; smallpox 2,294 cases in late April; child health contest; mosquito control; vital statistics course."},{"t":"Section 2: Education, Culture and Religion","lv":2,"p":[95,98],"pp":"83","s":"Covers dissolution of the Korean Committee on Education, curriculum revision, teacher training, special schools, the National Museum opening, concerts, festivals and Korea's soccer tournament appearance."},{"t":"Education","lv":3,"p":[95,97],"pp":"83","s":"Higher school curriculum committee; 15,000 Japanese teachers replaced; Pusan raised over ¥5 million for schools; nautical schools reopened; Seoul School of Law and Politics closed; compulsory schooling planned for September."},{"t":"Cultural Activities","lv":3,"p":[97,98],"pp":"85","s":"Law library move suspended; National Museum of Anthropology opened 25 April; Kyongju excavations; symphony concerts; Admiral Yi monument returned to Yosu; Children's Day and folk dance festival."},{"t":"Section 3: Public Information","lv":2,"p":[99,104],"pp":"87","s":"Covers provincial information sections, the Mobile Education Unit, film control, radio (Hodge and Eisenhower broadcasts), newspaper licensing and suspension, and press and public opinion on the Joint Commission."},{"t":"Public Information and Motion Pictures","lv":3,"p":[99,100],"pp":"87","s":"Ordinance 71 created provincial information sections; 16-member Mobile Education Unit; Ordinance 68 moved film control from Police to Information; newsreels and educational films."},{"t":"Radio","lv":3,"p":[100,103],"pp":"88","s":"Hodge's statement broadcast 9-11 May and Eisenhower's speech 15-16 May; Korea Broadcasting Corporation schedule; summer schedule of 10 hours 15 minutes; popular children's and music programs."},{"t":"Press and Public Opinion","lv":3,"p":[103,104],"pp":"91","s":"Ordinance 88 newspaper licensing; Dai Dong Ilbo suspended three weeks; newsprint rationing; Korean press reaction to the Joint Commission adjournment; tenant farmers' views on Japanese farmland; waning support for a separate southern government."},{"t":"Map of Korea","lv":1,"p":[105,105],"pp":"93","s":"Fold-out geographic map of Korea, printed page 93."},{"t":"Back Matter","lv":1,"p":[106,112],"pp":"","s":"Blank pages, University of Virginia Alderman Library due-date slip, library barcode page and end covers."}],"by":"claude-sonnet-5-5-high"},{"id":"J-09","ia":"summation-non-military-activities-japan-no-09-1946-06","s":"J","no":9,"d":"1946-06","d2":"","t":"Summation of Non-Military Activities in Japan, No. 9 (June 1946)","v":"","pg":146,"o":"Summation No. 9 covers non-military activities across occupied Japan during June 1946, highlighting political democratization, acute economic distress, and critical public health interventions. Politically, the 90th Imperial Diet opened under the new Yoshida Cabinet, with Emperor Hirohito delivering an unprecedented opening address in colloquial Japanese and formally submitting the draft revised constitution. SCAP and the Japanese government intensified the political purge, disqualifying prominent Diet members, while the International Military Tribunal for the Far East commenced its substantive prosecution phase. Economically, Japan faced a severe food crisis characterized by depleted urban rice stocks and widespread absenteeism, prompting emergency SCAP releases of 47,807 long tons of imported food. Industrial recovery was inaugurated by the arrival of the first postwar shipments of American raw cotton, even as critical shortages of coal and raw materials hindered heavy manufacturing. Meanwhile, union membership expanded to 2.7 million workers, and major holding company properties were impounded. Socially, authorities contained cholera outbreaks in Kyushu through strict maritime quarantine and launched nationwide teacher screening. For historians, the report provides invaluable data on constitutional deliberations, labor unrest, zaibatsu dissolution, and food relief logistics.","h":["Emperor Hirohito opened the 90th Imperial Diet speaking colloquial Japanese for the first time and submitted the government's draft revised constitution, while Prime Minister Yoshida outlined government policies.","Eight newly elected House of Representatives members were disqualified under the Purge Directive, and several peers were also purged; the Demobilization Board was created.","At the IMTFE, Chief Prosecutor Joseph B. Keenan delivered the opening statement, and defendant Yosuke Matsuoka died; SCAP also ordered 56 war crime suspects arrested.","Urban food deficits forced SCAP to release 47,807 long tons of imported cereals and flour, while staple food reserves fell to 45.3 days.","The first postwar American raw cotton arrived (21,712 bales), lifting cotton weaving 50 percent for export, though coal shortages continued to restrict heavy industry.","SCAP ordered the monopolistic Japan Lumber Company dissolved; May coal output of 1,695,000 metric tons fell below minimum quotas.","Registered union membership reached 2,695,988 in 7,207 unions, the April census counted 5,600,000 unemployed, and the Cabinet approved the Labor Disputes Adjustment Bill.","Strict quarantine contained cholera outbreaks on Tsushima and in Kyushu, 16,000,000 people were revaccinated against smallpox, and cumulative repatriates reached 3,685,378."],"k":["Diet","Constitution","Purge Directive","International Military Tribunal for the Far East","Food crisis","Repatriation","Raw cotton","Coal production","Labor unions","Zaibatsu dissolution","Cholera quarantine","Teachers' Manual"],"toc":[{"t":"Front matter","lv":1,"p":[1,3],"pp":"","s":"Title pages, table of contents, and list of charts, maps and graphs for Summation No. 9 (June 1946)."},{"t":"Part I: General","lv":1,"p":[4,12],"pp":"1","s":"June 1946 overview: the 90th Diet opened with the draft constitution, eight members were purged, raw cotton arrived, food reserves fell to 45.3 days, and cholera was quarantined in Kyushu."},{"t":"Organization Under SCAP","lv":2,"p":[5,5],"pp":"3","s":"SCAP reported no changes in its non-military organization during June 1946."},{"t":"Political Activities","lv":2,"p":[5,7],"pp":"3","s":"The 90th Imperial Diet opened with the Emperor speaking in colloquial Japanese and receiving the draft constitution. SCAP conferred with 31 women Diet members, eight members were purged, and the Demobilization Board was created."},{"t":"Economic Activities","lv":2,"p":[8,10],"pp":"9","s":"Fishing grounds were extended, while coal and lumber output stayed below target. First American raw cotton arrived, staple food reserves fell to 45.3 days, and currency circulation reached ¥40,644,000,000."},{"t":"Social Activities","lv":2,"p":[11,12],"pp":"15","s":"Public relief assisted 2,182,662 people and cumulative repatriates reached 3,685,378. Teacher screening began in Chiba, the new Teachers' Manual was published, and Kyushu cholera outbreaks were quarantined."},{"t":"Part II: Political","lv":1,"p":[13,43],"pp":"19","s":"Political part: the 90th Diet and draft constitution, purge of eight members, Ueno black-market raid, 1,550 occupation court cases, and Keenan's IMTFE opening statement with Matsuoka's death."},{"t":"Government and Civil Administration","lv":2,"p":[14,27],"pp":"21","s":"The 90th Diet convened as the Emperor submitted the draft constitution and Prime Minister Yoshida outlined policy. Eight Diet members and several peers were purged; the Demobilization Board was established; the Emperor toured Chiba and Shizuoka."},{"t":"Public Safety and Intelligence","lv":2,"p":[28,31],"pp":"49","s":"Mass demonstrations subsided after SCAP warnings, police raided Tokyo's Ueno black market, and lethal bootleg liquor was targeted. Police decentralization recommendations were submitted; prison death rates stayed critically high."},{"t":"Legal and War Crimes","lv":2,"p":[32,43],"pp":"57","s":"Occupation courts heard 1,550 cases and SCAP ordered 56 war crime suspects arrested. At the IMTFE, Chief Prosecutor Keenan gave opening statements, witnesses testified on militarism, and defendant Yosuke Matsuoka died."},{"t":"Part III: Economic","lv":1,"p":[44,128],"pp":"61","s":"Economic part: Japan Lumber Company dissolved, May coal at 1,695,000 tons, first raw cotton, 47,807 long tons of emergency cereals, 2,695,988 union members, and 10 zaibatsu families' property impounded."},{"t":"Agriculture and Fisheries","lv":2,"p":[45,49],"pp":"83","s":"A reconnaissance soil survey of Kyushu was completed and SCAP doubled authorized offshore fishing and whaling areas. Hokkaido herring catches reached 207,947 metric tons; ko-ayu fry were transplanted from Lake Biwa."},{"t":"Forestry and Mining","lv":2,"p":[50,55],"pp":"93","s":"SCAP ordered the monopolistic Japan Lumber Company dissolved. May coal output of 1,695,000 metric tons missed minimum quotas for lack of food, and a petroleum committee estimated reserves at 10,000,000 kiloliters."},{"t":"Heavy Industries","lv":2,"p":[56,64],"pp":"105","s":"Pig iron, steel, aluminum and cement output rose, but coal shortages restricted refining and chemicals. Nitrogenous fertilizer reached half of prewar peak and 250 merchant vessels were repaired."},{"t":"Manufacturing","lv":2,"p":[65,77],"pp":"123","s":"Canned fish and flour milling rose despite fuel and container shortages. Paper, sheet glass and bicycle output increased, and 1,272 truck chassis plus growing farm implement supplies were manufactured."},{"t":"Textile Industries","lv":2,"p":[78,91],"pp":"149","s":"First postwar American raw cotton arrived, raising cotton weaving 50 percent for export. Raw silk reached 7,540 bales, rayon pulp manufacture resumed, and silk fabric output more than doubled."},{"t":"Transportation and Public Utilities","lv":2,"p":[92,95],"pp":"177","s":"Rail freight rose to 8,527,200 metric tons and coastal shipping dipped slightly. Hydroelectric power was ample, but 70 percent of municipal gas capacity sat idle for lack of coal."},{"t":"Communications","lv":2,"p":[96,102],"pp":"195","s":"An Imperial ordinance raised the Board of Communications to a Ministry from 1 July. Postal and telephone rates rose, radio receiver output jumped 40 percent, and the 400,000-member All Japan Communications Workers' Union was inaugurated."},{"t":"Labor","lv":2,"p":[103,108],"pp":"199","s":"The Cabinet approved the Labor Disputes Adjustment Bill; union membership reached 2,695,988 in 7,207 unions. The April census found 5,600,000 totally or partially unemployed, and a separate Ministry of Labor was planned."},{"t":"Imports and Exports","lv":2,"p":[109,111],"pp":"211","s":"Arrival of 21,712 bales of American raw cotton began the textile export-import program. June exports included 11,053 bales of raw silk and 70,000 tons of coal; imports included 60,000 tons of food grains."},{"t":"Rationing and Price Control","lv":2,"p":[112,118],"pp":"217","s":"Urban food deficits forced SCAP to release 47,807 long tons of imported cereals and flour. New vegetable ceilings ended subsidies, rubber goods prices rose, and economic police logged 144,353 rationing and price violations."},{"t":"Finance","lv":2,"p":[119,122],"pp":"231","s":"Bank of Japan note issue reached ¥40,644,000,000 as daily growth slowed. The government ran on temporary budgets and short-term loans, local tax reform aimed at ¥3,327,000,000, and national debt hit ¥203,064,000,000."},{"t":"Property Control and Reparations","lv":2,"p":[123,128],"pp":"239","s":"SCAP impounded properties of 10 zaibatsu families, put four second-line holding companies under surveillance, and added 17 holding companies to the restricted schedule. Custody covered 594 potential reparations facilities and ¥63,024,347 in war criminal assets."},{"t":"Part IV: Social","lv":1,"p":[129,146],"pp":"251","s":"Social part: welfare relief and repatriation (3,685,378 returned), cholera quarantine in Tsushima and Kyushu, smallpox revaccination of 16,000,000, Chiba teacher screening and the new Teachers' Manual."},{"t":"Public Health and Welfare","lv":2,"p":[130,137],"pp":"253","s":"Relief assisted 2,182,493 people and repatriates totaled 3,685,378. Port quarantines and sanitary cordons contained cholera in Tsushima and Kyushu, 16,000,000 were revaccinated against smallpox, and DDT duster production expanded."},{"t":"Education, Religion and Media of Expression","lv":2,"p":[138,146],"pp":"269","s":"Teacher screening began in Chiba Prefecture, the Ministry of Education issued the new democratic Teachers' Manual, and Imperial portrait depositories were removed from schools. Tokyo trials and constitution debates dominated the media."}],"by":"gemini-3.8-flash-high"},{"id":"K-09","ia":"summation-usamg-activities-korea-no-09-1946-06","s":"K","no":9,"d":"1946-06","d2":"","t":"Summation of United States Army Military Government Activities in Korea, No. 9 (June 1946)","v":"","pg":100,"o":"Summation No. 9 chronicles United States Army Military Government activities in South Korea during June 1946, a month characterized by acute administrative reorganization, environmental crises, and political realignment following the adjournment of the Joint American-Soviet Commission. Major political developments include Ambassador Edwin W. Pauley's reparations inspection tour of North Korea, the closure of the Soviet Consulate in Seoul, moderate coalition initiatives led by Kim Kyu-sik and Lyuh Woon-hyung, and the creation of the National Economic Board. Catastrophic late June flooding along the Han River caused 88 deaths and devastated crops and infrastructure, compounding severe food shortages that necessitated the emergency distribution of 55,000 tons of imported American wheat. Meanwhile, military authorities vigorously combated a deadly cholera epidemic that claimed 651 lives by administering over 1.6 million inoculations. For historians, this report offers indispensable primary data, featuring detailed structural surveys of the Korean fishing industry under Japanese colonial rule, comprehensive cost-of-living and family expenditure analyses showing extreme inflation, thorough descriptions of the state monopoly bureau's operations in ginseng and tobacco, and cumulative statistics on postwar cross-border repatriation and rail rehabilitation.","h":["Ambassador Edwin W. Pauley toured industrial centers in North Korea on a reparations mission and found no evidence of substantial equipment removals by Soviet occupation authorities.","The Soviet Consulate in Seoul was officially closed, and its personnel, headed by Vice-Consul Alexander S. Poliansky, returned to Moscow.","Moderate leaders Dr. Kim Kyu-sik and Lyuh Woon-hyung met to negotiate a center coalition between moderate Left and Right factions after the Joint American-Soviet Commission adjourned.","The National Economic Board was established under Ordinance No. 90 to coordinate economic policy, price stabilization, commodity allocations, and rationing.","Late June flooding along the Han River caused 88 deaths and destroyed 13,686 homes, aggravating South Korea's critical summer food deficit.","Authorities contained a cholera epidemic that caused 651 deaths, administering more than 1.6 million inoculations across Seoul, Pusan, and Taegu.","About 55,000 tons of American wheat arrived to supplement depleted government rice stocks in critical deficit areas.","Masan Tuberculosis Sanatorium opened, college courses were extended to four years, and cumulative repatriation of Koreans reached nearly one million."],"k":["USAMGIK","Edwin W. Pauley","Kim Kyu-sik","Lyuh Woon-hyung","Han River flood","cholera epidemic","National Economic Board","Monopoly Bureau","grain collection","repatriation","Soviet Consulate Seoul","Korean Constabulary"],"toc":[{"t":"Front matter","lv":1,"p":[1,10],"pp":"","s":"Cover, title pages, table of contents, and list of charts, graphs, and maps for the June 1946 report."},{"t":"Part I: General","lv":1,"p":[11,22],"pp":"1","s":"Monthly overview of South Korea in June 1946: Kim Kyu-sik and Lyuh Woon-hyung's coalition talks, Pauley's North Korea tour, 55,000 tons of American wheat, a cholera outbreak, and repatriation."},{"t":"Political Activities","lv":2,"p":[13,16],"pp":"3","s":"Moderate unification talks between Kim Kyu-sik and Lyuh Woon-hyung, Ambassador Pauley's North Korea mission, closing of the Soviet consulate, civil service rules, and suspension of Ordinance No. 72."},{"t":"Economic Activities","lv":2,"p":[17,20],"pp":"7","s":"Flood damage to crops, arrival of 55,000 tons of American wheat, summer grain collection plans, creation of the National Economic Board and Price Administration, and currency circulation trends."},{"t":"Social Activities","lv":2,"p":[21,22],"pp":"11","s":"Measures against a cholera outbreak, opening of Masan Tuberculosis Sanatorium, four-year college courses, teacher summer schools, adult literacy drives, and repatriation movements."},{"t":"Part II: Political","lv":1,"p":[23,32],"pp":"13","s":"Detailed political reports: Korean Civil Service and National Economic Board, Kim-Lyuh coalition efforts, Pauley's tour, Soviet consulate closing, chief judges' meeting, Ordinance No. 72, Han River floods, and police matters."},{"t":"Government and Politics","lv":2,"p":[25,28],"pp":"15","s":"Korean Civil Service organization, National Economic Board creation, Kim Kyu-sik and Lyuh Woon-hyung's coalition efforts, Chungchong-pukto local elections, Pauley's reparations tour of North Korea, and the Soviet consulate's closing in Seoul."},{"t":"Legal and Public Safety","lv":2,"p":[29,32],"pp":"19","s":"First post-liberation meeting of chief judges, suspension of Ordinance No. 72, Han River floods, a scientific crime laboratory, Railroad Police graduations, and renaming of the Department of Internal Security."},{"t":"Part III: Economic","lv":1,"p":[33,72],"pp":"23","s":"Detailed economic reports: flood damage to summer grain, fishing industry reorganization, railroads and cables, a 300,000,000-yen public work relief program, wheat imports, inflation surveys, Bank of Chosen notes, and Monopoly Bureau operations."},{"t":"Natural Resources","lv":2,"p":[35,44],"pp":"25","s":"Flood damage to summer grain crops, rinderpest cattle vaccinations, reorganization and licensing in the fishing industry, forest fire prevention, and a census of provincial sawmills."},{"t":"Commerce and Industry","lv":2,"p":[45,66],"pp":"35","s":"Industrial reconversion of war material, railroad operations and communications, submarine cables to Japan, postal exchange with North Korea, a 300,000,000-yen public work relief program, wheat imports, and cost-of-living inflation surveys."},{"t":"Finance and Government Monopolies","lv":2,"p":[67,72],"pp":"57","s":"Bank of Chosen note circulation, and the Monopoly Bureau's organization, revenues, licensing and production of red and white ginseng, leaf tobacco, and evaporated sea salt."},{"t":"Part IV: Social","lv":1,"p":[73,92],"pp":"63","s":"Detailed social reports: cholera control by mass inoculation, nearly one million repatriated Koreans, Masan Sanatorium, education reorganization, four-year colleges, Korean history textbooks, Tan Oh Day, and radio and press coverage."},{"t":"Public Health and Welfare","lv":2,"p":[75,82],"pp":"65","s":"Control of a major cholera epidemic through mass inoculations and vaccine production, cumulative repatriation of nearly one million Koreans, opening of Masan Tuberculosis Sanatorium, and medical licensing examinations."},{"t":"Education, Culture and Religion","lv":2,"p":[83,88],"pp":"73","s":"Reorganization of the Department of Education, four-year college programs, new Korean history textbooks, adult literacy campaigns, civic university fundraising, cultural preservation, and Tan Oh Day festivals."},{"t":"Public Information","lv":2,"p":[89,92],"pp":"79","s":"Radio and mobile-unit campaigns on public health and grain collection, motion picture licensing, Korean Broadcasting Corporation coverage, and press commentary on North-South unification and the Joint Commission."},{"t":"Map of Korea","lv":1,"p":[93,100],"pp":"83","s":"Full-page reference map of Korea showing provincial boundaries, the 38th parallel division, and principal cities."}],"by":"gemini-3.8-flash-high"},{"id":"J-10","ia":"summation-non-military-activities-japan-no-10-1946-07","s":"J","no":10,"d":"1946-07","d2":"","t":"Summation of Non-Military Activities in Japan, No. 10 (July 1946)","v":"","pg":133,"o":"Summation No. 10 covers non-military activities in Japan during July 1946, a critical transitional month marked by severe food shortages and historic institutional reforms. The report documents the acute food crisis that forced SCAP to release over 174,000 metric tons of imported cereals and canned goods across 20 deficit prefectures to prevent widespread starvation. In the Diet, intense debates centered on the draft constitution, particularly the concepts of popular sovereignty, the symbolic status of the Emperor, and the renunciation of war. Concurrently, the government substantially completed the purge of militarists from public office and established the Ministry of Communications. In the economic realm, coal shortages severely curtailed industrial output, prompting the concentration of all steelmaking at Yawata, while the arrival of the first 30,000 bales of American raw cotton began revitalizing the export textile sector. At the International Military Tribunal for the Far East, prosecution witnesses provided harrowing eyewitness testimony regarding the Mukden Incident and the Rape of Nanking. For historians, this issue provides invaluable empirical data on May nutrition surveys, black-market gang warfare in Tokyo, labor union reorganization, and early Occupation governance.","h":["The Diet extended its session to 28 August to debate the draft constitution, focusing on popular sovereignty, the Emperor's status, and the renunciation of war.","A severe staple food crisis forced SCAP to release 174,708 metric tons of imported cereals and canned goods to 20 deficit prefectures; Tokyo's average daily intake was only 1,352 calories.","At the IMTFE, eyewitnesses including Dr. Robert Wilson and Miner Searle Bates gave harrowing testimony on the 1937 Rape of Nanking, and General Myron C. Cramer replaced Justice Higgins.","Screening under the 4 January Purge Directive was substantially completed, barring prominent figures including Mitsu Kono and Haruo Matsui from public office.","Rival black-market gangs fought gun battles with police in Tokyo's Shibuya and Shimbashi districts, leaving six dead and prompting police to shut open-air stalls.","The Board of Communications was elevated to the Ministry of Communications on 1 July with Sadakichi Hitotsumatsu as minister, and mail rates rose 200 percent.","The first American raw cotton, 30,000 bales, reached Japanese mills for export yarn production, while coal shortages concentrated steelmaking at Yawata and held chemical output to 32 percent of minimum needs.","SCAP ordered 65 suspected war criminals apprehended, and 524,005 Japanese were repatriated during the month."],"k":["Draft Constitution","Food Crisis","Purge Directive","International Military Tribunal for the Far East","Rape of Nanking","Black Market","Labor Disputes Adjustment Bill","Raw Cotton Imports","Holding Company Liquidation Commission","Repatriation","Ministry of Communications","Tanzan Ishibashi"],"toc":[{"t":"Front matter","lv":1,"p":[1,5],"pp":"","s":"Cover, title page, table of contents, and list of 77 charts, maps, and graphs."},{"t":"Part I: General","lv":1,"p":[6,13],"pp":"1","s":"July 1946 overview: Diet debate on the draft constitution, purge screening substantially completed, critical food shortages with emergency releases to 20 prefectures, cholera in Fukuyama and Yokosuka, and new Citizens' Public Halls."},{"t":"Organization under SCAP","lv":2,"p":[7,7],"pp":"3","s":"SCAP General Orders No. 30 of 26 July discontinued the Office of Civilian Personnel as a special staff section; USAFPAC assumed its functions."},{"t":"Political Activities","lv":2,"p":[7,8],"pp":"3","s":"The Diet debated the draft constitution, focusing on popular sovereignty and the Emperor. Screening under the Purge Directive was substantially completed, and the Social Democrats rejected communist collaboration."},{"t":"Economic Activities","lv":2,"p":[9,11],"pp":"7","s":"Finance Minister Ishibashi outlined a recovery program stressing coal and fertilizer. Staple food shortages forced emergency releases of imported foodstuffs to 20 prefectures, and note circulation expanded rapidly."},{"t":"Social Activities","lv":2,"p":[12,13],"pp":"13","s":"Public assistance aided 2.19 million people; cholera struck Fukuyama and Yokosuka. The Ministry of Education set up Citizens' Public Halls and banned deleted wartime textbooks from 1 August."},{"t":"Part II: Political","lv":1,"p":[14,37],"pp":"17","s":"Diet extension to 28 August, purge of figures like Mitsu Kono, Communications ministry creation, Shibuya and Shimbashi black-market gang clashes, 65 war-crimes arrests, and IMTFE testimony on Nanking."},{"t":"Government and Civil Administration","lv":2,"p":[15,24],"pp":"19","s":"The Diet extended its session to 28 August to consider the draft constitution and Ishibashi's economic policies. Purge screening removed figures like Mitsu Kono, and the Board of Communications became a ministry."},{"t":"Public Safety and Intelligence","lv":2,"p":[25,28],"pp":"37","s":"Rival black-market gangs fought gun battles in Shibuya and Shimbashi, prompting police to close open-air stalls. Tokyo crime nearly doubled since January; a fire destroyed 400 houses in Iida."},{"t":"Legal and War Crimes","lv":2,"p":[29,37],"pp":"45","s":"SCAP ordered 65 suspected war criminals apprehended. The IMTFE heard testimony on Chang Tso-lin's 1928 assassination and the Rape of Nanking; General Myron C. Cramer replaced Justice Higgins."},{"t":"Part III: Economic","lv":1,"p":[38,115],"pp":"63","s":"July economic developments: improved crop forecasts, coal at 1.66 million tons, steelmaking concentrated at Yawata, first 30,000 bales of American cotton, a 174,708-ton food release, and note circulation of 47.23 billion yen."},{"t":"Agriculture and Fisheries","lv":2,"p":[39,42],"pp":"65","s":"Crop forecasts indicated improved 1946 harvests despite poor wheat and barley. June fish landings fell with the end of Hokkaido's herring season; 263 converted fishing vessels operated by late July."},{"t":"Forestry and Mining","lv":2,"p":[43,48],"pp":"72","s":"Coal output reached 1.66 million metric tons in June, yet shortages hampered metallurgy and chemicals. Seventeen of 25 tracked minerals rose in May; Imperial Oil struggled with high costs."},{"t":"Heavy Industries","lv":2,"p":[49,57],"pp":"85","s":"To conserve coal, iron and steel production was concentrated at Yawata. June chemical output met only 32 percent of minimum needs; 12 steel ships were completed."},{"t":"Manufacturing","lv":2,"p":[58,65],"pp":"103","s":"Machine tool output fell as makers feared reparations, but truck chassis rose to 1,483 units in June. Watch and clock production expanded to 62,193 units."},{"t":"Textile Industries","lv":2,"p":[66,80],"pp":"119","s":"The first American raw cotton, 30,000 bales, arrived for export yarn production. Raw silk output dipped on coal and food shortages, while rayon pulp output tripled."},{"t":"Transportation and Public Utilities","lv":2,"p":[81,84],"pp":"149","s":"Rail freight fell to 8.14 million tons in June, and coal shortages left 72.5 percent of gas manufacturing capacity idle. The Nagamatsu hydroelectric plant was completed."},{"t":"Communications","lv":2,"p":[85,90],"pp":"157","s":"The Board of Communications became a ministry on 1 July, and mail rates rose 200 percent. Vacuum tubes remained critically short; long-distance telephone service for Occupation Forces expanded."},{"t":"Labor","lv":2,"p":[91,95],"pp":"169","s":"The Labor Disputes Adjustment Bill went to the Diet amid union protests. Government workers won a 50 percent wage increase; SCAP set a 40-hour week and paid leave for Occupation-employed laborers."},{"t":"Imports and Exports","lv":2,"p":[96,98],"pp":"177","s":"In July 50 ships delivered 326,000 tons of imports, chiefly food, cotton, and phosphate. Exports included coal to Hong Kong and Korea, and raw silk and metals to the United States."},{"t":"Rationing and Price Control","lv":2,"p":[99,108],"pp":"193","s":"SCAP released 174,708 tons of imported cereals and canned foods to 20 prefectures during a staple food crisis. Vegetable deliveries to urban legal markets plummeted after subsidies ended."},{"t":"Finance","lv":2,"p":[109,111],"pp":"203","s":"Bank of Japan note circulation reached 47.23 billion yen by 20 July through deficit financing. SCAP permitted dissolution of the Yokohama Specie Bank and reviewed proposed national tax revisions."},{"t":"Property Control and Reparations","lv":2,"p":[112,115],"pp":"209","s":"SCAP approved articles for the Holding Company Liquidation Commission, restricted further concerns including Titan Industrial, and revised the interim reparations list, deleting 60 plants and adding 21."},{"t":"Part IV: Social","lv":1,"p":[116,133],"pp":"217","s":"July social developments: cholera controlled in Hiroshima and Kanagawa, Tokyo diets averaging 1,352 calories, 524,005 repatriates, Citizens' Public Halls, new geography textbooks, and journalism canons adopted by publishers."},{"t":"Public Health and Welfare","lv":2,"p":[117,124],"pp":"219","s":"Cholera in Hiroshima and Kanagawa Prefectures was controlled and typhus fell sharply. Surveys found Tokyo's average daily intake only 1,352 calories; 524,005 Japanese were repatriated."},{"t":"Education, Religion and Media of Expression","lv":2,"p":[125,133],"pp":"235","s":"The Ministry of Education started Citizens' Public Halls, reinstated geography with new textbooks, and screened teachers in Chiba. The Japan Newspaper Publishers and Editors Association adopted democratic canons of journalism."}],"by":"gemini-3.8-flash-high"},{"id":"K-10","ia":"summation-usamg-activities-korea-no-10-1946-07","s":"K","no":10,"d":"1946-07","d2":"","t":"Summation of United States Army Military Government Activities in Korea, No. 10 (July 1946)","v":"","pg":85,"o":"Summation No. 10 documents United States Army Military Government in Korea (USAMGIK) activities during July 1946, reflecting an occupation administration grappling with natural disasters, epidemic disease, and acute economic distress. The month was dominated by the catastrophic aftermath of the late June floods, which disrupted rail and telegraph communications, aggravated severe grain shortages in urban centers like Seoul and Inchon, and spurred popular food demonstrations. Concurrently, USAMGIK combated a devastating cholera epidemic claiming nearly 5,000 lives, leading to widespread quarantines, port closures, and the suspension of mail exchanges with Soviet-occupied North Korea. Administratively, Military Government established an independent Department of Labor (Ordinance No. 97), designated Cheju-do as a separate province (Ordinance No. 94), and proposed an interim Korean legislative body. To rein in runaway inflation, the Military Governor enacted strict price ceilings on essential consumer goods and demonetized type A occupational currency. For historians, this issue provides indispensable quantitative data on retail prices, factory employment, and repatriation flows, alongside an extensive institutional retrospective on the reorganization and training of the South Korean National Police.","h":["The Military Governor proposed a Korean legislative body composed of party representatives and elected provincial delegates.","Ordinance No. 97 created an independent Department of Labor and affirmed workers' rights to form unions and bargain collectively.","Ordinance No. 94 made Cheju-do and the Chuja Archipelago a separate province, restoring administrative autonomy lost in 1906.","The National Police Academy graduated its first class of 55 policewomen on 12 July and its first commissioned officer class on 19 July.","The Korean Red Cross Society adopted a new charter under International Red Cross principles and elected Dr. Kim Kyu Sic its first president.","A cholera epidemic reached 7,642 reported cases and 4,878 deaths, prompting quarantines, travel restrictions, port closures and mass public inoculations across South Korea.","Price ceilings were set on cloth, shoes, soap, matches and other key commodities to counter severe post-flood retail inflation.","The trial of alleged Communist counterfeiters opened in Seoul on 29 July; police repulsed an attempted rescue by sympathizers."],"k":["USAMGIK","Korean Interim Legislative Assembly","Department of Labor","Cheju-do","National Police Academy","Korean Red Cross Society","Kim Kyu Sic","cholera epidemic","price controls","flood relief","counterfeiting trial","Bank of Chosen","Korean Constabulary","repatriation"],"toc":[{"t":"Front matter","lv":1,"p":[1,15],"pp":"","s":"Title pages, table of contents and list of charts, graphs and maps for Summation No. 10."},{"t":"Part I: General","lv":1,"p":[16,23],"pp":"1","s":"July 1946 overview: creation of the Department of Labor, a proposed Korean legislative body, flood damage and price ceilings, repatriation, the Korean Red Cross charter and cholera campaigns."},{"t":"Political Activities","lv":2,"p":[18,19],"pp":"3","s":"The new Department of Labor, the Military Governor's proposal for a Korean legislative body, and police academy graduations."},{"t":"Economic Activities","lv":2,"p":[20,21],"pp":"5","s":"Flood damage to railroads and communications, crop shortages, arrival of American food imports, and new consumer price ceilings."},{"t":"Social Activities","lv":2,"p":[22,23],"pp":"7","s":"Korean repatriation progress, the Korean Red Cross charter, distribution of 3 million textbooks, and public education campaigns against cholera."},{"t":"Part II: Political","lv":1,"p":[24,37],"pp":"9","s":"July 1946 administrative reorganization (Commerce, Labor, Cheju Province), political developments, Ordinances 91-99, the counterfeiters trial, National Police training and Constabulary flood relief."},{"t":"Government and Politics","lv":2,"p":[26,27],"pp":"11","s":"Reorganization of the Commerce and Labor departments, separation of Cheju Province, foreign trade licensing rules, and the proposed interim legislative assembly."},{"t":"Legal and Public Safety","lv":2,"p":[28,37],"pp":"13","s":"Ordinances 91-99, the Seoul counterfeiters trial, a review of National Police organization and training, and Constabulary flood relief operations."},{"t":"Part III: Economic","lv":1,"p":[38,61],"pp":"23","s":"Agriculture, fisheries, forestry and mining, plus factory employment, textiles, rail repairs, mail with the US, price ceilings and foreign exchange restrictions in July 1946."},{"t":"Natural Resources","lv":2,"p":[40,41],"pp":"25","s":"Stunted grain crops in Kyonggi-do, cholera-related fishing bans at Inchon, halted forestry operations, and surveys of pyrophyllite and talc mines."},{"t":"Commerce and Industry","lv":2,"p":[42,61],"pp":"27","s":"Factory employment surveys, Kwangju textile output, flood repairs to rail lines, resumed mail service with the US, price ceilings, and foreign exchange restrictions."},{"t":"Part IV: Social","lv":1,"p":[62,77],"pp":"47","s":"Public health and cholera control, child welfare, Red Cross charter, repatriation, teacher institutes, art treasures, radio campaigns and licensing of 242 periodicals in July 1946."},{"t":"Public Health and Welfare","lv":2,"p":[64,69],"pp":"49","s":"Institutional child welfare, the Korean Red Cross charter, repatriation statistics, rinderpest containment at the 38th parallel, and the cholera epidemic response."},{"t":"Education, Culture and Religion","lv":2,"p":[70,73],"pp":"55","s":"Summer teacher institutes, school attendance figures, return of the US educational mission, restoration of national art treasures, and incoming missionaries."},{"t":"Public Information","lv":2,"p":[74,77],"pp":"59","s":"Public health radio campaigns, mobile film-strip education, licensing of 242 periodicals, and press coverage of price ceilings and political unification."},{"t":"Map of Korea","lv":1,"p":[78,85],"pp":"63","s":"Reference map of Korea showing provincial borders, the 38th parallel demarcation line, and major transportation routes."}],"by":"gemini-3.8-flash-high"},{"id":"K-10-v2","ia":"summation-usamg-activities-korea-no-10-1946-07-v2","s":"K","no":10,"d":"1946-07","d2":"","t":"Summation of United States Army Military Government Activities in Korea, No. 10 (July 1946)","v":"Second copy","pg":84,"o":"Number 10 of the USAMGIK Summation covers July 1946, documenting a critical juncture in the American occupation of South Korea characterized by administrative institutionalization amid acute socioeconomic distress. Key political and administrative developments include the proposal by the Military Governor for an interim Korean legislative body, the separation of Cheju Island into an independent province, and the promulgation of Ordinances 97 and 98, which established the Department of Labor—recognizing collective bargaining rights—and reorganized the Department of Commerce into separate foreign and domestic commerce bureaus. The occupation faced compounding crises: late June floods severely crippled the national railway network and coal transport, while a virulent cholera epidemic claimed over 4,800 lives, prompting travel bans and the suspension of mail exchanges with Soviet-occupied North Korea. Acute food shortages in Seoul and Inchon forced increased reliance on imported American grain as domestic summer grain collections fell far behind targets. To combat spiraling inflation, USAMGIK imposed price ceilings on staple consumer goods and demonetized Type A military currency. For historians, this report provides invaluable quantitative data on commodity price inflation, police professionalization, prison industries, and massive cross-border repatriation movements.","h":["The Military Governor proposed a representative Korean interim legislative body to enact ordinances and screen governmental appointments.","Ordinance 97 created the Department of Labor, officially recognizing Korean workers' right to organize unions and bargain collectively; Ordinance 98 split Commerce into foreign and domestic bureaus.","Cheju-do and the Chuja Archipelago were separated from Cholla-namdo and made an independent province under Ordinance No. 94.","A nationwide cholera epidemic reached 7,642 reported cases and 4,878 deaths by 31 July, prompting strict travel quarantines, mass vaccinations, and suspension of mail exchange with North Korea.","Price ceilings took effect on 15 July for essential goods including cotton cloth, rubber shoes, soap, matches, and electric light bulbs.","Type A occupational currency was demonetized and had to be deposited in financial institutions between 1 and 10 July.","Late June floods crippled the railway network and coal transport, while summer grain collection lagged and Seoul and Inchon relied on imported American grain.","The National Police Academy graduated its first class of 55 policewomen on 12 July and its first commissioned officer class on 19 July."],"k":["USAMGIK","Korean Interim Legislative Assembly","Department of Labor","Price control","Cholera epidemic","Repatriation","Cheju Province","National Police Academy","Bank of Chosen","Summer grain collection","Foreign exchange control","Korean Red Cross"],"toc":[{"t":"Front Matter","lv":1,"p":[1,14],"pp":"","s":"Title pages, library markings, table of contents, and list of charts, graphs, and maps for the July 1946 report."},{"t":"Part I: General","lv":1,"p":[15,22],"pp":"1","s":"General monthly summary of South Korea in July 1946: Labor and Commerce department changes, a proposed legislative body, flood damage, price ceilings and currency changes, and cholera containment."},{"t":"Political Activities","lv":2,"p":[17,18],"pp":"3","s":"Reorganization of the Department of Commerce, creation of the Department of Labor, the proposed Korean legislative assembly, and police training milestones."},{"t":"Economic Activities","lv":2,"p":[19,20],"pp":"5","s":"Agricultural conditions, flood damage to rail and wire communications, the ¥200,000,000 road relief program, lagging summer grain collection, price ceilings, and currency changes."},{"t":"Social Activities","lv":2,"p":[21,22],"pp":"7","s":"Cholera containment, adoption of the Korean Red Cross charter, teacher training institutes, educational missions, and press commentary on price controls and national independence."},{"t":"Part II: Political","lv":1,"p":[23,36],"pp":"9","s":"Korean administration, party movements, statutory enactments, police reorganization, prison industries, and internal security operations."},{"t":"Government and Politics","lv":2,"p":[25,26],"pp":"11","s":"Creation of the Department of Labor, establishment of Cheju Province, foreign trade licensing rules, and the Military Governor's proposal for an interim Korean legislative body."},{"t":"Legal and Public Safety","lv":2,"p":[27,36],"pp":"13","s":"Ordinances 92-99, food protests and a counterfeiting trial, National Police reorganization, training and communications, plus prison administration and flood relief."},{"t":"Part III: Economic","lv":1,"p":[37,60],"pp":"23","s":"Natural resources, agricultural and mineral output, industry, transportation repairs, labor regulations, foreign trade, price controls, and financial conditions."},{"t":"Natural Resources","lv":2,"p":[39,40],"pp":"25","s":"Poor grain germination in Kyonggi-do, Inchon fisheries closed by cholera, halted forestry operations, Kyongsang-pukto coal output, and pyrophyllite and talc deposit surveys."},{"t":"Commerce and Industry","lv":2,"p":[41,60],"pp":"27","s":"Textile manufacturing, flood repairs to railways, reopened U.S. mail, Ordinance 97 on labor, food and coal imports, consumer price ceilings, and foreign exchange limits under Ordinance 93."},{"t":"Part IV: Social","lv":1,"p":[61,76],"pp":"47","s":"Public health, welfare institutions, disease control, repatriation figures, education, cultural property recovery, and public information media."},{"t":"Public Health and Welfare","lv":2,"p":[63,68],"pp":"49","s":"Increased welfare subsidies, Korean Red Cross reorganization under Kim Kyu Sic, cumulative repatriation figures, rinderpest cordons, cholera containment, and medical supply imports."},{"t":"Education, Culture and Religion","lv":2,"p":[69,72],"pp":"55","s":"School attendance data, teacher summer institutes, normal school sessions, the Korean Educational Mission's return, recovery of temple art treasures, and missionary arrivals."},{"t":"Public Information","lv":2,"p":[73,76],"pp":"59","s":"Public education campaigns on food and cholera, visual education film strips, new radio programming, periodical licensing, and press reaction to price controls and Right-Left unification."},{"t":"Map of Korea","lv":1,"p":[77,84],"pp":"63","s":"Full-page administrative map of Korea showing provincial borders, principal cities, and the 38th parallel boundary."}],"by":"gemini-3.8-flash-high"},{"id":"J-11","ia":"summation-non-military-activities-japan-no-11-1946-08","s":"J","no":11,"d":"1946-08","d2":"","t":"Summation of Non-Military Activities in Japan, No. 11 (August 1946)","v":"","pg":149,"o":"This eleventh monthly report covers August 1946 non-military activities of the Supreme Commander for the Allied Powers (SCAP) across occupied Japan, marking the close of the first full year of occupation. The month witnessed major constitutional and political milestones, notably the House of Representatives passing the amended draft constitution by 421 to 8 after intensive subcommittee debates, and the resignation of Speaker Senso Higai over political impartiality concerns. In the economic realm, acute food shortages prompted massive emergency distributions of imported American foodstuffs across 23 prefectures, while the Cabinet established the Price Board and submitted a second comprehensive land reform bill. U.S. cotton imports revitalized the textile industry, and SCAP placed 505 industrial facilities into reparations custody. At the International Military Tribunal for the Far East, prosecution presented evidence on aggression in China, featuring key testimony from Henry Pu Yi regarding Kwantung Army domination of Manchukuo. Historians will find especially valuable the special 33-page first-anniversary appendix reviewing occupation policies, administrative mechanisms, and statistical progress from August 1945 through August 1946.","h":["The House of Representatives passed the amended draft constitution 421 to 8 on 24 August and sent it to the House of Peers.","House Speaker Senso Higai resigned after bipartisan criticism of his political intervention in the constitutional drafting process.","Former Emperor Henry Pu Yi testified before the International Military Tribunal for the Far East that the Kwantung Army completely controlled Manchukuo.","The Japanese Government submitted a second land reform bill to the Diet, limiting tenant holdings and enabling tenant farmers to buy land.","Critical food shortages led to emergency releases of imported American food in 23 prefectures, and the Cabinet established the Price Board.","SCAP designated 505 industrial plants in eight categories, including 273 munitions facilities, for reparations custody.","Arrivals of American raw cotton tripled cotton yarn production over June levels, while coal output reached a postwar daily peak of 72,800 metric tons.","The NFLU and NCIU held inaugural national conventions in Tokyo, and a first-anniversary appendix reviewed the occupation since MacArthur's Atsugi landing on 30 August 1945."],"k":["Constitution of Japan","Land reform","Reparations","International Military Tribunal for the Far East","Henry Pu Yi","Economic Stabilization Board","Price Board","Textile industry","Labor unions","Antarctic whaling","Food crisis","Review of the Occupation"],"toc":[{"t":"Front matter","lv":1,"p":[1,5],"pp":"","s":"Title pages, table of contents, and list of charts, maps, and graphs."},{"t":"Part I: General","lv":1,"p":[6,13],"pp":"1","s":"General summary of August 1946: House approval of the draft constitution, Speaker Higai's resignation, land reform and Price Board, U.S. cotton-driven textile expansion, 505 reparations plants, and cholera control."},{"t":"Organization Under SCAP","lv":2,"p":[7,7],"pp":"2","s":"General Orders created two executive offices under the Deputy Chief of Staff and re-established the Civil Intelligence Section as a Special Staff Section."},{"t":"Political Activities","lv":2,"p":[7,9],"pp":"3","s":"The House of Representatives approved the new constitution and Speaker Senso Higai resigned; the section also notes police reforms and war crimes trials."},{"t":"Economic Activities","lv":2,"p":[10,11],"pp":"9","s":"Covers agricultural land reform proposals, peak daily coal output, textile expansion spurred by U.S. cotton, establishment of the Price Board, and reparations plant designations."},{"t":"Social Activities","lv":2,"p":[12,13],"pp":"13","s":"Covers welfare relief, repatriation and cholera control, the Cabinet-level Educational Reform Committee, Shinto restrictions, and public information media programs."},{"t":"Part II: Political","lv":1,"p":[14,34],"pp":"17","s":"Government administration, public safety, and legal matters: the 421-8 constitution vote, Speaker Higai's resignation, the Economic Stabilization Board, police reorganization, and Henry Pu Yi's tribunal testimony."},{"t":"Government and Civil Administration","lv":2,"p":[15,21],"pp":"19","s":"House passed the amended draft constitution 421 to 8 and Speaker Senso Higai resigned; also covers Diet attendance, purge screening, and the Economic Stabilization Board."},{"t":"Public Safety and Intelligence","lv":2,"p":[22,24],"pp":"33","s":"Covers labor demonstrations in Tokyo, tighter controls on illegal Korean entry, black-market raids, proposed police reorganization, prison instructions, and fire and crime statistics."},{"t":"Legal and War Crimes","lv":2,"p":[25,34],"pp":"39","s":"Covers property rulings, family code revisions, provost court cases, war criminal arrests and trials, and IMTFE proceedings including Henry Pu Yi's testimony on Manchukuo."},{"t":"Part III: Economic","lv":1,"p":[35,117],"pp":"59","s":"Economic reporting: second land reform bill, coal at a postwar daily peak of 72,800 tons, threefold rise in cotton yarn, new unions, Price Board, and 505 plants designated for reparations."},{"t":"Agriculture and Fisheries","lv":2,"p":[36,40],"pp":"61","s":"The second land reform bill went to the Diet; also a five-year sericulture plan, chemical fertilizer allocations, fish catches, and SCAP authorization of Antarctic whaling."},{"t":"Forestry and Mining","lv":2,"p":[41,46],"pp":"71","s":"Coal output reached a postwar daily peak of 72,800 metric tons; also covers lumber, plywood and wood pulp production, mineral extraction, and oil exploration delays."},{"t":"Heavy Industries","lv":2,"p":[47,55],"pp":"83","s":"Covers coke, fuel-constrained iron and steel output, nonferrous metals, petroleum refining, cement, highway construction expenditures, and chemical and machinery manufacturing."},{"t":"Manufacturing","lv":2,"p":[56,64],"pp":"101","s":"Covers food processing and brewing, paper, optical and medical instruments, refractories, electrical manufacturing, motor vehicles, tractors, and agricultural equipment."},{"t":"Textile Industries","lv":2,"p":[65,76],"pp":"119","s":"Cotton yarn output tripled after American cotton arrived; also covers raw silk, rayon, knit goods, clothing, and fish netting."},{"t":"Transportation and Public Utilities","lv":2,"p":[77,80],"pp":"143","s":"Covers motor vehicle operations, rail traffic and new rolling stock, shipping, electric power and hydroelectric construction, and the coal shortage's effect on the gas industry."},{"t":"Communications","lv":2,"p":[81,89],"pp":"151","s":"Covers Ministry of Communications reorganization and legislation, telephone and radiotelegraph expansion, radio frequency assignments, postal services, and communications equipment manufacturing."},{"t":"Labor","lv":2,"p":[90,93],"pp":"169","s":"The NFLU and NCIU held inaugural conventions; the Diet considered the Labor Relations Adjustment Bill; mass dismissals were disputed; a U.S. Labor Advisory Committee made recommendations."},{"t":"Imports and Exports","lv":2,"p":[94,95],"pp":"177","s":"Covers incoming U.S. food, cotton, and phosphate rock; Burma and Hong Kong trade missions; and exports of raw silk, tea, lead, and rubber."},{"t":"Rationing and Price Control","lv":2,"p":[96,105],"pp":"181","s":"Food shortages required imported food releases in 23 prefectures; the Cabinet-level Price Board was set up; also produce price controls, fertilizer distribution, and recovered hoarded goods."},{"t":"Finance","lv":2,"p":[106,108],"pp":"201","s":"Currency circulation rose to ¥55.36 billion; also commercial and savings bank statistics, liquidation of closed institutions, national budget and debt figures, and Imperial Household finances."},{"t":"Property Control and Reparations","lv":2,"p":[109,117],"pp":"207","s":"Covers Allied and Axis property administration, precious metals and gems custody, Schedule of Restricted Concerns revisions, dissolution of control associations, and 505 plants designated for reparations."},{"t":"Part IV: Social","lv":1,"p":[118,131],"pp":"223","s":"Social reporting on public health, welfare, education, religion, cultural preservation, and media, including cholera quarantine, the Educational Reform Committee, and Shinto restrictions."},{"t":"Public Health and Welfare","lv":2,"p":[119,125],"pp":"225","s":"Covers relief grants, the Daily Life Security Bill, repatriation and cholera quarantine for illegal Korean entrants, hospital and veterinary affairs, supply distribution, and nutrition surveys."},{"t":"Education, Religion and Media of Expression","lv":2,"p":[126,131],"pp":"237","s":"Covers a Diet resolution on education, the Cabinet Educational Reform Committee, Shinto financial restrictions, cultural property protection, and press, radio, film, and theatrical activities."},{"t":"Appendix: Review of the Occupation (30 August 1945 - 31 August 1946)","lv":1,"p":[132,149],"pp":"249","s":"First-anniversary review of the occupation, 30 August 1945 to 31 August 1946, covering SCAP organization, achievements, and economic progress."},{"t":"Organization under SCAP","lv":2,"p":[133,134],"pp":"251","s":"Reviews MacArthur's landing, the surrender signing, activation of 14 special staff sections, and indirect administration through the Japanese Government."},{"t":"Actions and Accomplishments of the Occupation","lv":2,"p":[135,142],"pp":"255","s":"Reviews the Occupation's first year: 4,402,357 Japanese repatriated by 1 September 1946, Directive No. 3 price controls, the 4 January 1946 purge of some 186,000, new labor union law, and the Tokyo war crimes trial of 28 suspects."},{"t":"Economic Progress","lv":2,"p":[143,149],"pp":"271","s":"Reports coal output peaking at 72,800 metric tons daily in August 1946, textile output tripled since January, rail freight rates raised 300 percent, public works for up to 2,000,000 workers, and 504 installations designated for reparations."}],"by":"gemini-3.8-flash-high"},{"id":"K-11","ia":"summation-usamg-activities-korea-no-11-1946-08","s":"K","no":11,"d":"1946-08","d2":"","t":"Summation of United States Army Military Government Activities in Korea, No. 11 (August 1946)","v":"","pg":126,"o":"This eleventh monthly summation covers United States Army Military Government activities in South Korea during August 1946, marking the first anniversary of liberation from Japanese colonial rule. Significant political events include the mass celebration of Liberation Day on 15 August, the ceremonial return of the 1910 Annexation Treaty and nine historic Korean royal seals, and published correspondence between Lieutenant General John R. Hodge and Soviet Colonel General I. M. Chistiakov regarding deadlock in the Joint Commission. The administration established the Department of Public Works (Ordinance 104) and the Property Claims Commission (Ordinance 103), while General Hodge directed the transfer of operational administration to Korean personnel. Economic reporting focuses on post-flood agricultural recovery, National Food Regulation No. 2 instituting the 1946-1947 rice collection system, and the extensive holdings of the New Korea Company. Social affairs highlight containment of a severe cholera epidemic that caused nearly 7,000 deaths, the founding of Seoul National University (Ordinance 102), and mass repatriation. A special one-year appendix comprehensively reviews occupation policies since September 1945. For historians, the issue provides critical primary documentation on Korean civil administration, political factionalism, educational reform, and public health management.","h":["More than one million Koreans celebrated the first anniversary of liberation in Seoul on 15 August, marked by the ceremonial return of the 1910 Annexation Treaty and nine historic royal seals.","Lt. Gen. John R. Hodge and Soviet Col. Gen. I. M. Chistiakov exchanged published letters over the Joint Commission deadlock, Hodge urging reconvening and rejecting ideological restrictions on Korean democratic participation.","General Hodge directed the transfer of operational civil administration to Korean officials, leaving American personnel largely in advisory roles; Ordinance No. 104 created the Department of Public Works.","Ordinance No. 103 established a seven-member Property Claims Commission with final authority to settle disputed titles to Japanese property.","Ordinance No. 102 founded Seoul National University, merging nine existing colleges and institutes under a unified Board of Regents.","National Food Regulation No. 2 set local quotas and administrative procedures for the 1946-1947 rice collection program, while post-flood crop rehabilitation continued.","A major cholera epidemic produced 10,648 cases and 6,894 deaths, with nearly ten million vaccine units distributed and fishery bans imposed.","An appendix reviews the first year of military government, 7 September 1945 to 31 August 1946, including education reaching 1.7 million students with Korean textbooks."],"k":["USAMGIK","Joint Commission","Liberation Day","John R. Hodge","Seoul National University","Cholera Epidemic","Rice Collection","Property Claims Commission","New Korea Company","Repatriation","National Police","Korean Constabulary"],"toc":[{"t":"Front matter","lv":1,"p":[1,14],"pp":"","s":"Cover, title pages, table of contents and list of charts, graphs and maps for the August 1946 monthly summation."},{"t":"Part I: General","lv":1,"p":[15,26],"pp":"1","s":"General overview of August 1946: Liberation Day and Hodge-Chistiakov letters, Property Claims Commission, Ordinance No. 102, National Food Regulation No. 2, New Korea Company assets, and cholera containment."},{"t":"Section 1. Political Activities","lv":2,"p":[17,20],"pp":"3","s":"Covers party leader resignations, Liberation Day observances, the Hodge-Chistiakov Joint Commission letters, creation of the Property Claims Commission, and public safety."},{"t":"Section 2. Economic Activities","lv":2,"p":[21,24],"pp":"7","s":"Covers post-flood crop rehabilitation, factory openings, sericulture goals, National Food Regulation No. 2, currency expansion, and New Korea Company asset custody."},{"t":"Section 3. Social Activities","lv":2,"p":[25,26],"pp":"11","s":"Covers cholera containment, Seoul National University's founding under Ordinance No. 102, distribution of Korean textbooks, and repatriation progress."},{"t":"Part II: Political","lv":1,"p":[27,42],"pp":"13","s":"Civil administration and politics: the new Department of Public Works, Liberation Day parade, Hodge-Chistiakov correspondence, Ordinance No. 103 creating the Property Claims Commission, an Rhee assassination plot, and crime trends."},{"t":"Section 1. Government and Politics","lv":2,"p":[29,34],"pp":"15","s":"Reports creation of the Department of Public Works, party resignations, the massive 15 August Liberation Day parade, and the Hodge-Chistiakov correspondence."},{"t":"Section 2. Legal and Public Safety","lv":2,"p":[35,42],"pp":"21","s":"Reports Ordinance No. 103 creating the Property Claims Commission, an assassination plot against Syngman Rhee, naval coastal patrols, crime trends, and Coast Guard pay scales."},{"t":"Part III: Economic","lv":1,"p":[43,84],"pp":"29","s":"Economic statistics and reports: post-flood agricultural recovery, 1944-1945 crop figures, fishery bans, coal, industrial reopenings, railroad repairs, National Food Regulation No. 2, living costs and the New Korea Company."},{"t":"Section 1. Natural Resources","lv":2,"p":[45,56],"pp":"31","s":"Reports flood recovery on arable land, 1944-1945 crop production statistics, fishery bans due to cholera, fuel wood shortages, and subsidized coal mining."},{"t":"Section 2. Commerce and Industry","lv":2,"p":[57,84],"pp":"43","s":"Covers industrial reopenings, sericulture plans, railroad flood repairs, National Food Regulation No. 2 for grain collections, rising living costs, and New Korea Company management."},{"t":"Part IV: Social","lv":1,"p":[85,106],"pp":"71","s":"Social affairs: the cholera epidemic and vaccination, welfare subsidies, repatriation, Ordinance No. 102 and Seoul National University, Korean textbooks, royal seals, and radio, film and press coverage."},{"t":"Section 1. Public Health and Welfare","lv":2,"p":[87,92],"pp":"73","s":"Documents the severe cholera epidemic and vaccination campaigns, welfare subsidies, repatriation of Japanese nationals, and Korea's admission as associate member of the World Health Assembly."},{"t":"Section 2. Education, Culture and Religion","lv":2,"p":[93,100],"pp":"79","s":"Details Ordinance No. 102 creating Seoul National University, new Korean-script textbooks, the return of the Education Mission, and recovery of historic royal seals."},{"t":"Section 3. Public Information","lv":2,"p":[101,106],"pp":"87","s":"Covers political education lecture teams, public health radio programming, motion picture attendance, Military Governor press conferences, and Korean press debates over political unification."},{"t":"Appendix: Review of Military Government Activities 7 September 1945 - 31 August 1946","lv":1,"p":[107,118],"pp":"93","s":"One-year review of American military government in South Korea, 7 September 1945 to 31 August 1946: occupation problems, civil administration and politics, and social reforms."},{"t":"Section 1. Problems of the Occupation","lv":2,"p":[109,112],"pp":"95","s":"Reviews early administrative difficulties, repatriation of Japanese military and civilians, elimination of Japanese influence, and responses to floods and cholera."},{"t":"Section 2. Civil Administration and Politics","lv":2,"p":[113,116],"pp":"99","s":"Chronicles transfer of governmental operations to Korean officials, civil service and police restructuring, internal security force development, and efforts toward a legislative assembly."},{"t":"Section 3. Social Reforms","lv":2,"p":[117,118],"pp":"103","s":"Summarizes a year of achievements in public health and epidemic prevention, and democratic education serving 1.7 million students with Korean textbooks."},{"t":"Map of Korea","lv":1,"p":[119,126],"pp":"105","s":"Reference map of the Korean peninsula showing provincial boundaries, major cities, transport lines, and the 38th parallel division."}],"by":"gemini-3.8-flash-high"},{"id":"J-12","ia":"summation-non-military-activities-japan-no-12-1946-09","s":"J","no":12,"d":"1946-09","d2":"","t":"Summation of Non-Military Activities in Japan, No. 12 (September 1946)","v":"","pg":120,"o":"Summation No. 12 covers non-military activities in Japan during September 1946 (with statistical data primarily from July and August 1946). Significant political developments include the House of Peers' line-by-line review of the draft democratic constitution, the Cabinet's announcement purging 899 high-ranking officials, and the Diet's passage of four historic local government reform bills establishing popular election of governors and mayors. In the economic realm, the Diet enacted the Labor Relations Adjustment Law amidst intense labor strife, notably a 10-day maritime strike and a narrowly averted nationwide railway strike. SCAP and the Japanese Government accelerated Zaibatsu dissolution, designating the Mitsui, Mitsubishi, Sumitomo, Yasuda, and Fuji holding companies, while introducing major tax bills to cancel war indemnities and impose a progressive capital levy. Food shortages remained severe, requiring substantial releases of imported United States grains and canned rations. International postal service resumed, and the Tokyo war crimes trials advanced with extensive evidence on state-sponsored opium operations in China. For historians, the report provides invaluable quantitative data on industrial reconversion, hyperinflationary note circulation, labor union expansion past 3.7 million members, and local government democratization.","h":["The Diet passed four local government reform bills providing for direct popular election of prefectural governors, city mayors, and local headmen, while the House of Peers reviewed the draft constitution line by line.","The Cabinet announced that 899 high-ranking government and political figures were forced to resign under the SCAP Purge Directive.","The Diet enacted the Labor Relations Adjustment Law amid labor strife, including a 10-day maritime strike and a narrowly averted railway strike; union membership passed 3.7 million.","The Holding Company Liquidation Commission designated five Zaibatsu holding companies (Mitsui, Mitsubishi, Sumitomo, Yasuda, and Fuji Industrial) for dissolution.","Bills for a 100 percent war indemnities tax and a progressive capital levy were introduced, while Bank of Japan note circulation reached a record ¥64.4 billion.","International postal service from Japan resumed on 10 September; General Orders No. 30 of 7 September created the Civil Transportation Section as a Special Staff Section of GHQ, SCAP.","Relief disbursements exceeded ¥101 million under the new Daily Life Security Law, and 248,677 Japanese were repatriated during the period.","The IMTFE heard prosecution evidence on Japanese state-sponsored opium monopolies and narcotics trafficking in occupied China."],"k":["Constitution of Japan","Local Government Reform","Purge Directive","Zaibatsu Dissolution","Labor Relations Adjustment Law","All-Japan Seamen's Union","International Military Tribunal for the Far East","Holding Company Liquidation Commission","War Indemnities Tax","Repatriation","Daily Life Security Law","Black Markets"],"toc":[{"t":"Front matter","lv":1,"p":[1,4],"pp":"","s":"Title page, table of contents, and list of charts, maps, and graphs."},{"t":"Part I: General","lv":1,"p":[5,12],"pp":"1","s":"General summary of September 1946: Civil Transportation Section created, House of Peers reviewing the draft constitution, Labor Relations Adjustment Law, Zaibatsu dissolution, international mail resumed, and repatriation of over 248,000 Japanese."},{"t":"Organization Under SCAP","lv":2,"p":[6,6],"pp":"3","s":"General Orders No. 30 of 7 September 1946 established the Civil Transportation Section as a Special Staff Section of GHQ, SCAP."},{"t":"Political Activities","lv":2,"p":[6,7],"pp":"3","s":"House of Peers debated the draft constitution; Shinsei-kai formed the New People's Party; four local government reform bills passed 20 September; 899 leaders purged; 165 war crimes suspects ordered delivered to Sugamo."},{"t":"Economic Activities","lv":2,"p":[8,9],"pp":"7","s":"Covers agricultural forecasts, industrial output, resumption of international mail, passage of the Labor Relations Adjustment Law, and Zaibatsu dissolution actions."},{"t":"Social Activities","lv":2,"p":[10,12],"pp":"11","s":"Covers passage of the Daily Life Security Bill, repatriation of over 248,000 Japanese, Educational Reform Committee meetings, and public health programs."},{"t":"Part II: Political","lv":1,"p":[13,28],"pp":"15","s":"Political activities in September 1946: the draft constitution, purge of 899 officials, local government reform bills, black-market crackdowns, juvenile delinquency, and IMTFE narcotics testimony."},{"t":"Government and Civil Administration","lv":2,"p":[14,16],"pp":"17","s":"House of Peers deliberated the draft constitution; political parties realigned; 899 officials were purged; the Diet passed four major local government reform bills."},{"t":"Public Safety and Intelligence","lv":2,"p":[17,18],"pp":"23","s":"Black-market crackdowns, rising juvenile delinquency, police adjustments, Tokyo fire prevention measures, and July fire losses of over ¥294 million."},{"t":"Legal and War Crimes","lv":2,"p":[19,28],"pp":"27","s":"Judicial code revisions, occupation court trials, sentencing of war criminals, and IMTFE testimony on Japanese-run narcotics trade, economic exploitation in China, and Axis treaty negotiations."},{"t":"Part III: Economic","lv":1,"p":[29,107],"pp":"47","s":"Economic activities in August and September 1946: rice harvest, coal and industrial output, textiles, 10-day maritime strike, trade, record note circulation, Zaibatsu dissolution, and reparations."},{"t":"Agriculture and Fisheries","lv":2,"p":[30,32],"pp":"49","s":"Favorable rice harvest estimates, August fish catch of 79,368 metric tons, Antarctic whaling expedition plans, and pearl oyster harvesting in Ago Bay."},{"t":"Forestry and Mining","lv":2,"p":[33,40],"pp":"55","s":"August coal production rose to 1,770,200 metric tons; a five-year coal goal is set; lumber production and crude petroleum output trends and labor disputes are reported."},{"t":"Heavy Industries","lv":2,"p":[41,47],"pp":"67","s":"Iron, steel, and coke output fell because of coal shortages; civilian shipyard repairs and fertilizer manufacture progressed; 396 machinery plants were designated for reparations."},{"t":"Manufacturing","lv":2,"p":[48,57],"pp":"81","s":"Production reports for food processing, paper, glass, optical instruments, medical supplies, electrical equipment, motor vehicles, bicycles, rubber products, tanned leather, and agricultural implements."},{"t":"Textile Industries","lv":2,"p":[58,69],"pp":"99","s":"Cotton yarn production rose 50 percent; 19 American cotton cargoes were received; raw silk, rayon, wool manufacturing and clothing deliveries are also reported."},{"t":"Transportation and Public Utilities","lv":2,"p":[70,73],"pp":"123","s":"Railway freight and coastwise shipping rose slightly, hydroelectric generation was adequate, and urban gas utilities were severely curtailed by coal shortages."},{"t":"Communications","lv":2,"p":[74,81],"pp":"131","s":"Telephone and telegraph rehabilitation, international postal service resumed 10 September, radio expansion, equipment manufacture, and postal and communications labor negotiations."},{"t":"Labor","lv":2,"p":[82,87],"pp":"147","s":"Diet passed the Labor Relations Adjustment Law; Labor Standards Bill hearings; 10-day maritime strike; railway strike averted; trade unions passed 3.7 million members."},{"t":"Imports and Exports","lv":2,"p":[88,90],"pp":"161","s":"Foreign trade planning with Allied nations, shipments of raw silk, tea, and minerals, and incoming United States food, raw cotton, and phosphate rock."},{"t":"Rationing and Price Control","lv":2,"p":[91,99],"pp":"167","s":"Staple food collections, releases of imported cereals and canned goods, new meat and vegetable price schedules, recovery of hoarded goods, and licensing controls over street stalls."},{"t":"Finance","lv":2,"p":[100,102],"pp":"185","s":"Bank of Japan note circulation hit a record ¥64.4 billion; bills introduced for a 100 percent war indemnities tax and a capital levy; national debt and local budgets covered."},{"t":"Property Control and Reparations","lv":2,"p":[103,107],"pp":"191","s":"German assets impounded, looted foreign films and vessels inventoried, precious metals held, five major Zaibatsu designated for dissolution, and reparations plants inspected."},{"t":"Part IV: Social","lv":1,"p":[108,120],"pp":"199","s":"Social activities in August and September 1946: relief under the Daily Life Security Law, repatriation, cholera, education reform, teacher screening, missionaries, and information media."},{"t":"Public Health and Welfare","lv":2,"p":[109,115],"pp":"201","s":"Relief disbursements exceeded ¥101 million under the new Daily Life Security Law; 248,677 Japanese repatriated; medical supply production rose and cholera declined."},{"t":"Education, Religion and Media of Expression","lv":2,"p":[116,120],"pp":"215","s":"Educational Reform Committee met weekly; adult education programs ran; over 63,000 teachers were screened; Christian missionaries returned; public information campaigns continued."}],"by":"gemini-3.8-flash-high"},{"id":"K-12","ia":"summation-usamg-activities-korea-no-12-1946-09","s":"K","no":12,"d":"1946-09","d2":"","t":"Summation of United States Army Military Government Activities in Korea, No. 12 (September 1946)","v":"","pg":105,"o":"This September 1946 monthly report documents a pivotal period of administrative transition and escalating civil unrest in the United States Army Military Government in Korea (USAMGIK). Markedly, the Military Governor ordered Korean directors to assume full operational control of government departments, relegating American personnel to advisory roles, while preparations advanced for an interim Korean legislative assembly. However, governance was severely disrupted by the outbreak of the nationwide railroad strike on 22 September, which paralyzed ground transportation, stranded repatriates, hampered coal distribution, and precipitated an acute food crisis with skyrocketing black-market rice prices. Authorities responded with emergency airlifts of medical supplies, arrests of top Communist leaders including Pak Heun Young, and suspensions of leftist newspapers. Other significant developments include the promulgation of the Child Labor Law (Ordinance No. 112), commencement of government-to-government barter trade with Japan, structural reorganization of public education into a 6-6 system, opening of Seoul National University colleges, and ongoing efforts to control a lethal cholera epidemic that claimed over 9,400 lives. For historians, the report provides invaluable quantitative data on labor unrest, industrial stagnation, disease mortality, currency expansion, and the administrative mechanics of early South Korean state formation.","h":["On 13 September the Military Governor ordered Korean directors to assume full executive charge of all government departments, reducing American personnel to advisory roles while plans advanced for a Korean legislative assembly.","A general railway strike began on 22 September, halting rail transport across South Korea, disrupting coal movement and mail, stranding repatriates, and driving an urban food crisis with rising black-market rice prices.","Military authorities ordered the arrest of Communist leaders Pak Heun Young, Lee Kang Kook and Lee Chu Ha, and the Military Governor suspended three leftist newspapers for inflammatory articles.","Dr. Syngman Rhee escaped unharmed when assassins fired into his automobile on 12 September on the way to an independence rally in Seoul.","Ordinance No. 112 established Korea's first comprehensive Child Labor Law, prohibiting industrial employment of children under fourteen and regulating maximum working hours.","Government-to-government barter trade with Japan began with 24 million yen of minerals and fish shipped for coal and industrial chemicals; Bank of Chosen notes in circulation exceeded 11.3 billion yen.","Cholera in South Korea reached 14,614 cases and 9,474 deaths by 30 September, prompting emergency air shipments of medical supplies during the transport strike.","A Women's Bureau was created in the Department of Public Health and Welfare, and Seoul National University opened its colleges under unified reorganization despite student protests."],"k":["USAMGIK","September railway strike","Pak Heun Young","Syngman Rhee","Koreanization of administration","Child Labor Law","cholera epidemic","barter trade with Japan","grain collection program","Seoul National University","repatriation","Bank of Chosen notes"],"toc":[{"t":"Front matter","lv":1,"p":[1,17],"pp":"","s":"Title page, table of contents, and list of charts, graphs and maps for Report No. 12 (September 1946)."},{"t":"Part I: General","lv":1,"p":[18,25],"pp":"1","s":"General summary of September 1946: Korean directors took charge of departments, the railway strike disrupted transport and food supply, barter trade with Japan began, and a Women's Bureau and cholera control featured socially."},{"t":"Political Activities","lv":2,"p":[20,21],"pp":"3","s":"Korean directors took full charge of departments as Americans became advisers; General Hodge condemned the illegal railway strike; police academy classes graduated; arrests were ordered for three Communist leaders."},{"t":"Economic Activities","lv":2,"p":[22,23],"pp":"5","s":"The railway strike paralyzed rail transport, disrupted coal movement and caused an urban food crisis. Barter trade with Japan began with mineral exports; Bank of Chosen notes in circulation passed 11.3 billion yen."},{"t":"Social Activities","lv":2,"p":[24,25],"pp":"7","s":"A Women's Bureau was established, UNRRA clothing arrived, and cholera climbed. Seoul National University opened its colleges under unified reorganization, and three leftist newspapers were suspended for inflammatory articles."},{"t":"Part II: Political","lv":1,"p":[26,39],"pp":"9","s":"Political developments in September 1946: Korean directors' executive charge, the Communist arrest orders, provost court sentences, the attempt on Syngman Rhee, police reorganization into three zones, and Coast Guard advisers."},{"t":"Government and Politics","lv":2,"p":[28,33],"pp":"11","s":"Korean directors assumed executive charge of departments and plans advanced for a Korean legislative assembly. Hodge addressed the rail strike's political motives; arrests were ordered for Communist leaders including Pak Heun Young."},{"t":"Legal and Public Safety","lv":2,"p":[34,39],"pp":"17","s":"Provost courts sentenced editors of suspended leftist papers; Syngman Rhee survived an assassination attempt in Seoul. Police were reorganized into three provincial zones and U.S. Coast Guard advisers arrived for the Korean Coast Guard."},{"t":"Part III: Economic","lv":1,"p":[40,73],"pp":"23","s":"Economic conditions: summer grain collection delayed by the rail strike, falling coal output, copper smelting, the strike's food-shortage effects, barter trade with Japan, child labor regulation, and currency circulation above 11.3 billion yen."},{"t":"Natural Resources","lv":2,"p":[42,51],"pp":"25","s":"Summer grain collections were delayed by the rail strike; the Korea Land Development Company was dissolved and its assets went to government reclamation. Hwasun mine strikes cut coal output; copper smelting at Changhang led mining."},{"t":"Commerce and Industry","lv":2,"p":[52,73],"pp":"33","s":"A massive rail strike halted transport and mail and worsened urban food shortages, raising black-market rice prices. Barter trade with Japan began, child labor rules were promulgated, and currency circulation exceeded 11.3 billion yen."},{"t":"Part IV: Social","lv":1,"p":[74,97],"pp":"55","s":"Social conditions: cholera epidemic and relief, repatriation, Seoul National University and school reorganization, 4-H clubs, returning missionaries, and information campaigns on child labor, rice collection and cholera prevention."},{"t":"Public Health and Welfare","lv":2,"p":[76,85],"pp":"57","s":"A Women's Bureau was established, UNRRA relief supplies arrived at Inchon, and over 812,000 Japanese had been repatriated. Cholera reached 14,614 cases and 9,474 deaths, requiring emergency air shipment of medical supplies."},{"t":"Education, Culture and Religion","lv":2,"p":[86,91],"pp":"67","s":"Seoul National University inaugurated its colleges under a unified system despite student protests; secondary schools adopted a 6-6 curriculum. Kyonggi-do households voted to establish 4-H clubs; American and Australian missionaries returned."},{"t":"Public Information","lv":2,"p":[92,97],"pp":"73","s":"Campaigns publicized the child labor law, rice collection and cholera prevention, and 'The Culture Hour' debuted on radio. The Military Governor suspended three leftist newspapers, prompting editorial debate on press freedom."},{"t":"Map of Korea","lv":1,"p":[98,105],"pp":"79","s":"Full-page reference map of Korea showing provincial borders, provincial capitals, principal ports and cities, and the 38th parallel demarcation line."}],"by":"gemini-3.8-flash-high"},{"id":"K-12-econ","ia":"summation-usamg-activities-korea-no-12-1946-09-economic","s":"K","no":12,"d":"1946-09","d2":"","t":"Summation of United States Army Military Government Activities in Korea, No. 12 (September 1946): Economic","v":"Economic section","pg":35,"o":"This September 1946 monthly report (Part III: Economic of Summation No. 12) documents the severe economic crisis confronting the United States Army Military Government in Korea (USAMGIK). Economic activity was paralyzed by the general railroad strike of 22 September, which involved over 30,000 railway employees protesting acute food shortages and runaway inflation, quickly spreading to telegraph, municipal, and newspaper workers. Transportation stoppages exacerbated a critical food supply emergency, leaving 6.5 million citizens dependent on official rations while summer grain collections attained only 52 percent of pre-flood goals and black-market rice hit record highs. The report details efforts to revive basic industry and mining, including copper smelting at Changhang, barter agreements exchanging Korean minerals for Japanese coal and chemicals, and the passage of Ordinance 112 (the Child Labor Law). For historians, this report is invaluable for understanding the explosive socio-economic conditions preceding the Autumn Uprising of 1946, illustrating the friction between military government policies, severe industrial bottlenecks, food distribution failures, and emerging Korean organized labor.","h":["Over 30,000 railroad workers staged a general strike on 22 September protesting living costs and food shortages, halting all rail traffic across South Korea.","Ordinance 112 (Child Labor Law) was signed on Labor Day, barring children under 14 from mercantile and industrial work and regulating youth working hours.","Government grain collections reached only 88,650 metric tons, 52 percent of pre-flood estimates, leaving 6,500,000 citizens dependent on public rations.","Black-market rice prices spiked to 43 yen per pound in Seoul and 49 yen in Pusan despite emergency grain transport permits.","A government-to-government barter agreement with Japan began, trading 24,000,000 yen of Korean minerals and marine products for coal, parts, and industrial chemicals.","The Korea Land Development Company was formally dissolved on 6 September, transferring its assets to the Department of Agriculture's Reclamation and Irrigation Section.","Seoul established a joint Public Utilities Board of four Koreans and three Americans to oversee city transit and gas service."],"k":["railroad strike","food rationing","black-market rice","Child Labor Law","Ordinance 112","barter trade","coal mining","Changhang Smelter","Korea Land Development Company","fisheries","Public Utilities Board","grain collections"],"toc":[{"t":"Front matter","lv":1,"p":[1,3],"pp":"","s":"War Department cover sheet and publication details for this industrial report supplement."},{"t":"Part III: Economic","lv":1,"p":[4,35],"pp":"23","s":"South Korean economy in September 1946: the 22 September railroad strike, grain and food shortages, Child Labor Ordinance 112, barter trade with Japan, mining and fisheries."},{"t":"Section 1: Natural Resources","lv":2,"p":[6,13],"pp":"25","s":"Grain collections reached only 52 percent of pre-flood estimates; the Korea Land Development Company was dissolved on 6 September; August fisheries production and mining output constraints are reported."},{"t":"Section 2: Commerce and Industry","lv":2,"p":[14,35],"pp":"33","s":"Over 30,000 railroad workers struck on 22 September; Child Labor Ordinance 112 was signed; food rationing shortages worsened; barter trade with Japan began."}],"by":"gemini-3.8-flash-high"},{"id":"K-12-v2","ia":"summation-usamg-activities-korea-no-12-1946-09-v2","s":"K","no":12,"d":"1946-09","d2":"","t":"Summation of United States Army Military Government Activities in Korea, No. 12 (September 1946)","v":"Second copy","pg":104,"o":"Summation No. 12 covers September 1946 in American-occupied South Korea. The month was dominated by the railroad strike of 22 September, which spread to postal, telegraph, newspaper and Seoul City Hall workers, halted transport of grain and supplies, and produced a food crisis; General Hodge's 27 September address blamed radical agitators. Military Government announced on 13 September that Korean directors would take full charge of departments, with Americans advisory, and new Civil Service classification rules took effect 30 September. Other items include the Child Labor Ordinance (No. 112), arrest orders for three Communist Party leaders, suspension of three leftist newspapers, an attempt on Syngman Rhee's life, rising cholera (14,614 cases), the opening of Seoul National University, barter trade with Japan, rationing figures, price indexes and Bank of Chosen note circulation. The report is rich in statistical charts and tables useful to economic and social historians.","h":["On 22 September more than 30,000 railroad workers struck without prior mediation, halting all rail transport in South Korea; on 27 September General Hodge blamed agitators and urged a return to work and negotiation with the National Labor Mediation Board.","The strike spread to postal, telegraph, newspaper and Seoul City Hall workers, halting grain and supply transport and deepening a food crisis in which 6,500,000 depended on rations and only 88,650 metric tons of summer grain were collected.","On 13 September the Military Governor declared that Korean directors must take full charge of departments with Americans only advisory; Rules Number 3 of 30 September set up a civil service classification and pay system.","Arrest of Communist Party leaders Pak Heun Young, Lee Kang Kook and Lee Chu Ha was ordered but not carried out; three leftist newspapers were suspended, two Chung Ang editors were sentenced to 18 months, and an attempt was made on Syngman Rhee's life on 12 September.","Rations were 270 grams daily in Seoul and 250 elsewhere, summer grain collection reached 52 percent of the pre-flood estimate, and black-market rice hit 43 yen per pound in Seoul.","Cholera reached 14,614 cases and 9,474 deaths by 30 September, worst in Kyongsang-namdo (1,549 cases); repatriation continued, with 812,559 Japanese and 915,500 Koreans repatriated to date.","Child Labor Ordinance No. 112, effective 14 September, barred industrial work by children 14 and under, with maximum hours effective 15 February 1947; government barter with Japan began, shipping 24,000,000 yen of graphite, fluorite, talc and fish for coal and industrial goods.","Seoul National University opened with 6,799 students registered by 21 September despite opposition; schools adopted a 6-6 structure, 4,225,000 textbooks were distributed, and Bank of Chosen notes reached 11,341,307,000 yen."],"k":["railroad strike","Hodge","Koreanization of government","civil service","Syngman Rhee","Communist Party arrests","newspaper suspensions","food crisis","grain collection","cholera","repatriation","Child Labor Ordinance","Seoul National University","barter trade with Japan","price indexes"],"toc":[{"t":"Front matter","lv":1,"p":[1,16],"pp":"","s":"Library covers and stamps, title page for No. 12 (September 1946), contents of four parts, and a list of 37 charts, graphs and maps."},{"t":"Part I: General","lv":1,"p":[17,24],"pp":"1","s":"Digest of the month: Koreans take over departments, the 22 September rail strike, the Rhee assassination attempt, grain collection shortfalls, the new Women's Bureau and rising cholera."},{"t":"Section 1. Political Activities","lv":2,"p":[19,20],"pp":"3","s":"The Military Governor announced Korean control of departments on 13 September; new civil service classification followed. Hodge addressed the strike on 27 September; Communist arrest orders and the attempt on Rhee also noted."},{"t":"Section 2. Economic Activities","lv":2,"p":[21,22],"pp":"5","s":"Strikes cut coal and mail service and a Seoul Public Utilities Board was formed. Barter shipments to Japan reached 24,000,000 yen; a food crisis developed and Bank of Chosen notes stood at 11,341,307,000 yen."},{"t":"Section 3. Social Activities","lv":2,"p":[23,24],"pp":"7","s":"Women's Bureau created; 278,905 repatriation spaces; cholera rising; 6,799 students at Seoul National University; new 6-6 school system; three newspapers suspended."},{"t":"Part II: Political","lv":1,"p":[25,38],"pp":"9","s":"Government and politics, including Hodge's statements and party activity, followed by legal developments, crime, police, prisons, and the constabulary and Coast Guard."},{"t":"Section 1. Government and Politics","lv":2,"p":[27,32],"pp":"11","s":"Legislature planning, Korean directors taking charge, civil service academy and pay rules, Hodge's statements on unity and the rail strike, party conventions, and 75 registered national organizations."},{"t":"Administration and Civil Service","lv":3,"p":[27,27],"pp":"11","s":"Legislature preparations; the 13 September policy of Korean control; a Civil Service Training Academy with 120 students; and the Rules Number 3 compensation plan."},{"t":"Political Affairs","lv":3,"p":[28,31],"pp":"12","s":"Hodge spoke on unity, the 8 September anniversary and the rail strike. Rhee's society held a convention; Communist arrest orders issued; Hong Jin died; 75 national organizations registered."},{"t":"Interzone Activities, Joint Commission and Foreign Affairs","lv":3,"p":[31,31],"pp":"15","s":"No North-South mail in September and a growing refugee flow. American delegates stood ready to reopen the Joint Commission; reactions to the Wallace address and a Chinese repatriation list."},{"t":"Section 2. Legal and Public Safety","lv":2,"p":[33,38],"pp":"17","s":"New revenue stamps, child labor trials, press convictions, the 12 September attempt on Rhee, police graduations, crime charts, prisons holding 17,049 inmates, and the constabulary and Coast Guard."},{"t":"Part III: Economic","lv":1,"p":[39,72],"pp":"23","s":"Natural resources (crops, fisheries, coal, minerals) and commerce and industry: production, transport, communications, labor, Japan trade, rationing, prices and finance."},{"t":"Section 1. Natural Resources","lv":2,"p":[41,50],"pp":"25","s":"Rice and grain collections, the Sung Duk project, fisheries output and China exports, fuel wood needs, August coal output of 26,516 tons, and mineral and smelter problems."},{"t":"Agriculture","lv":3,"p":[41,42],"pp":"25","s":"Grain collections were hampered by profiteering and the strike. Sung Duk project 80 percent complete; 700 orchards bonded; Land Development Company dissolved; 175,408 cho of mulberry."},{"t":"Fisheries","lv":3,"p":[42,47],"pp":"26","s":"Production of 3,601 tons in July and 968 in August, 44,700 kg exported to China, agar-agar capacity, 33,227 sail boats, and cloth allocated for sails."},{"t":"Forestry and Mining","lv":3,"p":[47,50],"pp":"29","s":"Winter fuel wood estimated at 150,000 pyong for Seoul; coal output 26,516 tons; mineral needs, Changhang smelter, Onjin and Ilkwan mines; gold mining prohibited."},{"t":"Section 2. Commerce and Industry","lv":2,"p":[51,72],"pp":"33","s":"Industry, rail and road transport, postal and telegraph services, strikes and labor law, Japan trade, food rationing and price indexes, and currency and finance."},{"t":"Industry","lv":3,"p":[51,53],"pp":"33","s":"Technical and material shortages persisted. Paper production peaked but the Hokusen newsprint mill at Kunsan faced shutdown; railway additions cost 717,000 yen; flour mills resumed."},{"t":"Transportation and Public Utilities","lv":3,"p":[53,56],"pp":"35","s":"Travel restrictions lifted 30 September; the rail strike stopped all trains from 22 September; 17 of 33 lighthouses operating; Kyonggi Trucking Service and the Seoul Public Utilities Board."},{"t":"Communications","lv":3,"p":[56,57],"pp":"38","s":"Post office strikes at Taegu, Kyongju and Seoul; a fourth mail shipment of 287 pieces to the U.S.; money order figures; Cheju-do cable repair; radio owners up to 180,342."},{"t":"Labor","lv":3,"p":[58,60],"pp":"40","s":"Strikes by 30,000 railroad workers, telegraph and City Hall staff; Child Labor Law effective 14 September; 42,265 union members in 239 unions; employment and wage tables."},{"t":"Imports and Exports","lv":3,"p":[61,63],"pp":"43","s":"Barter with Japan sent out 24,000,000 yen of graphite, fluorite, talc and fish; coal (511,504 tons January-September), locomotives, textiles and chemicals came in."},{"t":"Rationing and Price Control","lv":3,"p":[63,69],"pp":"45","s":"6,500,000 on rations; 88,650 tons of summer grain collected; 270-gram Seoul ration; black-market rice at 43 yen; price indexes, controlled commodity distribution and Food Regulation No. 3."},{"t":"Finance","lv":3,"p":[70,72],"pp":"52","s":"Bank of Chosen notes at 11,341,307,000 yen, rising loans and deposits, gold prices, and licensing of Tai Han Life Insurance Company with 10,000,000 yen capital."},{"t":"Part IV: Social","lv":1,"p":[73,104],"pp":"55","s":"Public health and welfare, education, culture and religion, and public information, followed by the map of Korea and blank or library end pages."},{"t":"Section 1. Public Health and Welfare","lv":2,"p":[75,84],"pp":"57","s":"Women's Bureau, repatriation totals, refugee camps with 27,657 people, medical college recognition, nursing and veterinary transfers, supply shipments, vaccine stocks, and cholera at 14,614 cases."},{"t":"Public Welfare and Repatriation","lv":3,"p":[75,77],"pp":"57","s":"Welfare training institutes, Women's Bureau duties, Yonghori Leprosarium with 600 patients, 812,559 Japanese and 915,500 Koreans repatriated, and 27,657 North Korean refugees."},{"t":"Medical, Nursing, Pharmaceutical and Veterinary Affairs","lv":3,"p":[77,78],"pp":"59","s":"2,906 physicians renewed licenses; five medical colleges recognized; child mortality of 180 per thousand in the first year; Pusan nursing institute; veterinary work transferred to Koreans."},{"t":"Supply","lv":3,"p":[78,81],"pp":"60","s":"Final 20,000,000 yen Civil Affairs Division medical allocation, 1,000,000 vitamin pills, 300 tons of UNRRA clothing at Inchon, and biological production tables for vaccines."},{"t":"Preventive Medicine","lv":3,"p":[81,84],"pp":"63","s":"Water plant survey, DDT spraying at Pusan, cholera at 14,614 cases and 9,474 deaths, communicable disease charts, and a typhus campaign begun in Seoul on 16 September."},{"t":"Section 2. Education, Culture and Religion","lv":2,"p":[85,90],"pp":"67","s":"Seoul National University opened with 6,799 students; 6-6 school system and core curriculums; 4,225,000 textbooks; Chu-suk festival; 4-H vote in Kyonggi-do; missionary returns."},{"t":"Education","lv":3,"p":[85,88],"pp":"67","s":"Seoul National University registration and opposition, teacher resignations, school construction shortages, the new 6-6 structure, core curriculum tables and textbook distribution."},{"t":"Culture, Athletics and Religion","lv":3,"p":[88,90],"pp":"70","s":"Chu-suk festival on 10 September; 4-H vote with 229,367 households in favor; Girl Scout leader school at Ewha; Olympic planning committee; 22 returned missionaries."},{"t":"Section 3. Public Information","lv":2,"p":[91,96],"pp":"73","s":"Campaigns on the Child Labor Ordinance and rice collection, film releases, the new Culture Hour radio program, suspension of three leftist newspapers, and press comment on food and political unity."},{"t":"Information Programs, Motion Pictures and Radio","lv":3,"p":[91,93],"pp":"73","s":"Child labor publicity, a black-market campaign, the Labor Information Party in Chungchong-namdo, eleven films released, and Culture Hour and Workers' Song broadcasts."},{"t":"Press and Public Opinion","lv":3,"p":[94,96],"pp":"76","s":"The Military Governor's 17 September free press statement, suspension of Chosun In Min-Po and two others, and press comment on the legislature, food, Wallace's resignation and Korean takeover."},{"t":"Map of Korea and end pages","lv":2,"p":[97,104],"pp":"79","s":"Map of Korea on page 97, followed by blank scans, a library due-date card and the back cover."}],"by":"claude-sonnet (Claude Code subagent)"},{"id":"J-13","ia":"summation-non-military-activities-japan-no-13-1946-10","s":"J","no":13,"d":"1946-10","d2":"","t":"Summation of Non-Military Activities in Japan, No. 13 (October 1946)","v":"","pg":128,"o":"Summation No. 13 covers non-military Occupation activities in Japan for October 1946, with statistical data mostly for September. The Diet completed its 114-day session on 12 October after passing the new Constitution (298-2 in the Peers, 342-5 in the Representatives), approved by the Privy Council on 29 October for promulgation on 3 November and effect on 3 May 1947. The Land Reform Bill passed on 11 October and MacArthur praised it. The report also covers the National Congress of Industrial Unions' 'October offensive' strikes, the Labor Relations Adjustment Act, a 20 percent rice ration increase, Bank of Japan note circulation of 70.6 billion yen, war indemnity cancellation and the capital levy, holding company liquidation plans, the Tokyo war crimes trial (French, Soviet and general-preparation phases), cholera eradication, reopening of Japanese history courses and the teacher screening. It also gives production series for coal, metals, textiles, power and communications, with the full constitutional text. Historians will find it useful for the political and legal transition and economic recovery indicators.","h":["On 6-7 October the Diet gave final approval to the new Constitution (Peers 298-2, Representatives 342-5); the Privy Council approved it 29 October, with promulgation set for 3 November and effect on 3 May 1947.","The 90th Diet, 20 June to 12 October, was the longest in Japanese history; it passed 94 other bills, raised members' pay from 230 to 1,750 yen a month, and received 878 petitions.","The Land Reform Bill passed 11 October: absentee landlords must sell all land, resident landlords keep one cho, and local commissions run purchases; MacArthur praised it on 12 October.","The NCIU 'October offensive' brought the widest strikes since the surrender (coal, press, radio, electric power); Yoshida denounced political general strikes on 15 October, and radio stations closed 5-25 October.","The basic rice ration rose about 20 percent from 1 November with a higher official rice price; imported food releases fell to their lowest since June and canned food distribution ended 31 October.","Bank of Japan notes reached 70,599,401,000 yen on 31 October and national debt 223 billion yen; Supplemental Budgets 1 and 2 totalled 9,744,028,337 yen, and war indemnity cancellation and capital levy laws advanced.","SCAP ordered Iva Toguri ('Tokyo Rose') released from Sugamo on 25 October; Philippine trials sentenced eight of ten defendants to death, and the Tokyo tribunal heard the French, Soviet and general-preparation phases.","Coal output was 1,742,400 tons in September and cotton cloth output doubled, while the Imperial Oil strike cut crude oil to 15,691 kiloliters; cholera was declared eradicated and 339,614 Japanese were repatriated."],"k":["New Constitution","Land Reform Bill","90th Diet","October offensive strikes","Labor Relations Adjustment Act","rice ration","war indemnity cancellation","holding companies","war crimes trials","Tokyo Rose","coal production","textile production","repatriation","cholera","Imperial Rescript on Education","purge directive"],"toc":[{"t":"Front matter","lv":1,"p":[1,4],"pp":"","s":"Cover for Summation No. 13 (October 1946), table of contents for Parts I-IV, and list of 89 charts, maps and graphs."},{"t":"Part I: General","lv":1,"p":[5,12],"pp":"1","s":"Digest of the month: Constitution passed, Social Democratic and labor politics, war crimes, September economic indicators, the rice ration increase, finance and social developments."},{"t":"Section 1. Organization under SCAP","lv":2,"p":[6,6],"pp":"3","s":"No change occurred in the non-military organization of General Headquarters, SCAP, during October."},{"t":"Section 2. Political Activities","lv":2,"p":[6,7],"pp":"3","s":"Diet gave final approval to the Constitution on 6-7 October; also the Social Democrats' convention, party merger talks, strikes, war crimes figures and the tribunal's tenth phase."},{"t":"Section 3. Economic Activities","lv":2,"p":[8,10],"pp":"7","s":"Land Reform Bill passed 11 October; coal at 1,742,400 tons; strikes; rice ration up 20 percent; note circulation of 70.6 billion yen; 466 reparations plants."},{"t":"Section 4. Social Activities","lv":2,"p":[11,12],"pp":"13","s":"Repatriation of 339,614 Japanese, penicillin licences, 17,000,000 children vaccinated, history courses reopened, teacher screening totals, Fire Prevention Week and the radio strike settlement."},{"t":"Part II: Political","lv":1,"p":[13,36],"pp":"17","s":"Government and politics including the Constitution's text, public safety, and legal affairs and war crimes, with the Tokyo tribunal's French, Soviet and general-preparation phases."},{"t":"Section 1. Government and Politics","lv":2,"p":[14,26],"pp":"19","s":"Constitution passed 298-2 and 342-5 with full text; longest Diet session; Social Democratic convention; strikes; Emperor's Aichi and Gifu tour; list of 877 purged officials."},{"t":"The Constitution: passage, amendments and Kanamori commentary","lv":3,"p":[14,15],"pp":"19","s":"Diet completed action 7 October; Peers added universal suffrage and civilian Cabinet clauses; Privy Council voted 29 October; Kanamori's commentary on popular sovereignty and the Emperor as symbol."},{"t":"Official translation of the Constitution of Japan","lv":3,"p":[16,20],"pp":"22","s":"Certified text of the Constitution's preamble and Chapters I-X, including Article 9 renouncing war and Article 15 guaranteeing universal adult suffrage."},{"t":"The Diet","lv":3,"p":[21,22],"pp":"33","s":"The 90th Session ran 20 June to 12 October and passed 94 bills; 878 petitions received; members' pay rose from 230 to 1,750 yen monthly."},{"t":"Political Parties","lv":3,"p":[22,23],"pp":"35","s":"Right wing won the Social Democrats' 28-30 September convention under Tetsu Katayama; 33 members formed the New People's Party; Liberal-Progressive and Progressive-Cooperative merger talks stalled."},{"t":"Labor and Politics","lv":3,"p":[23,24],"pp":"37","s":"Party reaction to the NCIU 'October Labor Offensive' strikes, and Yoshida's 15 October Osaka speech calling political general strikes an abuse of labor's rights."},{"t":"The Emperor and Local Government","lv":3,"p":[24,24],"pp":"38","s":"Emperor met MacArthur 17 October and toured Aichi and Gifu 21-26 October; a 72-member Local Government System Investigation Commission was appointed 11 October."},{"t":"The Purge","lv":3,"p":[24,25],"pp":"38","s":"Newspapers published the official alphabetical list of 877 top-ranking officials removed under the 4 January Purge Directive."},{"t":"Structural and Personnel Changes","lv":3,"p":[26,26],"pp":"42","s":"War Inquiry Commission abolished 30 September; Administrative System Investigation Board created under Yoshida; Saburo Ota named chief of the Yokosuka Liaison Office."},{"t":"Section 2. Public Safety and Intelligence","lv":2,"p":[27,28],"pp":"45","s":"NCIU strikes and daily five-minute blackouts, 41 Formosans on trial, 152,437 crimes in August, new police college, 62,661 prisoners and fire losses of 108,430,380 yen."},{"t":"Section 3. Legal and War Crimes","lv":2,"p":[29,36],"pp":"49","s":"Lese-majeste charges dropped 9 October; 19 implementing bills drafted; 1,595 occupation court cases; Tokyo Rose released; Philippine trials gave eight death sentences; tribunal phases on French and Soviet relations."},{"t":"Legal Affairs and Japanese Judicial Administration","lv":3,"p":[29,30],"pp":"49","s":"Trial of 41 Formosans before a mixed commission; lese-majeste dismissal praised by MacArthur; Provisional Legislative Investigating Committee ended 110 days' work with 19 draft bills."},{"t":"Military Occupation Courts; Apprehension and Investigation of War Criminals","lv":3,"p":[30,31],"pp":"50","s":"1,543 guilty verdicts in 1,595 occupation court cases; 817 internees in Sugamo; 754 held in the Philippines; Iva Toguri released."},{"t":"Prosecution of War Criminals (Philippines trials)","lv":3,"p":[31,31],"pp":"52","s":"247 cases in preparation in Japan; five Philippine trials of ten defendants ended with eight death sentences, plus five and 25 years; Negros civilian killings cases."},{"t":"International Prosecution: Eighth Phase (French Relations)","lv":3,"p":[31,33],"pp":"53","s":"Tribunal evidence on Japan's September 1940 entry into French Indo-China under ultimatum, the 1941 Vichy protocol and the 9 March 1945 takeover."},{"t":"International Prosecution: Ninth Phase (Soviet Relations)","lv":3,"p":[33,35],"pp":"56","s":"From 8 October the prosecution traced Japanese plans from the 1920 Siberian expedition to the Kwantung Army's 1,100,000 men; witnesses included Tatsugi, Kasahara, Kawabe, Tereshkin, Sejima and Matsumura."},{"t":"International Prosecution: Tenth Phase (General Preparations)","lv":3,"p":[35,36],"pp":"61","s":"Opened 21 October on industrial preparation for war and enforced cartelization; testimony of Liebert and documents on total control of industry."},{"t":"Part III: Economic","lv":1,"p":[37,113],"pp":"65","s":"September production and October policy data on agriculture, mining, industry, textiles, utilities, communications, labor, trade, rationing, finance and property control."},{"t":"Section 1. Agriculture and Fisheries","lv":2,"p":[38,42],"pp":"67","s":"Land Reform Bill terms and MacArthur's 12 October statement, 9,002,000-ton rice estimate, fertilizer plans, and September fish landings of 86,075 tons."},{"t":"Rural Land Reform","lv":3,"p":[38,40],"pp":"67","s":"Absentee landlords must sell; resident landlords keep one cho; commissions of tenants, landlords and owner-cultivators run purchases within two years; MacArthur praised the Bill on 12 October."},{"t":"Crop Production, Fertilizer and Mulberry Acreage","lv":3,"p":[40,41],"pp":"70","s":"Rice estimated at 9,002,000 metric tons, record sweet potatoes, falling wheat and barley estimates, phosphatic and potassic fertilizer distribution and preliminary mulberry acreage."},{"t":"Fisheries","lv":3,"p":[41,42],"pp":"73","s":"September landings up 6,707 tons to 86,075; coastal whaling took 431 whales in seven weeks; 40,518 kiloliters of fuel oil arranged for Antarctic whaling."},{"t":"Section 2. Forestry and Mining","lv":2,"p":[43,48],"pp":"77","s":"Log output of 485,360,000 board feet, charcoal at 57 percent of plan, coal at 1,742,400 tons, and Imperial Oil's strike cutting crude to 15,691 kiloliters."},{"t":"Forestry: Logs, Lumber, Plywood and Charcoal","lv":3,"p":[43,45],"pp":"77","s":"Highest log production since March; plywood fell to 14,068,000 square feet; first 200 tons of imported soybean-casein glue arrived; charcoal incentives for workers."},{"t":"Coal and Lignite","lv":3,"p":[45,47],"pp":"80","s":"Coal 1,742,400 tons (Kyushu 941,500); 1947-48 plan of 355,000 miners; stockpiles of 959,000 tons; lignite 231,755 tons; Coal Board 75,000,000 yen exploration program."},{"t":"Petroleum and Mining Industry","lv":3,"p":[48,48],"pp":"86","s":"Crude oil fell to 15,691 kiloliters after the 6 September Imperial Oil strike; copper near 1,500 tons, lead 416 tons, zinc 1,910 tons, gold 125 kilograms monthly."},{"t":"Section 3. Heavy Industries","lv":2,"p":[49,55],"pp":"89","s":"Coke 113,000 tons, aluminum 836 tons, cement 95,431 tons; rubber supply to run out by 1 January 1947; chemicals at 31 percent of minimum needs."},{"t":"Section 4. Manufacturing","lv":2,"p":[56,64],"pp":"103","s":"Wine output 21,466 hectoliters from 554 wineries, plus pulp and paper, glass, brick and tile, electrical goods, trucks, rubber, leather and agricultural equipment."},{"t":"Section 5. Textile Industries","lv":2,"p":[65,75],"pp":"121","s":"Cotton yarn up nearly 50 percent and cloth output doubled; 35 cotton cargoes totalling 649,871 bales shipped; raw silk 800 bales short of goal."},{"t":"Section 6. Transportation and Public Utilities","lv":2,"p":[76,78],"pp":"143","s":"105,806 motor vehicles; 2,271,341,000 kWh generated amid drought and rationing to industry; seven hydroelectric plants adding 30,030 kW; gas coal shortage."},{"t":"Section 7. Communications","lv":2,"p":[79,86],"pp":"149","s":"Radio strike closed stations 5-25 October; telephones up four percent; 35,000,000 of 200,000,000 postal savings records restored."},{"t":"Section 8. Labor","lv":2,"p":[87,92],"pp":"163","s":"Labor Relations Adjustment Act promulgated 12 October; 13,622 unions with 3,936,815 members; widest strikes since surrender; 123 arrests over indentured labor in Hokkaido."},{"t":"Labor Legislation and Organizations","lv":3,"p":[87,88],"pp":"163","s":"Labor Standards, unemployment insurance, workmen's compensation and seamen's bills; union statistics, seamen's union dissension and the Kanto Employers' Association conference."},{"t":"Labor Relations, Education, Wages and Employment","lv":3,"p":[89,93],"pp":"166","s":"Strike record of the NCIU offensive, wage incentive policy for coal, eight-hour day policy, employment exchanges expanded by 4,120 workers, and textile worker rice rations."},{"t":"Section 9. Imports and Exports","lv":2,"p":[93,94],"pp":"175","s":"Washington approved revised 1 July-31 December 1946 trade programs; first postwar tengujo paper shipment to the US; 9,187 tons of German potash received; salt diverted to Korea."},{"t":"Section 10. Rationing and Price Control","lv":2,"p":[95,102],"pp":"181","s":"Staple ration raised from 1 November with higher rice price; imported food releases lowest since June; rents rising; hoarded silk recovered."},{"t":"Food, Fertilizer and Chemicals","lv":3,"p":[95,99],"pp":"181","s":"Rice ration increase, imported canned food distribution ended 31 October, vegetable and fish prices, ammonium sulfate shipments, fertilizer and soda prices."},{"t":"Coal, Rents, Textiles and Consumer Goods","lv":3,"p":[99,101],"pp":"189","s":"Rents rising, textile distribution, 2,200 tons of imported salt, and cigarette monopoly sales of 240,000,000 'Peaces' monthly."},{"t":"Violations and Controls","lv":3,"p":[101,102],"pp":"193","s":"September violations fell below the 1946 monthly average; a special drive recovered hoarded foodstuffs, textiles and unreported silk."},{"t":"Section 11. Finance","lv":2,"p":[103,106],"pp":"197","s":"Bank of Japan notes of 70,599,401,000 yen; Bank of Tokyo authorized 11 October; national debt of 223 billion yen; supplementary budgets of 9.7 billion."},{"t":"Money and Banking","lv":3,"p":[103,105],"pp":"197","s":"Foreign exchange for Okinawan repatriates, currency circulation, Bank of Japan holdings of 47,890,000,000 yen in government bonds, and the Deposit Bureau losses bill."},{"t":"Public Finance","lv":3,"p":[105,106],"pp":"201","s":"National debt rose to 223,052,000,000 yen; Supplemental Budgets 1 and 2 with government pay raises; also local government finance and the Imperial Household."},{"t":"Section 12. Property Control and Reparations","lv":2,"p":[107,113],"pp":"205","s":"Allied property and deposits, patents, looted property and precious metals in SCAP custody, five holding companies' liquidation plans, and 466 reparations plants."},{"t":"Part IV: Social","lv":1,"p":[114,128],"pp":"219","s":"Public health and welfare, including repatriation and cholera eradication, plus education, religion and media, including reopened history courses."},{"t":"Section 1. Public Health and Welfare","lv":2,"p":[115,121],"pp":"221","s":"Welfare payments of 127,507,087 yen to 2,852,911 people; 339,614 repatriated; cholera eradicated; 17,000,000 children vaccinated against diphtheria; penicillin licences."},{"t":"Public Welfare and Repatriation","lv":3,"p":[115,116],"pp":"221","s":"339,614 Japanese repatriated 30 September-27 October and 38,117 foreign nationals left, mostly Ryukyuans; welfare aid and relief organization."},{"t":"Veterinary, Dental, Nursing and Medical Supply","lv":3,"p":[117,118],"pp":"224","s":"Dairy inspection, 938 dentists re-established, medical supply production, penicillin factory approval and narcotics control arrests."},{"t":"Preventive Medicine, Nutrition and Vital Statistics","lv":3,"p":[118,121],"pp":"227","s":"Last cholera cases in week ending 5 October; diphtheria immunization; venereal disease survey; Tokyo calorie intake up 35 percent; marriages up 39 percent."},{"t":"Section 2. Education, Religion and Media of Expression","lv":2,"p":[122,128],"pp":"235","s":"Imperial Rescript reading ended 8 October; history courses reopened by 15 October memorandum; 215,846 teachers screened; 1,250 new magazines noted."},{"t":"Education","lv":3,"p":[122,124],"pp":"235","s":"Rescript ceremonies abrogated; new history texts; teacher screening removed 1,126, dismissed 107 and reinstated 56; Education Reform Committee subcommittees and coeducation."},{"t":"Religion, Arts and Monuments","lv":3,"p":[124,125],"pp":"239","s":"Church of Christ in Japan adopted a constitution and creed; $25,000 pastor relief fund distributed; 1,261,000 yen allotted for cultural properties."},{"t":"Media of Expression","lv":3,"p":[125,128],"pp":"240","s":"Fire Prevention and Daily Life Security Law campaigns, 'Liberal Thinkers' radio series, 1,250 new magazines, press comment, films, theater pageant and libraries."}],"by":"claude-sonnet (Claude Code subagent)"},{"id":"K-13","ia":"summation-usamg-activities-korea-no-13-1946-10","s":"K","no":13,"d":"1946-10","d2":"","t":"Summation of United States Army Military Government Activities in Korea, No. 13 (October 1946)","v":"","pg":108,"o":"Summation No. 13 covers October 1946 in South Korea under the U.S. Army Military Government. The dominant events were the autumn strike wave and riots, centred on Taegu and the Kyongsang provinces, which ended in martial law, a Seoul curfew and three public statements by the Commanding General, USAFIK. Politically, the first elections since annexation chose 45 members of the Korean Interim Legislative Assembly (Ordinance No. 118), the Kim Kyu Sic-Lyuh Woon Hyeung Unification Committee issued seven principles, and a Socialist Labor Party formed. The report also records the settled railroad and marine strikes, the opening of rice collection (1 percent of quota by month end), new ordinances on patents, tariffs, grain mills, film licensing and Korean names, cholera totals of 15,481 cases, repatriation figures, and trade tables for imports from the U.S., China and Japan. Historians will find the official framing of the unrest, press comment, and detailed economic and labor statistics useful.","h":["Riots that began with the late-September railroad strike spread from Taegu to Seoul, Masan, Chinju, Kaesong and Inchon; at the Taegu stage 44 police were killed and 153 injured, 39 rioters killed and 148 injured, and martial law was invoked.","The Commanding General, USAFIK, issued public statements on 4, 14 and 23 October blaming outside agitators, and a 2200-0600 curfew for Koreans in the Seoul-Yongdong-po area began on 21 October.","Ordinance No. 118 created a 90-member Korean Interim Legislative Assembly, half elected; the first elections gave 15 seats to the Korean Democratic Party, 14 to the National Society, 2 to the Independence Party, 2 to the Cheju-do People's Committee and 12 to independents.","On 7 October the Kim Kyu Sic-Lyuh Woon Hyeung Unification Committee issued seven principles, including land redistribution, nationalization of key industries and reconvening the Joint Commission; the Military Governor announced a limited veto policy on 18 October.","The railroad strike was settled on 26 October (final agreement 12 October, average raise of 200 yen a month) and the Pusan marine strike on 17 October; gasoline shortages immobilized most motor transport.","Fall rice was estimated at 1,779,519 metric tons, 83 percent of the wartime average; by 31 October only 1 percent of the collection quota was in, against a 5 percent schedule, and the Seoul-Inchon ration rose to 277 grams.","October ordinances introduced a Patent Law (No. 91), a 10 percent ad valorem tariff ceiling (No. 116), licensing of grain mills (No. 113) and films (No. 115), and restoration of Korean names (No. 122).","Cholera reached 15,481 cases and 10,043 deaths by 30 October; repatriation had returned 860,534 Japanese and 918,143 Koreans, and the Inchon refugee camp, which shipped out 27,460 refugees, closed on 14 October."],"k":["USAMGIK","October 1946 riots","Taegu","Korean Interim Legislative Assembly","Unification Committee","Kim Kyu Sic","Lyuh Woon Hyeung","railroad strike","rice collection","cholera","repatriation","Ordinance No. 118","Maj. Gen. Hodge","price control","Korean Coast Guard"],"toc":[{"t":"Front matter","lv":1,"p":[1,14],"pp":"","s":"Library covers, title page for No. 13 (October 1946), table of contents of four parts, and list of 27 charts plus a map of Korea."},{"t":"Part I: General","lv":1,"p":[15,24],"pp":"1","s":"Monthly digest: Ordinance No. 118 and the election of the Legislative Assembly, the October riots, rice collection, railroad and marine strikes, repatriation, cholera and Hankul Day."},{"t":"Section 1. Political Activities","lv":2,"p":[17,18],"pp":"3","s":"Ordinances 114 and 118 and the election of 45 assembly members (31 right, 2 left, 12 independents); also the Unification Committee, the Socialist Labor Party, new ordinances and the October riots."},{"t":"Section 2. Economic Activities","lv":2,"p":[19,20],"pp":"5","s":"Fall crops 15 percent below normal, alcohol curtailment from 1 November, railroad strike ended 26 October, 311 foreign trade licenses, and Bank of Chosen notes at 12.2 billion yen."},{"t":"Section 3. Social Activities","lv":2,"p":[21,24],"pp":"7","s":"Repatriation totals, closure of the Inchon refugee camp, cholera at 15,481 cases, Seoul National University unrest, Hankul Day, the Korean Olympics and press focus on unification."},{"t":"Part II: Political","lv":1,"p":[25,42],"pp":"11","s":"Government and politics (Legislative Assembly, Unification Committee, nine-party committee) and legal and public safety matters, including the October riots and new ordinances."},{"t":"Section 1. Government and Politics","lv":2,"p":[27,34],"pp":"13","s":"Provincial reorganization, 90-member assembly, Military Governor's 18 October veto policy, Unification Committee principles, Joint Korean-American Conference, and Gen. Brown replacing Gen. Arnold."},{"t":"Administration","lv":3,"p":[27,28],"pp":"13","s":"Ordinance No. 114 abolishes provincial police departments; Ordinance No. 118 sets up the 90-member Assembly; Dr. Chyun Kyu Hong appointed secretary on 14 October."},{"t":"Political Affairs","lv":3,"p":[29,33],"pp":"15","s":"Election results by province, the seven-point Unification Committee declaration of 7 October, a nine-party left-wing committee, the Joint Conference agenda, and the Socialist Labor Party formed 14 October."},{"t":"Joint Commission and Foreign Affairs","lv":3,"p":[33,34],"pp":"27","s":"Maj. Gen. Albert E. Brown replaces Maj. Gen. A. V. Arnold as head of the U.S. delegation; 116 foreign nationals registered, including 41 stateless Tartars."},{"t":"Section 2. Legal and Public Safety","lv":2,"p":[35,42],"pp":"21","s":"Six bar admissions, price-violation trial rules, Ordinances 91, 113, 115, 116 and 122, riot chronology with casualties, police controversy, 19,407 prison inmates and Coast Guard seizures."},{"t":"Legal and Major Legislation","lv":3,"p":[35,37],"pp":"21","s":"Trial within 48 hours for price violators, Patent Law, 10 percent tariff ceiling, grain mill and film licensing, and restoration of Korean names by 23 December."},{"t":"Public Safety","lv":3,"p":[37,41],"pp":"23","s":"Strikes turned to riots, with martial law at Taegu and a Seoul curfew from 21 October; Gen. Hodge's statements, Military Governor on pro-Japanese police, and Coast Guard seizures of rice smugglers."},{"t":"Part III: Economic","lv":1,"p":[43,80],"pp":"29","s":"Natural resources (crops, fisheries, forestry, coal, minerals) and commerce and industry (production, transport, strikes, trade, rationing, prices, finance), with 25 charts."},{"t":"Section 1. Natural Resources","lv":2,"p":[45,58],"pp":"31","s":"Grain inspection rules, fall rice at 1,779,519 tons, 14,880 tons of October fish landings, fuel wood shortage, and September coal output of 25,579 tons."},{"t":"Agriculture","lv":3,"p":[45,49],"pp":"31","s":"Ordinance No. 111 requires inspection of 18 grain varieties; crop estimates, Agricultural Association franchise extension, and an animal census of 2,335,312."},{"t":"Fisheries","lv":3,"p":[49,51],"pp":"35","s":"14,880 tons landed in six provinces, 45 whales caught, 38,321 registered vessels and 338,238 fisheries workers; boats, nets and fuel in short supply."},{"t":"Forestry","lv":3,"p":[51,55],"pp":"37","s":"July lumber output of 690,000 board feet, 1946 charcoal at 69,000 tons, 55 trucks allocated for fuel wood, and 645 sawmills."},{"t":"Mining","lv":3,"p":[55,57],"pp":"41","s":"September coal 25,579 tons, problems at Hwasun and Kyongju mines, graphite 2,370 tons, plus kaolin, pyrophyllite and tungsten concentrates."},{"t":"Section 2. Commerce and Industry","lv":2,"p":[59,80],"pp":"45","s":"Industrial exhibition, alcohol curtailment, railroad and marine strikes, telephone and postal data, trade with the U.S., China and Japan, rice collection, rationing, price indexes and currency."},{"t":"Industry and Textiles","lv":3,"p":[59,61],"pp":"45","s":"National Industrial Exhibition opened 19 October, alcohol output curtailed from 1 November, Inchon fertilizer plant closed, 66,152 pounds of cocoons allocated to filatures."},{"t":"Transportation and Public Utilities","lv":3,"p":[61,62],"pp":"47","s":"Railroad strike ended 26 October, gasoline shortage halted motor transport, 4,614 ships registered, eight port districts planned, and 89 Seoul streetcars running."},{"t":"Communications","lv":3,"p":[63,64],"pp":"49","s":"Interzone mail resumed 12 October (119,919 pieces), telephone messages up to 214,035 in August, Mukden cable repaired, JODK power cut to 10 kilowatts."},{"t":"Labor","lv":3,"p":[64,67],"pp":"50","s":"Strikes in Seoul, Inchon and Kyongsang-pukto; railway settlement with a 200 yen average raise; Pusan marine strike settled 17 October; public works halted by gasoline shortage."},{"t":"Imports and Exports","lv":3,"p":[67,71],"pp":"53","s":"311 foreign trade licenses; U.S. imports of 16.8 million dollars to September; October imports from China of 68.5 million yen; Japanese import and export tables."},{"t":"Rationing and Price Control","lv":3,"p":[71,78],"pp":"57","s":"Rationed population 6,500,000, Seoul-Inchon ration of 277 grams, 1 percent of rice quota collected, cotton prices set 22 October, cost-of-living differential raised 250 yen, fines raised 50 times."},{"t":"Finance","lv":3,"p":[78,79],"pp":"64","s":"Bank of Chosen notes of 12,197,703,695 yen, up 856,396,695 yen in October; 26 million yen loan to the Water Utilization Association."},{"t":"Part IV: Social","lv":1,"p":[81,100],"pp":"67","s":"Public health and welfare, education, culture and religion, and public information: cholera, repatriation, school unrest, the Korean Olympics and press policy."},{"t":"Section 1. Public Health and Welfare","lv":2,"p":[83,90],"pp":"69","s":"Refugee flow falling from 28,728 to 3,761, repatriation totals, medical exams, vaccine stocks, UNRRA shipments, and cholera at 15,481 cases and 10,043 deaths."},{"t":"Public Welfare and Repatriation","lv":3,"p":[83,84],"pp":"69","s":"Kyongsang-pukto relief committee seeking 350 million yen, Seoul low-cost restaurants serving 456,966 meals, Inchon camp closed 14 October, last Chinese repatriation shipment."},{"t":"Hospital Administration and Nursing","lv":3,"p":[84,85],"pp":"70","s":"Refresher course for government physicians 14-19 October, 160 limited doctors examined, 17 nurses graduated and 230 took national nursing exams."},{"t":"Dental, Veterinary and Nutrition","lv":3,"p":[86,86],"pp":"72","s":"Seoul dentists' association formed, animal disease cases including 707 of fowlpest, and a book analyzing 414 Korean foods."},{"t":"Supply and Distribution","lv":3,"p":[86,88],"pp":"72","s":"Smallpox vaccine stock of 873,100 doses; first UNRRA shipment handed to the Soviets 12 October; UNRRA clothing and shoes allocated to five provinces."},{"t":"Preventive Medicine","lv":3,"p":[88,90],"pp":"74","s":"Autumn Cleanup Week from 6 October, cholera at 15,481 cases with highest incidence in the Kyongsang provinces, smallpox control delayed until cooler weather."},{"t":"Section 2. Education, Culture and Religion","lv":2,"p":[91,94],"pp":"77","s":"Seoul National University strikes and low faculty pay, Chosen Christian College made a university, 82 new middle schools, Hankul Day, 1946 Korean Olympics, nine missionaries arriving."},{"t":"Section 3. Public Information","lv":2,"p":[95,100],"pp":"81","s":"Department reorganized into five bureaus, rice and strike publicity, Ordinance No. 115 film licensing, JODK schedule, and Gen. Hodge's 11 October press-freedom statement."},{"t":"Information Programs, Motion Pictures and Radio","lv":3,"p":[95,96],"pp":"81","s":"Rice program film and pamphlet airdrops, newsreels of the Olympics, three Korean films reviewed, and JODK broadcasting schedule from 17 October."},{"t":"Press and Public Opinion","lv":3,"p":[96,99],"pp":"82","s":"Gen. Hodge's 11 October statement that there is no censorship; press reaction to the Assembly, unification, Rhee's demand to cancel the Moscow Decision, and the British Foreign Minister's remarks."},{"t":"Map of Korea and back matter","lv":1,"p":[101,108],"pp":"87","s":"Printed map of Korea (printed page 87), followed by blank pages, a library due-date slip and the back cover."}],"by":"claude-sonnet (Claude Code subagent)"},{"id":"J-14","ia":"summation-non-military-activities-japan-no-14-1946-11","s":"J","no":14,"d":"1946-11","d2":"","t":"Summation of Non-Military Activities in Japan, No. 14 (November 1946)","v":"","pg":155,"o":"Summation No. 14 covers non-military Occupation activities in Japan for November 1946 (with economic statistics mostly for October and some for September). The dominant political event is the 3 November promulgation of the new Constitution, effective 3 May 1947; the report reproduces the Emperor's rescript, party and press reactions and a Government Section analysis. It then traces the Cabinet's six fundamental policies, the extension of the purge to local officials and business leaders, the electrical workers' dispute and Social Democrat demands for dissolution, and the opening of the 91st Extraordinary Diet. Part II adds crime, amnesty, judicial and war crimes material, including the Tokyo tribunal's tenth and eleventh phases. Part III supplies detailed production, transport, communications, labor, trade, rationing, finance and property statistics, showing coal and power shortages, strikes and tentative export resumption. Part IV covers welfare, public health, education reform, religion and the media. Historians will find it useful for purge mechanics, constitutional commentary, labor politics and the state of the economy.","h":["The new Constitution was promulgated on 3 November 1946 before the Emperor, Cabinet and Diet, to take effect on 3 May 1947; more than 100,000 people rallied at the Imperial Palace grounds.","On 4 November the Cabinet issued six fundamental policies, including popularizing the Constitution, educational reform, local self-government and a labor solution; Social Democrats attacked them and demanded resignation or Diet dissolution.","On 8 November the 4 January purge directive was extended to prefectural, city, town and village officials, later to business and publishing leaders; about 170,000 were estimated removed, postponing the December local elections.","On 6 November the Government rejected the Central Labor Relations Committee's compromise in the electrical workers' dispute, which had proposed a 500 yen minimum wage, fueling opposition demands for dissolution.","The Emperor's rescript of 3 November granted amnesty, commutation or restored civil rights to 331,400 persons, including 93,900 under general pardon.","Coal shortages and a 12-day strike cut Imperial Oil's October crude output to 12,496 kiloliters, the lowest of the Occupation; coal output was 1,766,800 metric tons and gas capacity was 77.6 percent idle.","At the Tokyo trials the prosecution finished its tenth phase (war preparations) on 4 November and began the eleventh (US-British-Japanese relations); 223 more suspects were ordered apprehended and 793 held in Sugamo.","Bank of Japan notes reached 74,816,000,000 yen on 30 November and national debt 227,639,000,000 yen on 31 October; 40 more companies controlling 860 subsidiaries were designated for liquidation."],"k":["Constitution promulgation","purge extension","Yoshida Cabinet","electrical workers dispute","Social Democratic Party","91st Extraordinary Diet","war crimes trials","Imperial amnesty","coal shortage","labor unions","agrarian reform","holding company liquidation","rationing","public health","educational reform"],"toc":[{"t":"Front matter","lv":1,"p":[1,4],"pp":"","s":"Cover, title page, table of contents of the four parts, and list of 100 charts, maps and graphs for Summation No. 14 (November 1946)."},{"t":"Part I: General","lv":1,"p":[5,13],"pp":"1","s":"Monthly digest: no organizational change, Constitution promulgated 3 November, purge extended, electrical dispute, falling industrial output, 2,703,429 welfare recipients and education reform."},{"t":"Section 1. Organization Under SCAP","lv":2,"p":[6,6],"pp":"3","s":"One paragraph stating there was no change in the non-military organization of General Headquarters, SCAP, during November."},{"t":"Section 2. Political Activities","lv":2,"p":[6,8],"pp":"3","s":"Constitution promulgated 3 November, the Cabinet's six policies of 4 November, purge extension, electrical dispute, party reactions, the 91st Diet and war crimes trials."},{"t":"Section 3. Economic Activities","lv":2,"p":[9,11],"pp":"9","s":"Digest of agriculture, mining, industry, labor, trade, rationing, finance and property; Bank of Japan notes reached 74.8 billion yen and 40 more companies were slated for liquidation."},{"t":"Section 4. Social Activities","lv":2,"p":[12,13],"pp":"15","s":"Welfare spending of 144,192,802 yen, new Child Welfare Bureau, Ryukyuan repatriation, typhus, nine-year compulsory education recommendation, Shinto and funeral directives, and press comment on the Constitution."},{"t":"Part II: Political","lv":1,"p":[14,60],"pp":"19","s":"Government and politics, public safety, and legal and war crimes affairs, centered on the Constitution, the purge extension to local officials and business, and Tokyo tribunal proceedings."},{"t":"Section 1. Government and Politics","lv":2,"p":[15,43],"pp":"21","s":"Constitution text and analysis, six Cabinet policies, purge extension to local officials and business, electrical dispute, party maneuvers, local government plans, Diet composition and the Emperor's Ibaraki tour."},{"t":"The Constitution","lv":3,"p":[15,16],"pp":"21","s":"Emperor's rescript, Yoshida's radio address, MacArthur's eve-of-promulgation statement and party reactions; the Constitution takes effect 3 May 1947."},{"t":"Analysis of the Constitution","lv":3,"p":[17,24],"pp":"25","s":"Press digest, then a Government Section comparative analysis of the Emperor, Diet, Cabinet, judiciary, bill of rights and war renunciation, with enforcement and conclusion."},{"t":"Government Policy","lv":3,"p":[25,28],"pp":"35","s":"Text of the Cabinet's six fundamental policies of 4 November, with reaction of the parties and the press."},{"t":"The Purge","lv":3,"p":[29,39],"pp":"47","s":"On 8 November the 4 January directive was extended to local officials, with about 170,000 purgees estimated and elections postponed; later extended to business and publishing leaders."},{"t":"Labor and Politics","lv":3,"p":[40,40],"pp":"69","s":"On 6 November the Government rejected the Central Labor Relations Committee's settlement in the electrical workers' dispute, prompting demands for dissolution."},{"t":"Political Parties","lv":3,"p":[41,41],"pp":"70","s":"Social Democrats under Tetsu Katayama attacked the Cabinet; the Democratic League revived; Communist and Government party positions."},{"t":"Local Government","lv":3,"p":[42,42],"pp":"73","s":"Governors' conference with the Emperor, Home Minister's decentralization plan, postponed local elections, and proposed special city status for Osaka, Kyoto, Yokohama and Kobe."},{"t":"The Diet","lv":3,"p":[43,43],"pp":"75","s":"The 91st Extraordinary Diet convened 25 November for 30 days with five major bills; the Liberals held 148 seats."},{"t":"The Emperor and Structural Changes","lv":3,"p":[44,44],"pp":"76","s":"Emperor toured Ibaraki Prefecture on 17-18 November amid press reports of rising popularity; a new council was created."},{"t":"Section 2. Public Safety and Intelligence","lv":2,"p":[44,47],"pp":"77","s":"Orderly strikes, black-market raids with over 700 arrests, 173,776 offenses in September, new Metropolitan Police Juvenile Section, police reform proposal and amnesty of 331,400 persons."},{"t":"Section 3. Legal and War Crimes","lv":2,"p":[48,60],"pp":"85","s":"Legal reforms, Supreme Court independence, occupation court cases, war crimes work in Japan and the Philippines, and the Tokyo tribunal's tenth and eleventh phases."},{"t":"Legal Affairs and Japanese Judicial Administration","lv":3,"p":[48,49],"pp":"85","s":"Lawyers' Law revision, bar convention of 10 November, Japanese courts divested of civil claims against United Nations nationals, amnesty rescript and Chief Justice Hosono's statement."},{"t":"War Crimes Investigation and Trials","lv":3,"p":[50,50],"pp":"88","s":"Investigation of a massacre of 70 civilians in New Guinea; 223 persons ordered apprehended, 793 held in Sugamo, and 105 tried in Japan to date."},{"t":"International Prosecution of War Criminals","lv":3,"p":[51,60],"pp":"90","s":"Philippine and Australian trial results; at Tokyo the tenth phase (war preparations) ended 4 November and the eleventh (US-British-Japanese relations) began, with testimony of Ballantine and Richardson."},{"t":"Part III: Economic","lv":1,"p":[61,137],"pp":"111","s":"Twelve sections on agriculture, industry, utilities, labor, trade, rationing, finance and property control, showing output held down by coal and power shortages."},{"t":"Section 1. Agriculture and Fisheries","lv":2,"p":[62,64],"pp":"113","s":"Agrarian reform campaign with 630,000 posters and pamphlets, 1,002,000 tons of rice collected by 20 November, record sweet potato crop, October fish landings of 113,196 tons."},{"t":"Section 2. Forestry and Mining","lv":2,"p":[65,69],"pp":"119","s":"October log output of 448,088,000 board feet, coal output of 1,766,800 tons, coal stockpiles down to 882,000 tons and a nine-point miners' incentive program."},{"t":"Section 3. Heavy Industries","lv":2,"p":[70,77],"pp":"129","s":"Coke steady at 113,000 tons, falling steel output, 18,805 buildings constructed in October; shipbuilding, chemicals and machinery limited by coal and power."},{"t":"Section 4. Manufacturing","lv":2,"p":[78,88],"pp":"145","s":"Canning shifts to glass for lack of tin plate, pulp down 8 percent, record 231,729 syringes, 1,280 truck chassis, rubber output down 34.3 percent."},{"t":"Section 5. Textile Industries","lv":2,"p":[89,101],"pp":"165","s":"Cotton yarn reached 20,830,000 pounds, 3,000,000 short of goal; raw silk, rayon and wool held back by seasonal labor loss and power curtailment."},{"t":"Section 6. Transportation and Public Utilities","lv":2,"p":[102,106],"pp":"189","s":"107,845 motor vehicles, 15,948,849 train kilometers, power generation of 2,493,417,000 kWh amid drought, Shinosumi hydro plant opened 16 October, gas capacity 77.6 percent idle."},{"t":"Section 7. Communications","lv":2,"p":[107,112],"pp":"197","s":"946,283 telephones in service, facsimile telegraph authorized 14 November, new Tokyo-Sendai cable, and a Tokyo Shibaura strike cutting tube output."},{"t":"Section 8. Labor","lv":2,"p":[113,117],"pp":"209","s":"Union membership of 4,122,209 in 14,697 unions, federation merger talks, first labor-industry meeting 10 November, electric power dispute mediation and theater strikes."},{"t":"Section 9. Imports and Exports","lv":2,"p":[118,121],"pp":"219","s":"First postwar shipment of 250,000 yards of silk fabric to the United States, export samples for China and Korea, and over 600,000 of 890,000 scheduled cotton bales arrived."},{"t":"Section 10. Rationing and Price Control","lv":2,"p":[122,127],"pp":"229","s":"Government held 41 days of staple food on 31 October; rice collections well ahead of 1945, fish consumption down, charcoal and firewood prices raised, violations rising."},{"t":"Section 11. Finance","lv":2,"p":[128,131],"pp":"239","s":"Bank of Japan notes at 74,816,000,000 yen, financial institutions split into new and old accounts, central insurance corporations ordered dissolved, national debt 227,639,000,000 yen."},{"t":"Section 12. Property Control and Reparations","lv":2,"p":[132,137],"pp":"247","s":"SCAP Dollar Custody Account opened 4 November, six firms removed from restricted list, 40 more holding companies designated, 10 Zaibatsu families, plants held for reparations."},{"t":"Part IV: Social","lv":1,"p":[138,155],"pp":"257","s":"Public health and welfare, education, religion, arts and media of expression, ending with a map of Japan."},{"t":"Section 1. Public Health and Welfare","lv":2,"p":[140,148],"pp":"259","s":"Welfare aid to 2,703,429 persons, Child Welfare Bureau, Ryukyuan repatriation, penicillin price cut to 200 yen, narcotics registration, rising typhus and 190,727 births in October."},{"t":"Section 2. Education, Religion and Media of Expression","lv":2,"p":[149,154],"pp":"277","s":"Educational Reform Committee's nine-year coeducation proposal, Shinto fund-raising ban, public funeral prohibition, Ise-Shima National Park on 20 November, and press, radio and film comment."},{"t":"Map of Japan","lv":2,"p":[155,155],"pp":"287","s":"Closing page carrying the map of Japan."}],"by":"claude-sonnet (Claude Code subagent)"},{"id":"K-14","ia":"summation-usamg-activities-korea-no-14-1946-11","s":"J","no":14,"d":"1946-11","d2":"","t":"Summation of Non-Military Activities in Japan, No. 14 (November 1946)","v":"Second copy, misfiled as Korea No. 14","pg":310,"note":"This PDF was filed (and uploaded to archive.org) as the Korea Summation No. 14, but it is a Google/University of Virginia scan of the Japan Summation No. 14, November 1946. Korea No. 14 is not in this collection.","o":"This is the GHQ SCAP 'Summation of Non-Military Activities in Japan', No. 14, for November 1946 (the file is labelled as a Korea summation but its content is the Japan series). It reports on the promulgation of the new Constitution on 3 November, the Yoshida Cabinet's six 'fundamental policies', and the extension of the Purge to local officials and business leaders. Part II adds the Government Section's analysis of the Constitution, the electrical workers' dispute, party manoeuvres, crime statistics and the International Military Tribunal's tenth and eleventh prosecution phases. Part III gives monthly October figures on agriculture, fisheries, coal, metals, textiles, transport, communications, labor, trade, rationing, finance and property control, with many charts. Part IV covers welfare, public health, vital statistics, education reform, religion and the press. A historian will find it useful for the constitutional transition, purge scale estimates, and detailed production, price and ration statistics of the early Occupation.","h":["The new Constitution was promulgated on 3 November 1946 with the Emperor's rescript; over 100,000 people gathered at the Palace grounds, and it takes effect on 3 May 1947.","On 4 November the Cabinet announced six 'fundamental policies' (popularising the Constitution, educational reform, administrative reform, local self-government, industrial rehabilitation, labor problem), drawing partisan attacks led by the Social Democrats.","On 8 November the Government extended the 4 January Purge Directive to prefectural, city, town and village officials; the press estimated about 1,000,000 screened and 170,000 removed, and December local elections were postponed.","On 6 November the Government rejected the Central Labor Relations Committee's compromise in the electrical workers' dispute, prompting labor and Social Democrats to demand Cabinet resignation or Diet dissolution.","The 91st Extraordinary Diet convened on 25 November for 30 days to take up the House of Councillors election, Diet, Cabinet, Imperial House and Imperial House Economy bills.","The Emperor granted amnesty to 331,400 prisoners on 3 November; 173,776 offenses were reported in September, up 21,339 on August, and 29,021 persons awaited trial on 30 September.","The Tokyo war crimes tribunal prosecution closed its eleventh phase (US-British-Japanese relations, Pearl Harbor) on 26 November, completing the substance of its case against the 27 major defendants.","October coal output reached 1,756,800 metric tons, only one percent above September, while labor unrest and power shortages held industry down; Bank of Japan notes in circulation hit 74,816,000,000 yen on 30 November."],"k":["Japan Summation","November 1946","SCAP","New Constitution","Purge","Yoshida Cabinet","Electrical workers' dispute","International Military Tribunal","Agrarian reform","Coal production","Textiles","Labor unions","Rationing","Bank of Japan notes","Repatriation","Educational reform"],"toc":[{"t":"Front matter","lv":1,"p":[1,16],"pp":"","s":"Blank Google Books pages, cover of Summation No. 14 (November 1946), University of Virginia library stamp, table of contents and list of 100 charts, maps and graphs."},{"t":"Part I: General","lv":1,"p":[17,34],"pp":"1","s":"Digest of the month: no change in SCAP organization, Constitution promulgated 3 November, Purge extended to local officials, 91st Diet opened 25 November, October economic indicators, welfare and education developments."},{"t":"Section 1. Organization Under SCAP","lv":2,"p":[19,19],"pp":"3","s":"No change occurred in the non-military organization of General Headquarters, SCAP, during November."},{"t":"Section 2. Political Activities","lv":2,"p":[19,24],"pp":"3","s":"Constitution promulgated 3 November, six-point Cabinet policy, Purge extended to local officials, electrical workers' dispute, party attacks, 91st Diet opening 25 November, amnesty for 331,400 prisoners and war crimes trials."},{"t":"Section 3. Economic Activities","lv":2,"p":[25,30],"pp":"9","s":"October output: coal up, oil lowest of the Occupation, cotton yarn 20,840,000 pounds, notes in circulation 74.8 billion yen, 40 more companies designated for liquidation."},{"t":"Section 4. Social Activities","lv":2,"p":[31,34],"pp":"15","s":"Welfare aid to 2,703,439 persons, Child Welfare Bureau, nationwide school lunches, education reform committee's nine-year schooling proposal, Ise Shima National Park and press comment."},{"t":"Part II: Political","lv":1,"p":[35,126],"pp":"19","s":"Constitution and its analysis, Purge texts, labor and party politics, crime and prisons (173,776 September offenses), legal affairs, and the Tokyo tribunal's tenth and eleventh prosecution phases."},{"t":"Section 1. Government and Politics","lv":2,"p":[37,92],"pp":"21","s":"Promulgation ceremonies, Government Section's analysis of the Constitution, the Cabinet's six policies, text and scope of the extended Purge, labor politics, parties, local government, the Diet and the Emperor."},{"t":"The Constitution","lv":3,"p":[37,40],"pp":"21","s":"Ceremonies of 3 November in the House of Peers, MacArthur's statement, and reactions from the Liberal, Social Democratic and Communist parties and the press."},{"t":"Analysis of the Constitution","lv":3,"p":[41,55],"pp":"","s":"Government Section analysis of the new Constitution: popular sovereignty, a symbolic Emperor, Diet supremacy with a weaker House of Councillors, 10-year review of Supreme Court judges, rights guarantees, and effect from 3 May 1947."},{"t":"Government Policy","lv":3,"p":[56,63],"pp":"","s":"Text of the Cabinet's 4 November statement of six 'fundamental policies' and the partisan and press reaction to it."},{"t":"The Purge","lv":3,"p":[64,84],"pp":"","s":"8 November extension of the Purge Directive to local officials, screening criteria and procedure, estimated purgees, postponed local elections and the plan to purge business leaders."},{"t":"Labor and Politics","lv":3,"p":[85,86],"pp":"","s":"Government rejected the electrical workers' compromise on 6 November; demands followed for Diet dissolution, and the National Congress of Industrial Unions made new labor moves."},{"t":"Political Parties","lv":3,"p":[87,88],"pp":"","s":"Social Democrats (Katayama) opposed the Government outright; the Democratic League revived; Co-operative Democrats, Liberals and Progressives took their own positions."},{"t":"Local Government","lv":3,"p":[89,90],"pp":"","s":"Conference of prefectural governors in Tokyo on 30 October, with discussion of decentralization, local elections and the status of large cities."},{"t":"The Diet, the Emperor, Structural and Personnel Changes","lv":3,"p":[91,92],"pp":"","s":"91st Extraordinary Diet convened 25 November and its composition, the Emperor's inspection trip and popularity, and a newly created council."},{"t":"Section 2. Public Safety and Intelligence","lv":2,"p":[93,100],"pp":"77","s":"Orderly strikes, over 700 black-market arrests in two weeks, 173,776 September offenses, 29,021 persons awaiting trial, the amnesty rescript and fire losses."},{"t":"Section 3. Legal and War Crimes","lv":2,"p":[101,126],"pp":"85","s":"Legal and judicial reform, occupation courts, war crimes investigation and trials, and the Tokyo tribunal's tenth and eleventh phases of prosecution."},{"t":"Legal Affairs and Japanese Judicial Administration","lv":3,"p":[101,102],"pp":"85","s":"Courts left to define strikes, Fulbright Law not applicable, 10 November bar convention, Lawyers' Law revision, and a 30 October directive removing court jurisdiction over claims against Allied nationals."},{"t":"Military Occupation Courts","lv":3,"p":[103,103],"pp":"87","s":"Sendai and Kyoto provost court cases, five Allied seamen caught selling Occupation property at Enoshima, and 1,183 cases tried between 21 September and 20 October."},{"t":"Investigation, Apprehension and Prosecution of War Criminals","lv":3,"p":[104,106],"pp":"88","s":"Allied divisions investigated atrocities; 223 suspects were ordered apprehended; trials in Japan and the Philippines included the Ohugi, Nanjo and Watanabe cases."},{"t":"International Prosecution: Synopsis and Tenth Phase","lv":3,"p":[107,111],"pp":"91","s":"On 1 November the prosecution completed the general preparations for war phase: military and naval preparations, illegal fortifications and the testimony of Horiba."},{"t":"International Prosecution: Eleventh Phase","lv":3,"p":[112,126],"pp":"","s":"US-British-Japanese relations phase, 4 to 26 November, using State Department and Navy documents and testimony of Shirao, Tateno, Ballantine and Richardson on Pearl Harbor."},{"t":"Part III: Economic","lv":1,"p":[127,272],"pp":"111","s":"Monthly production, trade, price and financial statistics, mostly for October 1946: coal 1,756,800 tons, union membership 4,122,209, notes of 74,816,000,000 yen, across twelve sections."},{"t":"Section 1. Agriculture and Fisheries","lv":2,"p":[129,134],"pp":"113","s":"Agrarian reform ordinances of 22 November with 630,000 posters and pamphlets, crop prospects, October fish landings of 113,196 tons and the antarctic whaling fleet's departure."},{"t":"Section 2. Forestry and Mining","lv":2,"p":[135,144],"pp":"119","s":"Slight decline in log and lumber output, October coal production of 1,756,800 tons, lignite, the lowest oil output of the Occupation, and metal mining."},{"t":"Section 3. Heavy Industries","lv":2,"p":[145,160],"pp":"129","s":"Coke steady at 113,000 tons; iron and steel falling; nonferrous metals, rubber, cement, housing and chemicals affected by power shortage; machinery output tied to coal and power."},{"t":"Section 4. Manufacturing","lv":2,"p":[161,180],"pp":"145","s":"Canning shifted to glass because of tin plate shortage (glass pack 284 percent of July), pulp down 5.6 percent, plus medical supplies, electrical goods, vehicles, leather and sewing machines."},{"t":"Section 5. Textile Industries","lv":2,"p":[181,204],"pp":"165","s":"October cotton yarn 20,840,000 pounds and raw silk up 6.5 percent, with rayon, worsted and woolen output, held back by harvest labor loss and power curtailment."},{"t":"Section 6. Transportation and Public Utilities","lv":2,"p":[205,212],"pp":"189","s":"Motor vehicles 107,845, train kilometers up to 15,948,849, rail tonnage, coastwise shipping, power rationing, Shinosomi hydro plant opened 16 October, and gas capacity 77.6 percent idle."},{"t":"Section 7. Communications","lv":2,"p":[213,224],"pp":"197","s":"Rising telephones, toll calls and radio licenses; facsimile telegraph authorised; Tokyo-Sendai cable; postal construction; equipment output cut by strikes and a 27,000,000 yen transfer."},{"t":"Section 8. Labor","lv":2,"p":[225,234],"pp":"209","s":"Union membership 4,122,209 in 14,697 unions; Labor Standards, Unemployment Insurance and Seamen's bills; labor relations committees; disputes in theaters and Tokyo-Shibaura."},{"t":"Section 9. Imports and Exports","lv":2,"p":[235,242],"pp":"219","s":"Export tables for 1 January to 31 October 1946, first postwar silk fabric and vegetable wax shipments to the United States, and sample shipments to China and Hongkong."},{"t":"Section 10. Rationing and Price Control","lv":2,"p":[243,254],"pp":"229","s":"41 days' staple food supply on 31 October, rice collection, fertilizer, charcoal and firewood prices doubled from 30 November, rents, textiles, and 17 percent more violations."},{"t":"Section 11. Finance","lv":2,"p":[255,262],"pp":"239","s":"Bank of Japan notes of 74,816,000,000 yen on 30 November, financial institution accounts, national debt of 227,639,000,000 yen, and a plan to dissolve two insurance corporations."},{"t":"Section 12. Property Control and Reparations","lv":2,"p":[263,272],"pp":"247","s":"Allied property seized in Japan, SCAP Dollar Custody Account opened 4 November, looted property, six firms removed from the restricted list and 40 companies designated for liquidation."},{"t":"Part IV: Social","lv":1,"p":[273,310],"pp":"257","s":"Welfare payments to 2,703,439 persons, public health, vital statistics, nine-year schooling proposal, religion, cultural monuments, and the press and radio."},{"t":"Section 1. Public Health and Welfare","lv":2,"p":[275,292],"pp":"259","s":"Welfare payments to 2,703,439 persons, Ministry of Welfare reorganized into 11 bureaus, repatriation, penicillin price cut to 200 yen, typhus, narcotics registration and October births of 190,727."},{"t":"Section 2. Education, Religion and Media of Expression","lv":2,"p":[293,310],"pp":"277","s":"Educational Reform Committee's nine-year compulsory schooling, civic education manuals, ban on public funerals for war dead, Ise Shima National Park, cultural sites and press comment."}],"by":"claude-sonnet (Claude Code subagent)"},{"id":"J-15","ia":"summation-non-military-activities-japan-no-15-1946-12","s":"J","no":15,"d":"1946-12","d2":"","t":"Summation of Non-Military Activities in Japan, No. 15 (December 1946)","v":"","pg":134,"o":"Summation No. 15 covers December 1946 (with November production and trade statistics). Politically, the Diet's 91st extraordinary session passed the Cabinet Law, Imperial House Law, Imperial House Economy Law and House of Councillors Election Law, while the Social Democrats' 17 December dissolution resolution against the Yoshida Cabinet failed amid a 200,000-strong rally. The 21 December Nankai earthquake and tidal wave devastated Wakayama, Kochi and Shikoku. The Tokyo war crimes tribunal heard the B and C offenses, Japan-Netherlands and Philippines phases. Economically, coal output recovered, electric power was rationed, new coal prices and bonuses were set, Bank of Japan notes passed 93 billion yen, land reform machinery was created, and labor federations pressed for the cabinet's overthrow. Repatriation agreements with the Soviet Union were signed. A historian will find detailed production tables, union and dispute statistics, trade lists, and press comment.","h":["On 17 December more than 200,000 workers rallied before the Imperial Palace against the Yoshida Cabinet, and the Social Democrats' resolution for Diet dissolution was defeated 160 against after five hours of debate, with 69 members abstaining.","The 91st extraordinary Diet (26 November-25 December) enacted the Cabinet Law, Imperial House Law, Imperial House Economy Law and a House of Councillors Election Law creating a 250-seat upper house (150 prefectural, 100 national); the Diet Law failed in the House of Peers.","The 21 December earthquake and tidal wave, centered off Wakayama, caused more than 3,500 casualties, destroyed thousands of homes and left over 90,000 homeless, with Shingu City nearly destroyed by fire.","On 24 December the Cabinet issued the ordinance implementing the Land Reform Law (transfer within two years) and created the Central Agricultural Land Commission; 9,851 village land commissions were elected by early returns.","SCAP and the Soviet representative signed a repatriation agreement on 19 December providing for return of Japanese at 50,000 per month and of Koreans in Japan to north of the 38th parallel; about 25,000 were already removed under the 26 November interim accord.","New coal prices took effect 1 November (consumer price 401.70 yen per ton, 168 percent higher) with output bonuses of 35-60 yen per ton; November coal output was 2,022,400 tons, 231,000 above October.","Bank of Japan notes in circulation reached 93,397,000,000 yen on 31 December, and the Diet passed supplemental budgets of 17,217,097,000 yen plus an Increased Income Tax Law expected to yield 4 billion yen.","Labor: 15,172 unions with 4,168,305 members were registered by 31 October; a 14 December ordinance dissolved the Tokyo Autonomous Labor Federation and barred wartime labor-front leaders; the electric-power dispute settled without a strike."],"k":["December 1946","Yoshida Cabinet","Diet dissolution resolution","House of Councillors Election Law","Imperial House Economy Law","Nankai earthquake","war crimes tribunal","land reform","coal production","electric power shortage","labor unions","repatriation","Bank of Japan notes","reparations","penicillin"],"toc":[{"t":"Front matter","lv":1,"p":[1,5],"pp":"","s":"Cover, title page, table of contents for Parts I-IV, and the list of 101 charts, maps and graphs for Summation No. 15, December 1946."},{"t":"Part I: General","lv":1,"p":[6,13],"pp":"1","s":"Digest of the month: no change in SCAP organization, Diet and party politics, the earthquake, war crimes trials, production and labor, finance, repatriation, public health and press comment."},{"t":"Section 1. Organization under SCAP","lv":2,"p":[7,7],"pp":"3","s":"Reports no change in the non-military organization of General Headquarters, SCAP, during December 1946."},{"t":"Section 2. Political Activities","lv":2,"p":[7,9],"pp":"3","s":"The 91st Diet passed four implementing laws, and the 17 December dissolution resolution was defeated. Also covers the Korean illegal entry order, war crimes actions, crime, the 21 December earthquake and tribunal phases."},{"t":"Section 3. Economic Activities","lv":2,"p":[9,11],"pp":"7","s":"Digest of crops, whaling, coal and oil output, textiles, the power shortage, the 14 December labor ordinance, trade, rice collections, 93.4 billion yen note circulation, holding companies and reparations."},{"t":"Section 4. Social Activities","lv":2,"p":[12,13],"pp":"13","s":"Digest of 50 percent higher relief allowances, 25,000 Soviet-area repatriates, penicillin associations, falling dysentery, school lunches, women's meetings, radio series and press comment on the Yoshida Cabinet."},{"t":"Part II: Political","lv":1,"p":[14,34],"pp":"17","s":"The Diet's constitutional implementing laws, the opposition offensive against Yoshida, mass demonstrations and the Shikoku earthquake, and war crimes investigations and the international tribunal's three phases."},{"t":"Section 1. Government and Politics","lv":2,"p":[15,20],"pp":"19","s":"The Diet passed the Cabinet, Imperial House, Imperial House Economy and Councillors Election laws; the Diet Law failed. The Social Democrats' dissolution resolution lost. Also Communist role, labor campaign and Education Ministry reorganization."},{"t":"The Diet","lv":3,"p":[15,18],"pp":"19","s":"Four enacted laws, including the 250-member House of Councillors and Imperial Household property rules, and the Diet Law that failed over Article 84 joint conferences."},{"t":"Political Parties","lv":3,"p":[18,20],"pp":"24","s":"Social Democratic left-right split over tactics; the 17 December dissolution resolution lost 160 against with 69 abstaining; government party reaction, coalition talk and Co-operative Democrat merger negotiations."},{"t":"Labor and Politics; Structural and Personnel Changes","lv":3,"p":[20,20],"pp":"28","s":"Labor groups formed the National Movement for the Overthrow of the Cabinet on 24 December; the Education Ministry gained a Research Bureau; two advisory councils to the Finance Minister were created."},{"t":"Section 2. Public Safety","lv":2,"p":[21,25],"pp":"31","s":"Mass demonstrations including 200,000 at the Palace on 17 December, black-market and poison-liquor arrests, police reforms, a record prison population of 70,753, fires, and the 21 December earthquake toll."},{"t":"Law and Order","lv":3,"p":[21,22],"pp":"31","s":"Violence at the Tokyo Timepiece plant, a Korean demonstration at Yoshida's residence on 20 December, a sugar smuggling seizure, methyl-alcohol whiskey bootleggers and a narcotics ring broken."},{"t":"Police","lv":3,"p":[22,23],"pp":"32","s":"5 million yen for police communications, 600 prosecutors' assistants, year-end emergency police boxes, planned nationwide fingerprinting, October offenses down 9.5 percent, and juvenile crime incidents."},{"t":"Prisons","lv":3,"p":[23,24],"pp":"33","s":"Prison officials' school to open 13 January, clothing shortage, November prison deaths of 144 versus 635 a year earlier, population of 70,753, and 3,449 juveniles in correction houses."},{"t":"Fire and Public Casualties","lv":3,"p":[24,25],"pp":"36","s":"Shingu City burned after the quake, a Zushi repatriation center fire killed two, railway crowding casualties, 165 million yen of safety signs, a left-side traffic rule, and the earthquake damage table."},{"t":"Section 3. Legal and War Crimes","lv":2,"p":[25,34],"pp":"39","s":"Illegal Korean entry order, provost court caseload of 1,315, atrocity investigations (1,105 cases on hand), Sugamo internments totaling 834, and tribunal testimony on POW camps, Java and the Philippines."},{"t":"Legal, Occupation Courts and War Crimes Investigation, Apprehension and Prosecution","lv":3,"p":[25,27],"pp":"38","s":"1,315 provost court cases; 12 names deleted and 28 released from Sugamo; two trials in Japan with three convictions including Tsuda's life sentence; Philippine detainee figures of 693."},{"t":"International Prosecution of War Criminals: First B and C Offenses Phase","lv":3,"p":[27,29],"pp":"43","s":"Australian officers Coates and Blackburn testified on Burma-Siam railway camps, 55-Kilo camp ulcers and amputations, the Cycle Camp obedience form, and treatment in Formosa and Manchuria camps."},{"t":"International Prosecution of War Criminals: Japan-Netherlands Phase","lv":3,"p":[29,31],"pp":"46","s":"Documents on Japanese demands on the Netherlands East Indies in 1940, a Balikpapan massacre affidavit, and De Weerd's testimony on the occupation of Java, Putera and the Soekarno independence committee."},{"t":"International Prosecution of War Criminals: Philippines B and C Offenses Phase","lv":3,"p":[31,34],"pp":"51","s":"From 10 December: War Department casualty totals, testimony on the Bataan death march, Camp O'Donnell, Manila Hotel rapes, St. Paul's College and Lippa massacres, and US-Japan notes on prisoner treatment."},{"t":"Part III: Economic","lv":1,"p":[35,120],"pp":"59","s":"Twelve sections on agriculture, mining, industry, textiles, utilities, labor, trade, rationing, finance and property control, mostly on November data: coal output 2,022,400 tons, 15,172 unions, notes at 93.4 billion yen."},{"t":"Section 1. Agriculture and Fisheries","lv":2,"p":[36,42],"pp":"61","s":"Rice estimate raised to 9,150,000 tons, record sweet potatoes, 24 December land reform ordinance with 9,851 land commissions elected, November fish catch 156,655 tons, Antarctic whaling, 151 steel fishing vessels authorized."},{"t":"Agriculture","lv":3,"p":[36,37],"pp":"61","s":"Crop estimates, milk output at 30 percent of prewar average, feed imports down to 12,586 tons, a livestock census, and a Central Agricultural Land Commission of 23 commissioners."},{"t":"Fisheries","lv":3,"p":[37,42],"pp":"63","s":"Fish landings, whaling fleets arriving 15 and 24 December, agar-agar and liver oil forecasts, fishing vessel construction, December petroleum allocations and extra rice rations for boat crews."},{"t":"Section 2. Forestry and Mining","lv":2,"p":[42,50],"pp":"69","s":"November logs 415 million board feet, a charcoal rice incentive, coal output up to 2,022,400 tons, 1.9 billion yen coal rehabilitation loans, oil at 18,326 kiloliters, and October mineral gains."},{"t":"Forestry","lv":3,"p":[42,45],"pp":"69","s":"Log production fell 32.8 million board feet, pulpwood rose 22 percent, charcoal reached 89,320 tons, firewood stayed well below plan, and full-time charcoal workers received added rice rations."},{"t":"Mining","lv":3,"p":[45,50],"pp":"72","s":"Coal production, consumption and 887,000-ton stockpiles, lignite decline, power restored to mines, a petroleum quota of 226,320 kiloliters, drilling results and October metal output."},{"t":"Section 3. Heavy Industries","lv":2,"p":[50,58],"pp":"81","s":"Coke 110,000 tons, aluminum 834 tons, copper and zinc output, rubber stocks down 24 percent, cement 63,885 tons, 20,995 buildings completed, ship repairs, chemical output at 34 percent of need."},{"t":"Coke and Metal Industries; Rubber and Petroleum","lv":3,"p":[50,54],"pp":"81","s":"Coke allocations, iron and steel limited by coal, light metals, 3,725 tons blister copper, zinc and lead, 428 tons ferroalloys, rubber inventory and 828 kiloliters daily crude runs."},{"t":"Cement, Construction and Shipbuilding","lv":3,"p":[54,55],"pp":"88","s":"Cement output, housing completions, a road budget of 834.7 million yen, 79.6 million yen of railway additions for Occupation Forces, repairs to 293 vessels and 151 new fishing vessels authorized."},{"t":"Chemical Industries and Machinery","lv":3,"p":[55,58],"pp":"90","s":"Ammonium sulfate 46,700 tons, sulfuric acid at a postwar peak of 103,499 tons, salt output halved, machine tools up 13 percent, industrial machinery 278 million yen, textile machinery mostly repairs."},{"t":"Section 4. Manufacturing","lv":2,"p":[58,67],"pp":"97","s":"Plan for 50 model rural factories, shrinking raw stocks in food processing, and output of pulp and paper, glass, optical goods, electrical equipment, truck chassis, bicycles, leather and farm implements."},{"t":"Section 5. Textile Industries","lv":2,"p":[68,79],"pp":"117","s":"Cotton yarn up to 23,664,000 pounds, raw silk reeling 10,061 bales, rayon filament yarn below 1,000,000 pounds, wool gains, power cuts up to 50 percent, plus knit goods and dyeing."},{"t":"Cotton","lv":3,"p":[68,70],"pp":"117","s":"Yarn production 88 percent of schedule, power cuts costing 1,000,000 pounds, cloth 44,038,000 square yards, and 649,858 bales of American cotton arrived."},{"t":"Silk","lv":3,"p":[70,71],"pp":"120","s":"Raw silk 10,061 bales, 239 of 284 filatures operating, power use down 54 percent, cocoon stocks 148 million pounds, silk fabric 3,302,000 square yards."},{"t":"Rayon","lv":3,"p":[71,73],"pp":"123","s":"Pulp receipts rose to 1,907 tons; filament yarn fell to 904,000 pounds, staple 1,689,000 pounds, spun yarn 735,000 pounds; Shikoku mills were short of coal and pulp."},{"t":"Wool, Hard and East Fibers, and Miscellaneous","lv":3,"p":[73,76],"pp":"126","s":"Woolen yarn 2,062,000 pounds and worsted 842,000, flax and jute cut by power limits, throstle-spun yarn 534,000 pounds, and mixed fiber experiments."},{"t":"Knit Goods, Sewing Goods, Sundry Goods, Dyeing and Finishing","lv":3,"p":[76,79],"pp":"132","s":"Knit underwear and gloves rose, deliveries doubled after the 1 November price rise, clothing stocks piled up in mills, thread output fell, and 23,750,102 square yards were dyed or finished."},{"t":"Section 6. Transportation and Public Utilities","lv":2,"p":[79,82],"pp":"139","s":"Coal shortage cut train-kilometers to 13.9 million and freight to 8,504,100 tons; hydro shortfall forced power limits up to 30 percent; 2,329,000,000 kilowatt hours generated; gas plants at 24.9 percent capacity."},{"t":"Section 7. Communications","lv":2,"p":[83,88],"pp":"147","s":"Telephones reached 957,253, Tokyo-Osaka facsimile telegraph opened 22 December, Osaka phone rehabilitation finished, equipment output recovered after the Tokyo Shibaura strike, and broadcasting employed 7,018."},{"t":"Section 8. Labor","lv":2,"p":[89,97],"pp":"159","s":"14 December ordinance against wartime labor-front leaders, 15,172 unions, drive against the Yoshida Cabinet, Economic Reconstruction Conference, 1,501 collective agreements, winter allowances and lagging public works."},{"t":"Labor Legislation","lv":3,"p":[89,90],"pp":"159","s":"The ordinance dissolving the Tokyo Autonomous Labor Federation affects over 10,000 persons; the Labor Standards Bill was deferred; unemployment and workmen's compensation insurance bills advanced."},{"t":"Labor and Employer Organizations","lv":3,"p":[90,92],"pp":"160","s":"Union statistics by industry, 17 December joint demonstrations, Kyushu federation, coal miners' unification of 312,441 members, employer federations, and the 6 December Economic Reconstruction Conference."},{"t":"Labor Relations","lv":3,"p":[92,94],"pp":"164","s":"Labor Relations Committees mediated electric-power and Tokyo Express disputes; 1,501 collective agreements; Tokyo metropolitan workers' contract for 51,000; Ordinance 591; 1,744,426 workers in disputes."},{"t":"Labor Education; Wages and Working Conditions","lv":3,"p":[94,96],"pp":"168","s":"36 pamphlets issued, labor schools held, family expenditure rising to 951.60 yen, Wage Investigation Commission approved 7 December, and a government winter allowance of two months' pay from 27 December."},{"t":"Public Works, Employment, Occupation Labor and Administration","lv":3,"p":[96,97],"pp":"170","s":"Only 200,000 of 2,900,000 scheduled public works jobs were filled, 9,639 were in vocational training, 180,000 daily Occupation workers were paid 1.126 billion yen, and former police were excluded from labor posts."},{"t":"Section 9. Imports and Exports","lv":2,"p":[98,102],"pp":"175","s":"New Boeki Cho branches, 150,000,000 square yards of textiles allocated to Asian colonies, first raw fur shipment to the US, 400,000 tons of Red Sea salt, tea and equipment shipments to Korea and China."},{"t":"Section 10. Rationing and Price Control","lv":2,"p":[102,109],"pp":"183","s":"52.1 days of staple food held, rice collections at 36.7 percent of quota, 2 December food price increases, new coal price of 401.70 yen, 993,800 barrels of US petroleum, 89,524 violations."},{"t":"Section 11. Finance","lv":2,"p":[110,114],"pp":"199","s":"Foreign currency surrender to the Bank of Japan, notes at 93,397,000,000 yen, 17.2 billion yen supplemental budget, 9.3 billion yen bond issue, 240 billion yen national debt, Bank of Tokyo inaugural meeting 16 December."},{"t":"Money and Banking","lv":3,"p":[110,112],"pp":"199","s":"Foreign exchange surrender order of 29 November, Bank of Japan statement totaling 96,446 million yen, bank new and old accounts, postal savings of 50,262 million yen in 199 million accounts."},{"t":"Liquidation","lv":3,"p":[112,112],"pp":"203","s":"Five Nanyo Colonization and Manchuria subsidiaries were closed in November and added to the institutions under the Committee of Conservators for Closed Institutions."},{"t":"Public Finance","lv":3,"p":[113,114],"pp":"204","s":"Increased Income Tax Law for 4 billion yen, three supplemental budgets, special accounts, 150 billion yen domestic bonds, a 90 million yen farm settler fund, and Old Court Nobles Fund distribution."},{"t":"Section 12. Property Control and Reparations","lv":2,"p":[115,120],"pp":"209","s":"War-criminal suspects' property of 69.5 million yen, wartime patent dispositions, precious metals allocations, repatriates' seized currency, 20 holding companies designated 3 December, and reparations catalogs."},{"t":"Property Control","lv":3,"p":[115,118],"pp":"209","s":"721 suspects' inventories, 3,066 UN patents registered at 7 December 1941, release of gold and silver, SCAP custody accounts, and about 5,037,000 repatriates processed."},{"t":"Antitrust and Cartels","lv":3,"p":[118,119],"pp":"215","s":"Japan Precision Industry put under surveillance, 20 Mitsui, Mitsubishi and other firms designated for liquidation, Dental Materials Control Company dissolved, Economic Stabilization Board Regulations 2 to 4."},{"t":"Reparations","lv":3,"p":[119,120],"pp":"217","s":"Conversion permits limited to reparations plants, a catalog of 25,000 machine tools, 12 thermal plants listed, only 30 percent of aluminum plant equipment removable, and a new Reparations Division."},{"t":"Part IV: Social","lv":1,"p":[120,134],"pp":"219","s":"Public health, welfare and repatriation, communicable disease and vital statistics, then education, school lunches, information programs, radio, press comment, films and the library."},{"t":"Section 1. Public Health and Welfare","lv":2,"p":[121,129],"pp":"221","s":"2,658,769 relief recipients, 50 percent higher cash allowances, quake relief, the 19 December Soviet repatriation agreement, penicillin production, dysentery down to 73.4, and November births of 195,993."},{"t":"Public Welfare and Repatriation","lv":3,"p":[121,123],"pp":"221","s":"Relief grants of 115 million yen, US relief supplies, earthquake aid, 98,022 repatriated in December, and the 19 December agreement for 50,000 a month from Soviet areas."},{"t":"Hospitals, Veterinary, Dental and Nursing Affairs","lv":3,"p":[123,124],"pp":"224","s":"3,900 hospitals with 218,014 beds, 262 doctors licensed, milk and meat inspection faults, animal disease counts, and a nursing school recognized as a college."},{"t":"Supply","lv":3,"p":[124,125],"pp":"226","s":"Penicillin associations and 22 producing plants, medicine production of 194 million yen, vaccine figures, hydrogen cyanide fumigation, and narcotics convictions including a naval officer's three-year sentence."},{"t":"Preventive Medicine, Nutrition and Vital Statistics","lv":3,"p":[126,129],"pp":"230","s":"Typhus 2.1, diphtheria 78.6, dysentery 73.4 per 100,000; eleven quarantine ports declared; November births 195,993 and deaths 88,425; explanation of the September marriage spike."},{"t":"Section 2. Education and Media of Expression","lv":2,"p":[130,134],"pp":"239","s":"Social Education News launched 12 December, 6,164 usable school projectors, school lunch program, 1,601,781 yen from banned textbooks, women's meetings, radio series, press comment, films with 870,516 attendance."},{"t":"Education and Arts","lv":3,"p":[130,131],"pp":"239","s":"Hiroshima social education progress, a visual education survey, a vocational guidance exhibit, school lunches in Chiba, banned textbook disposal, and the Osaka-Kobe art society."},{"t":"Media of Expression","lv":3,"p":[131,134],"pp":"241","s":"Women's organizations, 'Information Please' broke records with 17,000 letters, Tokyo and prefectural press on the Yoshida Cabinet and reparations, educational film attendance and the library; page 134 is a map of Japan."}],"by":"claude-sonnet (Claude Code subagent)"},{"id":"K-15","ia":"summation-usamg-activities-korea-no-15-1946-12","s":"K","no":15,"d":"1946-12","d2":"","t":"Summation of United States Army Military Government Activities in Korea, No. 15 (December 1946)","v":"","pg":98,"o":"Summation No. 15 covers December 1946 in South Korea under USAMGIK. The dominant event was the opening of the Korean Interim Legislative Assembly on 12 December, with 45 appointed and 45 elected members, Kim Kiu Sic as chairman, a Hankook Democratic Party boycott over the annulled Seoul and Kangwon-do elections, and Syngman Rhee's departure for the United States. The Joint Korean-American Conference, set up after the autumn disorders, endorsed the rice collection programme; 62 percent of the quota was in by 31 December. Other topics include the closure of two Seoul National University colleges over student strikes, Ordinance No. 127 against rice smuggling, coal and power shortages, trade with China, the US and North Korea, fertilizer imports, price and ration violations, repatriation, typhus and smallpox, and press reaction. Many tables and charts give production, trade and price figures useful to economic historians.","h":["On 10 December the Commanding General, USAFIK, named the 45 appointive members of the Korean Interim Legislative Assembly, including four women; the other 45 were elected, and the Assembly convened 12 December under chairman Kim Kiu Sic.","Hankook Democratic Party members boycotted the opening session over the voiding of the Kangwon-do and Seoul elections, attending only from 23 December; the two Cheju-do People's Committee members never reported.","The Joint Korean-American Conference's second report (26 December) endorsed continued rice collection; 2,700,203 suk, 62 percent of the quota, was collected by 31 December, tied to distribution of 60,000 tons of imported fertilizer.","Ordinance No. 127, effective 15 December, set minimum penalties of ten years and a 100,000 yen fine for rice smuggling; 177 ships were seized since September, and 2,266 price and ration violations were recorded since 15 July.","The College of Arts and Sciences and the College of Law of Seoul National University were closed on 24 December and students suspended over strikes, with reopening set for 3 February 1947 on strict attendance conditions.","The 1946 rice crop was put at 2,105,748 metric tons, 82 percent of the wartime average; November coal output fell to 21,667 metric tons, stockpiles to 345,515 tons, and the Hwasun mine closed over labor trouble.","Bank of Chosen notes outstanding reached 17,710,623,249.90 yen on 31 December, and the first Korean cargo for the United States sailed on 25 December, valued at 112,000 dollars.","Syngman Rhee left for the United States on 2 December; Lyuh Woon Hyeung announced retirement on 4 December after failing to merge two labor parties, and the Socialist Labor Party expelled a dissident group on 30 December."],"k":["Korean Interim Legislative Assembly","Kim Kiu Sic","Syngman Rhee","Joint Korean-American Conference","rice collection","Ordinance No. 127","Seoul National University strikes","coal and electric power shortage","fertilizer imports","trade with China and United States","repatriation","typhus and smallpox","Korean Constabulary","price and ration violations","Socialist Labor Party"],"toc":[{"t":"Front matter","lv":1,"p":[1,14],"pp":"","s":"Library covers, title page for No. 15 (December 1946), a stray rice-yield extract, table of contents, and list of 34 charts plus a map of Korea."},{"t":"Part I: General","lv":1,"p":[15,24],"pp":"1","s":"Month overview: Legislative Assembly opened, Rhee left for the US, rice collection reached 62 percent of quota, Christmas relief went to 62 institutions, and repatriation was 99 percent complete."},{"t":"Section 1. Political Activities","lv":2,"p":[17,20],"pp":"3","s":"45 appointed Assembly members named 10 December, Hankook Democratic boycott, Rhee's US trip, Joint Conference rice recommendations, new ordinances, juvenile courts, and 192 Constabulary officers commissioned."},{"t":"Section 2. Economic Activities","lv":2,"p":[21,22],"pp":"7","s":"Rice crop 18 percent below wartime average, falling coal output, Chosen Heavy Industries transferred, notes outstanding at 17.7 billion yen, China mail resumed 10 December, 177 ships seized."},{"t":"Section 3. Social Activities","lv":2,"p":[23,24],"pp":"9","s":"4,000 Christmas boxes to 62 institutions, 2,460 refugees housed in former Japanese restaurants, dental statistics, Seoul National University college closures, and press and radio coverage of the Assembly."},{"t":"Part II: Political","lv":1,"p":[25,38],"pp":"11","s":"Politics around the Legislative Assembly, then legal matters: Ordinances 119, 126 and 127, juvenile courts, policing, prisons, Coast Guard rice-smuggling patrols, and Constabulary expansion."},{"t":"Section 1. Government and Politics","lv":2,"p":[27,34],"pp":"13","s":"Appointed members listed; opening speeches by Kim Kiu Sic, General Hodge, the Military Governor and MacArthur; 1947 budget, Ordinance No. 126, party shifts, and the Conference's rice report."},{"t":"Legislative Assembly","lv":3,"p":[27,29],"pp":"13","s":"45 members appointed 10 December, Assembly opened 12 December, Hankook Democratic Party boycotted over the voided elections, and the Commanding General addressed the session."},{"t":"Administration","lv":3,"p":[30,31],"pp":"16","s":"1947 budget to go to the Assembly, Ordinance No. 126 on elected officials (age 25, one year's residence), Governmental Reorganization Board, and 97 Civil Service Academy graduates on 6 December."},{"t":"Political Affairs","lv":3,"p":[31,32],"pp":"17","s":"Rhee left 2 December; Laboring Masses Party founded 12 December; Lyuh Woon Hyeung retired and the Socialist Labor Party split; Popular Alliance formed 23 December."},{"t":"Joint Korean-American Conference","lv":3,"p":[32,33],"pp":"18","s":"General Hodge's 26 December statement: the Conference backs rice collection, 2,000,000 suk of grain to be imported, four corrective measures, and a National Economic Board review."},{"t":"U. S.-U. S. S. R. Joint Commission","lv":3,"p":[34,34],"pp":"20","s":"The American delegates held themselves available at Seoul for discussions on pertinent matters, on the basis already set forth by the Commanding General, USAFIK (paragraph 26)."},{"t":"Foreign Affairs","lv":3,"p":[34,34],"pp":"20","s":"A liaison team went to Manchuria to plan the repatriation of Koreans by ship to Inchon and Pusan."},{"t":"Section 2. Legal and Public Safety","lv":2,"p":[35,38],"pp":"21","s":"Eight Americans admitted to the Korean Bar; Ordinances 119, 126 and 127; juvenile courts at Pusan, Kwangju and Taegu; railway police; rice-smuggling patrols; 192 Constabulary graduates."},{"t":"Legal","lv":3,"p":[35,35],"pp":"21","s":"Eight Americans, including one woman, admitted to the Korean Bar on 11 December; National Bar Association Central Council met 12-14 December."},{"t":"Major Legislation","lv":3,"p":[35,35],"pp":"21","s":"Ordinance 119 on narcotics, Ordinance 126 on elections, and Ordinance 127 setting ten-year minimum sentences for rice smuggling."},{"t":"Public Safety","lv":3,"p":[36,37],"pp":"22","s":"Minor disorders, juvenile courts, police inspections, ninth Railroad Police class of 250 graduated, prison work camps, and Fire Prevention Week from 9 December."},{"t":"Internal Security","lv":3,"p":[37,38],"pp":"23","s":"Coast Guard patrols against rice smuggling and Constabulary expansion, with 192 officers commissioned on 14 December."},{"t":"Part III: Economic","lv":1,"p":[39,74],"pp":"25","s":"Natural resources, industrial output, finance, trade, transport and communications, then rice collection at 2,700,203 suk, prices and 2,266 violations."},{"t":"Section 1. Natural Resources","lv":2,"p":[41,46],"pp":"27","s":"Rice crop 2,105,748 tons, October fish catch 28,801 tons, forestry allocation of 197,327 yen, and November coal output of 21,667 tons amid mine closures."},{"t":"Agriculture","lv":3,"p":[41,42],"pp":"27","s":"Rice crop at 82 percent of wartime yield, fall in grain acreage, 60,000 tons of fertilizer, livestock census of 556,220 cattle, and potato contest awards."},{"t":"Fisheries","lv":3,"p":[42,43],"pp":"28","s":"October catch revised to 28,801 metric tons worth 263,263,249 yen; jute rope and bamboo allocations; 66 vessels leased."},{"t":"Forestry","lv":3,"p":[43,44],"pp":"29","s":"September lumber output 657,740 board feet; 197,327 yen allocated 7 December for 1,074,337 acres; 12,420,000 cubic feet of fuelwood to be delivered."},{"t":"Mining","lv":3,"p":[44,45],"pp":"30","s":"November coal 21,667 tons, stockpile 345,515 tons, Hwasun mine closed by labor trouble, Kyongju mine abandoned, plus graphite and copper output."},{"t":"Section 2. Industry","lv":2,"p":[47,58],"pp":"33","s":"Raw-material shortages cut output; Standards Committee met 17 December; Chosen Heavy Industries transferred; September textiles from 107 factories; labor and employment figures."},{"t":"Heavy Industry and Manufacturing","lv":3,"p":[47,52],"pp":"33","s":"Korean Standards Committee metric program, New Korea Company breakup, iron, steel and consumer-goods charts, 825,640 bricks, and locomotive works at Inchon."},{"t":"Textiles","lv":3,"p":[53,55],"pp":"39","s":"September textiles worth 55,956,207 yen from 107 factories; cotton crop 71,981 tons; 42 silk factories at 70 percent of Japanese output; wool 8,011 kg."},{"t":"Labor","lv":3,"p":[56,58],"pp":"42","s":"Labor Week activities, 6 December conference on union ordinances, no major disturbances, and September employment of 36,486 in 608 operating factories."},{"t":"Section 3. Commerce","lv":2,"p":[59,68],"pp":"45","s":"Notes outstanding 17.7 billion yen, 65 trade licenses in December, curbs on China trade, first cargo to the US, rail, power and mail problems, and North-South mail exchange."},{"t":"Finance","lv":3,"p":[59,60],"pp":"45","s":"Bank of Chosen notes rose 2,829,629,889 yen to 17,710,623,249.90 yen; gold price rising; Life Insurance Association of Korea founded 24 December."},{"t":"Imports and Exports","lv":3,"p":[60,63],"pp":"46","s":"65 licenses in December against 82 in November, China trade suspended, US cargo worth 112,000 dollars, and 162,462 tons of wheat imported."},{"t":"Transportation and Public Utilities","lv":3,"p":[64,66],"pp":"50","s":"Railroad ties only 13 percent replaced, 34,384 railroad employees, road program 30 percent done, three customs control stations from 15 December, peak power 69,800 kW."},{"t":"Communications","lv":3,"p":[66,68],"pp":"52","s":"China mail resumed 10 December, North-South mail exchange 8 December, telegraph traffic with Japan up 20 percent, and postal insurance and savings charts."},{"t":"Section 4. Rationing and Price Control","lv":2,"p":[69,74],"pp":"55","s":"Rice collection reached 2,700,203 suk (62 percent), distribution shortfalls, salt and paper allocations, price indexes, Pusan ration stores taken over, and 2,266 violations."},{"t":"Food","lv":3,"p":[69,70],"pp":"55","s":"2,700,203 suk of rice collected by 31 December, collection tied to fertilizer delivery, inadequate transport to cities, and imported salt, corn starch and sugar allocated."},{"t":"Other Goods","lv":3,"p":[70,71],"pp":"56","s":"1,000 tons of ammonium nitrate to the Agricultural Association, 9,176 drums of pitch, 807,500 pounds of paper distributed, plus December salt and mulberry tree prices."},{"t":"Cost of Living","lv":3,"p":[71,73],"pp":"57","s":"December cost-of-living differential unchanged; free-market prices rose in line with wholesale prices. Pages 71-73 carry price charts (Dec 1946) with little text."},{"t":"Controls and Violations","lv":3,"p":[74,74],"pp":"60","s":"Provincial Food Service took over Pusan ration stores 15 December; 177 ships seized, 24 in December; 2,266 violations, 1,036 in Kyonggi-do and Seoul."},{"t":"Part IV: Social","lv":1,"p":[75,92],"pp":"61","s":"Public health and welfare, education, culture, religion and public information, with heavy coverage of the Assembly and the Seoul National University strikes."},{"t":"Section 1. Public Health and Welfare","lv":2,"p":[77,84],"pp":"63","s":"Welfare chiefs' conference 19-21 December, repatriation charts, 456 licensed dentists, bovine influenza, narcotics transfer, typhus, 35 smallpox cases at Kangnung, and the second census."},{"t":"Public Welfare","lv":3,"p":[77,77],"pp":"63","s":"Welfare chiefs met 19-21 December, 4,000 Christmas boxes were distributed, and private funds were raised for refugee shelters."},{"t":"Medical and Hospital Affairs","lv":3,"p":[78,78],"pp":"64","s":"2,524 Koreans arrived from Manchuria 27 December, liaison team sent 14 December, Masan sanitarium 116 of 144 beds, and Sorokdo leprosarium numbers."},{"t":"Nursing Affairs","lv":3,"p":[79,79],"pp":"65","s":"28 nurses finished Pusan courses, 52 of 159 passed midwifery exams on 3 December, and 10,212 persons were inoculated against typhus."},{"t":"Dental Affairs","lv":3,"p":[79,80],"pp":"65","s":"456 licensed dentists and 107 crown makers, 51,500 yen in fees for 1946, and Seoul Dental College closed 21 December for lack of fuel."},{"t":"Veterinary Affairs","lv":3,"p":[80,80],"pp":"66","s":"Bovine influenza under control, with 19,727 cases and 92 deaths in Kyongsang-namdo; 121 pullorum reactors slaughtered."},{"t":"Supply","lv":3,"p":[80,82],"pp":"66","s":"80,000 yards of cotton cloth and 8,922 dozen X-ray films to hospitals, sweet potatoes for medicinal alcohol, peptone production, and vaccine stock tables for Seoul and Pusan."},{"t":"Preventive Medicine","lv":3,"p":[83,83],"pp":"69","s":"Narcotics control moved to Pharmaceutical Affairs; 26 typhus and 91 typhoid cases 1-21 December; 35 smallpox cases at Kangnung; typhus lecture tour."},{"t":"Vital Statistics","lv":3,"p":[84,84],"pp":"70","s":"Vital statistics training conference held 4-7 December; second national census of South Korea completed."},{"t":"Section 2. Education, Culture and Religion","lv":2,"p":[85,88],"pp":"71","s":"Two Seoul National University colleges closed 24 December, winter institutes, 317,800 textbooks sold, 794 Boy Scout troops, and four Methodist missionaries returning to Ewha."},{"t":"Education","lv":3,"p":[85,86],"pp":"71","s":"American Adviser's 24 December order closed the Arts and Sciences and Law colleges until 3 February 1947; winter institutes held; 1,000 books given to 16 colleges."},{"t":"Culture","lv":3,"p":[86,87],"pp":"72","s":"Ten ancient court books translated into Korean, Playground Committee met 24 December, a soccer match drew over 15,000, and 16,639 Boy Scouts in 794 troops."},{"t":"Religion","lv":3,"p":[87,88],"pp":"73","s":"Four Methodist women missionaries returned on 9 December to teach at Ewha Women's College."},{"t":"Section 3. Public Information","lv":2,"p":[89,92],"pp":"75","s":"Rice-collection speakers, newsreels, recording of the Assembly opening, radio programs, and press comment on the Assembly, the second Seoul and Kangwon elections, and inflation."},{"t":"Information Programs","lv":3,"p":[89,89],"pp":"75","s":"Speakers sent to provinces on rice collection, smallpox and typhus health warnings, and American material for information centers and mobile education units."},{"t":"Motion Pictures","lv":3,"p":[89,90],"pp":"75","s":"Newsreels, photographs and recordings of the Assembly opening made for the historical record, with a chart of films reviewed."},{"t":"Radio","lv":3,"p":[90,90],"pp":"76","s":"Political group broadcasts, Department of Commerce talks, 25 December Military Government Hour on the Department of Justice, and Christmas choral programs."},{"t":"Press and Public Opinion","lv":3,"p":[90,92],"pp":"76","s":"Papers disputed the second Seoul election, Cho So Wang declined his seat, Chung Oi Sin Po attacked the Assembly, and Chosun Ilbo urged selling Japanese plants."},{"t":"Map of Korea","lv":1,"p":[93,93],"pp":"79","s":"Fold-out map of Korea, reproduced as a single page after Part IV."},{"t":"Back matter","lv":1,"p":[94,98],"pp":"","s":"Blank scanned pages and an Alderman Library due-date slip stamped 1949; no report content."}],"by":"claude-sonnet (Claude Code subagent)"},{"id":"J-16","ia":"summation-non-military-activities-japan-no-16-1947-01","s":"J","no":16,"d":"1947-01","d2":"","t":"Summation of Non-Military Activities in Japan, No. 16 (January 1947)","v":"","pg":137,"o":"Summation No. 16 covers non-military activities in Japan for January 1947 (with many statistics for December and November 1946). Politically it records Prime Minister Yoshida's failed coalition talks with the Social Democrats, the 31 January cabinet reshuffle, the extension of the purge to local government and key economic posts, the dissolution of neighborhood associations, and the Supreme Commander's 31 January ban on the planned 1 February general strike of about 2,000,000 government workers. War crimes sections report Yokohama and Philippine trials and the close of the prosecution case before the International Military Tribunal on 24 January, with extensive POW testimony. Economic sections document acute coal and electric power shortages, textile recovery with imported American cotton, trade with Korea, China and the United States, rationing, rice collection, finance and property control. Social sections cover earthquake relief, repatriation, disease rates, nutrition surveys, vital statistics and education reform. Useful for labor history, the purge, and the 1946 food and fuel crisis.","h":["Yoshida's coalition talks with the Social Democrats failed (15-17 January and again later); on 31 January four ministers left and four were transferred, Etsujiro Uyehara became Home Minister and Yoshida temporarily took Agriculture and Forestry.","Imperial Ordinances Nos. 1-3 of 4 January extended the purge to local government and specified economic and media posts, with penalties up to 15,000 yen or three years; about 80,700 persons were scheduled for screening February-April.","On the night of 31 January the Supreme Commander prohibited the 1 February general strike planned by the All-Japan Joint Struggle Committee (about 30 organizations, some 4,000,000 workers), citing the risk of starvation to the Japanese people.","On 22 January the Government announced a Wage Investigation Commission, a cash wage limit raised from 500 to 700 yen, and temporary government worker raises of 150 yen plus 25 percent of base pay, which the unions rejected.","The prosecution closed its case before the International Military Tribunal for the Far East on 24 January after nine months, with extensive POW testimony; the defense opened 27 January, and Admiral Nagano had died on 5 January.","December coal output was 2,172,900 metric tons, yet coal and electric power shortages kept throttling industry; Bank of Japan notes reached 100,040,145,000 yen on 31 January and the national debt stood at 255,307,000,000 yen.","The 21 December earthquake killed 1,321, left 227 missing and destroyed 19,418 buildings in 25 prefectures; 62,784 Japanese were repatriated from 1 to 26 January, with 1,254,432 still awaiting return from Soviet-controlled areas.","First exports made from postwar American cotton (yarn to Hongkong and Burma) and first postwar fish oil to the United States shipped, while Antarctic whaling fleets caught 422 whales from 1 to 27 January."],"k":["SCAP","Yoshida cabinet","coalition cabinet","purge","general strike","government workers","Wage Investigation Commission","International Military Tribunal for the Far East","war crimes trials","coal shortage","cotton textiles","Kyushu earthquake","repatriation","staple food rationing","neighborhood associations","agrarian reform"],"toc":[{"t":"Front matter","lv":1,"p":[1,4],"pp":"","s":"Cover for Summation No. 16 (January 1947), the contents of the four parts, and the list of 108 charts, maps and graphs."},{"t":"Part I: General","lv":1,"p":[5,12],"pp":"1","s":"Digest of the month: no change in SCAP's non-military organization, Yoshida's failed coalition talks, the 31 January cabinet reshuffle, economic shortages, the 1 February strike crisis, earthquake relief and repatriation."},{"t":"Section 1. Organization under SCAP","lv":2,"p":[6,6],"pp":"3","s":"Reports that the non-military organization of GHQ SCAP did not change during January 1947."},{"t":"Section 2. Political Activities","lv":2,"p":[6,7],"pp":"3","s":"Yoshida's coalition talks collapsed and the cabinet was reshuffled on 31 January; the Diet session was delayed, the Constitution publicized, neighborhood associations dissolved, and police, prison and war crimes matters noted."},{"t":"Section 3. Economic Activities","lv":2,"p":[8,10],"pp":"7","s":"Digest of resources, industry, textiles, labor, trade, rationing, finance and property control, including 1,100 tons of crude rubber arriving 20 January and the 15 January formation of the Joint Struggle Committee."},{"t":"Section 4. Social Activities","lv":2,"p":[11,12],"pp":"13","s":"Digest of welfare, Red Cross earthquake relief, 62,784 repatriates, disease and vital statistics, education measures, religion, and information programs on the new Constitution."},{"t":"Part II: Political","lv":1,"p":[13,38],"pp":"17","s":"Politics, public safety and war crimes: Yoshida's coalition efforts and 31 January reshuffle, the purge extended by 4 January ordinances, demonstrations and black-market raids, and the Tokyo war crimes trial."},{"t":"Section 1. Government and Politics","lv":2,"p":[14,23],"pp":"19","s":"MacArthur's New Year statement, failed coalition negotiations, Diet Law features, the 91st Diet session summary, the extended purge ordinances and the abolition of tonari-gumi."},{"t":"Supreme Commander's New Year Statement","lv":3,"p":[14,14],"pp":"","s":"Full text of MacArthur's New Year message, praising 1946 reforms and warning that the new Constitution and land reform depend on the Japanese people's own use of their rights."},{"t":"Tenets of Occupation Policy","lv":3,"p":[15,15],"pp":"","s":"Japanese reaction to MacArthur's letters saying Christian ideals guide occupation policy, including a Nippon Times editorial of 6 January on Japan's spiritual vacuum."},{"t":"Political Developments","lv":3,"p":[16,17],"pp":"","s":"Yoshida's New Year speech attacking malcontents, two failed coalition attempts (17 January and later), and the 31 January cabinet reshuffle with the full list of ministers."},{"t":"The Diet","lv":3,"p":[18,18],"pp":"","s":"The House of Representatives asserts the right to nominate a prime minister, the 92nd session is delayed, and 22 features of the unpassed Diet Law are listed."},{"t":"Summary of Ninety-first Extraordinary Diet Session","lv":3,"p":[19,20],"pp":"","s":"Statistics for the 31-day session of 26 November-26 December: 69 bills, 130 petitions, 12,269 visitors, Liberals at 148 seats, and a dissolution resolution defeated 256-160."},{"t":"The Constitution","lv":3,"p":[21,21],"pp":"","s":"Government plans to publicize the new Constitution before 3 May, with Daiei, Tano and Shochiku films and party women's committees led by Hanako Muraoka."},{"t":"The Purge","lv":3,"p":[21,22],"pp":"","s":"Ordinances of 4 January extend the purge to local government and key posts, with a screening schedule covering 80,700 persons and MacArthur's 31 January rebuttal of Newsweek."},{"t":"Local Government","lv":3,"p":[23,23],"pp":"","s":"A 21 January cabinet decision dissolves federations of tonari-gumi neighborhood associations by 1 April, with Home Minister Omura's statement, plus two personnel changes."},{"t":"Section 2. Public Safety","lv":2,"p":[24,29],"pp":"39","s":"Orderly demonstrations including 150,000 workers at the Palace on 28 January, black-market raids, 143,090 offenses in November, the Police College opening, prison reform and winter fires."},{"t":"Law and Order","lv":3,"p":[24,26],"pp":"","s":"Demonstrations, attacks on labor leader Kikunami, black-market raids arresting 1,437 persons, smuggling from Formosa, and November crime statistics with prefectural rate charts."},{"t":"Public Casualties","lv":3,"p":[27,27],"pp":"","s":"Railway accidents including an electric train overturning near Imaichi on 7 January, Tokyo traffic restrictions, and seven deaths from methyl alcohol whiskey."},{"t":"Police","lv":3,"p":[27,27],"pp":"","s":"New police chiefs, the Police College opening at Ogikubo on 14 January with 200 students, radio patrol cars, and 24,537 juvenile offenses in Tokyo in 1946."},{"t":"Prisons","lv":3,"p":[28,28],"pp":"","s":"Ministry of Justice reform campaign of 15 January, a Muroran escape attempt, and prison population falling from 70,753 in November to 67,995 in December."},{"t":"Fire","lv":3,"p":[29,29],"pp":"","s":"1,793 fires in December with 110 deaths, the Toyama fire destroying 350 houses on 2 January, Army fire losses and new theater fire rules."},{"t":"Section 3. Legal and War Crimes","lv":2,"p":[30,38],"pp":"51","s":"Occupation court cases, 1,204 atrocity investigations on hand, 860 suspects interned at Sugamo, Yokohama and Philippine trials, and the Tokyo Tribunal prosecution resting on 24 January."},{"t":"Legal and Military Occupation Courts","lv":3,"p":[30,30],"pp":"","s":"Disposition of seven criminal cases involving UN nationals, five Japanese sentenced for typewriter theft in Yokohama, and acquittal of three police over the 19 July riot."},{"t":"Investigation of Suspected War Criminals","lv":3,"p":[31,31],"pp":"","s":"118 new atrocity cases opened, 1,204 on hand, a Palawan Massacre survivor located, and Australian, British, Chinese and Philippine investigation results."},{"t":"Apprehension of Suspected War Criminals","lv":3,"p":[31,31],"pp":"","s":"Ten memorandums ordered 177 persons apprehended; 41 interned at Sugamo, bringing the total to 860; 22 suspects transferred to Australian and British custody."},{"t":"Prosecution of War Criminals","lv":3,"p":[32,32],"pp":"","s":"116 defendants tried at Yokohama (11 death sentences), six trials completed in January, a Philippine total of 153 tried, and 44 executions at Rabaul."},{"t":"International Prosecution of War Criminals","lv":3,"p":[33,38],"pp":"","s":"Prosecution rested 24 January after nine months; extensive POW testimony on the Burma railway, Sandakan and Sumatra; defense motions for mistrial rejected 27 January."},{"t":"Part III: Economic","lv":1,"p":[39,115],"pp":"69","s":"Agriculture through property control: farm and fisheries output, coal and power shortages, manufacturing and textiles, labor and the strike crisis, trade, rationing, finance and reparations."},{"t":"Section 1. Agriculture and Fisheries","lv":2,"p":[40,42],"pp":"71","s":"10,332 land commissions formed in December voting, 1946 staple food output of 18,477,000 tons, December landings of 121,214 tons and 422 whales caught in the Antarctic."},{"t":"Agriculture","lv":3,"p":[40,40],"pp":"","s":"Land commission elections, land reform pamphlets, wildlife conservation proposals, DDT toxicity to silkworms and radiation mutations in Nagasaki plants."},{"t":"Fisheries","lv":3,"p":[41,42],"pp":"","s":"December landings, 1946 marine products of 1,737,700 tons, Antarctic whaling by Taiyo and Nippon Suisan, petroleum allocations and 203 steel fishing ships built in 1946."},{"t":"Section 2. Forestry and Mining","lv":2,"p":[43,47],"pp":"77","s":"December log output of 421,800,000 board feet, coal production of 2,172,900 tons, stockpiles of 925,000 tons on 20 January, and oil output of 16,958 kiloliters hampered by Akita snow."},{"t":"Forestry","lv":3,"p":[43,44],"pp":"","s":"Log output, 944 new sawmills, 20,000,000 board feet of relief lumber approved for earthquake victims, and charts for plywood, timber products and charcoal."},{"t":"Mining","lv":3,"p":[45,47],"pp":"","s":"Coal production, consumption and stockpiles, lignite, petroleum drilling and November mineral output, including copper content of 1,784 tons and iron ore at 50,826 tons."},{"t":"Section 3. Heavy Industries","lv":2,"p":[48,55],"pp":"87","s":"Coke output of 108,000 tons, low metal production, 1,100 tons of crude rubber from Singapore on 20 January, shipbuilding approvals, chemicals at 35 percent of minimum, machine tools up six percent."},{"t":"Section 4. Manufacturing","lv":2,"p":[56,65],"pp":"103","s":"Food processing short of soybeans, pulp up 100 percent for rayon, 1,261 trucks produced, rubber use falling to 907 tons, plus glass, leather and farm implements."},{"t":"Section 5. Textile Industries","lv":2,"p":[66,75],"pp":"123","s":"Cotton yarn rose to 26,980,000 pounds, raw silk fell to 9,257 bales, rayon staple dropped by a third, and power restrictions limited wool, bast fibers and knit goods."},{"t":"Section 6. Transportation and Public Utilities","lv":2,"p":[76,79],"pp":"143","s":"Rail fares raised 25 percent and freight 100 percent, 193,012 train-kilometers cancelled, freight down 689,700 tons, and power generation of 2,475,000,000 kilowatt hours under severe restrictions."},{"t":"Section 7. Communications","lv":2,"p":[80,87],"pp":"151","s":"Telephone and telegraph trends, 495 radio stations, Tokyo-San Francisco radio programs authorized 17 January, expanded international mail, postal construction and Broadcasting Corporation finances."},{"t":"Section 8. Labor","lv":2,"p":[88,95],"pp":"167","s":"16,171 unions with 4,296,589 members, the 1 February strike threat, MacArthur's 31 January prohibition, the wage commission, wage limit raised to 700 yen, and unemployment and training."},{"t":"Labor Legislation","lv":3,"p":[88,88],"pp":"","s":"Draft bills on workmen's compensation, unemployment insurance, seamen and employment exchanges, and an 18 January ordinance barring wartime labor front leaders."},{"t":"Labor and Employer Organizations","lv":3,"p":[89,89],"pp":"","s":"Union statistics, Economic Reconstruction Conference preparations, formation of the 108,000-member Private Railway Workers federation, and teachers' union unification."},{"t":"Labor Relations","lv":3,"p":[90,92],"pp":"","s":"Government workers' strike plans, the Kikunami stabbing, failed negotiations, and the full text of MacArthur's 31 January statement prohibiting the general strike."},{"t":"Labor Education","lv":3,"p":[93,93],"pp":"","s":"Kanto Employers' Association labor school began 15 January with 300 students; union schools were postponed to April."},{"t":"Wages and Working Conditions","lv":3,"p":[93,93],"pp":"","s":"Wage rises in October-November, family expenditure of 1,017 yen biweekly, Wage Investigation Commission members, and temporary government worker raises of 150 yen plus 25 percent."},{"t":"Employment and Unemployment","lv":3,"p":[94,95],"pp":"","s":"Vocational training reached 331 centers, textile employment 493,762, coal miners up to 360,000, plus prevailing wage rules for public works and occupation labor."},{"t":"Section 9. Imports and Exports","lv":2,"p":[96,99],"pp":"183","s":"First U.S. sugar and 54,811 bales of raw cotton imported; cotton yarn to Hongkong and Burma, fabrics to Korea, fish oil to the United States, locomotives and printing supplies to China and Korea."},{"t":"Section 10. Rationing and Price Control","lv":2,"p":[100,106],"pp":"191","s":"91.4 days of staple stocks, 73 percent of rice quota collected by 20 January, 55,000 tons of imported wheat released, a fertilizer bag shortage, and 88,973 violations."},{"t":"Food","lv":3,"p":[100,102],"pp":"","s":"Staple stock levels, rice collection incentives, supplementary rations for heavy laborers and fishermen, imported food releases, and fresh fish, vegetable and fruit supplies."},{"t":"Rents and Fertilizer","lv":3,"p":[103,103],"pp":"","s":"Rent increases in four of 24 major cities, 98 percent of land rents registered, and a 5,055,000-bag shortfall of straw bags for fertilizer."},{"t":"Fuel and Miscellaneous Goods","lv":3,"p":[104,105],"pp":"","s":"Coal deliveries at 98.6 percent of allocation, petroleum restriction enforcement, shoe prices up 10 to 30 percent and price indexes for leather and aluminum ware."},{"t":"Controls and Violations","lv":3,"p":[106,106],"pp":"","s":"Occupation supervision of fuel distribution, the 22 January ban on critical materials in nonessential goods, and hoarded goods worth 2,578,598 yen recovered in December."},{"t":"Section 11. Finance","lv":2,"p":[107,110],"pp":"205","s":"Bank of Japan notes reached 100,040,145,000 yen, National City Bank remittance authority, 6,600,000,000 yen Treasury bills, national debt of 255,307,000,000 yen and Imperial Household downsizing."},{"t":"Money and Banking","lv":3,"p":[107,109],"pp":"","s":"National City Bank of New York authorized to handle US-to-Japan remittances; Bank of Japan notes hit 100,040,145,000 yen on 31 January. Bank of Japan notes issued rose from 78,049 to 92,433 million yen, 10 December to 10 January."},{"t":"Liquidation","lv":3,"p":[110,110],"pp":"","s":"Securities Co-ordinating Liquidation Committee created, two companies designated closed institutions, and liquidation proceeds of 1,968,564,000 yen to 30 November."},{"t":"Public Finance","lv":3,"p":[110,110],"pp":"","s":"Treasury bills and special account borrowings of 15,344,000,000 yen authorized, a national debt table, local pension withholding, and transfer of the Imperial Court Police."},{"t":"Section 12. Property Control and Reparations","lv":2,"p":[111,115],"pp":"213","s":"German property of 61,819,318 yen, 2,528 looted-property reports, 257,322,281 dollars of precious metals, a 600,000,000 yen cotton loan, and the Philippine reparations mission's arrival."},{"t":"Property Control","lv":3,"p":[111,113],"pp":"","s":"Patent agreements, German and looted property, assets of 848 suspected war criminals, Butoku Kai assets, precious metals and the SCAP custody account."},{"t":"Antitrust and Cartels","lv":3,"p":[114,114],"pp":"","s":"Loans approved for fishing vessels and a fertilizer plant, a 600,000,000 yen cotton rehabilitation loan, holding company proxies, and coal worker housing ordinances."},{"t":"Reparations","lv":3,"p":[115,115],"pp":"","s":"Changes in designated plant listings, arrival of the five-man Philippine Reparations Mission, and a new head of the Central Liaison Office Reparations Division."},{"t":"Part IV: Social","lv":1,"p":[116,136],"pp":"223","s":"Public health, welfare and education: earthquake relief, repatriation, communicable disease rates, nutrition surveys, vital statistics, school reforms and press comment on the cabinet and strike crises."},{"t":"Section 1. Public Health and Welfare","lv":2,"p":[117,131],"pp":"225","s":"2,837,207 persons on public assistance, 1,321 dead in the earthquake, 1,472,613 Japanese awaiting repatriation, penicillin standards, diphtheria and typhus rates, nutrition and vital statistics."},{"t":"Welfare","lv":3,"p":[117,117],"pp":"","s":"Welfare payments of 144,053,679 yen, a second LARA shipment of 386 tons, the earthquake toll, Red Cross reorganization and repatriation figures."},{"t":"Hospital Administration","lv":3,"p":[118,118],"pp":"","s":"3,047 hospitals with 216,354 beds on 20 December and SCAP's medical reference library for Japanese educators."},{"t":"Veterinary Affairs","lv":3,"p":[119,119],"pp":"","s":"Veterinary Education Council adopted minimum standards on 15 January, with a four-year course from 1948, plus meat and milk inspection and animal diseases."},{"t":"Supply","lv":3,"p":[120,121],"pp":"","s":"Penicillin testing rules, medical and dental supply output of 185,097,835 yen, typhus vaccine production, 78,350 needing prosthetics, DDT production and narcotics releases."},{"t":"Preventive Medicine","lv":3,"p":[122,124],"pp":"","s":"December disease rates and 1946 totals, including 87,737 dysentery and 31,141 typhus cases, venereal disease clinics, airfield quarantine and cyanide fumigation."},{"t":"Nutrition","lv":3,"p":[125,129],"pp":"","s":"November nutrition survey showing higher caloric intake after the rice ration increase, low protein, weight deviations and deficiency symptoms."},{"t":"Vital Statistics","lv":3,"p":[130,131],"pp":"","s":"December births 30.2 per 1,000, deaths 102,103, infant deaths up 47 percent, 9,350 stillbirths, 64,393 marriages and 6,627 divorces."},{"t":"Section 2. Education, Religion and Media of Expression","lv":2,"p":[132,136],"pp":"255","s":"Educational Reform Committee's second report, Constitution lecture courses, school lunches for 2,942 schools, missionary policy, a Nara cultural sites map and press comment on the strike."},{"t":"Education","lv":3,"p":[132,132],"pp":"","s":"Educational Reform Committee's 27 December report, Constitution lectures at 55 schools, Citizens' Public Halls subsidies, the school lunch program and an educational film committee."},{"t":"Religion","lv":3,"p":[133,133],"pp":"","s":"Liberalized missionary clearance policy and arrival of Greek Orthodox, Church of Christ and Nazarene survey representatives."},{"t":"Arts and Monuments","lv":3,"p":[133,133],"pp":"","s":"American art restoration publications passed to the Ministry of Education and no serious earthquake damage to arts; a Nara Prefecture cultural sites map follows."},{"t":"Media of Expression","lv":3,"p":[133,136],"pp":"","s":"Information programs on women, prisons, typhus and the Constitution, radio programs, Tokyo and prefectural press comment on the coalition and strike, magazines, theater and library attendance."},{"t":"Map of Japan","lv":1,"p":[137,137],"pp":"265","s":"Final page with a map of Japan, followed by the National Archives and Records Service stamp."}],"by":"claude-sonnet (Claude Code subagent)"},{"id":"K-16","ia":"summation-usamg-activities-korea-no-16-1947-01","s":"K","no":16,"d":"1947-01","d2":"","t":"Summation of United States Army Military Government Activities in Korea, No. 16 (January 1947)","v":"","pg":116,"o":"Summation No. 16 covers January 1947 in the U.S. Army Military Government in Korea (South Korea), issued by Commander-in-Chief Far East. The month is dominated by politics: the exchange of letters between General Hodge and the Soviet commander over reopening the Joint Commission, Kim Koo's anti-trusteeship movement, the Interim Legislative Assembly's 44-1 resolution against trusteeship of 20 January, and Hodge's public rejoinders. Acting Military Governor Lerch's 9 January address to the Assembly sets out finance, food, industry and reorganization problems. Other reports: reorganization of the National Economic Board, Ordinance No. 128 raising land taxes, suspension of the Child Labor Ordinance, Bank of Chosen note circulation of 18.28 billion yen, rice collection at 78 percent of quota, smallpox, typhus and typhoid outbreaks, refugee relief, repatriation figures and the Korean National Youth Movement. Statistical tables on industry, trade, prices and public health make it useful for economic and political history of the occupation.","h":["On 11 January Hodge published his exchange of letters with the Soviet commander on reopening the Joint Commission, proposing that signing Communique No. 5 count as good faith toward the Moscow Decision; Kim Koo's anti-trusteeship movement formed in response.","On 20 January the Korean Interim Legislative Assembly passed an anti-trusteeship resolution 44 to 1 with nine abstentions, rejecting an amendment 17 to 43; Hodge replied on 25 January that the resolution misstated the U.S. position.","On 9 January the Acting Military Governor told the Assembly to draft universal-suffrage election laws by end of February, support the rice collection and review labor ordinances; a single national budget for fiscal 1947 was promised.","The National Economic Board was reorganized with an American chairman and a Korean deputy chairman, absorbing the Korean Economic Advisory Board; it first met on 29 January.","Ordinance No. 128 (effective 10 January) raised land taxes and service fees; Bank of Chosen notes outstanding reached 18,277,613,497.90 yen on 31 January, up 566,990,248 yen in the month.","The Child Labor Ordinance (No. 112) was suspended until 1 June 1947 to allow Assembly review, while hiring children under 14 stayed prohibited; the National Labor Federation formed on 12 January.","Rice collection reached 78 percent of the 1946 quota by 31 January; 69,417 metric tons of imported staple food were released in December, and a twice-monthly ration period starts 12 February.","Health reports showed January typhus cases rising to 647 and typhoid to 457; 10,036 Japanese were repatriated from North Korea and 15,283 Koreans awaited evacuation from Japan."],"k":["Joint Commission","trusteeship","Moscow Decision","Kim Koo","Korean Interim Legislative Assembly","Hodge","National Economic Board","inflation","rice collection","Child Labor Ordinance","repatriation","Korean National Youth Movement","Korean Constabulary","smallpox typhus","USAMGIK January 1947"],"toc":[{"t":"Front matter","lv":1,"p":[1,14],"pp":"","s":"Library covers, title page for Summation No. 16 (January 1947), table of contents of four parts, and list of 34 charts and a map of Korea."},{"t":"Part I: General","lv":1,"p":[15,22],"pp":"1","s":"Digest of the month: Hodge's Joint Commission letters, Kim Koo's anti-trusteeship movement, Ordinance No. 128, rice collection at 78 percent, and the new National Labor Federation and Youth Movement."},{"t":"Section 1. Political Activities","lv":2,"p":[17,18],"pp":"3","s":"Hodge's 11 January letters on the Joint Commission, Kim Koo's anti-trusteeship movement, People's Party reorganization, the Acting Military Governor's 9 January address, and five new Constabulary and Coast Guard ranks."},{"t":"Section 2. Economic Activities","lv":2,"p":[19,20],"pp":"5","s":"Seed cotton down 14 percent, coal stockpiles up, National Labor Federation formed 12 January, notes in circulation at 18.28 billion yen, rail fares raised, 78 percent of rice quota collected."},{"t":"Section 3. Social Activities","lv":2,"p":[21,22],"pp":"7","s":"Relief supplies from Koreans in Japan, 10,036 Japanese repatriated from the north, Winter Institute at Seoul National University, Youth Movement with 19,270 members, and trusteeship dominating the press."},{"t":"Part II: Political","lv":1,"p":[23,48],"pp":"9","s":"Economic board reorganization, Hodge's policy statements, Joint Commission letters, Assembly debates and the 20 January trusteeship resolution, plus Ordinance No. 128, police, prisons, fires and the Coast Guard."},{"t":"Section 1. Government and Politics","lv":2,"p":[25,40],"pp":"11","s":"Reorganized National Economic Board, Hodge's 4 January statement, Joint Commission letters, party mergers, the Assembly's 44-1 anti-trusteeship vote of 20 January and Hodge's replies; 49 Japanese remain."},{"t":"Administration","lv":3,"p":[25,25],"pp":"11","s":"National Economic Board reorganized with an American chairman and Korean deputy chairman; first met 29 January on agricultural goals, food shipping and a copper-mining loan."},{"t":"American Policy","lv":3,"p":[25,27],"pp":"11","s":"Hodge's 4 January statement: the U.S. does not favor a separate southern government, and the Assembly is an interim body without final executive power."},{"t":"U.S.-U.S.S.R. Joint Commission","lv":3,"p":[27,28],"pp":"13","s":"Soviet letter of 26 November set three consultation conditions; the U.S. reply of 24 December proposed that signing Communique No. 5 count as good faith."},{"t":"Political Activities","lv":3,"p":[28,30],"pp":"14","s":"Kim Koo's anti-trusteeship committee, rightist merger talk, Coalition Committee criticism, National Independence Front, Democratic People's Front and the People's Party restoration committee."},{"t":"Korean Interim Legislative Assembly","lv":3,"p":[30,39],"pp":"16","s":"Assembly reconvened 6 January; Acting Military Governor's 9 January address; amnesty debate; 44-1 anti-trusteeship resolution on 20 January; Hodge's 25 January reply."},{"t":"Foreign Affairs","lv":3,"p":[39,40],"pp":"25","s":"Latest census reported 49 Japanese nationals still living in South Korea."},{"t":"Section 2. Legal and Public Safety","lv":2,"p":[41,48],"pp":"27","s":"Ordinance No. 128 land tax increase, Hodge's 16 January warning against demonstrations, police trial board, 3,683 prison staff, 266 fires costing 261 million yen, Coast Guard seizures."},{"t":"Part III: Economic","lv":1,"p":[49,90],"pp":"35","s":"Cotton at 131.9 million pounds, coal stockpiles of 368,183 tons, industrial shortages, textile output, foreign trade, finance, transport, communications, rice collection at 78 percent and price control."},{"t":"Section 1. Natural Resources","lv":2,"p":[51,58],"pp":"37","s":"Cotton at 131,879,359 pounds, 114,000-ton rice increase planned, November fish catch of 9,168 tons, charcoal at 80 percent of quota, December coal output 21,041 tons, tungsten 182 tons."},{"t":"Section 2. Industry","lv":2,"p":[59,70],"pp":"45","s":"Raw material shortages hamper steel, paper and shoes; 127 textile factories; Child Labor Ordinance suspended until 1 June; National Labor Federation; 48,703 industrial workers; 100-million-yen river restoration."},{"t":"Section 3. Commerce","lv":2,"p":[71,84],"pp":"57","s":"Acting Military Governor's 3 January anti-inflation statement, notes at 18.28 billion yen, gold at 2,400 yen, exports via U.S. Commercial Company, rail fare rise, mail extended to all countries."},{"t":"Finance and Property Control","lv":3,"p":[71,74],"pp":"57","s":"Inflation measures, 566,990,248-yen note increase, gold price up 17 percent, International Fire Reinsurance Company licensed 10 January with 20 million yen capital."},{"t":"Imports and Exports","lv":3,"p":[74,78],"pp":"60","s":"U.S. Commercial Company to sell Korean exports abroad; December permits worth 71.8 million yen; ore exports of 33.8 million yen to Japan; itemized 1946 and January imports."},{"t":"Transportation and Public Utilities","lv":3,"p":[78,81],"pp":"64","s":"Passenger rail rate raised to 1 yen per kilometer on 15 January, gasoline shortage, 116 marine disasters in 1946, four lighthouses finished, power generation down to 13.6 million kWh."},{"t":"Communications","lv":3,"p":[82,84],"pp":"68","s":"Mail extended to all countries on 14 January, postal savings re-registered under Korean names, 1,607 items of equipment ordered from Japan, JODK to resume 50-kilowatt output."},{"t":"Section 4. Rationing and Price Control","lv":2,"p":[85,90],"pp":"71","s":"Rice collections at 78 percent of quota, 69,417 tons of imported staples distributed in December, 43,900 tons of coal allocated, price ceilings, 146,000 U.S. light bulbs."},{"t":"Part IV: Social","lv":1,"p":[91,107],"pp":"77","s":"Refugee relief and repatriation, typhus and typhoid outbreaks, Winter Institute and Youth Movement, returning missionaries, information campaigns and press reaction to trusteeship; ends with a map of Korea."},{"t":"Section 1. Public Health and Welfare","lv":2,"p":[93,98],"pp":"79","s":"183 relief boxes distributed, 4,624 refugee houses built, 10,036 Japanese repatriated from North Korea, smallpox vaccine output, typhus cases up to 647, typhoid 457, narcotics drive."},{"t":"Section 2. Education, Culture and Religion","lv":2,"p":[99,102],"pp":"85","s":"Four-week Winter Institute from 6 January, first Korean-language science textbook, Youth Movement with 19,270 members, scientists' conference of 30-31 January, returning missionaries."},{"t":"Section 3. Public Information","lv":2,"p":[103,107],"pp":"89","s":"Rice collection pamphlets and Mobile Education Train, 'Cholera' film, radio programs, newspaper reaction to the trusteeship resolution and Coalition Committee, plus map of Korea."},{"t":"Back matter","lv":1,"p":[108,116],"pp":"","s":"Library due-date slip, blank digitization pages and rear cover of the University of Virginia copy."}],"by":"claude-sonnet (Claude Code subagent)"},{"id":"J-17","ia":"summation-non-military-activities-japan-no-17-1947-02","s":"J","no":17,"d":"1947-02","d2":"","t":"Summation of Non-Military Activities in Japan, No. 17 (February 1947)","v":"","pg":139,"o":"Summation No. 17 covers February 1947, a month dominated by Japanese politics and economic strain. General MacArthur's 20 February message to Congress defended occupation appropriations and food relief, and on 6 February he urged Prime Minister Yoshida to hold a new general election, which the Government scheduled for 5-30 April ahead of the new Constitution taking effect on 3 May. The report details failed coalition talks with the Social Democrats, Liberal-Progressive merger manoeuvres, labor politics after the cancelled 1 February general strike, and the 92nd Diet reconvening on 14 February. Economic sections give January figures showing coal, power and raw-material shortages depressing production, a 20 February plan to raise government wages to an average of 1,200 yen, rising inflation, the capital levy and a 10 billion yen supplemental budget. Social sections cover repatriation, relief, disease statistics, school reform and war crimes trials, including the opening of the defense case before the International Military Tribunal. Dense statistical tables and charts make it a useful reference for historians.","h":["On 20 February MacArthur sent Congress a message arguing that victory required a Japanese 'spiritual reformation' and that appropriated relief food was not charity, citing Bataan and the cost of starvation and unrest.","On 6 February MacArthur urged Prime Minister Yoshida to hold a new general election; the Government set 5 April for executives, 20 April for the House of Councillors, 25 April for the House of Representatives, 30 April for local assemblies.","Four coalition attempts involving the Social Democrats failed, their Central Executive Committee refusing on 14 February; Shidehara proposed a Liberal-Progressive merger on 19 February amid six Liberal and four Progressive factions.","After the 1 February general strike was called off, government wages were to rise to an average of 1,200 yen a month (announced 20 February); union membership stood at 4,415,482 in 17,163 unions.","January coal output was 2,009,400 metric tons, with a Government five-point plan aimed at a 30,000,000-ton 1947-48 goal; power generation rose 1.7 percent to 2,518,000,000 kWh but shortages stayed severe.","A train overturned near Hachioji on 25 February, killing 178 and injuring about 800; Bank of Japan notes in circulation reached 105,489,919,000 yen and capital levy collection began 15 February.","Twelve war crimes trials with 32 defendants finished in Japan with eight death sentences, 977 suspects were held in Sugamo, and the defense opened its case before the International Military Tribunal on 25 February.","Repatriation brought 95,644 Japanese home from 27 January to the end of February, with 1,273,278 still awaiting return from Soviet-controlled areas; January births totaled 275,221 and deaths 117,489."],"k":["Supreme Commander's message to Congress","general election 1947","Yoshida Cabinet","coalition and merger movements","Social Democratic Party","labor unions and general strike","92nd Diet","coal production","inflation and wages","capital levy","war crimes trials","International Military Tribunal","repatriation","textile industry","public health statistics"],"toc":[{"t":"Front matter","lv":1,"p":[1,4],"pp":"","s":"Cover for Summation No. 17 (February 1947), table of contents for Parts I-IV, and the list of 106 charts, maps and graphs."},{"t":"Part I: General","lv":1,"p":[5,13],"pp":"1","s":"Monthly digest: no SCAP reorganization, MacArthur's 20 February message to Congress and 6 February election letter, January economic indicators, and social items on relief, repatriation and education."},{"t":"Section 1. Organization Under SCAP","lv":2,"p":[6,6],"pp":"3","s":"No change occurred in the non-military organization of General Headquarters, SCAP, during February."},{"t":"Section 2. Political Activities","lv":2,"p":[6,7],"pp":"3","s":"MacArthur's 20 February message to Congress and 6 February election letter, the reorganized Yoshida Cabinet, the reconvened Diet, the Hachioji train wreck and war crimes trials."},{"t":"Section 3. Economic Activities","lv":2,"p":[8,10],"pp":"7","s":"January indicators: coal and steel gains, but power shortages cut chemicals to 30 percent of minimum needs; textiles declined; 1,200-yen government wage plan, 105 billion yen in notes, 10 billion yen budget."},{"t":"Section 4. Social Activities","lv":2,"p":[11,13],"pp":"13","s":"Relief to 2,891,336 persons, 95,644 repatriated, new Education Minister, women admitted to higher schools, Shinto directives, popular radio programs and record SCAP library attendance."},{"t":"Part II: Political","lv":1,"p":[14,39],"pp":"17","s":"Government and party politics around the Yoshida Cabinet and elections, public safety statistics, Japanese courts, and war crimes prosecutions including the opening of the defense at the Tokyo Tribunal."},{"t":"Section 1. Government and Politics","lv":2,"p":[14,28],"pp":"19","s":"MacArthur's Congress message with press comment, local government reform, failed coalition and merger moves, inflation, labor politics, the 92nd Diet and the April 1947 election schedule."},{"t":"Supreme Commander's Message to Congress","lv":3,"p":[14,16],"pp":"19","s":"Full text of MacArthur's message urging 'complete spiritual reformation' and relief funds, plus Nihon Keizai, Mainichi and Asahi editorials of 26-27 February."},{"t":"Local Government Reform","lv":3,"p":[17,17],"pp":"","s":"Thirteen planned changes under the new Constitution: popularly responsible governors, assembly powers over budgets and appointments, initiative, referendum and recall, and nonpartisan election committees."},{"t":"Political Developments","lv":3,"p":[17,21],"pp":"","s":"Cool reception of the revised Cabinet, four failed coalition proposals, six Liberal and four Progressive factions, merger plans, and the Japan Farmers' Party formed 21 February."},{"t":"Inflation and Politics","lv":3,"p":[21,22],"pp":"","s":"Social Democrats' eight-point anti-inflation program of 5 February rejected 'Ishibashi inflation' and was adopted by both wings of the party."},{"t":"Labor and Politics","lv":3,"p":[22,25],"pp":"","s":"Prewar labor parties, Matsuoka's re-election as head of the General Federation of Trade Unions, the NCIU, Social Democrats, Communists, and the Farmers' Union split of 12 February."},{"t":"The Diet","lv":3,"p":[25,27],"pp":"","s":"The 92nd Diet reconvened 14 February with Liberals holding 148 seats; Tokyo papers called Yoshida's opening speech on coal, controls and labor 'abstract'."},{"t":"General Elections","lv":3,"p":[27,28],"pp":"","s":"MacArthur's 6 February letter, party and union responses, the 17 February schedule of elections from 5 to 30 April, and Asahi and Yomiuri comment."},{"t":"Section 2. Public Safety","lv":2,"p":[29,32],"pp":"49","s":"Osaka black-market raids led to 5,215 arrests; 135,909 offenses in December; Hachioji train wreck killed 178; also radio patrol cars, juvenile delinquency, prison surveys and fires."},{"t":"Section 3. Legal and War Crimes","lv":2,"p":[33,39],"pp":"57","s":"Judicial appointments of 7 February, occupation courts, 977 suspects in Sugamo, trials in Japan and the Philippines, and the Tokyo Tribunal defense's opening statement of 25-26 February."},{"t":"Legal Affairs and Japanese Judicial Administration","lv":3,"p":[33,33],"pp":"","s":"Chinese representatives in provost courts; on 7 February Hosono became Supreme Court President, Tanimura Vice-minister of Justice and Kimura Prosecutor-general."},{"t":"Military Occupation Courts","lv":3,"p":[33,34],"pp":"","s":"Thefts of U.S. goods, impersonation of Occupation personnel and aiding deserters were tried; Eighth Army courts handled 1,137 cases from 21 December to 20 January."},{"t":"Investigation, Apprehension and Prosecution of War Criminals","lv":3,"p":[34,36],"pp":"","s":"Eight apprehension memorandums, 977 held in Sugamo, 571 Japanese held in the Philippines, 148 defendants tried in Japan to date, and British trials with 30 death sentences."},{"t":"International Prosecution of War Criminals","lv":3,"p":[36,39],"pp":"","s":"Tribunal denied dismissal motions on 3 February and reconvened 24 February; the defense opening covered Manchuria, China, the USSR and the Pacific War, arguing self-defense."},{"t":"Part III: Economic","lv":1,"p":[40,123],"pp":"69","s":"January production, trade, labor, price and finance data: coal, power and material shortages limited industry, while trade, rationing, the capital levy and property control advanced."},{"t":"Section 1. Agriculture and Fisheries","lv":2,"p":[40,42],"pp":"71","s":"1946 crop review citing fertilizer shortage (75,400 of 380,000 tons nitrate), land commissions, soil survey, January fish landings of 88,608 tons, and whaling and fishing fuel controls."},{"t":"Section 2. Forestry and Mining","lv":2,"p":[43,48],"pp":"77","s":"January logs at 453,040,000 board feet, coal output 2,009,400 tons with a five-point plan for a 30,000,000-ton goal, lignite shortfall, oil drilling and mineral production."},{"t":"Forestry","lv":3,"p":[43,44],"pp":"","s":"Log production rose 21,240,000 board feet to 453,040,000; stockpiles reached 1,610,120,000 board feet; 19,339 sawmills operated; firewood and charcoal figures."},{"t":"Mining","lv":3,"p":[45,48],"pp":"","s":"Coal, lignite, coal consumption by industry, petroleum drilling, and December mineral and metal output, with antimony the only significant gain."},{"t":"Section 3. Heavy Industries","lv":2,"p":[49,57],"pp":"99","s":"Coke 104,500 tons, aluminum 1,024 tons, cement 73,954 tons; chemical output at 30 percent of minimum needs, machinery down 25 percent, housing starts and textile machinery gains."},{"t":"Section 4. Manufacturing","lv":2,"p":[58,68],"pp":"107","s":"Food processing, pulp and paper, glass, ceramics, electrical goods, vehicles, 767 tons of crude rubber, leather and farm tools, mostly curtailed by power shortages."},{"t":"Section 5. Textile Industries","lv":2,"p":[69,81],"pp":"129","s":"Yarn output fell with holidays and power limits; mills received 34,332,000 pounds of cotton, 818,614 bales shipped, silk fabrics down to 1,458,000 square yards, rayon staple 684,000 pounds."},{"t":"Section 6. Transportation and Public Utilities","lv":2,"p":[82,85],"pp":"155","s":"Motor vehicles up to 115,587, rail freight down 417,200 tons, power generation 2,518,000,000 kWh (up 1.7 percent) with coal deliveries 66 percent short, gas at 23 percent of capacity."},{"t":"Section 7. Communications","lv":2,"p":[86,92],"pp":"163","s":"Telephones up one percent, 123,000 new radio licenses, postal money orders up 43 percent, Kyoto and Kobe exchange rehabilitation, and the 19 February freeze of International Telecommunications Company assets."},{"t":"Section 8. Labor","lv":2,"p":[93,101],"pp":"177","s":"Union membership 4,415,482, Coal Industry Reconstruction Council of 5 February, 2,000 collective agreements, strike statistics, 20 February wage rise plan and employment programs."},{"t":"Labor Legislation and Organizations","lv":3,"p":[93,95],"pp":"","s":"Labor Standards and other bills before the Diet, union statistics, the Economic Reconstruction Council, federation politics and the Kanto Employers' Association's principles."},{"t":"Labor Relations","lv":3,"p":[95,98],"pp":"","s":"Tokyo Labor Relations Committee elections of 12 February, tax collectors' agreement of 6 February, 101 disputes in December, and the Supreme Court ruling in the Toyo Watch case."},{"t":"Labor Education, Wages and Employment","lv":3,"p":[98,101],"pp":"","s":"Tokyo Imperial University labor lectures, price rises eroding wages, government wage raise to 1,200 yen, railway hours agreement, labor census, 1,991 handicraft centers and coal-mine schools."},{"t":"Section 9. Imports and Exports","lv":2,"p":[102,109],"pp":"195","s":"January trade: tea, silk goods and bamboo to the United States, textile machinery and chemicals to Korea, wheat, cotton and sugar imports, plus a 1946 review covering 10 countries."},{"t":"Section 10. Rationing and Price Control","lv":2,"p":[110,115],"pp":"211","s":"Staple food collections of 3,264,000 tons, 87,500 tons of imported food released, vegetable subsidy ended, fuel gas and tram fares raised, 48 critical items rationed."},{"t":"Section 11. Finance","lv":2,"p":[116,119],"pp":"223","s":"Notes in circulation 105,489,919,000 yen, capital levy from 15 February, 10 billion yen bond issue, national debt of 263,855,000,000 yen, and foreign exchange surrender extended to 31 January."},{"t":"Section 12. Property Control and Reparations","lv":2,"p":[120,123],"pp":"231","s":"Looted property valued at 6,397,012 yen in storage, approved loans to textile and steel firms, holding company liquidation and reparations delegations in Japan."},{"t":"Property Control","lv":3,"p":[120,122],"pp":"","s":"German properties, looted goods, firms in liquidation, dissolved organizations, precious metals, the SCAP yen custody account, four new claims and releases from custody."},{"t":"Antitrust, Cartels and Reparations","lv":3,"p":[122,123],"pp":"","s":"Credit approvals including 3,133,170,000 yen for 17 fertilizer firms, Holding Company Liquidation Commission instructions of 16 January, and reparations delegations from Australia, China and the Netherlands."},{"t":"Part IV: Social","lv":1,"p":[124,139],"pp":"239","s":"Public health, welfare, veterinary and disease statistics, then education, religion, information programs, press and media, ending with a map of Japan."},{"t":"Section 1. Public Health and Welfare","lv":2,"p":[125,133],"pp":"241","s":"Relief to 2,891,336 persons, 95,644 repatriated, Red Cross budget, meat and milk inspection, penicillin supply, narcotics, communicable disease and January vital statistics."},{"t":"Welfare and Repatriation","lv":3,"p":[125,127],"pp":"","s":"Relief payments of 160,232,312 yen, repatriate reception centers, Japanese Red Cross proposed budget, social security and hospital administration."},{"t":"Veterinary, Dental and Nursing Affairs","lv":3,"p":[127,128],"pp":"","s":"Meat and milk inspection figures, animal disease reports, rehabilitation of bombed-out dentists and the public health nurses' course."},{"t":"Supply","lv":3,"p":[128,130],"pp":"","s":"Simplified penicillin extraction, medical and dental supply production, biologicals, distribution list cut from 201 to 124 items, and narcotics controls."},{"t":"Preventive Medicine and Vital Statistics","lv":3,"p":[130,133],"pp":"","s":"Dysentery, typhoid, smallpox, typhus, malaria and cholera reports, communicable diseases by prefecture, and January births of 275,221 and deaths of 117,489."},{"t":"Section 2. Education, Religion and Media of Expression","lv":2,"p":[134,139],"pp":"259","s":"New Education Minister Takahashi, women admitted to higher schools, Shinto directives, election and women's information programs, radio, press comment, film attendance of 804,375 and library records."},{"t":"Education, Religion and Arts","lv":3,"p":[134,135],"pp":"","s":"New higher-school entrance plan, Parent-Teacher Association pamphlet, 6-3-3-4 school handbook, Shinto separation rules, missionaries and inventories of private art collections."},{"t":"Media of Expression","lv":3,"p":[135,139],"pp":"","s":"Information programs on women, health, labor and the Constitution, radio shows, press comment, kabuki, educational films and 639 daily library visitors."}],"by":"claude-sonnet (Claude Code subagent)"},{"id":"K-17","ia":"summation-usamg-activities-korea-no-17-1947-02","s":"K","no":17,"d":"1947-02","d2":"","t":"Summation of United States Army Military Government Activities in Korea, No. 17 (February 1947)","v":"","pg":108,"o":"Summation No. 17 covers South Korea under USAMGIK for February 1947. The central political event is the nomination (6 Feb) and swearing-in (10 Feb) of Ahn Chai Hong as the first Korean Civil Administrator, part of Hodge's policy of transferring administration to Koreans. The Interim Legislative Assembly dealt with the Antitrusteeship Resolution, and the Chief U.S. Commissioner issued four statements on the Moscow Decision and trusteeship, followed by Hodge's 15 February appeal to end agitation. Student strikes closed Seoul National University classes taught by Americans. Economic sections report 1947 crop goals, coal and paper shortages, a first fall in Bank of Chosen notes, new international postal service, rice collection at 83 percent of quota, and the new Services of Civilian Supply. Social sections cover refugee feeding, typhus and typhoid, Red Cross reorganization and press comment. The charts and tables give historians quantitative series.","h":["On 6 February the Military Governor nominated Ahn Chai Hong as first Korean Civil Administrator; the Legislative Assembly confirmed him and he took the oath on 10 February 1947 after resigning his Assembly seat.","The Chief U.S. Commissioner issued four statements on the Moscow Decision (Joint Commission functions, procedure, Provisional Government, trusteeship), and Hodge on 15 February urged Koreans to end antitrusteeship agitation delaying independence.","The Legislative Assembly defeated, 49 to none with 10 abstentions, a proposal to use Japanese technicians in industry; the Military Governor's letters listed 11 matters for action, including a franchise law and land reform.","On 14 February the Military Governor withdrew American instructors from Seoul National University after student strikes; middle-school students in Seoul, Inchon, Suwon and Kunsan struck in sympathy from 10 February.","Bank of Chosen notes outstanding fell by 588,417,315 yen to 17,689,196,182.90 yen on 28 February, the first reversal of the rise begun in 1945; 83 percent of the rice quota had been collected.","The Services of Civilian Supply replaced the Office of the Coordinator of Civilian Supply to plan and handle all civilian supply imports into Korea.","Typhus rose from 147 to 271 cases and typhoid reached 659, mostly in Kyongsang-pukto; 256 people were bitten in the Cheju-do rabies outbreak, with 17 deaths.","The Korean Coast Guard Academy opened at Chinhae on 7 February and the cutter Admiral Lee, the first steel ship built in Korea since liberation, was christened."],"k":["Ahn Chai Hong","Civil Administrator","Korean Interim Legislative Assembly","Joint Commission","Moscow Decision","trusteeship","Services of Civilian Supply","rice collection","Seoul National University strikes","Korean Coast Guard","typhus and typhoid","coal shortage","Bank of Chosen notes","postal service","Korean Red Cross"],"toc":[{"t":"Front matter","lv":1,"p":[1,14],"pp":"","s":"Library covers, title page for Number 17 (February 1947), table of contents, and a list of 37 charts plus a map of Korea."},{"t":"Part I: General","lv":1,"p":[15,22],"pp":"1","s":"Overview: Ahn Chai Hong named Civil Administrator, Services of Civilian Supply created, 533,929 tons of rice collected, student strikes and refugee feeding."},{"t":"Section 1. Political Activities","lv":2,"p":[17,18],"pp":"3","s":"Ahn Chai Hong sworn in 10 February; Assembly worked on the Antitrusteeship Resolution; Joint Commission reports issued; public order calm; Coast Guard Academy opened 7 February."},{"t":"Section 2. Economic Activities","lv":2,"p":[19,20],"pp":"5","s":"Nine-crop 1947 estimate of 133,365,911 bushels; coal and cement stoppages; Bank of Chosen notes at 17.69 billion yen; new international mail; 533,929 tons of rice collected."},{"t":"Section 3. Social Activities","lv":2,"p":[21,22],"pp":"7","s":"Refugee feeding at Pusan and Chinhae, Red Cross reorganization, typhus rise to 271, Seoul University strikes, and press focus on antitrusteeship."},{"t":"Part II: Political","lv":1,"p":[23,42],"pp":"9","s":"Government and politics (Civil Administrator, Assembly, Joint Commission statements, Chinese representative Liu Yu-wen) and public safety (police chiefs' conference, prisons, Coast Guard, Constabulary)."},{"t":"Section 1. Government and Politics","lv":2,"p":[25,38],"pp":"11","s":"Ahn Chai Hong's appointment and statement, Assembly business, four Joint Commission reports on the Moscow Decision, Hodge's 15 February statement, and arrival of Chinese representative Liu Yu-wen."},{"t":"Administration","lv":3,"p":[25,26],"pp":"11","s":"Ahn Chai Hong nominated 6 February and sworn in 10 February; his policy statement; new census in Kyonggi-do and Seoul; Services of Civilian Supply created."},{"t":"Korean Interim Legislative Assembly","lv":3,"p":[26,28],"pp":"12","s":"Antitrusteeship Resolution; 49-0 defeat of the Japanese-technicians proposal; Military Governor's letters on rice rumors, Seoul National University and 11 pending matters; budget and Red Cross."},{"t":"U. S.-U. S. S. R. Joint Commission","lv":3,"p":[28,37],"pp":"14","s":"Hodge's 5 February statement, four Chief Commissioner reports on Moscow Decision phases, Provisional Government and trusteeship, then Hodge's 15 February appeal against disorder."},{"t":"Foreign Affairs","lv":3,"p":[37,37],"pp":"23","s":"Liu Yu-wen, official Chinese Government representative, arrived in Seoul on 5 February and took up quarters in the former Chinese Legation."},{"t":"Section 2. Public Safety","lv":2,"p":[39,42],"pp":"25","s":"No disorders in February; first police chiefs' conference 7-8 February; 7,505 crimes in December; Taegu and Pusan prison camps; Coast Guard Academy, cutter Admiral Lee and Pohang base."},{"t":"Part III: Economic","lv":1,"p":[43,80],"pp":"29","s":"Natural resources, industry, commerce, and rationing and price control, with charts on production, finance, trade, mail, food stocks and prices."},{"t":"Section 1. Natural Resources","lv":2,"p":[45,50],"pp":"31","s":"1947 target of 86.6 million bushels of paddy rice; December fish landings of 33,609 tons; January coal output of 17,000 tons; tungsten 424 tons in 1946."},{"t":"Section 2. Industry","lv":2,"p":[51,60],"pp":"37","s":"Raw-material shortages closed the Inchon fertilizer plant and beer breweries; paper output 433,105 pounds; sericulture conference; 775 operating factories with 43,868 workers; wage indexes."},{"t":"Section 3. Commerce","lv":2,"p":[61,72],"pp":"47","s":"Currency fell 588 million yen; Monopoly Bureau; 122 property allocations; Hongkong liaison office; electricity use of 30.9 million kWh; new international mail rates and postal data."},{"t":"Section 4. Rationing and Price Control","lv":2,"p":[73,80],"pp":"59","s":"Services of Civilian Supply replaced the Coordinator's office; 83 percent of rice quota collected; food allocations, coal and petroleum, metal prices; 2,771 price violations."},{"t":"Part IV: Social","lv":1,"p":[81,108],"pp":"67","s":"Public health and welfare, education, culture and religion, and public information, ending with a map of Korea and blank scan pages."},{"t":"Section 1. Public Health and Welfare","lv":2,"p":[83,90],"pp":"69","s":"Refugee rations for 12,475 at Pusan; Red Cross plan with 60 incorporators; 356 Japanese repatriated; nursing schools with 610 students; typhus 271, typhoid 659; cholera program for 1 April."},{"t":"Section 2. Education, Culture and Religion","lv":2,"p":[91,94],"pp":"77","s":"American instructors removed from Seoul National University 14 February; new Board of Regents plan; middle-school strikes; Language Purification Committee; 20,808 Youth Movement members."},{"t":"Section 3. Public Information","lv":2,"p":[95,108],"pp":"81","s":"Rice-collection publicity, new Assembly radio program, Military Governor's 21 February rebuke of Dok Lib Sin Bo, press comment on antitrusteeship groups and school strikes; map of Korea."}],"by":"claude-sonnet (Claude Code subagent)"},{"id":"J-18","ia":"summation-non-military-activities-japan-no-18-1947-03","s":"J","no":18,"d":"1947-03","d2":"","t":"Summation of Non-Military Activities in Japan, No. 18 (March 1947)","v":"","pg":144,"o":"Summation No. 18 covers March 1947, the month before the first national and local elections under the new Constitution. MacArthur told foreign correspondents on 17 March that the United Nations should take over the occupation after a peace treaty, and on 22 March sent Prime Minister Yoshida a letter demanding firm Japanese action on food collection, rationing, wages, prices and inflation. The 92nd Diet adjourned on 31 March after passing the Fundamental Law of Education, School Education Law, Labor Standards Law, Local Autonomy Law, new tax laws, a revised House of Representatives election law and an anti-monopoly (Fair Trade) law, while the Progressive Party and Liberal defectors merged into the Japan Democratic Party. The IMTFE defence closed its general phase and opened the Manchurian phase. Economic sections report coal-limited production, a new rice-delivery bonus scheme, labor federations uniting, and extensive statistical tables on industry, finance, health and education. Historians will find election platforms, legislative tables, war-crimes trial results and detailed production figures useful.","h":["On 17 March MacArthur told Tokyo foreign press that the political and military phases of the occupation were substantially complete and that the United Nations should assume responsibility after a peace treaty, using 'protective rather than repressive' controls.","MacArthur's 22 March letter to Yoshida invoked Directive No. 3 of 22 September 1945, warned that the Allied powers had no obligation to import food to cover Japanese maldistribution, and called for integrated economic controls through the Economic Stabilization Board.","The Japan Democratic Party formed on 31 March with 145 Diet members (114 Progressives, 17 People's Cooperatives, eight Liberals, six independents), overtaking the Liberals' 142; the People's Cooperative Party had formed on 8 March.","The House of Representatives election law was revised on 30-31 March: 117 districts replaced 53, each voter casts one vote for three to five seats, and candidate deposits rose from 2,000 to 5,000 yen, after a filibuster and scuffle on 27 March.","The 92nd Diet adjourned 31 March having passed 77 government measures and 10 member bills, including the Labor Standards Law (27 March), Fundamental Law of Education, Local Autonomy Law (28 March), Securities and Exchange Law and Fair Trade Commission law.","A new rice bonus plan paid 150 yen per koku above 80 percent of quota delivered by 31 March and 300 yen per koku over quota by 30 April; by 10 March about 85 percent of farmers had met quotas, and tonari-gumi food distribution ended 1 April.","Six war-crimes trials involving 12 defendants ended in Japan with sentences up to 40 years; seven Philippine trials brought totals to 196 tried and 181 convicted, and 131 more suspects were ordered apprehended for Sugamo.","Bank of Japan notes in circulation reached 115,726,000,000 yen on 31 March; 1947-48 budgets of 114.5 billion yen (general account) and 124.9 billion (special accounts) were authorized, with income tax moved to pay-as-you-go and self-assessment."],"k":["MacArthur","United Nations control","peace treaty","Yoshida","Economic Stabilization Board","April 1947 elections","Japan Democratic Party","92nd Diet","Labor Standards Law","Fundamental Law of Education","rice collection bonuses","war crimes trials","IMTFE","land reform","coal production","Fair Trade Commission"],"toc":[{"t":"Front matter","lv":1,"p":[1,4],"pp":"","s":"Cover of Summation No. 18 for March 1947, contents for Parts I-IV, and the list of 97 charts, maps and graphs."},{"t":"Part I: General","lv":1,"p":[5,13],"pp":"1","s":"Monthly digest: no change in SCAP organization, MacArthur's UN statement, the new Democratic Party, Diet adjournment, economic output, labor laws, finance and social developments."},{"t":"Section 1. Organization Under SCAP","lv":2,"p":[6,6],"pp":"3","s":"States there was no change in the non-military organization of GHQ SCAP during March 1947."},{"t":"Section 2. Political Activities","lv":2,"p":[6,7],"pp":"3","s":"MacArthur's 22 March letter to Yoshida, formation of the People's Cooperative and Japan Democratic parties, 92nd Diet adjournment on 31 March, and Takase named Economic Stabilization Board head."},{"t":"Section 3. Economic Activities","lv":2,"p":[8,10],"pp":"7","s":"Natural resources, industry, labor, trade, rationing, finance and property control, including 28,000 houses completed in February and a 22 March release of 173,561 pairs of shoes."},{"t":"Section 4. Social Activities","lv":2,"p":[11,13],"pp":"13","s":"Meningitis rose to 282 cases; 64,898 repatriates; Fundamental Law of Education, shrine land law, election publicity and 581,207 educational film viewers."},{"t":"Part II: Political","lv":1,"p":[14,36],"pp":"19","s":"MacArthur's statements, the election campaign and new parties, Diet legislation, local government reform, public safety, and war-crimes proceedings including the IMTFE defence phases."},{"t":"Section 1. Government and Politics","lv":2,"p":[15,25],"pp":"21","s":"MacArthur-Yoshida letters, platforms for the April elections, the Democratic Party's formation, election law revision, 92nd Diet legislation tables, the purge amendment and local government law."},{"t":"Supreme Commander Advocates United Nations Control","lv":3,"p":[15,16],"pp":"21","s":"MacArthur's 17 March luncheon remarks urging UN supervision after a peace treaty, with Yoshida's praise and Asahi and Mainichi editorials."},{"t":"Economic Reforms: MacArthur-Yoshida Letters","lv":3,"p":[17,18],"pp":"24","s":"MacArthur's 22 March letter citing Directive No. 3; Yoshida's six-point reply promising 110 percent rice collection and 30,000,000 tons of coal; party reactions."},{"t":"Election Campaign","lv":3,"p":[19,19],"pp":"29","s":"Liberal, Progressive and Communist platforms, a joint emergency program, and legal limits on campaign spending, postcards and posters for the April elections."},{"t":"New Political Parties","lv":3,"p":[20,21],"pp":"30","s":"People's Cooperative Party formed 8 March, Workers' Vanguard Party 9 March, and Japan Democratic Party 31 March with 145 Diet members."},{"t":"The Diet","lv":3,"p":[21,24],"pp":"32","s":"Election law revision creating 117 districts, the 27 March scuffle, and tables of government and member bills passed by the 92nd Diet."},{"t":"The Purge","lv":3,"p":[24,25],"pp":"39","s":"Imperial Ordinance No. 1 of 1947 amended to bar purgees from campaigning, with 72-hour screening decisions; a party committee formed 14 March to help administer the purge."},{"t":"The Central Government","lv":3,"p":[25,25],"pp":"40","s":"Communications Ministry graft dispute involving Minister Hitotsumatsu and 300 resignations, Ushio's State Planning post, and Takase's appointment after Wada declined."},{"t":"Local Government","lv":3,"p":[25,26],"pp":"41","s":"Law of Local Government enacted 28 March, merging four laws into 300 articles, ending the Home Minister's power to dismiss governors and adding initiative and referendum."},{"t":"Section 2. Public Safety","lv":2,"p":[26,27],"pp":"43","s":"Fewer labor demonstrations, black-market crackdown, 111,328 offenses in January, 970 railway suicides, amnesty effects on 38,115 prisoners, and January fire losses of 302,966,754 yen."},{"t":"Section 3. Legal and War Crimes","lv":2,"p":[28,36],"pp":"47","s":"Chinese nationals' status, 161 new atrocity cases, 131 suspects to be apprehended, trial results in Japan and the Philippines, and IMTFE defence witnesses and the Manchurian phase."},{"t":"International Prosecution of War Criminals","lv":3,"p":[30,36],"pp":"51","s":"Defence closed its general phase on 18 March with treaties and witnesses Fujita, Okada, Mitarai and Tamura, then opened the Manchurian phase with the Lytton Report on 19 March."},{"t":"Part III: Economic","lv":1,"p":[37,123],"pp":"65","s":"Production, trade, labor, rationing, finance and property statistics for February-March 1947, dominated by coal, power and raw-material shortages and the rice collection drive."},{"t":"Section 1. Agriculture and Fisheries","lv":2,"p":[38,41],"pp":"67","s":"Land commission elections 20-25 February, 442 recall elections, rice bonus scheme, February fish landings of 75,227 tons, Antarctic whaling, and game laws revised effective 15 April."},{"t":"Section 2. Forestry and Mining","lv":2,"p":[42,47],"pp":"75","s":"February log output of 685,144,000 board feet, 20,279 sawmills, reforestation of 517,000 acres, and February coal output of 2,028,700 tons."},{"t":"Section 3. Heavy Industries","lv":2,"p":[48,55],"pp":"87","s":"February coke of 106,400 tons, iron and steel hit by short coal deliveries, aluminum 863 tons, cement, record housing construction, and new technical research committees."},{"t":"Section 4. Manufacturing","lv":2,"p":[56,65],"pp":"103","s":"Pulp output down 1,681 tons, camera output up 200 percent, plus food processing, paper, glass, medicines, electrical goods, rubber and leather; agricultural equipment limited by steel plate shortage."},{"t":"Section 5. Textile Industries","lv":2,"p":[66,75],"pp":"123","s":"February cotton yarn of 25,041,000 pounds with 55 of 59 mills meeting quotas, raw silk 8,252 bales, and wool yarn limited by power."},{"t":"Section 6. Transportation and Public Utilities","lv":2,"p":[76,79],"pp":"143","s":"Motor vehicles fell to 112,155, railway freight reached 7,567,600 tons, and power generated was 2,010,305,000 kWh despite a hydro drop."},{"t":"Section 7. Communications","lv":2,"p":[80,88],"pp":"151","s":"Order of 25 March to liquidate the International Telecommunications Company, accrual accounting law, telephone and telegraph rate rises, radio licensees, and postal services."},{"t":"Section 8. Labor","lv":2,"p":[89,98],"pp":"169","s":"Labor Standards Law enacted 27 March, National Liaison Council of unions formed 10 March, government workers' dispute settled 27 February, plus labor relations committees, wages and employment."},{"t":"Section 9. Imports and Exports","lv":2,"p":[99,102],"pp":"189","s":"February imports included about 50,000 tons of salt; first exports of cloth woven from imported cotton went to Hongkong and the Netherlands East Indies."},{"t":"Section 10. Rationing and Price Control","lv":2,"p":[103,110],"pp":"197","s":"Government held 69.9 days of staple food on 28 February; 3,686,633 tons of rice collected under a 110 percent collection order; fertilizer, coal, lumber and cement allocation; price violations."},{"t":"Section 11. Finance","lv":2,"p":[111,117],"pp":"213","s":"Bank of Japan notes of 115,726,000,000 yen, six new tax laws including pay-as-you-go income tax, and 1947-48 budgets passed by the Diet."},{"t":"Section 12. Property Control and Reparations","lv":2,"p":[118,123],"pp":"227","s":"Restitution directives for foreign-owned properties, 914 reports on overseas Japanese corporations, British property analysis, dissolved ultranationalist organizations and precious metals held by SCAP."},{"t":"Part IV: Social","lv":1,"p":[124,143],"pp":"239","s":"Public health, welfare, repatriation, vital statistics, education reform, religion and media, including the Fundamental Law of Education and election publicity."},{"t":"Section 1. Public Health and Welfare","lv":2,"p":[125,133],"pp":"241","s":"February nutrition survey showing 1,921 calories in Tokyo, 282 meningitis cases, DDT production, welfare aid to 2,701,670, and a tuberculosis plan, narcotics reregistration and repatriation."},{"t":"Section 2. Education, Religion and Media of Expression","lv":2,"p":[134,143],"pp":"259","s":"Fundamental Law of Education (25 March), new curriculums, shrine and temple land law, removal of militaristic monuments, and election information programs."},{"t":"Map of Japan","lv":1,"p":[144,144],"pp":"279","s":"Closing map of Japan with prefectural boundaries."}],"by":"claude-sonnet (Claude Code subagent)"},{"id":"K-18","ia":"summation-usamg-activities-korea-no-18-1947-03","s":"K","no":18,"d":"1947-03","d2":"","t":"Summation of United States Army Military Government Activities in Korea, No. 18 (March 1947)","v":"","pg":92,"o":"Summation No. 18 covers March 1947 in South Korea under USAMGIK. Politically it records the Military Governor's pledge of no government reorganization without Korean consent, the Interim Legislative Assembly's first reading of a rightist temporary constitution (11 March) and general franchise law (25 March), the Chungking group's 2 March claim to be the de jure government, and the final report of the Chief U.S. Commissioner on the Moscow Agreement. Public order centred on 1 March Independence Day clashes, Cheju-do strikes and the 22-23 March general strike called by the Chun Pyung Labor Union. Economic sections give coal, steel, textile, paper, banking, trade, rail, communications, ration and price data, including 30 American locomotives, new ration books and 30 percent coal price rises. Social sections cover typhoid, refugees, the Korean Red Cross, student strikes and press opinion. Useful for the transition toward Korean self-government and the strike wave.","h":["On 12 March the Military Governor announced that no reorganization of the South Korean Interim Government would occur without the agreement of a majority of the Korean Directorate under Civil Administrator Ahn Chai Hong, or an enacted Assembly law.","On 11 March Suh Sang Il and 54 rightist legislators presented a draft temporary constitution to the Interim Legislative Assembly; on 25 March Kim Pung Chung introduced a franchise law giving all citizens over 20 the vote, one representative per 150,000 persons.","On 2 March the former Chungking Provisional Government declared itself the de jure government, naming Syngman Rhee president and Kim Koo vice-president; Military Government declared the act illegal.","A general strike called by the Chun Pyung Labor Union on 22-23 March spread across South Korea, disrupting Seoul transport, printing and utilities; strikes lasted two to three days and disorder was kept minimal.","Thirty steam locomotives, the first of 100 loaned to the Department of Transportation, reached Pusan on 19 March; imports from Japan included 46,506 tons of coal and 236 trucks.","Typhoid cases rose from 659 in February to 1,409 in March, with 580 in Kyongsang-pukto; typhus fell to 208, and the Korean Red Cross received its charter on 16 March.","Rice collection ended 15 March with 650,646 kiloliters collected; 8,631 people were detained for non-compliance, of whom 6,339 were acquitted. New ration books were issued from 16 March, and coal dealer prices rose 1 April.","Korean National Police strength reached 26,386 in January and the February prison population rose to 17,431; the Chief U.S. Commissioner's final Joint Commission report defended the Moscow Agreement."],"k":["Interim Legislative Assembly","temporary constitution","general franchise law","Chungking Provisional Government","Joint Commission","general strike","Chun Pyung Labor Union","Ahn Chai Hong","Cheju-do","rice collection","ration books","Bank of Chosen notes","coal production","typhoid","Korean Red Cross","refugees"],"toc":[{"t":"Front matter","lv":1,"p":[1,12],"pp":"","s":"Covers, title pages for Summation No. 18 (March 1947), table of contents and list of 36 charts, maps and graphs, with Digitized by Google (University of Virginia) stamps."},{"t":"Part I: General","lv":1,"p":[13,20],"pp":"1","s":"Digest of the month: the Assembly's draft constitution and franchise law, the 22-23 March general strike, coal price rises, a typhoid increase and the founding of the Korean Red Cross."},{"t":"Section 1. Political Activities","lv":2,"p":[15,16],"pp":"3","s":"Fifty-five rightists presented a draft constitution on 11 March; a franchise law was reported 25 March. Also the Chungking Government's 2 March claim, the Joint Commission's final report and an Ordinance 118 amendment."},{"t":"Section 2. Economic Activities","lv":2,"p":[17,18],"pp":"5","s":"Fertilizer need of 330,463 tons, a February coal shortfall, 1946 cotton output, the 22 March general strike, 30 locomotives, quadrupled radio fees and new ration books."},{"t":"Section 3. Social Activities","lv":2,"p":[19,20],"pp":"7","s":"Typhoid rose to 1,409 cases; the Red Cross received its charter on 16 March. Also repatriation figures, 3,396 registered at Seoul National University, youth movement membership of 43,994 and radio programs."},{"t":"Part II: Political","lv":1,"p":[21,32],"pp":"9","s":"Government reorganization policy, Assembly business, strikes and Independence Day, police and prison figures, Constabulary and Coast Guard, and the Joint Commission's final report."},{"t":"Section 1. Government and Politics","lv":2,"p":[23,28],"pp":"11","s":"Military Governor's 12 March reorganization pledge, the Assembly's constitution and franchise law, Chungking claim, Women's League petition, Joint Commission's final report and observers sent to the Delhi conference."},{"t":"Administration","lv":3,"p":[23,24],"pp":"11","s":"Military Governor stated on 12 March that reorganization needs Directorate approval; civil-service examinations began, with 300 clerk applicants on 31 March; a statistics course started 24 March."},{"t":"Korean Interim Legislative Assembly","lv":3,"p":[24,26],"pp":"12","s":"Summer-grain collection request due 1 April, first act barring dual office, draft constitution of 11 March, franchise law of 25 March and Democratic Women's League demands."},{"t":"Political Developments","lv":3,"p":[26,27],"pp":"14","s":"Chungking Government's 2 March declaration naming Rhee and Kim Koo, the Cheju-do employee strike of 11-18 March, and a People's Front demand to punish collaborators."},{"t":"U.S.-U.S.S.R. Joint Commission","lv":3,"p":[27,28],"pp":"15","s":"Fifth and final report of the Chief U.S. Commissioner defending the Moscow Agreement and the word trusteeship, quoting General Hodge's appeal against agitation."},{"t":"Foreign Affairs","lv":3,"p":[28,28],"pp":"16","s":"Three Korean observers left 17 March for the Pan-Asiatic Conference at Delhi; the British Consulate General in Seoul was formally recognized on 27 March."},{"t":"Section 2. Legal and Public Safety","lv":2,"p":[29,32],"pp":"17","s":"Ordinances 131 and 133, a suffrage ordinance request, Independence Day and 22-23 March strikes, police strength of 26,386, a prison population of 17,431, and Constabulary and Coast Guard."},{"t":"Major Legislation and Legal","lv":3,"p":[29,29],"pp":"17","s":"Ordinance 131 (6 March) gives Assembly members immunity from arrest; Ordinance 133 (18 March) mandates raw silk inspection; a 31 March request seeks a suffrage ordinance if no law by 30 June."},{"t":"Public Safety","lv":3,"p":[29,32],"pp":"17","s":"Independence Day disorders on Cheju-do, with 20,000 at a meeting; strikes of 12 and 22-23 March; police strength table; 17,431 prisoners; Coast Guard seizures."},{"t":"Part III: Economic","lv":1,"p":[33,68],"pp":"21","s":"Natural resources, industry, commerce and rationing: coal at 44 percent of requirement, doubled steel output, 30 locomotives, falling Bank of Chosen notes, new ration books and coal price increases."},{"t":"Section 1. Natural Resources","lv":2,"p":[35,40],"pp":"23","s":"Agricultural society formed 29 March, 23,894 tons of meat, fat and oil planned, January fish landings of 26,922 tons, December lumber of 1,209,960 board feet, February coal of 21,366 tons."},{"t":"Agriculture","lv":3,"p":[35,36],"pp":"23","s":"Scientific Agriculture Society meeting, 44 kg of Japanese vegetable seed, Ginseng Growers' Association order revoked, and 21 irrigation projects adding 36,368 chungbo."},{"t":"Fisheries","lv":3,"p":[36,37],"pp":"24","s":"January landings fell to 26,922 tons, 378 tons of bamboo arrived at Pusan on 25 March, and the fishing fleet totals 38,742 vessels."},{"t":"Forestry","lv":3,"p":[37,37],"pp":"25","s":"December lumber fell 199,920 board feet from November; 15,000 seedlings for Planting Week from 5 April; fuelwood shipment hampered by poor roads."},{"t":"Mining","lv":3,"p":[38,40],"pp":"26","s":"February coal of 21,366 tons missed quota by 10,834; coal supplied 43.83 percent of need; Hwasun mine produced 5,022 tons; Han Il graphite and Moon Kyung iron mines transferred 1 April."},{"t":"Section 2. Industry","lv":2,"p":[41,50],"pp":"29","s":"Steel output doubled, paper fell to 282,914 pounds, 1946 cotton yarn reached 8,740,400 pounds, the 22 March strike occurred, and December saw 163 of 863 factories idle with 47,326 employees."},{"t":"Heavy Industries and Manufacturing","lv":3,"p":[41,45],"pp":"29","s":"Engineering tests from 17 March, flour at 87,513 bags, canned goods only 219 cases, paper collapse, first American light bulbs 16 March, and metric system adoption."},{"t":"Textiles","lv":3,"p":[45,46],"pp":"33","s":"Cotton mills made 8,740,400 pounds of yarn and about 21.7 million yards of cloth in 1946; Ordinance 133 silk testing; 11,031 employed in nine mills."},{"t":"Labor","lv":3,"p":[46,50],"pp":"34","s":"Chun Pyung strike of 22 March with its demands, railway yardmen's strike 21 March, WFTU delegation arrival 30 March, employment tables, falling wages and an irrigation deadline of 30 June."},{"t":"Section 3. Commerce","lv":2,"p":[51,62],"pp":"39","s":"Bank of Chosen notes of 17.19 billion yen, gold at 2,500 yen per momme, property custodians' conference, imports from Japan, 30 locomotives, rail rates, and mail, telephone and radio statistics."},{"t":"Finance","lv":3,"p":[51,52],"pp":"39","s":"Notes outstanding fell to 17,191,319,554 yen on 29 March, Industrial Bank led deposits, gold dropped to 2,500 yen, and tax delinquency was unparalleled."},{"t":"Property Control","lv":3,"p":[53,53],"pp":"41","s":"Provincial custodians met 20-21 March, where the Military Governor spoke of Koreanizing government; machinery returned from Japan; patent applications due 14 April."},{"t":"Imports and Exports","lv":3,"p":[53,55],"pp":"41","s":"Table of February and March imports from Japan including 46,506 tons of coal and 236 trucks; 30 steam locomotives; 285 tons of canned food from the United States."},{"t":"Transportation and Public Utilities","lv":3,"p":[55,56],"pp":"43","s":"Thirty locomotives arrived at Pusan 19 March; rail passenger and freight rates, motor rates from 1 March, eight wooden ships for sale, 117 million kWh delivered in February."},{"t":"Communications","lv":3,"p":[57,62],"pp":"45","s":"Mail volumes, postal savings and insurance, 141,776 telephone calls, radio fee raised from 10 to 40 yen from 1 May, a new 50-kilowatt Seoul transmitter, 18,602 employees."},{"t":"Section 4. Rationing and Price Control","lv":2,"p":[63,68],"pp":"51","s":"Cost-of-living differential raised to 1,512 yen in big cities, rice collection total of 650,646 kiloliters, new ration books from 16 March, coal price increases, petroleum figures and 8,631 detained."},{"t":"Cost of Living","lv":3,"p":[63,64],"pp":"51","s":"Government employee differentials of 1,512 to 1,058 yen by locality; steady rise in free-market food prices, with rice at 1,981.5 yen per bushel; ceiling prices."},{"t":"Food","lv":3,"p":[64,65],"pp":"52","s":"Rice collection ended 15 March at 650,646 kiloliters; 8,000 tons of imported barley allocated; new per-person ration books costing 2 yen; seed potato price 32 yen per kwan."},{"t":"Other Goods","lv":3,"p":[65,66],"pp":"53","s":"Coal dealer prices raised 1 April (anthracite lump 900 to 1,200 yen), pitch to 2,500 yen, 33,000 tons of coal allocated, and 1946 petroleum distribution totals."},{"t":"Violations","lv":3,"p":[66,68],"pp":"54","s":"8,631 detained over rice collection (6,339 acquitted, 367 imprisoned, 1,907 fined); five ships seized for smuggling 1-15 January."},{"t":"Part IV: Social","lv":1,"p":[69,82],"pp":"57","s":"Typhoid surge, Red Cross inauguration, student strikes, youth movement, Radio JODK programs and press reaction to events, across health, education and public information."},{"t":"Section 1. Public Health and Welfare","lv":2,"p":[71,76],"pp":"59","s":"Typhoid 1,409 cases, cholera program from 1 April, rinderpest zone completed, refugee feeding ended 10 March, Red Cross charter 16 March, 392 Koreans repatriated and 4,299 Japanese evacuated."},{"t":"Section 2. Education, Culture and Religion","lv":2,"p":[77,78],"pp":"65","s":"3,396 students at Seoul National University, Suwon and Pusan school strikes, normal-school principals' conference, youth movement of 43,994, and Seoul YMCA joining the international body."},{"t":"Section 3. Public Information","lv":2,"p":[79,82],"pp":"67","s":"Health films and booklets, the Secretary of State's democracy statement broadcast on JODK 21 March, new radio programs, licensing moved to Public Information, and press coverage of 1 March and the strike."},{"t":"Map of Korea","lv":1,"p":[83,83],"pp":"71","s":"Full-page map of Korea at printed page 71, closing the report."},{"t":"Back matter","lv":1,"p":[84,92],"pp":"","s":"University of Virginia Alderman Library date-due slip (due 20 July 1989), blank Google-digitized pages and back cover."}],"by":"claude-sonnet (Claude Code subagent)"},{"id":"J-19","ia":"summation-non-military-activities-japan-no-19-1947-04","s":"J","no":19,"d":"1947-04","d2":"","t":"Summation of Non-Military Activities in Japan, No. 19 (April 1947)","v":"","pg":150,"o":"Summation No. 19 covers April 1947 (with economic statistics mostly for March and February) in four parts: General digest, Political, Economic and Social. April was dominated by Japan's first postwar full cycle of elections under the new Constitution: governors and mayors on 5 April, the House of Councillors on 20 April, the House of Representatives on 25 April and prefectural assemblies on 30 April, with MacArthur praising the result on 27 April. The Social Democrats became the largest lower-house party, independents dominated executive posts, and the purge screened 190,685 persons. The economic chapters show recovery driven by better coal deliveries (March coal 2,256,600 tons), postwar-peak pig iron, steel, pulp, paper and raw silk, with tightening food collection, black-market crackdowns, price rises, a new antitrust act, zaibatsu liquidation and trade-union consolidation. Social chapters give nutrition and disease data, repatriation totals, and education, religion and press developments. A historian will find detailed election returns, Diet statistics, war-crimes trial tables and sector statistics.","h":["Japan voted four times in April 1947: 5 April for governors and mayors, 20 April for the House of Councillors, 25 April for the House of Representatives and 30 April for prefectural assemblies; MacArthur's 27 April message praised the result.","In the House of Representatives the Social Democrats won 143 of 466 seats, ahead of Liberals 132, Democrats 126, People's Cooperatives 31 and Communists 4; independents took 110 of 250 Councillor seats, Social Democrats leading the parties with 47.","Independents won 29 governorships and 3,716 village headships on 5 April; Seiichiro Yasui beat Social Democrat Daikichiro Tagawa 705,040 to 615,622 in Tokyo, and Toshibuni Tanaka became Socialist governor of Hokkaido after a run-off.","The purge screened 5,327 election candidates (190 barred) and 190,685 persons from 29 March to 26 April; Welfare Minister Yoshinari Kawai and several Democratic Party leaders were purged and an appeals board began work.","The Diet passed the antitrust Act Relating to Prohibition of Private Monopoly on 31 March, creating a seven-member Fair Trade Commission; the Holding Company Liquidation Commission took title to shares of 65 holding companies worth 5,154,152,000 yen.","March coal output was 2,256,600 metric tons, 230,500 under quota; better deliveries lifted pig iron and steel to a postwar peak, paper to 34,168 short tons and electric power to 2,432,541,000 kWh, up 21 percent.","Bank of Japan notes reached 122,400,000,000 yen on 30 April, the income-tax yield estimate rose from 4.5 to 16 billion yen, and electric power rates rose 300 percent on 1 April.","The coal miners' industry-wide contract of 12 April covered 203,316 underground workers; union membership stood at 5,326,969 in 19,983 unions on 31 March, over half of organized labor under recent agreements."],"k":["1947 general elections","House of Councillors","House of Representatives","Social Democratic Party","purge","92nd Diet","war crimes trials","Tokyo Tribunal defense","coal production","labor unions","antitrust law","Holding Company Liquidation Commission","rationing and price control","nutrition survey","repatriation","Bank of Japan notes"],"toc":[{"t":"Front matter","lv":1,"p":[1,4],"pp":"","s":"Cover for Summation No. 19 (April 1947), four-part contents and a list of 108 charts, maps and graphs."},{"t":"Part I: General","lv":1,"p":[5,11],"pp":"1","s":"Digest of April 1947: no SCAP reorganization, four elections, purge and war-crimes figures, gains in coal, steel and textiles, plus health, education and press items."},{"t":"Section 1. Organization Under SCAP","lv":2,"p":[6,6],"pp":"3","s":"Reports no change in the non-military organization of General Headquarters, SCAP, during April 1947."},{"t":"Section 2. Political Activities","lv":2,"p":[6,7],"pp":"3","s":"Summarizes four elections choosing 232,863 officials: Social Democrats won 143 lower-house seats, 190 candidates were purged, and 46 war-crimes arrest orders were issued."},{"t":"Section 3. Economic Activities","lv":2,"p":[7,9],"pp":"5","s":"Digest of land purchases of 151,566 cho, record pig iron and steel output, textile gains, Bank of Japan notes at 122.4 billion yen and 4,153,369 tons of rice collected."},{"t":"Section 4. Social Activities","lv":2,"p":[10,11],"pp":"11","s":"Digest of the February nutrition survey, 62,327 repatriates, teacher conferences, shrine land notification, 18,000,000 Constitution pamphlets and press comment."},{"t":"Part II: Political","lv":1,"p":[12,44],"pp":"15","s":"Covers the April elections, the 92nd Diet, the purge (190,685 screened), public safety and crime statistics, and war-crimes trials including the Tokyo Tribunal defense case."},{"t":"Section 1. Government and Politics","lv":2,"p":[13,31],"pp":"17","s":"Election results for governors, Councillors, Representatives and assemblies, the 92nd Diet record, the purge of 190,685 persons screened, and labor-union endorsements."},{"t":"The General Elections","lv":3,"p":[13,27],"pp":"","s":"MacArthur's 27 April statement and tallies for the executive, Councillors, Representatives and prefectural-assembly elections, with fraud cases and run-offs in eight prefectures."},{"t":"Executive Elections (5 April)","lv":3,"p":[14,17],"pp":"","s":"46 governors and 309 mayors and village heads elected; independents won 29 governorships, turnout 70.8 percent, Yasui beat Tagawa 705,040 to 615,622 in Tokyo, with run-offs."},{"t":"House of Councillors Election (20 April)","lv":3,"p":[17,21],"pp":"","s":"About 32,000,000 voters chose 250 Councillors; 110 independents and 47 Social Democrats won, with occupational analysis, press comment and the list of those elected."},{"t":"House of Representatives Election (25 April)","lv":3,"p":[21,26],"pp":"","s":"1,573 candidates contested 466 seats; Social Democrats won 143, Liberals 132 and Democrats 126, with party predictions and press reaction."},{"t":"Prefectural and Local Assembly Elections (30 April)","lv":3,"p":[27,27],"pp":"","s":"7,130 candidates ran for 2,502 prefectural seats; independents won 771 and controlled 13 assemblies, Democrats 517, Liberals 468, Social Democrats 414."},{"t":"The Diet","lv":3,"p":[27,30],"pp":"","s":"Statistics for the 95-day 92nd Diet: 173 measures submitted, 18,743 visitors, 315 petitions, eight demonstrations, and shifting party strength as the Democratic Party formed."},{"t":"The Purge","lv":3,"p":[30,30],"pp":"","s":"190,685 persons screened 29 March-26 April; 5,327 candidates examined, 190 barred; prominent Democrats purged, about 1,160 local executives barred, and an appeals board begun."},{"t":"Labor and Politics","lv":3,"p":[30,31],"pp":"","s":"NCIU (1,643,599 members) and the General Federation endorsed candidates; labor leaders won 32 of 350 Councillor seats and 16 Representatives seats."},{"t":"Section 2. Public Safety","lv":2,"p":[32,35],"pp":"55","s":"Elections passed without serious incident but produced 490 election-law cases; black-market penalties rose to 100,000 yen, 634 rail deaths in the first quarter, 129,440 February offenses."},{"t":"Section 3. Legal and War Crimes","lv":2,"p":[36,44],"pp":"63","s":"War-crimes investigations (1,749 cases on hand), 46 persons ordered apprehended, trial results in Japan and the Philippines, and the Tokyo Tribunal defense case."},{"t":"Legal Affairs, Investigation, Apprehension and Prosecution of War Criminals","lv":3,"p":[36,38],"pp":"","s":"Cases rose from 1,558 to 1,749; 80 persons delivered to Sugamo; eleven Japan trials with 23 defendants; Philippine trials produced five death sentences."},{"t":"International Prosecution of War Criminals (Tokyo Tribunal)","lv":3,"p":[38,44],"pp":"","s":"Proceedings against Okawa discontinued 9 April; the Manchurian phase of the defense ended 21 April and the China phase began 22 April, with witness testimony summaries."},{"t":"Part III: Economic","lv":1,"p":[45,131],"pp":"81","s":"Mostly March 1947 statistics: coal-led recovery (2,256,600 tons), tight food supply, price increases, and deconcentration through the antitrust act and holding-company liquidation, across twelve sections."},{"t":"Section 1. Agriculture and Fisheries","lv":2,"p":[46,49],"pp":"83","s":"Local Land Commissions bought 151,566 cho in March, prefectural retention rates were set, March fish landings totaled 101,815 tons, and the kiji was named national bird."},{"t":"Section 2. Forestry and Mining","lv":2,"p":[50,55],"pp":"91","s":"March log output 734,928,000 board feet; coal 2,256,600 tons with a 27,000,000-ton 1947-48 goal; lignite 231,197 tons; the 12 April miners' contract; oil drilling results."},{"t":"Section 3. Heavy Industries","lv":2,"p":[56,63],"pp":"103","s":"Coke 136,800 tons, postwar-peak pig iron and steel at 60 percent of minimum need, aluminum 1,869 tons, plus copper, rubber, cement, fertilizer, chemicals and machinery."},{"t":"Section 4. Manufacturing","lv":2,"p":[64,73],"pp":"119","s":"Food-processing shortages, pulp up 36 percent, paper 34,168 short tons, plus glass, medical supplies, electrical goods, truck chassis, leather, handicrafts and sewing machines."},{"t":"Section 5. Textile Industries","lv":2,"p":[74,82],"pp":"139","s":"Raw silk reached a postwar peak; cotton, rayon, wool and knit-goods output rose on eased power limits and better coal and raw-material deliveries."},{"t":"Section 6. Transportation and Public Utilities","lv":2,"p":[83,86],"pp":"157","s":"Government railways carried 270,899,116 passengers and 9,433,500 tons of freight in March; electric power generated 2,432,541,000 kWh, up 21 percent; gas industry data."},{"t":"Section 7. Communications","lv":2,"p":[87,94],"pp":"165","s":"New Labor Bureau at the Communications Ministry, 44 radio stations licensed, radiotelegraph to the United States opened 18 April, and 5,700,000 licensed receivers."},{"t":"Section 8. Labor","lv":2,"p":[95,105],"pp":"181","s":"Employment exchanges reorganized 8 April; 19,983 unions with 5,326,969 members; coal miners' agreement of 12 April; Central Labor Relations Committee elections; employment programs."},{"t":"Section 9. Imports and Exports","lv":2,"p":[106,110],"pp":"203","s":"Little change in March trade; sample shipments included 40 cases of crabmeat to the United States and textile, machinery, chemical and wood products to Europe and Asia."},{"t":"Section 10. Rationing and Price Control","lv":2,"p":[111,119],"pp":"213","s":"Government food stocks of 38 days, 4,153,369 tons of rice collected, 112,026 tons of imports released, electric rates up 300 percent, and new salt, lime, coke and petroleum prices."},{"t":"Section 11. Finance","lv":2,"p":[120,124],"pp":"231","s":"Bank of Japan notes at 122.4 billion yen; licenses to Times Publishing, Northwest and Pan American; income-tax estimate raised to 16 billion yen; April borrowing of 12,910,624,720 yen."},{"t":"Section 12. Property Control","lv":2,"p":[125,131],"pp":"241","s":"Allied and looted property tallies, dissolved organizations' accounts, the 31 March antitrust act with a Fair Trade Commission, and 65 holding companies' shares worth 5.15 billion yen."},{"t":"Antitrust and Cartels","lv":3,"p":[129,131],"pp":"","s":"26 restricted concerns allowed 82,675,838 yen for rehabilitation; the new antitrust act's prohibitions and seven-member Fair Trade Commission; zaibatsu family designations."},{"t":"Part IV: Social","lv":1,"p":[132,150],"pp":"255","s":"Health, nutrition and repatriation figures (62,327 repatriated, 35 typhus cases in Tokyo), then education, religion, information programs and press comment for April 1947."},{"t":"Section 1. Public Health and Welfare","lv":2,"p":[133,142],"pp":"257","s":"February nutrition surveys showed improvement; 35 typhus cases in Tokyo; 3,172 hospitals; patent medicine output of 117,497,793 yen; 62,327 Japanese repatriated."},{"t":"Section 2. Education, Religion and Media of Expression","lv":2,"p":[143,150],"pp":"277","s":"Teacher-training conferences, shrine and temple land notification of 2 April, election broadcasting, 18,000,000 Constitution pamphlets, press comment and 17,142 visitors to the SCAP library."}],"by":"claude-sonnet (Claude Code subagent)"},{"id":"K-19","ia":"summation-usamg-activities-korea-no-19-1947-04","s":"K","no":19,"d":"1947-04","d2":"","t":"Summation of United States Army Military Government Activities in Korea, No. 19 (April 1947)","v":"","pg":92,"o":"Number 19 of the Summation of United States Army Military Government Activities in Korea covers April 1947. Politically, the Military Governor restated the policy of Koreanization, the Interim Legislative Assembly amended and passed the summer-grain collection program, took up local self-government, a provisional constitution and a bill against collaborators, and Syngman Rhee returned to a mass welcome while Lyuh Woon Heung launched the Laboring Masses Party. Secretary of State Marshall's letter proposing that the U.S.-U.S.S.R. Joint Commission reconvene was printed in full with Molotov's reply naming 20 May. Economically, coal and silk output rose, seven New Korea Company subsidiaries went to the Department of Commerce, prices of cloth and grain fell, imports from Japan are tabulated, and the controlled-commodity list grew. Social sections cover cholera immunization, refugees from the North at about 3,000 a day, adult literacy and radio and press. Historians will find detailed statistics and verbatim documents.","h":["The Military Governor's policy statement said Americans would withdraw to advisory and inspectorial positions, and Ordinance No. 135 of 15 March 1947 gave Koreans full responsibility for appointment to public office.","The Legislative Assembly adopted six amending articles to the summer-grain collection program, limiting collection to one fifth of production and forbidding forcible methods, and on 4 April voted 42 to 0, with 12 abstentions, to have the National Food Administration carry it out.","On 21 April Kim Fung Chun presented a draft provisional constitution of seven chapters and 67 sections, with the Assembly serving as the Provisional Government, and a bill defining traitors, collaborators, war criminals and profiteers had its first reading on 22 April.","Secretary of State Marshall's letter of 14 April asked that the Joint Commission be reconvened; Molotov replied proposing it resume in Seoul on 20 May and report in July-August 1947.","Lyuh Woon Heung founded the Laboring Masses Party (preparatory committee of 38 on 7 April, platform 26 April), while Kim Koo and the Chungking group resigned from the Korean Independence Party's committees.","March anthracite output was 26,876 metric tons against a 29,500-ton quota; raw silk reached a 1947 peak of 61,707 pounds, and seven New Korea Company subsidiaries moved to the Department of Commerce on 1 April.","Tai Han No Chong won the Seoul Electric Company bargaining election on 19 April with 3,260 of 3,805 votes cast, and Ordinance No. 139 raised tax-delinquency penalties up to 30 percent from 19 April.","Refugees from North Korea entered at roughly 3,000 a day at Tongduchon-ni; the Department of Public Health and Welfare received an 8,450,000 yen grant, and 222 South Koreans were repatriated from 31 March to 27 April."],"k":["Koreanization","Interim Legislative Assembly","summer-grain collection","provisional constitution","collaborators bill","Joint Commission","Marshall-Molotov letters","Laboring Masses Party","Syngman Rhee","New Korea Company","Bank of Chosen","refugees from North Korea","price control","cholera","Seoul Electric Company labor election","adult education"],"toc":[{"t":"Front matter","lv":1,"p":[1,14],"pp":"","s":"Library covers, title page for No. 19 (April 1947), table of contents of four parts, and list of 17 charts plus a map of Korea."},{"t":"Part I: General","lv":1,"p":[15,22],"pp":"1","s":"Digest of the month: Assembly passage of the summer-grain program on 4 April, the draft constitution, the Marshall letter, and economic and social highlights."},{"t":"Section 1. Political Activities","lv":2,"p":[17,18],"pp":"3","s":"Assembly passed the summer-grain program on 4 April and received a draft constitution on 21 April; Laboring Masses Party announced; Marshall and Molotov letters summarized."},{"t":"Section 2. Economic Activities","lv":2,"p":[19,20],"pp":"5","s":"March coal was the fiscal year's highest and raw silk peaked at 61,707 pounds; seven subsidiaries moved to Commerce, mail to China resumed 4 April, and the controlled list expanded."},{"t":"Section 3. Social Activities","lv":2,"p":[21,22],"pp":"7","s":"Cholera immunization in port cities, typhoid down to 707 cases, 8,450,000 yen for northern refugees, and literacy of 1,169,863 in Kyongsang-namdo."},{"t":"Part II: Political","lv":1,"p":[23,40],"pp":"9","s":"Koreanization policy, the Assembly's grain, constitution and collaborator bills, party politics, the Marshall-Molotov correspondence, and fire and prison matters."},{"t":"Section 1. Government and Politics","lv":2,"p":[25,38],"pp":"11","s":"Policy of Koreanization, civil-service exams, Assembly debate on grain collection, local self-government, constitution and collaborator bills, party developments, Marshall and Molotov letters."},{"t":"Administration and Korean Civil Service","lv":3,"p":[25,26],"pp":"11","s":"Military Governor's statement on Koreanization and Ordinance No. 135; 155 of 232 clerk candidates passed the civil-service examination on 18 April."},{"t":"Korean Interim Legislative Assembly","lv":3,"p":[26,33],"pp":"12","s":"Six amending articles on summer-grain collection, a six-tier local government bill, child labor ordinance, 67-section constitution, membership raised to 90, and the collaborator bill."},{"t":"Political Developments","lv":3,"p":[33,34],"pp":"19","s":"National Society convention 19-20 April with Rhee's report; Kim Koo's group resigned from the Korean Independence Party; Lyuh Woon Heung's Laboring Masses Party; Independent Labor and Farmer Party boycott."},{"t":"U. S.-U. S. S. R. Joint Commission","lv":3,"p":[34,38],"pp":"20","s":"Full text of Marshall's letter of 14 April and Molotov's reply proposing the Commission resume in Seoul on 20 May 1947."},{"t":"Foreign Affairs","lv":3,"p":[38,38],"pp":"24","s":"By 5 April the Office of Foreign Affairs had authorized 841 Japanese women married to Koreans to stay in South Korea."},{"t":"Section 2. Legal and Public Safety","lv":2,"p":[39,40],"pp":"25","s":"No major disturbances; fires rose from 192 to 250 with 137,835,144 yen damage in February; prison wardens met 24-26 April; 250 Constabulary cadets commissioned."},{"t":"Part III: Economic","lv":1,"p":[41,66],"pp":"27","s":"Natural resources, industry, commerce and rationing: March coal at 26,876 tons, seven New Korea Company subsidiaries transferred, Bank of Chosen notes, Ordinance No. 139, and price-control changes."},{"t":"Section 1. Natural Resources","lv":2,"p":[43,46],"pp":"29","s":"New Korea Company's vested-land sale proposal, 1946 livestock census of 637,085 cattle, March coal output of 26,876 tons, and a 20,000,000 yen copper fund."},{"t":"Section 2. Industry","lv":2,"p":[47,50],"pp":"33","s":"Seven New Korea Company subsidiaries transferred 1 April, Hokusen Paper Mill shut for pulp shortage, 8,461 bales of Japanese cotton, Seoul Electric Company union election."},{"t":"Section 3. Commerce","lv":2,"p":[51,62],"pp":"37","s":"Bank of Chosen notes at 17,239,535,835 yen, Ordinance No. 139 tax penalties, revenue totals, imports from Japan, locomotives and streetcars, postal and radio communications."},{"t":"Finance and Revenues","lv":3,"p":[51,54],"pp":"37","s":"Currency up slightly, gold at 1,400 yen per momme, Ahn Chai Hong's tax appeal, Monopoly Bureau 69.4 percent of revenue to 31 March."},{"t":"Government Monopolies and Property Control","lv":3,"p":[54,54],"pp":"40","s":"Salt shortage survey, 13 of 25 saltern engineer candidates passed, 180 Japanese property liquidations processed."},{"t":"Imports and Exports","lv":3,"p":[55,57],"pp":"41","s":"4,243 tons of Burmese rice on 15 April, Japanese textiles, machinery and coal (48,985 tons in April), metals promised to the United States."},{"t":"Transportation and Public Utilities","lv":3,"p":[57,58],"pp":"43","s":"38 American locomotives imported, 16,733 freight cars moved in March, only 48 streetcars running daily, Seoul waterworks expansion."},{"t":"Communications","lv":3,"p":[58,62],"pp":"44","s":"First large mail shipment to China 4 April, postal savings of 6,981,049 depositors, wire traffic up 148 percent, radio transmitters and technical school."},{"t":"Section 4. Rationing and Price Control","lv":2,"p":[63,66],"pp":"49","s":"Summer-grain quotas, rent-in-kind change on 26 March, controlled list expanded 17 April, leather prices set, prices of cloth down 30 percent."},{"t":"Part IV: Social","lv":1,"p":[67,79],"pp":"53","s":"Public health and refugees, education and youth, and information: typhoid down to 707, 3,000 refugees a day, Youth Movement at 66,722 members, 341 broadcast hours."},{"t":"Section 1. Public Health and Welfare","lv":2,"p":[69,72],"pp":"55","s":"Typhoid fell from 1,409 to 707, 4,633 licensed physicians, 3,000 refugees a day at Tongduchon-ni, 222 repatriated and 6,122 Chinese registered."},{"t":"Section 2. Education, Culture and Religion","lv":2,"p":[73,76],"pp":"59","s":"Seoul National University extension credit, music festivals, adult literacy, Youth Movement at 66,722 members, Boston Marathon celebration, eight missionaries returned."},{"t":"Section 3. Public Information","lv":2,"p":[77,79],"pp":"63","s":"Health and current-events films, 341 hours of March broadcasting, and press reaction to Rhee's 27 April welcome and the Laboring Masses Party."},{"t":"Map of Korea","lv":1,"p":[80,81],"pp":"67","s":"Foldout map of Korea on the final pages; the OCR text holds only scan and digitization stamps, with no readable labels."},{"t":"Back matter","lv":1,"p":[82,92],"pp":"","s":"University of Virginia Alderman Library due-date slip (due 14 January 1949), blank pages and scan end sheets."}],"by":"claude-sonnet (Claude Code subagent)"},{"id":"J-20","ia":"summation-non-military-activities-japan-no-20-1947-05","s":"J","no":20,"d":"1947-05","d2":"","t":"Summation of Non-Military Activities in Japan, No. 20 (May 1947)","v":"","pg":130,"o":"Summation No. 20 covers May 1947 (with much economic data for March-April), the month the new Constitution took effect on 3 May and the first Diet under it met on 20 May. After a month of interparty bargaining, Social Democrat Tetsu Katayama was named Prime Minister on 24 May and a three-party Cabinet was announced on 29 May. SCAP created a Reparations Section and a Reparations Technical Advisory Committee. The report details the Tokyo war crimes tribunal's defense case (China and Russian phases), Japanese judicial reform, and war crimes trials. Economic chapters cover record coke, steel and housing output, textile recovery, coal shortfalls, the abolition of the 700-yen wage limit, rationing, imports, finance and property control. Social chapters report health statistics, repatriation, teacher screening, religion and media. A historian will find dense monthly statistics and charts for tracking Occupation-era recovery.","h":["Japan's new Constitution took effect on 3 May, and MacArthur restored the national flag for display over the Diet, Supreme Court, Prime Minister's premises and the Emperor's residence.","The first Diet under the Constitution met 20 May: Tsuneo Matsudaira became President of the House of Councillors, Komakichi Matsuoka Speaker of the House of Representatives, and the Ryokufu Kai formed with 90 members.","Social Democrat Tetsu Katayama was designated Prime Minister on 24 May by 420 to 6 in the lower house; after Liberal talks failed, a three-party Cabinet of eight Social Democrats, seven Democrats and two People's Cooperatives was announced 29 May.","General Orders No. 8 (8 May) created the SCAP Reparations Section and No. 9 (21 May) the Reparations Technical Advisory Committee; ten nations had reparations delegations in Japan.","At the Tokyo tribunal the defense finished the China Phase on 16 May and opened the Russian Phase, disputing responsibility for the Panay bombing and denying knowledge of Nanking atrocities.","April fish catch rose to five times March's on the herring run, while coal output in the first 20 days of May was 1,244,600 tons, 103,600 below the same April period.","The 700-yen wage and salary limit under the Emergency Financial Measures was abolished 1 May, and Bank of Japan notes in circulation reached 129,685,000,000 yen on 31 May.","Imperial ordinances created a Labor Standards Bureau in the Ministry of Welfare; about 400,000 workers attended Tokyo's quiet May Day; repatriates from 28 April to 31 May numbered about 69,700."],"k":["Constitution Day","National Diet","Katayama Cabinet","Ryokufu Kai","war crimes trials","International Military Tribunal","reparations","coal production","industrial production indexes","textiles","labor standards","rationing and price control","Bank of Japan notes","property control","repatriation","teacher screening"],"toc":[{"t":"Front matter","lv":1,"p":[1,4],"pp":"","s":"Cover for Summation No. 20 (May 1947), table of contents by part and section, and the list of 104 charts, maps and graphs."},{"t":"Part I: General","lv":1,"p":[5,13],"pp":"1","s":"Monthly digest: SCAP created reparations bodies, Constitution Day passed and the Katayama Cabinet formed, with economic gains and shortages and health, education and media news."},{"t":"Organization Under SCAP","lv":2,"p":[6,6],"pp":"3","s":"General Orders No. 8 (8 May) created the Reparations Section; No. 9 (21 May) set up the Reparations Technical Advisory Committee."},{"t":"Political Activities","lv":2,"p":[6,7],"pp":"3","s":"Constitution Day on 3 May, new Diet officers, Katayama's selection as Prime Minister on 24 May, the three-party Cabinet, public safety figures and war crimes trials."},{"t":"Economic Activities","lv":2,"p":[8,10],"pp":"7","s":"Herring-run fish surge, coal and log trends, record coke and steel output, textile expansion, labor ordinances, rationing, finance and property control, with production index charts."},{"t":"Social Activities","lv":2,"p":[11,13],"pp":"13","s":"Health officials' meetings, National Institute of Health dedication on 21 May, 3,181 hospitals, teacher dismissals, religious conventions, Constitution Week and library attendance of 15,421."},{"t":"Part II: Political","lv":1,"p":[13,35],"pp":"17","s":"Constitution, Diet and May Cabinet crisis, public safety statistics, Japanese judicial reform, and the Tokyo tribunal's China and Russian defense phases."},{"t":"Government and Politics","lv":2,"p":[14,25],"pp":"19","s":"Constitution Day, Diet organization, the May Cabinet crisis ending in Katayama's election and a three-party Cabinet, party developments, neighborhood associations and the purge."},{"t":"Public Safety","lv":2,"p":[23,25],"pp":"37","s":"Crime rose from 129,440 to 154,874 offenses in March; 134 were killed in April rail accidents; prison deaths ran 1.6 per 1,000; 2,107 fires in March."},{"t":"Legal and War Crimes","lv":2,"p":[26,35],"pp":"43","s":"Provisional laws revised the courts, arrest rules and Civil Code; 90 suspects ordered arrested; 21 trials of 34 defendants; tribunal testimony in the China and Russian phases."},{"t":"Part III: Economic","lv":1,"p":[35,113],"pp":"61","s":"Production, transport, communications, labor, trade, rationing, finance and property control, with statistical charts for March and April 1947, including record coke, steel and housing output."},{"t":"Agriculture and Fisheries","lv":2,"p":[36,37],"pp":"63","s":"Land reform progress; April fish catch five times March's on a herring run, gaining 357,017 metric tons; rope, salt and petroleum allocations."},{"t":"Forestry and Mining","lv":2,"p":[38,42],"pp":"67","s":"April log output fell 88,688,000 board feet to 646,340,000; coal output slipped in May; lignite, oil drilling and mineral output also covered."},{"t":"Heavy Industries","lv":2,"p":[43,50],"pp":"77","s":"Coke at a 1946-47 high, postwar-high iron and steel, ferroalloys nearly doubled, housing peak of about 43,000 structures, plus chemicals, machine tools and machinery."},{"t":"Manufacturing","lv":2,"p":[51,59],"pp":"93","s":"April output: flour milling up 70.5 percent in March on cereal imports; tin plate shortage cut can production; truck chassis fell to 643 against 869; watch and clock output hit a postwar high of 123,406."},{"t":"Textile Industries","lv":2,"p":[60,68],"pp":"111","s":"Textile output kept rising from February through April in all branches except raw silk reeling, across cotton, silk, rayon, wool and knit goods."},{"t":"Transportation and Public Utilities","lv":2,"p":[69,72],"pp":"129","s":"April operable trucks rose to 51,378 and buses to 7,604; railways carried 278,163,667 passengers; power generation reached 2,728,180,000 kWh, up 12.2 percent; gas output averaged 1,209,210 cubic meters daily."},{"t":"Communications","lv":2,"p":[73,80],"pp":"137","s":"International Telecommunications Company liquidated 25 May, Ministry of Communications paying $1,200,000 monthly rent; telephone and telegraph traffic hit peaks; Osaka-Colombo radiotelegraph circuit opened 10 May; 75 research laboratories counted."},{"t":"Labor","lv":2,"p":[81,88],"pp":"153","s":"Imperial ordinances created the Labor Standards Bureau; May Day drew 400,000 in Tokyo; disputes idled .19 percent of working time; union conventions and employment covered."},{"t":"Imports and Exports","lv":2,"p":[89,93],"pp":"169","s":"Imports by commodity group (food, textiles, machinery, chemicals, metals, wood) and exports including 15,425,000 linear yards of cotton fabric shipped to five countries."},{"t":"Rationing and Price Control","lv":2,"p":[94,102],"pp":"179","s":"Rice collections reached 4,222,100 tons since 30 September; imports made up 30 percent of the May ration; fertilizer deliveries, price increases and violations reported."},{"t":"Finance","lv":2,"p":[103,107],"pp":"197","s":"The 700-yen wage ceiling was abolished 1 May; note circulation hit 129,685,000,000 yen; Capital Levy collections reached 16,650,000,000 yen; banks, deposit bureau and liquidation covered."},{"t":"Property Control and Reparations","lv":2,"p":[108,113],"pp":"207","s":"278 looted ships reported; allied and war-criminal property, German patents, zaibatsu holding company shares, rehabilitation expenditures, and reparations delegations of ten nations."},{"t":"Part IV: Social","lv":1,"p":[113,130],"pp":"217","s":"Public health, welfare and repatriation, followed by education, religion, arts and media, ending with a map of Japan."},{"t":"Public Health and Welfare","lv":2,"p":[114,120],"pp":"219","s":"Communicable disease rates, veterinary affairs, hospitals, medical supplies, narcotics, relief to 2,763,041 persons, social security and repatriation from Soviet areas."},{"t":"Education, Religion and Media of Expression","lv":2,"p":[121,130],"pp":"233","s":"Teacher screening committees (120,000 teachers gone), religious conventions, cultural sites, radio, press, films with 475,637 attendance, theater, music, library, and a map of Japan."}],"by":"claude-sonnet (Claude Code subagent)"},{"id":"K-20","ia":"summation-usamg-activities-korea-no-20-1947-05","s":"K","no":20,"d":"1947-05","d2":"","t":"Summation of United States Army Military Government Activities in Korea, No. 20 (May 1947)","v":"","pg":104,"o":"Summation No. 20 covers South Korea under USAMGIK for May 1947. The dominant political event was the reconvening of the U.S.-U.S.S.R. Joint Commission in Seoul on 21 May after the Marshall-Molotov correspondence, with Joint Communiques Nos. 9 and 10 printed in full. The Interim Legislative Assembly wrangled over the Anticollaborator Bill and the General Franchise Law (voting age raised to 25), and Speaker Kimm Kiu Sic offered, then withdrew, his resignation. Economic coverage includes a 57.4 percent summer-grain outlook, Bank of Chosen notes at 17.4 billion yen, a 17-billion-yen budget, gold-mine reopening rules, wage reform for vested-factory workers and civil servants, trade tables, rail and utility data, a planned 20 percent ration cut and the summer-grain quota. Social sections report cholera immunization, refugee housing and the National Refugee Board, schooling, youth movements and press reaction. Historians gain primary texts of the Commission documents and statistical tables.","h":["The U.S.-U.S.S.R. Joint Commission reconvened in Seoul on 21 May; Soviet delegates under Col. Gen. T. F. Shtikov arrived 20 May with 65 staff, and Maj. Gen. A. E. Brown led the American side.","Joint Communique No. 9 (26 May) set a ten-member Commission, weekly alternating chairmanship and three subcommissions; Communique No. 10 (1 June) recorded agreement in principle on broad consultation with Korean parties.","The Legislative Assembly raised the General Franchise Law's minimum voting age to 25, defeating a 20-year proposal 39 to 17; the Anticollaborator Bill was tabled 35 to 26 after dissenters boycotted a 26 May session, leaving no quorum.","Speaker Dr. Kimm Kiu Sic resigned on 19 May citing ill health; the Assembly rejected the resignation 52 to 0 and he agreed to reconsider.","Estimated summer grain output reached only 57.4 percent of goal (4,481,881 of 7,814,367 suk); the ration was to drop from 2.5 to 2 hop for July-August, with a 711,000-suk collection quota.","A Wage Rate and Incentive Board program rescinded the 18 June 1946 wage freeze, allowed raises up to 25 percent for about 250,000 civil servants and 250,000 vested-factory workers, and issued eight yards of cotton cloth each.","Bank of Chosen notes in circulation reached 17,417,709,259 yen on 31 May; a balanced budget of up to 17 billion yen went to the Assembly, while April spending of 1,316,841,645 yen far exceeded revenue of 616,329,439.","A National Refugee Board was created 12 May; 20,042 refugees entered in April, and by 29 May 8,329 refugee houses were complete, 6,067 under construction and 772 yet to be built."],"k":["Joint Commission","Shtikov","Marshall-Molotov correspondence","Legislative Assembly","General Franchise Law","Anticollaborator Bill","Kimm Kiu Sic","summer grain collection","ration reduction","vested property","Wage Rate and Incentive Board","refugees","National Police Board","Bank of Chosen notes","gold mines"],"toc":[{"t":"Front matter","lv":1,"p":[1,14],"pp":"","s":"Library flyleaves, cover of Summation No. 20 (May 1947), table of contents for Parts I-IV, and a list of 26 charts plus a Map of Korea."},{"t":"Part I: General","lv":1,"p":[15,22],"pp":"1","s":"Digest of the month: Joint Commission reconvened 21 May, Assembly debated the franchise law, summer grain outlook 57.4 percent, ration to be cut 20 percent, cholera immunization and a new National Refugee Board."},{"t":"Section 1. Political Activities","lv":2,"p":[17,18],"pp":"3","s":"Assembly handled the Anticollaborator Bill and Franchise Law; Dr. Kimm's resignation was rejected 52 to 0; Joint Commission reconvened 21 May; National Police Board established."},{"t":"Section 2. Economic Activities","lv":2,"p":[19,20],"pp":"5","s":"Crop output at 57.4 percent of goal, coal averaging 20,927 tons monthly, Bank of Chosen notes at 17.4 billion yen, a 17-billion-yen budget and a 20 percent food ration cut."},{"t":"Section 3. Social Activities","lv":2,"p":[21,22],"pp":"7","s":"Cholera immunization 90 percent complete, Korean Medical Association formed 10 May, 20,042 April refugees, 1,211 repatriated, and Seoul National University entrance rules."},{"t":"Part II: Political","lv":1,"p":[23,42],"pp":"9","s":"Reorganization approved 17 May, Assembly proceedings, Marshall-Molotov letters and Joint Communiques Nos. 9 and 10; also the Civil Liberties Union, National Police Board, juvenile courts, prisons and fire losses."},{"t":"Section 1. Government and Politics","lv":2,"p":[25,36],"pp":"11","s":"Reorganization plan approved 17 May, Assembly franchise debate and Kimm resignation, Marshall-Molotov letters, Joint Communiques Nos. 9 and 10, and Rhee's and Kim Koo's reservations."},{"t":"Administration","lv":3,"p":[25,25],"pp":"11","s":"Military Governor approved departmental reorganization 17 May; governors met 9-10 May; property custodian made directly responsible; 3,195 of 5,916 central employees come from Seoul or Kyonggi-do."},{"t":"Korean Interim Legislative Assembly","lv":3,"p":[26,29],"pp":"12","s":"Anticollaborator Bill tabled 35 to 26; Franchise Law voting age set at 25; Kimm resignation rejected 52 to 0; talks with North Korea's People's Party proposed."},{"t":"Political Developments and U.S.-U.S.S.R. Joint Commission","lv":3,"p":[30,36],"pp":"16","s":"Rhee and Kim Koo withhold decision; Lyuh Woon Hyung elected Laboring People's Party chairman 24 May; Marshall-Molotov letters; Communiques Nos. 9 and 10 create three subcommissions."},{"t":"Section 2. Legal and Public Safety","lv":2,"p":[37,42],"pp":"23","s":"Korean Civil Liberties Union formed 22 May; National Police Board created; juvenile courts expanded to four; 20,282 prisoners in April; April fire losses 39,951,800 yen; Marine Academy opened with 312 students."},{"t":"Part III: Economic","lv":1,"p":[43,76],"pp":"29","s":"Natural resources, industry, commerce and rationing: summer grain at 57.4 percent of goal, 17.4 billion yen in notes, 258 of 614 locomotives operable, and a 20 percent ration cut, with many tables."},{"t":"Section 1. Natural Resources","lv":2,"p":[45,50],"pp":"31","s":"Summer grain at 57.4 percent of goal with provincial tables, 1946 flood loss of 1.72 billion yen, coal at 20,927 tons monthly, and conditions for reopening vested gold mines."},{"t":"Section 2. Industry","lv":2,"p":[51,54],"pp":"37","s":"20-million-yen copper loan, one of three fertilizer plants working, raw silk up to 88,890 pounds in April, and the Wage Rate and Incentive Board's eight-point plan with raises up to 25 percent."},{"t":"Section 3. Commerce","lv":2,"p":[55,72],"pp":"41","s":"Finance, property custody, trade with Japan, China, Hongkong and the U.S., railroads (258 of 614 locomotives operable), utility rate proposals, public works and communications statistics."},{"t":"Finance","lv":3,"p":[55,57],"pp":"41","s":"Bank of Chosen notes 17,417,709,259 yen; deposits 19,993,000,000 yen in February; April spending 1,316,841,645 yen against revenue 616,329,439; budget limited to 17 billion yen."},{"t":"Property Control and Imports and Exports","lv":3,"p":[58,61],"pp":"44","s":"Office of Property Custody took direct control 12 May; vested property sales 3,880,601.84 yen; National Economic Board foreign trade policy of 25 March; tables of imports from Japan and private trade."},{"t":"Transportation","lv":3,"p":[62,63],"pp":"48","s":"Hongkong trade office opened 10 April; 32,944 railroad ties laid 1-20 April; railroads employ 32,366; Seoul has 257 tramcars with under 50 running."},{"t":"Public Utilities","lv":3,"p":[64,65],"pp":"50","s":"Electric supply depends on North Korean power (837,678,737 kWh received); Public Utilities Committee recommends rate rises, such as freight up to 50 percent and wholesale power to 25 sen per kWh."},{"t":"Public Works","lv":3,"p":[66,68],"pp":"52","s":"Highway program completed March at 200 million yen; 1,051 highway projects costing 305,199,782 yen; 1946 flood damage 6.2 billion yen; waterworks subsidies totaling 100,631,000 yen."},{"t":"Communications","lv":3,"p":[69,72],"pp":"55","s":"646 post offices, three North-South mail exchanges in May, Pusan-Fukuoka cable used from 15 May, and seven broadcasting stations with 180,185 paying households."},{"t":"Section 4. Rationing and Price Control","lv":2,"p":[73,76],"pp":"59","s":"Ration to fall from 2.5 to 2 hop for July-August; summer-grain quota 711,000 suk led by Cholla-namdo with 163,000; 57 percent of fertilizer delivered; shoes at 300 yen for essential workers."},{"t":"Part IV: Social","lv":1,"p":[77,94],"pp":"63","s":"Public health, education, culture and public information: cholera control, 8,329 refugee houses, Seoul National University regents, youth organizations and press reaction to the Joint Commission."},{"t":"Section 1. Public Health and Welfare","lv":2,"p":[79,84],"pp":"65","s":"Cholera immunization phases, Korean Medical Association formed 10 May, LARA advisory committee, 20,042 April refugees, 8,329 refugee houses completed and 1,211 repatriates."},{"t":"Section 2. Education, Culture and Religion","lv":2,"p":[85,90],"pp":"71","s":"New Seoul National University regents 27 May, 392,496 textbooks distributed, Farmers' Music Festival 23-27 May, Youth Movement at 77,930 members, and 47 missionaries in South Korea."},{"t":"Section 3. Public Information","lv":2,"p":[91,94],"pp":"77","s":"28 films released (22 American), 273,360 radio sets in a 1 May census, Farmers' Weekly circulation halved to 300,000, and press comment on the Joint Commission and Rhee."},{"t":"Map of Korea and back matter","lv":1,"p":[95,104],"pp":"81","s":"Map of Korea on page 95, followed by a library due-date slip, blank scanned leaves and the digitization back cover."}],"by":"claude-sonnet (Claude Code subagent)"},{"id":"K-21","ia":"summation-usamg-activities-korea-no-21-1947-06","s":"K","no":21,"d":"1947-06","d2":"","t":"Summation of United States Army Military Government Activities in Korea, No. 21 (June 1947)","v":"","pg":117,"o":"Summation No. 21 covers June 1947 in South Korea under USAMGIK. The dominant political story is the resumed U.S.-U.S.S.R. Joint Commission: Joint Communique No. 11 set out consultation procedure and questionnaires, 344 parties obtained copies by 19 June, a joint meeting was held in Seoul on 25 June and delegations moved to Pyongyang. The Interim Legislative Assembly finally passed the General Franchise Law on 27 June with a voting age of 23 after the Speaker's resignation crisis, while Rightists split over cooperation with the Commission. Economic sections report record April coal output, record May salt, rising Bank of Chosen note circulation (18.0 billion yen), the new Korean Foreign Exchange Bank, trade tables, railroads, power and prices. Social sections cover cholera immunization, 102,185 refugees from the north, textbooks, schools and press opinion. A historian gets full texts of Joint Commission documents and dense statistics.","h":["The Joint Commission's Communique No. 11 of 12 June set out the method of consultation with Korean democratic parties, requiring a signed declaration upholding the Moscow Decision; 344 parties and organizations had obtained copies by 19 June.","On 27 June the Interim Legislative Assembly passed the General Franchise Law with a compromise minimum voting age of 23 (26 votes for 21, 40 for 23), after Speaker Kimm Kiu Sic and both Vice-speakers resigned on 3 June over a 25-year age requirement and withdrew on 9 June.","The Hankuk Democratic Party decided to join Joint Commission consultation, splitting the Rightists who followed Syngman Rhee and Kim Koo; the South Korea Labor Party demanded their expulsion and chose Pak Hun Yung as its representative.","The Military Governor left for Washington on 21 June, with Brigadier General C. G. Helmick acting in his place, and Korean became the official language of the Interim Government from 1 July.","Bank of Chosen notes in circulation rose 617,893,769 yen in June to 18,035,603,028 yen; the Korean Foreign Exchange Bank was created on 16 June with 500,000,000 yen capital; the revised Third Income Tax Law took effect.","April coal production of 31,318 metric tons was the highest since liberation and May salt output of 32,207 metric tons a postwar high, while private-trade imports rose 521.8 percent in value.","From 1 January to 28 June 102,185 refugees crossed south of the 38th parallel (24,501 in June); 74,637 were cared for in seven camps and 9,092 refugee housing units had been completed.","Criminal offenses in May rose to 10,226 from 8,540 in April, and 20,554 persons were held in prisons; more than 80 percent of police had no pre-liberation experience."],"k":["Joint Commission","Joint Communique No. 11","Moscow Decision","General Franchise Law","Interim Legislative Assembly","Kimm Kiu Sic","Syngman Rhee","Kim Koo","Foreign Exchange Bank","New Korea Company","coal production","refugees from North Korea","cholera","national police","trusteeship"],"toc":[{"t":"Front matter","lv":1,"p":[1,15],"pp":"","s":"Google Books and library covers, title page of Summation No. 21 for June 1947, table of contents and list of 33 charts, maps and graphs."},{"t":"Part I: General","lv":1,"p":[16,25],"pp":"1","s":"Digest of June 1947: Joint Commission consultations, the Franchise Law passed 27 June, record coal and salt output, 18.0 billion yen note circulation, cholera immunization and 102,185 northern refugees."},{"t":"Section 1. Political Activities","lv":2,"p":[18,19],"pp":"3","s":"Speaker Kimm's resignation, the Franchise Law's voting age of 23, the Rightist split over the Joint Commission, and the 25 June departure of delegations for Pyongyang."},{"t":"Section 2. Economic Activities","lv":2,"p":[20,23],"pp":"5","s":"Rice transplanting of 2,824,466 acres, record coal and salt output, the Pusan stevedore strike, note circulation of 18.0 billion yen, and creation of the Foreign Exchange Bank."},{"t":"Section 3. Social Activities","lv":2,"p":[24,25],"pp":"9","s":"Cholera immunization, 102,185 northern refugees, 9,129,922 textbooks distributed, Joint Commission publicity and the new World News Report."},{"t":"Part II: Political","lv":1,"p":[26,51],"pp":"11","s":"Government and the Legislative Assembly, full Joint Commission documents, foreign affairs, and law, police, prisons and fires for May and June 1947, including 20,554 prisoners."},{"t":"Section 1. Government and Politics","lv":2,"p":[28,45],"pp":"13","s":"Acting Military Governor Helmick, Korean as official language, the Assembly's voting-age crisis, Rightist and Leftist maneuvers, and full Joint Commission texts."},{"t":"Administration","lv":3,"p":[28,28],"pp":"13","s":"Military Governor left for Washington 21 June; Brigadier General Helmick acted; Korean became the official language from 1 July; Ordinance Drafting Committee met 11 June."},{"t":"Korean Interim Legislative Assembly","lv":3,"p":[28,30],"pp":"13","s":"Speaker Kimm and two Vice-speakers resigned 3 June and withdrew 9 June; the age vote (40 for 23 years) led to final passage of the franchise law on 27 June."},{"t":"Political Developments","lv":3,"p":[30,31],"pp":"15","s":"Hankuk Democratic Party joined consultation while Rhee's forces refused; the South Korea Labor Party demanded Rhee and Kim Koo be expelled and named Pak Hun Yung."},{"t":"U. S.-U. S. S. R. Joint Commission","lv":3,"p":[31,44],"pp":"16","s":"Communique No. 11, method of consultation, Decisions 5 and 6 questionnaires, 344 applicant parties, the 25 June joint meeting and departure for Pyongyang."},{"t":"Foreign Affairs","lv":3,"p":[44,45],"pp":"29","s":"121 travel permits issued in June to Chinese nationals being repatriated; 109 permanent-residence permits for Japanese wives, bringing the total to 1,110."},{"t":"Section 2. Legal and Public Safety","lv":2,"p":[46,51],"pp":"31","s":"Ordinances on inheritance tax and cattle slaughter, 23 June Rightist demonstrations, police experience, 20,554 prisoners, 92 fires in May and Constabulary training."},{"t":"Legal","lv":3,"p":[46,46],"pp":"31","s":"Ordinance No. 137 on inheritance tax and No. 140 limiting cattle slaughter, plus Public Act No. 2 on summer grain collection."},{"t":"Law and Order","lv":3,"p":[46,46],"pp":"31","s":"Orderly Rightist demonstrations before Duk Soo Palace and the Soviet Consulate on 23 June demanded independence under Kim Koo."},{"t":"Police","lv":3,"p":[46,47],"pp":"31","s":"Horses received from Japan; over 80 percent of police lack pre-liberation experience; policewomen assigned 1 June; most May offenses rose."},{"t":"Prisons","lv":3,"p":[47,48],"pp":"32","s":"669 prisoners released, total 1,056; 20,554 confined in May (17,669 sentenced); chart of criminal offenses since March 1946."},{"t":"Fires","lv":3,"p":[49,50],"pp":"34","s":"92 fires in May killed three, injured 49 and cost 116,404,472 yen; Kyonggi-do had the most fires."},{"t":"Internal Security","lv":3,"p":[49,51],"pp":"34","s":"Fourth class of 102 opened at the Constabulary Officers' Training School on 6 June; a three-month refresher course began 16 June."},{"t":"Part III: Economic","lv":1,"p":[52,89],"pp":"37","s":"Coal, salt, currency, trade, railroads, power and prices: April coal 31,318 tons, May salt 32,207 tons, notes at 18.0 billion yen, imports up 521.8 percent."},{"t":"Section 1. Natural Resources","lv":2,"p":[54,59],"pp":"39","s":"Rice transplanting from 16 June, New Korea Company operating 661,000 acres, fishing fleet census, forest erosion, and April coal output of 31,318 tons."},{"t":"Agriculture","lv":3,"p":[54,55],"pp":"39","s":"Fertilizer distribution of 57,953 tons, grain collection policy, New Korea Company income near 1 billion yen, Ordinance No. 140 protecting draft cattle."},{"t":"Fisheries","lv":3,"p":[55,56],"pp":"40","s":"February fish landings of 25,027 metric tons; revised census counts 35,806 sailboats, 2,305 powered craft and 464,172 persons."},{"t":"Forestry","lv":3,"p":[57,57],"pp":"42","s":"38,323 chungbo of Kyonggi-do forest need erosion control; the cutting rate is 2.7 times annual timber growth."},{"t":"Mining","lv":3,"p":[57,59],"pp":"42","s":"Coal quota 35,500 tons versus 31,318 produced, stockpiles of 424,000 tons, 6,275 coal workers, and gold and silver output falling."},{"t":"Section 2. Industry","lv":2,"p":[60,65],"pp":"45","s":"Salt at 32,207 tons, fertilizer production halted, paper, tobacco, leather and rubber output, silk and cotton figures, and the Pusan stevedore strike."},{"t":"Heavy Industries and Manufacturing","lv":3,"p":[60,62],"pp":"45","s":"Noodle output of 512 tons, salt at a postwar high, 231 tons of paper, common brick at 6,000,000 pieces, and adoption of metric measures."},{"t":"Textiles","lv":3,"p":[62,63],"pp":"47","s":"Cocoon ceiling price set in May, raw silk production of 79,490 pounds in June, 47 mills producing 819,245 square yards of silk fabric."},{"t":"Labor","lv":3,"p":[63,64],"pp":"48","s":"Pusan stevedore strike of 28 May settled by conciliation; Inchon labor school graduated 43; industrial immunization program progressed."},{"t":"Section 3. Commerce","lv":2,"p":[66,83],"pp":"51","s":"Currency, bank deposits, revenues, Foreign Exchange Bank, vested property, trade tables, railroads, power, public works and communications."},{"t":"Finance","lv":3,"p":[66,70],"pp":"51","s":"Notes reached 18,035,603,028 yen; deposits peaked at 23.0 billion yen; May revenue 1,022,599,160 yen against expenditure 1,177,290,509; Foreign Exchange Bank created."},{"t":"Property Control","lv":3,"p":[71,71],"pp":"56","s":"New Korea Company transferred all but one industrial property by 17 May; vested property sales reached 28,897,599 yen in May."},{"t":"Imports and Exports","lv":3,"p":[71,74],"pp":"56","s":"Private-trade imports up 521.8 percent, lead and tungsten exports to the U.S., 30,880 tons of superphosphate from Japan, food imports and coal."},{"t":"Transportation","lv":3,"p":[74,75],"pp":"59","s":"6,452,743 rail passengers in May, 17,571 freight cars moved, 617 locomotives of which 271 operable; gasoline rose from 18 to 35 yen."},{"t":"Public Utilities","lv":3,"p":[75,77],"pp":"60","s":"Power generated rose from 19 to 29 million kWh; plant capacity tables; permanent Public Utilities Committee appointed 3 June."},{"t":"Public Works","lv":3,"p":[77,78],"pp":"62","s":"362,831,452 yen spent in 1946-47 for 3,132,209 man-days; highway bridges completed; Seoul waterworks under 20 gallons per capita."},{"t":"Communications","lv":3,"p":[78,82],"pp":"63","s":"Domestic mail and postal savings, 242 telegraph offices, 42,000 telephone subscribers, 12 radio stations, Civilian Supply Program I at 80 percent."},{"t":"Section 4. Rationing and Price Control","lv":2,"p":[84,89],"pp":"69","s":"Rice ration of .23 pound, summer-grain purchase prices, fertilizer allocations, petroleum distribution down 24 percent, and falling Seoul retail and wholesale prices."},{"t":"Food","lv":3,"p":[84,84],"pp":"69","s":"Summer grain prices set at 1,792 yen per suk of wheat and 2,064 for barley; 55,000 pounds of candy made into syrup for relief."},{"t":"Other Goods","lv":3,"p":[85,89],"pp":"70","s":"46 percent of fertilizer allocations sold, Korean coal prices up 30 to 50 percent, May allocations of matches, soap, shoes and cloth."},{"t":"Part IV: Social","lv":1,"p":[90,107],"pp":"75","s":"Public health, refugees and repatriation, education, culture, religion and public information: cholera immunization, 102,185 refugees, 9,129,922 textbooks, franchise poll and press opinion."},{"t":"Section 1. Public Health and Welfare","lv":2,"p":[92,97],"pp":"77","s":"Cholera immunization, 348 licensed veterinarians, 4,863 renewed doctors, flood relief, low-cost feeding, 102,185 refugees and 1,691 repatriated Koreans."},{"t":"Preventive Medicine","lv":3,"p":[92,92],"pp":"77","s":"Cholera immunization of coastal areas and large inland cities completed; 1,011 typhoid cases in May."},{"t":"Veterinary Affairs","lv":3,"p":[92,92],"pp":"77","s":"348 veterinarians licensed, 49 of 50 Japanese horses passed quarantine, 37 milk plants and 715 slaughterhouses."},{"t":"Medical and Hospital Affairs","lv":3,"p":[93,93],"pp":"78","s":"4,863 doctors renewed licenses, 7,840 lepers in four institutions, 18 nursing teachers graduated 30 June."},{"t":"Supply","lv":3,"p":[93,94],"pp":"78","s":"25,288,900 cc of cholera vaccine produced since 1 March, 19,520,550 distributed; biologicals table."},{"t":"Narcotics","lv":3,"p":[94,94],"pp":"79","s":"Taegu investigation arrested eight and seized 2.25 kilograms of opium."},{"t":"Public Assistance","lv":3,"p":[94,96],"pp":"79","s":"Flood relief committees, low-cost feeding program, 24,501 refugees in June, seven camps with 74,637, housing for 9,092 completed."},{"t":"Repatriation","lv":3,"p":[96,97],"pp":"81","s":"1,691 Koreans repatriated 1-29 June, 1,546 from Japan; 199 Japanese repatriated from Korea."},{"t":"Section 2. Education, Culture and Religion","lv":2,"p":[98,103],"pp":"83","s":"Seoul National University morale, school finance survey, textbooks, adult education, libraries with 442,979 volumes, Youth Movement and Oslo delegates."},{"t":"Education","lv":3,"p":[98,102],"pp":"83","s":"Survey of 73 elementary and 39 secondary schools, 9,129,922 textbooks, school statistics for 1 May, eight adult education conferences."},{"t":"Culture","lv":3,"p":[102,103],"pp":"87","s":"Three Seoul libraries hold 442,979 volumes; Youth Movement about 150,000 members; Boy Scout handbook; support for Korean athletes at the 1948 Olympics."},{"t":"Religion","lv":3,"p":[103,103],"pp":"88","s":"Six delegates left 8 June for the World Conference of Christian Youth at Oslo; Americans gave $2,500 for a Seoul church organ."},{"t":"Section 3. Public Information","lv":2,"p":[104,107],"pp":"89","s":"Leaflets on the Joint Commission, 35 films reviewed, radio weather forecasts, a franchise poll of 1,149 persons, World News Report and press comment."},{"t":"Information Programs","lv":3,"p":[104,104],"pp":"89","s":"600,000 leaflets airdropped and 400,000 more distributed; posters on food conservation and narcotics control."},{"t":"Motion Pictures","lv":3,"p":[104,105],"pp":"89","s":"Newsreel on civil-service examinations released 9 June; 35 films reviewed for release."},{"t":"Radio","lv":3,"p":[105,105],"pp":"90","s":"Joint Commission interviews broadcast 25 June; Korean Broadcasting System began daily weather forecasts."},{"t":"Press and Public Opinion","lv":3,"p":[105,107],"pp":"90","s":"Poll: 55 percent of ages 20-39 favored a 20-year voting age; press split over Rhee, trusteeship and cooperation with the Commission."},{"t":"Map of Korea","lv":1,"p":[108,108],"pp":"93","s":"Foldout map of Korea reproduced as an image; the page holds no readable text beyond a 'Digitized by Google' stamp."},{"t":"Back matter","lv":1,"p":[109,117],"pp":"","s":"University of Virginia Alderman Library due-date slip, Google Books digitization pages and library barcode leaves."}],"by":"claude-sonnet (Claude Code subagent)"},{"id":"K-21-v2","ia":"summation-usamg-activities-korea-no-21-1947-06-v2","s":"K","no":21,"d":"1947-06","d2":"","t":"Summation of United States Army Military Government Activities in Korea, No. 21 (June 1947)","v":"Second copy","pg":116,"o":"Summation No. 21 covers USAMGIK activities in South Korea for June 1947, with economic statistics mostly for April and May. The Korean Interim Legislative Assembly ended its long franchise-law dispute on 27 June, setting the minimum voting age at 23 after Speaker Kimm Kiu Sic's resignation threat. The U.S.-U.S.S.R. Joint Commission issued Communique No. 11, circulated questionnaires on the provisional government's charter and platform to parties, and moved to Pyongyang for consultations. Rightist ranks split over participation. Economic sections report record April coal output, record salt output, rising Bank of Chosen note circulation, the new Korean Foreign Exchange Bank, a revised income tax law, rail and power figures, and grain-import tables. Social sections cover cholera immunization, refugees from the North (102,185 since January), repatriation, textbooks, school statistics, the National Youth Movement and press reaction. The volume is a Digitized-by-Google University of Virginia copy, useful to historians for the Joint Commission texts and detailed statistics.","h":["The Interim Legislative Assembly passed the General Franchise Law on 27 June with a compromise minimum voting age of 23 (40 votes to 26 for 21), after Speaker Kimm Kiu Sic and Vice-speakers Choe Tong O and Yun Ki Sop resigned over a 25-year requirement and withdrew on 9 June.","Joint Communique No. 11 (12 June) set the method of consulting Korean parties; 344 parties had obtained copies by 19 June, applications were due 23 June, answers extended from 1 July to 5 July, and the delegations left for Pyongyang on 25-26 June.","The Hankuk Democratic Party decided to join Joint Commission deliberations, splitting the Rightists led by Syngman Rhee and Kim Koo, while the South Korea Labor Party named Pak Hun Tung its representative and demanded Rhee and Kim be expelled.","April coal production reached 31,318 metric tons, the highest since liberation but 4,182 tons short of the 35,500-ton quota; anthracite supplies of 35,305 tons met only part of the 85,000-ton requirement.","Bank of Chosen notes in circulation rose by 617,893,769 yen to 18,035,603,028 yen on 30 June; government revenues rose 22.4 percent in May to 1,022,599,160 yen against expenditures of 1,177,290,509 yen.","An ordinance of 16 June created the Korean Foreign Exchange Bank, capitalized at 500,000,000 yen with the Interim Government subscribing 200,000,000; the revised Third Income Tax Law took effect 21 June with rates of 3 to 90 percent.","Refugee movement from North Korea totaled 24,501 in June (102,185 for 1947 to 28 June); seven 38th-parallel camps cared for 74,637 persons and 9,092 refugee housing units were complete by 30 June.","A Pusan stevedore strike began 28 May and was settled through conciliation with wage increases under the Wage Rate and Incentive Board plan; salt production hit a postwar high of 32,207 metric tons in May."],"k":["Joint Commission","Joint Communique No. 11","General Franchise Law","Interim Legislative Assembly","Kimm Kiu Sic","Syngman Rhee","Kim Koo","coal production","Korean Foreign Exchange Bank","Bank of Chosen notes","refugees from North Korea","repatriation","New Korea Company","cholera immunization","Korean National Youth Movement"],"toc":[{"t":"Front matter","lv":1,"p":[1,14],"pp":"","s":"Library covers, title pages for Summation No. 21 (June 1947), table of contents and list of charts, maps and graphs."},{"t":"Part I: General","lv":1,"p":[15,24],"pp":"1","s":"Overview of June 1947: the franchise law, Joint Commission consultations, coal and finance news, health, refugees, education and public information."},{"t":"Section 1. Political Activities","lv":2,"p":[17,18],"pp":"3","s":"Speaker Kimm's resignation threat and the 27 June franchise law (voting age 23), the Rightist split, Joint Commission Communique No. 11 with 344 parties applying, and the 25 June move to Pyongyang."},{"t":"Section 2. Economic Activities","lv":2,"p":[19,22],"pp":"5","s":"Rice transplanting of 2,824,466 acres, April coal at 31,318 tons, salt at 32,207 tons, the new Foreign Exchange Bank, note circulation of 18,035,603,028 yen, and summer-grain prices."},{"t":"Section 3. Social Activities","lv":2,"p":[23,24],"pp":"9","s":"Cholera immunization, 4,863 renewed doctors, 102,185 refugees from the North, 9,129,922 textbooks distributed, the youth movement, and a poll favoring a 20-year voting age."},{"t":"Part II: Political","lv":1,"p":[25,50],"pp":"11","s":"Franchise law and Joint Commission documents, plus legal and public safety matters: police, prisons and fires."},{"t":"Section 1. Government and Politics","lv":2,"p":[27,44],"pp":"13","s":"Assembly resignations and the 23-year voting-age compromise, Rightist and Leftist maneuvering, full text of Joint Communique No. 11, Decisions 5 and 6 questionnaires, and the 25 June Seoul meeting."},{"t":"Section 2. Legal and Public Safety","lv":2,"p":[45,50],"pp":"31","s":"Orderly Rightist demonstration of 23 June, 80 percent of police lacking pre-liberation experience, 20,554 prisoners in May, 92 fires, and Constabulary training courses."},{"t":"Part III: Economic","lv":1,"p":[51,88],"pp":"37","s":"Coal, salt, silk, currency, trade, railroads, power, communications and price statistics, including record April coal and the Foreign Exchange Bank."},{"t":"Section 1. Natural Resources","lv":2,"p":[53,58],"pp":"39","s":"Rice transplanting, New Korea Company operating 661,000 acres, February fish landings of 25,027 tons, Kyonggi-do forest erosion, and April coal output of 31,318 tons."},{"t":"Section 2. Industry","lv":2,"p":[59,64],"pp":"45","s":"Flour, noodle, salt, paper and brick output, a silk ceiling price of 54.40 yen, cotton and silk cloth production, and the Pusan stevedore strike settlement."},{"t":"Section 3. Commerce","lv":2,"p":[65,82],"pp":"51","s":"Note circulation of 18.0 billion yen, the Foreign Exchange Bank, revised income tax, vested property sales, May import jump, rail and power data, public works spending of 362,831,452 yen."},{"t":"Section 4. Rationing and Price Control","lv":2,"p":[83,88],"pp":"69","s":"Basic rice ration of about 0.23 pound, summer-grain purchase prices (wheat 1,792 yen per suk), 46 percent of fertilizer sold, petroleum distribution down 24 percent, falling April free-market prices."},{"t":"Part IV: Social","lv":1,"p":[89,106],"pp":"75","s":"Refugee relief, school statistics and press commentary on the Joint Commission, within public health, education, culture, religion and public information."},{"t":"Section 1. Public Health and Welfare","lv":2,"p":[91,96],"pp":"77","s":"Cholera immunization, 348 veterinarians, 7,840 lepers, flood-relief and low-cost feeding programs, 24,501 June refugees, 9,092 refugee houses and 1,691 Koreans repatriated."},{"t":"Section 2. Education, Culture and Religion","lv":2,"p":[97,102],"pp":"83","s":"Seoul National University morale, school financing survey, 9,129,922 textbooks, 442,979 library volumes, National Youth Movement at 150,000 members, Boy Scout handbook and Oslo delegates."},{"t":"Section 3. Public Information","lv":2,"p":[103,106],"pp":"89","s":"600,000 leaflets dropped on Joint Commission news, 35 films reviewed, radio weather forecasts, a suffrage-age poll of 1,149 persons, and press reaction to the Rightist division over cooperation."},{"t":"Map of Korea","lv":1,"p":[107,107],"pp":"93","s":"Fold-out map of Korea listed in the charts, maps and graphs index."},{"t":"Back matter","lv":1,"p":[108,116],"pp":"","s":"University of Virginia library due-date slip, blank pages, barcode page and back cover of the digitized copy."}],"by":"claude-sonnet (Claude Code subagent)"},{"id":"J-22","ia":"summation-non-military-activities-japan-no-22-1947-07","s":"J","no":22,"d":"1947-07","d2":"","t":"Summation of Non-Military Activities in Japan, No. 22 (July 1947)","v":"","pg":149,"o":"Summation No. 22 covers non-military activities in Occupied Japan for July 1947, under the Katayama coalition Cabinet. Its centrepiece is the Economic Stabilization Board's first-ever \"White Paper\" (4 July), which reproduces in full the Government's account of deficits in state finance, industry and household budgets, exhausted stockpiles, inflation, falling labor productivity and the food and fuel crisis. Political coverage follows the proposed nationalization of coal mines, the Super-Emergency Food Program, civil-service reform demands, party realignment rumours, the Seko hoarded-materials scandal and further purge measures. Economic sections give June/May statistics for agriculture and land reform, coal, metals, chemicals, housing, textiles, power, communications, labor (union mergers, the 5 July standard-wage schedule, 12-percent coal wage rise), foreign trade resumption, rationing, finance, liquidation, antitrust (Fair Trade Commission) and property control. Social sections report typhus/DDT campaigns, repatriation, new education workshops and media activity. Historians will find dense monthly series and primary-source Japanese policy statements.","h":["On 4 July the Economic Stabilization Board released Japan's first \"White Paper\" on the economy, citing exhausted stockpiles, inflation, delayed remedies and labor productivity below 40 percent of prewar, and warning of subsidy cuts, heavier taxes and cancelled ration days.","A June price stabilization program set official prices on cost of production, capping 13 key commodities at 65 times 1934-36 levels, backed by a 12-billion-yen subsidy fund and wages averaging 1,600 yen a month.","Local Land Commissions bought 536,745 acres of tenant land in July, 131,192 acres above plan, bringing land purchased for resale under the Land Reform Law to 853,506 acres of a 3,712,195-acre program.","Coal control dominated politics: a Social Democratic committee voted 3 July to nationalize mines, Democrats and Liberals resisted, and coal workers and operators agreed on 25 July to a 12-percent sliding-scale wage rise.","Liberal Diet member Koichi Seko charged that 50 billion yen of war materials were hoarded in hiding, and the House of Representatives appointed a 30-man investigating committee on 25 July.","Staple deliveries ran up to 21.6 days behind in major cities (Fukuoka), so the Cabinet approved a Super-Emergency Food Program on 23 July with fertilizer and silk bonuses for farmers delivering above 110-percent quotas; 41 percent of the July ration was imported.","The Fair Trade Commission was established 1 July and its seven commissioners appointed 14 July; 92 control companies were designated for liquidation, and Mitsui and Mitsubishi Trading Companies were ordered dissolved.","War crimes trials had tried 286 defendants by 24 July (276 convicted, 26 sentenced to death), and Japanese repatriation from 30 June to 27 July totaled 65,672, mostly from Soviet-controlled areas."],"k":["Economic Stabilization Board","White Paper","price stabilization","land reform","coal nationalization","Katayama Cabinet","Seko case","purge","Fair Trade Commission","labor unions","food rationing","repatriation","war crimes trials","Zaibatsu liquidation","foreign trade resumption","Occupation"],"toc":[{"t":"Front matter","lv":1,"p":[1,5],"pp":"","s":"Cover for Summation No. 22 (July 1947), the table of contents for Parts I-IV, and the list of 114 charts, maps and graphs."},{"t":"Part I: General","lv":1,"p":[6,13],"pp":"1","s":"Digest of the month: no SCAP reorganization, the 4 July White Paper and coal-control politics, June production and labor items, finance, property control, and social, education and media activity."},{"t":"Section 1. Organization Under SCAP","lv":2,"p":[7,7],"pp":"3","s":"Reports no change in the organization of General Headquarters, SCAP, during July."},{"t":"Section 2. Political Activities","lv":2,"p":[7,8],"pp":"3","s":"Covers the 4 July White Paper, coal-control disputes, civil-service demands, the 12-25 July Diet recess, Cabinet Order No. 119 on the purge, and 1,870 pending war-crimes investigations."},{"t":"Section 3. Economic Activities","lv":2,"p":[8,10],"pp":"5","s":"Digest of agriculture (853,506 acres of land bought), coal and oil, heavy industry, textiles, transport, the 12-percent coal wage rise, trade, rationing, finance and the new Fair Trade Commission."},{"t":"Section 4. Social Activities","lv":2,"p":[11,13],"pp":"11","s":"Digest of DDT dusting of 1,500,000 schoolchildren, 65,672 repatriates, the first education workshops, 1,213 foreign missionaries, women's broadcasts and SCAP Library attendance of 686 daily."},{"t":"Part II: Political","lv":1,"p":[13,42],"pp":"15","s":"Politics dominated by the White Paper, coal control and the Seko case, followed by May-June public safety statistics, judicial administration and war-crimes processing."},{"t":"Section 1. Government and Politics","lv":2,"p":[14,37],"pp":"17","s":"Reproduces the 4 July White Paper, then covers coal nationalization, the 23 July Super-Emergency Food Program, party maneuvers, the Seko scandal, the purge, personnel changes and governors' autonomy complaint."},{"t":"Analysis of Japan's Economic Position and Text of the White Paper","lv":3,"p":[14,33],"pp":"17","s":"Analysis and text of the White Paper: government, industry and household deficits, prices, wages, food, coal, power, transport, finance, labor and trade; cost of living cited at 60-70 times prewar."},{"t":"Political Developments","lv":3,"p":[33,35],"pp":"","s":"Coal-control bill delayed by Democratic refusal; bonuses for farmers delivering excess rice; 5 July civil-service reform demands; party statements by Liberals, Democrats, Cooperatives and Communists."},{"t":"The Diet","lv":3,"p":[35,36],"pp":"","s":"House of Representatives held 143 Social Democrats among 466 seats; the Diet recessed 12-25 July; Seko alleged 50 billion yen in hidden war goods and a special committee was named."},{"t":"The Purge","lv":3,"p":[36,37],"pp":"","s":"569,400 persons screened since 4 January; Cabinet Order No. 119 (2 July) widened designation; Butoku-kai officials presumed purgeable; 41 media firms added to the blacklist."},{"t":"Personnel Changes and Local Government","lv":3,"p":[37,37],"pp":"","s":"Ashida was named deputy Prime Minister on 3 July; prefectural governors complained on 5 July that central branch offices violate local autonomy."},{"t":"Section 2. Public Safety","lv":2,"p":[37,39],"pp":"63","s":"May crime: 146,045 offenses and 69,893 arrests; June rail accidents killed 97; prison population 74,720; 1,361 fires; Tokyo adopted the azimuthal fire-spotting method."},{"t":"Section 3. Legal Affairs and War Crimes","lv":2,"p":[40,42],"pp":"69","s":"A 15-man committee recommended 30 Supreme Court candidates on 18 July; atrocity cases fell to 1,870; 286 defendants tried by 24 July, 276 convicted and 26 sentenced to death."},{"t":"Part III: Economic","lv":1,"p":[42,130],"pp":"73","s":"June and July data for agriculture, mining, industry, utilities, communications, labor, trade, price control, finance and property control, mostly showing flat output limited by coal, power and materials shortages."},{"t":"Section 1. Agriculture and Fisheries","lv":2,"p":[43,47],"pp":"75","s":"Land purchases reached 853,506 acres; religious-institution land made subject to reform; cool weather delayed northern crops; June fish catch fell 30,367 tons; 686 whales taken January-June."},{"t":"Section 2. Forestry and Mining","lv":2,"p":[47,53],"pp":"83","s":"June logs 545,856,000 board feet; coal 2,128,000 tons with stockpiles at 738,800; crude oil 17,790 kiloliters with three new producers; 14 of 25 minerals gained in May."},{"t":"Section 3. Heavy Industries","lv":2,"p":[54,65],"pp":"97","s":"Coke 153,500 tons; copper output cut by a mine strike; record 31,551 housing starts; 2,259,874 dwellings destroyed in major cities; chemical index 44 percent of requirements; 555 machine tools built."},{"t":"Coke, Metal Industries, Rubber, Petroleum and Cement","lv":3,"p":[54,57],"pp":"97","s":"Pig iron up 6 percent and steel up 13 percent over May; aluminum fell to 871 tons; crude rubber imports 2,452 tons; 36 cement plants made 100,502 tons."},{"t":"Construction and Reconstruction of Bombed Cities","lv":3,"p":[57,58],"pp":"103","s":"June building permits 33,229; second coal-miners' housing program (7,843 new, 14,645 repairs); 433,165 dwellings rebuilt in bombed cities against a shortage over 4,000,000."},{"t":"Shipbuilding and Chemical Industries","lv":3,"p":[59,62],"pp":"106","s":"438 vessels repaired in a month; calcium superphosphate 67,340 tons; cyanamide hit a postwar high of 24,903 tons; sulfuric acid fell to 122,755 tons for lack of pyrite."},{"t":"Machinery","lv":3,"p":[62,65],"pp":"112","s":"Industrial machinery output 444.6 million yen; 13 steam locomotives and 95 passenger cars built; textile machinery value fell to 176 million yen as new units dropped 53 percent."},{"t":"Section 4. Manufacturing","lv":2,"p":[65,75],"pp":"119","s":"Canned food fell from 3,012 to 692 tons; paper output a record 29,900 tons; trucks 660, bicycles 16,028; sewing machines a postwar-high 6,689; toys 7,300,540 pieces."},{"t":"Section 5. Textile Industries","lv":2,"p":[75,85],"pp":"139","s":"Cotton yarn peaked at 39,004,000 pounds; raw silk dropped to 9,171 bales; filament rayon hit 1,357,000 pounds; first wool cargo (7,481 bales) arrived from Australia in June."},{"t":"Section 6. Transportation and Public Utilities","lv":2,"p":[86,90],"pp":"161","s":"Registered trucks and buses rose to 94,731 at 65.5-percent operation; 286,252,634 rail passengers; power generation 2,899,884,000 kWh, down 3 percent, with restrictions only in Chugoku and Kyushu."},{"t":"Section 7. Communications","lv":2,"p":[91,96],"pp":"171","s":"9,773 new telephones installed; Ministry of Communications took over ship radio licensing (1,683 stations) on 15 July; mail volume fell after the 1 April rate rise; receiver tubes up 18 percent."},{"t":"Section 8. Labor","lv":2,"p":[97,104],"pp":"183","s":"Unions numbered 21,963 with 5,529,086 members; Transport Workers' Council formed 9 July; NCIU convention 10-14 July; standard wages released 5 July; coal wage rose 12 percent on 25 July."},{"t":"Labor Legislation and Employer/Labor Organizations","lv":3,"p":[97,99],"pp":"183","s":"Labor Ministry Bill submitted; Coal Industry Reconstruction Council launched a six-point program 1 July; Kansai Economic Reconstruction Council opened 8 July; 600,000-member transport council formed."},{"t":"Labor Relations, Negotiations and Disputes","lv":3,"p":[99,101],"pp":"187","s":"110 unfair-practice charges since the Trade-union Law; Mitsubishi Heavy Industry agreed a 2,600-yen base for 50,000 workers; May disputes numbered 72 involving 107,110 workers; no major July stoppages."},{"t":"Labor Education, Wages and Employment","lv":3,"p":[102,104],"pp":"193","s":"Kyoto Labor College opened 1 July with 135 students; standard wage schedule for 35 industries; 130 vocational counsellors assigned; 20,785 coal miners recruited in June."},{"t":"Section 9. Imports and Exports","lv":2,"p":[105,113],"pp":"199","s":"Food imports fell 3 percent; cotton cloth shipped for the first time since the war to Argentina, Ceylon, Kenya and Turkey; first machinery shipment to the Ryukyus; 50 horses sent to Korea."},{"t":"Section 10. Rationing and Price Control","lv":2,"p":[113,119],"pp":"215","s":"Price stabilization program with 65-times ceiling; staple deliveries up to 21.6 days late (Fukuoka); emergency 26-day July ration; 219,300 tons of imported food released; 90,088 violations in June."},{"t":"Price Stabilization and Food","lv":3,"p":[113,116],"pp":"215","s":"Government stocks held 23.2 days of staple food on 30 June; farmers allowed 8-kilogram rice parcels to relatives; fresh fish 24,663 tons and vegetables 32,254 tons reached six cities."},{"t":"Producers' and Consumers' Goods; Violations and Controls","lv":3,"p":[116,120],"pp":"220","s":"Fertilizer allotments linked to grain collections; crude oil raised to 3,451 yen per kiloliter; utility rates up one to four times; 582,077 price and ration violations January-June."},{"t":"Section 11. Finance","lv":2,"p":[120,126],"pp":"229","s":"Six foreign banks licensed; Bank of Japan notes reached 143,745,746,371 yen on 31 July; 28 organizations closed in June; liquidation proceeds 2.92 billion yen; national debt 289,007,000,000 yen."},{"t":"Section 12. Property Control and Reparations","lv":2,"p":[126,130],"pp":"241","s":"Neutral and Allied property totaled 909,648,000 yen; 299 looted ships reported; 2.1 billion yen authorized for rehabilitation, mostly coal; Fair Trade Commission seated 14 July; Indian reparations delegation arrived."},{"t":"Property Control","lv":3,"p":[126,129],"pp":"241","s":"1,580 reports processed in a month; property of 1,055 suspected war criminals in custody; SCAP held 257.5 million dollars in precious metals; yen custody account rose 40 million yen."},{"t":"Antitrust and Cartels; Reparations","lv":3,"p":[129,130],"pp":"","s":"92 control companies closed; Mitsui and Mitsubishi Trading ordered dissolved; 56 Zaibatsu family members told to deliver securities; Antitrust Act fully effective 20 July; 32 machine-tool plants inspected."},{"t":"Part IV: Social","lv":1,"p":[131,148],"pp":"351","s":"Public health, welfare and repatriation figures, then education reforms, religion, cultural monuments and the month's information and media programs."},{"t":"Section 1. Public Health and Welfare","lv":2,"p":[132,139],"pp":"353","s":"1,500,000 students dusted with DDT; 644 typhus cases typed; 3,363 hospitals; penicillin 355,560,000 units; welfare payments 285,248,000 yen; 65,672 repatriated; pensions raised 300 percent."},{"t":"Preventive Medicine and Veterinary Affairs","lv":3,"p":[132,135],"pp":"353","s":"Hokkaido typhus vaccination reached 638,857 full doses; Okayama encephalitis vaccine for 14,473; diphtheria, scarlet fever and smallpox fell below May; animal disease and meat inspection tables."},{"t":"Medical Supply, Narcotics and Public Assistance","lv":3,"p":[135,138],"pp":"360","s":"Medical supplies 337,828,289 yen; 10-percent DDT dust 1,162,796 kg; 146 narcotics violators; new relief grant scale (10.80 yen daily for one person in large cities); LARA shipments arrived."},{"t":"Social Security, Repatriation and Vital Statistics","lv":3,"p":[138,139],"pp":"365","s":"Compensation insurance moved to the Labor and Transportation ministries; 43,850 repatriated from Soviet areas; May births 201,070 against 94,232 deaths under new reporting procedures."},{"t":"Section 2. Education, Religion and Media of Expression","lv":2,"p":[140,148],"pp":"369","s":"First workshops for teachers of handicapped children (181 enrolled); 3.1 billion yen for lower secondary schools; chrysanthemum crest dropped from stamps; Kyoto cultural sites map; press, radio and film activity."},{"t":"Education","lv":3,"p":[140,143],"pp":"369","s":"University Accreditation Association formed 8 July; 62,309,327 textbooks authorized; correspondence courses planned; Chuo University won the first Emperor's Cup track meet."},{"t":"Religion, Arts and Monuments","lv":3,"p":[143,144],"pp":"374","s":"Cabinet orders on state land used by shrines and temples; Fukuoka Village Shinto Directive conviction; 1,213 foreign missionaries; 1,066 National Treasure objects inspected."},{"t":"Media of Expression","lv":3,"p":[144,148],"pp":"376","s":"42 opinion-survey agencies; land-reform kami-shibai campaign; Katayama's first broadcast to women; Saga newspaper convention 14-19 July; press criticism of Cabinet and Diet; film, theater and music notes."},{"t":"Map of Japan","lv":1,"p":[149,149],"pp":"387","s":"Closing map of Japan, with a U.S. Army Corps of Engineers credit."}],"by":"claude-sonnet (Claude Code subagent)"},{"id":"K-22","ia":"south-korean-interim-government-activities-no-22-1947-07","s":"K","no":22,"d":"1947-07","d2":"","t":"South Korean Interim Government Activities, No. 22 (July 1947)","v":"","pg":233,"o":"Summation No. 22 covers South Korean Interim Government activities for July 1947, prepared by the Statistical Research Division, Office of Administration, under Acting Military Governor Brig. Gen. C. G. Helmick. The month is dominated by the stalled US-USSR Joint Commission over which parties may be consulted, the 19 July assassination of Lyuh Woon-hyung, and the Interim Legislative Assembly's passage of the anti-collaborator law and second reading of a Temporary Constitution. Administration reports detail the new policy for selling vested Japanese residences and small businesses, rent increases, New Korea Company landholdings, Helmick's objections to the franchise law, and the 1948-49 budget directive. Social sections give refugee quotas, public health guidance to provincial advisers, vaccine and narcotics tables, school statistics, Korean National Youth data, and a long press digest. Part III (Economic) is not included. Historians will find full texts of Joint Commission statements, directives and ordinances.","h":["Lyuh Woon-hyung, leader of the Laboring People's Party, was shot dead in his car in Seoul on 19 July; Han Chi-kun, a young man from North Korea, was arrested, and General Hodge publicly deplored the killing.","The Interim Legislative Assembly passed the Special Law Dealing with National Traitors, Collaborators, and Profiteers (formerly the Anti-Collaborator Bill) on 2 July, and by 31 July had passed most articles of a Temporary Constitution on second reading.","On 12 July Helmick announced the sale of vested Japanese small businesses and urban residences to Koreans: buyers pay at least 20 percent down, contracts run up to ten years, and provincial advisory committees screen purchasers.","Helmick's 21 July letter asked the Assembly to reconsider the franchise law, objecting to the voting age of 23, disqualification of ex-prisoners and alleged collaborators, a special northern district, the written-ballot requirement and Supreme Court jurisdiction.","The US-USSR Joint Commission deadlocked from the 37th to the 42nd meeting and beyond over consulting parties linked to the Anti-Trusteeship Committee; Gen. Brown and Soviet chief Shtykov issued rival statements on 15, 21, 27 and 31 July.","A Property Custodian rental decree let rents on vested residences rise up to 300 percent for government employees and up to fifty times 1937 levels for others; the New Korea Company controls 309,658 chungbo of agricultural land.","The National Refugee Board suspended refugee settlement in Seoul from 1 July and set provincial quotas (20 percent each for Kyongsang-pukto, Cholla-pukto and Cholla-namdo); 38,728 people passed through the 38th-parallel camps by June.","Acting Military Governor Helmick said US civilian supply aid to Korea from May 1946 to March 1947 totalled ninety-one million dollars, and rebutted rumors that Korean food was being exported."],"k":["Joint Commission","Anti-Trusteeship Committee","Lyuh Woon-hyung assassination","Anti-Collaborator law","Temporary Constitution","franchise law","vested Japanese property","New Korea Company","Helmick","Hodge","refugees","civilian supply aid","public health","National Police and prisons","Korean press digest"],"toc":[{"t":"Front matter","lv":1,"p":[1,4],"pp":"","s":"Cover, title page for Summation No. 22 (July 1947), foreword signed by Lt. Col. William Wall O'Hearn, and table of contents."},{"t":"Part I: General","lv":1,"p":[5,18],"pp":"1","s":"Overview of July: sale of Japanese property, franchise law, Legislative Assembly action, Lyuh's assassination and the Joint Commission, plus a social summary. The economic section was deferred to the National Economic Board."},{"t":"Section 1. Political Activities","lv":2,"p":[6,13],"pp":"1","s":"Summarizes sale of Japanese property, rent increases, governor transfers, Assembly laws, Lyuh's 19 July assassination, the Joint Commission consultation dispute, and Constabulary and Coast Guard activities."},{"t":"Section 2. Economic Activities","lv":2,"p":[14,14],"pp":"9","s":"Placeholder page stating that the economic section is to be submitted by the National Economic Board."},{"t":"Section 3. Social Activities","lv":2,"p":[15,18],"pp":"10","s":"Provincial health advisers' 14 July conference, UNRRA aid of about one million dollars, a penicillin plan, Seoul National University's year-end, Olympic celebrations, and bulletin and radio coverage of the Joint Commission."},{"t":"Part II: Political","lv":1,"p":[19,143],"pp":"14","s":"Government and political reporting (property policy, Assembly, Joint Commission texts), then legal developments, police, prisons and internal security. Part III (Economic) was not submitted."},{"t":"Section 1. Government and Politics","lv":2,"p":[20,111],"pp":"14","s":"Full texts of property sale directives and Helmick's franchise-law letter, Assembly debates on collaborators and the constitution, party trends, Joint Commission statements, interzone mail and Foreign Affairs."},{"t":"Administration","lv":3,"p":[20,52],"pp":"14","s":"Policy and directives for selling Japanese urban residences and small businesses, rent increases, New Korea Company's 309,658 chungbo, governor transfers, the franchise letter, the budget directive, and US aid statements."},{"t":"Korea Civil Service","lv":3,"p":[53,53],"pp":"47","s":"Training Division prepared the first Performance Rating Reports, due in the first fifteen days of August; clerical examinations continued in the provinces."},{"t":"Korean Interim Legislative Assembly","lv":3,"p":[54,83],"pp":"48","s":"Passage of the Special Law on traitors, collaborators and profiteers; second reading of the Temporary Constitution; a 39-22 vote returning governor transfers; the Ordinance 118 dispute; July attendance table."},{"t":"Political Activities","lv":3,"p":[84,86],"pp":"78","s":"Rightist 4 July conference demanded Civil Administrator Ahn's resignation; Leftists issued joint statements; Lyuh was assassinated 19 July; Wedemeyer was designated presidential envoy."},{"t":"US-USSR Joint Commission","lv":3,"p":[87,107],"pp":"81","s":"Pyongyang sessions 2-3 July, dispute over consulting Anti-Trusteeship Committee parties, statements by Brown and Shtykov, a message from Koreans in Japan, and membership tables of 118 South Korean groups."},{"t":"Interzone Activities","lv":3,"p":[108,108],"pp":"102","s":"Two mail exchanges with the Soviet zone, on 12 July (10,643 letters sent north) and 26 July (10,108 letters sent north)."},{"t":"Foreign Affairs","lv":3,"p":[109,111],"pp":"103","s":"18,672 Koreans apprehended entering Japan illegally, 300 permits for Chinese repatriation, Dr. Moon Chang-wook's US mission for fifty students, and July repatriation ship tables."},{"t":"Section 2. Legal and Public Safety","lv":2,"p":[112,143],"pp":"106","s":"Court dockets, juvenile courts and new ordinances, then public safety: Lyuh's assassination, provincial disturbances, National Police strength of 28,552, prison population of 19,833, and Constabulary and Coast Guard news."},{"t":"Legal","lv":3,"p":[112,128],"pp":"106","s":"Criminal dockets brought current by 20 July; 1,240 juvenile cases in five months; texts of Ordinances 142 (income tax), 143 (Crop Reporting Board), 145 (Foreign Exchange Bank), and Executive Order 4 making Korean official."},{"t":"Public Safety","lv":3,"p":[129,143],"pp":"123","s":"Law and order after Lyuh's assassination, police strength of 28,552, prison population of 19,833, and Constabulary and Coast Guard help to farmers, with statistical tables."},{"t":"Law and Order","lv":3,"p":[129,130],"pp":"123","s":"Lyuh Woon-hyung assassinated 19 July, General Hodge's statement, and mob clashes in Cholla-pukto, Kyongsang-namdo, Kyongsang-pukto and Chungchong-pukto."},{"t":"Police","lv":3,"p":[131,134],"pp":"125","s":"Crime Laboratory completed 45 cases in June (2,103 examinations to date); 3,500 Basic English manuals printed; police strength 28,552 and 1,977 installations."},{"t":"Prisons","lv":3,"p":[135,141],"pp":"129","s":"Kwangju Prison industries, 10,000 blankets received, and June tables: 19,833 prisoners against capacity of 21,600, 25 deaths, 340 in reformatories."},{"t":"Internal Security","lv":3,"p":[142,143],"pp":"138","s":"Constabulary and prisoners helped Kunsan farmers transplant rice; Coast Guard rescued a rice ship off Ullung-do on 26 July; supply receipts of 17,563 cots and 1,214 tents."},{"t":"Part IV: Social","lv":1,"p":[144,233],"pp":"138","s":"Public health and welfare, education and culture, and public information for July, including the health guidance letter, refugee quotas, vaccine and school tables, and a long press digest."},{"t":"Section 1. Public Health and Welfare","lv":2,"p":[145,193],"pp":"138","s":"Adviser conference and guidance letter, refugee quotas, welfare institutions, UNRRA and LARA aid, nursing, dental, pharmacy, veterinary and laboratory reports, sanitation and communicable disease figures."},{"t":"Administration","lv":3,"p":[145,165],"pp":"138","s":"Provincial health advisers met 14 July; the guidance letter covers disease control, vital statistics, veterinary and nursing advice, medical supply distribution and welfare; includes the May 1947 organizational chart."},{"t":"Public Welfare","lv":3,"p":[166,169],"pp":"159","s":"Refugee influx fell thirty percent in June; Seoul settlement suspended 1 July; provincial quotas set; cumulative repatriation totals; 38,728 people cared for in camps."},{"t":"Welfare Institutions and Agencies","lv":3,"p":[170,171],"pp":"163","s":"317 homeless children and adults placed in institutions on 24-25 July; UNRRA program closed 30 June after about one million dollars; first LARA supplies arrived."},{"t":"Women's Bureau","lv":3,"p":[172,172],"pp":"165","s":"Monday lectures on Korean industry, home economics classes for telephone exchange women, and a Women's Section opened in Seoul's Bureau of Public Welfare on 1 July."},{"t":"Medical Services","lv":3,"p":[173,173],"pp":"166","s":"Plan formulated for distributing and accounting for three metric tons of UNRRA penicillin."},{"t":"Nursing Affairs","lv":3,"p":[174,174],"pp":"167","s":"Inspection of hospitals in Pusan, Chinju, Taegu and Masan found lack of funds; nursing curriculum revised so third-year students can specialize."},{"t":"Dental Affairs","lv":3,"p":[175,176],"pp":"168","s":"Only 80 of 242 applicants admitted to the Dental College; three rural dental teams left 16 July; 491 licensed dentists in South Korea."},{"t":"Pharmaceutical Affairs","lv":3,"p":[177,180],"pp":"170","s":"Thirty of seventy new-medicine applications approved, 567 pharmacists registered, narcotics classes held, and monthly tables of confiscated narcotics from July 1946 to July 1947."},{"t":"Veterinary Affairs","lv":3,"p":[181,181],"pp":"174","s":"First veterinary medicine school established within Seoul National University; 175 bicycles sought for veterinarians short of transport."},{"t":"Laboratories","lv":3,"p":[182,186],"pp":"175","s":"Cholera vaccine production (5,375,300 cc on hand 25 July), veterinary biologicals consolidated at Pusan, and tables of vaccine stocks at Seoul, Anyang and Pusan institutes."},{"t":"Housing","lv":3,"p":[187,187],"pp":"180","s":"Winter 1946-47 study put South Korea's population at 19,300,000, up twenty-two percent since 1944; Seoul grew 38.2 percent."},{"t":"Vital Statistics","lv":3,"p":[188,188],"pp":"181","s":"Directive ended duplicate vital statistics transcripts previously prepared for the Census Division of the Office of Administration."},{"t":"Sanitation","lv":3,"p":[189,191],"pp":"182","s":"Inspectors sought the yellow dye \"killing sugar\" in restaurants and bakeries; rodent control continued; June tables of food and beverage establishments by province."},{"t":"Preventive Medicine","lv":3,"p":[192,193],"pp":"185","s":"2,864 communicable disease cases in June (1,073 typhoid), down about forty-five percent from June 1946; typhoid inoculation program to start about 15 September."},{"t":"Section 2. Education, Culture and Religion","lv":2,"p":[194,205],"pp":"187","s":"Seoul National University disturbances, summer institutes, textbook distribution, school statistics, Korea's admission to the 1948 Olympics, and Korean National Youth activity."},{"t":"Education","lv":3,"p":[194,201],"pp":"187","s":"Eight students expelled and 169 suspended at Seoul National University; 560 diplomas awarded; 282 attended Summer Institute; 427,760 textbooks distributed; school statistics at 31 May."},{"t":"Culture","lv":3,"p":[202,205],"pp":"195","s":"Olympic celebration at Seoul Stadium on 19 July before 50,000 spectators; National Youth held 13,080 discussion meetings, graduated 197 leaders, and had 147,481 members."},{"t":"Section 3. Public Information","lv":2,"p":[206,233],"pp":"199","s":"Information programs, newsreels, 341 hours of Korean Broadcasting System programming, publications, a newspaper circulation table, and a digest of press opinion on politics, the Joint Commission and Lyuh's assassination."},{"t":"Information Programs","lv":3,"p":[206,206],"pp":"199","s":"News posters moved indoors because of heavy rain; 1,067 discussion groups with 34,311 members organized by 7 July."},{"t":"Motion Pictures","lv":3,"p":[207,208],"pp":"200","s":"Monthly newsreel to start in August; Newsreel No. 21 issued 26 July; 30 films reviewed in July (18 Korean, 11 American, 1 Japanese)."},{"t":"Radio","lv":3,"p":[209,215],"pp":"202","s":"Text of Kim Yong-jeung's 8 July broadcast, first Man on the Street program on 27 July, 341 hours of KBS broadcasting, and a typical daily schedule."},{"t":"Publications","lv":3,"p":[216,218],"pp":"209","s":"Dr. C. C. Hahn's Principles of Democracy published; Farmers' Weekly suspended because of paper shortage; Marshall's 1 July press speech translated and distributed."},{"t":"Press and Public Opinion","lv":3,"p":[219,233],"pp":"212","s":"Circulation table of 21 Seoul dailies, then press comment on the franchise law, Joint Commission, Wedemeyer visit, Lyuh's death and Rhee-Hodge dispute; a poll found 67 percent favored the interim government."}],"by":"claude-sonnet (Claude Code subagent)"},{"id":"K-22-in-K-32","ia":"south-korean-interim-government-activities-no-32-1948-05","s":"K","no":22,"d":"1947-07","d2":"","t":"South Korean Interim Government Activities, No. 22 (July 1947): misbound pages","v":"Misbound pages in K-32","host":"K-32","rg":[[47,113],[134,137]],"kind":"misbound","found":"Pages stamped 'NND 785084' (the rest of the volume is 'NND 785083'); content dated to June-July 1947","pg":71,"o":"Pages 47-113 and 134-137 of the host PDF are stray pages of South Korean Interim Government Activities No. 22 (July 1947), misbound into the May 1948 issue; pages 114-133 are absent. The text is the tail of Section 1 (paragraphs 22-57) and fragments of Section 2 (Legal and Public Safety). Contents: Marshall's speech and Acting Military Governor Helmick's statements on American aid and food-export rumors; Korea Civil Service; the Interim Legislative Assembly's passage of the Anti-Collaborator Bill as the Special Law on National Traitors, Collaborators and Profiteers and its second reading of a Temporary Constitution; the dispute over transferring governors; Rightist and Leftist trends and the assassination of Lyuh Woon Hyung; verbatim American and Soviet statements on the Joint Commission deadlock over consultation with anti-trusteeship parties; mail exchanges, repatriation, courts, police installations and prison population. The identification as No. 22 / July 1947 is confirmed by the text: all dated events fall in July 1947 and the statistics refer to June 1947.","h":["The Assembly completed second reading of the Anti-Collaborator Bill on 2 July, retitling it the Special Law Dealing with National Traitors, Collaborators, and Profiteers, with a three-year limit and no third reading.","Second reading of the Temporary Constitution passed all chapters by 31 July, dropping \"democratic\" from the title 31 to 8, and began local government (Section 36) on 31 July.","On 9 July the Assembly voted 39 to 22 to return the proposed transfer of four officials, ruling it lacked jurisdiction; on 28 July Kim Pyong No argued Ordinance 118 left Military Government supreme.","Leftist leader Lyuh Woon Hyung was shot dead in Seoul on 19 July by Hahn Chi Kun, and the Nation Saving Society was ordered dissolved.","The Joint Commission deadlocked over consulting Anti-Trusteeship Committee parties; party tables showed 71 Rightist parties (19.3 million claimed members) against 75 Leftist (32.3 million).","The Acting Military Governor reported 91 million dollars of civilian supply aid, May 1946 to March 1947, including 395,836 metric tons of grain, and on 19 July denounced food-export rumors as malicious lies.","Lt. Gen. Albert C. Wedemeyer was announced as President Truman's special envoy to Korea, shifting Rightist attention from the Joint Commission.","June 1947 prisons held 19,833 inmates, of whom 17,216 were sentenced; South Korea had 1,977 police installations as of 1 February 1947."],"k":["South Korean Interim Government","July 1947","Interim Legislative Assembly","Anti-Collaborator Bill","Temporary Constitution","US-USSR Joint Commission","Anti-Trusteeship Committee","Lyuh Woon Hyung","Civilian Supply Program","Helmick","Brown","repatriation","prisons","Ordinance 118","franchise law"],"toc":[{"t":"Section 1 (continued): Government and Politics, paragraphs 22-57","lv":1,"p":[47,111],"pp":"41","s":"July 1947 general section, paragraphs 22-57: Marshall's speech and aid figures, civil service ratings, the Anti-Collaborator Bill and Temporary Constitution, Lyuh Woon Hyung's assassination, the Joint Commission deadlock, and interzone mail."},{"t":"American aid to Korea and food-export rumors","lv":2,"p":[47,52],"pp":"41","s":"Marshall's 1 July speech rejecting charges of American imperialism was released in Korean 8 July. On 9 July the Acting Military Governor reported 91 million dollars of aid, including 395,836 tons of grain; on 19 July he denounced food-export rumors."},{"t":"Korea Civil Service","lv":2,"p":[53,53],"pp":"47","s":"The Training Division completed Performance Rating Reports, required for the first time of provincial and national employees, due in the first fifteen days of August from staff with six months' service. Clerical examinations continued."},{"t":"Korean Interim Legislative Assembly: Anti-Collaborator Bill","lv":2,"p":[54,59],"pp":"48","s":"The Assembly passed the remaining collaborator and profiteer paragraphs and on 2 July retitled the measure the Special Law Dealing with National Traitors, Collaborators, and Profiteers, adopting a three-year limit and omitting a third reading."},{"t":"The Temporary Constitution of Democratic Korea (Chosun)","lv":2,"p":[60,77],"pp":"54","s":"A compromise revised draft was presented 7 July and passed to second reading 11 July despite Leftist protests. \"Democratic\" was dropped from the title, 31 to 8; chapters were adopted and local government began 31 July."},{"t":"Assignment of officials","lv":2,"p":[78,79],"pp":"72","s":"On 8 July the Assembly debated the proposed transfer of governors Park Keun Hon, Sunn Min Ho and Park Chong Man and Chung Il Hyung. On 9 July it voted 39 to 22 to return the communication for lack of jurisdiction."},{"t":"Clarification of Assembly authority (Ordinance 118)","lv":2,"p":[80,80],"pp":"74","s":"On 28 July Justice Director Kim Pyong No held that Ordinance 118 leaves Military Government supreme and able to legislate without Assembly approval; Suh U Sok and Yang Che Pak disputed this and cited the Assembly's power to review appointments."},{"t":"The Franchise Law","lv":2,"p":[81,82],"pp":"75","s":"On 31 July the Assembly took up the Acting Military Governor's 21 July letter on franchise provisions, including the age limit of twenty-three. Members defended it; the matter went to the Legislative and Judicial Committee, 44 to 0."},{"t":"Attendance of representatives, July 1947","lv":2,"p":[83,83],"pp":"77","s":"Table of Assembly sessions 102 to 120 in July 1947, with members present (55 on 15 July to 76 on 9 July, of 90 authorized) and presiding official. Quorum is 45 to hold session and 60 for measures."},{"t":"Political Activities: the Right","lv":2,"p":[84,84],"pp":"78","s":"Rightist hopes for the Joint Commission fell in July. On 4 July, 150 representatives of about seventy organizations rejected General Brown's criticism and Hodge's letter to Rhee, demanded a general election and Ahn's resignation."},{"t":"Political Activities: the Left","lv":2,"p":[85,85],"pp":"79","s":"The Left sought to exclude Rightists from consultation; nine parties issued statements on 10 and 19 July. Lyuh Woon Hyung was shot dead 19 July by Hahn Chi Kun, and the Nation Saving Society was ordered dissolved."},{"t":"Political Activities: General (Presidential envoy)","lv":2,"p":[86,86],"pp":"80","s":"Announcement that Lt. Gen. Albert C. Wedemeyer would go to Korea as President Truman's special envoy brightened Rightist outlook and shifted attention from the Joint Commission; the Leftist press barely mentioned it."},{"t":"US-USSR Joint Commission","lv":2,"p":[87,107],"pp":"81","s":"The Joint Commission met in Pyongyang 2-3 July, then deadlocked in Seoul over consultation with Anti-Trusteeship Committee parties. Brown (15 July) and Shtikov (21 July) stated positions; party tables list 71 Rightist and 75 Leftist parties."},{"t":"Interzone Activities","lv":2,"p":[108,108],"pp":"102","s":"Mail went north twice in July: 10,643 letters, 1,703 cards and 537 registered items on 12 July, and 10,108, 1,568 and 542 on 26 July. Smaller volumes came south each time."},{"t":"Foreign Affairs","lv":2,"p":[109,111],"pp":"103","s":"As of 26 July, 18,672 Koreans entering Japan illegally were apprehended in Japan and 416 in Korean waters; 300 permits went to Chinese for repatriation. Dr. Moon Chang Wook left 22 July to find sponsors for fifty students."},{"t":"Section 2 (fragment): Legal and Public Safety","lv":1,"p":[112,137],"pp":"105","s":"Fragment of Section 2 on legal and public safety: court docket and juvenile court figures, then police installations and the June 1947 prison population. Pages 114-133 are absent."},{"t":"Legal","lv":2,"p":[112,113],"pp":"106","s":"Chief Justice Kim Young Moo's directive brought criminal dockets current by 20 July. Juvenile courts tried 1,240 cases in five months of 1947; twenty-nine probationers became judges and prosecutors; 2,500 law books went to the National Law Library."},{"t":"Public Safety: police installations and prisons","lv":2,"p":[134,137],"pp":"128","s":"South Korea had 1,977 police installations on 1 Feb. 1947: 156 stations, 414 boxes, 1,495 sub-stations, 68 branches. Kwangju Prison used about 500 working prisoners; June 1947 prisons held 19,833, of whom 17,216 were sentenced."}],"by":"claude-sonnet (Claude Code subagent)"},{"id":"J-23","ia":"summation-non-military-activities-japan-no-23-1947-08","s":"J","no":23,"d":"1947-08","d2":"","t":"Summation of Non-Military Activities in Japan, No. 23 (August 1947)","v":"","pg":174,"o":"Summation No. 23 covers August 1947 (many statistics are for July or June), in the second year of the Occupation. Politically it records the creation of a Ministry of Labor on 28 August under Mitsusuke Yonekubo, the Katayama Cabinet's coal-control dispute with the Liberal Party, which scrapped the Four-party Agreement on 12 August, Prime Minister Katayama's 17 August appeal to the nation, and Councillor by-elections. A long analysis of the April 1947 elections gives vote counts, Purge screening, women candidates and campaign finance. Legal sections report the new Supreme Court and war crimes trials, including Pacific-phase tribunal testimony. Economic sections give production, labor, trade, rationing, price-control, banking and property-custody figures, with floods, coal shortfalls and first postwar foreign-trade shipments. Social sections cover epidemic disease, textbooks and media. The appendix reviews the second Occupation year (1 September 1946 to 31 August 1947), a useful overview for historians. The scan ends at printed page 336, so the Map of Japan is the last item.","h":["A Ministry of Labor was established on 28 August under Social Democrat Mitsusuke Yonekubo, its first Minister, after the Labor Ministry Law passed on 29 August; it handles all labor matters except those of seamen.","The Liberal Party scrapped the Four-party Agreement on 12 August and went into opposition; Secretary General Banboku Ono announced on 23 August a movement to overthrow the Katayama Cabinet.","Coal control caused wide political controversy: the Democrats announced a 19-point program on 4 August, opposed by the People's Cooperative and Social Democratic Parties, while the July coal quota was beaten by 10,300 metric tons.","In the April 1947 local elections, independents won 9,257 of 10,419 mayoralties, 31 of 46 governorships and 173,999 of 193,881 assembly seats; ten of 19 women candidates won House of Councillors seats.","The first Supreme Court under the new Constitution was organized with a Chief Justice and 14 associate justices; war crimes work completed 12 trials of 37 persons, with 4 acquitted and 33 sentenced to 18 months to life.","First agricultural land sales under the Land Reform law were completed, with tenants buying 24,325 cho (59,621 acres) in 12 prefectures; northern Honshu flood damage was estimated above 7,000,000,000 yen.","Foreign trade reopened: cotton fabrics went to 19 countries in July (68,138,000 linear yards), the first postwar Davao hemp arrived from the Philippines, and Bank of Japan notes in circulation reached 150,684,000,000 yen on 31 August.","Mitsui Trading and Mitsubishi Trading began liquidation under the Holding Company Liquidation Commission, with operating loans of 200,000,000 and 120,000,000 yen; the Reconstruction Finance Bank had lent 17,982,000,000 yen."],"k":["Katayama Cabinet","Ministry of Labor","coal control","April 1947 elections","Purge","Supreme Court","war crimes trials","land reform","foreign trade","price control","Bank of Japan","Reconstruction Finance Bank","Holding Company Liquidation Commission","labor disputes","second Occupation year"],"toc":[{"t":"Front matter","lv":1,"p":[1,5],"pp":"","s":"Cover for Summation No. 23 (August 1947), table of contents, and list of 106 charts, maps and graphs, from industrial production indexes to the Map of Japan."},{"t":"Part I: General","lv":1,"p":[6,13],"pp":"1","s":"Monthly digest: no SCAP reorganization, new Ministry of Labor, coal-control politics, first land-reform sales, steel and trade gains, rationing, finance and property control."},{"t":"Section 1. Organization Under SCAP","lv":2,"p":[7,7],"pp":"3","s":"Reports no change in the organization of General Headquarters, SCAP, during August 1947."},{"t":"Section 2. Political Activities","lv":2,"p":[7,8],"pp":"3","s":"Ministry of Labor created 28 August; Katayama's 17 August appeal; party reactions to coal control; April election analysis; Purge Appeal Board members named to the Supreme Court; juvenile police departments; war crimes figures."},{"t":"Section 3. Economic Activities","lv":2,"p":[9,11],"pp":"7","s":"First land-reform sales, northern Honshu floods, July coal quota beaten by 10,300 tons, steel and aluminum gains, first Davao hemp, note circulation of 150,684,000,000 yen, and property custody."},{"t":"Section 4. Social Activities","lv":2,"p":[12,13],"pp":"13","s":"Disease rates kept falling apart from malaria and dysentery; Hokkaido residents got first typhus inoculations; welfare payments rose from 1 July; 60,000+ repatriates arrived; teacher workshops prepared for compulsory handicapped schooling in April 1948."},{"t":"Part II: Political","lv":1,"p":[14,52],"pp":"17","s":"Covers the new Ministry of Labor, coal control, parties, the Diet, the April 1947 elections, public safety statistics, the new Supreme Court and war crimes trials."},{"t":"Section 1. Government and Politics","lv":2,"p":[15,41],"pp":"19","s":"Yonekubo named Labor Minister, coal-control disputes, Liberal break with the coalition, Communist and Democratic Party positions, and a long analysis of the April 1947 elections with vote tables."},{"t":"Administration and Political Developments","lv":3,"p":[15,17],"pp":"19","s":"Ministry of Labor creation, coal control, House of Councillors by-elections in five prefectures, food release, and Prime Minister Katayama's 17 August appeal to the nation."},{"t":"Political Parties","lv":3,"p":[18,20],"pp":"","s":"Democratic 19-point coal plan; Liberal sponsorship of the All-Japan Farmers' Union on 3 August and break with the coalition on 12 August; Communist attacks on the Cabinet."},{"t":"The Diet","lv":3,"p":[20,20],"pp":"","s":"Joint Legislative Committee (10 representatives, eight councillors) met 26 August under chairman Senso Higai; standing committees did field studies and held two public hearings on Criminal and Civil Code bills; free debates covered housing and rice."},{"t":"Analysis of the April Elections","lv":3,"p":[20,41],"pp":"","s":"April 1947 elections: laws, Purge screening, publicity, local and national results, women's participation, abstention rates and campaign financing, with vote charts."},{"t":"The Purge","lv":3,"p":[42,42],"pp":"","s":"As of 27 August, committees had screened 590,130 persons since 4 January; 1,548 central officials removed. Tanaka and Yamamoto were cleared on appeal, media screening reached 330 cases, and four Purge Appeal Board members joined the Supreme Court."},{"t":"Section 2. Public Safety","lv":2,"p":[42,44],"pp":"73","s":"Railway casualties, criminal offenses by prefecture, police juvenile departments, prison population of 78,147 in July, and June fire losses of 226,738,226 yen."},{"t":"Section 3. Legal Affairs and War Crimes","lv":2,"p":[45,52],"pp":"79","s":"Organization of the first Supreme Court under the new Constitution; 79 new suspects ordered arrested; 1,089 held in Sugamo; tribunal testimony on Pacific-phase, diplomatic and naval planning."},{"t":"Part III: Economic","lv":1,"p":[53,145],"pp":"95","s":"July and August 1947 statistics on production, labor, trade, prices, finance and property across twelve economic sections, with charts and tables."},{"t":"Section 1. Agriculture and Fisheries","lv":2,"p":[54,57],"pp":"97","s":"Tenant purchases of 24,325 cho of land, 269,943 cho received for taxes, northern Honshu flood damage, July fish catch and fishing vessel fuel allotments."},{"t":"Section 2. Forestry and Mining","lv":2,"p":[58,63],"pp":"105","s":"Log output fell in July; coal beat its quota by 10,300 tons then declined in August; also firewood, charcoal, camphor, stockpiles, lignite and the Imperial Oil Company's new well."},{"t":"Section 3. Heavy Industries","lv":2,"p":[64,73],"pp":"117","s":"July coke output was 172,500 metric tons, up 19,000; rolled steel rose five percent to 53,358 tons; cement 100,597 tons; chemical production index hit 48 percent of requirements; machine tools 635 units; rolling stock fell 26 percent."},{"t":"Section 4. Manufacturing","lv":2,"p":[74,82],"pp":"137","s":"July food fats and oils rose to 3,036 metric tons from 1,737; sake fell to 414 hectoliters, beer rose to 90,686; bicycles reached a postwar high of 18,069; sewing machines 6,860; matches hit a yearly high."},{"t":"Section 5. Textile Industries","lv":2,"p":[83,93],"pp":"155","s":"July cotton yarn fell to 25,285,000 pounds from June's postwar peak of 29,004,000 as raw cotton ran short; rayon filament yarn and rayon pulp rose to a postwar peak; raw silk reached 9,281 bales; wool output slipped."},{"t":"Section 6. Transportation and Public Utilities","lv":2,"p":[94,98],"pp":"177","s":"Electric power generation of 3,482,000 kW with water availability at 112 percent of average; June consumption of 2,112,100,000 kWh; also motor, rail, coastal and foreign shipping, and gas."},{"t":"Section 7. Communications","lv":2,"p":[99,104],"pp":"187","s":"August: radiotelegraph and international mail opened to commercial traffic (25 August) and air mail authorized 28 August; about 12,000 new telephones in July; JT&T staff moved to the Ministry; 120 postal construction projects completed."},{"t":"Section 8. Labor","lv":2,"p":[105,114],"pp":"199","s":"Labor Ministry Law passed 29 August; 72 disputes involving 45,223 workers in June; Central Labor Relations Committee ruling; union membership, wage demands, labor schools and Occupation labor."},{"t":"Section 9. Imports and Exports","lv":2,"p":[115,125],"pp":"219","s":"July cotton fabric exports of 68,138,000 yards to 19 countries and first postwar Philippine Davao hemp, plus food, machinery, chemicals and metals trade."},{"t":"Section 10. Rationing and Price Control","lv":2,"p":[126,132],"pp":"241","s":"Full month's ration for the nine largest cities, potato and summer-grain collections, controls on processed fish and vegetables, producers' goods price rises and urban family expenditures."},{"t":"Section 11. Finance","lv":2,"p":[133,140],"pp":"255","s":"Note circulation of 150,684,000,000 yen; Reconstruction Finance Bank loans of 17,982,000,000 yen; Japan Securities Exchange offering of 945,000 shares; national debt of 300,882,000,000 yen."},{"t":"Section 12. Property Control and Reparations","lv":2,"p":[141,145],"pp":"271","s":"Foreign nationals' property of 1,399,909,000 yen, war suspects' property of 85,377,000 yen, Mitsui and Mitsubishi Trading liquidation, Hayashikaze restricted, and 147 plants inspected for reparations."},{"t":"Part IV: Social","lv":1,"p":[145,161],"pp":"279","s":"August 1947 social report: communicable disease and DDT campaigns, medical supply output, relief for 2,656,538 persons, repatriation of 60,850; teacher workshops, O-bon, radio fee rise to ¥17.50, press comment; appendix opens."},{"t":"Section 1. Public Health and Welfare","lv":2,"p":[146,153],"pp":"281","s":"Murine typhus in 12 prefectures; Hokkaido vaccination reached 991,803; July hospitals 3,371; penicillin output fell to 175,290,000 units; relief paid ¥234,158,053 to 2,656,538 persons; 60,850 repatriated; June births 197,747."},{"t":"Section 2. Education, Religion and Media of Expression","lv":2,"p":[154,160],"pp":"297","s":"Handicapped-teacher workshop ended 22 August with 159 teachers; Ibaraki upper-secondary survey; O-bon festivals; monument removal in Tokyo; radio license fee raised to ¥17.50; press on Katayama Cabinet and foreign trade; Coizumi Studio founded."},{"t":"Appendix: Review of the Second Occupation Year, 1 September 1946 - 31 August 1947","lv":1,"p":[161,174],"pp":"311","s":"Review of the second Occupation year (1 September 1946 to 31 August 1947): food, inflation, democratization, labor, industry, trade, communications, finance, property control and reparations."},{"t":"Section 1. Organization under SCAP","lv":2,"p":[162,162],"pp":"313","s":"Two new special staff sections were set up in GHQ in the second Occupation year: Civil Transportation Section (7 September 1946) and Reparations Section (8 May 1947), plus a Reparations Technical Advisory Committee (21 May 1947)."},{"t":"Section 2. Securing of Necessities of Life","lv":2,"p":[163,164],"pp":"315","s":"Food imports and the summer food crisis, land reclamation, shelter, the White Paper and stabilization program against inflation, and public welfare and health."},{"t":"Section 3. Progress of Democratization","lv":2,"p":[165,169],"pp":"319","s":"New Constitution, 1947 elections, Purge, courts, International Military Tribunal and Class B and C trials, agrarian reform, labor, antitrust, education, religion and media."},{"t":"Section 4. Economic Progress","lv":2,"p":[170,174],"pp":"329","s":"Year's progress in coal, heavy industry, textiles, trade, transport, communications and finance (Reconstruction Finance Bank 17,982,000,000 yen); reparations committee set up May 1947."}],"by":"claude-sonnet (Claude Code subagent)"},{"id":"K-23-1","ia":"south-korean-interim-government-activities-no-23-part-1-1947-08","s":"K","no":23,"d":"1947-08","d2":"","t":"South Korean Interim Government Activities, No. 23 (Part 1) (August 1947)","v":"","pg":220,"o":"South Korean Interim Government Activities No. 23 (Part 1), August 1947, covers mainly July-August 1947 for the US Army Military Government in Korea, compiled by the National Economic Board and the Statistical Research Division. It reports on population and refugee flows, property custody, food supply and the planned fall rice-collection regulation, prices, agriculture, coal and minerals, industrial output, foreign trade, labor, finance, transport, communications, utilities and flood repair. Part IV records the deadlocked US-USSR Joint Commission, the exchange of statements between Generals Brown, Hodge and Shtikov, Marshall's 12 August letter to Molotov, the Liberation Day ceremonies, Interim Legislative Assembly laws and the arrival of the Wedemeyer mission on 26 August. Part V covers health, education, culture and the press. Historians will find dense statistical tables, full texts of official statements, and a detailed picture of economic dependence on US civilian supply imports.","h":["By 16 August 1947 repatriation left South Korea with 1,428,778 more arrivals than departures (2,313,966 inbound, 885,188 outbound); refugees from the North numbered 13,672 in July, about half the June figure.","National Food Regulation No. 6 of 18 August set fall 1947 rice-collection rules: committee-set farm quotas, delivery by 15 January 1948, and seizure of undelivered rice; the Assembly renamed it the Rice Collection Law.","The first official 1947 rice estimate, released 28 August, was 14,200,000 suk (2,166,920 metric tons), about 18 percent above 1946; the Seoul daily ration was cut to 2.0 hop on 7 August.","The Joint Commission stayed deadlocked over consulting Anti-Trusteeship parties; Shtikov protested leftist arrests on 20 August, Hodge denied the charges on 25 August, and Marshall asked Molotov on 12 August for a reply by 21 August.","Lt. Gen. Albert C. Wedemeyer's presidential fact-finding mission reached Seoul on 26 August, met SKIG officials on 29 August and political leaders on 30 August.","Private imports hit a record 271.7 million won in June against exports of 143.8 million; the Korean Foreign Exchange Bank opened 15 July, and Hongkong objected to the Macao trade loophole.","Bank of Chosun notes in circulation rose 1,128,779,000 won in August to 19,496,514,000; overdrafts to Military Government reached 24,191,284,000 won on 31 July; the 1947-48 budget stood at 17,908,679,200 won.","A Coast Guard vessel collided with an overloaded ferry off Tong Yong on 19 August (62 dead, 93 survivors), and 172 prisoners escaped Kongju Prison on 30 August."],"k":["South Korean Interim Government","USAMGIK","US-USSR Joint Commission","Wedemeyer mission","rice collection","Civilian Supply Program","refugees from North Korea","Liberation Day 1947","coal shortage","private trade Macao Hongkong","Bank of Chosun","property custody","flood damage","Interim Legislative Assembly","labor unions Chun Pyung"],"toc":[{"t":"Front matter","lv":1,"p":[1,5],"pp":"","s":"Cover, title page, table of contents and list of 28 charts, maps and graphs for Number 1, August 1947, prepared by the National Economic Board and Statistical Research Division."},{"t":"Part I: Primary Considerations","lv":1,"p":[6,17],"pp":"1","s":"Population and refugee data, including a net repatriate inflow of 1,428,778, and property custody rulings, among them that the Bank of Chosun is a Korean juridical person."},{"t":"Section 1. Population","lv":2,"p":[8,13],"pp":"3","s":"Repatriation totals to 16 August, 13,672 refugees from the North in July, population shifts in Chungchong-pukto and Kyonggi-do, and data on Koreans in Japan and Manchuria."},{"t":"Section 2. Property Custody","lv":2,"p":[14,17],"pp":"9","s":"Justice Department rulings on the Taiduk Agricultural Association and Bank of Chosun, emergency loans of 3,900,000 won to two textile mills, mining property sales, and bankers' questions on requisitioned dwellings."},{"t":"Part II: Production and Supply","lv":1,"p":[18,109],"pp":"13","s":"Economic section covering food collection and rationing, housing, prices, agriculture, coal and minerals, industrial output, imports and exports, and labor, with the 18 August rice regulation and a 14,200,000 suk crop estimate."},{"t":"Section 1. Food Supply","lv":2,"p":[20,41],"pp":"15","s":"Grain collections of 599,744 metric tons, summer-grain quota 61.4 percent filled by 25 August, ration cut to 2.0 hop, National Food Regulation No. 6, and food imports of 513,829 tons."},{"t":"Rice Collection Regulation (National Food Regulation No. 6)","lv":3,"p":[26,32],"pp":"21","s":"Full text of the 18 August regulation giving duties to governors, the Controller of Commodities and the New Korea Company, with quotas, receipts, a 15 January 1948 delivery deadline and penalties."},{"t":"Food Imports and Processed Foods","lv":3,"p":[33,41],"pp":"28","s":"Korean rice trade 1926-1941, Civilian Supply foodstuff imports through 31 July, planting calendars, and flour, noodle, soy sauce, sugar and salt output and imports."},{"t":"Section 2. Housing","lv":2,"p":[42,43],"pp":"37","s":"National Housing Administration (founded 16 November 1946) has no completed houses; 24,000,000 won for winter shelters for 30,000 Seoul refugees; 400 houses for dispossessed Koreans, 50 done by 30 August."},{"t":"Section 3. Prices","lv":2,"p":[44,51],"pp":"39","s":"Revised controlled prices on 16 commodities from 1 August, coal prices set 2 September, rising free-market food, textile and gold prices, and weekly National Price Administration decisions."},{"t":"Section 4. Agriculture and Fisheries","lv":2,"p":[52,65],"pp":"47","s":"Rice estimate of 14,200,000 suk, cotton plan needing a 1,052,000,000 won loan, straw-bag shortage, fertilizer distribution of 153,116 tons, irrigation projects, livestock and fisheries."},{"t":"Section 5. Natural Resources","lv":2,"p":[66,79],"pp":"61","s":"Coal below minimum needs, a Coal Committee formed 10 July, copper poured at Changhang on 26 July, a flash flood at Sandong tungsten mine, and timber regulation."},{"t":"Coal","lv":3,"p":[66,73],"pp":"61","s":"Anthracite output near 28,000 tons a month, 53,254 tons of monthly Japanese bituminous imports, stockpiles, and railroads consuming about half of all coal."},{"t":"Minerals and Metals","lv":3,"p":[73,78],"pp":"68","s":"Wartime peak output of gold, iron ore, graphite, copper, tungsten and zinc, May-July 1947 output, a 1951 forecast, Ongjin reopening and Mangdong barter of tungsten for Egyptian cotton."},{"t":"Forestry","lv":3,"p":[78,79],"pp":"73","s":"Committee to regulate timber and firewood use, LSTs hauling logs from Hosan to Inchon, 1,000,000 board feet of US lumber, and inspection of vested forests such as Mitsui Norin."},{"t":"Section 6. Production and Allocations","lv":2,"p":[80,95],"pp":"75","s":"Cotton shortage limiting 240,000 spindles, silk filature loans, paper, tobacco, brick and cement output, 480 vehicles for SKIG, six ships for the Coast Guard, and petroleum sales."},{"t":"Section 7. Imports and Exports","lv":2,"p":[96,107],"pp":"91","s":"Record June private imports of 271.7 million won, Korean Foreign Exchange Bank opened 15 July, Hongkong and Macao trade problems, Civilian Supply Program IV worth 84.7 million dollars, and UNRRA final account."},{"t":"Section 8. Labor","lv":2,"p":[108,109],"pp":"103","s":"Tai Han No Chong and Chun Pyung conflict, the 29 July Kyung Sung Spinning strike, the Seoul Electric Company contract signed 13 August, and a poor factory dispensary inspection."},{"t":"Part III: Public Services","lv":1,"p":[110,153],"pp":"105","s":"Finance, transport, communications, utilities and public works: 19.5 billion won notes in circulation, 628 locomotives with 372 inoperative, record July communications revenue, and flood repairs needing 477,451,000 won."},{"t":"Section 1. Finance","lv":2,"p":[112,123],"pp":"107","s":"Notes in circulation 19,496,514,000 won; loan limits revised 26 August; July revenue 910,674,274 won against expenditure 2,004,096,655; budget 17,908,679,200 won; deposits of 27.9 billion won."},{"t":"Section 2. Transportation","lv":2,"p":[124,135],"pp":"119","s":"Railway revenue exceeding expenditure without subsidy, 628 engines with 372 inoperative on 1 September, 10 locomotives arriving from Germany, highway tire shortage and marine bureau reorganization."},{"t":"Section 3. Communications","lv":2,"p":[136,147],"pp":"131","s":"Record July revenue, staff down to 17,720, postal savings of 843,951,503 won, air mail to the US begun 8 August, and 20 post offices closed 1 August."},{"t":"Section 4. Public Utilities","lv":2,"p":[148,149],"pp":"143","s":"Power from North Korea (42 million kWh in June), barge Electra assigned to Samchok, gas plants idle for lack of coal, 70 streetcars running, and approved freight and bus fare increases."},{"t":"Section 5. Public Works","lv":2,"p":[150,153],"pp":"145","s":"Flood repairs needing 1,839,198 man-days and 477,451,000 won, 50,000 bags of Army cement lent, Seoul-Pusan highway slide repair, and city water projects."},{"t":"Part IV: Government and Politics","lv":1,"p":[154,203],"pp":"149","s":"Liberation Day, Assembly laws, the deadlocked Joint Commission with statements by Shtikov, Brown and Hodge, Marshall's 12 August letter to Molotov, the Wedemeyer mission's 26 August arrival, and the Kongju prison escape."},{"t":"Section 1. Administration","lv":2,"p":[156,161],"pp":"151","s":"General Lerch resumed command 7 August, Helmick's statement on sale of Civilian Supply goods, Central Election Committee, Liberation Day speeches, and the Wedemeyer mission arriving 26 August."},{"t":"Section 2. Interim Legislative Assembly","lv":2,"p":[162,165],"pp":"157","s":"Twelve August meetings averaging 67 members, Temporary Constitution second reading 6 August, Franchise Law amendment, Rice Collection Law, temple property protection and abolition of licensed prostitution."},{"t":"Section 3. Political Activities","lv":2,"p":[166,167],"pp":"161","s":"Politics revolved around the Joint Commission; the Rhee-Kim Koo merger attempt of 8 August failed; a rightist newspaper association formed 10 August; leftists objected to the Liberation Day order."},{"t":"Section 4. Interzone Activities and Foreign Affairs","lv":2,"p":[168,199],"pp":"163","s":"Full texts of Joint Commission statements, Marshall's 12 August letter to Molotov, the Soviet arrest protest, Hodge's reply, bi-weekly mail exchange, and 352 travel permits for Chinese nationals."},{"t":"U.S.-U.S.S.R. Joint Commission","lv":3,"p":[168,197],"pp":"163","s":"Statements by Shtikov (2, 9, 14, 20, 28 August) and Brown (7, 9, 23 August) on consulting Anti-Trusteeship parties, Marshall's 12 August letter, and Soviet charges of repression."},{"t":"Interzone Activities","lv":3,"p":[198,198],"pp":"193","s":"Bi-weekly mail exchanges between North and South Korea took place on 9 and 23 August."},{"t":"Foreign Affairs","lv":3,"p":[198,199],"pp":"193","s":"By 23 August the Office of Foreign Affairs had issued 352 permits to Chinese nationals to travel to China on private watercraft for repatriation."},{"t":"Section 5. Justice","lv":2,"p":[200,201],"pp":"195","s":"Executive Order No. 5 restricted 15 August ceremonies (548 localities, 773,000 participants); 1,596 prisoners released; 172 escaped Kongju Prison on 30 August; 19,777 inmates on 31 July."},{"t":"Section 6. Internal Security","lv":2,"p":[202,203],"pp":"197","s":"Constabulary and Coast Guard in Liberation Day ceremonies, Constabulary guarding Seoul utilities, Coast Guard maneuvers on 17 August, and the 19 August Tong Yong ferry collision killing 62."},{"t":"Part V: Social","lv":1,"p":[204,219],"pp":"199","s":"Health, education, culture and information media: smallpox down to 143 cases, Hangul promotion ordered 14 August, 109 missionaries, and press comment on the Joint Commission and Wedemeyer mission."},{"t":"Section 1. Health and Welfare","lv":2,"p":[206,209],"pp":"201","s":"Quarantine rules strengthened 4 September, no cholera in 1947, smallpox down to 143 cases from 20,574, 60,000 UNRRA penicillin vials, and LARA shipments worth 202,000 dollars."},{"t":"Section 2. Education","lv":2,"p":[210,213],"pp":"205","s":"Hangul promotion ordered 14 August, 160 Seoul National University medical students reinstated, Chosun Christian College admits women, 301 music teachers trained, and 1,010 new secondary classes."},{"t":"Section 3. Culture and Religion","lv":2,"p":[214,215],"pp":"209","s":"Korean observers to the Institute of Pacific Relations conference at Stratford-on-Avon, National Youth Movement training for 55,000 men and 20,000 women, and 109 missionaries on 30 August."},{"t":"Section 4. Media of Information","lv":2,"p":[216,219],"pp":"211","s":"200 portable projectors received, newsreel No. 22, radio memorial for Lyuh Woon Hyeung, forum programs replacing political speeches, and press comment on the Joint Commission, Wedemeyer mission and trade deficit."},{"t":"Map of Korea","lv":1,"p":[220,220],"pp":"215","s":"Closing page with a map of Korea from Light Area Printing Plant records, the last item in the charts and maps index."}],"by":"claude-sonnet (Claude Code subagent)"},{"id":"J-24","ia":"summation-non-military-activities-japan-no-24-1947-09","s":"J","no":24,"d":"1947-09","d2":"","t":"Summation of Non-Military Activities in Japan, No. 24 (September 1947)","v":"","pg":147,"o":"Summation No. 24 covers September 1947 in Occupied Japan. Politically, the Katayama coalition settled the Temporary State Control of Coal Mining Bill (three-year control, introduced 25 September) after MacArthur's letter demanding higher production goals, while the four-party policy agreement lapsed and the Political Parties and National Public Service Bills went before the Diet. MacArthur's 2 September anniversary statement urged a tolerant peace treaty. The 16 September typhoon and floods killed 1,043 in 17 Honshu prefectures and dominated public safety, health and press reporting. The IMTFE defense moved from the general phase to individual defenses (Araki, Doihara, Hashimoto, Hata, Hoshino, Hiranuma). The Ministry of Labor was established 1 September. Part III gives detailed production, trade, price, banking and property-control statistics, with industrial output reported near 45 percent of prewar. Part IV covers epidemic control, flood relief, repatriation, vital statistics, education, religion and media. Historians will find the report useful for statistics and for official framing of the coal and party politics crises.","h":["On 2 September, the second anniversary of the surrender, MacArthur issued a statement urging the Powers to approach the peace treaty tolerantly so Japan could become self-sustaining, noting industrial output had risen above 45 percent of prewar normal.","The Cabinet agreed on 5 September to limit state control of coal mines to three years and on 18 September to administer them through the Coal Board and Industrial Rehabilitation Corporation; the bill entered the Diet on 25 September with MacArthur's letter demanding output above 50,000,000 tons.","The 16 September typhoon and floods hit 17 Honshu prefectures, leaving 1,043 dead, 1,841 injured, 488 missing and about 1,755,000 sufferers, with 418,004 houses submerged and 2,397 bridges washed away; Gunma and Tochigi were worst hit.","Prime Minister Katayama announced the Government was no longer bound by the four-party policy agreement after Liberal Party opposition; the Liberals and Communists issued their first-ever joint statement against Cabinet speech campaigns during the Diet recess.","The Ministry of Labor was established 1 September, bringing into force much of the Labor Standards Law, the Workmen's Compensation Insurance Law and the Seamen's Law; no major work stoppages occurred in September.","At the IMTFE the defense closed its general phase on 9-10 September, and Sadao Araki opened the individual phase for 25 accused, followed by Doihara, Hashimoto, Hata, Hoshino and Hiranuma.","By 24 September 365 war crimes suspects had been tried: 349 convicted (33 sentenced to death) and 16 acquitted; 1,640 atrocity cases remained under investigation and 1,051 suspects were interned at Sugamo.","The land purchase goal for 2 October was raised from 300,000 to 500,000 cho, with 348,286 cho bought by 31 August, and Bank of Japan notes in circulation reached 156,416,000,000 yen on 30 September."],"k":["coal state control","Katayama Cabinet","MacArthur statement","typhoon and flood","IMTFE defense","war crimes trials","Ministry of Labor","agrarian reform","purge screening","Political Parties Bill","National Public Service Bill","industrial production","foreign trade resumption","Bank of Japan","textile industry","Supreme Court"],"toc":[{"t":"Front matter","lv":1,"p":[1,5],"pp":"","s":"Cover of Summation No. 24 (September 1947), table of contents for Parts I-IV, and the list of 108 charts, maps and graphs."},{"t":"Part I: General","lv":1,"p":[6,13],"pp":"1","s":"Digest of the month: coal control, the typhoon and flood, the IMTFE defense, the new Ministry of Labor, production statistics, and health, education and media notes."},{"t":"Section 1. Organization Under SCAP","lv":2,"p":[7,7],"pp":"3","s":"Reports no change in the organization of General Headquarters, SCAP, during September."},{"t":"Section 2. Political Activities","lv":2,"p":[7,9],"pp":"3","s":"Coal-mine control agreed for three years, Katayama ending the four-party agreement, the Political Parties Bill, Diet reconvening 15 September, 15,145 persons screened in the purge, and typhoon deaths and war crimes figures."},{"t":"Section 3. Economic Activities","lv":2,"p":[9,12],"pp":"7","s":"Land purchase goal raised to 500,000 cho, August coal, steel, cement and textile output, Ministry of Labor founding, first postwar consumer-goods exports, and finance and property control figures."},{"t":"Section 4. Social Activities","lv":2,"p":[12,13],"pp":"13","s":"Flood-area epidemic prevention, teacher workshops at Kyoto University from 29 September, 1,275 foreign missionaries, a Cambodian sculpture exhibition and a new SCAP library at Nagoya."},{"t":"Part II: Political","lv":1,"p":[14,35],"pp":"17","s":"MacArthur's anniversary statement, coal control and party politics, the Diet, the typhoon and crime, the judiciary, and war crimes trials including the IMTFE defense phase."},{"t":"Section 1. Government and Politics","lv":2,"p":[15,21],"pp":"19","s":"MacArthur's 2 September statement, the coal control bill and his letter, party positions, the National Public Service Bill, legislation status, local government and purge totals."},{"t":"Statement of the Supreme Commander","lv":3,"p":[15,16],"pp":"19","s":"Full text of MacArthur's 2 September surrender-anniversary statement calling for a tolerant peace treaty, and the Mainichi editorial reply."},{"t":"Political Developments","lv":3,"p":[17,20],"pp":"23","s":"Cabinet plan for state coal control, Katayama's statement, the 25 September bill, MacArthur's letter, dissolution of the four-party agreement and each party's position."},{"t":"The Diet","lv":3,"p":[20,21],"pp":"28","s":"Diet resumed 15 September; National Public Service Bill submitted for effect 1 July 1948; lists of bills enacted, passed and withdrawn, including the Home Ministry abolition bills."},{"t":"Local Government and the Purge","lv":3,"p":[21,21],"pp":"30","s":"Plan to cut over 30,000 local branch agencies, mayors' conference, governors censured in Osaka and Shimane, and 15,145 persons screened in the purge with 563 removed."},{"t":"Section 2. Public Safety","lv":2,"p":[22,25],"pp":"33","s":"Transport certificates against the black market from 15 September, 170,041 offenses in July, 402 rail accidents in August, typhoon flood damage, prison population and fire losses."},{"t":"Section 3. Legal Affairs and War Crimes","lv":2,"p":[26,35],"pp":"41","s":"Seven new high court chief judges, the Kinosuke Oru case, 16 trials with seven death sentences, and the IMTFE defense of Araki, Doihara, Hashimoto, Hata, Hoshino and Hiranuma."},{"t":"Japanese Judicial Administration","lv":3,"p":[26,27],"pp":"41","s":"Chief judges of seven district high courts named 25 September, and the Supreme Court's inquiry into the Oru suspended-sentence affair."},{"t":"Investigation, Apprehension and Prosecution of War Criminals","lv":3,"p":[27,28],"pp":"42","s":"1,640 atrocity cases on hand, 33 new apprehension orders, 365 tried to date with 349 convicted, and a table of 16 trials completed this period."},{"t":"International Prosecution: General Phase of Defense","lv":3,"p":[29,31],"pp":"46","s":"Testimony of Yamamoto, Okochi, Sawada, Sugita, Ikejiri, Sakurai, Nishiura and Saito; the defense closed its general phase on 9 September."},{"t":"International Prosecution: Individual Defenses","lv":3,"p":[31,35],"pp":"","s":"Individual defenses opened by Araki (10-16 September), then Doihara, Hashimoto, Hata, Hoshino and Hiranuma, with witnesses such as Yonai and Okada."},{"t":"Part III: Economic","lv":1,"p":[36,129],"pp":"61","s":"August economic statistics and narrative: agrarian reform, coal, heavy industry, textiles, utilities, labor, trade, prices, finance and property control, with output near 45 percent of prewar."},{"t":"Section 1. Agriculture and Fisheries","lv":2,"p":[37,39],"pp":"63","s":"Land purchase goal raised to 500,000 cho with 348,286 cho bought by 31 August, flood crop losses in Saitama, June milk output of 13,373,000 liters, and August marine landings."},{"t":"Section 2. Forestry and Mining","lv":2,"p":[40,45],"pp":"69","s":"August logs rose to 466,752,000 board feet; coal output was 2,086,100 tons in August and 1,506,400 tons in 1-20 September; also consumption, lignite and oil drilling."},{"t":"Section 3. Heavy Industries","lv":2,"p":[46,55],"pp":"81","s":"Coke 174,800 tons; August pig iron 33,467 and steel ingots 87,476 tons fell on power shortage; aluminum 761 tons; also cement, construction, chemicals, machinery and rolling stock."},{"t":"Section 4. Manufacturing","lv":2,"p":[56,65],"pp":"101","s":"Staple-food, paper, glass, optical, medical equipment and motor vehicle output; bicycle production at a postwar high; handicrafts, watches and sewing machines."},{"t":"Section 5. Textile Industries","lv":2,"p":[66,77],"pp":"121","s":"August decreases in cotton, silk and wool for lack of materials and power; cotton and woolen yarn lowest in over a year; rayon rose; knit goods, dyeing and machinery."},{"t":"Section 6. Transportation and Public Utilities","lv":2,"p":[78,83],"pp":"145","s":"More trucks and buses from Army surplus, freight tonnage down 2.3 percent, coastwise shipping down, drought cut hydroelectric generation, and gas loss of 12 percent."},{"t":"Section 7. Communications","lv":2,"p":[84,90],"pp":"157","s":"Relaxed international communications for trade, typhoon damage to facilities of about 500,000,000 yen, three ship-distress radio stations, mail, postal savings and equipment production."},{"t":"Section 8. Labor","lv":2,"p":[91,99],"pp":"171","s":"Ministry of Labor established 1 September; labor-management councils, union membership and dispute statistics, labor relations committees, labor education, employment and public works."},{"t":"Section 9. Imports and Exports","lv":2,"p":[100,110],"pp":"189","s":"Private foreign commerce reopened; 34,378,000 yards of cotton fabric exported to nine countries in August, first postwar consumer-goods exports, and import and export tables by commodity."},{"t":"Section 10. Rationing and Price Control","lv":2,"p":[111,117],"pp":"211","s":"Imported food released for rations, summer-grain collections, price increases under the new stabilization program, fish and vegetable prices, urban family expenditures and ten rationed commodities."},{"t":"Section 11. Finance","lv":2,"p":[118,125],"pp":"225","s":"Bank of Japan notes at 156,416,000,000 yen, Bank of China Tokyo license, 59 control organizations closed, savings and Deposit Bureau figures, national debt of 304,630,000,000 yen."},{"t":"Section 12. Property Control and Reparations","lv":2,"p":[125,129],"pp":"239","s":"315 ships reported looted, 970 arrested persons' property held, Mitsui and Mitsubishi Trading dissolution, coal-mine rehabilitation loans of 2,829,410,000 yen, and reparations inspections."},{"t":"Part IV: Social","lv":1,"p":[129,147],"pp":"247","s":"Public health, flood relief and vital statistics, then education, religion, arts and the media of expression for September 1947."},{"t":"Section 1. Public Health and Welfare","lv":2,"p":[130,139],"pp":"249","s":"Epidemic prevention in flooded Tokyo, Saitama and Chiba, 140 suspected Japanese B encephalitis cases, narcotics law, flood relief and repatriation, and a revised census gain of 5,766,090."},{"t":"Section 2. Education, Religion and Media of Expression","lv":2,"p":[139,147],"pp":"267","s":"Teacher workshops at Kyoto University, free school lunch plan, 1,275 foreign missionaries, Cambodian sculpture exhibition, flood-dominated press, radio and films, and the Nagoya SCAP library."}],"by":"claude-sonnet (Claude Code subagent)"},{"id":"K-24","ia":"south-korean-interim-government-activities-no-24-1947-09","s":"K","no":24,"d":"1947-09","d2":"","t":"South Korean Interim Government Activities, No. 24 (September 1947)","v":"","pg":333,"o":"South Korean Interim Government Activities No. 24 covers September 1947 and is the first monthly report for which the South Korean Interim Government, rather than SCAP's Statistics and Research Section, takes full responsibility. Its six parts report population movements, vested property, food collection, prices, agriculture, mining, industry, trade, labor, transport, communications, finance, politics and social affairs. The month was dominated by the death of Military Governor Major General Archer L. Lerch on 11 September, Brigadier General C. G. Helmick becoming Acting Military Governor, the second anniversary of USAMGIK, the franchise law (Public Law No. 5) for popular election of the Legislative Assembly, and press reaction to the American four-power conference proposal and the Soviet withdrawal proposal. Historians will find detailed tables on rice collection quotas, coal output, the 18 billion won budget, bank note issue, refugee inflow from the North and school enrollment, plus frank statements of the limits of Korean statistical reporting.","h":["Military Governor Major General Archer L. Lerch died at 0758 on 11 September; Brigadier General C. G. Helmick, Acting Military Governor since 7 September, announced it, and funeral services were held before the National Capitol in Seoul on 16 September.","On 3 September Helmick signed Public Law No. 5, the franchise law for popular election of Korean Interim Legislative Assembly members; no election was to be held until at least 80 days after a 15-member Central Election Committee is chosen.","Moderate leaders including Civil Administrator Ahn Chai Hong and KILA Speaker Kimm Kiu Siu announced the Minju Dok Dang (Democratic Independence Party) on 23 September, with Hong Myung Hi as temporary chairman.","The 1947 rice collection program from 1 November sets the farmer price at 640 won per 54-kg bag; summer grain collection reached 88.3 percent of its 711,000 suk quota by 25 September.","Population movement from October 1945 to 22 September 1947 shows 885,532 outbound, mostly Japanese, against 2,332,458 inbound, including 1,110,513 Koreans from Japan and 838,263 Koreans from north of the 38th parallel.","The SKIG 1947-48 budget stood at about 18 billion won on 30 September after supplementals, up from the 17,720,227,310 won approved on 2 July; Bank of Chosun note issue rose 1.8 billion won from July to September.","Labor Department Orders No. 1 and 2 of 15 September implemented the Child Labor Law, and the Seoul Electric Company reinstatement dispute over three discharged workers drew pressure from the Northwestern Youth Association.","Education statistics report 3,058,433 students enrolled in South Korean schools at 31 July 1947, including 13,827 in 25 universities and colleges; the fall typhoid immunization program began 15 September."],"k":["South Korean Interim Government","USAMGIK","Archer L. Lerch","C. G. Helmick","franchise law","Korean Interim Legislative Assembly","rice collection","price administration","vested property","repatriation and refugees","coal production","Bank of Chosun","labor","Joint Commission","press opinion"],"toc":[{"t":"Front matter","lv":1,"p":[1,13],"pp":"","s":"Cover, title page and table of contents for Number 24, September 1947, listing six parts from Basic Aspects to Social."},{"t":"Introduction","lv":1,"p":[14,15],"pp":"1","s":"States that No. 24 is the first report for which the South Korean Interim Government has full responsibility; warns that statistics are incomplete and charts await SCAP drawings."},{"t":"Part I: Basic Aspects","lv":1,"p":[16,27],"pp":"3","s":"Population movement since 1945, with net inflow near 1.5 million, and Office of Property Custody income, leases, staff and policy on vested Japanese property."},{"t":"Section 1. Population","lv":2,"p":[16,21],"pp":"3","s":"Net inflow of about 1.45 million people; 20,394 illegal Korean entrants returned from Japan; refugee flow from the North rising; Office of Foreign Affairs handled Chinese junks and Japanese repatriation costs."},{"t":"Repatriation","lv":3,"p":[16,16],"pp":"3","s":"Since October 1945, 885,532 people left and 2,332,458 arrived; the ship Kogane Maru carried 797 Koreans from Japan on 12 September."},{"t":"Refugees","lv":3,"p":[17,18],"pp":"4","s":"Refugee arrivals from north of the 38th parallel exceeded 1946 levels from May to August 1947, with Pyongan-pukto the leading origin."},{"t":"Activities","lv":3,"p":[19,21],"pp":"6","s":"Chinese junk permits at Inchon, Japanese repatriation cost of $177,199.56 in CARIOA supplies, Korean registration in Japan, and 13 Korean students cleared for study in the US."},{"t":"Section 2. Vested Property","lv":2,"p":[22,27],"pp":"9","s":"Property Custody income shifted from rents to sales and confiscations; Korean staff rose to 2,062 by 30 September; Helmick ruling and 1 September bonding memorandum for managers."},{"t":"Income","lv":3,"p":[22,23],"pp":"9","s":"Rents fell from over 75 percent of income before 1947 to 27 percent in the first half of 1947 as sales and confiscations grew."},{"t":"Properties Under Lease","lv":3,"p":[24,24],"pp":"11","s":"Vested residences under lease rose from 51,256 at 31 December 1946 to 57,505 at 30 June 1947, Seoul City alone reaching 11,494; no income breakdown between residential and non-residential leases exists."},{"t":"Employment","lv":3,"p":[25,25],"pp":"12","s":"Korean employees of the Office of Property Custody grew from 895 in December 1946 to 2,062 (budgeted) in September 1947."},{"t":"Activities","lv":3,"p":[25,27],"pp":"12","s":"Rising Sun Petroleum frozen-asset inquiry for the British Consul General, Helmick's ruling on companies occupying Army-needed buildings, and a bonding memorandum for managers."},{"t":"Part II: Production and Supply","lv":1,"p":[28,83],"pp":"15","s":"Summer grain and rice collection, prices, agriculture, coal and metals, industrial output, foreign trade and labor in South Korea, August-September 1947."},{"t":"Section 1. Food Supply","lv":2,"p":[28,31],"pp":"15","s":"Summer grain collection reached 88.3 percent of the 711,000 suk quota by 25 September; 1947 rice collection set at 640 won per 54-kg bag with provincial quotas assigned."},{"t":"Indigenous Foods","lv":3,"p":[28,28],"pp":"15","s":"Cumulative summer grain collection from July to 25 September 1947 against a 711,000 suk polished-grain quota."},{"t":"Rice Collection, 1946 Crop","lv":3,"p":[29,29],"pp":"16","s":"On 10 September 413,130 suk of 1946 rice remained on hand out of 3,560,866 suk purchased."},{"t":"Imports","lv":3,"p":[30,30],"pp":"17","s":"September grain imports to South Korea were 33,574 metric tons, near the January-August monthly average; cumulative imports since May 1946 reached 520,256 tons, mostly wheat."},{"t":"Grain Released for Rations","lv":3,"p":[30,30],"pp":"17","s":"Grain other than rice released for rations roughly equalled rice May-July 1947; in August it was 356,376 suk against 184,661 suk of rice, 65 percent of the total."},{"t":"Population on Rations","lv":3,"p":[30,31],"pp":"17","s":"Population on general and special rations rose from 7,718,526 in March to 8,720,198 in August 1947; supplementary rations peaked at 395,697. Ration set at 2.2 hop (1,155 calories) daily."},{"t":"Rice Collection Program, 1947","lv":3,"p":[31,31],"pp":"18","s":"Program beginning 1 November sets the farmer price at 640 won per bag and removes subsidies; provincial quotas are in polished rice."},{"t":"Section 2. Prices","lv":2,"p":[32,41],"pp":"19","s":"National Economic Board Administrative Study No. 5 on the 'rice formula', Seoul price tables through July 1947, gold prices, and pricing of vested-factory goods."},{"t":"Administrative Study No. 5","lv":3,"p":[32,33],"pp":"19","s":"Explains the October 1946 rice formula, polished rice at 55 times base prices, revocation of National Price Regulation No. 1 on 30 June, and 10 to 15 percent factory profit margins."},{"t":"Price Trends","lv":3,"p":[34,39],"pp":"21","s":"Bank of Chosun Seoul tables show grain and bean prices still rising through July 1947 while other foods and textiles held steady."},{"t":"Gold","lv":3,"p":[40,40],"pp":"27","s":"The August 1947 open-market wholesale gold price was a preliminary 2,425 won per don, over five times the 473 of August 1945 and more than three times the year before."},{"t":"Activities, National Price Administration","lv":3,"p":[40,41],"pp":"27","s":"In September prices were set on over 20 lots of goods, from underwear to pharmaceuticals and tar paper, balancing inflation against manufacturer profit."},{"t":"Section 3. Agriculture, Forestry and Fisheries","lv":2,"p":[42,49],"pp":"29","s":"1947 rice estimate held at 14,200,000 suk (2,164,000 metric tons); winter hay goal of 1,500,000 tons, cotton on 125,000 hectares, rural youth clubs, forestry and fisheries."},{"t":"Bureau of Agricultural Economics","lv":3,"p":[42,42],"pp":"29","s":"Statistics staff sent to the field for a final pre-harvest rice estimate; straw bag work."},{"t":"Livestock","lv":3,"p":[43,44],"pp":"30","s":"Winter hay program to store 1,500,000 tons, hay selling at 12 won per kwan; livestock conditions across South Korea."},{"t":"Production","lv":3,"p":[45,45],"pp":"32","s":"Potatoes from imported seed were harvested in Kangwon-do despite late planting and wild boar losses."},{"t":"Cotton and Fiber","lv":3,"p":[45,45],"pp":"32","s":"Cotton sown on about 125,000 hectares, expected yield over 17 million kg of seed cotton against 10,651,434 kg in 1946; sericulture."},{"t":"Farm Supply","lv":3,"p":[46,46],"pp":"33","s":"Cholla-namdo collected 4,000 suk of vetch seed for green manure; meeting held on compost and fertilizer distribution."},{"t":"Irrigation","lv":3,"p":[46,46],"pp":"33","s":"Korean Irrigation Association let contracts to resume five tidal projects (estimated cost 57,473,253 won; bids 40,544,000 won), and on 20 September marked completion of the Son San, Ko Ah and Kim O San projects."},{"t":"Suwon Experiment Station","lv":3,"p":[46,46],"pp":"33","s":"Suwon station drew up a new table of organization placing the Central Station at bureau level, and a Japanese study of fertilizers and rice root development was translated and mimeographed."},{"t":"Korea Agricultural Association","lv":3,"p":[46,47],"pp":"33","s":"KAA ran a farmer lecture course for 50 men in Kyonggi-do and filed its fiscal-year report; fertilizer sales reached 119,347 metric tons, 85 percent of receipts, with 457,685,968 won collected."},{"t":"Rural Youth Activities","lv":3,"p":[47,47],"pp":"34","s":"Rural youth clubs specialize in gardening, livestock, sericulture and forestry; some elect girls as officers."},{"t":"Forestry","lv":3,"p":[48,48],"pp":"35","s":"Provincial Forestry Advisers met in Seoul 7-9 October; rough Army lumber was exchanged for dressed lumber on 2 September; Inchon plywood plant cleared to produce about 20,000 sheets monthly."},{"t":"Fisheries","lv":3,"p":[49,49],"pp":"36","s":"Fisheries data were limited; the motor mine sweeper YNS 200 was released for deep-sea fishing, and Seoul Public Market received 68.65 tons of fish and marine products in the week ending 20 September."},{"t":"Section 4. Mining and Minerals","lv":2,"p":[50,57],"pp":"37","s":"Anthracite and lignite output fell in August; bituminous coal demand and Coal Committee proceedings; gold, silver, copper and tungsten, with tungsten hit by the 3 August flood."},{"t":"Coal","lv":3,"p":[50,54],"pp":"37","s":"Anthracite peaked in June and lignite in July before dropping in August; Samchuk, Eunsung and Hwasun mines, bituminous coal and the Coal Committee."},{"t":"Metals","lv":3,"p":[55,57],"pp":"42","s":"Gold, silver, copper, electrolytic copper and tungsten output; tungsten production fell after the August flood."},{"t":"Section 5. Industrial Production and Allocations","lv":2,"p":[58,70],"pp":"45","s":"Flour output fell to 236 metric tons, the lowest on record; textiles, paper, nails, briquettes, rubber, tobacco and salt, plus allocations of metal and controlled commodities."},{"t":"Processed Foods","lv":3,"p":[58,58],"pp":"45","s":"One of two government flour mills closed for lack of wheat; Tong-A Food Products canned peaches and made soy sauce."},{"t":"Textiles","lv":3,"p":[59,59],"pp":"46","s":"1,589 bales of raw cotton arrived in early August (1,200 UNRRA), keeping vested mills running at reduced output; two of seven mills made 46-52 percent of July-August yarn."},{"t":"Paper","lv":3,"p":[60,60],"pp":"47","s":"August paper output was 312.8 metric tons, below July's 362.3, from one mill breakdown and coal shortage though 10 of 14 vested mills increased; first Japanese cylinder-face parts arrived."},{"t":"Machinery and Hardware","lv":3,"p":[60,61],"pp":"47","s":"August nail output in two vested plants was 140.9 metric tons, nearly 20 percent below July, from sulphuric acid and wire shortages; Inchon Nail Company furnaces were repaired and another added in September."},{"t":"Other Industries","lv":3,"p":[62,63],"pp":"49","s":"Briquette output fell almost 50 percent in two months to 11,000 tons in August; Chosun Onada Cement reopened 1 August making 6,147 tons; brick, firebrick, and rubber shoe output declined."},{"t":"Monopoly Bureau Products","lv":3,"p":[64,64],"pp":"51","s":"Tobacco output declined in August; salt production rose but stayed low because rains lasted until mid-August."},{"t":"Allocations","lv":3,"p":[65,68],"pp":"52","s":"Metal allocated to manufacturers 18 August to 13 September, controlled commodities, and 80 tons of aluminum scrap at Kimpo Air Base."},{"t":"Agencies of Distribution","lv":3,"p":[69,70],"pp":"56","s":"The Federation of Financial Associations and Federation of Distributing Associations distribute domestic controlled goods; FFA purchases fell from 164,266 thousand won in July to 37,627 thousand in September."},{"t":"Section 6. Imports and Exports","lv":2,"p":[71,77],"pp":"58","s":"July private-trade imports fell 86.6 percent from June and exports fell 21 percent; trade with the US, Japan, Hong Kong and Egypt; civilian supply imports of salt and fertilizer."},{"t":"Private Trade","lv":3,"p":[71,72],"pp":"58","s":"Largest July exports were 965 tons of metals worth 64 million won; imports included salt, cornstarch and 4,906 bottles of penicillin."},{"t":"Trade with United States, Japan, Hong Kong and Egypt","lv":3,"p":[73,74],"pp":"60","s":"USCC will cancel its Korea export contract at 31 December 1947; direct Hong Kong trade resumed; Egypt barter of 3,000 cotton bales for 700 tons scheelite; PX souvenir sales reached 3,406 dollars."},{"t":"Imports, Services of Civilian Supply","lv":3,"p":[74,77],"pp":"61","s":"Civilian supply imports of salt (908 tons total in August), fertilizer, foodstuffs and other goods, with transportation."},{"t":"Section 7. Labor","lv":2,"p":[78,83],"pp":"65","s":"Child labor orders of 15 September, Seoul Electric Company reinstatement dispute, Korean Industrial Health Association, and unemployment sampling in Seoul, Kyonggi-do and Chungchong-pukto."},{"t":"Labor Regulations","lv":3,"p":[78,78],"pp":"65","s":"Labor Department Orders No. 1 and 2 of 15 September implement the Child Labor Law and require education for child workers under eighteen."},{"t":"Labor Union Activities","lv":3,"p":[79,79],"pp":"66","s":"Seoul Electric Company shop stewards and the dispute over three discharged workers, pressed by the Northwestern Youth Association."},{"t":"Labor Education","lv":3,"p":[79,79],"pp":"66","s":"Translation of 'Duties of a Shop Steward' and radio talks on the Child Labor Law and collective bargaining."},{"t":"Labor Conference","lv":3,"p":[80,80],"pp":"67","s":"Provincial labor bureau chiefs met for three days from 25 August 1947; they backed the Child Labor Law, thought Maximum Working Hours too strict, and were told to staff a new Industrial Health and Welfare section."},{"t":"Industrial Health and Welfare","lv":3,"p":[80,80],"pp":"67","s":"Korean Industrial Health Association formed 19 August by 14 industrial physicians, who began drafting a charter."},{"t":"Unemployment","lv":3,"p":[80,83],"pp":"67","s":"Samples from 1947 registration show 20-30 percent of persons over 15 unemployed, and employment near 40 to 43 percent."},{"t":"Part III: Public Services","lv":1,"p":[84,105],"pp":"71","s":"Railroad finances and marine fuel costs, communications revenue and air mail start, electric power, public works man-days, and housing for dispossessed Koreans and refugees."},{"t":"Section 1. Transportation","lv":2,"p":[84,91],"pp":"71","s":"August railroad outlay exceeded income for the first time in fiscal 1947-48; rising bunker fuel prices may force a 40 percent marine freight rate increase."},{"t":"Field of Transportation","lv":3,"p":[84,84],"pp":"71","s":"Railroads accounted for 99 percent of Department of Transportation income and 92 percent of expenditures in August."},{"t":"Railroads","lv":3,"p":[85,89],"pp":"72","s":"Passenger revenue over 90 percent of income but passengers declining since May; freight tonnage and rates, equipment, employment and coal consumption."},{"t":"Marine Transport","lv":3,"p":[90,90],"pp":"77","s":"Diesel oil rose from 8 to 16 won per gallon and bituminous coal from 650 to 1,200 won per ton; Ports and Harbors Committee report."},{"t":"Highway Transport and Selected Activities","lv":3,"p":[91,91],"pp":"78","s":"About 5,000 Army-condemned tires went to the Korean Automobile Supply Company; 50,774 railroad ties arrived from the United States (212,000 in six weeks), and a Taejon creosoting plant started."},{"t":"Section 2. Communications","lv":2,"p":[92,100],"pp":"79","s":"Revenue rose from 19.2 million won in June to 31.9 million in August; worldwide air mail began 1 October with a 50-won stamp; postal savings and bureau activities."},{"t":"Postal Savings and Insurance","lv":3,"p":[92,97],"pp":"79","s":"Postal depositors held near 6.99 million since January; deposits were 838.5 million won in August 1947. Insurance policies fell to 5,357,113 while premiums and insurance in force rose, probably reflecting inflation."},{"t":"Activities","lv":3,"p":[98,100],"pp":"85","s":"Postal, Communications, Fiscal and Supply Bureau work, including air mail to all parts of the world from 1 October and weekly mail volume to Japan and the United States."},{"t":"Section 3. Public Utilities","lv":2,"p":[101,102],"pp":"88","s":"Electricity delivered reached 65 million KWH in July then fell 4 million in August; Bureau of Utilities work and streetcar operations."},{"t":"Section 4. Public Works","lv":2,"p":[103,104],"pp":"90","s":"Public works man-days and wages peaked in May, dipped in June and rose in August; highways, city planning and the National Board of Fire Commissioners."},{"t":"Section 5. Housing","lv":2,"p":[105,105],"pp":"92","s":"The 350 twenty-pyung houses for dispossessed Koreans were 85 percent complete on 30 September, delayed to 15 October by glass shortage; occupation set for 13 October."},{"t":"Part IV: Finance","lv":1,"p":[106,121],"pp":"93","s":"Government budget of 18 billion won, note issue, receipts, taxation and civilian supply program, plus bank cash, deposits, loans and insurance."},{"t":"Section 1. Government Finance","lv":2,"p":[106,112],"pp":"93","s":"Bank of Chosun note issue up 1.8 billion won July to September; 1947-48 budget at 18 billion won; 69 percent of 1946-47 taxes collected; civilian supply program and Controller of Commodities."},{"t":"Note Issue of the Bank of Chosun","lv":3,"p":[106,106],"pp":"93","s":"Note issue grew 1.8 billion won, probably linked to SKIG overdraft borrowing."},{"t":"Budget of the South Korea Interim Government","lv":3,"p":[107,108],"pp":"94","s":"Budget approved 2 July at 17,720,227,310 won reached 18 billion with supplementals; the National Economic Board reviewed supplementals on 30 September."},{"t":"Monthly Receipts and Payments","lv":3,"p":[109,109],"pp":"96","s":"Interim Government revenue fell from July to August 1947, with Monopoly Bureau income down nearly 51 percent; tobacco was below estimates and salt sales were low. Tax collection kept declining."},{"t":"Taxation","lv":3,"p":[110,110],"pp":"97","s":"69 percent of 1946-47 assessments collected, 31 percent outstanding; a study found enforcement facilities wholly inadequate."},{"t":"Civilian Supply Program","lv":3,"p":[111,111],"pp":"98","s":"Office of Civilian Supply receipts, mostly from sales of goods imported from the U.S., left a balance of 2,901 million won at end-September 1947, with 71 million won on fixed deposit in Seoul."},{"t":"Office of Controller of Commodities","lv":3,"p":[112,112],"pp":"99","s":"On 18 September, advisers reported to the National Economic Board that receipts and expenditures of the Controller of Commodities were nearly equal, with a slight deficit likely; rice and summer grain losses were about 2 percent, imported grain about 10 percent."},{"t":"Section 2. Non-Government Finance","lv":2,"p":[113,121],"pp":"100","s":"Seven banks and 401 financial associations: cash balances, deposits, government-guaranteed industrial loans, life and damage insurance, and institution activities."},{"t":"Financial Institutions and Cash Balance","lv":3,"p":[113,113],"pp":"100","s":"Seven banks including the Bank of Chosun and 401 associations tied to the Federation of Financial Associations."},{"t":"Deposits in Financial Institutions","lv":3,"p":[114,115],"pp":"101","s":"Monthly deposits and withdrawals in South Korean financial institutions rose about 275 and 290 percent over July 1946; government funds were 52 percent of deposits, banks 10, public 38. Public deposits grew steadily."},{"t":"Loans by Financial Institutions","lv":3,"p":[116,117],"pp":"103","s":"Loans to industry and commerce, with government-guaranteed loans reflecting SKIG interest in industrial development."},{"t":"Life and Damage Insurance","lv":3,"p":[118,118],"pp":"105","s":"In August 1947 life insurance policy-holders and insurance in force rose slightly over July, premiums collected fell, and claims paid rose 277 percent. Damage insurance claims paid rose to 160 percent of July's."},{"t":"Activities of the Financial Institutions","lv":3,"p":[119,121],"pp":"106","s":"September loans approved included 410 million won to Nambak Cotton and 1,157,500,000 won to the Korea Agricultural Association. Bankers debated dividends and long-term credit; 90 percent of 5,000 registered Japanese-company policyholders wished to keep policies."},{"t":"Part V: Government and Politics","lv":1,"p":[122,135],"pp":"109","s":"Death of Military Governor Lerch and Helmick's succession, the franchise law, Ordinance No. 151, the new Minju Dok Dang, party statements and the National Safety Campaign."},{"t":"Section 1. Administration","lv":2,"p":[122,126],"pp":"109","s":"Helmick became Acting Military Governor 7 September; Lerch died 11 September, with services on 16 September; Helmick's 12 September statement on USAMGIK's second anniversary."},{"t":"Section 2. Korean Interim Legislative Assembly","lv":2,"p":[127,129],"pp":"114","s":"Public Law No. 5 on popular election signed 3 September; Ordinance No. 151 on judges and prosecutors, 30 September; Japanese currency use, KILA sessions and roster."},{"t":"Section 3. Political Developments","lv":2,"p":[130,132],"pp":"117","s":"Moderates formed the Minju Dok Dang on 23 September; General Albert E. Brown spoke at the Democratic Party of Korea's second anniversary; rightist, leftist and neutral party statements."},{"t":"Section 4. Internal Security","lv":2,"p":[133,135],"pp":"120","s":"National Safety Campaign extended to 1 October after 127 accidents and 15 deaths in August; 100 police cadets began advanced training on 3 September."},{"t":"Part VI: Social","lv":1,"p":[136,151],"pp":"123","s":"Public information broadcasts and film review, Korean press reaction to the four-power conference and Soviet withdrawal proposals, education enrollment, and public health."},{"t":"Section 1. Public Information","lv":2,"p":[136,137],"pp":"123","s":"Department of Public Information reviewed 44 films (21 Korean, 17 American); 'Government Hour' radio programs covered the general election, plus a new opera series."},{"t":"Section 2. Public Opinion and the Press","lv":2,"p":[138,142],"pp":"125","s":"Editorials on the American four-power conference proposal, UN presentation of the Korean problem, the Soviet withdrawal proposal and Lerch's death, plus tables of South Korean publications."},{"t":"Four-Power Conference Proposal","lv":3,"p":[138,138],"pp":"125","s":"Han Sung Ilbo welcomed the American proposal, while extreme rightist papers attacked the Moscow Decision."},{"t":"Presentation of Korean Problem to the United Nations","lv":3,"p":[139,139],"pp":"126","s":"Right-wing papers welcomed Marshall's presentation of the Korean problem to the UN General Assembly. Cha Yoo Shin Mun was pessimistic about a UN solution, and Chosun Ilbo saw an opportunity for U.S.-Soviet accommodation."},{"t":"Soviet Proposal for Withdrawal from Korea","lv":3,"p":[139,139],"pp":"126","s":"Choong Ang Shin Mun called the Soviet withdrawal plan a diplomatic gesture; Dai Dong Sinmoon warned early withdrawal meant civil war."},{"t":"Political Activities, Death of Lerch, and Publications","lv":3,"p":[140,142],"pp":"127","s":"Editorials of condolence on Lerch's death on 12 September and tables of publications by frequency and political character."},{"t":"Section 3. Education","lv":2,"p":[143,145],"pp":"130","s":"3,058,433 students enrolled at 31 July 1947, 13,827 in 25 universities; new School of Veterinary Medicine, American Language Institute, textbooks and Korean National Youth leadership training."},{"t":"Section 4. Public Health and Welfare","lv":2,"p":[145,151],"pp":"132","s":"Typhoid immunization began 15 September after August vaccine output of 7,091,000 cc; quarantine regulations of 4 September; midwife licensing rule of 12 September; dental, sanitation, narcotics and veterinary work."}],"by":"claude-sonnet (Claude Code subagent)","emb":["K-25-in-K-24"]},{"id":"J-25","ia":"summation-non-military-activities-japan-no-25-1947-10","s":"J","no":25,"d":"1947-10","d2":"","t":"Summation of Non-Military Activities in Japan, No. 25 (October 1947)","v":"","pg":158,"o":"Summation No. 25 reports on Occupied Japan for October 1947 (statistics mostly for August-September), under the Katayama coalition Cabinet. Politically it covers the National Public Service Law, the Police Reform Bill following MacArthur's 16 September letter demanding decentralization, the Economic Decentralization Bill, the fight over the 1,800-yen wage standard and the new 1,700-yen rice price, coal control, 51 Imperial princes becoming commoners, and Liberal Party moves toward a new conservative party. Legal chapters give the revised Criminal Code, new judicial laws, war crimes trial totals, and the IMTFE defense cases of Hirota, Itagaki, Kaya, Kido and Kimura. Economic sections track the September typhoon and flood damage, coal, steel, textile, power, rail, labor, trade, rationing, finance, property and reparations figures. Social sections cover nutrition surveys, disease rates, welfare, schools, religion and media. Historians will find detailed production, price and wage data and the full text of SCAP's police-reform letter.","h":["On 16 September MacArthur replied to Prime Minister Katayama's police plan, demanding full decentralization into local police under civilian commissions, a national rural police and a 125,000-man ceiling; the Cabinet approved a 90-article Police Reform Bill on 13 October.","The Diet enacted the National Public Service Law on 16 October, creating a three-member National Personnel Commission to set civil-service standards, with competitive examinations and merit promotion.","On 22 October the Government fixed the 1947 producer rice price at 1,700 yen per koku (up from 550), while insisting on the 1,800-yen monthly wage standard, which labor federations and the press attacked.","Fifty-one Imperial princes and princesses became commoners on 14 October, leaving only the Emperor's immediate family; 47,475,000 yen was approved in retirement allowances, with none for 11 former militarist princes.","The revised Criminal Code, enacted 26 October for effect on 15 November, abolished lese-majeste, wartime treason provisions and the adultery clause discriminating against women.","War crimes figures: 385 suspects tried by 24 October, 368 convicted and 17 acquitted, with 33 death sentences (5 executed); 92 more were interned in Sugamo, bringing the total to 1,062.","September floods and typhoons damaged 572 schools in nine prefectures, cut rail traffic in central Honshu and caused over 7,000,000,000 yen in farm damage; Bank of Japan notes in circulation reached 167,665,000,000 yen.","On 2 October SCAP designated 17 arsenals holding about 19,054 metalworking machines for advance reparations transfer to China, the Netherlands, the Philippines and the United Kingdom."],"k":["Katayama Cabinet","Police Reform Bill","National Public Service Law","1,800-yen wage standard","rice price","Economic Decentralization Bill","Imperial Family commoners","Purge","Criminal Code revision","IMTFE defense cases","war crimes trials","September floods","coal control","reparations","nutrition survey"],"toc":[{"t":"Front matter","lv":1,"p":[1,5],"pp":"","s":"Cover for Summation No. 25 (October 1947), table of contents, and the list of 113 charts, maps and graphs."},{"t":"Part I: General","lv":1,"p":[6,13],"pp":"1","s":"Brief digest of the month: no change in SCAP organization, plus short political, economic and social summaries covering the Police Reform Bill, the 1,800-yen wage dispute and the September floods."},{"t":"Section 1. Organization Under SCAP","lv":2,"p":[7,7],"pp":"3","s":"Single paragraph stating there was no change in the organization of General Headquarters, SCAP, during October."},{"t":"Section 2. Political Activities","lv":2,"p":[7,8],"pp":"3","s":"Digest of the National Public Service Law (16 Oct), 1,800-yen wage fight, Police Reform Bill, Economic Decentralization Bill, 51 princes losing status, party maneuvers and the Purge (315 removed centrally)."},{"t":"Section 3. Economic Activities","lv":2,"p":[8,10],"pp":"5","s":"Digest of land sales (69,568 cho), flood damage, coal up 188,300 tons, trade firsts, new rice quotas and price rises, finance (167.7 billion yen notes) and property control."},{"t":"Section 4. Social Activities","lv":2,"p":[10,13],"pp":"11","s":"Digest of nutrition survey, flood sanitation, National Disaster Law (20 Oct), 572 flood-damaged schools, Shosoin airing, SCAP exhibits, and press, radio and film developments."},{"t":"Part II: Political","lv":1,"p":[13,34],"pp":"15","s":"Covers the National Public Service Law, police decentralization after MacArthur's 16 September letter, the Purge, the revised Criminal Code, war crimes totals and the IMTFE defense phase."},{"t":"Section 1. Government and Politics","lv":2,"p":[14,20],"pp":"17","s":"Demobilization bureaus ordered transferred or abolished, National Public Service Law, wage and rice-price controversy, party maneuvers, local autonomy bill, Diet extension to 30 November and Purge totals."},{"t":"Section 2. Public Safety","lv":2,"p":[21,24],"pp":"31","s":"Hidden army supplies in Tochigi, 185,229 August offenses, MacArthur's 16 September police letter, gangster drive (about 8,000 arrests), prison population of 82,789 and the Chofu fire destroying 700 houses."},{"t":"Section 3. Legal Affairs and War Crimes","lv":2,"p":[25,34],"pp":"39","s":"Five judicial laws and the revised Criminal Code, war crimes apprehensions and trial results (385 tried), and defense presentations before the International Military Tribunal."},{"t":"Japanese Judicial Administration","lv":3,"p":[25,26],"pp":"39","s":"Diet passed laws on court reserve funds, popular review of Supreme Court judges, judge status and impeachment; the Criminal Code abolishes lese-majeste and adultery clauses."},{"t":"Investigation, Apprehension and Prosecution of War Criminals","lv":3,"p":[26,27],"pp":"41","s":"1,498 atrocity cases on hand, 31 apprehension orders, 1,062 interned at Sugamo, and 11 trials of 21 persons with sentences up to 20 years."},{"t":"International Prosecution of War Criminals","lv":3,"p":[27,34],"pp":"42","s":"Court sat throughout October with 11 of 75 accused having finished defenses; evidence and defense statements for Hirota, Itagaki, Kaya, Kido and Kimura."},{"t":"Defense of Koki Hirota","lv":3,"p":[27,28],"pp":"42","s":"Hirota's defense, heard 26 September-3 October, portrayed the former Foreign Minister and Prime Minister as a liberal diplomat who strove for peace."},{"t":"Defense of Seishiro Itagaki","lv":3,"p":[29,30],"pp":"","s":"Defense of the former War Minister and Kwantung Army officer Itagaki, including cross-examination on whether the China war intensified after he became Minister of War."},{"t":"Defense of Okinori Kaya","lv":3,"p":[31,31],"pp":"","s":"Kaya's defense as Finance Minister under Konoye and Tojo and as president of the North China Development Company."},{"t":"Defense of Koichi Kido","lv":3,"p":[32,33],"pp":"","s":"Kido, diarist and Privy Seal keeper (heard 14-23 Oct), testified he opposed fascism, the Axis alliance and war, backed Tojo only to avert war, and said the Emperor could not stop a Cabinet-approved war."},{"t":"Defense of Heitaro Kimura","lv":3,"p":[34,34],"pp":"","s":"Kimura, Kwantung Army chief of staff 1940-41 and Vice-minister of War under Tojo, presented his defense on 24-27 October."},{"t":"Part III: Economic","lv":1,"p":[35,132],"pp":"59","s":"Twelve sections on agriculture through property control, dominated by flood and typhoon effects and power shortages; figures include 312,280 tenants buying land, coal up 188,300 tons and 167.7 billion yen in notes."},{"t":"Section 1. Agriculture and Fisheries","lv":2,"p":[36,40],"pp":"61","s":"Land sales to 312,280 tenants (69,568 cho), 265,979 hectares reclaimed since 1945, flood damage, July milk output of 15,469,000 liters, and marine landings of 113,280 tons."},{"t":"Section 2. Forestry and Mining","lv":2,"p":[41,46],"pp":"71","s":"September log output fell to 453,320,000 board feet, 6,300,000 acres of forest were inaccessible, and coal output rose 188,300 tons but missed quota by 17,700."},{"t":"Section 3. Heavy Industries","lv":2,"p":[47,55],"pp":"83","s":"Coke 176,900 tons, pig iron falling to 31,063 tons, crude runs 22,557 kiloliters and cement down 13 percent; power shortages hampered fertilizer, chemicals, machine tools and rolling stock."},{"t":"Section 4. Manufacturing","lv":2,"p":[56,65],"pp":"101","s":"Food processing, brewing, tobacco, pulp and paper, glass, instruments, motor vehicles, leather and farm implements; canned food fell from 848 to 350 tons."},{"t":"Section 5. Textile Industries","lv":2,"p":[66,81],"pp":"121","s":"905,353 bales of U.S. cotton received, raw silk up 540 bales, rayon exports prioritized for power, and wool and cotton output down."},{"t":"Section 6. Transportation and Public Utilities","lv":2,"p":[82,87],"pp":"153","s":"Registered trucks and buses hit 99,492, rail passengers fell 6.6 percent to 284,246,252 after typhoon damage, and electric generation dropped 3.8 percent to 2,368,168,000 kWh."},{"t":"Section 7. Communications","lv":2,"p":[88,94],"pp":"165","s":"Radiotelegraph service extended to Egypt and about 75 British Commonwealth points on 15 October; 1,090,426 telephones in service; flood repairs."},{"t":"Section 8. Labor","lv":2,"p":[95,103],"pp":"179","s":"Employment Security Law delayed to 1 December, communications workers' dispute over the 1,800-yen standard, a 762,000-yen labor education plan, wage rises and retrenchment."},{"t":"Section 9. Imports and Exports","lv":2,"p":[104,115],"pp":"197","s":"First postwar trade shipments: Korean apples, canned peaches to Singapore, wool via Singapore, tungsten from Korea and buttons to Sweden, with tables by commodity group."},{"t":"Section 10. Rationing and Price Control","lv":2,"p":[116,122],"pp":"221","s":"Quotas of 4,582,500 tons of rice and 2,163,750 tons of sweet potatoes, rice price raised to 1,700 yen per koku, imported food 44 percent of rations, new textile coupon system."},{"t":"Section 11. Finance","lv":2,"p":[123,128],"pp":"235","s":"46 licensed foreign concerns, notes at 167,665,000,000 yen, and national debt of 312,091,000,000 yen on 30 September."},{"t":"Section 12. Property Control and Reparations","lv":2,"p":[129,132],"pp":"247","s":"7,184 reports on neutral and Allied properties, looted property returned to China and the Philippines, 378 cartel applications cleared, and 17 arsenals designated for reparations."},{"t":"Part IV: Social","lv":1,"p":[133,158],"pp":"255","s":"Public health and welfare plus education, religion and media: nutrition and disease statistics, 572 flood-damaged schools and a map of Japan."},{"t":"Section 1. Public Health and Welfare","lv":2,"p":[134,147],"pp":"257","s":"August nutrition survey (Tokyo 1,704 calories), medical supplies worth 609,550,258 yen, 3,210,865 persons on public assistance, plus communicable diseases, narcotics, repatriation and vital statistics."},{"t":"Section 2. Education, Religion and Media of Expression","lv":2,"p":[148,158],"pp":"283","s":"572 flood-damaged schools, teacher training, new university standards, Shosoin airing, 81 showings of SCAP exhibits, and press, radio, film and SCAP Information Library developments."}],"by":"claude-sonnet (Claude Code subagent)"},{"id":"K-25","ia":"south-korean-interim-government-activities-no-25-1947-10","s":"K","no":25,"d":"1947-10","d2":"","t":"South Korean Interim Government Activities, No. 25 (October 1947)","v":"","pg":174,"o":"Issue No. 25 of South Korean Interim Government Activities covers October 1947 and is the second monthly report produced wholly in Korea, prepared by the National Economic Board. It reports on population movements, vested property, food and rice collection, prices, agriculture, mining, industry, trade, labor, transport, communications, finance, administration, the Legislative Assembly, politics, security, police, legal affairs, health, education, information and Korean National Youth. The month was dominated by the start of the 1947 rice collection, with quotas, Executive Order No. 8 banning private rice transport, and a dispute over the purchase price. Other notable items are the appointment of Major General William F. Dean as Military Governor, record note issue and budget deficits, power talks with the North in Pyongyang, and the political debate over the Russian withdrawal proposal, a general election and a Korean delegate to the United Nations. The extensive statistical tables make it a rich source for South Korean economic history.","h":["On 30 October 1947 Lieutenant General John R. Hodge announced the appointment of Major General William F. Dean as Military Governor of Korea.","The 1947 rice collection quota was set at 5,156,000 polished suk, but by 5 November only 174,291 suk (3.2 percent) had been collected; the summer grain collection ended at 1,237,576 hectoliters, 97.1 percent of quota.","Executive Order No. 8 (20 October) banned unauthorized rice transportation; the Assembly voted 45 to 1 to seek its rescission and amended the KILA Rice Collection Law to allow carrying one mal for family use.","The final 1947 rice estimate was 24,930,000 hectoliters of brown rice, about 15 percent above 1946, while cotton fell to 42,125,574 kilograms from 59,945,165.","Bank of Chosun note issue rose almost 1.4 billion won to 21,803,700 thousand won, with cumulative government overdrafts of 29.4 billion won; the approved FY 1947/48 budget reached 18.79 billion won against a revenue budget of 15.43 billion.","The rationed population reached 9,476,856 in October, while polished rice stocks fell to 434,955 hectoliters from 1,851,049 in June.","US and Soviet delegates met in Pyongyang on 21-24 October on payment for North Korean electric power; South Korea had received 537,678,737 KWH from 16 August 1945 to 31 May 1947.","Korean National Youth celebrated its first anniversary on 7-11 October, and membership grew from 199,014 to 290,333 in one month, amid attacks by rightist Taedong youth groups."],"k":["South Korean Interim Government","USAMGIK","October 1947","rice collection","Executive Order No. 8","Korean Interim Legislative Assembly","National Economic Board","vested property","inflation and note issue","Civilian Supply Program","North Korean electric power","Korean National Youth","Military Governor Dean","refugees from North Korea","price control"],"toc":[{"t":"Front matter","lv":1,"p":[1,7],"pp":"","s":"Cover of No. 25, October 1947, prepared by the National Economic Board, a blank page, and the five-page table of contents."},{"t":"Explanations and Implications","lv":1,"p":[8,9],"pp":"1","s":"Editorial note that this second Korea-produced issue lacks charts, and that SKIG departments compete for scarce power, coal and food with little integration, shaped by non-economic social controls."},{"t":"Part I: Basic Aspects","lv":1,"p":[10,21],"pp":"3","s":"Population movements, with 2,342,852 inbound against 885,667 outbound since 1945, and the status and finances of vested property under the Office of Property Custody."},{"t":"Section 1. Population","lv":2,"p":[10,15],"pp":"3","s":"Cumulative inbound and outbound movements, 147,739 refugees from the North in 1947, county population estimates for Kyonggi-do and Chungchong-pukto, and Korean affairs in China and Japan."},{"t":"Section 2. Vested Property","lv":2,"p":[16,21],"pp":"9","s":"Kangwon-do inventory of 56,730 pieces of vested property, 361,850,000 won of guaranteed textile loans, rental income of 14,043,744 won and Operations Division work on 161 cases."},{"t":"Part II: Production and Supply","lv":1,"p":[22,85],"pp":"15","s":"Food collection and rationing, official prices, 1947 crop estimates, coal and metal output, industry, commodity allocation, foreign trade and labor conditions."},{"t":"Section 1. Food Supply","lv":2,"p":[22,26],"pp":"15","s":"Rice stocks fell to 434,955 hectoliters, summer grain collection reached 97.1 percent of quota, the rice quota was 5,156,000 suk, and rationed population reached 9,476,856."},{"t":"The Problem and the Supply","lv":3,"p":[22,23],"pp":"15","s":"Grain and pulse stocks rose in October, but polished rice stocks fell steadily from 1,851,049 hectoliters in June to 434,955 at 31 October."},{"t":"Summer Grain Collection","lv":3,"p":[23,23],"pp":"16","s":"Final summer collection of 1,237,576 hectoliters, 97.1 percent of quota; Cheju-do reached only 16.4 percent because of floods, while Kyonggi-do collected 106.2 percent."},{"t":"Rice Collection","lv":3,"p":[24,25],"pp":"17","s":"Three-category provincial rice policy, Executive Order No. 8 of 20 October regulating transport, quotas totaling 5,156,000 suk, and only 3.2 percent collected by 5 November."},{"t":"Imports","lv":3,"p":[25,25],"pp":"18","s":"Grain imports of 39,259 metric tons in October, mostly 35,659 tons of barley, bringing totals since May 1946 to 559,515 tons."},{"t":"Rationing","lv":3,"p":[26,26],"pp":"19","s":"Population on general rations rose to 9,343,890 in October from 7,622,478 in March, possibly reflecting a ghost population in cities and refugee inflow."},{"t":"Section 2. Prices","lv":2,"p":[27,37],"pp":"20","s":"Official rice price of 16.10 won per kilogram polished, Economic Board price-policy minutes, Seoul price indices to August 1947, gold prices and routine price adjustments."},{"t":"Official Price of Rice and Other Grains","lv":3,"p":[27,29],"pp":"20","s":"Prices set 15 September but published in October: controlled rice at 120 won per small mal against 579 on the open market, with 30 October Board minutes defending it."},{"t":"Price Trends","lv":3,"p":[30,36],"pp":"23","s":"Seoul retail and wholesale price indices (August 1945 = 100) through August 1947, plus ten-day average prices in nine cities, with firewood, pepper powder and charcoal rising."},{"t":"Gold","lv":3,"p":[36,36],"pp":"29","s":"Open market wholesale gold price rose to 2,578 won per don in September 1947 even as illegal mining declined under the Military Government control plan."},{"t":"Activities, National Price Administration","lv":3,"p":[36,37],"pp":"29","s":"Railroad coal fixed at 736.20 won per ton, tobacco purchase prices doubled to 120 and 200 won per kilogram, and truck tires raised 20 percent to 14,500 won."},{"t":"Section 3. Agriculture, Forestry and Fisheries","lv":2,"p":[38,44],"pp":"31","s":"Rice estimated at 24,930,000 hectoliters, cotton down to 42,125,574 kilograms, fertilizer distribution of 124,890 tons, plus livestock, irrigation, forestry data and weak fishery statistics."},{"t":"Section 4. Mining and Minerals","lv":2,"p":[45,53],"pp":"38","s":"September anthracite output of 32,395 tons, 64,588 tons of Japanese bituminous coal received in October, coal sub-allocations, the Yong Wol mine and metal production tables."},{"t":"Coal","lv":3,"p":[45,50],"pp":"38","s":"Anthracite and lignite output, falling stockpiles, Samchuk as leading mine, Yong Wol deposits of 300,000,000 tons, and October allocation of 5,000 tons bituminous and 10,000 anthracite to Commerce."},{"t":"Pitch","lv":3,"p":[50,50],"pp":"43","s":"Pitch shortage for briquettes led the Coal Committee to test road asphalt as a substitute until pitch can be imported."},{"t":"Metals","lv":3,"p":[50,53],"pp":"43","s":"Kobayashi Mining's 32,000,000 won Daisung wolframite rehabilitation, gold at 400 won per gram, 100 tons of Sam Sung copper sold, and May-September metal output tables."},{"t":"Section 5. Industrial Production","lv":2,"p":[54,62],"pp":"47","s":"September output from mostly vested plants: flour 430 tons, paper 375.1 tons, cement 3,546 tons, plus cotton yarn, nails, bulbs, rubber, ginseng, salt and tobacco."},{"t":"Section 6. Allocation and Distribution","lv":2,"p":[63,73],"pp":"56","s":"Allocations of cloth, shoes, soap, tires and civilian supplies, distribution by FFA (66,697,000 won in October) and FDA, a petroleum drum deposit, and a cut in the salt ration."},{"t":"Allocations, Korean Produced Commodities","lv":3,"p":[63,66],"pp":"56","s":"Commerce Department allocation tables July 1946 to October 1947 for cotton and other cloth, socks, shoes, soap, matches, cement, tires and beer."},{"t":"Allocations, Civilian Supplies","lv":3,"p":[66,68],"pp":"59","s":"837 allocations in October, trucks to the New Korea Company and National Food Administration, 400,000 clothing units, 44,658 bags of cement and 26,500 drums of asphalt pitch."},{"t":"Distribution, Korean Produced Commodities","lv":3,"p":[68,71],"pp":"61","s":"FFA and FDA handle distribution; a 13-week lag from production report to consumer, FFA sales of 985,700,000 won in a year, and FDA province-by-province tables."},{"t":"Petroleum","lv":3,"p":[72,72],"pp":"65","s":"Drum deposit raised from 150 to 1,000 won to recover empties; sales table shows September gasoline sales of 5,900 thousand liters."},{"t":"Salt","lv":3,"p":[72,73],"pp":"65","s":"Basic salt ration dropped for lack of supply and the kimchi ration cut from 3.9 to 2.7 kilograms per capita; distribution so far ranges 0.9 to 2.7 kilograms."},{"t":"Section 7. Import and Export","lv":2,"p":[74,81],"pp":"67","s":"October private trade nearly balanced at 270,293,966 won imports and 283,207,975 won exports, barter with Hong Kong and Egypt, shipments to Japan, and Civilian Supply Program IV."},{"t":"Private Trade","lv":3,"p":[74,75],"pp":"67","s":"September imports rose 8.45 times over August (215.8 million won), including 371 tons of raw rubber; exports included ginseng, graphite, tungsten and molybdenum."},{"t":"Trade with United States","lv":3,"p":[76,76],"pp":"69","s":"A Frazer and Hansen representative arrived without completing barter deals; earlier exports to the US Commercial Company included 1,000 tons of copper ingots and 700 tons of scheelite."},{"t":"Trade with Japan","lv":3,"p":[76,77],"pp":"69","s":"September and October shipments of graphite, copper, lead and zinc were credited to SCAP's special account for Korea, and SCAP accepted 3,000,000 bundles of laver."},{"t":"Trade with Hong Kong","lv":3,"p":[77,77],"pp":"70","s":"Red ginseng sold for HK$1,800,000, 25 tons of ferro-tungsten sold, and 300 tons of smoked rubber sheets bartered for about 45,000 pounds of raw silk."},{"t":"Trade with Egypt","lv":3,"p":[77,77],"pp":"70","s":"Barter of 3,000 bales of Egyptian cotton for 730 tons of tungsten advanced, with 1,000 bales to ship first and 2,000 bales on the SS Manchuria in November."},{"t":"Korean Items for Americans in Korea","lv":3,"p":[77,77],"pp":"70","s":"Souvenirs sold to the Army Post Exchange were worth $5,733 in September and $9,139 in October, totaling $18,278 credited over four months."},{"t":"Commercial Shipping Agency","lv":3,"p":[77,78],"pp":"70","s":"A shipping agency of two American firms and one British firm was set up in September; the M/S Turk's Head left Pusan 13 October with 9,957 cases of apples."},{"t":"Imports, Civilian Supply","lv":3,"p":[78,81],"pp":"71","s":"Program IV Part 2 released 20 October ($19,910,739 for other supplies, including 900,000 tons of coal), price data requested from the War Department, and coal, salt, fertilizer and food import tables."},{"t":"Section 8. Labor","lv":2,"p":[82,85],"pp":"75","s":"National Labor Week from 20 October, Tai Han No Chong labor college in Inchon, union contests in Pusan, a Seoul employment census of 59,009 workers and industrial health work."},{"t":"Part III: Public Services","lv":1,"p":[86,109],"pp":"79","s":"Railroads, shipping, communications, power, public works and housing: transport revenue, a first Korean-crewed voyage to Shanghai, postal rates doubled and a 400-house project completed."},{"t":"Section 1. Transportation","lv":2,"p":[86,94],"pp":"79","s":"September rail receipts of 324,460 thousand won, 7,106 thousand passengers, 643 locomotives of which 55 percent were inoperative, and a stevedore strike in Pusan."},{"t":"General and Revenue and Expenditures","lv":3,"p":[86,87],"pp":"79","s":"Railroads carry over 90 percent of freight; transportation revenue rose 7 percent in September after a 50 percent freight rate rise, while expenditures rose 18 percent."},{"t":"Railroads","lv":3,"p":[87,93],"pp":"79","s":"Record passenger traffic, freight tonnage of 391,151 tons, locomotive census, back-shop repairs of 161 freight cars, wages and coal consumption of 57,331 tons."},{"t":"Marine Transport","lv":3,"p":[93,93],"pp":"86","s":"LST 002 sailed from Pusan to Shanghai on 12 October, the first Korean-flag, Korean-crewed ship in China in 40 years, carrying 400 tons of hospital equipment."},{"t":"Highway Transport","lv":3,"p":[94,94],"pp":"87","s":"The Bureau of Highway Transportation operates without an American adviser, and a critical tire shortage looms before the rice collection."},{"t":"Rates and Regulation Committee","lv":3,"p":[94,94],"pp":"87","s":"After a three-day stevedore strike at Pusan the committee granted a 29 percent increase in handling charges for coal ships."},{"t":"Section 2. Communications","lv":2,"p":[95,103],"pp":"88","s":"Revenue of 35.8 million won in September, expenditures up 72 percent to 74 million, staff cut by 4,466, postal rates doubled on 1 October, and postal savings and insurance data."},{"t":"Section 3. Public Utilities","lv":2,"p":[104,105],"pp":"97","s":"October power deliveries of 80 million KWH (55 from the North), 130 streetcars running in Seoul, 54 busses, and power plants hit by water shortages."},{"t":"Section 4. Public Works","lv":2,"p":[106,107],"pp":"99","s":"150,839 man-days and 26,750 thousand won in wages in October, Yong Dong Po highway repaving, the Han River to Ascom City pipeline with 290 leaks, and a Cheju water survey."},{"t":"Section 5. Housing","lv":2,"p":[108,109],"pp":"101","s":"Eleven experimental 8-pyung prefabricated houses finished at Yong Dong Po, design changed to 7 pyung, a 40-house project planned, and the 400-house Seoul project completed."},{"t":"Part IV: Finance","lv":1,"p":[110,129],"pp":"103","s":"Government finance, with note issue at 21.8 billion won and an 18.79 billion won budget, and non-government finance covering banks, deposits, loans and insurance."},{"t":"Section 1. Government Finance","lv":2,"p":[110,117],"pp":"103","s":"Note issue up 1.4 billion won in October, overdrafts of 29.4 billion, revenue at 72-75 percent of projection, approved budget of 18,785,864,050 won, and civilian supply surplus of 3.996 billion."},{"t":"Note Issue of the Bank of Chosun","lv":3,"p":[110,111],"pp":"103","s":"Note issue rose to 21,803,700 thousand won, the largest monthly rise since August 1946 apart from the 1946 rice collection months."},{"t":"National Depository of the South Korean Interim Government","lv":3,"p":[111,113],"pp":"104","s":"Government overdrafts reached 29,425,434 thousand won against 9,566,386 thousand revenue deposits; cumulative excess withdrawals stood at 19,859 million won, with 3 percent interest."},{"t":"Revenue Budget of the South Korean Interim Government","lv":3,"p":[113,114],"pp":"106","s":"Revenue budget of 15,434,854,856 won passed 2 July; first and second quarter collections were only 75 and 72 percent of projections, with taxes at 66 and 49 percent."},{"t":"Expenditure Budget of SKIG","lv":3,"p":[114,115],"pp":"107","s":"Budget of 17,720,227,310 won approved 2 July grew to 18,785,864,050 won by 31 October, with 15 pending requests of nearly 3.3 billion won under Board review."},{"t":"Monthly Receipts and Payments","lv":3,"p":[115,116],"pp":"108","s":"September expenditures fell 433 million won while revenue rose 461 million; revenue covered 64 percent of spending in 1947/48 against 37 percent in 1946/47."},{"t":"Comparison of Expenditure Budget with Revenue Collections","lv":3,"p":[117,117],"pp":"110","s":"Approved expenditures exceed projected revenues by 3,351 million won; semi-annual collections were 5,687,898 thousand won, 61 percent of the budget average."},{"t":"Civilian Supply Program","lv":3,"p":[117,117],"pp":"110","s":"October receipts of 1,224,752,160 won exceeded disbursements of 130,216,169, leaving a balance of 3,995,730,267 won, with 75 million won on fixed deposit."},{"t":"Section 2. Non-Government Finance","lv":2,"p":[118,129],"pp":"111","s":"Seven banks and 142 financial associations, September deposits up 3.6 billion won to 34,441 million, loan balances of 14,310 million, and credit lines of 12 billion won for rice collection."},{"t":"Financial Institutions and Cash Balances","lv":3,"p":[118,119],"pp":"111","s":"Financial associations reorganized from 401 offices to 142 with 409 branches; cash balances rose 427 million won to 1,861,983 thousand in September for rice purchases."},{"t":"Deposits in Financial Institutions","lv":3,"p":[119,122],"pp":"112","s":"Deposit balances rose over 3.6 billion won in September, 55 percent of them government; the Bank of Chosun held 53 percent of all deposits."},{"t":"Loans by Financial Institutions","lv":3,"p":[123,124],"pp":"116","s":"New loans of 7,842 million won in September; balances rose to 14,310 million, with government-guaranteed loans up 324 million and Chosun Industrial Bank holding 30 percent."},{"t":"Life Insurance","lv":3,"p":[125,125],"pp":"118","s":"September insurance in force rose 16 percent to 393,277 thousand won; policyholders were down about 48 percent from September 1946 after large lapses."},{"t":"Damage Insurance","lv":3,"p":[126,126],"pp":"119","s":"Five damage insurers wrote 7,260 policies in September with 1,339,870 thousand won in liabilities; claims paid fell to 31 thousand won."},{"t":"Activities of the Financial Institutions","lv":3,"p":[127,128],"pp":"120","s":"Loans of 642 million won to cotton gins, 12 billion won of credit lines for rice collection, a bankers' deposit drive for November-December, and gold registration under Ordinance No. 150."},{"t":"Activities of Life and Damage Insurance Companies","lv":3,"p":[128,128],"pp":"121","s":"Seoul Fire and Marine seeks to buy its vested stock and raise capital from 12.5 to 20 million won; life insurers' reinsurance placed 2,475,000 won."},{"t":"Part V: Government and Politics","lv":1,"p":[130,152],"pp":"123","s":"Dean's appointment as Military Governor, Assembly debates on organization and rice, political reactions to election and UN questions, security, police, interzone power talks and legal affairs."},{"t":"Section 1. Administration","lv":2,"p":[130,135],"pp":"123","s":"Hodge appointed Major General Dean Military Governor on 30 October, KNY anniversary speeches, civil service training, Executive Orders Nos. 6 and 7, and a 30 October Economic Board session on the rice price."},{"t":"Appointment of Military Governor","lv":3,"p":[130,130],"pp":"123","s":"Lieutenant General John R. Hodge announced on 30 October 1947 that Major General William F. Dean was appointed Military Governor of Korea."},{"t":"Speeches to Korean National Youth, Inc.","lv":3,"p":[130,131],"pp":"123","s":"Speeches by General Hodge and Brigadier General Helmick on 9 October at the first anniversary, urging non-partisan citizenship and praising the late General Lerch."},{"t":"Korean Civil Service","lv":3,"p":[133,133],"pp":"125","s":"Weekly work of the seven civil service divisions, a language course for Japanese-educated employees, and classification actions for the National Food Administration."},{"t":"Executive Orders","lv":3,"p":[133,133],"pp":"125","s":"Executive Order No. 6 (6 October) required notice before police impound vested-business books; No. 7 (22 October) closed the Haibang News Agency under Proclamation No. 2."},{"t":"National Economic Board","lv":3,"p":[134,135],"pp":"126","s":"Approved 547,440,000 won for Internal Security and 17,000,000 for grain inspectors; on 30 October defended the 640 won per bag rice price before Assembly members."},{"t":"Section 2. Korean Interim Legislative Assembly","lv":2,"p":[136,139],"pp":"128","s":"Sixteen sessions averaging 66 members, the Temporary Administrative Organizational Law, recommendation of Rhee as UN delegate, rice law amendment, and approval of Lee Choon Ho for Seoul National University."},{"t":"General","lv":3,"p":[136,136],"pp":"128","s":"Sixteen sessions (Nos. 149-164) held in October with average attendance of 66 of 90 members; vice-speakers Yun Ki Sop and Choe Tong O presided."},{"t":"Korean Interim Administrative Organizational Law","lv":3,"p":[137,138],"pp":"129","s":"On 14 and 16 October the Assembly renamed it the Temporary Administrative Organizational Law, kept a Department of Police by 44 to 4, and deadlocked on splitting Agriculture and Forestry."},{"t":"Delegate to the United Nations","lv":3,"p":[138,138],"pp":"130","s":"A petition from 72 groups for Syngman Rhee as UN delegate was forwarded to the administrative branch by 34 to 7 after leftists argued for troop withdrawal instead."},{"t":"Ordinance No. 118","lv":3,"p":[138,139],"pp":"130","s":"A 40 to 1 vote amended Section V so the Military Governor must promulgate or return ordinances within fifteen days."},{"t":"Rice Collection Program","lv":3,"p":[139,139],"pp":"131","s":"Confusion between National Food Regulation No. 6 and KILA Public Act No. 6, a 45 to 1 vote to rescind Executive Order No. 8, and a family-use exemption of one mal."},{"t":"President of Seoul National University","lv":3,"p":[139,139],"pp":"131","s":"Lee Choon Ho was approved on 10 October as president of Seoul National University by a vote of 45 to 4."},{"t":"Temporary Law for the Cessation of Professional Entertainment Activities","lv":3,"p":[139,139],"pp":"131","s":"First reading completed 28 October of a bill to temporarily close restaurants, night clubs and dance halls as demoralizing."},{"t":"Section 3. Political Developments","lv":2,"p":[140,142],"pp":"132","s":"Rhee's call for an immediate election, formation of the moderate National Independence Federation under Kimm Kiu Sic, and uproar over the police report of the South Korea Labor Party's 15 August riot plan."},{"t":"Section 4. Internal Security","lv":2,"p":[143,143],"pp":"135","s":"Coast Guard harbor block at Pusan boarded 672 vessels in one week, Constabulary guarded Pusan coal, and conferences addressed police friction with Constabulary and terroristic youth groups."},{"t":"Section 5. Police","lv":2,"p":[144,146],"pp":"136","s":"Second police anniversary, rules limiting rice-quota abuses, Executive Order No. 6 on inspections, curbs on forced fund solicitation, venereal disease clinics, and arrests of 84 Labor Party members in Cholla-namdo."},{"t":"Section 6. Interzone Activities","lv":2,"p":[147,148],"pp":"139","s":"Pyongyang talks of 21-24 October on payment for 537,678,737 KWH of North Korean power, and an 18 October mail exchange of 6,348 South Korean letters."},{"t":"Section 7. Legal Affairs","lv":2,"p":[149,152],"pp":"141","s":"Ordinance No. 150 on gold, Executive Order No. 8 on rice transport, three Justice Department opinions, 20,652 prisoners, and 54 of 700 applicants admitted to the bar."},{"t":"Part VI: Social","lv":1,"p":[153,174],"pp":"145","s":"Public health, education, information, press opinion and Korean National Youth, including a Sanchong cholera outbreak, 3,122,140 pupils, and youth membership of 290,333."},{"t":"Section 1. Public Health and Welfare","lv":2,"p":[153,160],"pp":"145","s":"Cholera outbreak with 14 proven cases in Sanchong, vaccine stocks, typhus control plan, 1,764 narcotic licenses, refugee camp winterization, 19,707 refugee dwellings, and Women's Bureau work."},{"t":"Section 2. Education","lv":2,"p":[161,167],"pp":"153","s":"Korean Alphabet Day on 9 October, 12,452 schools with 3,122,140 pupils, textbook output of 339,754, scholarships to the US, Technological Training Board projects, and a Cheju-do teacher shortage."},{"t":"Cheju-do Education","lv":3,"p":[165,167],"pp":"157","s":"Eight accredited middle schools with 2,921 students and 60 teacher vacancies, 96 elementary schools short by 471 teachers, and a school dispute with the 9th Constabulary."},{"t":"Section 3. Public Information","lv":2,"p":[168,170],"pp":"160","s":"95 news releases, 1,060,000 pamphlets with Hodge's UN letter air-dropped on villages, seven information centers, 11 radio stations, and 21 US films licensed."},{"t":"Section 4. Press and Public Opinion","lv":2,"p":[171,172],"pp":"163","s":"Moderate, leftist and rightist papers split over the Russian withdrawal proposal, Rhee's election call, and the ban on rice transportation, which was blamed for sudden price rises."},{"t":"Section 5. Korean National Youth, Inc.","lv":2,"p":[173,174],"pp":"165","s":"Helmick's statement of aims, membership up to 290,333 by 31 October, first anniversary celebrations of 7-11 October with 10,000 in the Seoul parade, and terrorism by Taedong groups."}],"by":"claude-sonnet (Claude Code subagent)"},{"id":"K-25-in-K-24","ia":"south-korean-interim-government-activities-no-24-1947-09","s":"K","no":25,"d":"1947-10","d2":"","t":"South Korean Interim Government Activities, No. 25 (October 1947)","v":"Bound in K-24","host":"K-24","rg":[[152,333]],"kind":"bound","found":"Library call-number title page 'M 105.13:25' on PDF p. 152","pg":182,"o":"This is South Korean Interim Government Activities No. 25 for October 1947, prepared in Korea by the National Economic Board for USAMGIK. It opens with an editorial on poor integration among SKIG agencies and covers population and repatriation, vested property, food supply, prices, agriculture, mining, industry, trade, labor, public services, finance, government and politics, and social matters. The dominant concerns are the 1947 rice collection and the contested rice price, with a falling rice stock, a 30 October National Economic Board session and legislative wrangling over collection law. Politically it records the appointment of Maj. Gen. William F. Dean as Military Governor, the Russian troop withdrawal proposal, the general election and UN representation debates, leftist and rightist clashes, and the Korean National Youth anniversary. Historians will find dense statistical tables, import and allocation detail from the Civilian Supply Program, financial data, and records of Assembly proceedings and press opinion.","h":["Hodge announced on 30 October 1947 that Maj. Gen. William F. Dean would become Military Governor of Korea.","Summer grain collection reached 1,237,576 hectoliters, 97.1 percent of quota, though Cheju-do managed only 16.4 percent, while polished rice stocks fell from 1,851,049 hl in June to 434,955 in October.","The rice selling price was raised from 85 to 120 won per small mal against a 640 won per bag purchase price, projecting a 1.06 billion won deficit, as debated at the 30 October National Economic Board session.","The final 1947 rice estimate was 24,930,000 hectoliters of brown rice, about 15 percent above 1946; only 3.2 percent of the 5,156,000 suk quota was collected by 5 November.","Executive Order No. 8 of 20 October restricted rice transportation; the Legislative Assembly voted to rescind it and debated rival rice collection laws, and most papers attacked the ban.","Bank of Chosun note issue rose almost 1.4 billion won in October to 21.8 billion, driven by rice collection and deficit spending, with the 1947/48 budget at 18.79 billion won.","Alien registration in Japan counted 458,359 Koreans by 15 September, and Korean National Youth membership rose from 157,218 to 290,333 between 31 July and 31 October.","A 21-24 October Pyongyang conference fixed payment for North Korean electric power in goods at controlled ceiling prices; use from August 1945 to May 1947 totaled 837,678,737 KWH."],"k":["South Korean Interim Government","USAMGIK","rice collection","price control","vested property","Civilian Supply Program","Korean National Youth","Legislative Assembly","repatriation","National Economic Board","Military Governor Dean","coal","North-South interzone","finance","Cheju-do"],"toc":[{"t":"Front matter","lv":1,"p":[152,161],"pp":"","s":"Cover of South Korean Interim Government Activities No. 25 (October 1947), prepared by the National Economic Board, with blank pages and a contents list of Parts I to VI."},{"t":"Explanations and Implications","lv":1,"p":[162,163],"pp":"1","s":"Editors call this the second monthly report produced wholly in Korea. They say poor integration among SKIG departments, such as competing claims on electric power and bituminous coal, limits the report."},{"t":"Part I: Basic Aspects","lv":1,"p":[164,175],"pp":"3","s":"Population movement and repatriation (458,359 Koreans registered in Japan) and vested property, including a 56,730-piece Kangwon-do inventory and six loans guaranteed totaling 361,850,000 won."},{"t":"Section 1: Population","lv":2,"p":[164,169],"pp":"3","s":"Since 15 October 1945, 885,667 left and 2,342,852 entered; 21,363 Koreans were caught entering Japan illegally. Northern refugees numbered 9,661 in October, and 458,359 Koreans registered in Japan."},{"t":"Section 2: Vested Property","lv":2,"p":[170,175],"pp":"9","s":"The Office of Property Custody completed a Kangwon-do inventory of 56,730 pieces of former Japanese property, guaranteed six loans totaling 361,850,000 won, and planned a 150 million won rehabilitation of 12 textile units."},{"t":"Part II: Production and Supply","lv":1,"p":[176,239],"pp":"15","s":"Food supply, prices, agriculture, mining, industry, allocation, trade and labor: summer grain reached 97.1 percent of quota, rice price rose to 120 won per small mal, and 64,588 tons of coal arrived from Japan."},{"t":"Section 1: Food Supply","lv":2,"p":[176,180],"pp":"15","s":"Summer grain collection reached 1,237,576 hectoliters, 97.1 percent of quota, with Cheju-do at 16.4 percent. Polished rice stocks fell from 1,851,049 hl in June to 434,955 in October; Executive Order No. 8 restricted rice transport."},{"t":"Section 2: Prices","lv":2,"p":[181,191],"pp":"20","s":"The 30 October National Economic Board minutes defended the 640 won per bag purchase price and 120 won per small mal selling price, projecting a 1.06 billion won deficit. Seoul price indices and coal and tobacco actions are also reported."},{"t":"Section 3: Agriculture, Forestry and Fisheries","lv":2,"p":[192,198],"pp":"31","s":"Final 1947 rice estimate is 24,930,000 hectoliters of brown rice, about 15 percent above 1946. Cotton fell to 42,125,574 kg and cocoon collection reached 45.6 percent of goal; irrigation budget was 967 million won."},{"t":"Section 4: Mining and Minerals","lv":2,"p":[199,207],"pp":"38","s":"Anthracite output rose to 32,395 tons in September from 21,043 in August. Yong Wol mine could reach 100,000 tons monthly with a 24-mile railroad; bituminous coal from Japan was 64,588 tons in October."},{"t":"Section 5: Industrial Production","lv":2,"p":[208,216],"pp":"47","s":"September flour output was 430.4 tons, up 83 percent, and machine-made paper reached 375.1 tons, the highest since November 1946. Onada cement fell 44 percent and firebrick 72 percent; Inchon welding plant began making nails."},{"t":"Section 6: Allocation and Distribution","lv":2,"p":[217,227],"pp":"56","s":"Commerce made 367 civilian supply allocations in September and 837 in October. FFA spent 66,697,000 won in October, FDA sales to provinces reached 269,575,779 won, and the salt kimchi ration was cut to 2.7 kg."},{"t":"Section 7: Import and Export","lv":2,"p":[228,235],"pp":"67","s":"October private trade nearly balanced on permit estimates: 270 million won imports, 283 million exports. Covers trade with the U.S., Japan, Hong Kong and Egypt, Civilian Supply Program IV, and 64,588 tons of imported coal."},{"t":"Section 8: Labor","lv":2,"p":[236,239],"pp":"75","s":"National Labor Week opened 20 October; a Seoul employee survey counted 59,009 workers. Rightist and leftist unions contested Pusan seamen, two employers faced court for hours violations, and about 15 plants had health inspections."},{"t":"Part III: Public Services","lv":1,"p":[240,263],"pp":"79","s":"Transportation, communications, utilities, public works and housing: a 50 percent freight-rate rise lifted revenue, 55 percent of locomotives stayed idle, postal rates doubled on 1 October, and the housing agency finished 11 prefabricated houses."},{"t":"Section 1: Transportation","lv":2,"p":[240,248],"pp":"79","s":"Department revenue rose 7 percent in September after the 20 August 50 percent freight-rate rise; expenditures rose 18 percent. 55 percent of locomotives stayed inoperative, and a three-day Pusan stevedore strike won a 29 percent increase."},{"t":"Section 2: Communications","lv":2,"p":[249,257],"pp":"88","s":"Revenue rose from 31.9 to 35.8 million won in September while expenditures rose 72 percent to 74 million won. Staff fell by 4,466 since April, and postal rates doubled on 1 October."},{"t":"Section 3: Public Utilities","lv":2,"p":[258,259],"pp":"97","s":"Power delivered from North Korea rose 25 percent in October to 55 million kWh of 80 million total. Seoul ran 130 streetcars carrying about 500,000 passengers daily, and 23 trucks were converted to busses in 1947."},{"t":"Section 4: Public Works","lv":2,"p":[260,261],"pp":"99","s":"October public works reached 150,839 man-days and 26.75 million won in wages, the highest since May. Yong Dong Po to Si Hung highway resurfacing began, and Han River pipeline repairs found 290 leaks."},{"t":"Section 5: Housing","lv":2,"p":[262,263],"pp":"101","s":"The National Housing Administration finished 11 prefabricated low-cost houses at Yong Dong Po, shifting design from 8 to 7 pyung. A forty-house project with Seoul is planned; Seoul completed its 400-house project."},{"t":"Part IV: Finance","lv":1,"p":[264,283],"pp":"103","s":"Government and non-government finance: Bank of Chosun note issue reached 21.8 billion won, the 1947/48 budget stood at 18.79 billion, and deposit balances rose to 34.4 billion won."},{"t":"Section 1: Government Finance","lv":2,"p":[264,271],"pp":"103","s":"Bank of Chosun note issue rose almost 1.4 billion won in October to 21.8 billion, driven by rice collection. The 1947/48 budget was 18.79 billion won with 3.3 billion more requested; excess government withdrawals reached 19,859 million."},{"t":"Section 2: Non-Government Finance","lv":2,"p":[272,283],"pp":"111","s":"Deposit balances rose 3.6 billion to 34.4 billion won, with the Bank of Chosun holding 53 percent; loans outstanding reached 14.3 billion. Credit lines of 12 billion won were arranged for rice collection."},{"t":"Part V: Government and Politics","lv":1,"p":[284,305],"pp":"123","s":"Administration, legislature, politics, security, police, interzone and legal affairs: Dean named Military Governor on 30 October, Assembly debated rice collection laws, and the Pyongyang conference set power payment in goods."},{"t":"Section 1: Administration","lv":2,"p":[284,288],"pp":"123","s":"Hodge announced on 30 October that Maj. Gen. William F. Dean would become Military Governor. Hodge's and Helmick's Korean National Youth speeches are reprinted, with Executive Orders 6 and 7 and the 640-won rice price session."},{"t":"Section 2: Korean Interim Legislative Assembly","lv":2,"p":[289,292],"pp":"128","s":"In 16 sessions averaging 66 members, the Assembly forwarded Rhee's UN delegate petition 34 to 7, debated rival rice collection laws, voted to rescind Executive Order No. 8, and approved Lee Choon Ho as Seoul National University president."},{"t":"Section 3: Political Developments","lv":2,"p":[293,295],"pp":"132","s":"Politics centered on Rhee as UN representative, election timing and the Russian withdrawal proposal. Moderates formed the National Independence Federation under Kimm Kiu Sic; release of the 15 August riot plan prompted demands to disband leftist groups."},{"t":"Section 4: Internal Security","lv":2,"p":[296,296],"pp":"135","s":"The Department of Internal Security comprises the Coast Guard and Constabulary. Pusan harbor block boarded 436 vessels in one week and 672 the next; Constabulary guarded supply areas including the Pusan coal pile."},{"t":"Section 5: Police","lv":2,"p":[297,299],"pp":"136","s":"Police Director Chough marked the force's second anniversary and warned of a difficult rice collection. Executive Order No. 8 and citizen quota committees aimed to curb abuses; labor party arrests, clashes and three murders were reported."},{"t":"Section 6: Interzone Activities","lv":2,"p":[300,301],"pp":"139","s":"North Korean power use from 16 August 1945 to 31 May 1947 was 837,678,737 KWH. A 21-24 October Pyongyang conference fixed payment in goods at ceiling prices; mail exchanged 18 October included 6,348 letters from the South."},{"t":"Section 7: Legal Affairs","lv":2,"p":[302,305],"pp":"141","s":"Covers air mail Order No. 19, Ordinance No. 150 on gold transactions and Executive Order No. 8 on rice transport. Prisoners numbered 20,652, and 54 of 700 applicants passed the bar."},{"t":"Part VI: Social","lv":1,"p":[306,327],"pp":"145","s":"Health, education, information, press and youth: cholera in Sanchong, 12,452 schools with 3,122,140 pupils, 1,060,000 copies of Hodge's letter dropped by air, and Korean National Youth membership of 290,333."},{"t":"Section 1: Public Health and Welfare","lv":2,"p":[306,313],"pp":"145","s":"A cholera outbreak in Sanchong had 14 cases and 6 deaths. Other items include six leper clinics, swine plague treated with sulfanilamide, 19,707 refugee dwellings, camp winterization and Women's Bureau committees."},{"t":"Section 2: Education","lv":2,"p":[314,320],"pp":"153","s":"South Korea had 12,452 schools and 3,122,140 pupils; textbook printing fell to 339,754 for lack of paper. Covers Rockefeller scholarships, the Korea University incident and Cheju-do's severe teacher shortage."},{"t":"Section 3: Public Information","lv":2,"p":[321,323],"pp":"160","s":"The Department of Public Information released 95 news items and printed one issue of Farmers Weekly. 1,060,000 copies of Hodge's letter on the UN proposal were dropped by air; it also reports 11 radio stations and 65 theaters."},{"t":"Section 4: Press and Public Opinion","lv":2,"p":[324,325],"pp":"163","s":"The press debated the Russian troop withdrawal proposal, with leftist Dok Lib Sinbo praising the Soviet plan. Rhee's 18 October election call was quoted; most papers attacked the rice transport ban."},{"t":"Section 5: Korean National Youth, Inc.","lv":2,"p":[326,327],"pp":"165","s":"Helmick's 20 October memorandum called Korean National Youth an official non-political activity for ages 18 to 35, led by Lee Bum Suk. Membership rose from 157,218 on 31 July to 290,333 on 31 October."},{"t":"Back matter","lv":1,"p":[328,333],"pp":"","s":"Blank or nearly blank pages 328 to 333, holding only Google digitization notices, a library barcode and a due-date slip stamped June 23 2010."}],"by":"claude-sonnet (Claude Code subagent)"},{"id":"J-26","ia":"summation-non-military-activities-japan-no-26-1947-11","s":"J","no":26,"d":"1947-11","d2":"","t":"Summation of Non-Military Activities in Japan, No. 26 (November 1947)","v":"","pg":155,"o":"Summation No. 26 covers November 1947 (with many statistics for September and October) in four parts: General, Political, Economic and Social. The month was dominated by the Katayama coalition's fight to pass the Bill for Temporary State Control of Coal Mines, which split the Democratic Party under Shidehara, and by the dismissal of Agriculture Minister Hirano under Article 68. The Diet passed Supplementary Budget No. 7 (about 85.6 billion yen) with a drastic tax revision, and the 1,800-yen standard wage came under pressure from Central Labor Relations Committee rulings in the communications and railway disputes. Labor legislation advanced with the Unemployment Allowance, Unemployment Insurance and Employment Security Laws. Economic sections record record coal output (2,422,000 tons), the dissolution of the agricultural associations, price decontrol and staple-food price rises. The war crimes section reproduces defense testimony before the Tokyo Tribunal for Koiso, Matsui, Minami, Muto, Oka and Oshima, useful for historians of the trials, the purge, labor and the economic recovery programme.","h":["The Bill for Temporary State Control of Coal Mines passed the House of Representatives 233 to 155 on 25 November after the Democratic Party split, with 24 members of Shidehara's faction voting against and later forming the Doshi Club.","On 4 November Prime Minister Katayama dismissed Agriculture and Forestry Minister Rikizo Hirano, the first use of the Article 68 power to remove a minister, deepening divisions in the Social Democratic Party.","Supplementary Budget No. 7 of 85,602,368,000 yen was approved by the Diet on 29 November with a tax revision aiming at 133,240,000,000 yen in 1947-48 taxes, including a levy on undamaged houses.","The Diet enacted the Unemployment Allowance and Unemployment Insurance Laws on 21 November, covering over 7,000,000 workers in firms of five or more, with insurance benefits starting 1 May 1948, plus the Employment Security Law.","The Diet dissolved the government-dominated agricultural associations (11,000 associations, 55,000 practice associations, over 40,000,000,000 yen assets) on 7 November, with a deadline of 15 August 1948 and a legal basis for voluntary cooperatives.","October coal production reached 2,422,000 metric tons, the highest of the Occupation, while the Central Labor Relations Committee ruled on 14 November that government wages should be raised above the 1,800-yen standard.","War crimes data showed 397 suspects tried, 380 found guilty and 33 sentenced to death; the Tokyo Tribunal heard defense cases for Koiso, Matsui, Minami, Muto, Oka and Oshima, with Maj. Gen. Myron C. Cramer acting president from 8 November.","Repatriation brought 46,589 Japanese home between 31 October and 27 November, 43,424 of them from Soviet and Soviet-controlled areas, and price controls on about 200 minor commodities ended 27 October."],"k":["Katayama Cabinet","coal mines state control bill","Hirano dismissal","Democratic Party split","Supplementary Budget No. 7","1,800-yen wage standard","unemployment insurance","agricultural cooperatives","purge","Tokyo war crimes trial","repatriation","price decontrol","Central Labor Relations Committee","coal production","Doshi Club"],"toc":[{"t":"Front matter","lv":1,"p":[1,5],"pp":"","s":"Cover, table of contents for Parts I-IV and the list of charts, maps and graphs numbered 1 to 104."},{"t":"Part I: General","lv":1,"p":[6,13],"pp":"1","s":"Digest of November: no change in SCAP organization, the coal bill and Hirano dismissal, record coal output, an 85.6 billion yen budget, and new social security laws."},{"t":"Section 1. Organization Under SCAP","lv":2,"p":[7,7],"pp":"3","s":"No change occurred in the nonmilitary organization of General Headquarters, SCAP, during November."},{"t":"Section 2. Political Activities","lv":2,"p":[7,8],"pp":"3","s":"Coal bill passed 233 to 155 on 25 November, Hirano removed 4 November, Heima Hayashi purged 26 November, Domestic Relations Court Law enacted, and 61 atrocity investigations completed."},{"t":"Section 3. Economic Activities","lv":2,"p":[8,11],"pp":"5","s":"Agricultural associations dissolved 7 November, October coal at 2,422,000 tons, Supplementary Budget No. 7 approved 29 November, price controls lifted on 200 minor items, and new labor laws."},{"t":"Section 4. Social Activities","lv":2,"p":[12,13],"pp":"13","s":"Typhus immunization on Hokkaido, public assistance raised 10 percent on 1 November, Child Welfare Law of 21 November, education conferences, and first Book Week on 17 November."},{"t":"Part II: Political","lv":1,"p":[14,33],"pp":"17","s":"The coal bill and budget fights, party realignment, the purge, public safety statistics, and Tokyo Tribunal defense testimony for six accused."},{"t":"Section 1. Government and Politics","lv":2,"p":[15,20],"pp":"19","s":"Coal bill passed the lower house 233 to 155, the Democratic Party split under Shidehara, Hirano was dismissed, and 19,709 persons were screened in the purge."},{"t":"Political Developments","lv":3,"p":[15,17],"pp":"","s":"Coal bill stalemate, the 11 November leaders' agreement, passage on 25 November, and Supplementary Budget No. 7 introduced 1 November with a tax revision aiming at 133,240,000,000 yen."},{"t":"Political Parties","lv":3,"p":[18,19],"pp":"","s":"Hirano removed under Article 68, a Liberal drive of 9 November for a new conservative party, and the Shidehara faction's split from the Democrats into the Doshi Club."},{"t":"The Diet","lv":3,"p":[20,20],"pp":"","s":"By 30 November the First National Diet had received 140 Cabinet bills, seven member bills and five committee bills, and enacted 65."},{"t":"The Purge","lv":3,"p":[20,20],"pp":"","s":"19,709 persons screened 26 October to 28 November, Heima Hayashi purged 26 November, and 16 information media organizations blacklisted on 25 November."},{"t":"Section 2. Public Safety","lv":2,"p":[21,23],"pp":"31","s":"Economic Police found 19,885,253 yen of hoarded goods in October; 181,915 offenses in September; prisons held 83,875; 868 fires; Akita Prison fire on 12 November."},{"t":"Section 3. Legal Affairs and War Crimes","lv":2,"p":[24,33],"pp":"37","s":"Two judicial laws enacted, 61 investigations completed, 397 war criminals tried to date, and defense testimony before the Tokyo Tribunal for six accused."},{"t":"Legal Affairs","lv":3,"p":[24,24],"pp":"","s":"Diet enacted the Domestic Relations Court Law and Sea Casualties Inquiry Law; the Supreme Court heard three constitutional review cases, with decisions due in December."},{"t":"Investigation of Suspected War Criminals","lv":3,"p":[25,25],"pp":"","s":"Sixty-one atrocity investigations completed in the 30 days to 24 November, leaving 1,446 cases on hand, including 451 B-29 flyer cases."},{"t":"Apprehension of Suspected War Criminals","lv":3,"p":[25,25],"pp":"","s":"Japan ordered to apprehend 11 persons for delivery to Sugamo; 34 statuses clarified; 39 suspects interned, bringing the total interned to 1,036."},{"t":"Prosecution of War Criminals","lv":3,"p":[25,25],"pp":"","s":"397 suspects tried to 30 November, 380 guilty and 33 sentenced to death; seven trials of 12 accused completed, including life imprisonment for Lt. Col. Mori."},{"t":"International Prosecution of War Criminals","lv":3,"p":[26,26],"pp":"","s":"Court sat 18 days in November; Maj. Gen. Myron C. Cramer became acting president on 8 November while Sir William Webb was in Australia."},{"t":"Defense of Kuniaki Koiso","lv":3,"p":[26,26],"pp":"","s":"Koiso testified on his 1945 Miao Pin peace overture to Chungking and his Cabinet's resignation on 5 April 1945; prosecution introduced Toda's document on Manchukuo advisers."},{"t":"Defense of Iwane Matsui","lv":3,"p":[27,27],"pp":"","s":"Witnesses Ouchi, Wakizaka and Okada described orderly conduct at Nanking; Matsui denied knowing of atrocities and said he lacked authority over army discipline."},{"t":"Defense of Jiro Minami","lv":3,"p":[28,29],"pp":"","s":"Nakamura's affidavit, September 1931 telegrams urging nonexpansion, Kodama on the Korean Army's border crossing, and Shidehara's testimony on the Mukden Incident."},{"t":"Defense of Akira Muto","lv":3,"p":[30,30],"pp":"","s":"Opening statement on Muto's four periods of service; Yamamoto testified on the General Staff's 16 November 1941 war plan that Muto handed to the Foreign Office."},{"t":"Defense of Takasumi Oka","lv":3,"p":[31,31],"pp":"","s":"Oikawa and Nomura testified that Oka, as Naval Affairs Bureau director, only supplied information at conferences; Oka said the Navy sought to avert war."},{"t":"Defense of Hiroshi Oshima","lv":3,"p":[32,33],"pp":"","s":"Wakamatsu, Kasahara and Usami testified on the 1935-39 Japan-Germany treaty talks; Oshima could not recall Ribbentrop's 29 November 1941 pledge to join a war on the United States."},{"t":"Part III: Economic","lv":1,"p":[34,134],"pp":"57","s":"Thirteen sections of production, trade, labor, finance and science data for September and October, with record coal output, new labor laws, price revisions and budget figures."},{"t":"Section 1. Agriculture and Fisheries","lv":2,"p":[35,38],"pp":"59","s":"Cooperative Association laws of 7 November dissolve 11,000 associations; land sales reach 111,714 cho; October fish landings 110,952 tons; antarctic whaling fleets sail 6 November."},{"t":"Section 2. Forestry and Mining","lv":2,"p":[39,43],"pp":"67","s":"October log output 468,248,000 board feet; coal 2,422,000 metric tons, an Occupation high; coal mine employment 486,681; crude oil 17,607 kiloliters; 12 of 30 minerals gained."},{"t":"Section 3. Heavy Industries","lv":2,"p":[44,53],"pp":"77","s":"Coke fell to 165,930 tons, pig iron 30,385 tons, ammonium sulfate reached 61,771 tons, 8,409 coal-miners' houses built, and machine tool output stayed low at 435 units."},{"t":"Coke","lv":3,"p":[44,44],"pp":"","s":"October coke fell to 165,930 metric tons from 176,900; stockpiles dropped to 29,710 tons, with chemical and metal industries receiving the largest allocations."},{"t":"Metal Industries","lv":3,"p":[45,46],"pp":"","s":"Pig iron 30,385 tons, rolled steel down to 47,433 tons from the Kyushu power shortage, aluminum 1,016 tons, lead up with Kanioka mine reopened, ferroalloys 1,121 tons."},{"t":"Construction","lv":3,"p":[47,47],"pp":"","s":"8,409 new coal-miners' houses built since 1 January; October building permits 48,706 for new construction and 53,927 units started."},{"t":"Shipbuilding","lv":3,"p":[48,48],"pp":"","s":"Repairs on 473 vessels of 989,325 gross tons from 10 October to 10 November; four steel cargo ships and 14 steel fishing vessels launched."},{"t":"Chemical Industries","lv":3,"p":[48,50],"pp":"","s":"Basic chemicals index at 44 percent of minimum requirements; calcium superphosphate 74,583 tons, ammonium sulfate 61,771 tons; critical salt shortage with imports down to 25,178 tons."},{"t":"Machinery","lv":3,"p":[51,51],"pp":"","s":"Machine tools 435 units from 53 plants; small tools used 263 tons of steel; industrial machinery output valued at 560,032,000 yen."},{"t":"Railway Rolling Stock","lv":3,"p":[52,52],"pp":"","s":"Locomotive output fell; 73 new passenger cars and 95 freight cars built; passenger car repairs up 52 to 1,063."},{"t":"Textile Machinery","lv":3,"p":[53,53],"pp":"","s":"September textile machinery output weighed 5,203 metric tons and was valued at 387,562,000 yen, with a shift from repairs to parts."},{"t":"Section 4. Manufacturing","lv":2,"p":[54,63],"pp":"97","s":"Flour output 157,107 tons, figured window glass made for the first time since the war, small trucks 192 versus 90, toys 7,103,210 pieces, matches at 94.4 percent of schedule."},{"t":"Food Processing","lv":3,"p":[54,54],"pp":"","s":"Flour production 157,107 metric tons from 131,648 tons of imported wheat; soy sauce 37,583 tons; brewing cut by lack of blending alcohol."},{"t":"Glass and Ceramics","lv":3,"p":[55,56],"pp":"","s":"Window glass up three percent with figured glass at 2,095,300 square feet; refractory bricks 21,546 tons; 17 tons of optical glass melted but none finished."},{"t":"Medical Equipment","lv":3,"p":[57,57],"pp":"","s":"Medical instrument output valued at 27,340,000 yen in October, with tables listing items such as 1,572,517 surgical needles and 750 stethoscopes."},{"t":"Electrical Manufacturing","lv":3,"p":[58,58],"pp":"","s":"Electrical equipment edged up despite shortages; tables list 3,595,938 flashlight lamps and 31,676 watt-hour meters for October."},{"t":"Transportation Equipment","lv":3,"p":[59,59],"pp":"","s":"Standard trucks fell by 133 from 909, small trucks reached 192, tractors 17, light carts 29,527; bicycles dropped 4,932 from 19,143."},{"t":"Agricultural Equipment","lv":3,"p":[60,61],"pp":"","s":"292 producers with 26,771 employees reported agricultural implement output; sickles rose to 234,326 and plows to 18,476."},{"t":"Handicrafts","lv":3,"p":[62,62],"pp":"","s":"Lacquerware, silverware and antimony ware rose; antimony ware reached 46,500 pieces after the September floods; 130 ivory factories made 3,820 pieces."},{"t":"Miscellaneous Manufacturing","lv":3,"p":[62,63],"pp":"","s":"Wrist watches 31,980, toys 7,103,210 pieces, matches 18,035 match tons, and phonograph records down 174,011 from the postwar high of 869,356."},{"t":"Section 5. Textile Industries","lv":2,"p":[64,76],"pp":"117","s":"First postwar Indian cotton (712,000 pounds) arrived 31 October with 30,406,000 pounds of CCC cotton; raw silk up 808 bales; export priority for power lifted rayon and woolen yarn."},{"t":"Cotton","lv":3,"p":[64,65],"pp":"","s":"Raw cotton stocks reached 52,820,000 pounds; cotton yarn fell 875,000 pounds from power shortages, while cloth output rose 2,045,000 square yards for export."},{"t":"Silk","lv":3,"p":[66,68],"pp":"","s":"Raw silk production 10,049 bales, 808 more than September; 277 mills operating; new-crop cocoon receipts 18,407,000 pounds; spun silk yarn mostly down."},{"t":"Rayon","lv":3,"p":[69,69],"pp":"","s":"Rayon filament yarn up 301,000 pounds over September; staple trend broken by export restrictions; cloth output down 1,295,000 square yards for lack of yarn funds."},{"t":"Wool","lv":3,"p":[70,71],"pp":"","s":"Wool waste stocks rose 379,000 pounds while other raw wool fell; woolen cloth rose 20,000 square yards; worsted spindles operating fell to 58,286."},{"t":"Hard and Bast Fibers","lv":3,"p":[72,72],"pp":"","s":"Flax, hemp and jute supplies continued to decline; rope output fell to 1,908,000 pounds from 2,245,000."},{"t":"Throstle-spun and Reprocessed Materials","lv":3,"p":[73,73],"pp":"","s":"Throstle yarn output fell to 431,000 pounds from 458,000; reprocessed yarn was 29,000 pounds; cloth output cut by power and labor shortages."},{"t":"Knit Goods","lv":3,"p":[74,74],"pp":"","s":"Cotton knit fabric production rose to 1,042,000 pounds for export; yarn receipts fell except export allocations."},{"t":"Clothing and Household Goods","lv":3,"p":[75,75],"pp":"","s":"Clothing output fell for lack of fabric and power; cotton cloth stocks in sewing plants dropped from 7,926,000 to 6,941,000 square yards."},{"t":"Sundry Goods","lv":3,"p":[76,76],"pp":"","s":"Sewing thread output rose sharply after new price ceilings; fish netting, rope and cordage fell on power and yarn shortages."},{"t":"Dyeing and Finishing","lv":3,"p":[76,76],"pp":"","s":"Cotton cloth processed rose to 24,961,000 square yards on export orders; gray fabric stocks fell 1,232,000 square yards; three new mills added."},{"t":"Section 6. Transportation and Public Utilities","lv":2,"p":[77,82],"pp":"143","s":"Buses and trucks registered reached 101,037; rail freight rose 835,200 tons; electric power generated 2,524,000,000 kWh; daily gas production 1,436,903 cubic meters."},{"t":"Section 7. Communications","lv":2,"p":[83,89],"pp":"155","s":"Nearly 19,000 telephones installed; Great Northern Telegraph licensed 3 November to reopen Nagasaki; domestic mail rose 21 percent to 212,093,843 pieces; Ministry of Communications ran a September deficit."},{"t":"Section 8. Labor","lv":2,"p":[90,100],"pp":"169","s":"Unemployment laws enacted 21 November, Central Labor Relations Committee ruling of 14 November, Hitachi's 32,000 strikers returned 13 November, and 25,060 unions with 5,840,251 members."},{"t":"Labor Legislation and Policy","lv":3,"p":[90,92],"pp":"","s":"Unemployment Allowance and Insurance Laws, Employment Security Law effective 1 December, Labor Standards Law provisions in force 1 November, and a 4,000,000 yen grant for labor-management councils."},{"t":"Labor and Employer Organizations","lv":3,"p":[93,94],"pp":"","s":"People's Economic Council of 66 members formed; rally of about 100,000 workers on 12 November; National Congress of Industrial Unions convention 17-20 November; Anti-Communist League formed 7 November."},{"t":"Labor Relations","lv":3,"p":[95,96],"pp":"","s":"Government workers' disputes settled by mediation awards; Hitachi, Japan Electric and Japan Radio strikes ended; coal miners demanded 6,270 yen average pay with a 7 December strike deadline."},{"t":"Labor Education","lv":3,"p":[97,98],"pp":"","s":"Eleven resident labor schools; five percent of members attended a lecture April-September; Ministry of Labor received about 4,000 books from the U.S. Department of Labor on 29 November."},{"t":"Wages and Working Conditions","lv":3,"p":[99,99],"pp":"","s":"The Committee did not uphold the 1,800-yen standard wage; rayon firms agreed to 2,700 yen monthly on 1 November; agreements averaged 2,500 to 3,500 yen."},{"t":"Employment and Unemployment","lv":3,"p":[100,100],"pp":"","s":"Seaman retraining funds suspended; textile employment fell 2,109 to 536,391; coal mine employment reached 438,291 at end of October, above the 1945 wartime peak."},{"t":"Public Works","lv":3,"p":[100,100],"pp":"","s":"Third-quarter public works certifications reached 980,597,681 yen; the Economic Stabilization Board inspection section made seven inspections."},{"t":"Administration","lv":3,"p":[100,100],"pp":"","s":"A training institute for 100 prefectural and local employment security officials ran in Tokyo from 6 November to 5 December, the first of its type in Japan."},{"t":"Section 9. Imports and Exports","lv":2,"p":[101,112],"pp":"191","s":"14 import contracts worth $4,104,685 authorized in October; first postwar beans from China (2,690 tons), cotton fabrics exported to the U.S., tractors and shipyard cranes imported."},{"t":"Section 10. Rationing and Price Control","lv":2,"p":[113,118],"pp":"215","s":"Price controls ended on about 200 minor commodities 27 October; staple prices raised 1 November (polished rice 99.00 to 148.50 yen per 10 kg); 132,826 violations reported in October."},{"t":"Section 11. Finance","lv":2,"p":[119,126],"pp":"227","s":"Bank of Japan notes in circulation about 178 billion yen; Supplementary Budget No. 7 of 85,602,368,000 yen; national debt 321,424,000,000 yen; 22 control organizations closed in October."},{"t":"Money and Banking","lv":3,"p":[119,122],"pp":"","s":"Three foreign insurance groups licensed; Bank of Japan note issue 85.2 percent of liabilities; Securities Democratization Committee formed 4 November; 20 life insurers reorganized."},{"t":"Liquidation","lv":3,"p":[123,123],"pp":"","s":"22 control organizations closed in October; cumulative liquidation proceeds to 30 September were 11,937,577,000 yen."},{"t":"Public Finance","lv":3,"p":[124,126],"pp":"","s":"Three tax bills, Supplementary Budget No. 7 passed 29 November, government borrowing of 106,257,000,000 yen authorized, and settlers' housing loan ceiling raised to 25,000 yen."},{"t":"Section 12. Property Control and Reparations","lv":2,"p":[127,130],"pp":"243","s":"378 property reports tabulated; 337 looted ships reported; SCAP dollar custody account 382,399.16; Industrial Recovery Corporation limited 24 November; seven of 17 arsenals inspected by reparations delegations."},{"t":"Property Control","lv":3,"p":[127,128],"pp":"","s":"Neutral and Allied property reports totaled 7,562 owners; 1,450 ammonia cylinders restituted to the United Kingdom; property of 914 arrested persons under custody."},{"t":"Antitrust and Cartels","lv":3,"p":[129,129],"pp":"","s":"No objection to 466 restricted-concern applications totaling 327,683,925 yen; 30 control organizations ordered closed; Fair Trade Commission hearing notices of 14 November."},{"t":"Reparations","lv":3,"p":[130,130],"pp":"","s":"China, Netherlands, Philippines and U.K. delegations accepted 5,289 of 9,745 machines in seven arsenals; 750 items packaged by 30 November."},{"t":"Section 13. Science and Technology","lv":2,"p":[131,134],"pp":"251","s":"Renewal Committee for the Organization of Science planned a charter for the Diet by March 1948; research grants of 27,853,520 yen; 838 postwar standards; 9 May 1948 eclipse expedition to Rebun Island."},{"t":"Part IV: Social","lv":1,"p":[135,155],"pp":"259","s":"Typhus immunization, Child Welfare Law, 46,589 repatriates, the university chartering committee, and press comment on the budget and coal bill."},{"t":"Section 1. Public Health and Welfare","lv":2,"p":[136,144],"pp":"261","s":"Hokkaido typhus booster for 1,256,312 residents, public health train from 1 November, penicillin output 1,605,450,000 units, Child Welfare Law of 21 November, and 46,589 repatriates."},{"t":"Preventive Medicine","lv":3,"p":[136,138],"pp":"","s":"Typhus immunization on Hokkaido, a public health train dedicated 1 November, a sanitation committee, and disease charts showing rates fell except diphtheria."},{"t":"Veterinary Affairs","lv":3,"p":[139,139],"pp":"","s":"662 suspected equine encephalitis cases in 28 prefectures; animal diseases other than brucellosis rose; 21,467 cattle slaughtered in September."},{"t":"Medical and Hospital Administration","lv":3,"p":[139,139],"pp":"","s":"3,389 hospitals with 212,100 beds, 97,991 occupied on 31 October; a genetics director for the Atomic Bomb Casualty Commission study was yet to be chosen."},{"t":"Supply","lv":3,"p":[140,141],"pp":"","s":"Medical supplies valued at 732,353,544 yen; penicillin up 1,085,790,000 units to 1,605,450,000; typhus vaccine sufficient; laboratory animal breeding set back by the Saitama flood."},{"t":"Narcotics","lv":3,"p":[142,142],"pp":"","s":"87,508 persons registered; 114 narcotics arrests in September; law effective 27 September gave 95 agents power of arrest."},{"t":"Public Assistance","lv":3,"p":[142,142],"pp":"","s":"Welfare payments of 358,731,288 yen to 2,947,295 people in October; grants raised 10 percent 1 November; a 686,800,000 yen fund drive began 25 November."},{"t":"Social Security","lv":3,"p":[143,143],"pp":"","s":"Unemployment Allowance and Insurance Laws passed 21 November, covering over 7,000,000 workers in firms of five or more."},{"t":"Repatriation","lv":3,"p":[143,143],"pp":"","s":"46,589 Japanese repatriated 31 October to 27 November, 43,424 from Soviet areas; 1,085 repatriates left Japan for Formosa, China and the Ryukyus."},{"t":"Vital Statistics","lv":3,"p":[144,144],"pp":"","s":"September births 235,896, deaths 80,036, marriages 72,080 and divorces 7,325; the death rate fell 14 percent from August."},{"t":"Section 2. Education, Religion and Media of Expression","lv":2,"p":[145,155],"pp":"279","s":"University Chartering Committee approved 18 November, 135,756,768 textbook copies authorized, Shosoin exhibition with 70,000 visitors, first Book Week, and press comment on the budget and coal bill."},{"t":"Education","lv":3,"p":[145,147],"pp":"","s":"School conferences, University Chartering Committee, scholarship loans doubled 17 November, vocational conferences, and 114,337,886 textbooks distributed by 15 November."},{"t":"Religion","lv":3,"p":[148,148],"pp":"","s":"Christian Rural Service and Training Center organized under a 25-year plan; 14 November meeting at the Prime Minister's residence; 30,000 Japanese hymnals shipped."},{"t":"Arts and Monuments","lv":3,"p":[149,149],"pp":"","s":"Toro excavation exhibit opened 20 November at the National Museum; third Shosoin exhibition drew 70,000; Nitten attracted 121,105 visitors."},{"t":"Media of Expression","lv":3,"p":[149,155],"pp":"","s":"Public opinion conference 24-25 November, first Book Week from 17 November, press comment on Hirano and the budget, 90 Japanese feature films since January, and SCAP library attendance of 19,851."}],"by":"claude-sonnet (Claude Code subagent)"},{"id":"K-26","ia":"south-korean-interim-government-activities-no-26-1947-11","s":"K","no":26,"d":"1947-11","d2":"","t":"South Korean Interim Government Activities, No. 26 (November 1947)","v":"","pg":495,"o":"South Korean Interim Government Activities No. 26 covers November 1947, prepared by the National Economic Board for USAMGIK. The month opened with the inauguration of Maj. Gen. William F. Dean as Military Governor (3 November) after Lerch's death, and was dominated politically by the controversy over the Korean directors' 'Outline of Measures for Meeting the Current Situation', the UN-supervised election question, disputes between the executive and the Interim Legislative Assembly, and the Council of 12 Parties. Economic sections report a worsening coal and electric power crisis (58 trains cancelled, power cut from the North), half-complete rice collection, rising prices (index 1,476 in October), a 9.2 billion won note-issue increase, refugee inflows and housing, and detailed production, trade, transport, communications and finance tables. Social sections cover public health, the New Education Exhibit, culture and radio, public information, and Korean National Youth. A historian will find the primary-source text of the Outline, KILA debates, Ordinance 153/156 and the Beverage Tax ordinance, plus Radio Pyongyang monitoring.","h":["Maj. Gen. William F. Dean was inaugurated Military Governor on 3 November, succeeding the late Maj. Gen. Archer L. Lerch, and restated three aims: independent Korea, democratic government, and a sound economy and education system.","The 25 September 'Outline of Measures for Meeting the Current Situation', signed by Ahn Chai Hong and Chough Pyung Ok, drew leftist and rightist protest; KILA spent six sessions on it and rejected withdrawal 32 to 20.","Hodge (19 November) and Dean (27 November) refused a unilateral South Korean election before the UN Commission arrived, while Rhee pressed for one by mid-December and the Council of 12 Parties lost members.","Coal and power shortages removed 58 trains from service; load reached 162,000 kw on 10 November against 105,000 available, 135,000 kw of it from north of the 38th parallel, and the Chung Pyung, Unam and Sunjin Gang plants stopped for lack of water.","The Bank of Chosun note issue rose 9.2 billion won in November, the largest monthly rise since August 1946, driven by rice-collection loans and deficit spending; the 1947/48 budget stood over 45 per cent above 1946/47.","Rice collection was only half complete on 30 November, with Cholla-pukto furthest behind; 9,476,000 people were rationed 2.5 hop (1,316 calories) daily, and the 26-commodity price index reached 1,476 (August 1945 = 100).","Refugee arrivals from north of the 38th parallel numbered 7,715 in November and 155,454 for 1947; total inbound movement since October 1945 was 2,350,133.","KILA passed the Temporary Law for Cessation of Professional Entertainment Activities, Dean signed the Public Prostitution Law (Public Act No. 7), and Helmick withheld approval of the Temporary Constitution and collaborators' bill; Education Director Yu Uck Kyum died 8 November."],"k":["USAMGIK","South Korean Interim Government","Military Governor Dean","Korean Interim Legislative Assembly","United Nations Commission election","Outline of Measures","Council of 12 Parties","coal and electric power shortage","rice collection and rationing","refugees from North Korea","vested property","Bank of Chosun note issue","prices and inflation","New Education Exhibit","Korean National Youth"],"toc":[{"t":"Front matter","lv":1,"p":[1,15],"pp":"","s":"Google Books notice, cover of No. 26 (November 1947, National Economic Board, call number M 105.13:26) and the six-page table of contents for Parts I-VI."},{"t":"Part I. Basic Aspects","lv":1,"p":[16,31],"pp":"1","s":"Population movements (2,350,133 inbound since 1945; 7,715 refugees in November), foreign affairs with China, Japan and the U.S., and vested property operations, loans, sales and Ordinance 156."},{"t":"Section 1. Population and Foreign Affairs","lv":2,"p":[16,25],"pp":"1","s":"Repatriation and refugee totals, the 1945-47 migration pattern from the North, and Office of Foreign Affairs work on Chinese, Japanese and U.S.-European matters."},{"t":"General","lv":3,"p":[16,16],"pp":"1","s":"Repatriate and refugee inflow keeps creating problems for the South Korean Interim Government; the Office of Foreign Affairs keeps inflow records and attends to the individual problems of Koreans abroad."},{"t":"Repatriation","lv":3,"p":[16,16],"pp":"1","s":"Since 15 October 1945: 885,785 outbound and 2,350,133 inbound, including 1,110,544 Koreans from Japan; SS Kaiwo Maru returned 509 illegal entrants on 7 November."},{"t":"Refugees","lv":3,"p":[17,20],"pp":"2","s":"7,715 refugees in November and 155,454 in 1947, with origins by province; contrasts the 1945 Manchurian flood, 1946 seasonal migration and 1947 movement attributed to Sovietization."},{"t":"Chinese Affairs","lv":3,"p":[21,21],"pp":"6","s":"155 travel permits for Chinese nationals, SS Kim Kiu Jhan sailing for Tientsin and Shanghai, Manchurian refugee relief, and 11,126 registered Chinese in Korea."},{"t":"Japanese Affairs","lv":3,"p":[22,23],"pp":"7","s":"Residence permits for Japanese wives of Koreans suspended; 39 Japanese retained in essential posts; re-entry clearances; recovery of Korean property and vessels from Japan."},{"t":"U.S. - European Affairs","lv":3,"p":[24,25],"pp":"9","s":"Six Korean students left for the United States in November; papers for 39 others were in process."},{"t":"Section 2. Vested Property","lv":2,"p":[26,31],"pp":"11","s":"Property Custody handled 184 cases, reopened the Taichong gold mine, guaranteed loans, had 2,345 enterprises assigned to agents, and received Ordinance 156 on stockholders' meetings."},{"t":"General","lv":3,"p":[26,26],"pp":"11","s":"This month's vested property report gives only limited information on the Office of Property Custody; fuller accounting awaits completion of current operations, with more to come in future issues."},{"t":"Operations","lv":3,"p":[26,26],"pp":"11","s":"Operations Division handled 184 cases; Taichong Gold Mine reopening approved (4,200 grams estimated early 1948); timber land survey begun; table of November guaranteed loans."},{"t":"Property Disposal","lv":3,"p":[27,27],"pp":"12","s":"Vested property sales and appraisals in November; Materials Control Corporation absorbed under Ordinance 155; provincial buyer-selection committees nearly complete."},{"t":"Assignment of Responsibility","lv":3,"p":[28,28],"pp":"13","s":"2,345 industrial and financial enterprises assigned to government department directors acting as agents of the Property Custodian."},{"t":"Ordinance No. 156","lv":3,"p":[29,29],"pp":"14","s":"Ordinance 156, published 21 November, lets the Property Custodian call stockholders' meetings on 30 days' notice."},{"t":"Current Activities","lv":3,"p":[30,30],"pp":"15","s":"Rice-polishing mills leased by the Department of Commerce, Li Household income petition and audit, and a changed eviction policy for unauthorized occupants."},{"t":"Administrative Problems and Decisions","lv":3,"p":[30,30],"pp":"15","s":"Real estate handling by departments and banks, and a decision that shares in vested property are vested legally rather than administratively."},{"t":"General Counsel Division","lv":3,"p":[31,31],"pp":"16","s":"Suits by Korean nationals against corporations holding vested Japanese shares, with jurisdictional defenses."},{"t":"Provincial Affairs Division","lv":3,"p":[31,31],"pp":"16","s":"Rental control procedure to operate in all provinces by 31 January 1948; banks asked to report leasing breakdowns."},{"t":"Repair Funds Advanced","lv":3,"p":[31,31],"pp":"16","s":"Table of Property Custody funds advanced for repairs, June to November 1947, with January-November totals."},{"t":"Part II. Production and Supply","lv":1,"p":[32,105],"pp":"17","s":"Food supply and rationing, price indexes, agriculture, coal and metals, industry, allocation, trade and labor in November 1947, with coal and grain shortages the main themes."},{"t":"Section 1. Food Supply","lv":2,"p":[32,37],"pp":"17","s":"Summer grains nearly exhausted; 9,476,000 people rationed at 2.5 hop daily; rice collection half done, with Cholla-pukto lagging for lack of inspectors, trucks and bags."},{"t":"General","lv":3,"p":[32,32],"pp":"17","s":"Four-point summary of the end-November food position: summer grains gone, small producers' rice stocks falling, imported grain supply uncertain."},{"t":"The Situation","lv":3,"p":[32,32],"pp":"17","s":"Outlook pessimistic but no immediate starvation; non-rice grains and pulses near exhaustion; grain status table for 30 September-30 November."},{"t":"Imports","lv":3,"p":[33,33],"pp":"18","s":"November grain imports near the monthly average, a little over 40 per cent of the tonnage distributed."},{"t":"Rationing","lv":3,"p":[34,34],"pp":"19","s":"Rationed population unchanged at 9,476,000 of over 20 million; ration of 2.5 hop (1,316.2 calories) daily."},{"t":"The Rice Program","lv":3,"p":[35,36],"pp":"20","s":"Collection one-half complete at 30 November, Cholla-pukto furthest behind; provincial quota table and revised 1947-48 shipping schedule for a national reserve."},{"t":"Problems of Collection","lv":3,"p":[37,37],"pp":"22","s":"National Food Administration on Cholla-pukto: too few inspectors, tire and battery shortages, straw bags; Cholla-namdo at 64 per cent on 28 November."},{"t":"Section 2. Prices","lv":2,"p":[38,46],"pp":"23","s":"New retail price indexes for 26 commodities: composite 1,476 in October 1947 (August 1945 = 100); controlled prices, gold price and National Price Administration actions."},{"t":"Price Indexes","lv":3,"p":[38,38],"pp":"23","s":"Composite index of 1,476 in October, over twice the year-earlier level; polished rice only 6 times its August 1945 price."},{"t":"Actual Prices","lv":3,"p":[38,41],"pp":"23","s":"Index and average price tables for Seoul and ten-day open-market prices in nine cities, September-November 1947."},{"t":"Controlled Prices","lv":3,"p":[42,42],"pp":"27","s":"Controlled rice price stable since November 1945, explaining its smaller open-market rise; tables on controlled commodity and tobacco prices."},{"t":"Gold","lv":3,"p":[43,45],"pp":"28","s":"Wholesale gold price rising steadily since April despite illegal trading, used as a price-trend indicator; tobacco price increase tables."},{"t":"Activities of the National Price Administration","lv":3,"p":[46,46],"pp":"31","s":"Price settings from weekly reports: rolled barley 110 won per small mal, imported ginned cotton 50 won per pound, 60 textile types."},{"t":"Section 3. Agriculture, Forestry and Fisheries","lv":2,"p":[47,56],"pp":"32","s":"Livestock markets, seed potatoes, fertilizer receipts of 97,973 tons of ammonium nitrate, cotton and silk, irrigation, forestry, and the January-July 1947 fish catch."},{"t":"Bureau of Agricultural Economics","lv":3,"p":[47,47],"pp":"32","s":"1947 production statistics compiled, draft 1948 production plan emphasizing cereals, and extra grain inspectors hired for rice collection."},{"t":"Livestock","lv":3,"p":[47,48],"pp":"32","s":"1946 market data (624,595 cattle brought, 346,810 sold), 1,280 work cattle at breeding stations, Seoul Milk Plant taking 250 gallons daily, race-track bets of 183 million won."},{"t":"Production","lv":3,"p":[49,49],"pp":"34","s":"Poultry conference at Suwon 27-28 November, swine increases in Kyonggi-do, poor honey output, and 408 tons of American seed potatoes arriving at Pusan."},{"t":"Agricultural Experiment Station, Suwon","lv":3,"p":[50,50],"pp":"35","s":"Fruit tree nursery shortage, apple pest damage in Taegu orchards, and fertilizer yield experiments on rice, barley, wheat, soybeans and potatoes."},{"t":"Cotton and Fiber","lv":3,"p":[51,51],"pp":"36","s":"23,000 chungbo of cotton lost to floods, 10,900 chungbo hemp quota for 1948, and straw bag movements for fertilizer shipments."},{"t":"Sericulture","lv":3,"p":[52,52],"pp":"37","s":"October raw silk output of 6,030 kvan, 52,000 kvan of waste silk in stock, and a 1947 cocoon collection of 600,000 kvan."},{"t":"Irrigation","lv":3,"p":[52,52],"pp":"37","s":"Ground-breaking at the Shi Heung tidal reclamation project on 25 November, a technician training school for 60 men, and completion of the Sam Sung project."},{"t":"Korean Agricultural Association","lv":3,"p":[53,54],"pp":"38","s":"Fertilizer receipts and sales to 1 November, 20,000 tons moved by small boat, and sales receipts of 538 million won."},{"t":"Forestry","lv":3,"p":[55,55],"pp":"40","s":"Trial arming of 20 forest policemen with U.S. carbines near Seoul and final management rules for vested forest lands."},{"t":"Fisheries","lv":3,"p":[55,56],"pp":"40","s":"78 million won loan to the Korean Fisheries Corporation for agar-agar producers, Seoul market arrivals, and a January-July 1947 catch table."},{"t":"Section 4. Mining and Minerals","lv":2,"p":[57,67],"pp":"42","s":"Coal stockpiles falling despite higher output, bituminous coal wholly imported from Japan, a Sangdong mill fire, an explosion killing four at Chang Hang, and metals production tables."},{"t":"General","lv":3,"p":[57,57],"pp":"42","s":"Coal situation more acute: November mining rose but mine stockpiles declined, signaling approaching exhaustion."},{"t":"Coal","lv":3,"p":[58,62],"pp":"43","s":"Anthracite and lignite output up 75 and 100 per cent over August, truck tire shortages, Hwasun at 400 tons daily, 55,600 tons of anthracite shipped against 115,000 required."},{"t":"Pitch","lv":3,"p":[63,63],"pp":"48","s":"Briquette production falling for lack of coal-tar pitch; asphaltic pitch being tested; no control over briquette factories at end-November."},{"t":"Metals","lv":3,"p":[63,67],"pp":"48","s":"2 November fire at Sangdong mill, copper cost surveys, explosion killing four at Chang Hang smelter, and May-October tables for gold, silver, copper, tungsten, graphite, zinc and lead."},{"t":"Section 5. Industrial Production","lv":2,"p":[68,77],"pp":"53","s":"October flour output of 3,208 tons from imported wheat, cotton and paper output, nails near 200 tons, cement, rubber, ginseng purchase of 141,336 cha, and tobacco up 14 per cent."},{"t":"General","lv":3,"p":[68,68],"pp":"53","s":"Industrial production data remain extremely inadequate, covering mainly major vested and Korean-owned factories receiving allocated raw materials; many vested plants send no reports, and numerous small Korean-owned plants add materially to local markets."},{"t":"Processed Food","lv":3,"p":[68,68],"pp":"53","s":"Flour output in two vested mills rose from 430 to 3,208 tons using imported wheat; noodle output fell 38 per cent; Tong-A Food Company output."},{"t":"Textiles","lv":3,"p":[69,70],"pp":"54","s":"Cotton yarn and cloth rose after 20,000 bales of cotton were imported, though still below October 1946; 4,097 meters of mixed cloth."},{"t":"Paper","lv":3,"p":[71,71],"pp":"56","s":"Machine-made paper fell about 55 tons from September's high because of coal scarcity; Puksan mill shut for repairs."},{"t":"Hardware","lv":3,"p":[72,72],"pp":"57","s":"Nail production approached 200 metric tons for the first time in the 12-month series."},{"t":"Other Industry","lv":3,"p":[72,74],"pp":"57","s":"Briquettes unchanged, coke declining; Onada Cement made 1,414 tons; brick and pottery up after refractory clay imports; rubber items declined."},{"t":"Monopoly Bureau Products","lv":3,"p":[75,77],"pp":"60","s":"141,336 cha of ginseng bought for 77 million won, salt output limited by winter, tobacco up 14 per cent (600 per cent over 1945), Chonju factories consolidated."},{"t":"Section 6. Allocation and Distribution","lv":2,"p":[78,90],"pp":"63","s":"Allocation tables for cloth, shoes, soap, tires and beer; Civilian Supply allocations by agency; Materials Control Corporation stocks; FFA and FDA purchases; petroleum and salt."},{"t":"Allocations, Korean Produced Commodities","lv":3,"p":[78,82],"pp":"63","s":"Cotton cloth allocations dropped almost 40 per cent while finished goods rose; shoe, soap and match allocations rose; tables July 1946-November 1947."},{"t":"Allocations of Civilian Supplies","lv":3,"p":[83,85],"pp":"68","s":"Allocations of 44 fire trucks, 1,863 tons of crude rubber, 6,000 radios, steel landing strips, tents and textile dyes to named departments."},{"t":"Allocations, Materials Control Corporation","lv":3,"p":[86,86],"pp":"71","s":"Allocation of former Japanese Army stocks including 1,000 pounds of Chinese nickel coins and 2 tons of bismuth; inspection of MCC stockpiles near Seoul."},{"t":"Distribution, Korean Produced Commodities","lv":3,"p":[87,88],"pp":"72","s":"FFA spent 119.6 million won on commodities in November, up 69 per cent; FDA spent 13.8 million won, down 77 per cent."},{"t":"Petroleum","lv":3,"p":[89,89],"pp":"74","s":"85,405 empty drums returned against 75,519 delivered, and a doubled November kerosene allocation for seasonal demand."},{"t":"Salt","lv":3,"p":[90,90],"pp":"75","s":"Kimchi-season salt distribution continued, with the full 2.7 kg ration reached in Chunchon, Yongwol and Kangnung by mid-November."},{"t":"Section 7. Imports and Exports","lv":2,"p":[91,100],"pp":"76","s":"Department Order No. 2 on won proceeds of imports, record October private imports of 542 million won, trade with Japan, Hong Kong and Egypt, and Civilian Supply imports of coal, fertilizer and food."},{"t":"Private Trade","lv":3,"p":[91,93],"pp":"76","s":"Order No. 2 requires import won proceeds deposited at the Korea Foreign Exchange Bank; November estimate imports 303.7 million won against exports 221.5 million; October imports a record 542 million."},{"t":"Trade with United States","lv":3,"p":[94,94],"pp":"79","s":"Three American trading company representatives visited Korea; payments for July shipments redirected to the National City Bank of New York."},{"t":"Trade with Japan","lv":3,"p":[94,95],"pp":"79","s":"SCAP declined some goods and 1,000 tons of anchovies; 100 tons of lead ingots accepted at $286.60 per ton; shipments September-November."},{"t":"Trade with Hong Kong","lv":3,"p":[96,96],"pp":"81","s":"SS Cannonbar sailed 26 November with fluorspar, silk waste and canned fish; 400 bales of Indian cotton bought at $0.2475 per pound."},{"t":"Trade with Egypt","lv":3,"p":[96,96],"pp":"81","s":"1,000 bales of raw cotton shipped on the Leopold Skirk toward 3,000 bales bartered in August."},{"t":"Korean Items for Americans in Korea","lv":3,"p":[96,96],"pp":"81","s":"Souvenir sales through the Army Post Exchange brought $16,548.25 in November, credited to the Department of Commerce."},{"t":"Imports, Civilian Supply","lv":3,"p":[97,100],"pp":"82","s":"GARIOA and FLC import values and tonnage; 12,391.9 tons of salt in October, 62,517 tons of Japanese coal in November, a 69,663-ton ammonium nitrate request, and foodstuffs."},{"t":"Section 8. Labor","lv":2,"p":[101,105],"pp":"86","s":"Wage study of 14 controlled factories, Pusan stevedores after the Chung Pyung arrests, Seoul Electric union-management council, Yong Wol mine wage dispute, and child labor and maximum-hours enforcement."},{"t":"General","lv":3,"p":[101,101],"pp":"86","s":"In November the Department of Labor continued work in labor education, industrial health and union-management relations; the page also lists the section's contents: wages, union activities, education, health."},{"t":"Wages","lv":3,"p":[101,101],"pp":"86","s":"Wage study of 14 controlled Seoul and Yong Dong Po factories; Pusan stevedores shifted from the leftist Chung Pyung union after August arrests."},{"t":"Labor Union Activities","lv":3,"p":[102,102],"pp":"87","s":"First Seoul Electric Company Union-Management Council met 18 November under the August contract with the Tai Han No Chung Union."},{"t":"Labor Education","lv":3,"p":[103,103],"pp":"88","s":"Yong Wol mine wage dispute, 5,000-copy Labor News, Tai Han Labor College lectures, and 157 maximum-hours complaints of which only 2 were prosecuted."},{"t":"Industrial Health","lv":3,"p":[104,105],"pp":"89","s":"Factory immunization, inspection of 8 plants with over 1,500 workers, and a Seoul Electric hospital building opened 10 November for Constabulary and Coast Guard."},{"t":"Part III. Public Services","lv":1,"p":[106,145],"pp":"91","s":"Transportation hit by coal shortage (58 trains removed), communications revenue after 1 October rate rises, the power deficit, public works and refugee housing."},{"t":"Section 1. Transportation","lv":2,"p":[106,117],"pp":"91","s":"October revenue highest since April 1946, record rail freight and coal burn of 62,520 tons, 46 marine vessels, new Yosu port authority, and 58 trains cancelled in November."},{"t":"General","lv":3,"p":[106,106],"pp":"91","s":"58 trains removed from service in November for lack of coal; effects to show in next month's statistics."},{"t":"Revenue and Expenditures","lv":3,"p":[106,106],"pp":"91","s":"October revenue up 19 per cent over September; expenditures down 11 per cent because September included a three-month Japanese coal bill."},{"t":"Railroads","lv":3,"p":[107,113],"pp":"92","s":"Income, record passenger and freight volumes (heavy rice-buying travel), locomotive availability, repairs of 236 freight cars, employment, wages and coal consumption."},{"t":"Marine Transportation","lv":3,"p":[114,115],"pp":"99","s":"Bureau controls 46 vessels (8 Baltic Coasters, 34 LSTs, 5 FS vessels); income rising since April; Korean seamen employment expanding."},{"t":"Highway Transportation","lv":3,"p":[116,116],"pp":"101","s":"Bureau has no income and is subsidized; Yosu port authority created 15 November; Mukko port working well; Kunsan barge shortage."},{"t":"Air Transportation","lv":3,"p":[117,117],"pp":"102","s":"Bureau still in planning stage, with watchmen at seven airfields, no planes, and a small, declining staff."},{"t":"Section 2. Communications","lv":2,"p":[118,128],"pp":"103","s":"Income flat in October with telephone rates raised 2.5 to 4 times on 1 October, rising expenditures, postal savings and insurance, telephone repairs, and postal orders for China and Japan."},{"t":"General","lv":3,"p":[118,118],"pp":"103","s":"Department of Communications runs postal, telephone, telegraph and radio services, postal insurance and certain banking functions."},{"t":"Revenue and Expenditures","lv":3,"p":[118,120],"pp":"103","s":"Revenue steady; 1 October telephone, stamp and telegraph rate increases; expenditures up for salaries, travel (per diem 175 to 300 won) and land costs; 2,000 poles set."},{"t":"Employment","lv":3,"p":[121,122],"pp":"106","s":"Permanent employment continued to decline in October; table of government income turned over to the Department of Finance."},{"t":"Special Functions","lv":3,"p":[123,123],"pp":"108","s":"Government income collected for Finance fell 11 per cent from September to October."},{"t":"Postal Deposits and Insurance","lv":3,"p":[123,124],"pp":"108","s":"Postal savings deposits rose slightly, book transfer deposits rose some 30 million won, and policyholders fell though insurance in force rose 3 per cent."},{"t":"Telephone and Telegraph","lv":3,"p":[125,125],"pp":"110","s":"Telephone receipts up 174 per cent in October on new rates; telegrams up 17 and 14 per cent."},{"t":"Activities","lv":3,"p":[125,128],"pp":"110","s":"Repeater station repairs, Yong San exchange inspection of 14 November, Fiscal Bureau voucher system, and Orders 15 and 23 on postal service with China and Japan."},{"t":"Section 3. Public Utilities","lv":2,"p":[129,134],"pp":"114","s":"Power crisis: load of 162,000 kw on 10 November against 105,000 available, 63.2 million KWH from the North, hydro plants idled by low water, plus electric company finance tables."},{"t":"Electricity","lv":3,"p":[129,130],"pp":"114","s":"Seoul and Pusan thermal plants lacked 13,000 tons of coal monthly, Chung Pyung nearly stopped, and the North supplied 135,000 kw at peak and 63.2 million KWH for the month."},{"t":"Streetcars","lv":3,"p":[131,131],"pp":"116","s":"129 to 135 Seoul streetcars in use carrying 450,000 to 500,000 passengers daily."},{"t":"Activities","lv":3,"p":[131,131],"pp":"116","s":"Seoul Electric hospital opened, Pusan steam plant fired, 700 tons of Japanese coal arranged, and a power-rationing plan of 19 November."},{"t":"Revenue and Expenditures","lv":3,"p":[132,134],"pp":"117","s":"Revenue and expense tables for Korea Electric Power, South Korea Electric and Seoul Electric companies."},{"t":"Section 4. Public Works","lv":2,"p":[135,139],"pp":"120","s":"Man-days and wage spending down about 25 per cent for winter, Andong and Kyopchong bridge contracts, Seoul-Pusan highway paving, water works needs, and Fire Prevention Week 3-8 November."},{"t":"Man-Days and Expenditures","lv":3,"p":[135,135],"pp":"120","s":"Man-days and wages dropped about 25 per cent from October as winter approached."},{"t":"Roads and Bridges","lv":3,"p":[136,136],"pp":"121","s":"Contracts let for the 18-span Andong Bridge and others (Kotang 3.4 million won); asphalt paving half done between Yong Dong Po and Amyang."},{"t":"Flood Control and Water Supply","lv":3,"p":[136,136],"pp":"121","s":"Kun Nam Je levee work from 20 November, five river projects done in Kyongsang-pukto, and Han River-Ascom City water project jobs completed."},{"t":"Urban Water Supply","lv":3,"p":[137,137],"pp":"122","s":"Inspection of Pusan, Chinhae, Masan, Kosong, Tongyang and Chinju waterworks; Masan limited to 8 hours daily service."},{"t":"Geodetic Survey","lv":3,"p":[138,138],"pp":"123","s":"Resurveys of Inchon and Sosa completed and map copper plates received from Japan."},{"t":"Fire Control","lv":3,"p":[138,139],"pp":"123","s":"Fire Prevention Week 3-8 November, poster prize on 29 November, and allocation of 44 fire trucks and 325 pumps received through USOCS."},{"t":"Section 5. Housing","lv":2,"p":[140,145],"pp":"125","s":"Forty-house low-cost project 50 per cent complete, 400 Seoul houses nearly all occupied, 100 million won refugee housing plan, and refugee-built An Chang Ho and quonset villages."},{"t":"Low Cost Housing","lv":3,"p":[140,140],"pp":"125","s":"National Housing Administration's forty-house project begun in November with 20 houses erected, for Koreans evicted for American personnel housing."},{"t":"Seoul Housing Project","lv":3,"p":[140,140],"pp":"125","s":"Nearly all 400 city-built houses occupied; 60 held in reserve but families allowed in temporarily."},{"t":"Refugee Housing","lv":3,"p":[141,142],"pp":"126","s":"1947-48 Welfare Housing Plan (100 million won, 24 million for winterized tents), provincial progress table, and 90 per cent of 400 Refugee Shelter Construction Society houses done."},{"t":"Cooperative Villages","lv":3,"p":[143,145],"pp":"128","s":"An Chang Ho refugee village of 25 buildings west of Seoul, and a quonset hut village for 660 people with orphanage and match factory."},{"t":"Part IV. Finance","lv":1,"p":[146,181],"pp":"131","s":"Government finance (note issue up 9.2 billion won, budget 45 per cent above last year) and non-government finance (deposits, loans, insurance, bank statistics)."},{"t":"Section 1. Government Finance","lv":2,"p":[146,158],"pp":"131","s":"Largest monthly note issue rise since August 1946, 302 million won excess withdrawals, supplementary budgets for tobacco (569.8 million won) and flood repair, and revenue at 66 per cent of budget."},{"t":"Note Issue of the Bank of Chosun","lv":3,"p":[146,146],"pp":"131","s":"Note issue rose 9.2 billion won in November, driven by rice-collection credit (12 billion won lines) and deficit spending."},{"t":"Overdrafts and Revenue Deposits","lv":3,"p":[147,149],"pp":"132","s":"SKIG deposited 917 million won of revenue and withdrew 1,219 million, an excess withdrawal of 302 million won; tobacco purchases also expanded issue."},{"t":"Expenditure Budget of the South Korean Interim Government","lv":3,"p":[150,150],"pp":"135","s":"Budget of 17,720,227,310 won approved 2 July; November supplements of 89.5 million (Transportation) and 569.8 million won (Monopoly Bureau); 18 requests over 2,722 million pending."},{"t":"Total Adjusted Expenditures of the South Korean Interim Government, Fiscal Year 1946-47","lv":3,"p":[150,150],"pp":"135","s":"Table of adjusted expenditures chargeable to 1946/47 through 30 September 1947."},{"t":"Comparison of Total Adjusted Expenditures for Fiscal Year 1946-47 and Total Expenditure Budget for Fiscal Year 1947-48","lv":3,"p":[150,153],"pp":"135","s":"Budget up over 45 per cent; Transportation, Civil Service, Internal Security, Agriculture and Monopoly Bureau rise sharply, National Food Administration falls 2,777 million won."},{"t":"Per Capita Expenditure for Fiscal Year 1946-47","lv":3,"p":[154,154],"pp":"139","s":"On 20 million population, per capita spending was 668.91 won in 1946/47 against 972.26 won proposed for 1947/48."},{"t":"Monthly Receipts and Payments","lv":3,"p":[154,155],"pp":"139","s":"October expenditures rose 1,553 million won (Monopoly Bureau 1,123 million, Education 398 million); revenue rose, led by Monopoly Bureau (449 million)."},{"t":"Comparison of Monthly Average of Projected Revenues for Fiscal Year 1947-48 and Monthly Average Collection","lv":3,"p":[156,156],"pp":"141","s":"Average collection was 70 per cent of projected revenue April-August; improved by end October on Monopoly and tax receipts."},{"t":"Relationship Between Revenue Collections and Approved Budget","lv":3,"p":[157,157],"pp":"142","s":"Collections stood at 66 per cent of proposed monthly average expenditures at end October, up from 61 per cent at end September."},{"t":"Taxation","lv":3,"p":[157,157],"pp":"142","s":"Tax investigators' training school opening in December and new Beverage Tax Ordinance effective 1 November."},{"t":"Delinquent Taxes Due From Vested Properties","lv":3,"p":[157,157],"pp":"142","s":"328,284,073 won of assessed national taxes unpaid by vested properties at 31 May 1947; conferences held on collection."},{"t":"Civilian Supply Program","lv":3,"p":[158,158],"pp":"143","s":"Office of Civilian Supply receipts and disbursements; surplus funds rose over 371 million won in November."},{"t":"Section 2. Non-Government Finance","lv":2,"p":[159,181],"pp":"144","s":"Seven banks and 142 Financial Associations with 577 branches; October deposits up 2,282 million won, loans up 1,733 million, insurance growth, and a 3 billion won deposit campaign."},{"t":"Financial Institutions","lv":3,"p":[159,159],"pp":"144","s":"Seven banks and 142 Financial Associations had 577 branches; FFA headquarters in Seoul with branches in nine provincial capitals."},{"t":"Cash Balances in Financial Institutions","lv":3,"p":[160,160],"pp":"145","s":"Cash balances fell 175 million won in October amid payments to farmers for rice, tobacco, straw products and seed cotton."},{"t":"Deposits in Financial Institutions","lv":3,"p":[161,163],"pp":"146","s":"Deposits rose 2,282 million won (7 per cent): government up 3,420 million, inter-bank down 1,088 million, public down 50 million, the first public decline since January."},{"t":"Deposit Balance of Each Financial Institution","lv":3,"p":[164,164],"pp":"149","s":"Bank of Chosun up 2,322 million won, Financial Associations up 350 million, Mutual Aid Bank up 18 million; five other banks down 408 million."},{"t":"Loans by Financial Institutions","lv":3,"p":[165,166],"pp":"150","s":"Net loan balance rose 1,733 million won (12 per cent): government guaranteed loans up 711 million, others 1,022 million, mainly for seed cotton, rice and straw products."},{"t":"Analysis of Loans Made by Financial Institutions","lv":3,"p":[167,168],"pp":"152","s":"Industrial Bank held 28 per cent of loans, Bank of Chosun 25 per cent; 16,067 million won approved 5 May-18 November, 97 per cent for government policy."},{"t":"Life Insurance","lv":3,"p":[169,169],"pp":"154","s":"Insurance in force up 45 million won (11 per cent) and monthly premiums up nearly 40 per cent in October."},{"t":"Damage Insurance","lv":3,"p":[170,170],"pp":"155","s":"Liabilities assumed up 1,005 million won (75 per cent) and premiums up 55 per cent, the largest rises since April 1946."},{"t":"Activities of Financial Institutions","lv":3,"p":[171,173],"pp":"156","s":"November loans including 150 million won for straw bags, bank shareholder meetings, precious-metals sale license, 3 billion won deposit drive, and destruction of 400 million yen of Bank of Japan notes."},{"t":"Activities of Life and Damage Insurance Companies","lv":3,"p":[174,174],"pp":"159","s":"95 policies reinsured for 9.675 million yen, 561,551 policyholders with claims on Japanese insurers, and a 40 million won risk on the Chosun Printing Plant."},{"t":"Other Financial Statistics","lv":3,"p":[174,181],"pp":"159","s":"Tables 15-19: Bank of Chosun interest rates, assets and liabilities of each bank, and clearing house transactions."},{"t":"Part V. Government and Politics","lv":1,"p":[182,227],"pp":"167","s":"Dean's inauguration, election and UN issues, executive-legislature friction, KILA debate on the Outline, party maneuvering, ordinances, police history and interzone monitoring."},{"t":"Section 1. Administration","lv":2,"p":[182,190],"pp":"167","s":"Dean inaugurated 3 November; Hodge and Dean refuse an early election; Helmick returns KILA measures; National Police Board created under Ordinance 157; National Economic Board handles power and budget."},{"t":"Inauguration of Military Governor","lv":3,"p":[182,182],"pp":"167","s":"Dean replaces the late Maj. Gen. Lerch on 3 November and states three U.S. aims: independent Korea, democratic government, sound economy and education."},{"t":"Election Under United Nations","lv":3,"p":[183,184],"pp":"168","s":"Hodge's 19 November statement and Dean's 27 November release refuse an election before the UN Commission arrives; 15-member Election Committee met 18 November."},{"t":"Relations Between the Executive and the Assembly","lv":3,"p":[185,187],"pp":"170","s":"Helmick's replies on frozen yen, a delegation to Japan, UN representatives, two-thirds confirmation rule and temple property; Dean's Ordinance 153; Public Prostitution Law signed 14 November."},{"t":"Korea Civil Service","lv":3,"p":[188,188],"pp":"173","s":"Statistics course in its tenth week, training officers in each province, and CAF 10-13 clerk examinations in Kyongsang-pukto."},{"t":"National Police Board","lv":3,"p":[188,188],"pp":"173","s":"Ordinance 157 board appointed 25 November with six members to rule on senior police appointments and appeals."},{"t":"National Economic Board","lv":3,"p":[189,190],"pp":"174","s":"Meetings of 10, 18 and 25 November approved supplemental budgets, reviewed the Sumgin Gang dam, and recommended emergency power measures and payment for North Korean power."},{"t":"Section 2. Korean Interim Legislative Assembly","lv":2,"p":[191,197],"pp":"176","s":"13 sessions averaging 65 members; six on the Outline; Temporary Entertainment Law passed; Ordinance 153 amended; Dean's 10 November speech; Helmick's letters on the constitution and collaborators bill."},{"t":"General","lv":3,"p":[191,191],"pp":"176","s":"13 sessions in November, average attendance 65 and length 3.5 hours; business included the Outline, the entertainment law and an executive-legislative misunderstanding."},{"t":"Outline of Measures for Meeting the Current Situation in South Korea","lv":3,"p":[191,193],"pp":"176","s":"Directors Ahn and Chough questioned 7 November; 'sovereign' wording and leftist objections; KILA split on a no-confidence demand, then rejection motions failed 32-20 and 33-20."},{"t":"Temporary Law for Cessation of Professional Entertainment Activities","lv":3,"p":[194,194],"pp":"179","s":"Suspends high-class restaurants, bars, cabarets, dance halls and licensed kisaeng; second reading 24 November, third reading waived 48-0."},{"t":"Ordinance No. 153","lv":3,"p":[195,195],"pp":"180","s":"Ordinance 153 on aiding American personnel to violate military directives; first reading 6 November; text and later wording changes."},{"t":"Speech by General Dean","lv":3,"p":[196,196],"pp":"181","s":"Dean addressed KILA on 10 November, urging executive-legislative cooperation and praising its legislative record."},{"t":"Letters from Executive Branch","lv":3,"p":[196,197],"pp":"181","s":"Helmick's letters on Kangwon-do and Chungchong-namdo governors, UN representative and frozen yen, and withholding of the Temporary Constitution and collaborators' law, angering some members."},{"t":"Section 3. Political Developments","lv":2,"p":[198,207],"pp":"183","s":"Full text of the Outline and reactions; three groups on independence (UN election, immediate election under Rhee, Council of 12 Parties); Dean's reception; party anniversaries."},{"t":"Outline of Measures for Meeting the Current Situation in South Korea","lv":3,"p":[198,202],"pp":"183","s":"Quotes Part II of the 25 September Outline, including sovereignty and U.S. withdrawal conditions, and protests by leftists, the National Society and rightist parties."},{"t":"Independence and Unification","lv":3,"p":[203,206],"pp":"188","s":"Moderates back UN-observed elections; Rhee demands elections by mid-December; the Council of 12 Parties' four-point program, defections by Korean Democratic and Hankook parties, and loss of power."},{"t":"Military Governor","lv":3,"p":[207,207],"pp":"192","s":"Most political factions gave Dean a courteous but pointedly qualified reception at his 3 November inauguration."},{"t":"Organizations and Anniversaries","lv":3,"p":[207,207],"pp":"192","s":"Korean Democratic Party's second anniversary 3 November, South Korea Labor Party's first on 23 November, and Hong Myawng Hi elected chairman of the Democratic Independence Party."},{"t":"Section 4. Legal Affairs","lv":2,"p":[208,215],"pp":"193","s":"Ordinances 149 and 153-156 including the Beverage Tax Law, Public Act No. 7 abolishing licensed prostitution, legal opinions, 21,372 prisoners, and Han Chi Keun's life sentence for the Lyuh Woon Hyong assassination."},{"t":"Functions of Department of Justice","lv":3,"p":[208,208],"pp":"193","s":"Department frames ordinances and orders, renders legality opinions, and assists court modernization."},{"t":"Ordinances and Orders","lv":3,"p":[208,212],"pp":"193","s":"Executive Order 7 closing Haibang News Agency; Ordinances 149, 153, 154 (Beverage Tax with rates and license fees), 155 and 156; Communications Orders 22-23; Public Act No. 7."},{"t":"Legal Opinions","lv":3,"p":[213,213],"pp":"198","s":"Opinions on foreigners' land purchase and national defense areas, validity of a Korean-American soldier's marriage, and Chungang Pharmaceutical's bismuth claim."},{"t":"Penal Institutions","lv":3,"p":[214,214],"pp":"199","s":"17 prisons and 6 work camps held 21,372 prisoners at 30 November; 202 boys moved from Kumchon to Inchon Boys' Prison."},{"t":"Korean Courts","lv":3,"p":[214,214],"pp":"199","s":"Conference in the week ending 8 November on the Court Reorganization Law dispute between judges and Justice Department officials."},{"t":"Claims Bureau","lv":3,"p":[215,215],"pp":"200","s":"Han Chi Keun sentenced to life for assassinating Lyuh Woon Hyong; Claims Bureau handles claims against SKIG and vested property claims."},{"t":"Section 5. Police","lv":2,"p":[216,222],"pp":"201","s":"Department of Police summary of Japanese-era police powers, about 580 policewomen, Ordinance 157 police board, crime laboratory with 15,118 fingerprint requests, and a new Police Welfare Association."},{"t":"General","lv":3,"p":[216,216],"pp":"201","s":"This month's report gives background material instead of statistics, including practices under the Japanese and work for women."},{"t":"Administration of Justice Under Japanese","lv":3,"p":[216,218],"pp":"201","s":"Account of colonial police powers, six departments including censorship and thought control, absence of civil rights, and attitudes of people, police and officials."},{"t":"Police Women","lv":3,"p":[219,219],"pp":"204","s":"About 580 policewomen, mostly in Seoul, Taegu, Inchon and Pusan, trained four weeks and deployed for juvenile and female cases."},{"t":"Organizational Changes","lv":3,"p":[220,220],"pp":"205","s":"National Police Board under Ordinance 157 and another reorganization of police powers."},{"t":"Crime Laboratory","lv":3,"p":[221,221],"pp":"206","s":"Equipment, ballistics, handwriting and photography units; 22 detective cases referred, 20 completed, 15,118 fingerprint requests answered."},{"t":"Police Welfare Association","lv":3,"p":[222,222],"pp":"207","s":"Association formed in the first week of November and began negotiating with the welfare bureau for UNRRA clothing."},{"t":"Section 6. Interzone Activities","lv":2,"p":[223,227],"pp":"208","s":"Alternate-Saturday mail exchanges at Tosong and Radio Pyongyang monitoring: Kim Il Sung's 2 November anniversary speech, rice price claims, education, Soviet films and wheat planting."},{"t":"General","lv":3,"p":[223,223],"pp":"208","s":"Report incomplete for security reasons, covering mail exchanges and Radio Pyongyang intercepts."},{"t":"Mail Exchange","lv":3,"p":[223,223],"pp":"208","s":"Mail exchanged between North and South on alternate Saturdays at Tosong, with November counts by date."},{"t":"North Korean Radio","lv":3,"p":[223,227],"pp":"208","s":"Kim Il Sung blamed the U.S. for the Joint Commission's failure; Pyongyang cited South Korean rice price rises, 286 Soviet films, literacy gains and wheat planting over quota."},{"t":"Part VI. Social","lv":1,"p":[228,267],"pp":"213","s":"Public health and welfare, education with the New Education Exhibit, culture and radio, public information, press and public opinion, and Korean National Youth."},{"t":"Section 1. Public Health and Welfare","lv":2,"p":[228,237],"pp":"213","s":"Vital statistics planning, a tenth cholera death in 1947, plague control program, X-ray machines, laboratories and biologicals, narcotics licenses, refugee camp winterization and a Women's Center."},{"t":"Vital Statistics","lv":3,"p":[228,228],"pp":"213","s":"Plans for birth, death, marriage and morbidity statistics from 1 January 1948; 16,854 marriages and 1,280 divorces April-December 1946."},{"t":"Preventive Medicine","lv":3,"p":[229,229],"pp":"214","s":"Tenth cholera death of 1947 at Hyopchon, communicable disease drop, child vaccination, 56,894 rats killed in Cheju-do, plague control program."},{"t":"Medical Services","lv":3,"p":[230,230],"pp":"215","s":"Five U.S. X-ray machines distributed, six leper clinics, and the unheated Women's Medical College with 500 students."},{"t":"Nursing Affairs","lv":3,"p":[231,231],"pp":"216","s":"Four-day course for 62 public health nurses, Helmick at the graduation of 20 nurses on 4 November, 1,214 midwives and 1,261 nurses registered."},{"t":"Dental Affairs","lv":3,"p":[231,231],"pp":"216","s":"Seoul National University Dental College moved to the Department of Education and the American dental advisory section discontinued."},{"t":"Veterinary Affairs","lv":3,"p":[231,231],"pp":"216","s":"Animal hospital construction begun, anthrax deaths of 8 cattle in Kyongsang-pukto, and 58 police horses quarantined at Pusan."},{"t":"Laboratories","lv":3,"p":[232,232],"pp":"217","s":"Survey of seven provincial laboratories, plague vaccine study after a case near the Manchurian border, and tables on biologicals."},{"t":"Pharmaceutical Affairs","lv":3,"p":[233,235],"pp":"217","s":"Tables on Korean, American and veterinary biologicals and 2,526 narcotic licenses issued since August 1945."},{"t":"Public Assistance","lv":3,"p":[236,236],"pp":"221","s":"Welfare institution survey, winterization of nine camps along the 38th parallel, 300 blankets to Chunchon, Red Cross clothing plan."},{"t":"Women's Bureau","lv":3,"p":[237,237],"pp":"222","s":"Women's Center took over the former Hokuksa building; child-training meetings 15-17 November; first sub-section heads' meeting 13-14 November."},{"t":"Section 2. Education","lv":2,"p":[238,244],"pp":"223","s":"New Education Exhibit (21 November-5 December, 104,976 visitors), Association of Colleges and Universities founded 28 November, normal school supplies, and Education Director Yu Uck Kyum's death on 8 November."},{"t":"General","lv":3,"p":[238,238],"pp":"223","s":"Education opens with the New Education Exhibit held in Seoul in November and early December, which drew educators from all of South Korea and became the focus of daily conferences on education."},{"t":"New Education Exhibit","lv":3,"p":[238,239],"pp":"223","s":"First national exhibit at Seoul Middle School drew 104,976 visitors; seven educator conferences; 105 top education donors honored 23 November."},{"t":"Korean Association of Colleges and Universities","lv":3,"p":[240,241],"pp":"225","s":"All 29 college presidents and deans founded the association on 28 November, with officers from Seoul National and Korea universities; tables of approved institutions."},{"t":"Technological Training Board","lv":3,"p":[242,242],"pp":"227","s":"Board approved training projects in November; table of other universities and colleges."},{"t":"Conference of Educational Leaders","lv":3,"p":[243,243],"pp":"228","s":"American national and provincial education advisers met in Seoul 31 October-1 November."},{"t":"Supplies for Teacher Training","lv":3,"p":[243,243],"pp":"228","s":"Notebooks and light bulbs from Civilian Supplies distributed to normal schools; American professional books received."},{"t":"Oratorical Contest","lv":3,"p":[244,244],"pp":"229","s":"27 students from 12 colleges entered the English oratorical contest ending 29 November at Seoul National University."},{"t":"Death of Director of Education, Yu, Uck Kyum","lv":3,"p":[244,244],"pp":"229","s":"Yu Uck Kyum died 8 November aged 53; he had been appointed Education Director on 19 December 1945."},{"t":"Section 3. Culture","lv":2,"p":[245,252],"pp":"230","s":"Introduction to Korean art, Korean United Fine Arts Exhibition 9-30 November, chrysanthemum show with 27,684 visitors, YWCA, Korean Broadcasting System programming and a musical contest."},{"t":"General","lv":3,"p":[245,246],"pp":"230","s":"Culture opens by quoting Andreas Eckardt's 1929 History of Korean Art on Korea's art and early kingdoms, then cites museums, the National Music Institute and the Korean Chorus's 1945 Messiah as signs of culture."},{"t":"Korean United Fine Arts Exhibition","lv":3,"p":[247,247],"pp":"232","s":"Held at Kyung Bok Palace 9-30 November from over 300 entries, with 63 Western and 24 Oriental paintings."},{"t":"Chrysanthemum Exhibition","lv":3,"p":[247,247],"pp":"232","s":"250 chrysanthemums at Duk Soo Palace, 23 October-25 November; 27,684 visitors and 259,109 won in fees."},{"t":"Altrusa Club and YWCA","lv":3,"p":[247,247],"pp":"232","s":"Altrusa Club meetings and a 22 November report on the Hangchow YWCA World Council with nine Korean delegates."},{"t":"YWCA in South Korea","lv":3,"p":[248,248],"pp":"233","s":"Seoul Central YWCA with nearly 2,000 members, three schools, 14 city and rural associations and 28 student associations."},{"t":"Korean Broadcasting System","lv":3,"p":[248,251],"pp":"233","s":"Seoul station plus nine affiliates on a 10.5 hour daily schedule; children's, women's, educational, drama, music and religious programs; typical 17 November schedule and November breakdown."},{"t":"Musical Contest","lv":3,"p":[252,252],"pp":"237","s":"Second yearly contest held 6 November under the Bureau of Culture, with prizes awarded 18 November."},{"t":"Section 4. Public Information","lv":2,"p":[253,258],"pp":"238","s":"Press releases on Civilian Supply, refugees, forests and Ordinance 153, Farmers Weekly (300,000 circulation), opinion sampling, publication licensing, and a Chunchon mobile education trip."},{"t":"General","lv":3,"p":[253,253],"pp":"238","s":"Department of Public Information keeps Koreans informed of government and world events and gauges public opinion, working with the Office of Civil Information (USAFIK) and XXIV Corps' Office of Public Information."},{"t":"Bureau of Public Information","lv":3,"p":[253,254],"pp":"238","s":"Press releases on USOCS, New Korea Company advances, migration figures, forest theft, a narcotics factory seizure and 170 price-ceiling prosecutions; special releases and Farmers Weekly."},{"t":"Bureau of Public Opinion","lv":3,"p":[255,256],"pp":"240","s":"15 party registrations received, publication licensing figures, four opinion samplings, 20 illustrated lectures, translation of 25 editorials and political education work."},{"t":"Motion Picture Section","lv":3,"p":[257,257],"pp":"242","s":"One-line cross-reference: the Motion Picture Section's work is described under CULTURE, with no further detail given here."},{"t":"Radio Bureau","lv":3,"p":[257,257],"pp":"242","s":"Political broadcasts including a Labor Department speech and a talk on Korean National Youth."},{"t":"Office of Civil Information","lv":3,"p":[257,258],"pp":"242","s":"Mobile Education train in Chunchon 7-10 November; of 290 questioned, 38.6 per cent read Farmers Weekly, 88.4 per cent of those liking it."},{"t":"Section 5. Press and Public Opinion","lv":2,"p":[259,263],"pp":"244","s":"Korean press reaction to Dean's inauguration, the Political Affairs Council Outline, 12-party talks and Rhee's election demands, the governor's vetoes and a court-jurisdiction dispute, plus a monthly poll."},{"t":"General","lv":3,"p":[259,259],"pp":"244","s":"Korean press in November focused on Major General Dean's inauguration as Military Governor, the Political Affairs Council's document for SKIG, and Korean-question developments at the United Nations."},{"t":"Inauguration of the Military Governor, Major General William F. Dean","lv":3,"p":[259,259],"pp":"244","s":"Dae Han Ilbo and Dong-A Ilbo editorials urged Dean to complete transfer of administration to Koreans and hoped he would be the last military governor."},{"t":"Political Affairs Council","lv":3,"p":[260,260],"pp":"245","s":"Press attacks on the Outline; Seoul Shin Mun on 11 November called its spirit anti-national and impure."},{"t":"Political Developments","lv":3,"p":[261,261],"pp":"246","s":"Interest in the 12-party conferences, Hodge's 19 November election statement and Rhee's widely quoted attack on treatment of Koreans."},{"t":"The Governor and KILA","lv":3,"p":[262,262],"pp":"247","s":"Conflict over the governor's vetoes prompted press debate on whether KILA should disband."},{"t":"The Governor and the Courts","lv":3,"p":[262,262],"pp":"247","s":"Outcry over transfer of a political murder case to the Provost Marshal's court; three Court of Appeals judges threatened to resign; Dean's 1 December release."},{"t":"Public Opinion","lv":3,"p":[263,263],"pp":"248","s":"Party demands and a Korean Patriotic Women's Alliance poll on UN success, Soviet-American withdrawal, and the newspaper law."},{"t":"Section 6. Korean National Youth, Inc.","lv":2,"p":[264,267],"pp":"249","s":"Membership of about 350,000 (70,000 in the Sea Youth) more than doubled in four months; community programs, Suwon training conferences, rivalry with Dai Dong, and a hostel for officials."},{"t":"General","lv":3,"p":[264,264],"pp":"249","s":"Korean National Youth, Inc., promotes democratic procedures but faces conflict; membership rose from 157,218 on 31 July to a preliminary 350,000 on 30 November 1947, including about 70,000 in the Sea Youth."},{"t":"Membership","lv":3,"p":[264,264],"pp":"249","s":"More than doubled in four months to 350,000, including 70,000 in Korean National Sea Youth."},{"t":"The Program","lv":3,"p":[264,264],"pp":"249","s":"Discussion groups, literacy classes, tree planting, road repair and farm help; women's section teaches home nursing and crafts."},{"t":"Training","lv":3,"p":[265,265],"pp":"250","s":"Four weekly Suwon training conferences for chiefs; 196 Organization and 216 Activities trainees graduated."},{"t":"Opposition","lv":3,"p":[265,265],"pp":"250","s":"Rivalry with the rightist Dai Dong youth group eased; Dai Dongs accused of tearing down posters and coercing members."},{"t":"Continued Activities","lv":3,"p":[265,267],"pp":"250","s":"Repair of a former Constabulary building as a hostel for visiting officials and dormitory for refugee students."}],"by":"claude-sonnet (Claude Code subagent)","emb":["K-27-in-K-26"]},{"id":"J-27","ia":"summation-non-military-activities-japan-no-27-1947-12","s":"J","no":27,"d":"1947-12","d2":"","t":"Summation of Non-Military Activities in Japan, No. 27 (December 1947)","v":"","pg":183,"o":"Summation No. 27 covers non-military activities in Japan for December 1947, the month the First National Diet closed (9 December) after passing 174 of 186 bills. Political coverage centres on the Kato Committee's report on hoarded and concealed goods, the dissolution of the Home Ministry, the new Attorney General's Office and Police Law, party realignments, and the near-completion of the purge. War-crimes sections follow the IMTFE defence cases of Oshima, Sato, Shigemitsu, Shimada, Shiratori and Suzuki. The economic part reports production, trade, price, banking and tax statistics with charts, plus the Elimination of Excessive Concentration of Economic Power Law, the public food-distribution corporations (kudan), coal control and reparations transfers. Labor, health, education, and information media are covered in detail. A historian will find month-end statistics, legislative lists and the Occupation's own framing of Japanese reform.","h":["The Diet's Special Committee headed by Kanju Kato estimated goods disposed of by the Japanese Government after the surrender at over 50 billion yen; on 11 December the House of Representatives formed an Illegal Transactions Investigation Committee.","The Home Ministry Dissolution Act (8 December) abolished the ministry controlling police, local government and State Shinto; the Police Law created a National Public Safety Commission, and a National Fire Defense Law followed on 9 December.","The First National Diet closed on 9 December after sitting since 20 May: 186 bills introduced, 174 passed, 12 rejected, including new civil and criminal codes, a Ministry of Labor and the Attorney General's Office law (effective 15 February).","The Elimination of Excessive Concentration of Economic Power Law, aimed at zaibatsu control, passed on 9 December and was promulgated as Law No. 207 on 18 December; the Closed Institutions Liquidating Commission was ordered to liquidate 145 control organizations.","Purge figures from 4 January to 13 December: 656,550 persons screened, 4,789 removed and 954 barred; the Cabinet announced on 10 December the virtual completion of the purge.","Coal production reached a record 2,481,000 metric tons in November, while the House of Councillors passed the Bill for Temporary State Control of Coal Mines by 132 to 79 on 8 December.","Five public corporations (kudan) were formed by law on 9 December to control distribution of staple foods, groceries, fats and oils, feed and liquor; November food imports were 106,719 metric tons, 94 percent US flour and wheat.","Bank of Japan notes outstanding reached 219,142,000,000 yen on 31 December, and labor unions numbered 25,896 with 5,926,986 members at 31 October."],"k":["Summation December 1947","Hoarded goods","Kato Committee","Home Ministry dissolution","Police Law","First National Diet","Purge","IMTFE defence","Zaibatsu dissolution","Coal control","Kudan food distribution","Labor unions","Imports and exports","Bank of Japan","Reparations"],"toc":[{"t":"Front matter","lv":1,"p":[1,5],"pp":"","s":"Cover for Summation No. 27 (December 1947), table of contents for Parts I-IV, and the list of charts, maps and graphs numbered 1-104."},{"t":"Part I: General","lv":1,"p":[6,15],"pp":"1","s":"Digest of the month: no SCAP reorganization, the hoarded-goods drive, Home Ministry dissolution, end of the First Diet, near-complete purge, economic indicators and social developments."},{"t":"Organization Under SCAP","lv":2,"p":[7,7],"pp":"3","s":"No change occurred in the non-military organization of GHQ SCAP during December."},{"t":"Political Activities","lv":2,"p":[7,8],"pp":"3","s":"Hoarded-goods investigation, Katayama's denial of a secret government, Home Ministry dissolution, party realignments, the Diet's closing on 9 December, purge totals, the Police Law and war-crimes trial progress."},{"t":"Economic Activities","lv":2,"p":[9,13],"pp":"7","s":"Digest of resources, industry, transport, labor, trade, rationing, finance and property control, including Law No. 207 on economic concentration and 340 looted ships reported."},{"t":"Social Activities","lv":2,"p":[14,15],"pp":"17","s":"December 1947: separate prefectural health and welfare departments were legislated 7 December; 4,000,000 children got school lunches; National Treasures registered rose to 8,211; Katayama Cabinet politics dominated press comment."},{"t":"Part II: Political","lv":1,"p":[16,54],"pp":"21","s":"Government and politics, public safety and legal affairs: the Kato Committee report, Home Ministry abolition, the Diet's legislation, police and prison statistics, and Tokyo war-crimes trial defence cases."},{"t":"Government and Politics","lv":2,"p":[17,41],"pp":"23","s":"Kato Committee findings on hoarded goods, Home Ministry dissolution, Attorney General's Office, party shifts, the First Diet's 186 bills, and screening of information media companies."},{"t":"Investigation of Hoarded Goods","lv":3,"p":[17,22],"pp":"23","s":"Kato Committee report estimating over 50 billion yen of goods disposed of after the surrender; conclusions blame the Suzuki Cabinet's secret order of 14 August 1945."},{"t":"Political Supplements","lv":3,"p":[23,23],"pp":"","s":"Katayama's 29 November denial of a secret government, the 132-79 vote on coal-mine state control, and the budget and economic rehabilitation debate."},{"t":"The National Government","lv":3,"p":[24,25],"pp":"","s":"Home Ministry dissolved on 8 December, an Attorney General's Office law replacing the Ministry of Justice, and new ministers of Transportation, State and Agriculture."},{"t":"Political Parties","lv":3,"p":[26,27],"pp":"","s":"Social Democratic fight over Hirano's successor (Hatano appointed 13 December), Liberal-Doshi Club New Party Promotion League, Democratic reorganization and Communist Party convention on 21 December."},{"t":"The Diet","lv":3,"p":[28,40],"pp":"","s":"Niigata by-election, party strength in both Houses, and a ministry-by-ministry list of legislation passed by the First Diet, which closed 9 December."},{"t":"Screening of Information Media Companies","lv":3,"p":[41,41],"pp":"","s":"Purge screening of 1,424 principal officeholders in information media companies: 1,258 passed, 162 barred, four pending."},{"t":"Public Safety","lv":2,"p":[42,45],"pp":"73","s":"Black-market crime, 177,815 offenses and 99,817 arrests in October, the Police Law and National Public Safety Commission, 82,785 prisoners in November and 1,051 fires in October."},{"t":"Legal Affairs and War Crimes","lv":2,"p":[46,54],"pp":"81","s":"1,403 atrocity investigations on hand, six trials of 32 war criminals completed, and IMTFE defence cases of Oshima, Sato, Shigemitsu, Shimada, Shiratori and Suzuki."},{"t":"Legal Affairs and National Prosecution of War Criminals","lv":3,"p":[46,47],"pp":"","s":"Diet passed the Attorney General's Office law on 8 December; 1,403 atrocity cases remained under investigation; 429 suspects tried by 23 December (410 guilty, 32 death sentences); six trials of 32 defendants completed."},{"t":"International Prosecution of War Criminals","lv":3,"p":[48,54],"pp":"","s":"Tokyo tribunal: Sir William Webb back on the bench 15 December; defence of Oshima, Sato, Shigemitsu, Shimada, Shiratori and Suzuki, with Prince Higashi-kuni testifying."},{"t":"Part III: Economic","lv":1,"p":[55,159],"pp":"99","s":"Thirteen sections on agriculture, mining, industry, textiles, transport, communications, labor, trade, rationing, finance, property control and science, with extensive production tables and charts; record coal output of 2,481,000 tons in November."},{"t":"Agriculture and Fisheries","lv":2,"p":[56,58],"pp":"101","s":"New Resources Committee (13 December), fourth government land purchase of 471,636 cho, crop insurance law effective 27 December, and 232 whales caught in antarctic whaling."},{"t":"Forestry and Mining","lv":2,"p":[59,63],"pp":"107","s":"Log output at 56 percent of annual goal, firewood and charcoal, record coal output of 2,481,000 tons in November, crude oil declines and mineral production."},{"t":"Heavy Industries","lv":2,"p":[64,73],"pp":"117","s":"Coke up 5.8 percent, iron and steel limited by power, chemicals at 39 percent of requirements, machine tools down 14 percent, plus shipbuilding and railway rolling stock."},{"t":"Manufacturing","lv":2,"p":[74,83],"pp":"137","s":"Food processing, newsprint at a postwar peak, glass, optical instruments, motor vehicles, leather, agricultural implements and toys up 65 percent for Christmas."},{"t":"Textile Industries","lv":2,"p":[84,97],"pp":"157","s":"Cotton, silk, rayon, wool and bast fibers: raw cotton from America and India offset by power shortages, with silk fabric for export increased by a Government program."},{"t":"Transportation and Public Utilities","lv":2,"p":[98,103],"pp":"185","s":"More buses and trucks registered in November, lower average daily freight tonnage, coastal shipping up, electric generation down and gas production up over October."},{"t":"Communications","lv":2,"p":[104,111],"pp":"197","s":"Collect radiotelephone calls to the US from 5 January 1948, first premium postal savings certificates, savings limit raised to 30,000 yen, and equipment production."},{"t":"Labor","lv":2,"p":[112,120],"pp":"213","s":"Employment Security Law, 25,896 unions with 5,926,986 members, coal and electric power disputes settled, and anti-Communist takeover of the railway workers' union."},{"t":"Imports and Exports","lv":2,"p":[121,133],"pp":"231","s":"Private trade contracts (32 imports, 651 exports), 106,719 tons of food imports, 18,564,833 yards of cotton exports and many first postwar shipments."},{"t":"Rationing and Price Control","lv":2,"p":[134,140],"pp":"257","s":"Five kudan public corporations for food distribution formed 9 December, perishable food program from 27 November, textile distribution, urban family expenditures and consumer price index."},{"t":"Finance","lv":2,"p":[141,149],"pp":"271","s":"Bank notes of 219,142,000,000 yen, Reconstruction Finance Bank loans of 37,904,260,000 yen, Interest Rate Adjustment Law, tax collections of 32.8 billion yen and national debt of 330.9 billion yen."},{"t":"Property Control and Reparations","lv":2,"p":[150,155],"pp":"289","s":"8,183 foreign property reports, 340 looted ships, SCAP custody account of 252,604,035 yen, Law No. 207, and 11,938 of 19,052 arsenal machines accepted for reparations."},{"t":"Science and Technology","lv":2,"p":[156,159],"pp":"299","s":"New Institute of Physical and Chemical Research Co., a cancer diagnostic test, 158 patents granted in November, 48 new Japanese Standards and earthquake forecasting."},{"t":"Part IV: Social","lv":1,"p":[160,183],"pp":"307","s":"Public health and welfare, then education, religion, arts and the media of expression including press, radio, films and SCAP information libraries."},{"t":"Public Health and Welfare","lv":2,"p":[161,170],"pp":"309","s":"Typhus control drives, communicable disease rates, milk inspection, hospital capacity, medical supplies, Red Cross, public assistance, repatriation and vital statistics."},{"t":"Education, Religion and Media of Expression","lv":2,"p":[171,183],"pp":"327","s":"School administration, new textbook printing, Christian missions, newly designated national treasures, press and radio, film production and 29,392 visits to SCAP libraries."},{"t":"Education","lv":3,"p":[171,174],"pp":"","s":"Tokyo approved about $12,000,000 to reimburse private schools taking overflow pupils; 40,050 boxes of window glass allocated; aptitude test for higher schools set for 31 January 1948; 2,302,275 surplus batteries donated; 141,038,394 textbooks authorized."},{"t":"Religion, Arts and Monuments","lv":3,"p":[175,176],"pp":"","s":"Lutheran and Nazarene churches left the United Church of Christ on 2 and 6 December; Chien-in split from the Jodo Sect; 194 more National Treasures designated, total 8,211; Fenollosa memorial held at Otsu."},{"t":"Media of Expression","lv":3,"p":[176,183],"pp":"","s":"December press and radio campaigns on coal, rice collection and the new Civil Code; 778 books published in November, a postwar record; Catholic Digest licensed; French films licensed; SCAP libraries drew 29,392 visitors."}],"by":"claude-sonnet (Claude Code subagent)"},{"id":"K-27","ia":"south-korean-interim-government-activities-no-27-1947-12","s":"K","no":27,"d":"1947-12","d2":"","t":"South Korean Interim Government Activities, No. 27 (December 1947)","v":"","pg":218,"o":"South Korean Interim Government Activities No. 27 covers December 1947 (some economic series end in November) and was prepared by the National Economic Board for USAMGIK. The month was dominated by an electric power emergency after North Korea capped deliveries to South Korea at 80,000 KW, leading to the Emergency Electric Power Board, coal and briquette shortages and curtailed railroad service. Rice collection reached 96.3 percent of quota, while refugees from the north fell to 4,487. Politically, the report covers the assassination of Dr. Chang Duk Soo, the transfer of terrorism cases to American military courts, the Helmick-KILA dispute, the founding of the National Independence Federation, Rhee and Kim Koo merger talks, and preparations for the UN Temporary Commission. Statistical tables on prices, trade, finance, banking, labor, health, education, and Korean National Youth membership give historians a detailed economic and administrative baseline for the last weeks before the 1948 elections.","h":["North Korea notified on 25 November that South Korea must keep its draw within 80,000 KW; a seven-member Emergency Electric Power Board with legal force and a priority list (waterworks, coal mines, hospitals, railways) was created on 15 December.","Refugees from north of the 38th parallel numbered 4,487 in December, the lowest since February, bringing 1947 to 163,253 against 185,441 in 1946; about 60 percent of May-December arrivals went to Seoul or Kyonggi-do.","Rice collection reached 96.29 percent of the 5,156,000 suk quota (744,793 metric tons), but Cholla-pukto managed only 77.17 percent, and the Korean Interim Legislative Assembly heard complaints that quotas and the 640-won price were too harsh.","Bank of Chosun note issue reached 33,388 million won, nearly 13 billion more than at end of September, driven by rice-collection financing; the Seoul retail index stood roughly 19 times its August 1945 base.","Dr. Chang Duk Soo, political head of the Korean Democratic Party, was assassinated in early December; his killers were arrested 4 December, and Military Governor Dean transferred the Dai Han Min Chung terrorism case to a military court.","The moderate National Independence Federation, a 15-party body under Dr. Kimm Kiu Sic, was inaugurated on 20 December, while Rhee Syngman and Kim Koo's merger announcement stalled and parties split over the coming UN Commission and a South-only election.","The Legislative Assembly passed the Law Dealing with Public Theatrical Performances, rejected a no-confidence resolution 35 to 27, held a first reading of a land reform bill limiting holdings to 3 chungbo, and marked its first anniversary on 12 December.","The Agricultural Improvement Service was created on 15 December under Ordinance No. 160, and the National Venereal Disease Center opened in Seoul on 3 December; cholera fell to 14 cases in 1947 from 15,748 in 1946."],"k":["South Korean Interim Government","USAMGIK","December 1947","electric power emergency","rice collection","North Korean refugees","inflation and bank note issue","coal and mining","vested property","Korean Interim Legislative Assembly","National Independence Federation","Chang Duk Soo assassination","UN Temporary Commission","land reform bill","Korean National Youth"],"toc":[{"t":"Front matter","lv":1,"p":[1,7],"pp":"","s":"Cover of No. 27 (December 1947) prepared by the National Economic Board, map of Korea, and the six-part table of contents ending with Korean National Youth, Inc."},{"t":"Introduction","lv":1,"p":[8,9],"pp":"1","s":"Figures are for December except Transportation, Communications, Industrial Production, Mining and Imports/Exports (November); the report is largely American-prepared, with Koreanization of statistics growing."},{"t":"Part I: Basic Aspects","lv":1,"p":[10,23],"pp":"3","s":"Population movement, including 4,487 refugees from the north in December, and the Office of Property Custody's corporate reorganization, loan guarantees and Koreanization of vested property."},{"t":"Section 1. Population","lv":2,"p":[10,17],"pp":"3","s":"Refugees from North Korea fell to 4,487 in December (163,253 for 1947); 21,916 Koreans were apprehended trying to enter Japan; Foreign Affairs handled Japanese, Chinese and travel matters."},{"t":"Refugees","lv":3,"p":[11,12],"pp":"4","s":"December arrivals were the lowest since February 1947 but seven times December 1946; tables give monthly counts, origin by province and the four refugee camps."},{"t":"Distribution","lv":3,"p":[13,13],"pp":"6","s":"Plan for processing and dispersing refugees; about 60 percent of May-December arrivals went to Seoul or Kyonggi-do, with yen over 1,000 deposited in blocked accounts."},{"t":"Japanese Affairs","lv":3,"p":[14,14],"pp":"7","s":"Thirty-six Japanese remained in essential posts; two Korean-owned ships returned from Japan; SCAP extended Category II property claims to 30 June 1948."},{"t":"Chinese Affairs","lv":3,"p":[15,15],"pp":"8","s":"Permits issued to 91 Chinese for travel to China (798 cumulative); 27 Chinese junks arrived at Inchon; four Korean students cleared for Nanking."},{"t":"U.S.-European Affairs","lv":3,"p":[16,17],"pp":"9","s":"Travel orders for 19 students to the United States in December (111 in 1947) and four athletes bound for the St. Moritz Winter Olympics; weekly refugee chart follows."},{"t":"Section 2. Office of Property Custody (Vested Property)","lv":2,"p":[18,23],"pp":"11","s":"Corporate reorganization of Seoul firms (285 under investigation), 235 million won in guaranteed loans, 16 property sales worth 5.2 million won, and royal estates passing to SKIG control from 1 January 1948."},{"t":"Part II: Production and Supply","lv":1,"p":[24,101],"pp":"17","s":"Rice collection and food stocks, prices, agriculture, coal and minerals, industrial output, allocations, trade and labor, all shaped by power and coal shortages and by fertilizer and grain imports."},{"t":"Section 1. Food Supply","lv":2,"p":[24,31],"pp":"17","s":"Rice collection reached 96.29 percent of quota; 586,419 tons of rice on hand at year end; 34,804 tons of grain imported; about 225,000 tons of imports projected for 1949."},{"t":"Rice Collection","lv":3,"p":[26,29],"pp":"19","s":"Quota table by province (Cholla-pukto only 77.17 percent) and National Food Administration reports on poor-farmer rationing in Chungchong-namdo, a Seoul fraud case and Kyongsang-pukto delays."},{"t":"The Outlook","lv":3,"p":[30,31],"pp":"23","s":"1947 crop estimates and a Department of Agriculture memo projecting 350,000 tons of imports for 1948, with scheduled monthly arrivals of 10,000 to 34,000 tons and calorie targets."},{"t":"Section 2. Prices","lv":2,"p":[32,42],"pp":"25","s":"Seoul retail index for 26 goods stood about 19 times the August 1945 level, driven by note issue and North Korean currency conversion; wholesale prices rose on power and coal shortages."},{"t":"Section 3. Agriculture, Forestry, and Fisheries","lv":2,"p":[43,51],"pp":"36","s":"1948 goals plan 3,211,844 chungbo cropped; Agricultural Improvement Service created 15 December (Ordinance 160); 220,953 tons of fertilizer shipped; fuelwood quotas set for Seoul."},{"t":"Agricultural Economics Bureau","lv":3,"p":[43,43],"pp":"36","s":"Completed 1948 Production Goals with 3,211,844 chungbo available, 97 percent of the 1940-44 cropped area, and compared 1947 goals with harvests."},{"t":"Agricultural Production Bureau","lv":3,"p":[44,45],"pp":"37","s":"Livestock goals, a breeding station planned on Cheju-do, 70 tons of pig bristles, apple exports, and cotton collection of about 2,020 tons."},{"t":"Forestry Bureau","lv":3,"p":[46,46],"pp":"39","s":"Six provinces assigned 12,100 tons of charcoal and 1,764,000 cords of fuelwood for Seoul's winter; saltern permits to be coordinated with forestry."},{"t":"Fisheries Bureau","lv":3,"p":[47,47],"pp":"40","s":"Canned marine products for June-November reported; deals concluded for 1,000 tons of anchovy and 100 tons of glue seaweed for Japan."},{"t":"Korean Agricultural Association","lv":3,"p":[48,48],"pp":"41","s":"220,953 tons of fertilizer shipped to railheads by 20 December and 196,095 received; 1948 allocation of 221,718 long tons secured after SCAP adjustment."},{"t":"Agricultural Research and Education","lv":3,"p":[48,49],"pp":"41","s":"Ordinance 160 text: board of six voting members, transfer of experiment stations and the Suwon and Taegu agricultural colleges, and a National Extension Service."},{"t":"Four-H Clubs","lv":3,"p":[50,50],"pp":"43","s":"Kyonggi-do 4-H activities: a dairy-farm school at Anyang for ten boys every two weeks, a straw-product contest and a rat campaign."},{"t":"Vested Property","lv":3,"p":[51,51],"pp":"44","s":"Table 6 counts 325 vested companies under Department of Agriculture control in South Korea at 31 December 1947: 79 textile, 96 marine and 150 forest (128 provincial sawmills); field-office counts incomplete."},{"t":"Section 4. Mining and Minerals","lv":2,"p":[52,63],"pp":"45","s":"November anthracite output fell 5.3 percent; 134.5 million won subsidy for Samchuk, Hwasun and Eunsung mines; only 64,823 tons of bituminous coal imported; copper mines under review."},{"t":"Coal","lv":3,"p":[52,58],"pp":"45","s":"Anthracite and lignite output, stockpiles, Coal Committee allocations and bituminous imports from Japan, hampered by power shortage, truck-tire shortage and scarce briquettes."},{"t":"Metals","lv":3,"p":[59,63],"pp":"52","s":"Pitch shortage, Taichong gold mine repossessed, copper mines owing about 37.2 million won net, Kobayashi tungsten sales of 241.45 million won, and production tables."},{"t":"Section 5. Industrial Production","lv":2,"p":[64,72],"pp":"57","s":"Under 10 percent of 2,012 factories report; November flour fell 24.3 percent, cotton yarn 13.2 percent and paper 22.2 percent, and the cement plant sat idle most of the month."},{"t":"Processed Food","lv":3,"p":[64,64],"pp":"57","s":"Flour milling in two vested mills fell 24.3 percent; Korean-owned mills milled 210 tons; noodle output recovered by 65 percent."},{"t":"Textiles","lv":3,"p":[65,65],"pp":"58","s":"Cotton yarn fell 13.2 percent and cloth 15.7 percent from October as raw cotton ran short; reporting yarn mills rose from seven to nine."},{"t":"Paper","lv":3,"p":[66,66],"pp":"59","s":"Machine-made paper fell 22.2 percent; Chosun Paper Mill at Sunchon dropped 78 percent amid material shortages."},{"t":"Hardware","lv":3,"p":[67,67],"pp":"60","s":"Nail production in three vested plants was the second best of the year, only slightly under October."},{"t":"Chemical Group","lv":3,"p":[68,69],"pp":"61","s":"Briquettes limited by pitch, coke at under one-third of the May-June rate, 1,722 tons of cement at Samchuk, plus brick, pottery and rubber items."},{"t":"Monopoly Bureau Products","lv":3,"p":[70,72],"pp":"63","s":"No government salt output in November-December and private output fell in December; cigarette production down 15 percent while tobacco purchases exceeded budget."},{"t":"Section 6. Allocations and Distribution","lv":2,"p":[73,85],"pp":"66","s":"Surplus Army clothing to be dyed at Chosun Boseki; FFA distributed 89.8 million won of goods; about 2,000,000 gallons of emergency kerosene ordered; kimchi salt ration cut to 2.7 kg."},{"t":"Allocations, Civilian Supplies","lv":3,"p":[73,74],"pp":"66","s":"Clothing consigned to Internal Security, 15,881 kg of scrap metal, Australian wool, scrap rubber and equipment allocated to agencies."},{"t":"Allocations, Materials Control Corporation","lv":3,"p":[75,75],"pp":"68","s":"Captured stockpiles reallocated, including 200 tons of Chinese copper coins and Kobayashi Mining bismuth, tungsten and graphite stocks."},{"t":"Allocations, Korean Produced Commodities","lv":3,"p":[76,79],"pp":"69","s":"Tables of cloth, textiles, shoes, tires, soap, matches, cement and beer allocated through mid-December; allocations dropped sharply."},{"t":"Distribution, Korean Produced Commodities","lv":3,"p":[80,82],"pp":"73","s":"FFA and FDA distributed 89,820,257 won in December and 1,208,526,920 won since July 1946; FFA purchases fell 60 percent while FDA purchases rose seven-fold."},{"t":"Petroleum","lv":3,"p":[83,84],"pp":"76","s":"Order for about 2,000,000 gallons of emergency kerosene, gasoline allocations raised 25 percent, kerosene doubled, and over 20,000 empty drums stranded by the rail shortage."},{"t":"Salt","lv":3,"p":[85,85],"pp":"78","s":"Kimchi salt ration cut from 3.9 to 2.7 kg per person in 1947; a 1.2 kg soya sauce ration advanced."},{"t":"Section 7. Imports and Exports","lv":2,"p":[86,97],"pp":"79","s":"Estimated December imports of 203 million won against exports of 276.7 million; Pusan took 48.2 percent of November imports; 988,344 tons arrived under Civilian Supply in 1947."},{"t":"Private Trade","lv":3,"p":[86,91],"pp":"79","s":"November private imports of 463 million won, 80-90 percent via Hong Kong; exports mostly sea products, plus ship cargoes and Indian cotton purchases."},{"t":"Government to Government Transactions","lv":3,"p":[92,92],"pp":"85","s":"Exports to Japan worth $134,389 in December, 100 tons of glue seaweed accepted by SCAP, and lead and zinc concentrates toll-smelted in Japan."},{"t":"Korean Items for Americans in Korea","lv":3,"p":[93,93],"pp":"86","s":"Souvenirs sold to the Army Post Exchange reached $27,193.63 in December and $64,701.38 over five months."},{"t":"Imports, Civilian Supply","lv":3,"p":[93,97],"pp":"86","s":"988,344 tons received in 1947, Yosu port opened 15 November; December receipts included 102,787 tons of salt, 18,443 tons of ammonium nitrate and 61,863 tons of bituminous coal."},{"t":"Section 8. Labor","lv":2,"p":[98,101],"pp":"91","s":"Labor Department reorganized into four bureaus; power-shortage orders suspended Child Labor and working-hours laws; Kyongsang Textile dispute; 193 reporting unions with 58,556 members."},{"t":"Part III: Public Services","lv":1,"p":[102,135],"pp":"95","s":"Railroad cutbacks from coal shortage, communications finances, the electric power emergency and its board, public works, and low-cost and refugee housing."},{"t":"Section 1. Transportation","lv":2,"p":[102,112],"pp":"95","s":"Bituminous coal shortage forced removal of 58 trains in November; transport receipts fell 21 percent; creosoted ties shipped from Agasaki; marine revenue rose 44 percent."},{"t":"Railroads","lv":3,"p":[102,110],"pp":"95","s":"Rail income fell for the first time in five months; 49 passenger schedules cancelled, freight down 25,000 tons, fewer locomotive repairs, and briquette and power problems."},{"t":"Marine Transportation","lv":3,"p":[111,111],"pp":"104","s":"Marine income rose 44 percent partly from late manifests; ships to carry a 45-day food supply from 1 January 1948."},{"t":"Highway and Air Transportation","lv":3,"p":[112,112],"pp":"105","s":"Highway and air spending fell; 1,000 tires from Japan and automotive supplies expedited for the rice collection."},{"t":"Section 2. Communications","lv":2,"p":[113,122],"pp":"106","s":"Receipts rose 18 percent in November on higher telephone rates; expenditures up 8 million won; exchanges inspected for emergency power; 41,000,000 Japanese stamps ordered destroyed."},{"t":"Revenue and Expenditures","lv":3,"p":[113,115],"pp":"106","s":"Telephone income rose 7.3 million won, stamp sales dipped after higher rates, and spending rose on pole handling, supplies and interest."},{"t":"Employment","lv":3,"p":[116,117],"pp":"109","s":"Staff rose by 17 persons in November, all in telephone and telegraph; tables of employees and government income handed to Finance."},{"t":"Postal Deposits and Insurance","lv":3,"p":[118,119],"pp":"111","s":"Postal deposits fell while depositors rose slightly; book-transfer balances up 6 million won; insurance in force up 3 percent but policyholders declining."},{"t":"Telephone and Telegraph","lv":3,"p":[120,120],"pp":"113","s":"Telephone revenue 171 percent above September under the 1 October rate schedule; telegraph revenue high despite fewer messages."},{"t":"Activities","lv":3,"p":[120,122],"pp":"113","s":"Inspections of Seoul Central and Tong Dai Moon exchanges found faulty emergency generators; repeater technician course held 11-20 December; Pusan telegraph absenteeism noted."},{"t":"Section 3. Public Utilities","lv":2,"p":[123,128],"pp":"116","s":"North Korea limited power to 80,000 KW; a seven-member Emergency Electric Power Board with legal force set priorities; plants ran at about 20 percent capacity; 130-134 streetcars operated."},{"t":"Section 4. Public Works","lv":2,"p":[129,132],"pp":"122","s":"Man-days fell one-fourth and spending one-third; Andong and Namchang bridge projects inaugurated; 15 of 26 miles of Seoul-Suwon paving done; 248 fires caused 244.7 million won of losses."},{"t":"Section 5. Housing","lv":2,"p":[133,135],"pp":"126","s":"A 40-house low-cost project in Seoul was completed and occupied; 200 winterized tents and a 400-house refugee project nearly finished; refugee housing progress tabulated by province."},{"t":"Part IV: Finance","lv":1,"p":[136,153],"pp":"129","s":"Bank note issue, supplemental budgets and tax collection problems, bank deposits, rice-collection loans and insurance activity."},{"t":"Section 1. Government Finance","lv":2,"p":[136,142],"pp":"129","s":"Note issue at 33,388 million won; SKIG deficit of 2,747 million since October; 629 million won of supplemental appropriations approved; revenue only 65 percent of approved budget."},{"t":"Note Issue of the Bank of Chosun","lv":3,"p":[136,138],"pp":"129","s":"Circulation rose nearly 13 billion won since September because of rice collection; Civilian Supply accumulated 4,463 million won in deposits as a brake."},{"t":"Expenditure Budget and Revenue","lv":3,"p":[139,141],"pp":"132","s":"Eight supplemental requests approved by the Military Governor; November revenue 1,225.1 million won, down 524.6 million from October; revenue 65 percent of budget."},{"t":"Taxation","lv":3,"p":[142,142],"pp":"135","s":"Tax collection ran at 50-65 percent of projection; 30 employees finished an enforcement course on 27 December and provinces were radioed to speed collections."},{"t":"Civilian Supply Program","lv":3,"p":[142,142],"pp":"135","s":"Table 6 shows Civilian Supply receipts of 298 million won (May-Dec 1946) rising to a 4.46 billion won balance by December 1947; 75 million won sat in fixed deposits, the rest at the Bank of Chosun."},{"t":"Section 2. Non-Government Finance","lv":2,"p":[143,153],"pp":"136","s":"Deposits rose 2,569 million won in November; loans rose 16.2 billion won, mostly rice-collection credit; Finance directives on loan renewals and North Korean currency; life and damage insurance data."},{"t":"Cash Balances in Financial Institutions","lv":3,"p":[143,143],"pp":"136","s":"Cash balances in financial institutions are driven mainly by seasonal demand rather than investment policy; text cut off at page end, so no figures given."},{"t":"Deposits in Financial Institutions","lv":3,"p":[144,146],"pp":"137","s":"November deposits rose 2,569 million won, among the largest monthly rises since March 1946, including 1,302 million from the public."},{"t":"Loans by the Financial Institutions","lv":3,"p":[147,149],"pp":"140","s":"Loan balances rose 16.2 billion won, including 7.2 billion government-guaranteed and over 7 billion in Bank of Chosun rediscounts for rice collection; insurance tables."},{"t":"Activities of Financial Institutions","lv":3,"p":[150,150],"pp":"143","s":"Loans approved included 78,138 thousand won for agar-agar collection; directives covered loan renewal, North Korean cash, and a 3 billion won deposit campaign."},{"t":"Activities of Life Insurance Companies","lv":3,"p":[151,153],"pp":"144","s":"Four Korean life insurers reinsured 307 policies for 12.4 million won in November; 63,320 holders of Japanese policies registered; new household fire policy requested."},{"t":"Part V: Government and Politics","lv":1,"p":[154,181],"pp":"147","s":"Civil service and NEB actions, the Legislative Assembly, political realignment and the Chang assassination, legal affairs, police, and North-South interzone mail and Radio Pyongyang intercepts."},{"t":"Section 1. Administration","lv":2,"p":[154,156],"pp":"147","s":"Helmick-KILA vetoes resolved; Civil Service ran courses and examinations (4,108 clerical candidates); NEB approved 432.9 million won extra for Police and a 120 million won bonus fund."},{"t":"Section 2. Korean Interim Legislative Assembly","lv":2,"p":[157,163],"pp":"150","s":"Ten sessions averaged 66 members; theatrical law passed; no-confidence motion rejected 35 to 27; governors approved; land reform bill read; first anniversary on 12 December."},{"t":"Law Dealing with Public Theatrical Performances","lv":3,"p":[158,158],"pp":"151","s":"Second reading approved Sections I-XVIII giving the Education Director power over permits, restrictions and fines up to 50,000 won, replacing Government-General Order 197."},{"t":"Legislative-Executive Misunderstanding","lv":3,"p":[159,159],"pp":"152","s":"Helmick explained to the 180th session his non-concurrence in the Temporary Constitution and Anti-Collaborators Bill; the Assembly closed the issue by 41 to 9."},{"t":"Approval of Governors","lv":3,"p":[160,160],"pp":"153","s":"Rules amended 44 to 0 so a majority of a quorum approves appointments; governors of Kangwon-do and Chungchong-namdo then approved by secret ballot."},{"t":"Rice Collection","lv":3,"p":[160,160],"pp":"153","s":"Members Chong and Lee reported complaints of high quotas and the low 640-won purchase price; the Assembly took no action."},{"t":"Land Reform Bill","lv":3,"p":[161,161],"pp":"154","s":"First reading of a bill dividing holdings above 3 chungbo, with compensation, 15-year installments and price at three times average annual yield."},{"t":"Other Business","lv":3,"p":[161,163],"pp":"154","s":"Meatless Friday proposal, braille voting petition, failed no-confidence vote on the Outline of Measures, Public Act No. 8 delay, and anniversary speeches by Hodge and Helmick."},{"t":"Section 3. Political Developments","lv":2,"p":[164,169],"pp":"157","s":"National Independence Federation inaugurated 20 December with 15 parties; Rhee and Kim Koo merger stalled; parties split on a South-only election; Rhee attacked General Hodge."},{"t":"National Independence Federation","lv":3,"p":[164,166],"pp":"157","s":"Founded 20 December under Dr. Kimm Kiu Sic with five principles and six policies, including a North-South meeting and withdrawal of US and Soviet troops."},{"t":"Extreme Rightist Merger","lv":3,"p":[167,167],"pp":"160","s":"Rhee and Kim Koo announced unity on 1-5 December, but the joint meeting was postponed; the merger principle was restated on 27 December."},{"t":"United Nations Commission","lv":3,"p":[167,168],"pp":"160","s":"Rightists backed the UN Commission and a South election, leftists opposed it, and a reception committee under Police Director Chough was organized on 12 December."},{"t":"Council of Twelve Parties","lv":3,"p":[168,168],"pp":"161","s":"A 6 December preparatory meeting for the ninth session was never followed up; the Korean Independence Party expelled three executives and deferred joining."},{"t":"Statements by Rhee, Syngman","lv":3,"p":[169,169],"pp":"162","s":"Rhee criticized General Hodge on 5 and 16 December and demanded an immediate election, then approved Hodge's 17 December anti-Communist warning and quieted down."},{"t":"Section 4. Legal Affairs","lv":2,"p":[170,173],"pp":"163","s":"Ordinances 158-160 published; opinions on Labor Mediation Board powers; prison population 20,718; Dai Han Min Chung terrorism case moved to military court; Chang Duk Soo trial recommended for a commission."},{"t":"Section 5. Police","lv":2,"p":[174,176],"pp":"166","s":"Centralized police structure outlined; Chang Duk Soo's killers arrested 4 December; Chough ordered political neutrality; 170 arrested in Kyongsang-pukto over rice collection."},{"t":"Section 6. Interzone Activities","lv":2,"p":[177,181],"pp":"170","s":"Mail exchanged at Tosang on 13 and 27 December; Radio Pyongyang praised North Korea's currency conversion and rejected the UN decision; Radio Moscow attacked US policy and MacArthur."},{"t":"Part VI: Social","lv":1,"p":[182,218],"pp":"175","s":"Public health and welfare, the education budget, culture, public information, a press digest, and Korean National Youth growing to 404,585 members."},{"t":"Section 1. Public Health and Welfare","lv":2,"p":[182,190],"pp":"175","s":"National Venereal Disease Center opened 3 December; cholera down to 14 cases in 1947; nutrition survey of 4,676 families; 57 refugee-camp tents winterized; Christmas aid for 4,000 children."},{"t":"Preventive Medicine","lv":3,"p":[182,183],"pp":"175","s":"School tuberculin and X-ray survey began, venereal disease center opened, plague control and a rat school for 40, and typhus fell to 4 cases from 117."},{"t":"Veterinary Affairs","lv":3,"p":[183,184],"pp":"176","s":"Swine plague killed 6,123 pigs in six provinces (about 123 million won loss); 58 horses given to police; Kyonggi dairy cattle tested free of tuberculosis."},{"t":"Pharmaceutical Affairs","lv":3,"p":[184,184],"pp":"177","s":"2,856 narcotic licenses issued, 13 raids in Seoul with 15 arrests, five X-ray generators allocated and 30 carloads of supplies shipped."},{"t":"Laboratories","lv":3,"p":[184,187],"pp":"177","s":"Biologicals production tables for the Seoul and Anyang/Pusan veterinary institutes; 3,937,000 smallpox vaccinations produced; nutrition survey found farm families above 2,400 calories."},{"t":"Public Assistance","lv":3,"p":[188,189],"pp":"181","s":"Examined 320 blind and deaf pupils and 2,000 children in 17 Seoul institutions; nine border refugee camps reviewed; Welfare Housing Plan one-third complete."},{"t":"Korean Red Cross","lv":3,"p":[189,190],"pp":"182","s":"Ten chapters, five clinics and a 230-bed Seoul hospital; December disaster-relief and volunteer garment conferences; trucks lacking."},{"t":"Section 2. Education","lv":2,"p":[191,195],"pp":"184","s":"1948-49 education budget of 3,128,979,081 won, about 80 percent for common schools; advisors' conference 16-17 December; teacher certification from 1 January 1948; English institute with 594 students."},{"t":"Budget Estimate 1948-49","lv":3,"p":[191,192],"pp":"184","s":"Requested budget of 3,128,979,081 won against 1,660,242,480 in 1947-48, aimed at universal schooling for the first three grades."},{"t":"Bureau of Handicapped Children","lv":3,"p":[193,193],"pp":"186","s":"Blind and deaf schools enrolled 268 boys and 102 girls; equipment lacking; transfer to Education and a vocational committee for the blind."},{"t":"Teacher Certification","lv":3,"p":[193,194],"pp":"186","s":"New rules effective 1 January 1948 grant permanent certificates only after specified professional training, called a major achievement of the year."},{"t":"American Language Institute","lv":3,"p":[194,195],"pp":"187","s":"252 day and 342 evening students; Seoul private English schools to meet minimum standards; three teaching syllabuses distributed."},{"t":"Bureau of Research","lv":3,"p":[195,195],"pp":"188","s":"Lectures to the National Police College, probe of Rapid Realization Party adult schools, a school reporting plan, and test construction."},{"t":"Section 3. Culture","lv":2,"p":[196,201],"pp":"189","s":"Historical survey of Korean literature; National Library used 291,285 times in 1947; Korea Symphony Orchestra to stage La Traviata; KBS broadcast 337 hours; skaters left for St. Moritz."},{"t":"Korean Literature","lv":3,"p":[196,198],"pp":"189","s":"Survey from King Se-jo's 1487 Tong-moon-syen and the 1446 alphabet to Japanese suppression and current publishing despite paper shortage."},{"t":"National Library","lv":3,"p":[198,199],"pp":"191","s":"About 800 readers daily; 291,285 books used and 265,275 readers in 1947; library school of 30 students and rare Yi Kyu Bo volumes."},{"t":"Art and Music","lv":3,"p":[200,200],"pp":"193","s":"Symphony farewell concert for Ely Haimowitz on 2 December, Christmas programs, an ancient dance performance and a Kim Eun Ho exhibit."},{"t":"Korean Broadcasting System","lv":3,"p":[200,201],"pp":"193","s":"337 broadcast hours including Christmas programs, translated world dramas, and a 1,000-selection American transcription library."},{"t":"Miscellaneous","lv":3,"p":[201,201],"pp":"194","s":"Three skaters left 7 December for St. Moritz; Royal Asiatic Society reorganized 18 December; W. P. Kim returned from the IPR London conference."},{"t":"Section 4. Public Information","lv":2,"p":[202,207],"pp":"195","s":"Public Information issued 74 press releases including Dean's terrorism-case statement; Farmers' Weekly issues 86-88; a Nonsan Mobile Education train found only 35.8 percent saw Americans as beneficial."},{"t":"Bureau of Public Information","lv":3,"p":[202,205],"pp":"195","s":"Special releases on terrorism cases, the UN Commission, power conservation and rice collection; Farmers' Weekly issues No. 86, 87 and 88 published."},{"t":"Bureau of Public Opinion","lv":3,"p":[205,205],"pp":"198","s":"Registered 4 parties and 9 publications, suspended 3 publications, ran six opinion surveys and 11 lectures."},{"t":"Office of Civil Information","lv":3,"p":[206,207],"pp":"199","s":"Mobile Education train visited Nonsan 6-8 December distributing 4,000 Farmers' Weekly; 99 photo panels and 133 cartons of books sent out."},{"t":"Section 5. Press and Public Opinion","lv":2,"p":[208,215],"pp":"201","s":"Korean press digest on the Helmick-KILA dispute, Dean's military-court decision, Rhee's attacks, the National Independence Federation, the Chang murder, editor arrests and a women's alliance poll."},{"t":"The Deputy Military Governor and KILA","lv":3,"p":[208,209],"pp":"201","s":"Papers printed KILA's questions on Helmick's vetoes and his reply; rightist papers split on dissolving KILA."},{"t":"The Military Governor and the Courts","lv":3,"p":[209,211],"pp":"202","s":"Full text of Dean's 1 December statement transferring the Dai Han Min Chung case to a Military Court, with press reactions."},{"t":"Maneuvers of Political Parties","lv":3,"p":[211,211],"pp":"204","s":"Rhee-Kim Koo accord reported then collapsed; the 12-14 December joint meeting was cancelled after the Chang assassination."},{"t":"National Independence Federation","lv":3,"p":[211,212],"pp":"204","s":"Press coverage of the new moderate coalition, Rhee's sneer, and the 20 December meeting."},{"t":"Murder of Chang, Duk Soo","lv":3,"p":[212,212],"pp":"205","s":"Assassination of the Democratic Party strategist overshadowed other news; Hodge promised military trials for such cases on 27 December."},{"t":"Police and the Press","lv":3,"p":[212,213],"pp":"205","s":"Police roundups, 117 communications workers arrested, 46 officials charged with graft, and arrests of editors of Hyun Dai Ilbo and Dai Han Ilbo."},{"t":"Economic Matters","lv":3,"p":[213,214],"pp":"206","s":"Papers blamed Military Government for inflation, protested imports, and debated the power shortage and North Korean currency change."},{"t":"Other Topics","lv":3,"p":[214,214],"pp":"207","s":"Coverage of the Chinese UN Commission member's arrival, the failed London ministers' conference, and local matters such as rat-caused fires."},{"t":"Public Opinion Poll","lv":3,"p":[214,215],"pp":"207","s":"Women's Alliance poll: 106 favored and 80 opposed a North-South leaders' meeting; 125 wanted the new Military Governor to check inflation."},{"t":"Section 6. Korean National Youth, Inc.","lv":2,"p":[216,218],"pp":"209","s":"Membership reached 404,585 in December; Lee Bum Suk and Lee Chawn Chun of Dai Dong issued a joint truce statement on 11 December; 11,843,113 seedlings requisitioned for planting."}],"by":"claude-sonnet (Claude Code subagent)"},{"id":"K-27-in-K-26","ia":"south-korean-interim-government-activities-no-26-1947-11","s":"K","no":27,"d":"1947-12","d2":"","t":"South Korean Interim Government Activities, No. 27 (December 1947)","v":"Bound in K-26","host":"K-26","rg":[[268,495]],"kind":"bound","found":"Title page 'M 105.13:27' (OCR: 108/13:27) on PDF p. 268","pg":228,"o":"Report No. 27 of South Korean Interim Government Activities, prepared by the National Economic Board for USAMGIK, covering December 1947 (with November data for transportation, communications, finance and several other sections). It records a month dominated by an acute electric power shortage after North Korea cut supply on 25 November, falling coal, textile and paper output, and food supply concerns despite rice collection reaching 96.2 per cent of quota. Politically it covers the assassination of Chang Duk Soo, the founding of the National Independence Federation, Rhee and Kim Koo's rightist merger, preparation for the UN Commission, and Legislative Assembly disputes with the executive. Tables give prices, trade, finance, refugees (163,253 entered in 1947), and vested property disposal, while the interzone section summarizes North Korean and Soviet radio. Historians will find it useful for the economic statistics and the occupation's own view of politics.","h":["North Korea cut electric power on 25 November 1947, capping South Korea's draw at 80,000 KW; December deliveries fell to 68 million KWH, and an Emergency Electric Power Board was set up with legal powers and heating bans.","Rice collection reached 96.2 per cent of quota by late December 1947 (232,213 metric tons collected), though Cholla-pukto lagged at 77 per cent and imports of 10,000-34,000 tons monthly are needed to last past July 1948.","Seoul's composite retail price index stood at 1,936 in December 1947, over 19 times the August 1945 base, while the wholesale index rose to 7,066, driven by coal and power shortages and rice-collection loans.","Chang Duk Soo, Korean Democratic Party head, was assassinated on 3 December; police were ordered out of politics, youth groups restrained, and Gen. Hodge and Military Governor Dean issued statements.","The moderate National Independence Federation of 15 parties held its first meeting on 20 December under Kimm Kiu Sic, while the announced Rhee Syngman and Kim Koo merger stalled.","The power shortage cut output: November cotton yarn fell 13.2 per cent, paper 22.2 per cent and flour 24.3 per cent in vested mills, mines ran about 18 per cent under quota, and 58 trains were removed.","Ordinance No. 160 created the Agricultural Improvement Service on 15 December, and the Legislative Assembly marked its first anniversary on 12 December after settling its veto dispute with Gen. Helmick.","163,253 refugees from North Korea were counted in 1947 against 185,441 in 1946; December's 4,487 was the lowest since February 1947, and about 60 per cent settled in Seoul or Kyonggi-do."],"k":["South Korean Interim Government","USAMGIK","December 1947","power shortage","rice collection","vested property","prices","refugees","National Economic Board","Legislative Assembly","Rhee Syngman","Kim Koo","UN Commission","Korean National Youth","imports and exports"],"toc":[{"t":"Front matter","lv":1,"p":[268,277],"pp":"","s":"Cover of Report No. 27 (December 1947), prepared by the National Economic Board for USAMGIK, with a map of the U.S. and U.S.S.R. zones and contents for Parts I-VI."},{"t":"Introduction","lv":1,"p":[278,279],"pp":"1","s":"Notes that most data are for December, but transportation, communications, industrial production, mining and trade carry November figures. Admits provincial statistics are unreliable, non-economic data thin, and the report largely American-authored."},{"t":"Part I: Basic Aspects","lv":1,"p":[280,293],"pp":"1","s":"Covers refugees from North Korea (163,253 in 1947) and Office of Property Custody work, including 285 companies under investigation and 16 vested-property sales worth 5,205 thousand won."},{"t":"Section 1: Population","lv":2,"p":[280,287],"pp":"3","s":"December refugee arrivals from the north were 4,487, the lowest since February 1947; the 1947 total was 163,253 against 185,441 in 1946. About 60 per cent went to Seoul or Kyonggi-do. Also 21,916 illegal Japan entrants."},{"t":"Section 2: Office of Property Custody (Vested Property)","lv":2,"p":[288,293],"pp":"11","s":"A Corporate Reorganization Sub-section began, with 285 companies under investigation. Loans guaranteed reached 235 million won; 16 sales totaled 5,205 thousand won. Royal Family estates pass to SKIG control from 1 January 1948."},{"t":"Part II: Production and Supply","lv":1,"p":[294,371],"pp":"17","s":"Rice collection reached 96.2 per cent of quota; Seoul's retail price index hit 1,936. Mining, textile and paper output fell amid power shortages, while 1947 civilian supply imports totaled 988,344 tons and 193 unions reported 58,556 members."},{"t":"Section 1: Food Supply","lv":2,"p":[294,301],"pp":"17","s":"232,213 metric tons of rice were collected, 35,038 distributed and 586,419 held. Collection reached 96.2 per cent of quota (Cholla-pukto 77 per cent). Imports of 10,000-34,000 tons monthly are needed to avoid exhausting stocks by July 1948."},{"t":"Section 2: Prices","lv":2,"p":[302,312],"pp":"25","s":"Seoul's retail price index stood at 1,936 in December 1947, over 19 times August 1945; the wholesale index rose to 7,066. Tobacco prices rose 50-233 per cent on 15 December."},{"t":"Section 3: Agriculture, Forestry, and Fisheries","lv":2,"p":[313,321],"pp":"36","s":"Sets 1948 goals of 3,211,844 chungbo cropped, with 1947 rice at 81.6 per cent of goal. Sericulture was cut by power shortages; 220,953 tons of fertilizer shipped. Ordinance 160 created the Agricultural Improvement Service on 15 December."},{"t":"Section 4: Mining and Minerals","lv":2,"p":[322,333],"pp":"45","s":"November anthracite output fell 5.3 per cent from October; mines ran about 18 per cent under quota amid power, truck and tire shortages. Only 64,823 tons of bituminous came from Japan. Copper mines owed 42,208,455 won."},{"t":"Section 5: Industrial Production","lv":2,"p":[334,342],"pp":"57","s":"Reports covered under 10 per cent of 2,012 factories. November flour fell 24.3 per cent, cotton yarn 13.2 per cent, cloth 15.7 per cent, paper 22.2 per cent and cigarettes 15 per cent; coal, pitch and power were short."},{"t":"Section 6: Allocations and Distribution","lv":2,"p":[343,355],"pp":"66","s":"Surplus Army clothing is to be dyed at the Chosun Boseki plant at Pusanjin. The Federation of Financial Associations distributed 89,820,257 won of goods, and an emergency import of about 2,000,000 gallons of kerosene was ordered."},{"t":"Section 7: Imports and Exports","lv":2,"p":[356,367],"pp":"79","s":"Private imports in November were 463,123,673 won against exports of 107,700,696 won; Pusan handled 48.2 per cent of imports. About 570 tons of scheelite sailed for the U.S. Civilian Supply imports in 1947 totaled 988,344 tons."},{"t":"Section 8: Labor","lv":2,"p":[368,371],"pp":"91","s":"The Department of Labor planned a four-bureau reorganization. Power-shortage orders suspended child labor and hours laws. The Kyongsang Textile dispute over seven discharged union men stalled; 193 unions had 58,556 members."},{"t":"Part III: Public Services","lv":1,"p":[372,405],"pp":"95","s":"Railroads removed 58 trains in November for lack of coal; North Korea's 25 November power cut limited deliveries to 68 million KWH in December. Also covers communications, public works and the 40-house Seoul housing project."},{"t":"Section 1: Transportation","lv":2,"p":[372,382],"pp":"95","s":"Lack of bituminous coal forced removal of 58 trains in November. Department receipts fell 21 per cent and expenditures 12 per cent; under 38 per cent of locomotives were operable. Marine income rose 44 per cent."},{"t":"Section 2: Communications","lv":2,"p":[383,392],"pp":"106","s":"Communications receipts rose 18 per cent in November, led by a 7.3 million won rise in telephone income after the 1 October rate increase. About 41,000,000 Japanese stamps were ordered destroyed."},{"t":"Section 3: Public Utilities","lv":2,"p":[393,398],"pp":"116","s":"After North Korea's 25 November notice capping South Korea's draw at 80,000 KW, power delivered fell to 68 million KWH in December (52 million from the north). An Emergency Electric Power Board set priorities and banned heating and cooking use."},{"t":"Section 4: Public Works","lv":2,"p":[399,402],"pp":"122","s":"December was the low point for Public Works: man-days fell to 72,633 and wages to 14.5 million won. Andong and Namchang bridge projects began, and 248 fires cost 244.7 million won."},{"t":"Section 5: Housing","lv":2,"p":[403,405],"pp":"126","s":"The 40-house low-cost Seoul project was finished and occupied; a site for 1,000 houses is planned. Refugee houses under the Welfare Housing Plan reached 5,736 of 15,730 planned, and 200 winterized tents were occupied."},{"t":"Part IV: Finance","lv":1,"p":[406,423],"pp":"129","s":"Bank of Chosun note issue reached 33,388 million won; November deposits rose 2,569 million and loans 16.2 billion, mostly for rice collection. Eight supplemental appropriations of 629 million won raised the budget to 20,089 million."},{"t":"Section 1: Government Finance","lv":2,"p":[406,412],"pp":"129","s":"Note issue reached 33,388 million won, driven by rice-collection loans of 11,772 million. Eight supplemental appropriations of 629 million won raised the budget to 20,089 million. November revenue was 1,225 million won."},{"t":"Section 2: Non-Government Finance","lv":2,"p":[413,423],"pp":"136","s":"November deposits rose 2,569 million won and loans 16.2 billion, mostly for rice collection. Directives tightened loan renewals; a deposit drive added 2.5 billion won. A scheelite shipment near US$750,000 was sent."},{"t":"Part V: Government and Politics","lv":1,"p":[424,451],"pp":"147","s":"Covers the Legislative Assembly's dispute with Gen. Helmick over vetoes, the 3 December assassination of Chang Duk Soo, the 20 December National Independence Federation, the stalled Rhee-Kim Koo merger and interzone radio."},{"t":"Section 1: Administration","lv":2,"p":[424,426],"pp":"147","s":"Legislative Assembly relations with Deputy Military Governor Helmick over his vetoes were stormy, then smoothed. Civil Service held 4,108 clerical examinations and abolished 383 Seoul positions."},{"t":"Section 2: Korean Interim Legislative Assembly","lv":2,"p":[427,433],"pp":"150","s":"KILA settled its dispute with Gen. Helmick over vetoed bills and approved governors of Kangwon-do and Chungchong-namdo. The Land Reform Bill (3 chungbo ceiling) had its first reading; a no-confidence motion failed 35 to 27."},{"t":"Section 3: Political Developments","lv":2,"p":[434,439],"pp":"157","s":"The moderate National Independence Federation of 15 parties was founded 20 December under Kimm Kiu Sic. Rhee Syngman and Kim Koo announced a merger in principle that stalled; parties split over a South-only election."},{"t":"Section 4: Legal Affairs","lv":2,"p":[440,443],"pp":"163","s":"Ordinances 158-160 and Executive Order No. 9 were published. Prison population stood at 20,718. A rightist terrorism case went to a military commission and the Chang Duk Soo assassination was referred."},{"t":"Section 5: Police","lv":2,"p":[444,446],"pp":"167","s":"After Chang Duk Soo's assassination on 3 December, police were ordered to stay out of politics and youth groups were restrained. Rice-collection resisters were arrested; weekly fingerprint requests fell from about 5,000 to 1,760-2,889."},{"t":"Section 6: Interzone Activities","lv":2,"p":[447,451],"pp":"170","s":"Mail was exchanged at Tosang on 13 and 27 December. Radio Pyongyang hailed North Korea's currency conversion and rejected the UN decision; Radio Moscow denounced the UN Commission and MacArthur's Japan policy."},{"t":"Part VI: Social","lv":1,"p":[452,488],"pp":"175","s":"Covers health, education, culture, public information, press and Korean National Youth, including a 3,128,979,081-won education budget request and youth membership of 404,585 by December 1947."},{"t":"Section 1: Public Health and Welfare","lv":2,"p":[452,460],"pp":"175","s":"The National Venereal Disease Center opened in Seoul on 3 December with 65 beds. Typhus fell from 117 cases to 4 and typhoid from 239 to 24 against December 1946. A nutrition survey covered 4,676 families."},{"t":"Section 2: Education","lv":2,"p":[461,465],"pp":"184","s":"The 1948-49 budget request was 3,128,979,081 won, nearly 80 percent for common schools, against 1,660,242,480 won the year before. Provincial advisors met 16-17 December on school taxation; teacher certification rules start 1 January 1948."},{"t":"Section 3: Culture","lv":2,"p":[466,471],"pp":"189","s":"The National Library logged 291,285 books used and 265,275 readers in 1947. Seven Koreans left to study in the United States and four in Nanking; the Korean Broadcasting System aired 337 hours."},{"t":"Section 4: Public Information","lv":2,"p":[472,477],"pp":"195","s":"The Department issued 74 press releases in December, including statements by Dean and Hodge on the Chang Duk Soo murder. The Public Opinion Bureau registered 4 parties and 9 publications; a Nonsan trip found 35.8 percent saw U.S. benefit."},{"t":"Section 5: Press and Public Opinion","lv":2,"p":[478,485],"pp":"201","s":"The press covered the Helmick-KILA veto dispute, Dean's transfer of the Dai Han Min Chung terror case to a military court, and the failed Rhee-Kim Koo accord. The Chang Duk Soo murder overshadowed all, prompting press curbs."},{"t":"Section 6: Korean National Youth, Inc.","lv":2,"p":[486,488],"pp":"209","s":"Membership grew from 19,270 in January to 404,585 in December 1947, with 15,304 leaders. Lee Bum Suk and Dai Dong's leader issued an 11 December appeal against mutual slander; 11,843,113 seedlings were planted."},{"t":"Back matter","lv":1,"p":[489,495],"pp":"","s":"Blank scanned pages marked as digitized by Google, a library barcode and date-due slip stamped 23 June 2020, and a back cover image. No report content."}],"by":"claude-sonnet (Claude Code subagent)"},{"id":"J-28","ia":"summation-non-military-activities-japan-no-28-1948-01","s":"J","no":28,"d":"1948-01","d2":"","t":"Summation of Non-Military Activities in Japan, No. 28 (January 1948)","v":"","pg":367,"o":"Summation No. 28 covers January 1948 (with many statistics for November-December 1947). Politically, MacArthur's New Year message, the Katayama coalition's troubles (Social Democratic convention abrogating the four-party pact, Hirano's purge and secession, the arrest of Susumu Hara), abolition of the Home Ministry and the opening of the Second Diet session dominate. The Tokyo war crimes trial reaches the close of the defense, with Tojo's and Shimada's testimony printed at length. Economic sections report record postwar coal output (2,953,200 tons in December), steady gains in industry and textiles limited by power shortages, first postwar export shipments, the 'substitute rice' program, currency and bank totals, and first machine-tool reparations shipments to China and the Netherlands East Indies. Social sections cover epidemic control, public assistance, repatriation, school enrollments and textbook printing, the war-damage survey of art and structures, and press, radio and film activity. A historian will find detailed month-by-month production, trade, labor, finance and health statistics alongside primary-source speeches and trial testimony.","h":["On 1 January MacArthur's New Year message said the design of a reconstructed Japan was nearing completion and urged the Japanese to 'remain true to the great spiritual revolution'; the Asahi editorial replied that much renovation remained.","The Social Democratic convention voted 366 to 345 on 19 January to abrogate the four-party policy agreement and re-elected Katayama chairman; Rikizo Hirano and 15 colleagues had seceded on 5 January, cutting the party to 124 House seats.","Liberal Representative Susumu Hara was arrested on 31 January for illegal public property transactions after the House Management Committee agreed on 29 January; Hirano was purged on 14 January and indicted on 20 January.","The Second Diet Session opened 21 January with the Emperor present; on 22 January Prime Minister Katayama called 1948 the year of economic reconstruction and promised administrative reorganization, real-wage increases and state-managed elections.","Home Ministry successors (Construction Board, Local Finance Committee, Domestic Affairs Bureau, National Election Management Commission) began operating; the Central Liaison Office was to be abolished 1 February, and 1,146 municipalities had autonomous police by 25 January.","At the International Military Tribunal for the Far East the defense rested on 12 January, with Hideki Tojo's and Shigetaro Shimada's testimony reproduced; ten trials of 29 suspects ended with nine death sentences.","Coal production hit an Occupation peak of 2,953,200 metric tons in December; first postwar exports included a Toyopet automobile to Egypt, raw silk to Egypt and India, and cloisonne and lacquerware to the United States.","The first machine-tool reparations advance transfers left in January: 974 tools (1,694 tons) to China and 600 tools (1,440 tons) to the Netherlands East Indies; currency in circulation reached 218,049,000,000 yen on 31 January."],"k":["Summation January 1948","SCAP","Katayama Cabinet","Social Democratic Party","Hirano purge","Home Ministry dissolution","Second Diet session","Tojo defense","Tokyo war crimes trial","coal production","textile industry","foreign trade","reparations","labor unions","public health","education reform","Media of expression"],"toc":[{"t":"Front matter","lv":1,"p":[1,13],"pp":"","s":"Google Books and library covers, title page for Summation No. 28 (January 1948), table of contents, and the list of 109 charts, maps and graphs."},{"t":"Part I: General","lv":1,"p":[14,33],"pp":"1","s":"Monthly digest: no change in SCAP organization, Hirano purge and Diet opening, record coal output, first postwar export shipments, reparations shipments, epidemic control and press activity."},{"t":"Organization Under SCAP","lv":2,"p":[16,16],"pp":"3","s":"GHQ SCAP's non-military organization did not change in January."},{"t":"Political Activities","lv":2,"p":[16,19],"pp":"3","s":"Digest of New Year messages, Hara's arrest, Hirano's purge, the Social Democratic convention, abolition of the Home Ministry, the Second Diet session and 192 atrocity investigations."},{"t":"Economic Activities","lv":2,"p":[20,29],"pp":"7","s":"Digest of agriculture, record coal output of 2,953,200 tons, industrial indexes, first postwar exports, the 'substitute rice' program, bank totals, 974 machine tools to China, and science items."},{"t":"Social Activities","lv":2,"p":[30,33],"pp":"17","s":"Digest of typhus immunization of 1,300,000 people, a 71 percent drop in dysentery, 17,682,938 pupils, textbook printing, 1,130 missionaries, the art war-damage survey and the Osaka SCAP library."},{"t":"Part II: Political","lv":1,"p":[34,91],"pp":"21","s":"Government and politics, public safety and legal affairs, including the war crimes trials with Tojo's and Shimada's testimony reproduced."},{"t":"Government and Politics","lv":2,"p":[36,65],"pp":"23","s":"MacArthur's New Year message, Hara's arrest and Hirano's purge, the Social Democratic split over the four-party pact, Home Ministry successors, the Kochi governor election and Katayama's 22 January Diet speech."},{"t":"Political Developments","lv":3,"p":[36,46],"pp":"23","s":"MacArthur's and Asahi's New Year statements, hoarded goods drive, Susumu Hara's arrest, and Hirano's purge, indictment and new National Farmers' Union Diet Club."},{"t":"Political Parties","lv":3,"p":[47,53],"pp":"","s":"Social Democratic left-wing revolt, convention vote of 366 to 345 for abrogation, Liberal plan for a New Party Formation Preparatory Committee, and Democratic 10-point program."},{"t":"National Government","lv":3,"p":[54,55],"pp":"","s":"Plan to abolish the Central Liaison Office on 1 February, new Reparations Board and Repatriation Division, and the 15 January personnel-reduction plan."},{"t":"Local Government","lv":3,"p":[56,56],"pp":"","s":"Naomi Momoi won the Kochi governor election 221,475 to 93,100; Supreme Court on 17 January confirmed Tadao Annaka as Miyazaki governor."},{"t":"The Diet","lv":3,"p":[57,65],"pp":"","s":"Second Session opened 21 January with the Emperor; Katayama's 22 January policy speech, Nagasaki by-election won by Democrat Kadoya, and party vote tables."},{"t":"Public Safety","lv":2,"p":[66,71],"pp":"53","s":"Hoarded goods worth 10,680,325 yen found in December, 162,404 offenses in November, prison population down to 79,913, 458 rail mishaps and sharply higher fires, with charts."},{"t":"Legal Affairs and War Crimes","lv":2,"p":[72,91],"pp":"59","s":"532 judges appointed in December, 192 atrocity investigations, ten trials with nine death sentences, and the IMTFE defense including Tojo's and Shimada's testimony."},{"t":"Legal Affairs","lv":3,"p":[72,72],"pp":"59","s":"1,414 inferior-court judges appointed to date, Supreme Court caseload, and instructions on private attorney interviews for accused persons."},{"t":"Investigation and Apprehension of Suspected War Criminals","lv":3,"p":[73,73],"pp":"","s":"192 atrocity investigations completed between 24 December and 24 January, and status of apprehensions of suspects."},{"t":"Prosecution of War Criminals","lv":3,"p":[74,75],"pp":"","s":"Ten trials of 29 suspected war criminals completed in January: nine sentenced to death, four acquitted, the rest imprisoned."},{"t":"International Prosecution of War Criminals","lv":3,"p":[76,91],"pp":"","s":"The defense rested on 12 January, the tribunal ruled on evidence on 14 January, and long excerpts of Tojo's and Shimada's testimony follow."},{"t":"Defense of Hideki Tojo","lv":3,"p":[76,84],"pp":"","s":"Tojo's defense, 26 Dec–7 Jan: he accepts political but not criminal responsibility, describes the 1941 Imperial Conferences and the Hull Note, calls the war self-defense, and in cross-examination denies war is a crime."},{"t":"Testimony of Shigetaro Shimada","lv":3,"p":[85,91],"pp":"","s":"Shimada, recalled 9 January, denied Togo's claim of a threat at Ichigaya; he said Togo's testimony reflected guilt, and blamed the Foreign Office for the late Pearl Harbor note (delivered 2:10 p.m.)."},{"t":"Part III: Economic","lv":1,"p":[92,317],"pp":"79","s":"Production, trade, labor, price, finance, property control and science statistics for December 1947 and January 1948, including record coal output of 2,953,200 tons and 218,049,000,000 yen in currency."},{"t":"Agriculture and Fisheries","lv":2,"p":[94,99],"pp":"81","s":"44,169 cho of land sold to tenants in December, milk and marine landings, fishing vessel and petroleum allocations, flood damage and an antarctic whaling catch of 354.5 blue whale units."},{"t":"Forestry and Mining","lv":2,"p":[100,113],"pp":"87","s":"Log production of 54,587,020 cubic feet, forest survey (19,687,243 accessible hectares), record coal output of 2,953,200 tons, crude oil and mineral production."},{"t":"Heavy Industries","lv":2,"p":[114,135],"pp":"101","s":"Coke up 8,660 tons, cement 132,699 tons, plus steel, shipbuilding, chemicals, machine tools, locomotives, rolling stock and textile machinery output tables."},{"t":"Manufacturing","lv":2,"p":[136,157],"pp":"123","s":"Trucks up 76 percent and a postwar high in sewing machines, with food, alcohol, tobacco, paper, glass, optical and medical instruments, rubber, leather and agricultural implements."},{"t":"Textile Industries","lv":2,"p":[158,189],"pp":"145","s":"Cotton, silk, rayon and wool output limited by electric power; raw silk stocks of 110,857 bales; cloth, spindle, loom and knit goods tables."},{"t":"Transportation and Public Utilities","lv":2,"p":[190,201],"pp":"177","s":"Commercial vehicle operating ratio down one percent, railway freight down about five percent, coastal cargo down 9.5 percent, electric power generation up three percent, gas consumption up 13.7 percent."},{"t":"Communications","lv":2,"p":[202,221],"pp":"189","s":"8 January radio receiver standards ordinance, radiotelegraph rates, postal insurance maximum raised to 25,000 yen, equipment output, and a December Ministry deficit of 347,011,466 yen."},{"t":"Labor","lv":2,"p":[222,235],"pp":"209","s":"26,421 unions with 5,993,613 members on 30 November, calm January labor relations, government and coal worker wage talks, a labor press of over 100 publications, and employment statistics."},{"t":"Imports and Exports","lv":2,"p":[236,265],"pp":"223","s":"December trade tables; first postwar exports including a Toyopet to Egypt; 1947 totals of 360,892,000 yards of cotton cloth and 759,093 tons of salt imported."},{"t":"Rationing and Price Control","lv":2,"p":[266,281],"pp":"253","s":"Staple-food purchases and quota fulfillment, the 'substitute rice' program begun in January, December sugar distribution of 22,394 tons, tobacco price increases and ten rationed commodities."},{"t":"Finance","lv":2,"p":[282,295],"pp":"269","s":"Currency of 218,049,000,000 yen, Reconstruction Finance Bank loans of 44,210,356,000 yen, national debt of 374,957,000,000 yen, plus bank deposits, liquidation proceeds, tax collections and local finance."},{"t":"Property Control and Reparations","lv":2,"p":[296,309],"pp":"283","s":"1,813,965,000 yen of neutral and Allied property reported, German property freeze lifted for nonobjectionable nationals, 4,371 companies under holding company control, and reparations shipments."},{"t":"Science and Technology","lv":2,"p":[310,317],"pp":"297","s":"Renewal Committee on science organization, 6,563,600 yen in grants to 19 institutes, 46 patents granted, and Rebun Island eclipse expedition plans."},{"t":"Part IV: Social","lv":1,"p":[318,363],"pp":"305","s":"Public health and welfare, then education, religion, cultural resources and media of expression, including typhus immunization of 1,300,000 and 17,682,938 pupils enrolled."},{"t":"Public Health and Welfare","lv":2,"p":[320,339],"pp":"307","s":"Typhus immunization of over 1,300,000, dysentery down 71 percent, communicable disease tables, milk inspection, medical supplies, narcotics, public assistance, repatriation and vital statistics."},{"t":"Education, Religion, Cultural Resources and Media of Expression","lv":2,"p":[340,363],"pp":"327","s":"17,682,938 pupils enrolled, 141,790,968 textbooks printed, 1,130 foreign missionaries, 1.5 percent of art objects and 13 percent of structures war damaged, plus press, radio, film and library news."},{"t":"Map of Japan and back matter","lv":1,"p":[364,367],"pp":"351","s":"Map of Japan showing administrative regions and prefectures, followed by library catalogue label and back covers."}],"by":"claude-sonnet (Claude Code subagent)"},{"id":"K-28","ia":"south-korean-interim-government-activities-no-28-1948-01","s":"K","no":28,"d":"1948-01","d2":"","t":"South Korean Interim Government Activities, No. 28 (January 1948)","v":"","pg":499,"o":"South Korean Interim Government Activities No. 28, prepared by the National Economic Board, covers January 1948 for USAMGIK and the South Korean Interim Government, with some economic data for December 1947. The month was dominated by the arrival of the UN Temporary Commission on Korea, which shaped politics, the press and the Legislative Assembly. Economic reporting shows rice collection at 96.9 per cent of quota, falling coal, power and industrial output, rising prices (Seoul retail index 2,231) and a 60-fold electric rate increase, while note issue fell 1.77 billion won from its peak. Executive Order No. 12 reorganized consumer-goods allocation. Refugee inflow from the north dropped 45 per cent. A new section covers Women's Activities. Historians will find detailed price, trade, finance, labor, legislative and press-opinion material, including strikes, a rightist merger, North Korean radio propaganda, and public opinion polls on forced contributions.","h":["The 1947/48 rice collection reached 749,313 metric tons, 96.9 per cent of the 773,477-ton quota, with Cholla-pukto lowest at 80.2 per cent; four months of undelivered rations were issued in January.","Executive Order No. 12 (12 January, signed by Maj. Gen. William F. Dean) created the Korean Government Employees Welfare Office and the seven-member Consumer Goods Allocation Board, abolishing the Civil Service Relief Committee.","Refugees entering South Korea from the north numbered 2,541 in January, down 45 per cent from December; the cumulative count since 1945 stood at 803,434, with 1,264,530 Koreans repatriated from abroad.","The Seoul retail price index (August 1945 = 100) reached 2,231 in January 1948 against 1,916 in December; on 8 January the Permanent Public Utilities Board recommended a roughly 60-fold rise in retail electric rates.","Anthracite output fell to 31,333 tons in December (12.2 per cent below November), 26.2 per cent short of quota, and bituminous imports from Japan reached only 44,452 tons against an expected 60,000.","Bank of Chosun notes outstanding fell 1,767,000,000 won in January from the December peak as the rice collection passed its high point; supplementary appropriations of over 601,000,000 won were approved.","Labor unrest included a Yong Dong Po strike at 12 factories (Kyungsung Textile, about 10,000 workers) and a Seoul streetcar walkout of 259 employees; the Tai Han No Chung Federation, 864 delegates, re-elected Chun Jin-han.","The UN Temporary Commission dominated politics: the Legislative Assembly often lacked a quorum, Kim Koo and Rhee Syngman merged rightist groups on 9 January, and Kim Koo urged troop withdrawal and a North-South leaders' conference."],"k":["South Korean Interim Government","USAMGIK","National Economic Board","UN Temporary Commission on Korea","rice collection","vested property","consumer goods allocation","electric rate increase","coal shortage","Korean Interim Legislative Assembly","refugees from North Korea","Korean National Youth","inflation and note issue","Civilian Supply Program","labor disputes"],"toc":[{"t":"Front matter","lv":1,"p":[1,15],"pp":"","s":"Google Books pages, cover of No. 28 (January 1948) prepared by the National Economic Board, map legend, and contents for Parts I-VI."},{"t":"Introduction","lv":1,"p":[16,17],"pp":"1","s":"Editors' overview of winter 1947-48: falling industrial output and coal, the Bank of Chosun note-issue peak, social progress, new graphic tables and the new Women's Activities section."},{"t":"Part I: Basic Aspects","lv":1,"p":[18,29],"pp":"3","s":"Population movement, Koreans abroad, 10,420 foreign residents, and vested property work, including Koreanization of companies and 30,263,000 won in January sales."},{"t":"Section 1. Population and Foreign Affairs","lv":2,"p":[18,24],"pp":"3","s":"Southbound refugees fell to 2,541; 508,190 Koreans in Japan, with ten League of Korea members deported after the 20 December Tokyo riot; 419 Koreans travelled abroad; 10,420 foreigners resident."},{"t":"Section 2. Vested Property","lv":2,"p":[25,29],"pp":"10","s":"Office of Property Custody sold 39 properties for 30,263,000 won, audited 374 companies, pursued ghost companies, and held former Government General property for the successor Korean government."},{"t":"General","lv":3,"p":[25,25],"pp":"10","s":"More than 20 per cent of vested industrial properties operating; almost half of ghost companies deactivated or rehabilitated; 30,000,000 won of enterprises passed to Korean management."},{"t":"Operations","lv":3,"p":[25,26],"pp":"10","s":"Re-organization unit completed 129 preliminary investigations; six loan applications totalled 212,000,000 won, led by Korean Forwarding Co.; 1,381 financial statements received; over 40 ghost companies investigated."},{"t":"Property Disposal","lv":3,"p":[27,27],"pp":"12","s":"Thirty-nine sales yielded 30,263,000 won, including five ships (7,993,000 won) and 11 residences; residence sales were delayed by appraisal difficulties."},{"t":"Audits and Inventories","lv":3,"p":[27,27],"pp":"12","s":"Audits of 374 companies were in progress at 31 January; reports on 10 major companies were completed."},{"t":"Real Estate Requisitions","lv":3,"p":[27,27],"pp":"12","s":"Twenty-six requisitions received and 15 releases effected for formerly Japanese property used by Occupation Forces; leases negotiated for adjoining Korean tracts."},{"t":"Provincial Affairs","lv":3,"p":[28,28],"pp":"13","s":"Sixty-six field inspection trips checked inventory progress toward the 30 June 1948 deadline; transport was the main bottleneck and 400 bicycles were bought."},{"t":"Policies and Decisions","lv":3,"p":[29,29],"pp":"14","s":"General Counsel policy: property registered to the Government General of Korea on 9 August 1945 will not be sold without approval of the owning government agency."},{"t":"Part II: Production and Supply","lv":1,"p":[30,123],"pp":"15","s":"Food, prices, agriculture, mining, industry, allocations, trade and labor: rice collection 96.9 per cent, Seoul price index 2,231, coal shortfalls, Executive Order No. 12, 1947 trade totals, strikes."},{"t":"Section 1. Food Supply","lv":2,"p":[30,34],"pp":"15","s":"Rice collection reached 749,313 tons (96.9 per cent of quota); stocks 636,757 tons; 76,586 tons rationed; first sugar (16,127 tons) and dried milk imports arrived."},{"t":"Summary","lv":3,"p":[30,30],"pp":"15","s":"At 31 January the rice quota was 96.9 per cent complete; sugar and dried milk were on hand; the ration population probably rose."},{"t":"January Transactions","lv":3,"p":[30,30],"pp":"15","s":"January collections of 81,832 tons of polished rice, distribution of 76,586 tons, and month-end stocks of 636,757 tons."},{"t":"Trends","lv":3,"p":[31,32],"pp":"16","s":"Rice distribution rose steadily over six months, other grains nearly ran out, and ration population stayed frozen at 9,476,856 since October; 510,524 suk released in January."},{"t":"Rice Collections","lv":3,"p":[33,33],"pp":"18","s":"Most provinces exceeded quota; Cholla-pukto reached only 80.2 per cent while Chungchong-pukto reached 117.9 per cent."},{"t":"Imports","lv":3,"p":[33,34],"pp":"18","s":"January grain imports of 37,849 tons: wheat 17,269, rice 3,076, dry milk 2,907 and sugar 16,127 tons."},{"t":"Section 2. Prices","lv":2,"p":[35,47],"pp":"20","s":"Seoul retail index rose to 2,231 in January (1,916 in December) on scarcity, hoarding and fear of currency flows from the north; wholesale prices rose faster; National Price Administration fixed prices."},{"t":"Retail Prices","lv":3,"p":[35,40],"pp":"20","s":"Among 26 Seoul items only rubber shoes and firewood fell; soy beans, sesame oil, leather shoes and silk rose sharply; index tables run January 1946 to January 1948."},{"t":"Wholesale Prices","lv":3,"p":[41,41],"pp":"26","s":"Seoul wholesale index for 27 items rose faster than retail from October to January, led by barley, soy beans, cotton yarn, lumber, cement and copper."},{"t":"Controlled Prices","lv":3,"p":[41,44],"pp":"26","s":"Controlled retail prices for rice and some rationed goods damped open-market increases; wholesale index tables for 27 commodities follow."},{"t":"Activities, National Price Administration","lv":3,"p":[45,47],"pp":"30","s":"Price-setting examples: wool serge at 200 won per yard, Puksan paper at 66 won per pound; tables of controlled prices to 31 January 1948."},{"t":"Section 3. Agriculture, Forestry and Fisheries","lv":2,"p":[48,60],"pp":"33","s":"1948 production goals, 63,710 tons of ammonium sulphate agreed, Ordinance No. 165 reorganizing the Korean Agricultural Association, and a New Korea Company census of 619,000 farms."},{"t":"Agricultural Economics","lv":3,"p":[48,49],"pp":"33","s":"Crop reporting reorganized under Ordinance 143; summer-grain acreage fell short of 1948 goals; a grain inspector school trained 1,417 inspectors."},{"t":"Agricultural Production","lv":3,"p":[50,52],"pp":"35","s":"1,245 dairy cattle, pyrethrum on Cheju-do, 5,628,000 keun of seed cotton collected, silk exported to Hong Kong for rubber, and irrigation contracts."},{"t":"Forestry","lv":3,"p":[53,53],"pp":"38","s":"Fuelwood goals for winter 1947/48 were reached, though transport limited city supplies; forest production figures given for October and November 1947."},{"t":"Fisheries","lv":3,"p":[53,54],"pp":"38","s":"272,760 bundles of laver shipped to Japan; marine product goals set to 1953; 13,000,000 won fisheries training budget approved."},{"t":"Korea Agricultural Association","lv":3,"p":[55,58],"pp":"40","s":"SCAP agreed to ship 63,710 tons of ammonium sulphate; 241,181 tons of fertilizer moved to railheads; seed potato losses ran 6-10 per cent."},{"t":"New Korea Company","lv":3,"p":[59,60],"pp":"44","s":"Ordinance 165 revised the Association's chain of authority; first post-liberation census counted 619,000 NKC farms averaging 0.87 chungbo per tenant."},{"t":"Section 4. Mining and Minerals","lv":2,"p":[61,72],"pp":"46","s":"Anthracite 31,333 tons in December, 26.2 per cent below quota; Samchuk Mine failed; bituminous imports 44,452 tons; pitch shortage halted briquettes; tungsten and copper problems."},{"t":"General","lv":3,"p":[61,61],"pp":"46","s":"December data came from large repossessed mines and a few private units. South Korean coal is all poor anthracite and lignite, so imported bituminous coal matters more than local output."},{"t":"Indigenous Anthracite and Lignite Coal","lv":3,"p":[61,65],"pp":"46","s":"Anthracite fell 12.2 per cent on power cuts and truck shortages; Samchuk made 13,392 tons against a 25,000-ton quota; stockpiles of 160,000 tons."},{"t":"Bituminous Coal","lv":3,"p":[66,67],"pp":"51","s":"Japanese imports of 44,452 tons were 31.4 per cent below December; 6,000 tons allocated to industry; tables of shipments since October 1945."},{"t":"Pitch","lv":3,"p":[68,68],"pp":"53","s":"Imported pitch ran out, cutting briquette output; the National Coal Committee allocated pitch like coal, and briquette factories moved under Commerce's Chemical Section."},{"t":"Other Minerals","lv":3,"p":[69,72],"pp":"54","s":"Kimbuk copper suffered car shortages, three copper mines might close, and the Dalsung tungsten mine (40,000,000 won rehabilitation) sat idle for lack of power."},{"t":"Section 5. Industrial Production","lv":2,"p":[73,81],"pp":"58","s":"December output fell on cold, coal and power shortages: flour 51 per cent of November, cotton yarn down 28.4 per cent, paper 171.8 tons, briquettes down 42.5 per cent; cement rose to 3,251 tons."},{"t":"General","lv":3,"p":[73,73],"pp":"58","s":"Data cover perhaps 10 per cent of plants; cold, snow and electricity shortage caused drastic production declines."},{"t":"Processed Food","lv":3,"p":[73,73],"pp":"58","s":"Flour fell to 51 per cent of November for lack of wheat; six small mills produced only 210 tons; noodle output down 27.3 per cent."},{"t":"Textiles","lv":3,"p":[74,74],"pp":"59","s":"Ten plants reported cotton yarn, output 28.4 per cent below November; mixed cotton and flax cloth totalled 17,506 meters."},{"t":"Paper","lv":3,"p":[75,75],"pp":"60","s":"Machine-made paper reached 171.8 tons in December, lowest since February 1947, owing to pulp and power scarcity."},{"t":"Hardware","lv":3,"p":[76,76],"pp":"61","s":"Nail production fell 14.7 per cent to 161.2 tons, with all three plants limited by coal and power."},{"t":"Chemical Group","lv":3,"p":[77,78],"pp":"62","s":"Briquettes down 42.5 per cent for lack of pitch; Samchuk cement plant made 3,251 tons; pottery down 48.2 per cent; rubber shoes 43,578 pairs."},{"t":"Monopoly Bureau Products","lv":3,"p":[79,81],"pp":"64","s":"Government salterns idle for a third month; cigarette output down 23.4 per cent; supplemental fund of 309,193,000 won for tobacco purchases."},{"t":"Section 6. Allocations and Distribution","lv":2,"p":[82,99],"pp":"67","s":"Executive Order No. 12 created the consumer goods allocation machinery; allocations of civilian supply, petroleum and ex-Japanese materials; year-end bonus in kind of about 445 won for 500,000 employees."},{"t":"Reorganization of Allocation Procedures","lv":3,"p":[82,87],"pp":"67","s":"Full text of Executive Order No. 12 (12 January) and its memorandum on priorities, eligibility, allocation and distribution through the Federation of Financial Associations."},{"t":"Allocations of Civilian Supply","lv":3,"p":[88,88],"pp":"73","s":"Allocations to departments included 45,000 bags of cement and tractors to Agriculture, 1,500 rolls of asphalt roofing for housing, and zinc powder for Health."},{"t":"Petroleum","lv":3,"p":[89,89],"pp":"74","s":"Petroleum Distributing Agency allocations included a 25 per cent gasoline increase voted by provincial officers and 1,000 extra drums of kerosene."},{"t":"Allocations of Ex-Japanese Materials","lv":3,"p":[90,90],"pp":"75","s":"Surrendered Japanese goods allocated: brass and copper for souvenir makers, 400 submarine batteries, 100 tons of scrap, and 20 tons of zinc found at Inchon."},{"t":"Allocations of Korean-Produced Commodities","lv":3,"p":[91,95],"pp":"76","s":"Maj. Gen. Dean approved a year-end bonus in kind for about 500,000 employees, valued near 445 won each, not yet distributed; tables of cloth, shoe, tire and soap allocations."},{"t":"Distribution of Korean-Produced Commodities","lv":3,"p":[96,99],"pp":"81","s":"Federation of Financial Associations bought 30,246,000 won of goods and the Federation of Distributing Associations 65,784,000 won from vested factories; provincial distribution compared with population."},{"t":"Section 7. Imports and Exports","lv":2,"p":[100,119],"pp":"85","s":"Private trade grew in 1947 (imports 2,088,125,206 won); December exports exceeded imports; Hong Kong trade; FLC surplus purchases; 57,889 tons of civilian supply arrived in January."},{"t":"General","lv":3,"p":[100,100],"pp":"85","s":"Gives a short 1947 trade summary alongside January 1948 activities. January statistics cover government-to-government and Civilian Supply imports; private trade figures follow next month."},{"t":"Private Trade Statistics","lv":3,"p":[100,106],"pp":"85","s":"Barter trade rose after the Foreign Exchange Bank opened 15 July 1947; December imports 246,703,305 won, exports 271,691,240 won; 68 import and 103 export permits in January."},{"t":"Private Trade Activities","lv":3,"p":[107,107],"pp":"92","s":"Hong Kong trade reopened August 1947 with two to three ships monthly; SS Sinkiang and Leopold Skeik cargoes; Pusan-Hong Kong freight HK$65 per ton."},{"t":"Government-to-Government Transactions","lv":3,"p":[108,110],"pp":"93","s":"Hong Kong office closed December 1947; 1,350 tons of scheelite shipped to New York in 1947; exports to Japan valued at $2,510,736.30; apple shipments spoiled."},{"t":"Activities","lv":3,"p":[111,111],"pp":"96","s":"Souvenir items worth $32,541.90 sold to Army Exchange Service; new Pusan office opened for export customers; mineral export stockpiles noted."},{"t":"Status of FLC Imports","lv":3,"p":[112,113],"pp":"97","s":"Seven purchase agreements against a $25,000,000 FLC credit for surplus stocks from Japan, Philippines, Iwo Jima and Hawaii at 21 to 50 cents on the dollar."},{"t":"Other Civilian Supply Imports","lv":3,"p":[114,119],"pp":"99","s":"1947 imports from Japan $21,127,949 and from the US $69,830,725; 57,889 tons arrived in January, including 16,127 tons of sugar, fertilizer, coal and penicillin."},{"t":"Section 8. Labor","lv":2,"p":[120,123],"pp":"105","s":"Yong Dong Po strike (8-16 January) and Seoul streetcar walkout; Tai Han No Chung Federation convention of 864 delegates; working-hours exemptions; child labor survey."},{"t":"Part III: Public Services","lv":1,"p":[124,157],"pp":"109","s":"Transportation, communications, utilities, public works and housing: over 62 per cent of locomotives inoperative, a 60-fold electric rate rise, highway plans and 560 refugee houses."},{"t":"Section 1. Transportation","lv":2,"p":[124,134],"pp":"109","s":"December revenue rose 113,000,000 won on a 115,000,000-won Army payment; over 62 per cent of locomotives inoperative; freight 138,000 tons below November; coal consumption down 20 per cent."},{"t":"General","lv":3,"p":[124,124],"pp":"109","s":"Railroads carry most freight and passengers; trucks and ox carts serve only short hauls. Lack of bituminous coal eliminated 107 of 262 scheduled trains through December, congesting ports."},{"t":"Income and Expenditures","lv":3,"p":[124,125],"pp":"109","s":"Department revenue rose 113,000,000 won in December; expenditures rose as back bills were paid, including 81,000,000 won for Japanese coal."},{"t":"Railroads","lv":3,"p":[126,126],"pp":"111","s":"Railroad income was 36 per cent above November owing to the Army payment; passenger, freight, equipment and employment tables follow."},{"t":"Marine Transportation","lv":3,"p":[127,133],"pp":"112","s":"Marine revenue up 72 per cent and expenditures up 84 per cent owing to a 27,000,000-won back payment to seamen; passenger traffic lowest since February 1947."},{"t":"Air and Highway Transportation","lv":3,"p":[134,134],"pp":"119","s":"Air Bureau expenditures rose sharply to repair control equipment at airports other than Kimpo; highway expenditures also increased."},{"t":"Supply Division","lv":3,"p":[134,134],"pp":"119","s":"Daily coal reporting system set up; specifications completed for the Dan Yang sub-station to enable electric locomotives."},{"t":"Section 2. Communications","lv":2,"p":[135,147],"pp":"120","s":"December revenue rose 62 per cent to the highest since January 1946; 37,183 telephone users on 624 exchanges; 623 post offices; 175,000 radios and 11 stations."},{"t":"General","lv":3,"p":[135,135],"pp":"120","s":"The Interim Government's Department of Communications runs telephone, telegraph, radio and postal services; only the Korean Broadcasting Corporation is outside it. Postwar disrepair is being fixed with American and Japanese materials."},{"t":"Revenues and Expenditures","lv":3,"p":[135,135],"pp":"120","s":"December revenue rose 25,700,000 won (62 per cent) after rate increases; expenditures still ran 30 per cent above revenue."},{"t":"Employment","lv":3,"p":[136,136],"pp":"121","s":"Employment rose slightly for a second month as clerical staff shifted from postal to telephone, telegraph and radio sections."},{"t":"Special Functions","lv":3,"p":[136,140],"pp":"121","s":"Post offices collect income, restaurant and tobacco taxes for the Department of Finance; tables of revenue, expenditures, employees and tax receipts."},{"t":"Postal Deposits and Insurance","lv":3,"p":[141,142],"pp":"126","s":"Postal deposits fell while depositors rose; book transfer balances up 9,000,000 won; National Life Insurance policies grew steadily."},{"t":"Telephone and Telegraph","lv":3,"p":[143,144],"pp":"128","s":"2,000 people await telephones in Seoul; 624 exchanges serve 37,183 users; telegraph messages and revenue (up 32 per cent) rose in December."},{"t":"Postal Bureau","lv":3,"p":[144,144],"pp":"129","s":"Classification of 623 post offices; two railway post offices at Seoul and Pusan and 23 mail cars."},{"t":"Radio","lv":3,"p":[145,147],"pp":"130","s":"Korean Broadcasting Corporation monopoly: 11 stations, about 175,000 receivers (50,000 broken), 112,000 paying the 40-won monthly fee."},{"t":"Section 3. Public Utilities","lv":2,"p":[148,151],"pp":"133","s":"74,000,000 kwh delivered in January; Emergency Power Committee restricted use; streetcars fell from 137 to 30 during a walkout; Board recommended a 60-fold retail rate increase on 8 January."},{"t":"Section 4. Public Works","lv":2,"p":[152,155],"pp":"137","s":"Highway program continued despite a 367,000,000-won F/Y 1948/49 cut; Seoul-Kangnung survey funded at 300,000 won; 214 fires caused 135,218,400 won damage; Heavy Equipment school opened 12 January."},{"t":"Section 5. Housing","lv":2,"p":[156,157],"pp":"141","s":"National Housing Administration stockpiled materials for low-cost Seoul houses; 560 refugee units completed in provinces and nearly all of 400 Seoul refugee houses finished."},{"t":"Part IV: Finance","lv":1,"p":[158,179],"pp":"143","s":"Government and non-government finance: note issue fell 1,767,000,000 won, supplementary appropriations, revenue 71 per cent of expenditure, bank deposits up 6,034 million won in December."},{"t":"Section 1. Government Finance","lv":2,"p":[158,167],"pp":"143","s":"Notes outstanding fell 1,767,000,000 won; six supplementary appropriations over 601,000,000 won; revenue was 71 per cent of expenditure; Civilian Supply accounts receivable over 500,000,000 won."},{"t":"Section 2. Non-government Finance","lv":2,"p":[168,179],"pp":"153","s":"December deposits rose 6,034 million won (15 per cent) and loans 4,801 million won; 'Fight Inflation With Life Insurance' campaign; Chosun Life capital increase; dishonored bills table."},{"t":"Part V: Government and Politics","lv":1,"p":[180,207],"pp":"165","s":"Administration, the Legislative Assembly, political reactions to the UN Commission, legal affairs, police and inter-zone activities, including the 9 January Kim Koo-Rhee merger."},{"t":"Section 1. Administration","lv":2,"p":[180,184],"pp":"165","s":"400 tons of paper stockpiled for the UN election; Maritime Board preliminary report 15 January; National Economic Board approved rate increases and refused 100,000 pounds of Chinese sugar."},{"t":"Section 2. Korean Interim Legislative Assembly","lv":2,"p":[185,190],"pp":"170","s":"Twelve sessions, average attendance 63 and frequent lack of quorum; debates on weights and measures, electric rates and prostitution; Philippine delegate Arranz visited 30 January."},{"t":"General","lv":3,"p":[185,185],"pp":"170","s":"Little action owing to missing quorum after the holiday recess; 12 sessions averaging 2 hours 45 minutes, 90 members authorized."},{"t":"Law for Weights and Measures","lv":3,"p":[186,186],"pp":"171","s":"First reading of a law letting Commerce restrict the Chok-kwan system to enforce metric units; Won Se-hun objected; title approved 32 to 6."},{"t":"Electric Power","lv":3,"p":[187,187],"pp":"172","s":"Assembly challenged the sixfold power rate announcement, summoned officials on 22 January and referred investigation to two committees."},{"t":"Public Prostitution Law","lv":3,"p":[188,188],"pp":"173","s":"Debate on amending Public Act No. 7; a three-month delay of the effective date was rejected 37 to 7."},{"t":"Public Act No. 8","lv":3,"p":[188,188],"pp":"173","s":"Revised Ordinance 153 passed as an act prohibiting aid to US personnel in violating military directives, with 'off limits' and prostitution provisions; penalty one year or 50,000 won."},{"t":"Other Business","lv":3,"p":[189,189],"pp":"174","s":"First reading of Temporary Nationality Law provisions; 38 of 56 senior appointees approved; Assembly refused 30 to 0 to join a rice-collection commendation committee."},{"t":"Visit of Philippine Representative to UN Commission on Korea","lv":3,"p":[190,190],"pp":"175","s":"Melecio Arranz addressed the 199th session on 30 January, conveying Philippine greetings; Dr. Kimm Kiu-sic thanked him."},{"t":"Section 3. Political Developments","lv":2,"p":[191,192],"pp":"176","s":"Kim Koo and Rhee Syngman merged rightist groups on 9 January; Kimm Kiu-sic urged referral to the Little Assembly; Kim Koo proposed troop withdrawal and a leaders' conference."},{"t":"Section 4. Legal Affairs","lv":2,"p":[193,200],"pp":"178","s":"Ordinances 161-166, Public Act No. 8 and Executive Order No. 12; opinions on KILA quorum and missionary customs duties; prison population statistics at 31 October 1947."},{"t":"Ordinances and Orders","lv":3,"p":[193,193],"pp":"178","s":"Ordinances 161-166 covered automobile fees, vested property title and revising the Korea Agricultural Association; also Civil Service Rules No. 10."},{"t":"Legal Opinions","lv":3,"p":[193,195],"pp":"178","s":"Justice opinions on enforcing KILA attendance, a vested company's funeral-expense liability (up to 1,000,000 won), the Theatrical Performance Law as censorship, and missionary car customs duties."},{"t":"Penal Institutions","lv":3,"p":[196,199],"pp":"181","s":"199 student guards enrolled 7 January; inspections of Inchon, Kaesong, Uijongbu and Ascom City camps; prison population tables of 31 October 1947."},{"t":"Korean Courts","lv":3,"p":[200,200],"pp":"185","s":"UN legal counselor Dr. Marc Schreiber addressed judges and lawyers on 31 January; investigation of a Korean National Youth member's arrest at Wonju."},{"t":"Property Claims Commission","lv":3,"p":[200,200],"pp":"185","s":"Final decisions were made in six property claims cases during January."},{"t":"Juvenile Courts","lv":3,"p":[200,200],"pp":"185","s":"Juvenile court centers expanded to four provinces in 1947; lack of funds blocked wider extension."},{"t":"Section 5. Police","lv":2,"p":[201,202],"pp":"186","s":"Korean National Police Women's Division trained policewomen at the Police Academy; a weekly report listed about 60 arrests including a wild-boar incident."},{"t":"Section 6. Inter-zone Activities","lv":2,"p":[203,207],"pp":"188","s":"North-South mail exchanged once (10 January); Radio Pyongyang propaganda against the UN Commission, a North Korean constitution broadcast; Station HLKA counter-programming."},{"t":"Part VI: Social","lv":1,"p":[208,255],"pp":"193","s":"Health, education, culture, public information, press opinion, Korean National Youth (475,000 members) and the first Women's Activities section."},{"t":"Section 1. Public Health and Welfare","lv":2,"p":[208,217],"pp":"193","s":"Mainly diphtheria, typhus and typhoid; Chongju health center opened 20 January; eight narcotics raids; 2,958 narcotics licenses; Sajik Park receiving home for 200 children."},{"t":"Vital Statistics","lv":3,"p":[208,209],"pp":"193","s":"Monthly and annual tabulations of births, deaths, marriages and divorces planned; April-December 1946 mortality showed high infant deaths from pneumonia and similar diseases."},{"t":"Preventive Medicine","lv":3,"p":[210,210],"pp":"195","s":"Diphtheria, typhus and typhoid were the main communicable diseases; examination of licensed Seoul prostitutes before release planned."},{"t":"Medical Services","lv":3,"p":[211,211],"pp":"196","s":"Chongju health center opened 20 January; tuberculosis training at Masan; Materia Medica translation; conditions at Chonju and Pusan hospitals and the Pusan leprosarium."},{"t":"Nursing Affairs","lv":3,"p":[212,212],"pp":"197","s":"Nurse and midwife examination set for May 1948; Presbyterian Hospital School of Nursing accredited 26 January."},{"t":"Veterinary Affairs","lv":3,"p":[212,212],"pp":"197","s":"Meat inspection withdrawn from East Gate and Yong Dong Po slaughterhouses on 23 January; fish market building near Seoul Station returned to use."},{"t":"Pharmaceutical Affairs","lv":3,"p":[212,212],"pp":"197","s":"Pharmacists examination planned for 25 March to 1 April; gauze capacity idle for lack of cotton; 400 tons of coal allocated to medical supply plants."},{"t":"Narcotics Section","lv":3,"p":[213,213],"pp":"198","s":"Eight raids, 12 arrests; 52 investigators; raw opium rose from 1,200 to 3,000 won per 37 grams; 2,958 licenses issued, 2,752 to doctors."},{"t":"Laboratories","lv":3,"p":[214,214],"pp":"199","s":"Biologicals production negligible for lack of power; fire destroyed an incubator at Anyang on 10 January."},{"t":"Public Assistance","lv":3,"p":[215,215],"pp":"200","s":"1,200 tons of powdered skim milk arrived at Pusan; Blind and Deaf School moved to Education; Sajik Park children's home opened 10 January."},{"t":"Women's Bureau","lv":3,"p":[216,216],"pp":"201","s":"Conferences on abolishing legalized prostitution under Public Act No. 7 and housing displaced girls; travelers' aid started at Seoul Station 26 January."},{"t":"Welfare Supply","lv":3,"p":[217,217],"pp":"202","s":"4,000 mattresses sent to Pusan and 1,500 to the National Leprosarium at Sorokto."},{"t":"Section 2. Education","lv":2,"p":[218,224],"pp":"203","s":"13,579,080 won approved for a Teachers' Training Center opening about 1 July; 200,000,000-won elementary subsidy; textbooks; 111 students sent to the US; American Language Institute."},{"t":"Section 3. Culture","lv":2,"p":[225,229],"pp":"210","s":"Lee Orchestra played for the UN Commission on 14 January; La Traviata sung in Korean from 16 January; Spring Fragrance drama; Korean American Cultural Association; athletics."},{"t":"Section 4. Public Information","lv":2,"p":[230,236],"pp":"215","s":"101 press releases and 23 foreign items issued; rice collection 93 per cent by 5 January; 31 January directive on press freedom; 16 political parties registered."},{"t":"Section 5. Press and Public Opinion","lv":2,"p":[237,246],"pp":"222","s":"Press centered on the UN Commission, with rightist, leftist and moderate demands; rate-increase complaints; police plots and the attack on Chang Paik-sang; polls showing 53 per cent faced contribution demands."},{"t":"Introduction","lv":3,"p":[237,237],"pp":"222","s":"January 1948 Korean press focused on the UN Temporary Commission and the coming election; parties' demands dragged on, Commission arrived 8 January, and Kim Koo's late-month change of heart was the one surprise."},{"t":"New Year's Statements","lv":3,"p":[237,239],"pp":"222","s":"Rightist Dae Han Ilbo warned the US to back Rhee and Kim Koo; leftists demanded troop withdrawal; both sides demanded release of political prisoners; moderates urged self-help."},{"t":"\"World Situation in the New Year\"","lv":3,"p":[240,240],"pp":"225","s":"Cha Yoo Shin Mun surveyed global conflicts; leftist Chosun Ilbo on 20 January distrusted American friendliness toward Japan."},{"t":"UN Commission Activities","lv":3,"p":[240,240],"pp":"225","s":"Papers fully covered the Commission; Dr. K. P. S. Menon elected temporary chairman; Soviet refusal to cooperate; Kim Koo's 28 January statement on free elections."},{"t":"\"Racial Spirit\"","lv":3,"p":[241,241],"pp":"226","s":"Cha Yoo Shin Mun (4 January) urged a new national culture; Choong Ang Shin Mun (7 January) proposed cutting wedding and funeral costs, and attacked mammonism (18 January) and profit-driven publishing (29 January)."},{"t":"Economic Discussions","lv":3,"p":[242,242],"pp":"227","s":"Leftist Chosun Choong Ang Ilbo (5 January) blamed falling living standards on material shortages, bank-note over-issue and 20 percent plant output; Chosun Ilbo (18 January) said the land reform bill was stalled for lack of a quorum."},{"t":"Police","lv":3,"p":[243,244],"pp":"228","s":"Police warned against disturbances and described alleged leftist plots, an attack on Metropolitan Chief Chang Paik-sang, the Chang Duk-soo murder case, and charges of police abuse."},{"t":"Public Opinion","lv":3,"p":[245,246],"pp":"230","s":"Polls: 87.1 per cent expected North Korean currency changes to affect the South; 84 per cent opposed the electric rate rise; 88 per cent backed textbook-first publishing; 53 per cent faced contribution demands."},{"t":"Section 6. Korean National Youth, Inc.","lv":2,"p":[247,250],"pp":"232","s":"Membership rose 15 per cent to 475,000, including 17,500 women and 10,000 students; training goal of 200,000; 550,000 daily service hours; threats from rivals such as Dai Dong."},{"t":"Section 7. Women's Activities","lv":2,"p":[251,255],"pp":"236","s":"Women's Bureau cocktail party for the UN Commission 14 January; monthly meeting of eight provinces; new Federation of Women's Organizations; Mother's School opened; YWCA delegates feted."}],"by":"claude-sonnet (Claude Code subagent)","emb":["K-29-in-K-28"]},{"id":"J-29","ia":"summation-non-military-activities-japan-no-29-1948-02","s":"J","no":29,"d":"1948-02","d2":"","t":"Summation of Non-Military Activities in Japan, No. 29 (February 1948)","v":"","pg":161,"o":"Summation No. 29 covers February 1948 (with most economic statistics for January or December). Politically it is dominated by the resignation of the Katayama Cabinet on 10 February after the Social Democratic left wing revolted over rail and postal rate rises in a supplementary budget, followed by a prolonged contest in which the Democratic leader Hitoshi Ashida was chosen prime minister over Liberal Shigeru Yoshida. The report also covers the hunt for hoarded goods, the National Diet Library Law, purge screening totals, police decentralization, war-crimes trials, and the prosecution's summation before the International Military Tribunal for the Far East. Economic sections give January output for coal, steel, textiles, power and transport, the 1947 foreign-trade totals, price controls, banking and liquidation figures, and reparations transfers. Social sections report nutrition surveys, the 1 October 1947 census (79.6 million), education conferences and media. Historians will find the Cabinet-crisis narrative and the quantitative series most useful.","h":["The Katayama Cabinet resigned on 10 February, after eight months in office, when the Social Democratic left wing refused the supplementary budgets that raised railway and postal rates.","After weeks of interparty talks, Democratic leader Hitoshi Ashida was elected prime minister with 216 votes in the House of Representatives, though the Councillors favored Yoshida; the Lower House choice prevailed.","Hoarded and concealed goods worth about 1,400,000,000 yen were uncovered between November and January; a Diet committee probed members, and a comprehensive inventory of critical goods was ordered.","The National Diet Library Law passed both Houses unanimously on 4 February, creating a national library modeled on the Library of Congress after the 1947 United States Library Mission.","Purge screening provisionally designated 202,413 persons by 21 February, 173,602 of them finally; 163 of 1,424 information-media officials screened were barred, and the Hirano court injunction was ruled void.","At the Tokyo war crimes trial the defense ended surrebuttal on 10 February and the prosecution began its summation on 11 February against 25 defendants.","Japan's 1947 trade was about $526,000,000 in imports and $175,000,000 in exports, with roughly 92 percent of imports from the United States and foodstuffs 58 percent of import value.","The 1 October 1947 census counted 79,626,729 persons, about 5.6 percent above 1946; Bank of Japan notes outstanding stood at 215,219,000,000 yen on 29 February."],"k":["Katayama Cabinet resignation","Ashida cabinet","Social Democratic Party split","hoarded goods","National Diet Library Law","purge screening","police decentralization","war crimes trials","IMTFE summation","coal production","textile production","foreign trade 1947","price control","Bank of Japan","census 1947"],"toc":[{"t":"Front matter","lv":1,"p":[1,3],"pp":"","s":"Cover of Summation No. 29 (February 1948), table of contents for Parts I-IV, and lists of charts, maps and tables numbered 1-113."},{"t":"Part I: General","lv":1,"p":[4,13],"pp":"1","s":"Digest of the month: no change in SCAP organization, Katayama's resignation and Ashida's election, January output figures, and social items including the 1 October 1947 census."},{"t":"Section 1. Organization Under SCAP","lv":2,"p":[5,5],"pp":"2","s":"States that the non-military organization of GHQ SCAP did not change during February 1948."},{"t":"Section 2. Political Activities","lv":2,"p":[5,6],"pp":"3","s":"Katayama Cabinet resigned 10 February and Ashida was elected prime minister; also hoarded-goods drive, National Diet Library Law, 202,413 provisional purge designations, police decentralization and war-crimes trials."},{"t":"Section 3. Economic Activities","lv":2,"p":[7,11],"pp":"7","s":"January output in agriculture, mining, industry, textiles and transport, plus 1947 trade totals, finance, antitrust (267 concerns designated), reparations and science."},{"t":"Section 4. Social Activities","lv":2,"p":[12,13],"pp":"17","s":"Nutrition surveys, venereal disease campaign, the 79,626,729 census total, education and Christian conferences, and a newspaper survey covering 2,978 papers."},{"t":"Part II: Political","lv":1,"p":[14,39],"pp":"21","s":"Detailed account of the Katayama Cabinet crisis and Ashida's election, hoarded goods, purge, police and crime statistics, and war-crimes trials including the IMTFE prosecution summation."},{"t":"Section 1. Government and Politics","lv":2,"p":[15,28],"pp":"21","s":"Katayama Cabinet fell 10 February over rate increases; Ashida elected prime minister with 216 votes; hoarded-goods inquiries, Diet Library Law, and purge screening of 671,120 persons."},{"t":"Cabinet Crisis","lv":3,"p":[15,18],"pp":"","s":"Social Democratic left wing opposed the rate-increase budget bills; the Cabinet decided 7 February to pass the issue to the Diet; Katayama resigned 10 February."},{"t":"Record of the Katayama Cabinet","lv":3,"p":[19,20],"pp":"","s":"Review of the cabinet's eight months: Ministry of Labor created, Home Ministry dissolved, eight-point economic program, anti-hoarding drive and the Law for Decentralization of Excessive Concentrations."},{"t":"Election of New Prime Minister","lv":3,"p":[21,24],"pp":"","s":"Interparty conferences from 10 February and Democratic and Social Democratic programs; Ashida elected in the Lower House, Yoshida in the Upper, ending in joint committee deadlock."},{"t":"Hoarded Goods","lv":3,"p":[25,26],"pp":"","s":"About 1.4 billion yen of hidden goods found November-January, Economic Stabilization Board finds worth 100,000,000 yen, weapons seized at Kawasaki, and Diet committee probes including the Hara and Kondo cases."},{"t":"Political Parties and the Diet","lv":3,"p":[26,28],"pp":"","s":"Party positions in the crisis, National Diet Library Law of 4 February, Central Liaison Office dissolved, Liberal wins in Upper House by-elections, and a 26 February fire at the purge records building."},{"t":"Section 2. Public Safety","lv":2,"p":[29,31],"pp":"51","s":"December crime and arrests, autonomous police in 1,604 of 1,808 municipalities, 82,760 prisoners in January, 943 railway accidents, and December fire losses."},{"t":"Section 3. Legal Affairs and War Crimes","lv":2,"p":[32,39],"pp":"57","s":"Office of Attorney General began 15 February; Class B and C trial results; IMTFE defense surrebuttal ended 10 February and the prosecution's summation began 11 February."},{"t":"Legal Affairs and National War Crimes Trials","lv":3,"p":[32,34],"pp":"","s":"Attorney General's office, 85 investigations completed, 40 persons ordered apprehended, and 19 trials of 48 accused, including death sentences for Ishizaki and Katsudo."},{"t":"International Prosecution of War Criminals","lv":3,"p":[34,39],"pp":"","s":"February IMTFE sessions, defense surrebuttal for Hirota and Taku, and the prosecution's summation on conspiracy, Manchuria, the Southwest Pacific and aggression against the USSR."},{"t":"Part III: Economic","lv":1,"p":[40,133],"pp":"70","s":"January and February economic reporting on farm land sales, coal, metals, chemicals, manufacturing, textiles, transport, communications, labor, trade, prices, finance, property control and science."},{"t":"Section 1. Agriculture and Fisheries","lv":2,"p":[41,42],"pp":"70","s":"Government sold 32,700 cho of land in January and purchased 94,655 cho on 2 February; also livestock population changes and fish landings."},{"t":"Section 2. Forestry and Mining","lv":2,"p":[43,48],"pp":"79","s":"Whaling catch of 271 blue whale units, January log production of 57,107,500 cubic feet, coal output of 2,829,200 tons, stockpiles of 667,200 tons, and crude oil production."},{"t":"Section 3. Heavy Industries","lv":2,"p":[49,58],"pp":"91","s":"January pig iron fell to 38,922 metric tons on hydroelectric shortage; coke stocks ended at 38,220 tons; cement 103,281 tons; ammonium sulfate 56,875 tons; machine tools 271 units; 17,084 coal-miner dwellings built since January 1947."},{"t":"Section 4. Manufacturing","lv":2,"p":[59,67],"pp":"111","s":"January flour and food processing, pulp (down 2,230 tons), glass, optical and electrical goods, motor vehicles, leather (758,200 pounds), agricultural equipment and miscellaneous goods."},{"t":"Section 5. Textile Industries","lv":2,"p":[68,81],"pp":"122","s":"January cotton yarn gained about 2,700,000 pounds on eased power limits; first postwar Egyptian cotton arrived (1,000 bales); raw silk fell to 6,209 bales; rayon staple dropped about 25 percent for lack of pulp."},{"t":"Section 6. Transportation and Public Utilities","lv":2,"p":[82,87],"pp":"157","s":"108,231 motor vehicles registered, 291,629,562 railway passengers, 1,480,747 tons of shipping cargo, electric generation of 2,233,000,000 kWh, and gas production."},{"t":"Section 7. Communications","lv":2,"p":[88,94],"pp":"169","s":"Radiotelephone extended to Canada, Cuba and Mexico, radiotelegraph rates to the U.S. revised, and a December Ministry of Communications deficit of 1,208,818,199 yen."},{"t":"Section 8. Labor","lv":2,"p":[95,103],"pp":"183","s":"Labor ministry ordinance of 7 February on illegal labor supply, anti-Communist union drives, no major strikes, union education programs, wages and employment security."},{"t":"Section 9. Imports and Exports","lv":2,"p":[104,107],"pp":"201","s":"Calendar 1947 trade was about $526,000,000 imports and $175,000,000 exports, with 92 percent of imports from the United States."},{"t":"Section 10. Rationing and Price Control","lv":2,"p":[108,115],"pp":"205","s":"Staple-food rations met in full, sugar incentive plan liberalized 19 February, price changes for explosives, rolling stock and cement, Tokyo water rates, and urban family expenditures."},{"t":"Section 11. Finance","lv":2,"p":[116,123],"pp":"225","s":"Bank of Japan notes at 215,219,000,000 yen, Reconstruction Finance Bank loans, securities liquidation, tax receipts, supplementary budgets approved 24 February, and national debt."},{"t":"Section 12. Property Control and Reparations","lv":2,"p":[124,129],"pp":"241","s":"943 looted ships recorded, 267 industrial firms designated excessive concentrations, antitrust orders, and 3,850 packages (7,294 tons) of advance reparations shipments."},{"t":"Section 13. Science and Technology","lv":2,"p":[130,133],"pp":"253","s":"Science council's sixth meeting 30-31 January, 31 Japanese Standards promulgated, 103 patents granted, and Kyushu University research publications."},{"t":"Part IV: Social","lv":1,"p":[134,161],"pp":"261","s":"Public health, nutrition, population, welfare, education, religion, cultural resources and information media for February 1948, including the 1947 census and textbook printing."},{"t":"Section 1. Public Health and Welfare","lv":2,"p":[135,149],"pp":"261","s":"Nutrition surveys, venereal disease campaign, medical supply production, narcotics, public assistance, Red Cross, repatriation, and vital statistics including the 1947 census."},{"t":"Section 2. Education, Religion, Cultural Resources and Media of Expression","lv":2,"p":[150,161],"pp":"291","s":"Teacher conferences, authorization of 147,214,307 textbook copies, Christian conferences, first postwar art exchange, and press, radio and film reports."}],"by":"claude-sonnet (Claude Code subagent)"},{"id":"K-29","ia":"south-korean-interim-government-activities-no-29-1948-02","s":"K","no":29,"d":"1948-02","d2":"","t":"South Korean Interim Government Activities, No. 29 (February 1948)","v":"","pg":235,"o":"Report No. 29 of South Korean Interim Government Activities covers February 1948, prepared by the National Economic Board for USAMGIK. The month was dominated by the cold-war split over elections: leftist sabotage and the attempted general strike of 7-10 February, the UN Interim Committee's 27 February decision to observe elections in South Korea alone, and the collapse of KILA after Speaker Kimm Kiu-sic and about 30 members resigned. Economically it records record bituminous coal imports (83,330 tons), a completed rice collection at 97.6 percent of quota, cuts in urban rations, falling wholesale prices, the first month in which government revenue deposits exceeded Bank of Chosun withdrawals, and the Military Governor's directive to balance the 1948/49 budget. It also reports vested property disposal and land-sale planning, new railroad and bus rate increases, police and civil-liberties reforms, public health, education, culture, press opinion, Korean National Youth, women's activities, a Seoul 1947 census analysis, and North Korean broadcasts and draft constitution. Historians will find dense statistical tables alongside political narrative.","h":["A leftist revolt and attempted general strike on 7-10 February damaged 52 locomotives, cut telephone and telegraph lines and attacked police boxes; a police report for 6-12 February listed 6 police and 29 rioters killed, with 1,489 arrests reported by the press.","On 27 February the UN Interim Committee voted 31 to 2, with 11 abstentions, to carry out Resolution II by observing elections in South Korea alone; Rhee Syngman welcomed it while Kimm Kiu-sic and Kim Koo expressed disappointment.","KILA collapsed after rightists pushed an 'Urgent Proposal' to the UN Commission; Speaker Kimm Kiu-sic and Vice-Speaker Choe Tong-O resigned on 27 February, and about 29 moderate and leftist members signed a protest.","The 1947/48 rice collection ended 15 February at 97.6 percent of the 773,477-ton quota, but import shortfalls forced urban rations down to about 2 hop (290 grams) in some cities; 225,000 tons of grain imports are projected for 1948/49.","Bituminous coal imports from Japan reached 83,330 metric tons, the best month since October 1945, and anthracite output hit 41,357 tons, yet coal-tar pitch and rail transport shortages kept briquettes and industry depressed.","For the first time Bank of Chosun revenue deposits (1,391 million won) exceeded withdrawals (1,005 million), cutting cumulative excess withdrawals by 386 million won, while note issue fell 722 million won in the month.","On 25 February the Military Governor directed the National Economic Board to balance the 1948/49 budget; the Board judged 22,000 million won the realistic revenue figure, and Maj.-Gen. Helmick reported on his Washington trip for GARIOA funds.","The New Korea Company drafted a land valuation system for its 324,604 chungbo, priced at three times annual crop and payable over 15 years, and a Land Reform Committee drafted an ordinance for disposing of vested farmland."],"k":["South Korean Interim Government","USAMGIK","UN Temporary Commission on Korea","7 February 1948 strike and sabotage","Korean Interim Legislative Assembly","Kimm Kiu-sic","Kim Koo","Rhee Syngman","rice collection","vested property","bituminous coal imports","inflation and prices","budget 1948/49","National Police reform","Korean National Youth","Radio Pyongyang"],"toc":[{"t":"Front matter","lv":1,"p":[1,7],"pp":"","s":"Cover of No. 29 (February 1948, National Economic Board), a blank page, and the six-page table of contents for Parts I-VI."},{"t":"Introduction","lv":1,"p":[8,9],"pp":"1","s":"Overview of February: the 7 February revolt, the 27 February UN decision on South-only elections, KILA's near-collapse, a 58 percent rise in northern refugees, and bleak industry despite better coal supplies."},{"t":"Part I: Basic Aspects","lv":1,"p":[10,24],"pp":"3","s":"Covers population movements, the 1947 Seoul census, Korean schools and officials in Japan, and vested property disposal, including 35 sales, textile rehabilitation and guaranteed loans."},{"t":"Section 1. Population and Foreign Affairs","lv":2,"p":[10,19],"pp":"3","s":"Refugee inflow rose 58 percent to 4,074; Seoul's 779,890 adults were profiled; Osaka planned to close a quarter of its Korean schools; 192 Koreans were interned for illegal entry to Japan."},{"t":"Population Migration","lv":3,"p":[10,12],"pp":"3","s":"Northern refugees numbered 4,074 in February against 2,541 in January; cumulative inbound movement since 1945 reached 2,360,527 against 885,995 outbound."},{"t":"Seoul Census for 1947","lv":3,"p":[13,14],"pp":"6","s":"March 1947 census tables: housewives 29.2 percent of adults, 40.9 percent with no education, 60.9 percent married, and a total Seoul population of 1,178,951."},{"t":"Koreans in Manchuria","lv":3,"p":[15,16],"pp":"8","s":"About 800,000 Koreans live in Manchuria, half in the Chientao area, down from a 1945 peak of 1,548,368 (Table 8)."},{"t":"Osaka School Activities","lv":3,"p":[17,17],"pp":"10","s":"The Osaka team planned to consolidate 56 elementary and five middle Korean schools into about 10 accredited schools, removing them from Korean League control before the 1 April school year."},{"t":"Korean Affairs in Japan","lv":3,"p":[18,18],"pp":"11","s":"SKIG Liaison staff in Tokyo faced billeting and food-payment problems; per diem rose from 300 to 400 yen; Nagoya Korean groups were investigated over 1,130,000 yen of charity funds."},{"t":"Miscellaneous","lv":3,"p":[19,19],"pp":"12","s":"192 Koreans were interned for illegal entry to Japan; draft immigration regulations were prepared; the SS Pung Chu Whan was detained in Shanghai."},{"t":"Section 2. Vested Property","lv":2,"p":[20,24],"pp":"13","s":"Thirty-five sales totaled 4,600,800 won; 409 companies were screened for reorganization; 299 plants were leased; textile loans above 200,000,000 won were guaranteed; theatres and the Tong Dai Mon factory were priced for sale."},{"t":"Part II: Production and Supply","lv":1,"p":[25,106],"pp":"19","s":"Food, agriculture, prices, industry, mining, allocations, trade and labor: rice collection completed and bituminous coal imports up, but power, pitch and wheat were short and prices rose."},{"t":"Section 1. Food Supply","lv":2,"p":[26,34],"pp":"19","s":"Rice collection closed 15 February at 97.6 percent of quota; urban rations were cut to about 2 hop; 225,000 tons of grain imports are projected for 1948/49."},{"t":"Section 2. Agriculture, Forestry and Fisheries","lv":2,"p":[35,40],"pp":"28","s":"New Korea Company land valuation plan for 324,604 chungbo; 13,898,000 straw bags distributed; Ordinance No. 165 on the Agricultural Association; laver exports to Japan; Kyonggi 4-H clubs rose to 1,072."},{"t":"Section 3. Prices","lv":2,"p":[41,51],"pp":"34","s":"Seoul retail index reached 2,310 (August 1945 = 100), up four percent; the wholesale index fell six percent to 6,910, its first decline since April 1947."},{"t":"Section 4. Industrial Production","lv":2,"p":[52,64],"pp":"45","s":"January 1948 output of flour, cotton, paper, cement, rubber and tobacco was hampered by pulp, pitch and power shortages; Puksan Paper Mill resumed; a full 1947 production table is included."},{"t":"Section 5. Mining and Minerals","lv":2,"p":[65,76],"pp":"58","s":"Anthracite output was 41,357 tons in January; bituminous imports 83,330 tons in February; pitch scarce; gold, silver, tungsten, zinc and lead output up, copper down."},{"t":"Section 6. Allocations and Distribution","lv":2,"p":[77,90],"pp":"70","s":"Consumer Goods Allocation Board still organizing; petroleum prices raised effective 1 April; 223,000,000 won of year-end bonus goods allocated through the Federation of Financial Associations."},{"t":"Section 7. Imports and Exports","lv":2,"p":[91,102],"pp":"84","s":"January private imports were 352,286,000 won and exports 348,095,000 won; trustee-shipment and foreign-trade loan rules; laver exports to Japan worth $2,430,576; Egyptian cotton warehouse fire."},{"t":"Section 8. Labor","lv":2,"p":[103,106],"pp":"96","s":"Seoul Electric contract extended; Marine Bureau union election set for 5-7 March; 84,363 union members in 277 unions at end 1947; child labor inspections at Yong Dong Po mills."},{"t":"Part III: Public Services","lv":1,"p":[107,136],"pp":"101","s":"Rate increases, 7 February rail sabotage, improved power delivery (72,000,000 KWH), Seoul tram fare rise and flood-control planning across transportation, communications, utilities and public works."},{"t":"Section 1. Transportation","lv":2,"p":[108,118],"pp":"101","s":"Rail fares rose 25 to 50 percent; sabotage damaged 52 locomotives on 7 February; January railroad income and passengers (2,181,000) fell sharply; a Pusan pier warehouse burned."},{"t":"Section 2. Communications","lv":2,"p":[119,126],"pp":"112","s":"January Communications Department revenue and expenditure, postal deposits of 864,895,000 won, 172,970 telephone messages, and a 500-watt emergency transmitter installed 4 February."},{"t":"Section 3. Public Utilities","lv":2,"p":[127,130],"pp":"120","s":"72,000,000 KWH delivered in February; the Emergency Power Board extended the curfew to 2300; Seoul tram fares approved to rise from two to five won on 5 March."},{"t":"Section 4. Public Works","lv":2,"p":[131,136],"pp":"124","s":"The Seoul-Pusan highway cost 236,753,124 won to date; a Seoul-East Coast road planned at 100,000,000 won; 50,000,000 won flood-control allocation; 149 fires caused 219,143,550 won of loss."},{"t":"Part IV: Finance","lv":1,"p":[137,162],"pp":"131","s":"Revenue deposits exceeded Bank of Chosun withdrawals for the first time in February; non-government finance showed January deposit growth of 15 percent."},{"t":"Section 1. Government Finance","lv":2,"p":[138,150],"pp":"131","s":"Note issue fell 722 million won; supplementary appropriations of 627,299,553 won approved; the Military Governor's 25 February directive and 22,000-million-won revenue estimate for 1948/49; guaranteed loans of 12,420,275,259 won."},{"t":"Section 2. Non-Government Finance","lv":2,"p":[151,162],"pp":"144","s":"January deposits rose 6,882 million won to 52,208 million; loans fell 1,062 million; Foreign Exchange Bank made its first trade loan, on the SS Carrick Bend; new borrower registration rules."},{"t":"Part V: Government and Politics","lv":1,"p":[163,194],"pp":"157","s":"Administration, KILA's collapse, the election dispute, legal reform, police and the February disturbances, and North Korean interzone propaganda including the draft constitution."},{"t":"Section 1. Administration","lv":2,"p":[164,167],"pp":"157","s":"General Helmick's Washington mission for GARIOA funds; silk decontrolled; coal price increases recommended; reorganization to cut 40 percent of bureaus; vested farmland sale ordinance drafted; MG personnel at 2,583."},{"t":"Section 2. Korean Interim Legislative Assembly","lv":2,"p":[168,176],"pp":"161","s":"The Assembly approved the 1947 budget 38 to one, then split over the rightists' 'Urgent Proposal' to the UN; Speaker Kimm Kiu-sic and Vice-Speaker Choe Tong-O resigned on 27 February."},{"t":"General","lv":3,"p":[168,168],"pp":"161","s":"Sessions 200-207 in February, with the 1947 budget passed on 2 February and quorum failures, ending in the Speaker's and Vice-Speaker's resignations."},{"t":"Regular Business","lv":3,"p":[169,170],"pp":"162","s":"Kukmin College eviction dispute, Koje Island fishing petition, nationality law provisions, rice rationing complaints and the 7,000,000 won prostitution bribery inquiry."},{"t":"Rightist Proposal to UN Commission","lv":3,"p":[171,176],"pp":"164","s":"The 19 February Urgent Proposal signed by 43 members, the walkout of moderates and leftists, Yu Nae-wan's modified text passed 40 to 0, and the 27 February resignations."},{"t":"Section 3. Political Developments","lv":2,"p":[177,180],"pp":"170","s":"Menon's 19 February speech surveying parties, Kim Koo's six principles, his agreement with Kimm Kiu-sic, and the UN Interim Committee's 31-2 vote for elections in South Korea."},{"t":"General","lv":3,"p":[177,177],"pp":"170","s":"Rhee's South-only election camp opposed Kimm Kiu-sic's supporters of nationwide elections; Dr. Menon described the rightist, moderate and leftist parties."},{"t":"Kim Koo's Statement","lv":3,"p":[178,178],"pp":"171","s":"Kim Koo's six principles to UN Sub-Committee No. 2, including nationwide elections, release of political prisoners and a North-South leaders' conference, drew extreme rightist censure."},{"t":"Agreement Between Kim Koo and Kimm Kiu-sic","lv":3,"p":[179,179],"pp":"172","s":"Announced 7 February; Kim Koo and Kimm Kiu-sic backed a leaders' conference and on 10 February met Rhee, whose brief compromise collapsed over that proposal."},{"t":"UN Interim Committee Decision","lv":3,"p":[179,180],"pp":"172","s":"Menon's three alternatives and the 27 February 31-2 vote approving elections in South Korea, welcomed by Rhee and regretted by Kimm Kiu-sic and Kim Koo."},{"t":"Section 4. Legal Affairs","lv":2,"p":[181,184],"pp":"174","s":"Executive Orders Nos. 10, 11 and 13 restricted Sam Il Day ceremonies; Justice opinions Nos. 1415-1438; 191 prison guards graduated; a prosecutor beating case; a draft warrant and right-to-counsel ordinance."},{"t":"Section 5. National Police","lv":2,"p":[185,189],"pp":"178","s":"Civil-liberties reform proposals, Military Governor Dean's letter placing provincial police chiefs under governors, the 7-10 February strike and riots with casualty counts, and a commendation for catching 12 counterfeiters."},{"t":"Civil Liberties","lv":3,"p":[185,185],"pp":"178","s":"Police Department recommendations to cap detention at three days without charge, inspect jails, allow counsel, and curb graft and brutality."},{"t":"Police Under Civil Administration","lv":3,"p":[186,186],"pp":"179","s":"General Dean's 26 January letter, released 2 February, made provincial police chiefs answerable to governors for law and order, but not for organization, finance or personnel."},{"t":"Strikes and Disturbances","lv":3,"p":[187,188],"pp":"180","s":"The 7-10 February attempted general strike, locomotive sabotage at Taejon and Pusan, police-box attacks, a police casualty table, and a feeble second attempt on 26-28 February."},{"t":"Commendation by Military Governor","lv":3,"p":[189,189],"pp":"182","s":"General Dean commended four patrolmen who raided a Seoul basement counterfeiting operation on 8 February and arrested 12 suspects."},{"t":"Section 6. Interzone Activities","lv":2,"p":[190,194],"pp":"183","s":"Over 45,000 mail items exchanged at Tosong; Radio Pyongyang attacked General Hodge and the UN; the 8 February Korean People's Army; a draft North Korean constitution; a reported 12 February border clash."},{"t":"Mail Exchange","lv":3,"p":[190,190],"pp":"183","s":"Mail was swapped at Tosong on 7 and 21 February, with 13,465 letters sent south-to-north on the first date."},{"t":"Intercepts","lv":3,"p":[190,192],"pp":"183","s":"Radio Pyongyang propaganda, the 8 February inauguration of the Korean People's Army, Kim Il-sung's 1948 production quotas, and the 16 February draft constitution of the Democratic People's Republic."},{"t":"Border Incident","lv":3,"p":[193,194],"pp":"187","s":"Dae Han Ilbo claimed 50 North Korean police attacked a police box near Chungdae, Kyonggi-do, on 12 February and were routed by 20 mechanized South Korean police."},{"t":"Part VI: Social","lv":1,"p":[195,235],"pp":"189","s":"Health, education, culture, press opinion, Korean National Youth and women's activities, including the 14 February abolition of licensed prostitution and the related bribery scandal."},{"t":"Section 1. Public Health and Welfare","lv":2,"p":[196,202],"pp":"189","s":"Infant mortality study (62,198 deaths of children under five in 1946), typhus in Kyongsang-pukto, Public Act No. 7 ending licensed prostitution on 14 February, and 296,000 vials of penicillin received."},{"t":"Section 2. Education","lv":2,"p":[203,208],"pp":"196","s":"Middle school tuition to rise from 600 to 1,800 won on 1 April; elementary budget cut to 1,600 million won; Teachers' Training Center planned; 95,615 textbooks distributed in February."},{"t":"Section 3. Culture","lv":2,"p":[209,214],"pp":"202","s":"The Tai Guk flag symbolism, Lunar New Year 4281 on 10 February, museum fellowships for Kim Che-won, a Mozart concert, the first volume of the New Korean Dictionary and HLKA's 21st anniversary."},{"t":"Section 4. Press and Public Opinion","lv":2,"p":[215,224],"pp":"208","s":"Press reaction to the UN Commission, strikes, police reform, the prostitution-law bribery case and inflation; a poll of 938 Seoulites found 84.5 percent opposed the City Hall house sale."},{"t":"Introduction","lv":3,"p":[215,215],"pp":"208","s":"The UN Commission dominated press coverage; world affairs got more space, and a bribery scandal emerged late in the month."},{"t":"Appeals to Reason","lv":3,"p":[215,215],"pp":"208","s":"Editorials on Gandhi's murder, Korean disunity, militarism, narcotics and the plight of scholars, from papers such as Cha Yoo Shin Mun."},{"t":"UN Commission Activities","lv":3,"p":[216,217],"pp":"209","s":"Press coverage of Sub-Committee No. 2 interviews, the Political Affairs Council's view, Menon's 19 February speech, and the North Korean People's Republic announcement."},{"t":"Strikes","lv":3,"p":[218,218],"pp":"211","s":"Press accounts of the 7-10 February strike, Hodge's 10 February statement, and totals of 1,489 arrests (later 8,479 by 26 February) and 39 locomotives damaged."},{"t":"Police","lv":3,"p":[218,220],"pp":"211","s":"Newspaper debate over subordinating police to governors, UN criticism, rightist defense of Chough Pyong-ok's force, and the trial of Kim Doo-han for two murders."},{"t":"Bribery Case","lv":3,"p":[221,221],"pp":"214","s":"Eviction of 568 to 670 prostitutes on 14 February and their claim to have paid 7,000,000 won to legislators; General Dean's 27 February statement on press discussion."},{"t":"Economics","lv":3,"p":[222,222],"pp":"215","s":"Editorials on inflation, railroad rate increases, falling cloth prices, budget balancing and a petition to postpone land reform."},{"t":"Bureau of Public Opinion Polls","lv":3,"p":[223,223],"pp":"216","s":"Poll of 938 Seoul residents on 23-26 February: 84.5 percent of those aware opposed the sale of City Hall employee houses; a report on forced police contributions."},{"t":"Department of Public Information","lv":3,"p":[224,224],"pp":"217","s":"94 press releases issued, 16,000 Korean Pictorial copies printed, nightly monitoring of Radio Pyongyang begun, and a list of political parties given to the UN Commission."},{"t":"Section 5. Korean National Youth, Inc.","lv":2,"p":[225,231],"pp":"218","s":"Membership audited at 519,938; 227 delegates re-elected Lee Bum-suk on 22-23 February; leftist infiltration foiled during the 7 February riots; 55 training centers operating."},{"t":"Section 6. Women's Activities","lv":2,"p":[232,235],"pp":"225","s":"Korean women hosted the UN Commission as equals; the Women's Bureau (founded 1946) moved to Hokuksa Temple; the Altrusa Club and YWCA ran English and sewing classes."}],"by":"claude-sonnet (Claude Code subagent)"},{"id":"K-29-in-K-28","ia":"south-korean-interim-government-activities-no-28-1948-01","s":"K","no":29,"d":"1948-02","d2":"","t":"South Korean Interim Government Activities, No. 29 (February 1948)","v":"Bound in K-28","host":"K-28","rg":[[256,499]],"kind":"bound","found":"Title page 'M 105.13:29' on PDF p. 256","pg":244,"o":"Number 29 of South Korean Interim Government Activities, prepared by the National Economic Board for USAMGIK, covers February 1948. Cold-war tension dominates: leftist sabotage and strikes from 7 February, the UN Interim Committee's 27 February decision to observe elections in South Korea alone, the split between Rhee, Kim Koo and Kimm Kiu-sic, and the walkout of Speaker Kimm and 30 legislators that crippled the Interim Legislative Assembly. The report also records completion of rice collection at 97.6 per cent of quota, continued industrial shortages of bituminous coal, power and parts, record revenues exceeding expenditures for the first time since liberation, price indexes still rising, vested property disposal and land reform, police and civil-liberties reforms ahead of the May elections, and a 58 per cent rise in northern refugees. Detailed statistical tables on population, prices, production, trade, finance and labor, plus feature studies on the Korean National Youth, women and the flag, make it a rich source for historians of the occupation.","h":["The UN Interim Committee decided on 27 February, 31 to 2, to observe elections in South Korea alone, overshadowing every other development of the month.","Leftists began a revolt on 7 February; sabotage and riots brought 1,489 arrests in 7-12 February, with 6 police and 29 rioters killed, but no nation-wide general strike developed.","Speaker Kimm Kiu-sic and 30 legislators walked out of KILA over the rightists' Urgent Proposal to the UN, which passed amended 40 to 0 on 23 February and halted the Assembly.","Rice collection ended 15 February with 97.6 per cent of quota (754,579 metric tons) collected, though 225,000 tons of cereal imports are projected as needed in 1948/49.","For the first time since liberation, February revenue deposits exceeded withdrawals, by a net 386 million won, while Bank of Chosun note circulation fell 722 million won.","Refugee inflow from north of the 38th parallel rose 58 per cent over January, with 4,074 processed in February.","The Seoul retail price index reached 2,310, rising for a ninth straight month, while bituminous coal imports from Japan hit a record 83,330 tons, with 50,000 extra tons allotted for power.","Police administrative authority was placed under provincial governors, except on organization, administration, finances and personnel, as a safeguard against a police state, alongside new civil-liberties recommendations."],"k":["South Korean Interim Government","USAMGIK","UN Temporary Commission on Korea","May 1948 elections","Kimm Kiu-sic","Kim Koo","Rhee Syngman","KILA","rice collection","vested property","inflation and prices","bituminous coal","refugees from North Korea","National Police","Korean National Youth","February 1948"],"toc":[{"t":"Front matter","lv":1,"p":[256,265],"pp":"","s":"Cover of No. 29 (February 1948), prepared by the National Economic Board for USAMGIK, Google scan pages and printed contents (pp. i-v) listing Parts I-VI."},{"t":"Introduction","lv":1,"p":[266,267],"pp":"1","s":"Reviews February 1948 under cold-war tension: leftist revolt and sabotage from 7 February, the UN Interim Committee's 27 February decision on South-only elections, and Speaker Kimm Kiu-sic's walkout with 30 legislators from KILA."},{"t":"Part I: Basic Aspects","lv":1,"p":[268,283],"pp":"3","s":"Covers population and foreign affairs, where refugees from the North rose 58 per cent, and vested property, where sales stayed slow and leasing (299 plants) was tried."},{"t":"Section 1: Population and Foreign Affairs","lv":2,"p":[268,277],"pp":"3","s":"Refugees from the North rose 58 per cent over January (4,074 processed). The 1947 Seoul census counted 779,890 adults. Also Koreans in Manchuria (about 800,000), Osaka Korean schools and 192 illegal entrants."},{"t":"Section 2: Vested Property","lv":2,"p":[278,283],"pp":"13","s":"Vested property sales stalled for lack of reputable Korean buyers, so leasing (299 plants) and reorganization (409 companies examined) were tried; February sales totaled 35 for 4,600,800 won, with 423 audits."},{"t":"Part II: Production and Supply","lv":1,"p":[284,365],"pp":"19","s":"Covers food, agriculture, prices, industry, mining, allocation, trade and labor: rice collection reached 97.6 per cent of quota, retail prices rose a ninth month, coal and power stayed short, and unions reported 84,363 members."},{"t":"Section 1: Food Supply","lv":2,"p":[284,292],"pp":"19","s":"The 1947/48 rice collection ended 15 February at 97.6 per cent of quota (754,579 metric tons); only Cholla-pukto fell well short. Grain imports were 4,102 tons, and 225,000 tons of cereal imports are projected for 1948/49."},{"t":"Section 2: Agriculture, Forestry and Fisheries","lv":2,"p":[293,298],"pp":"28","s":"Covers FAO 1950 census planning, 123 crop-reporting employees trained, and the New Korea Company's 324,604 chungbo, to be sold at three times annual crop value over 15 years. Kyonggi-do 4-H clubs grew to 1,072."},{"t":"Section 3: Prices","lv":2,"p":[299,309],"pp":"34","s":"Seoul retail prices rose a ninth straight month, the index reaching 2,310, nearly four per cent above January. The wholesale index fell to 6,910, over six per cent below January's record. Rice control price steady since November 1945."},{"t":"Section 4: Industrial Production","lv":2,"p":[310,322],"pp":"45","s":"Industrial output stayed low, with data mostly for January. Shortages of wheat, pulp, pitch, coal and power hit flour, paper, briquettes and cement; Puksan paper mill resumed after 10 months, and government salterns were idle."},{"t":"Section 5: Mining and Minerals","lv":2,"p":[323,334],"pp":"58","s":"January anthracite output reached 41,357 metric tons, 32 per cent above December. Bituminous imports from Japan hit a record 83,330 tons in February, with 50,000 extra tons allotted for power; pitch stayed scarce."},{"t":"Section 6: Allocations and Distribution","lv":2,"p":[335,348],"pp":"70","s":"The new Consumer Goods Allocation Board and National Consumer Goods Office only organized in February. About 22,000,000 won of Civil Service Relief Committee goods were inventoried; 223,000,000 won of year-end bonus goods were distributed."},{"t":"Section 7: Imports and Exports","lv":2,"p":[349,360],"pp":"84","s":"Private trade estimates for February were imports of 260 million won against exports of 421 million. New Trustee Shipment and export-loan rules were issued; government cargoes included laver exports and coal, fertilizer, rice and salt imports."},{"t":"Section 8: Labor","lv":2,"p":[361,365],"pp":"96","s":"A seamen's bargaining election was set for 5-7 March in six ports, and Pungkuk Flour Mills agreed to pay back wages. Unions reported 84,363 members; Seoul Electric Company's union contract was extended."},{"t":"Part III: Public Services","lv":1,"p":[366,395],"pp":"101","s":"Covers transportation, communications, utilities and public works: rail fares rose 25 to 50 per cent, sabotage damaged 52 locomotives, power delivered was 72,000,000 KWH, and highway man-days reached 171,451."},{"t":"Section 1: Transportation","lv":2,"p":[366,376],"pp":"101","s":"New rail, marine and highway rates took effect, rail fares rising 25 per cent (first and second class) and 50 per cent (third). Sabotage from 7 February damaged 52 locomotives and ended night passenger trains."},{"t":"Section 2: Communications","lv":2,"p":[377,384],"pp":"112","s":"About 7,500 would-be telephone subscribers went unserved despite marked growth in 1947. January revenue fell from December, expenditures rose 1,000,000 won, an emergency transmitter was installed and 20,000 radios were repaired."},{"t":"Section 3: Public Utilities","lv":2,"p":[385,388],"pp":"120","s":"Power delivered to distributors in February was 72,000,000 KWH, of which 24,000,000 were produced in South Korea. The Emergency Power Board extended the curfew from 2200 to 2300; Seoul tram fares rose to five won from 5 March."},{"t":"Section 4: Public Works","lv":2,"p":[389,395],"pp":"124","s":"Mild weather let road and bridge repairs begin early; highway man-days reached 171,451. Some 236,753,123.85 won was spent on the Seoul-Pusan road, 50,000,000 won of flood funds allocated, and 149 fires caused 219,143,550 won loss."},{"t":"Part IV: Finance","lv":1,"p":[396,421],"pp":"131","s":"Covers government finance, where revenue deposits exceeded withdrawals for the first time and notes outstanding fell 722 million won, and non-government finance, where January deposits rose 6,882 million won to 52,208 million."},{"t":"Section 1: Government Finance","lv":2,"p":[396,408],"pp":"131","s":"Bank of Chosun notes fell 722 million won, and revenue deposits exceeded withdrawals by a net 386 million won for the first time. The Military Governor ordered a balanced 1948/49 budget of about 22,000 million won."},{"t":"Section 2: Non-Government Finance","lv":2,"p":[409,421],"pp":"144","s":"January data only: total deposits rose 6,882 million won, over 15 per cent, to 52,208 million, and cash balances rose over 614 million. Loans fell 1,062 million won; the Foreign Exchange Bank made its first trade loan."},{"t":"Part V: Government and Politics","lv":1,"p":[422,453],"pp":"157","s":"Covers administration, the legislature, politics, law, police and interzone affairs: the Urgent Proposal split KILA, the UN Interim Committee approved South Korean elections 31 to 2, and the 7-12 February riots brought 1,489 arrests."},{"t":"Section 1: Administration","lv":2,"p":[422,425],"pp":"157","s":"Maj.-Gen. Helmick was in Washington 19 January to 4 February to justify 1948/49 GARIOA and rehabilitation funds. Silk was decontrolled, coal prices raised, and a reorganization plan would cut 40 per cent of bureaus."},{"t":"Section 2: Korean Interim Legislative Assembly","lv":2,"p":[426,434],"pp":"161","s":"KILA approved the 1947 budget 38 to one on 2 February. The rightists' Urgent Proposal asking the UN Commission for a South Korean election passed amended 40 to 0 on 23 February; Speaker Kimm Kiu-sic and Vice-Speaker Choe Tong-O resigned."},{"t":"Section 3: Political Developments","lv":2,"p":[435,438],"pp":"170","s":"Rhee Syngman's rightists favored a separate South Korean election, while Kimm Kiu-sic and Kim Koo wanted a North-South leaders' conference. The three leaders met 10 February; the UN Interim Committee approved a South Korean election 31 to 2 on 27 February."},{"t":"Section 4: Legal Affairs","lv":2,"p":[439,442],"pp":"174","s":"The Department of Justice published an order barring separate Sam Il Day demonstrations and issued opinions on overtime pay and vested property. 191 guards graduated, and Korean courts admitted five northern lawyers."},{"t":"Section 5: National Police","lv":2,"p":[443,447],"pp":"178","s":"Police Department civil-liberties recommendations included release after three days without charge. The 7-12 February strike and riot wave brought 1,489 arrests, with 6 police and 29 rioters killed; provincial police chiefs answer to governors."},{"t":"Section 6: Interzone Activities","lv":2,"p":[448,453],"pp":"183","s":"More than 45,000 pieces of mail crossed the 38th parallel at Tosong. Radio Pyongyang covered the 8 February inauguration of the Korean People's Army; the draft North Korean constitution (102 articles) was broadcast 16 February."},{"t":"Part VI: Social","lv":1,"p":[454,493],"pp":"189","s":"Covers health, education, culture, press, youth and women: 62,198 of 173,305 deaths were children under five, middle school tuition rises to 1,800 won, and Korean National Youth reached 519,938 members."},{"t":"Section 1: Public Health and Welfare","lv":2,"p":[454,460],"pp":"189","s":"A study found 62,198 of 173,305 deaths in April-December 1946 were children under five, about 40 per cent preventable with penicillin or sulfonamides. Typhus, typhoid and diphtheria led; prostitution was banned 14 February."},{"t":"Section 2: Education","lv":2,"p":[461,466],"pp":"196","s":"Provincial education directors met 18-19 February. Middle school tuition rises from 600 to 1,800 won from 1 April and elementary tuition is abolished; 500,000 surplus textbooks were obtained and 182 English teachers completed a course."},{"t":"Section 3: Culture","lv":2,"p":[467,472],"pp":"202","s":"An essay explains the Tai Guk symbol on the Korean flag; year 4281 was celebrated 10 February. Kim Che-won and Kim Won-yong won Rockefeller fellowships; HLKA marked its 21st anniversary and the first New Korean Dictionary volume appeared."},{"t":"Section 4: Press and Public Opinion","lv":2,"p":[473,482],"pp":"208","s":"Press centered on the UN Temporary Commission, with calls for unity after Gandhi's murder. Papers covered the 7-10 February strikes (8,479 arrests by 26 February) and a 7,000,000 won bribery scandal."},{"t":"Section 5: Korean National Youth, Inc.","lv":2,"p":[483,489],"pp":"218","s":"The 22-23 February council of 227 delegates re-elected Lee Bum-suk as president. Membership reached 519,938 against a 600,000 goal after leftist infiltration during the 7 February riots was blocked."},{"t":"Section 6: Women's Activities","lv":2,"p":[490,493],"pp":"225","s":"Korean women served as UN Commission hostesses. The Women's Bureau (Director Koh Whang-kyung, founded September 1946) published \"New Homemaking\" and moved to Hokuksa Temple 27 February; the Altrusa Club awarded a nursing fellowship."},{"t":"Back matter","lv":1,"p":[494,499],"pp":"","s":"Blank Google-digitized pages, a back-cover image, a library date-due slip (stamped June 2010) and a final page of meaningless OCR symbols."}],"by":"claude-sonnet (Claude Code subagent)"},{"id":"J-30","ia":"summation-non-military-activities-japan-no-30-1948-03","s":"J","no":30,"d":"1948-03","d2":"","t":"Summation of Non-Military Activities in Japan, No. 30 (March 1948)","v":"","pg":167,"o":"Summation No. 30 covers March 1948, the month in which Hitoshi Ashida formed a coalition cabinet of Democrats (six), Social Democrats (eight) and People's Cooperative Party members (two) on the basis of a three-party policy agreement. It reports the new Police Reorganization Law taking effect on 7 March, the founding of the Democratic Liberal Party on 15 March, the reconvened Second Diet and the Prime Minister's policy address, the winding up of the purge screening committees by 10 May, discoveries of hoarded goods, and the defense summation at the International Military Tribunal. The economic sections give February and March statistics on agriculture, mining, heavy industry, textiles, communications, labor disputes (coal, electric power, express transport), trade, rationing, prices, banking and finance, science and technology. Social sections cover communicable disease, public assistance, repatriation, vital statistics, education, religion and the media. Historians will find the detailed cabinet formation narrative, party realignments and production and finance tables especially useful.","h":["A three-party policy agreement among the Democratic, Social Democratic and People's Cooperative Parties, with concessions to the Social Democratic left wing, let Prime Minister Hitoshi Ashida form a cabinet of six Democrats, eight Social Democrats and two People's Cooperative members.","The Liberal Party and minor groups formed the Democratic Liberal Party on 15 March, the largest Diet group, with 153 members in the House of Representatives and 44 in the House of Councillors.","The Police Reorganization Law took effect 7 March, and autonomous police forces were set up in all 1,605 municipalities with populations over 5,000; the shokutaku system was abolished on 31 March.","Purge screening examined 674,579 persons, removing 4,861 from office and barring 2,816; 165 of 1,424 information-media executives were barred, and public-office screening committees were to end on 10 May.","The Second National Diet reconvened on 20 March; Ashida gave his administrative address and Economic Stabilization Board head Kuroki outlined economic policy, followed by interpellations on 22-25 March.","At the International Military Tribunal the defense summation ran from 2 to 31 March, arguing that ex-post-facto penalization of Japanese leaders might be seen as a gross injustice.","Labor disputes included coal-miner wage demands, a mediated settlement accepted 25 March by the electric power union, an express company union award on 9 March, and the arrest of 13 union officials at Aikodo Printing.","Bank of Japan notes outstanding reached 218,775,000,000 yen on 31 March, Reconstruction Finance Bank loans totaled 54,439,200,000 yen on 29 February, and February land sales to tenants were 61,857 cho."],"k":["Ashida cabinet","three-party policy agreement","Democratic Liberal Party","Police Reorganization Law","shokutaku system","purge screening","Second National Diet","International Military Tribunal","hoarded goods","labor disputes","industrial production","Reconstruction Finance Bank","agrarian reform","public health","SCAP"],"toc":[{"t":"Front matter","lv":1,"p":[1,5],"pp":"","s":"Cover for Summation No. 30 (March 1948), table of contents by part and section, lists of charts, maps and graphs (94 items), and the map of Japan."},{"t":"Part I: General","lv":1,"p":[6,15],"pp":"1","s":"Digest of the month: no change in SCAP organization, formation of the Ashida cabinet, police reform, purge figures, war crimes trials, and economic and social highlights."},{"t":"Section 1. Organization Under SCAP","lv":2,"p":[7,7],"pp":"3","s":"No change occurred in the non-military organization of General Headquarters, SCAP, during March."},{"t":"Section 2. Political Activities","lv":2,"p":[7,8],"pp":"3","s":"Covers the Ashida coalition cabinet, the 7 March police law, the Democratic Liberal Party's 153 House seats, the Diet reconvening on 20 March, purge totals, crime figures and war crimes trials."},{"t":"Section 3. Economic Activities","lv":2,"p":[9,13],"pp":"7","s":"February output rose in coke, copper, textiles and power; cement fell to 85,358 tons. SCAP warned on 29 March that communications work stoppages breached its 31 January 1947 strike statement; the Diet passed Supplementary Budget No. 15."},{"t":"Section 4. Social Activities","lv":2,"p":[14,15],"pp":"17","s":"Tokyo opened a Model Health Center demonstrating 12 basic health services; the Japan Medical Association was reorganized; 12 institutions became four-year universities; press debated the Ashida cabinet and the new ¥2,920 wage base."},{"t":"Part II: Political","lv":1,"p":[16,50],"pp":"21","s":"Detailed account of the Ashida cabinet formation, parties, Diet and purge, police and prison statistics, Japanese courts, and war crimes trials including the Tokyo tribunal's defense summation."},{"t":"Section 1. Government and Politics","lv":2,"p":[17,37],"pp":"23","s":"Narrates the Ashida cabinet formation, hoarded goods, budgets, the police reorganization, party realignment, the Diet session and the winding up of the purge committees."},{"t":"Political Developments: Formation of New Cabinet","lv":3,"p":[17,22],"pp":"23","s":"Three-party policy agreement, left-wing Social Democratic reaction, cabinet membership, the Prime Minister's statement, and ministers' policy statements with biographical sketches."},{"t":"Hoarded Goods","lv":3,"p":[23,24],"pp":"","s":"Procurators found goods worth $9,000,000,000 at former Nakajima Aircraft plants, and Gifu Diet member Keichi Yamamoto was indicted. A 27 March ordinance, effective 31 March, required declaration of undeclared stocks, with false reports confiscated."},{"t":"National Government","lv":3,"p":[24,26],"pp":"","s":"Cabinet agreed on 1948-49 budgets and three supplementary budgets passed; the new police system began 7 March and the shokutaku system was abolished."},{"t":"Political Parties","lv":3,"p":[26,29],"pp":"","s":"Positions of the Democratic, Social Democratic, People's Cooperative, Liberal, Social Renovation and Communist parties and the Green Breeze Society; the Democratic Liberal Party formed 15 March."},{"t":"The Diet","lv":3,"p":[29,36],"pp":"","s":"Ashida's administrative address, party comments, interpellations by Saito, Asanuma and Itaya, Director General Kuroki's economic address, and bills enacted and introduced."},{"t":"The Purge","lv":3,"p":[36,37],"pp":"","s":"Public office and information media screening results, loss of records, and discontinuance of the purge committees by 10 May."},{"t":"Section 2. Public Safety","lv":2,"p":[38,40],"pp":"65","s":"Black-market and crime reports and police reorganization; 146,669 offenses and 76,802 arrests in January, and 83,872 prisoners on 29 February."},{"t":"Section 3. Legal Affairs and War Crimes","lv":2,"p":[41,50],"pp":"71","s":"Supreme Court caseload, 78 atrocity investigations, 12 trials of 73 suspects, and Tokyo tribunal summations on Shiratori, Suzuki, Togo, Tojo and Umezu, and defense of Hata, Doihara and Hiranuma."},{"t":"Part III: Economic","lv":1,"p":[51,144],"pp":"91","s":"Statistical sector reports for February-March 1948 on agriculture, mining, industry, textiles, transport, communications, labor, trade, rationing, finance, property control and science."},{"t":"Section 1. Agriculture and Fisheries","lv":2,"p":[52,54],"pp":"93","s":"Land sales to tenants reached 61,857 cho in February (cumulative 280,763 cho); also livestock slaughter, sericulture, marine landings, fishing petroleum allocations and whaling."},{"t":"Section 2. Forestry and Mining","lv":2,"p":[55,60],"pp":"99","s":"February log production of 71,133,700 cubic feet, plus firewood and charcoal output, coal production and deliveries, crude oil drilling and mine production."},{"t":"Section 3. Heavy Industries","lv":2,"p":[61,70],"pp":"111","s":"February coke output of 192,570 metric tons; also iron and steel, nonferrous rolling, cement, shipbuilding, chemicals, machine tools, industrial machinery and rolling stock."},{"t":"Section 4. Manufacturing","lv":2,"p":[71,77],"pp":"131","s":"February pulp output rose to 29,313 tons from 23,230; window glass fell 18,903 cases; Nissan led truck chassis (511 units); bicycles reached 38,011; three-wheelers hit a postwar high of 952; watches and sewing machines also set highs."},{"t":"Section 5. Textile Industries","lv":2,"p":[78,92],"pp":"145","s":"Cotton fabric output at a postwar high, raw silk back to December level, rayon increases, and wool, bast fiber, knit goods and clothing supply and stock tables."},{"t":"Section 6. Transportation and Public Utilities","lv":2,"p":[93,98],"pp":"175","s":"156,740 registered commercial vehicles in February; rail traffic indexes, water-borne cargo, air transport, and electric energy and gas generation and consumption."},{"t":"Section 7. Communications","lv":2,"p":[99,106],"pp":"187","s":"Competitive bidding reached 35 percent of telecommunications contract value; 1,179,972 telephones in service on 29 February; 6,330,057 radio licensees; Hongkong radiotelegraph circuit opened 1 March; 436,732 employees; Supplementary Budget No. 10 raised wages to the ¥2,920 level."},{"t":"Section 8. Labor","lv":2,"p":[107,114],"pp":"203","s":"Kanju Kato named Labor Minister; union membership, coal, electric power and express-company wage disputes, the Aikodo Printing production-control case, wages and employment."},{"t":"Section 9. Imports and Exports","lv":2,"p":[115,119],"pp":"219","s":"January private trade approvals of 21 import contracts ($1,838,395) and 839 export contracts ($6,050,598), with breakdowns by country, balances and commodities."},{"t":"Section 10. Rationing and Price Control","lv":2,"p":[120,126],"pp":"229","s":"Staple crop collection quota exceeded by 0.9 percent, rice stocks near 3,000,000 tons, consumer price index and ten rationed commodities."},{"t":"Section 11. Finance","lv":2,"p":[127,134],"pp":"243","s":"Bank of Japan notes stood at 218,775,000,000 yen on 31 March; Reconstruction Finance Bank loans were 54,439,200,000 yen; also banks, deposit bureau, securities liquidation and budget."},{"t":"Section 12. Property Control and Reparations","lv":2,"p":[135,138],"pp":"259","s":"Looted property worth 436,000 yen reported and 35,000 yen restituted; also looted ships, antitrust and cartel actions, and reparations."},{"t":"Section 13. Science and Technology","lv":2,"p":[139,144],"pp":"267","s":"Renewal Committee for the Organization of Science met 23-24 February; also research activities, personnel, patents and standards, and publications."},{"t":"Part IV: Social","lv":1,"p":[145,167],"pp":"279","s":"Model health center opened 15 March; also public health, welfare, education, religion and media reports, repatriation, vital statistics and SCAP information libraries."},{"t":"Section 1. Public Health and Welfare","lv":2,"p":[146,155],"pp":"281","s":"Model Health Center opened in Tokyo 15 March; communicable diseases, medical supplies, narcotics, public assistance, LARA relief, repatriation and vital statistics."},{"t":"Section 2. Education, Religion, Cultural Resources and Media of Expression","lv":2,"p":[156,167],"pp":"301","s":"Teacher salary study, teacher conferences, Catholic church statistics, national treasures, press, radio, films, theater, music, and SCAP libraries with 48,729 visits; ends with map page."}],"by":"claude-sonnet (Claude Code subagent)"},{"id":"K-30","ia":"south-korean-interim-government-activities-no-30-1948-03","s":"K","no":30,"d":"1948-03","d2":"","t":"South Korean Interim Government Activities, No. 30 (March 1948)","v":"","pg":655,"o":"Number 30 of South Korean Interim Government Activities covers March 1948, bound in a Google Books volume with Nos. 31 (April) and 32 (May). It reports a turbulent month: Korean politics divided over the UN-observed 10 May election in the south, communist-inspired riots under a South Korea Labor Party plan captured by police, a wildcat Seoul Electric strike, and Kim Koo and Kimm Kiu-sic's exchange with Pyongyang leaders over a joint conference. Administrative items include Ordinance No. 176 (protection from unlawful arrest), creation of the National Land Administration to sell former Japanese farmland, and formation of the National Election Committee. Economic sections give detailed tables on prices, industrial production, coal, trade, finance, transport and labor, with prices softening and imports exceeding exports. Historians will find useful the National Economic Board statistics, FY 1948/49 aid planning, refugee flows, Mukden repatriation planning, and press and youth-group reports.","h":["Ordinance No. 176, effective 1 April, gave Koreans protection against unlawful arrest and set time allowances for prisoners, while Ordinance No. 174 of 22 March created the National Land Administration and dissolved the New Korea Company.","A South Korea Labor Party riot master plan was seized, with attacks on police boxes, Chinhae city hall burned, and 41 arrests in Kyongsang-namdo, as Korean politics split over the UN-observed 10 May election.","Seoul Electric Company workers struck without authorization from 13 March, halting Seoul streetcars 12-17 March; Declaration No. 6 declared the strike unlawful on 15 March.","Southbound refugee flow rose nearly 200 percent over February, and a plan was drawn up to fly 2,000 Korean leaders from Mukden to Tientsin and ship them to Inchon.","Kim Koo and Kimm Kiu-sic wrote to Kim Il-sung and Kim Doo-bong in Pyongyang; North Korea accepted a joint conference and suggested 14 April, while Kimm Kiu-sic's Assembly resignation was accepted 2 March.","February flour output rose 123 percent and paper 73.6 percent, anthracite quota rose to 76,000 tons, and bituminous coal imports from Japan reached 75,243 tons in March.","Private imports of 625.9 million won exceeded exports of 384.4 million, about 95 percent of exports going to Hong Kong; Seoul retail prices fell, cleaned rice down nearly seven percent.","Korean National Youth reported 646,331 members, and 2,151,740 members worked 75,289,176 volunteer hours in 15 months, amid opposition from rightist and leftist groups."],"k":["South Korean Interim Government","USAMGIK","National Economic Board","May 1948 election","National Election Committee","Ordinance No. 176","National Land Administration","vested property","refugees from North Korea","Mukden repatriates","Seoul Electric strike","Korean National Youth","price and production statistics","coal and electric power","North-South leaders' conference","Radio Pyongyang"],"toc":[{"t":"Front matter","lv":1,"p":[1,19],"pp":"","s":"Google Books cover pages, cover of No. 30 (March 1948, National Economic Board), Board membership list at 31 March, Arthur G. Peterson's foreword on statistics, and full table of contents."},{"t":"Summary","lv":1,"p":[20,23],"pp":"1","s":"Chairman Wilhelm Anderson's overview: factional split over the UN-observed May election, communist riots under the South Korea Labor Party plan, Ordinance No. 176, land reform, falling prices, and a $125 million FY 1948/49 aid program."},{"t":"PART I. BASIC ASPECTS","lv":1,"p":[24,35],"pp":"","s":"Refugee influx from the north up nearly 200 percent, Mukden and Japan-based Koreans, and Office of Property Custody work including 714 company investigations and rulings on Korean directors."},{"t":"Section 1. Population and Foreign Affairs","lv":2,"p":[24,31],"pp":"5","s":"Southbound refugees up nearly 200 percent over February, 2,000 Mukden leaders to be repatriated, and Korean complaints in Japan over repatriation, suffrage, schools and organizations."},{"t":"Population Migration","lv":3,"p":[24,24],"pp":"5","s":"Southbound refugee flow jumped about 200 percent over February; refugees spread among eight provinces; those bypassing camps lack ration cards, fueling crime."},{"t":"Koreans in Manchuria","lv":3,"p":[25,27],"pp":"6","s":"58,558 Koreans came from Manchuria since the surrender; plan to fly 2,000 leaders from Mukden to Tientsin and ship them to Inchon, with 15,000 more under study."},{"t":"Repatriation from Japan","lv":3,"p":[28,28],"pp":"9","s":"Osaka Koreans petitioned against slow repatriation processing (three months minimum) and the 1,000-yen exchange limit."},{"t":"Korean Suffrage Ruling","lv":3,"p":[28,28],"pp":"9","s":"Koreans remaining in Japan have kept Japanese status since 20 November 1946 and cannot vote; Japan-based factions nevertheless split over the election."},{"t":"Korean Protest Meeting in Japan","lv":3,"p":[29,29],"pp":"10","s":"About 15,000 Koreans at an Osaka rally on 1 March, organized by the communist League, adopted ten resolutions, including simultaneous US-Soviet withdrawal."},{"t":"Korean Education in Japan","lv":3,"p":[30,30],"pp":"11","s":"Japanese vice-minister rejected Korean school autonomy; schools consolidated in Osaka and Kyoto; pupils sent form letters to General Mullins."},{"t":"Other Korean Affairs in Japan","lv":3,"p":[31,31],"pp":"12","s":"Korea Foreign Trade Association showrooms in Tokyo and Osaka, the Tamkyo mediation club, and Japanese press reaction to North Korea's draft constitution."},{"t":"Section 2. Vested Property","lv":2,"p":[32,35],"pp":"13","s":"Office of Property Custody sales and appraisals, decentralized audits, 714 company investigations, and rulings on Korean directors and stolen-goods claims."},{"t":"General","lv":3,"p":[32,32],"pp":"13","s":"Four OPC divisions were active; provincial affairs, disposal, audit and operations are summarized."},{"t":"Property Disposal","lv":3,"p":[32,32],"pp":"13","s":"Unusually large March sales of moveables and properties, including agar agar, coins and Datsun cars; vested company counts given by province."},{"t":"Operations","lv":3,"p":[33,33],"pp":"14","s":"Corporate reorganization sub-section finished 714 investigations; 54 sales of vested shares ordered; 3,505 financial statements received."},{"t":"Audits and Investigations","lv":3,"p":[33,33],"pp":"14","s":"Audit Division decentralized desk audits to nine provincial OPC branches."},{"t":"Provincial Affairs","lv":3,"p":[34,34],"pp":"15","s":"Nine Korean liaison men appointed, alternating weeks between field and national office; more inspection trips made."},{"t":"Policies and Decisions","lv":3,"p":[34,35],"pp":"15","s":"Rulings on Korean board representation in firms with small Korean holdings and on claims to proceeds of stolen property."},{"t":"PART II. PRODUCTION AND SUPPLY","lv":1,"p":[36,115],"pp":"","s":"Agriculture and land administration, price softening, industrial and coal output, allocations, trade (imports 625.9 million won, exports 384.4 million) and labor, including the Seoul Electric strike."},{"t":"Section 1. Agriculture, Forestry and Fisheries","lv":2,"p":[36,48],"pp":"17","s":"National Land Administration created, straw-products collection completed, silk decontrolled, fertilizer imports over 277,000 tons, plus seed, forestry and fisheries items."},{"t":"Agricultural Economics Bureau","lv":3,"p":[36,37],"pp":"17","s":"Farm household tables for 1945-46 show fewer large farms; provinces authorized to adjust seed rice prices."},{"t":"Agricultural Production Bureau","lv":3,"p":[38,39],"pp":"19","s":"Livestock feed, 14 million chicks hatched in 1947, cotton goal of 182,000 chungbo, silk decontrol of 27 February, 38 million mulberry seedlings, irrigation ponds."},{"t":"Bureau of Forestry","lv":3,"p":[40,40],"pp":"21","s":"104 million seedlings available for spring planting; 7 million won for planting vested forest; 1947 forest product output."},{"t":"Bureau of Fisheries","lv":3,"p":[40,40],"pp":"21","s":"Exports to Japan of 517 tons of dried anchovy-type fish and 656,760 bundles of laver completed contracts."},{"t":"Korean Agriculture Association","lv":3,"p":[40,44],"pp":"21","s":"Fertilizer imports exceeded 277,000 tons, 67 percent sold; straw bag and rope collection finished; seed potato shipments."},{"t":"Agricultural Improvement Service","lv":3,"p":[45,46],"pp":"26","s":"Experiment station 1948 project lists, sericulture technicians' meeting, and Extension Service school; 1,100 4-H clubs with 25,000 members."},{"t":"National Land Administration","lv":3,"p":[47,48],"pp":"28","s":"Ordinance No. 174 of 22 March dissolved the New Korea Company; its land passes to the new National Land Administration, the remainder to OPC."},{"t":"Section 2. Prices","lv":2,"p":[49,56],"pp":"30","s":"Seoul prices softened in March, cleaned rice down nearly seven percent; unweighted indexes dropped while wholesale metals, building materials and textiles rose."},{"t":"Retail Prices","lv":3,"p":[49,49],"pp":"30","s":"Most retail prices fell; cleaned rice down nearly seven percent, suggesting inflation had halted."},{"t":"Wholesale Prices","lv":3,"p":[49,56],"pp":"30","s":"Metals, building materials and textiles rose; fuel unchanged; grains and fish fell."},{"t":"Section 3. Industrial Production","lv":2,"p":[57,68],"pp":"38","s":"February output recovered: flour up 123 percent, paper 73.6 percent, cotton yarn 10.6 percent; cement halted by power shortage; data coverage criticized."},{"t":"Processed Food","lv":3,"p":[57,57],"pp":"38","s":"Flour 2,315 tons versus 1,039 in January; noodles down 39.5 percent seasonally."},{"t":"Textiles","lv":3,"p":[58,58],"pp":"39","s":"Cotton yarn up 10.6 percent, cloth up 11.8 percent, both still below the 1947 monthly average."},{"t":"Paper","lv":3,"p":[59,59],"pp":"40","s":"Production 392 tons; Pukun Mill set records but faced pulp and coal shortages."},{"t":"Hardware","lv":3,"p":[60,60],"pp":"41","s":"Nail output down 17 percent because of the New Year holiday and coal and power shortages."},{"t":"Chemicals","lv":3,"p":[61,64],"pp":"42","s":"Briquettes nearly stopped, no cement in February, rubber tires mixed; first data on oxygen, carbide, matches and soap."},{"t":"Miscellaneous","lv":3,"p":[65,65],"pp":"46","s":"South Korean electric insulator output rose from 3,120 pieces in January to 11,760 in February 1948; lead pencil output climbed from 3,089 gross in January to 4,521 in March."},{"t":"Monopoly Bureau Products","lv":3,"p":[66,68],"pp":"47","s":"Government salterns idle for a fifth month; private plants made 148 tons of salt; cigarettes up 19.1 percent."},{"t":"Section 4. Mining and Minerals","lv":2,"p":[69,80],"pp":"50","s":"Record bituminous coal imports (75,243 tons), rising anthracite output and quotas, a coal tar pitch shortage, and tungsten, copper and zinc notes."},{"t":"Indigenous Anthracite and Lignite Coal","lv":3,"p":[69,73],"pp":"50","s":"Anthracite exceeded the January record; quota raised to 76,000 tons; stockpiles 182,000 tons; March allocations by the Coal Committee."},{"t":"Bituminous Coal","lv":3,"p":[74,74],"pp":"55","s":"Japanese imports 75,243 tons in March against a record 83,330 in February; Commerce allotted 7,500 tons."},{"t":"Shipments to Consumers","lv":3,"p":[75,75],"pp":"56","s":"Consumer receipts lagged production owing to transit losses and rail limits."},{"t":"Pitch","lv":3,"p":[75,75],"pp":"56","s":"Coal tar pitch shortage cut briquette output 40 percent; US supply on order."},{"t":"Other Minerals","lv":3,"p":[76,80],"pp":"57","s":"Copper, tungsten (Sangdong flotation cells) and zinc concentrates (300 tons from Mitsubishi) reported."},{"t":"Section 5. Allocations and Distribution","lv":2,"p":[81,95],"pp":"62","s":"Consumer Goods Allocation Board, civilian supply and petroleum allocations, salt, ex-Japanese metals, and distribution of Korean-produced goods and 1947 year-end bonus goods."},{"t":"Consumer Goods Allocation Board","lv":3,"p":[81,81],"pp":"62","s":"New unified distribution procedure for government and vested-industry employees; publicity efforts."},{"t":"Allocation of Civilian Supplies","lv":3,"p":[82,82],"pp":"62","s":"March allocations to Commerce, Agriculture and Finance; 273 miscellaneous Honolulu FLC items."},{"t":"Petroleum","lv":3,"p":[83,85],"pp":"64","s":"Petroleum allocations and sales; rush before the 1 April price rise; drum deposit doubled; fuel oil to the Pusan power barge."},{"t":"Salt Distribution","lv":3,"p":[86,86],"pp":"67","s":"29,516 tons distributed in FY 1947/48; spring soy-sauce distribution began 1 March."},{"t":"Allocation of Ex-Japanese Materials","lv":3,"p":[87,87],"pp":"68","s":"Brass, copper coins, scrap iron, steel and minerals from captured Japanese stocks allocated; zinc exhausted."},{"t":"Allocation of Korean-Produced Commodities","lv":3,"p":[88,91],"pp":"69","s":"Board met 18 March; goods worth 3.5 million won allocated to employees of 13 departments."},{"t":"Distribution of Korean-Produced Commodities","lv":3,"p":[92,95],"pp":"73","s":"1947 year-end bonus goods distributed through the Federation of Financial Associations."},{"t":"Section 6. Imports and Exports","lv":2,"p":[96,107],"pp":"77","s":"Private imports 625.9 million won against exports 384.4 million; 95 percent of exports to Hong Kong; first Korean merchant ship to Hong Kong; coal, fertilizer and grain imports."},{"t":"Private Trade Statistics","lv":3,"p":[96,100],"pp":"77","s":"Imports 625,941,711 won, exports 384,449,388 won (agar agar, eel, ginseng, furs); Pusan handled most trade."},{"t":"Private Trade Activities","lv":3,"p":[101,101],"pp":"82","s":"Korea Steamship's Aeng Do Wan sailed for Hong Kong 31 March; ship arrivals and cargoes at Pusan."},{"t":"Government-to-Government Transactions","lv":3,"p":[101,102],"pp":"82","s":"Government trade with the United States, China and Japan; activities at Pusan."},{"t":"Korean Items for Americans in Korea","lv":3,"p":[103,103],"pp":"84","s":"Sales to the Army Exchange Service in March were the highest since the program began in August 1947."},{"t":"Civilian Supply Imports","lv":3,"p":[104,107],"pp":"85","s":"75,243 tons coal, 49,605 tons fertilizer (37,336 ammonium nitrate), wheat, rice, sugar, 34,619 tons salt; petroleum figures first included."},{"t":"Section 7. Labor","lv":2,"p":[108,115],"pp":"89","s":"Department of Labor field training conference, Seoul Electric Company wildcat strike and Declaration No. 6, collective bargaining, child labor, and labor education."},{"t":"Organization and Organizational Training","lv":3,"p":[108,109],"pp":"89","s":"New National Labor Mediation Board terms; 25-27 March conference on lectures, reports, bargaining quotas, police interference and a cost-of-living survey."},{"t":"Labor Union Activities","lv":3,"p":[110,112],"pp":"91","s":"Seoul Electric strike from 13 March, declared unlawful 15 March; Marine Bureau election won by Tai Han; conciliation cases."},{"t":"Labor Laws","lv":3,"p":[113,113],"pp":"94","s":"Employment of minors in government offices and enforcement of the Maximum Working Hours Law; child labor investigation."},{"t":"Labor Education","lv":3,"p":[114,115],"pp":"95","s":"Three booklets published, 7,000 copies of Labor News, and HLKA weekly radio programs."},{"t":"PART III. PUBLIC SERVICES","lv":1,"p":[116,145],"pp":"","s":"Rainbow Coastal Service and rail traffic gains, sabotage-hit communications, 73.8 million KWH electricity with Seoul streetcars halted by strike, and the Seoul-Pusan highway returned to provincial control."},{"t":"Section 1. Transportation","lv":2,"p":[116,126],"pp":"97","s":"Rainbow Coastal Service launched, no repeat of February rail sabotage, February rail receipts up 12 percent, freight up 14 percent, LST recalls."},{"t":"General and Revenue and Expenditures","lv":3,"p":[116,116],"pp":"97","s":"Five-schedule Rainbow coastal passenger and freight service; department revenue up 24.5 million won."},{"t":"Railroads","lv":3,"p":[117,121],"pp":"98","s":"Railroad receipts and traffic up; three more locomotives operating; employment down for a seventh month; coal use slightly lower."},{"t":"Marine Transportation","lv":3,"p":[122,125],"pp":"102","s":"Marine receipts down 4 million won, staff down 10 percent, two LSTs recalled to SCAP, shipping demand exceeding supply."},{"t":"Highway and Air Transportation","lv":3,"p":[126,126],"pp":"107","s":"Expenditures of the highway and air transportation bureaus rose slightly in February 1948, while their employee numbers fell; figures are given in tables 11 and 12."},{"t":"Section 2. Communications","lv":2,"p":[127,135],"pp":"108","s":"Leftist sabotage interrupted lines; revenue fell 16 million won; postal savings, telephone, telegraph, power-carrier survey and broadcasting repairs."},{"t":"General, Revenue and Expenditures, Employment, Special Functions","lv":3,"p":[127,128],"pp":"108","s":"Sabotage campaign, revenue decline, spending cuts, and the first staff decrease since October 1947."},{"t":"Postal Deposits and Insurance","lv":3,"p":[129,132],"pp":"110","s":"Depositors up 1,819 but deposits down 22 million won; insurance policies slightly fewer."},{"t":"Telephone and Telegraph","lv":3,"p":[133,133],"pp":"114","s":"Messages and revenue fell; Korean engineers to survey power-carrier systems."},{"t":"Communications Bureau","lv":3,"p":[134,134],"pp":"115","s":"Weather-station space at Inchon provided for radio equipment; related technical work."},{"t":"Korean Broadcasting Corporation","lv":3,"p":[135,135],"pp":"116","s":"Public address systems repaired by month end; auxiliary transmission line under construction."},{"t":"Section 3. Public Utilities","lv":2,"p":[136,139],"pp":"117","s":"Electricity distribution 73.8 million KWH, Seoul streetcars halted 12-17 March by strike, Pusan fare increases."},{"t":"Electricity Distribution","lv":3,"p":[136,136],"pp":"117","s":"Distribution 73.8 million KWH; generation 26.8 million KWH."},{"t":"Emergency Power Board Actions","lv":3,"p":[137,137],"pp":"118","s":"Capability estimates and conservation measures; illegal floor heating a major loss."},{"t":"Power Plants","lv":3,"p":[137,138],"pp":"118","s":"High thermal and hydro output; 13 trouble calls repaired."},{"t":"Street Railways","lv":3,"p":[139,139],"pp":"120","s":"Seoul service halted by the strike; Pusan fares raised on Committee advice."},{"t":"Permanent Public Utilities Committee","lv":3,"p":[139,139],"pp":"120","s":"The committee met Bureau of Utilities, Finance and Korean businessmen on a proposed one-billion-won bond issue for electric power; judged advisable but tabled pending currency decentralization. It earlier backed Pusan streetcar fare increases."},{"t":"Section 4. Public Works","lv":2,"p":[140,145],"pp":"121","s":"Seoul-Pusan highway returned to provincial control 31 March, volunteer road labor, bridge projects, Han River water works completed, 180 fires."},{"t":"General and Roads and Bridges","lv":3,"p":[140,142],"pp":"121","s":"Seoul-Samchok highway survey; volunteer maintenance; Hyopchong, Andong and Chonan bridges."},{"t":"Irrigation and Flood Control","lv":3,"p":[143,143],"pp":"124","s":"Water Resources Bureau tour; Chungchong-namdo completed four projects."},{"t":"Urban Water Supply System","lv":3,"p":[143,143],"pp":"124","s":"Han River-Ascom City reservoir project completed; Japanese chlorinators installed."},{"t":"Geodetic Surveys","lv":3,"p":[144,144],"pp":"125","s":"New topographic map plates; triangulation of the Sosa area."},{"t":"Fire Control","lv":3,"p":[144,144],"pp":"125","s":"180 fires, 397 million won damage, five killed."},{"t":"Education","lv":3,"p":[144,145],"pp":"125","s":"86 graduates of the Heavy Equipment Operators' Training School at Ascom City."},{"t":"PART IV. FINANCE","lv":1,"p":[146,163],"pp":"","s":"Revenue was 75 percent of expenditure; Bank of Chosun note issue and overdraft; and non-government finance, with February cash balances down 1.5 billion won and rice-loan rediscount rate raised."},{"t":"Section 1. Government Finance","lv":2,"p":[146,153],"pp":"127","s":"Revenue 75 percent of expenditure; supplemental appropriations; Bank of Chosun note issue and overdraft; Civilian Supply Program accounts."},{"t":"Note Issue of the Bank of Chosun","lv":3,"p":[146,148],"pp":"127","s":"Note issue and currency circulation trend through March."},{"t":"Overdraft to South Korean Interim Government","lv":3,"p":[149,149],"pp":"130","s":"Table 3 compares cumulative Overdraft Account and Revenue Deposit Account balances of the South Korean Interim Government at the Bank of Chosun; Table 4 lists budgets, with total appropriations of 21,406,709 as of 31 March 1948."},{"t":"Expenditure Budget of the South Korean Interim Government","lv":3,"p":[149,149],"pp":"130","s":"Supplemental appropriations including 60 million won for the election; 1.25 billion won pending."},{"t":"Monthly Revenues and Expenditures","lv":3,"p":[150,150],"pp":"131","s":"February revenue up 152 million won; expenditures down 715 million, lowest since May 1947."},{"t":"Ratios Between Revenues and Expenditures","lv":3,"p":[151,151],"pp":"132","s":"Revenue was 75 percent of expenditure and 62 percent of budget over 11 months."},{"t":"Civilian Supply Program","lv":3,"p":[152,153],"pp":"133","s":"Civilian supply financial activity; 12.2 billion won receivable."},{"t":"Section 2. Non-Government Finance","lv":2,"p":[154,163],"pp":"135","s":"February cash balances fell 1.5 billion won, deposits down 3.18 billion, loans down 7 percent; rice-loan rediscount rate raised; ban on political credit."},{"t":"Cash Balances and Deposits","lv":3,"p":[154,156],"pp":"135","s":"Largest monthly cash decline since liberation; deposit trends of government, banks and public."},{"t":"Deposit Balances in Each Financial Institution","lv":3,"p":[157,157],"pp":"138","s":"Deposits fell at each institution; government deposits slightly higher."},{"t":"Loans by Financial Institutions","lv":3,"p":[158,159],"pp":"139","s":"Net loan decrease of 2.34 billion won; Bank of Chosun held 32 percent of loans."},{"t":"Life Insurance","lv":3,"p":[160,160],"pp":"141","s":"Insurance in force rose 83.8 million won."},{"t":"Damage Insurance","lv":3,"p":[161,161],"pp":"142","s":"9,920 new policies, over 3 billion won in liabilities."},{"t":"Life and Damage Insurance Activities","lv":3,"p":[161,161],"pp":"142","s":"Han Kook Fire and Marine report, FY 1948/49 budgets, and a uniform bank examination form."},{"t":"Activities of Financial Institutions","lv":3,"p":[162,163],"pp":"143","s":"Rediscount rate on rice loans raised to 4.02 percent; directive barring credit to finance politics."},{"t":"PART V. GOVERNMENT POLITICS","lv":1,"p":[164,187],"pp":"","s":"Election-year politics: the National Election Committee, Kimm Kiu-sic's resignation, the Kim Koo letter to Pyongyang, Ordinance No. 176, riot plan reports, and 37,000 pieces of North-South mail."},{"t":"Section 1. Administration","lv":2,"p":[164,168],"pp":"145","s":"FY 1948/49 budget review, GARIOA import program, Personnel Ceiling Board, civil service exam, National Election Committee, MG personnel."},{"t":"National Economic Board","lv":3,"p":[164,164],"pp":"145","s":"Supplemental budgets reviewed; agency funding limited to one-twelfth for April."},{"t":"Requirements Program","lv":3,"p":[165,165],"pp":"146","s":"GARIOA import-export program for FY 1948/49 sent to Joint Chiefs of Staff; separate Japan-sourced program."},{"t":"Personnel Ceiling Board","lv":3,"p":[166,166],"pp":"147","s":"Board organized 9 January sets maximum personnel for agencies."},{"t":"Korean Civil Service","lv":3,"p":[167,167],"pp":"148","s":"31 March clerk examination, grades CAF 10-13, about 1,200 applicants."},{"t":"National Election Committee","lv":3,"p":[167,167],"pp":"148","s":"Executive Order No. 14 created a 15-member committee; four resigned 30 March; over 50 percent registered."},{"t":"Military Government Personnel","lv":3,"p":[168,168],"pp":"149","s":"Personnel numbers at end of March; civilian turnover high from one-year contracts."},{"t":"Section 2. Korean Interim Legislative Assembly","lv":2,"p":[169,170],"pp":"150","s":"Almost no legislation; Kimm Kiu-sic's resignation accepted 2 March; 29 more resignations; nationality law reading; bribery allegations."},{"t":"General and Resignations","lv":3,"p":[169,169],"pp":"150","s":"Rightist Urgent Proposal split the Assembly; Speaker and Vice Speaker resigned."},{"t":"Action of the Assembly","lv":3,"p":[170,170],"pp":"151","s":"Second reading of Temporary Provisions on Nationality; prostitution-abolition bribery charges."},{"t":"Section 3. Political Developments","lv":2,"p":[171,175],"pp":"152","s":"Politics split over the UN election; Kim Koo and Kimm Kiu-sic's letter to Kim Il-sung and Kim Doo-bong; Rhee's Hankook Democratic Party dispute."},{"t":"General Election","lv":3,"p":[171,171],"pp":"152","s":"Division between supporters led by Rhee and opponents among moderates, leftists and Kim Koo."},{"t":"North-South Leaders' Conference","lv":3,"p":[172,173],"pp":"153","s":"Letters sent to Pyongyang; North Korea accepted and suggested 14 April as the date."},{"t":"Organizations","lv":3,"p":[174,175],"pp":"155","s":"National Representative Group formed 7 March; friction within Rhee's coalition; moderate unification and women's parties."},{"t":"Section 4. Legal Affairs","lv":2,"p":[176,182],"pp":"157","s":"Ordinance No. 176 protects against unlawful arrest; time allowances for prisoners; prison population 22,446; guard graduation; court liaison."},{"t":"Ordinances and Orders","lv":3,"p":[176,179],"pp":"157","s":"Ordinances in the Official Gazette and drafts; Ordinance No. 176 on criminal procedures effective 1 April."},{"t":"Legal Opinions","lv":3,"p":[180,180],"pp":"161","s":"Department of Justice legal opinions for March 1948: law books vested under Ordinance No. 33 cannot be transferred free; KILA cannot raise pay or travel allowances without the Military Governor's consent; stolen Chosun Mining equipment recovery."},{"t":"Penal Institutions","lv":3,"p":[181,181],"pp":"162","s":"Time-allowance ordinance, 142 guards graduated 29 March, prison population 22,446, camp expansion."},{"t":"Court Liaison","lv":3,"p":[182,182],"pp":"163","s":"Taegu judges' dispute settled; 26 March judges' and prosecutors' meeting on election law."},{"t":"Section 5. National Police","lv":2,"p":[183,185],"pp":"164","s":"Riot plan and disturbed-area reports from Kyongsang-namdo, Chungchong-namdo, Cholla-namdo and Kyongsang-pukto."},{"t":"Disturbances, Riot Plan and Disturbed Areas","lv":3,"p":[183,185],"pp":"164","s":"Master riot plan seized; attacks on police boxes, Chinhae city hall burned, killings and 41 arrests in Kyongsang-namdo."},{"t":"Section 6. Interzone Activities","lv":2,"p":[186,187],"pp":"167","s":"More than 37,000 pieces of North-South mail exchanged, and Radio Pyongyang propaganda against the election and for Kim Il-sung analyzed."},{"t":"Mail Exchanges","lv":3,"p":[186,186],"pp":"167","s":"More than 37,000 pieces of mail exchanged between North and South."},{"t":"Radio Pyongyang Intercepts","lv":3,"p":[186,187],"pp":"167","s":"Propaganda against the election, the UN Commission and Menon and Arranz; promotion of Kim Il-sung and the joint conference."},{"t":"PART VI. SOCIAL","lv":1,"p":[188,221],"pp":"","s":"Cholera prevention and 2,000 Mukden repatriates, teachers' workshops, Sam Il Day and Winter Olympics, press on the election, Korean National Youth (646,331 members), and women's election drive."},{"t":"Section 1. Public Health and Welfare","lv":2,"p":[188,192],"pp":"169","s":"Cholera prevention program, 2,000 Mukden repatriates, leprosarium transfer to Presbyterian missions, nursing training, refugee camps, relief rates raised."},{"t":"General, Preventive Medicine and Medical Services","lv":3,"p":[188,189],"pp":"169","s":"Typhus, typhoid and diphtheria were the main diseases; 25 doctors licensed; internships in Army hospitals."},{"t":"Nursing, Veterinary and Pharmaceutical Affairs","lv":3,"p":[190,190],"pp":"171","s":"Nursing capping, refresher courses, Eastgate slaughterhouse reopened, Cheju dog rabies vaccination."},{"t":"Narcotics, Laboratories and Public Assistance","lv":3,"p":[191,192],"pp":"172","s":"LARA relief goods, Uijongbu camp inspection, new children's homes, increased home relief rates."},{"t":"Section 2. Education","lv":2,"p":[193,197],"pp":"174","s":"Teachers' workshops spread to provinces, technician training survey, textbook output, medical and nursing education, American Language Institute."},{"t":"Bureaus of Common Schools and Teachers' Training","lv":3,"p":[193,193],"pp":"174","s":"Home economics workshop for 60 teachers, English pronunciation drills, women teachers' meeting at Taegu."},{"t":"Bureau of Higher Education","lv":3,"p":[194,194],"pp":"175","s":"Nurse refresher courses, library objectives, medical education goals."},{"t":"Textbooks, Research and Adult Education, American Language Institute","lv":3,"p":[195,197],"pp":"175","s":"New textbooks, skilled-worker survey, 300-woman election familiarization conference, language institute enrollment."},{"t":"Section 3. Culture","lv":2,"p":[198,204],"pp":"179","s":"Sam Il Day, Korean alphabet, Confucian festival, puppet shows, Nam San sunrise service, HLKA broadcasts, art and music, and sports including Winter Olympics."},{"t":"Sam Il Day and Korean Alphabet","lv":3,"p":[198,199],"pp":"179","s":"29th anniversary of the 1919 Declaration of Independence; King Se-jong's 1443 alphabet."},{"t":"Festival, Puppet Show and Sunrise Service","lv":3,"p":[200,200],"pp":"181","s":"Confucian Spring Festival 13 March; Kyonggi puppet shows; 20,000 at the Nam San Easter service."},{"t":"Korean Broadcasting System and Other Cultural Activities","lv":3,"p":[201,202],"pp":"182","s":"HLKA programs, art exhibitions, a symphony concert, theater rates."},{"t":"Sports","lv":3,"p":[203,204],"pp":"184","s":"Seoul-Suwon relay and soccer schedule, Winter Olympics results."},{"t":"Section 4. Press and Public Opinion","lv":2,"p":[205,211],"pp":"186","s":"Press reaction to the election, Pyongyang conference and reforms; procurator-police dispute; Ordinance No. 176; strike and riot coverage; fear of Japan."},{"t":"The Election and Reforms","lv":3,"p":[205,208],"pp":"186","s":"Factional attitudes, free atmosphere debate, reactions to land sale and Ordinance No. 176."},{"t":"Youth Groups, Police, Strikes, Foreign Affairs and Public Information","lv":3,"p":[209,211],"pp":"190","s":"Political murder trials, 55 arrests, streetcar strike poll, Philippine base report, cost-of-living survey of 288 families."},{"t":"Section 5. Korean National Youth, Inc.","lv":2,"p":[212,216],"pp":"193","s":"Membership 646,331, 75 million volunteer hours in 15 months, training and election work; opposition from rightist and leftist groups."},{"t":"General, Membership and Training","lv":3,"p":[212,213],"pp":"193","s":"Membership 646,331; 36,599 volunteer leaders trained; merger with a rightist youth group refused."},{"t":"Community Service","lv":3,"p":[214,214],"pp":"195","s":"2,151,740 members worked 75,289,176 hours; classes, playgrounds, tree planting."},{"t":"Other Projects, Election and Opposition","lv":3,"p":[215,216],"pp":"196","s":"Speakers' bureaus, election awareness work, and attacks from rival groups."},{"t":"Section 6. Women's Activities","lv":2,"p":[217,221],"pp":"198","s":"Women's Bureau election drive, English classes, Salvation Army Home, nurses, policewomen, YWCA, Red Cross, and Cheju Island women."},{"t":"Women's Bureau and Pioneer Institution","lv":3,"p":[217,217],"pp":"198","s":"Seven Seoul women selected as candidates; election pamphlets; first home for unmarried mothers."},{"t":"Nurses, Policewomen, YWCA, Red Cross and Cheju Island","lv":3,"p":[218,221],"pp":"199","s":"Candle-light capping at Severance, YWCA leadership conference, Cheju women divers."}],"by":"claude-sonnet (Claude Code subagent)","emb":["K-31-in-K-30","K-32-in-K-30"]},{"id":"J-31","ia":"summation-non-military-activities-japan-no-31-1948-04","s":"J","no":31,"d":"1948-04","d2":"","t":"Summation of Non-Military Activities in Japan, No. 31 (April 1948)","v":"","pg":168,"o":"Summation No. 31 covers April 1948 (statistics mostly for February-March). Politically, government employees' unions accepted the 2,920-yen wage scale on 16 April after SCAP forbade a communications strike; the Ashida coalition was strained by the Social Democratic demand to suspend war bond interest, cabinet disagreement over labor law revision, and the Tsuji hoarded-goods inquiry. Korean protests against school closures in Osaka and Kobe required Occupation troops. The Diet created the Maritime Safety Board and restored parliamentary vice-ministers; the purge continued and the IMTFE heard final defense summations. Economically, March output hit postwar highs in steel, chemicals, rayon, cotton and silk fabrics, with coal at 2.86 million tons, while the February trade deficit was $60.9 million. Finance, rationing, labor, health, education and media statistics follow. Historians will find detailed tables on production, trade, repatriation and the war crimes trials.","h":["Government employees' unions accepted the 2,920-yen wage scale on 16 April, ending their dispute, after SCAP three times barred a general strike by communications workers.","The Social Democrats' demand to suspend war bond interest strained the Ashida coalition, alongside Cabinet disagreement over labor-law revision and the Tsuji hoarded-goods inquiry.","Koreans protested school closures in Osaka and Kobe on 23-26 April; 23 Koreans and 58 police were injured, 183 arrested, and Occupation troops helped disperse the Kobe crowds.","The Diet passed the Maritime Safety Authorities Bill on 15 April, authorizing up to 10,000 men and 125 vessels, and 22 parliamentary vice-ministers were appointed.","IMTFE defense summations for all 25 accused were completed 15 April, and the court adjourned 16 April to prepare its judgment.","March output set postwar highs in steel, chemicals, rayon, cotton and silk fabrics, with coal at 2,862,500 tons; the February trade deficit was $60.9 million, 73 percent of imports from the United States.","By 17 April 711,036 persons had been screened in the purge, with 4,880 removed and 2,876 barred; 189,162 were finally designated.","764,900 persons were still awaiting repatriation to Japan, while 396 returned in April, bringing the total to 5,843,095."],"k":["Ashida cabinet","government employees wage dispute","war bond interest","Tsuji case","purge","Korean schools","Maritime Safety Board","IMTFE","coal production","industrial production","foreign trade","rationing","repatriation","textbooks","reparations"],"toc":[{"t":"Front matter","lv":1,"p":[1,5],"pp":"","s":"Cover of Summation No. 31 (April 1948), contents for Parts I to IV (General, Political, Economic, Social) and a list of charts, maps and graphs ending with the Map of Japan."},{"t":"Part I: General","lv":1,"p":[6,15],"pp":"1","s":"Paragraph-numbered digest: the Restitution Advisory Committee (13 April), government employees' 2,920-yen wage settlement, March production gains, February trade deficit of $60.9 million, and 764,900 persons awaiting repatriation."},{"t":"Organization Under SCAP","lv":2,"p":[7,7],"pp":"3","s":"The Restitution Advisory Committee was established 13 April to advise the Supreme Commander on looted property found in Japan; the Civil Property Custodian chairs it, with delegates from participating Far Eastern Commission nations."},{"t":"Political Activities","lv":2,"p":[7,8],"pp":"3","s":"Government employees accepted the 2,920-yen wage scale; Cabinet split on labor-law changes; war bond interest went to interparty talks; Tsuji fund inquiry, 18 bills, purge totals, Korean school protests and the Maritime Safety bill also noted."},{"t":"Economic Activities","lv":2,"p":[9,14],"pp":"7","s":"March output rose in coal, steel, chemicals and bicycles (47,120). February imports of $70.3 million against exports of $9.4 million left a $60.9 million deficit; note circulation reached 220.4 billion yen; 7,805 machine tools shipped as reparations."},{"t":"Social Activities","lv":2,"p":[14,15],"pp":"17","s":"Japanese B encephalitis research began at the National Institute of Health, penicillin output rose 138 percent, 764,900 persons awaited repatriation, and 94,650,553 textbooks were authorized, with 647 million yen for lower secondary schools."},{"t":"Part II: Political","lv":1,"p":[16,47],"pp":"21","s":"Government and politics, public safety, and legal affairs: wage dispute settlement and war bond interest controversy, Korean school riots in Osaka and Kobe, and the final IMTFE defense summations."},{"t":"Government and Politics","lv":2,"p":[17,32],"pp":"23","s":"The government-employee wage dispute ended, the war bond interest controversy and Tsuji hoarded-goods inquiry continued, party positions shifted, the Diet enacted 18 laws, and the purge screened 711,036 persons."},{"t":"Political Developments","lv":3,"p":[17,26],"pp":"23","s":"The 2,920-yen dispute ended 16 April after SCAP three times barred communications strikes; Ashida and Labor Minister Kato clashed over labor law. Also: war bond interest conference, Tsuji inquiry, Vice-minister Ino's bribery arrest."},{"t":"Political Parties","lv":3,"p":[27,29],"pp":"","s":"The Democratic Party defined 'modified capitalism' and lost three members to the Democratic Liberals; Social Democrats defended war bond interest suspension and gained to 123 lower house seats; the People's Cooperative Party backed the Cabinet."},{"t":"The Diet","lv":3,"p":[30,31],"pp":"","s":"SCAP urged curbs on Diet absenteeism; Ashida asked to extend the session to end of May. The Diet enacted 18 laws including the Coal Board and Maritime Safety bills; 22 parliamentary vice-ministers were appointed 15 April."},{"t":"The Purge","lv":3,"p":[31,32],"pp":"","s":"By 17 April 711,036 persons were screened, 4,880 removed and 2,876 barred; 189,162 finally designated. The Appeals Board rejected 135 appeals; the Special Investigation Bureau was organized 20 April with nine regional headquarters."},{"t":"Public Safety","lv":2,"p":[33,35],"pp":"55","s":"Korean-school protests in Osaka (23-26 April) left 23 Koreans and 58 police injured and 183 arrested; Occupation Forces helped disperse Kobe crowds. February saw 149,885 offenses and 79,220 arrests; prison population was 87,233."},{"t":"Legal Affairs and War Crimes","lv":2,"p":[36,47],"pp":"61","s":"The Supreme Court upheld capital punishment 12 March. By 20 April 610 war criminals were tried (572 convicted, 94 sentenced to death). IMTFE defense summations for all 25 accused ended 15 April; the court adjourned 16 April to prepare judgment."},{"t":"Part III: Economic","lv":1,"p":[48,146],"pp":"85","s":"Statistics and narrative on production, trade, labor, prices, finance, property and science: March coal 2.86 million tons, postwar highs in steel and textiles, February trade deficit $60.9 million, 6,243,017 union members."},{"t":"Agriculture and Fisheries","lv":2,"p":[49,51],"pp":"87","s":"Land sales to tenants reached 146,558 cho in March (427,320 cumulative); 12,051 agricultural cooperatives held preliminary meetings. February marine production totaled 110,464 tons; Antarctic whaling yielded 17,544.6 long tons of whale oil."},{"t":"Forestry and Mining","lv":2,"p":[52,58],"pp":"93","s":"March log production rose to 88,984,500 cubic feet; 1947 cutting outran replanting (866,170 against 430,810 cho). March coal output was 2,862,500 tons with 516,897 mine employees; crude oil 16,016 kiloliters."},{"t":"Heavy Industries","lv":2,"p":[59,69],"pp":"107","s":"Steel set postwar records in March: pig iron 44,777 tons, rolled steel 76,927 tons. Coke reached 221,910 tons, cement 131,669 tons, ammonium sulfate 66,186 tons; aluminum fell to 374 tons."},{"t":"Manufacturing","lv":2,"p":[70,77],"pp":"129","s":"February flour output jumped 290 percent to 103,066 tons; March paper reached 34,492 tons, three-wheelers a postwar high of 1,093 and bicycles a record 47,120; trucks fell to 942 chassis."},{"t":"Textile Industries","lv":2,"p":[78,92],"pp":"145","s":"March textile output rose, with postwar peaks in rayon, cotton and silk fabrics. Cotton yarn reached 23,181,000 pounds; cotton cloth topped 65.7 million square yards; raw silk rose 1,236 bales to 10,106."},{"t":"Transportation and Public Utilities","lv":2,"p":[93,98],"pp":"175","s":"Registered commercial vehicles rose to 163,766 (67.5 percent operable). Government railways carried 303,921,713 passengers, up 9.5 percent, and 9,861,800 metric tons of freight; electric generation was 2,607,867,000 kilowatt hours."},{"t":"Communications","lv":2,"p":[99,106],"pp":"187","s":"Telephones in service rose 1.3 percent to 1,195,238; new radio links opened to Latin America, Europe and Asia. Radio households reached 6,443,206; a Diet appropriation of 3,289,493,000 yen was noted."},{"t":"Labor","lv":2,"p":[107,116],"pp":"203","s":"Government workers' dispute ended 16 April with acceptance of the 2,920-yen wage level and a 15-grade pay plan. Unions numbered 29,378 with 6,243,017 members; February disputes involved 189,558 workers."},{"t":"Imports and Exports","lv":2,"p":[117,121],"pp":"223","s":"February imports of $70,261,684 and exports of $9,400,019 left a deficit near $60.9 million. About 73 percent of imports came from the United States; textiles were 41 percent of exports."},{"t":"Rationing and Price Control","lv":2,"p":[122,127],"pp":"233","s":"April rations were met from carryover stocks after 1947 crop quotas were fulfilled in March (rice 100.1 percent); the substitute-rice program ended. February family expenditures were 6,214 yen; 6,646 enforcement cases reported."},{"t":"Finance","lv":2,"p":[128,136],"pp":"245","s":"Bank of Japan notes outstanding reached 220.44 billion yen on 30 April. Reconstruction Finance Bank loans stood at 59.46 billion yen; 1947-48 tax receipts were 124.9 billion yen; national debt 360.8 billion yen."},{"t":"Property Control and Reparations","lv":2,"p":[137,141],"pp":"263","s":"Looted property worth 3.76 million yen was taken into custody; 353 looted ships were reported, 55 percent sunk. SCAP approved 282 restricted-concern transactions; advance reparations shipments reached 7,805 machine tools."},{"t":"Science and Technology","lv":2,"p":[142,146],"pp":"273","s":"The Renewal Committee for the Organization of Science held its final meeting 25-27 March, recommending a Science Council under the Prime Minister's office. Japanese Standards reached 1,009; 175 patents were granted."},{"t":"Part IV: Social","lv":1,"p":[147,168],"pp":"283","s":"Public health and welfare, and education, religion, cultural resources and media: typhoid inoculations, penicillin record, 3,077,031 persons on welfare aid, repatriation, textbook authorization and public opinion surveys."},{"t":"Public Health and Welfare","lv":2,"p":[148,156],"pp":"285","s":"About 36.4 million typhoid inoculations were given by 26 April; a 98-case typhus outbreak at an Osaka hospital was contained. Penicillin hit a record 157,019 vials; 396 repatriates returned in April (5,843,095 total)."},{"t":"Education, Religion, Cultural Resources and Media of Expression","lv":2,"p":[157,168],"pp":"303","s":"A 647 million yen supplementary budget for lower secondary schools, 12,123 new lower secondary schools, and 94.7 million textbooks authorized. Public opinion surveys, press, radio and films are covered; a Fukuoka SCAP library closes the section."}],"by":"claude-sonnet (Claude Code subagent)"},{"id":"K-31","ia":"south-korean-interim-government-activities-no-31-1948-04","s":"K","no":31,"d":"1948-04","d2":"","t":"South Korean Interim Government Activities, No. 31 (April 1948)","v":"","pg":222,"o":"South Korean Interim Government Activities No. 31, prepared by the National Economic Board, covers April 1948, the month before the 10 May election. It reports voter registration of 7,730,000 (94 percent) and 939 candidates for 200 Assembly seats, communist violence including the Cheju-do revolt, and the Pyongyang North-South Conference attended by Kim Koo and Kimm Kiu-sic. It records 15,848 refugees entering from the north, the first month of National Land Administration farm sales (28 percent sold), vested property disposal rules, and Military Government amnesty for 3,000 political prisoners. Economic chapters give prices, industrial output (paper, cotton, anthracite), coal and fertilizer imports, private trade (exports above imports), railroads, power, public works, budgets for F/Y 1948/49, bank deposits and note issue. Later chapters cover KILA, legal ordinances and the draft Civil Code, police, interzone mail and Radio Pyongyang, health, education, culture, press, youth and women. Useful for election-eve conditions and statistics.","h":["A 10-day registration ending 9 April enrolled 7,730,000 voters, over 94 percent of those eligible, and 939 candidates, 17 of them women, contested 200 Assembly seats in the 10 May election.","The Pyongyang North-South Conference met 19-23 April with Kim Koo and Kimm Kiu-sic attending, and a 30 April joint communique rejected the southern election.","Cheju-do rioting began 3 April; communists attacked about 52 of 13,455 registration booths, and 29 rioters were killed in casualties reported for 3-19 April.","By 30 April, 28.7 percent of 587,974 former Japanese farms had been sold to farmers under Ordinance No. 173 through the National Land Administration.","15,848 refugees entered South Korea from the north in April, 20 percent above March, and a refugee ship sailed to Tientsin on 30 April for Mukden repatriates.","Railroads carried over 480,000 tons of freight, the most since October 1945, while March private imports reached 822.7 million won and exports of 654.1 million went over 93 percent to Hong Kong.","The Military Governor's amnesty freed over 3,000 minor political prisoners, and Judge Lobingier's proposed first Korean Civil Code was completed by the Department of Justice.","KILA met once on 19 April and adjourned 30 days by 23 to 0; bank deposits hit a record 55,305 million won and SKIG's overdraft a record 22,005 million."],"k":["10 May 1948 election","voter registration","Pyongyang conference","Cheju-do","National Land Administration","vested property","Office of Property Custody","refugees from North Korea","coal and fertilizer imports","private trade Hong Kong","KILA","Civil Code","Korean National Youth","National Economic Board","Seoul Electric strike"],"toc":[{"t":"Front matter","lv":1,"p":[1,11],"pp":"","s":"Cover of No. 31 (April 1948), SKIG and USAMGIK organization charts as of 1 January 1948, National Economic Board members under Chairman Dr. Wilhelm Anderson, and a six-part table of contents."},{"t":"Summary","lv":1,"p":[12,13],"pp":"1","s":"Arthur G. Peterson's overview: 94 percent of 7,730,000 voters registered despite communist terror, Cheju revolt and the Pyongyang conference. Over 15,000 refugees came south, 28 percent of Japanese-held farmland sold, and literacy reached 71 percent."},{"t":"Part I. Basic Aspects","lv":1,"p":[14,29],"pp":"3","s":"Refugee arrivals and Korean schools in Japan, then vested property: Property Disposal sales rules, the simplified claims directive of 17 April, and rental data on 102,822 leases."},{"t":"Section 1. Population and Foreign Affairs","lv":2,"p":[14,17],"pp":"3","s":"Refugee arrivals rose 20 percent over March to 15,848; the Kaesong camp was inspected. Osaka and Hyogo ordered Korean schools closed, leading to 73 parental arrests and about 4,000 protesters dispersed."},{"t":"Section 2. Vested Property","lv":2,"p":[18,29],"pp":"7","s":"Vested property covered sales rules for small businesses and homes, a simplified claims procedure, guaranteed bank loans for vested firms, audit decentralization and rental tables for 102,822 leases."},{"t":"Property Disposal","lv":3,"p":[21,22],"pp":"10","s":"Rules for selling former Japanese small businesses and homes: auction or sealed bid, 20 percent down, 5 to 7 percent interest, 10-year contracts. Table 3 shows 96 April sales worth 62.4 million won."},{"t":"Claims Section","lv":3,"p":[24,25],"pp":"13","s":"A 17 April directive simplified claims against vested property; only 50 of over 50,000 claims had been processed in a year. A seven-step OPC review now handles about 12 cases per day."},{"t":"Property Rentals","lv":3,"p":[26,29],"pp":"15","s":"At 1 January 1948 there were 102,822 leases on 82,339 vested properties, with rents up in 83 percent of cases. Arrears were 87.2 million won; net rental revenue since 1945 was 283.5 million won."},{"t":"Part II. Production and Supply","lv":1,"p":[30,107],"pp":"19","s":"Covers April 1948 agriculture (28.7 percent of former Japanese farms sold), prices, record paper and anthracite output, petroleum, trade with exports above imports, and labor disputes including the Seoul Electric streetcar case."},{"t":"Section 1. Agriculture, Forestry and Fisheries","lv":2,"p":[30,41],"pp":"19","s":"Fertilizer sales reached 35,091 metric tons. Under Ordinance No. 173, 28.7 percent of 587,974 former Japanese farms were sold by 30 April, priced at three times annual crop output."},{"t":"Section 2. Prices","lv":2,"p":[42,49],"pp":"31","s":"Retail prices fell seasonally in April while wholesale prices barely moved. Seoul cleaned rice fell to 1,320 won per mal; National Price Administration controlled prices are given."},{"t":"Section 3. Industrial Production","lv":2,"p":[50,60],"pp":"39","s":"March output rose over February: cotton yarn 28.5 percent, cotton cloth 26.2 percent. Paper hit a post-liberation record of 533.4 tons; government salterns produced 16,036 tons in April."},{"t":"Section 4. Mining and Minerals","lv":2,"p":[61,72],"pp":"50","s":"Anthracite output hit a record 62,796 tons in March, 40,596 from Samchuk, but under half could be moved by rail. April bituminous imports from Japan were 83,171 tons."},{"t":"Section 5. Allocations and Distribution","lv":2,"p":[73,85],"pp":"62","s":"April 1948: the Consumer Goods Allocation Board set a 40/60 split between employees and the public; civilian supplies, petroleum (6,414,345 liters of gasoline) and 25,803 tons of salt were allocated. FFA distributed bonus goods worth 41,000,000 won."},{"t":"Petroleum","lv":3,"p":[75,77],"pp":"64","s":"The Petroleum Distributing Company allocated 6.4 million liters of gasoline, 3.8 million of kerosene and 2.9 million of diesel in April. Sales were below March for most products; shortfalls were blamed on stock limits and late arrivals."},{"t":"Section 6. Imports and Exports","lv":2,"p":[86,98],"pp":"75","s":"April permits (113 import, 94 export) implied exports of 443.8 million won against imports of 376.8 million. March exports of 654.1 million went over 93 percent to Hong Kong; government exports to Japan totaled $351,976."},{"t":"Section 7. Labor","lv":2,"p":[99,107],"pp":"88","s":"General Dean's 7 April fact-finding committee recommended reorganizing Seoul Electric management and a union election. Marine Bureau talks continued, a Pusan stevedore petition was investigated, and the refugee-camp labor project was abandoned."},{"t":"Part III. Public Services","lv":1,"p":[108,141],"pp":"97","s":"Covers transportation (railroads carrying over 480,000 tons), communications and Cheju sabotage, electricity deliveries of 75.9 million KWH, and public works including the Sumjingang dam."},{"t":"Section 1. Transportation","lv":2,"p":[108,117],"pp":"97","s":"March Transportation revenue rose 119 percent to 283 million won, partly from a 161 million won Army payment. Railroads carried over 480,000 tons, the most since October 1945; Pusan received 48 percent of marine shipping space."},{"t":"Section 2. Communications","lv":2,"p":[118,126],"pp":"107","s":"Communist sabotage waned on the mainland but hit Cheju, where 16 poles were cut and 26 wires severed. The radio license fee rose from 40 to 50 won on 15 April."},{"t":"Section 3. Public Utilities","lv":2,"p":[127,131],"pp":"116","s":"Electricity delivered in April totaled 75.9 million KWH. The Emergency Power Board met 7 and 21 April on conservation and a plan for a North Korean cutoff; the Pusan barge YFP-1 began production."},{"t":"Section 4. Public Works","lv":2,"p":[132,141],"pp":"121","s":"Road spending was virtually nil for lack of an appropriation, offset by volunteer labor. A Water Resources Committee met 2 and 23 April on the Sumjingang dam; 144 fires caused 76 million won in losses."},{"t":"Part IV. Finance","lv":1,"p":[142,161],"pp":"131","s":"Bank of Chosun note issue fell to 28.2 billion won; SKIG overdraft hit a record 22,005 million. March bank deposits rose to a record 55,305 million won, while loans fell for the third month."},{"t":"Section 1. Government Finance","lv":2,"p":[142,151],"pp":"131","s":"Note issue fell to 28.2 billion won, with 5,213 million returned since December. SKIG's overdraft hit a record 22,005 million; March revenue of 2,135 million was a record; 787 million in extra 1947/48 appropriations were authorized."},{"t":"Section 2. Non-Government Finance","lv":2,"p":[152,161],"pp":"141","s":"March deposits rose 6,279 million won to a record 55,305 million, while loans fell 1,591 million. Ordinance No. 182 required exchange of Japanese-printed 100-won notes by 24 April; 39 loans totaling 713 million were approved."},{"t":"Part V. Government and Politics","lv":1,"p":[162,195],"pp":"151","s":"Registration of 7,730,000 voters, KILA's April dissolution, the Pyongyang conference, 939 candidates, Ordinances 182-191, the draft Civil Code, police handling of Cheju-do violence, and interzone mail."},{"t":"Section 1. Administration","lv":2,"p":[162,164],"pp":"151","s":"Registration closed 9 April with 7,730,000 voters (over 94 percent) for 200 districts and 942 candidates. The Economic Board approved 400 million won irrigation credit and 250 million for Sumjingang Dam; Military Government personnel rose to 3,041."},{"t":"Section 2. Korean Interim Legislative Assembly","lv":2,"p":[165,166],"pp":"154","s":"KILA met only on 19 April, rejecting three resignations, tabling pending bills 20 to 0 and adjourning 30 days by 23 to 0. It had 90 members since December 1946 and passed 11 Public Acts."},{"t":"Section 3. Political Developments","lv":2,"p":[167,176],"pp":"156","s":"The Pyongyang North-South Conference of 19-23 April, which Kim Koo and Kimm Kiu-sic attended, and election preparations: 939 candidates for 200 seats, rightist splits, and Choi Nung-chin's challenge to Rhee."},{"t":"North-South Conference","lv":3,"p":[167,173],"pp":"156","s":"Kim Koo and Kimm Kiu-sic attended the Pyongyang sessions of 19-23 April; a resolution was signed by 16 parties and 45 organizations, and a 30 April communique rejected the southern election."},{"t":"Election Preparations","lv":3,"p":[173,176],"pp":"162","s":"A rightist committee under O Sei-chang formed 1 April to speed the election. 939 candidates, including 17 women, sought 200 seats; Choi Nung-chin's challenge to Rhee was nullified on 7 May."},{"t":"Section 4. Legal Affairs","lv":2,"p":[177,188],"pp":"166","s":"Covers Ordinances 182-191, Hodge's Proclamation of Rights, Kaesong prison conditions, amnesty releases, and the draft Civil Code by Judge Lobingier with four books."},{"t":"Codification Bureau","lv":3,"p":[183,188],"pp":"172","s":"The Department of Justice finished a proposed first Korean Civil Code, drafted by Judge Lobingier, who drew heavily on China's code. Its syllabus lists four books: Persons, Obligations, Property and Succession."},{"t":"Section 5. National Police","lv":2,"p":[189,190],"pp":"178","s":"Police resented the court-warrant rule and the release of over 3,000 minor political prisoners. About 52 of 13,455 registration booths were attacked; Cheju-do rioting began 3 April, with 29 rioters killed by 19 April."},{"t":"Section 6. Interzone Activities","lv":2,"p":[191,195],"pp":"180","s":"Mail exchange with North Korea became weekly from 15 April: 59,000 pieces against 37,000 in March. Radio Pyongyang attacked the southern election; Chough Pyong-ok reported 21 Koreans killed and 74 border violations."},{"t":"Part VI. Social","lv":1,"p":[196,222],"pp":"185","s":"Covers cholera immunization and the Tientsin refugee ship, literacy at 71 percent, Arbor Day tree planting, the press on the 10 May election, Korean National Youth with 774,949 members, and women's election work."},{"t":"Section 1. Public Health and Welfare","lv":2,"p":[196,200],"pp":"185","s":"Cholera immunization expanded along the coasts and 38th parallel. The refugee ship sailed to Tientsin on 30 April; 520,000 pounds of DDT arrived and 10,053 low-cost housing units were finished."},{"t":"Section 2. Education","lv":2,"p":[201,206],"pp":"190","s":"Literacy reportedly rose from 21 percent in 1945 to 71 percent by August 1947. The department was cut from 177 to 143 positions; 13,230,670 textbooks had been published since 1945."},{"t":"Section 3. Culture","lv":2,"p":[207,211],"pp":"196","s":"Arbor Day, 5 April, became a national holiday with 100 million trees planted. Hodge presented instruments to the Korea Symphony Orchestra on 25 April; 58 athletes were selected for the London Olympics."},{"t":"Section 4. Press and Public Opinion","lv":2,"p":[212,214],"pp":"201","s":"The press centred on the 10 May election and Pyongyang conference. It reported 91.7 percent registration and 939 candidates, 48 percent independents, plus Cheju-do disorders and anger at the closing of Korean schools in Japan."},{"t":"Section 5. Korean National Youth, Inc.","lv":2,"p":[215,217],"pp":"204","s":"Korean National Youth mobilized 67,475 members on Arbor Day to plant 3,451,300 trees. Membership reached 774,949 by 30 April, with seven new training centers bringing the total to 62."},{"t":"Section 6. Women's Activities","lv":2,"p":[218,222],"pp":"207","s":"The Women's Bureau gave over 50 election speeches, showed the film \"The Peoples Choice\" and published 5,000 copies of \"New Homemaking.\" It also covered a mothers' school and a new home economics association."}],"by":"claude-sonnet (Claude Code subagent)"},{"id":"K-31-in-K-30","ia":"south-korean-interim-government-activities-no-30-1948-03","s":"K","no":31,"d":"1948-04","d2":"","t":"South Korean Interim Government Activities, No. 31 (April 1948)","v":"Bound in K-30","host":"K-30","rg":[[222,449]],"kind":"bound","found":"Title page '105.13:31' on PDF p. 222","pg":228,"o":"Report No. 31 of South Korean Interim Government Activities (USAMGIK), prepared by the National Economic Board, covers April 1948, the month before the 10 May election. It records 94% voter registration (7,730,000), 939-942 candidates for 200 seats, communist sabotage and the Cheju revolt, and the Pyongyang North-South conference rejected by American authorities. Economic sections show higher industrial and anthracite output, stable prices, a trade balance turning to export surplus, record rail freight, rising deposits and a revenue surplus. Political chapters cover KILA's de facto dissolution, amnesty for 3,000 political prisoners, police reform, the draft civil code and land sales. Vested-property policy, refugee flow of 15,848, Korean schools in Japan, youth and women's organizations and literacy gains are also documented. Historians will find dense tables, official framing of the election period and detailed reporting on Property Custody and agrarian reform.","h":["About 7,730,000 voters, over 94 percent of those eligible, registered for the 10 May election by 9 April despite communist terror, with 939-942 candidates contesting 200 seats; about 52 of 13,455 registration booths were attacked.","The Pyongyang North-South conference opened 19 April; Kim Koo and Kimm Kiu-sic attended, and a 30 April communique rejected the South-only election, which the report judged a Soviet victory.","Cheju-do revolt broke out from 3 April with about 2,000 rioters; to 19 April, 22 civilians and seven police were killed, and 16 telephone poles and 26 wires were cut there.","KILA met once on 19 April and adjourned for 30 days, effectively dissolving itself after 11 Public Acts; an amnesty freed about 3,000 political prisoners and police now needed court warrants for arrests.","Anthracite output rose 49.5 percent to 62,796 tons, 40,596 of it from the Samchuk mine, while Japanese bituminous imports reached 83,171 tons and railroads carried a record 480,000 tons of freight.","By 30 April, 168,735 of 587,974 former Japanese farms (28.7 percent) had been sold to Korean farmers under Ordinance No. 173, and fertilizer sales through KAA reached 35,091 tons.","April licenses showed exports of 443.8 million won exceeding imports of 376.8 million by about 15 percent, against a 1,000 percent import excess a year earlier.","Refugee entries from North Korea reached 15,848, up 20 percent on March, while Korean schools in Japan were closed, leading to 73 parent arrests in Hyogo on 15 April."],"k":["USAMGIK","South Korean Interim Government","National Economic Board","10 May 1948 election","voter registration","Pyongyang conference","Cheju-do","vested property","Office of Property Custody","land reform","refugees from North Korea","Korean Interim Legislative Assembly","fertilizer","anthracite coal","Korean National Youth"],"toc":[{"t":"Front matter","lv":1,"p":[222,235],"pp":"","s":"Cover of Report No. 31 (April 1948) by the National Economic Board, a map, USAMGIK and SKIG organization charts as of 1 January 1948, the NEB roster under Dr. Wilhelm Anderson, and a six-part contents."},{"t":"Summary","lv":1,"p":[236,237],"pp":"1","s":"Thirteen paragraphs: 7,730,000 voters (94 percent) registered for the 10 May election despite communist terror; Cheju revolt; Pyongyang conference; over 15,000 refugees; 28 percent of farmland sold; literacy up from 21 to 71 percent."},{"t":"Part I: Basic Aspects","lv":1,"p":[238,253],"pp":"","s":"Refugee inflow of 15,848 from North Korea, Korean League and school disputes in Japan, and Office of Property Custody work: 895 financial forms, 96 sales worth 62.4 million won, 102,822 leases."},{"t":"Section 1: Population and Foreign Affairs","lv":2,"p":[238,241],"pp":"3","s":"Refugee inflow rose 20 percent over March; the Korean League in Japan expelled dissenters; Osaka and Hyogo clashes over closure of non-conforming Korean schools led to 73 arrests on 15 April."},{"t":"Population Migration","lv":3,"p":[238,239],"pp":"3","s":"April refugee entries from North Korea were 15,848, up 20 percent over March, as Russians tightened travel limits. The Kaesong camp was inspected 19 April; cumulative inbound total was 2,384,553 against 886,203 outbound."},{"t":"Korean League in Japan","lv":3,"p":[240,240],"pp":"5","s":"A minority in the Korean League in Japan trying to end communist domination was defeated and expelled by leftist leaders, according to the Office of Foreign Affairs; dissenters formed separate political groups."},{"t":"Korean Education in Japan","lv":3,"p":[241,241],"pp":"6","s":"Osaka ordered 19 non-compliant Korean schools closed by 15 April and 14 kept teaching. In Hyogo, 73 parents were arrested on 15 April and about 4,000 protesters dispersed on 18 April."},{"t":"Section 2: Vested Property","lv":2,"p":[242,253],"pp":"7","s":"Office of Property Custody work: Form 20 and 21 financial statements, guaranteed loans, rules for selling small businesses and residences, an expedited claims procedure, audits and rental statistics."},{"t":"General","lv":3,"p":[242,242],"pp":"7","s":"Operations centered on Forms 20 and 21 financial statements from supervised industries, and standard rules for disposing of vested property were widely published. Policy memoranda were issued and 21 chemical plants inspected."},{"t":"Operations","lv":3,"p":[242,244],"pp":"7","s":"Operations received 895 financial forms, 519 tabulated showing a net loss of 389.5 million won before taxes. Inspections found 21 chemical plants under 30 percent capacity; 51 loan cases totaling 2,138 million won were tabulated."},{"t":"Property Disposal","lv":3,"p":[245,246],"pp":"10","s":"Rules for selling Japanese small businesses and residences give managers or occupants first chance, require 20 percent down, 5-7 percent interest and contracts up to 10 years. April sales completed: 96, worth 62.4 million won."},{"t":"Real Estate Section","lv":3,"p":[247,247],"pp":"12","s":"The Real Estate Section handled 19 transfers of Army-released property, including six covering 180 Seoul houses, processed 21 Army requisitions, and planned transfer of Army real property to the Korean government should USAFIK withdraw."},{"t":"Policies and Precedents","lv":3,"p":[248,248],"pp":"13","s":"A statement held that property registered to Allied nationals on 9 August 1945 is not vested but is protected by the Custodian. Further papers addressed ending brothel use of vested properties and barring lease of critical industrial assets."},{"t":"Claims Section","lv":3,"p":[248,248],"pp":"13","s":"On 17 April the Military Governor signed a directive simplifying ownership claims against vested property. Only 50 of over 50,000 Ordinance No. 33 claims were processed in a year; the new eight-step procedure reached 12 cases a day."},{"t":"Audit Division","lv":3,"p":[249,249],"pp":"14","s":"The Audit Division completed audits of seven organizations and five investigation reports; 52 of 922 requested desk audits were done. Audit work was decentralized to provincial offices, with balance-sheet audits replacing detailed ones."},{"t":"Property Rentals","lv":3,"p":[250,253],"pp":"15","s":"As of 1 January 1948 there were 102,822 leases on 82,339 properties; 21.4 percent were delinquent (87.2 million won). Gross collections since liberation were 319.3 million won, net 283.5 million; residences numbered 56,080."},{"t":"Part II: Production and Supply","lv":1,"p":[254,331],"pp":"","s":"Agriculture, prices, industry, mining, allocation, trade and labor: fertilizer sales of 35,091 tons, 28.7 percent of vested farms sold, anthracite at 62,796 tons, exports exceeding imports, and a Seoul Electric strike threat."},{"t":"Section 1: Agriculture, Forestry and Fisheries","lv":2,"p":[254,265],"pp":"19","s":"Fertilizer sales through KAA reached 35,091 tons; 168,735 vested farms were sold; Crop Reporting Service staff reached 664; livestock, silk, forestry and fisheries are also reported."},{"t":"Agricultural Economics Bureau","lv":3,"p":[254,254],"pp":"19","s":"The Crop Reporting Service added 281 persons, bringing appointments to 664 as of 20 April. Gun statistician training began in Kyonggi-do, and a grain inspectors' school at Yongdungpo graduated 200."},{"t":"Agriculture Production Bureau","lv":3,"p":[254,258],"pp":"19","s":"Livestock numbers rose from 1946. The Seoul milk plant lost 30 percent of its milk for lack of ammonia. USGS arranged 400 tons each of sulphur and copper sulphate from Japan; exportable silk graded poorly."},{"t":"Bureau of Forestry","lv":3,"p":[259,259],"pp":"24","s":"Forest output for January and February 1948 was 39,660 and 36,092 cubic meters of logs. Seoul fuelwood fell from 4,000 to 2,500 won per cubic meter, and nurseries received 222 tons of ammonium nitrate."},{"t":"Bureau of Fisheries","lv":3,"p":[259,259],"pp":"24","s":"Processed marine products for 1947 were 54,870 metric tons, 3,736 below 1946, blamed on shortages of tinplate, salt and transport. A 14-vessel deep-sea expedition from Pusan landed 389 tons."},{"t":"Korea Agricultural Association","lv":3,"p":[260,262],"pp":"25","s":"KAA imported 38,464 tons of fertilizer and sold 35,091 in April; since July, 306,895 tons were received and 75 percent sold. KAA collected 839.7 million won and bought 22.2 million straw bags."},{"t":"Agricultural Improvement Service","lv":3,"p":[263,263],"pp":"28","s":"The service investigated unexplained fees levied by 20 organizations in the two Kyongsang provinces. Maj.-Gen. Dean said AIS would unify research, teaching and extension; extension agents agreed to use village discussion clubs."},{"t":"National Land Administration","lv":3,"p":[264,265],"pp":"29","s":"Under Ordinance No. 173, 168,735 of 587,974 former Japanese farms (28.7 percent) were sold by 30 April. Buyers were capped at two chungbo, price was three times annual output, paid at 20 percent of crop for 15 years."},{"t":"Section 2: Prices","lv":2,"p":[266,273],"pp":"31","s":"Retail prices in Seoul and nine cities eased seasonally in April while wholesale prices were nearly unchanged; Seoul rice retailed at 1,320 won per mal, and controlled price lists were published."},{"t":"Retail and Wholesale Prices","lv":3,"p":[266,271],"pp":"31","s":"Retail prices slipped seasonally except Korean turnip, salt, cotton shirting and briquettes. Wholesale dried fish, hemp, lumber, cement and coal rose, while cleaned rice, cotton yarn and copper fell. Seoul rice: 1,320 won per mal."},{"t":"Controlled Prices","lv":3,"p":[272,273],"pp":"37","s":"The National Price Administration's March 1948 schedule listed controlled prices, such as cleaned rice at 280 won per large mal, cotton sheeting 56 won per yard, lump anthracite 3,500 won per ton and calf leather shoes 1,660 won."},{"t":"Section 3: Industrial Production","lv":2,"p":[274,284],"pp":"39","s":"March output rose sharply: cotton yarn 28.5 percent, cloth 26.2 percent, paper 36.1 percent, nails 19.3 percent and oxygen 80 percent; salt output and cigarette shortfalls from the Monopoly Bureau are also reported."},{"t":"Processed Food","lv":3,"p":[274,274],"pp":"39","s":"The Taihan mill and six small mills made 2,386 tons of flour in March, 3.1 percent above February. Six of nine small mills made 628 tons; noodle output was 77 tons."},{"t":"Textiles","lv":3,"p":[275,275],"pp":"40","s":"Cotton yarn rose 28.5 percent to 522,000 kg, the best since June 1947; cotton cloth rose 26.2 percent to 2,286,000 meters despite power shortages, one mill getting 300 KW daily against 1,000 KW needed."},{"t":"Paper","lv":3,"p":[276,276],"pp":"41","s":"Machine-made paper from 14 mills hit a post-liberation record of 533.4 metric tons in March, against 391.9 in February. Puksun exceeded its best Japanese-era month by 16 percent."},{"t":"Hardware","lv":3,"p":[277,277],"pp":"42","s":"Nail production in March was 148.9 metric tons, 19.3 percent above February's 124.8 and near the 1947 monthly average of 142.1, from three South Korean plants."},{"t":"Chemicals","lv":3,"p":[278,280],"pp":"43","s":"Briquettes rose 41.3 percent to 6,398 tons, under a third of March 1947, for lack of pitch. Rubber shoes reached 1,899,834 pairs, oxygen 90,455 cubic meters, and common brick resumed at 10,000 tons."},{"t":"Miscellaneous","lv":3,"p":[281,281],"pp":"46","s":"Electric insulators fell to 5,512 pieces from 11,760 in February because coal was scarce, while lead pencils rose to 4,521 gross from 3,684."},{"t":"Monopoly Bureau Products","lv":3,"p":[282,284],"pp":"47","s":"Government salterns produced 16,036 tons in April, nearly triple April 1947. Cigarette output fell 17.6 percent to 288.8 tons after a week's shutdown for lack of endless tape; cut tobacco rose to 686.6 tons."},{"t":"Section 4: Mining and Minerals","lv":2,"p":[285,296],"pp":"50","s":"Anthracite output jumped 49.5 percent to 62,796 tons, mostly from the Samchuk mine; bituminous imports from Japan were 83,171 tons; pitch shortage cut briquettes; gold and other mineral output are tabulated."},{"t":"General","lv":3,"p":[285,285],"pp":"50","s":"Coal rehabilitation was beginning to meet its goal: anthracite output expanded sharply in March and April Japanese bituminous imports nearly matched February's record, nearly doubling coal supply versus three months earlier."},{"t":"Indigenous Anthracite and Lignite Coal","lv":3,"p":[285,289],"pp":"50","s":"Anthracite rose 49.5 percent to 62,796 tons, 40,596 from Samchuk; lignite reached 5,112 tons. Under half reached market, with 53,000 tons stranded at railheads. Yong Wol made 10,250 tons in April."},{"t":"Bituminous Coal","lv":3,"p":[290,290],"pp":"55","s":"Bituminous imports from Japan, the only source, were 83,171 tons in April against February's record 83,330. Railroads received 58,150 tons but needed at least 100,000 monthly."},{"t":"Shipments to Consumers","lv":3,"p":[291,291],"pp":"56","s":"Deliveries to consumers lagged production and imports because of pilferage and transport limits. In March 36,659 tons of anthracite and 51,983 tons of bituminous coal were actually supplied."},{"t":"Pitch","lv":3,"p":[291,291],"pp":"56","s":"Coal tar pitch, the binder for briquettes, was virtually unavailable in March, holding briquettes to about one-third of the 1947 average. Pitch ordered from the United States was not expected for two months."},{"t":"Other Minerals","lv":3,"p":[292,296],"pp":"57","s":"Five Bureau of Mining teams visited over 30 vested gold properties and found 683 stamps in 70 mills yielding about 20,000 grams monthly with 3,500 miners. Kobayashi Mining planned 10 tons monthly of ferro-tungsten."},{"t":"Section 5: Allocations and Distribution","lv":2,"p":[297,309],"pp":"62","s":"Consumer Goods Allocation Board procedure, civilian supply allocations, April petroleum allocations (6,414,345 liters gasoline), salt distribution of 25,803 tons, and allocation of Korean-produced commodities."},{"t":"Consumer Goods Allocation Board","lv":3,"p":[297,297],"pp":"62","s":"The National Consumer Goods Office distributed its Standard Operating Procedure summarizing Executive Order No. 12. G-4, XXIV Corps agreed incentive goods go to unit commanders where employee co-operatives exist."},{"t":"Allocation of Civilian Supplies","lv":3,"p":[297,298],"pp":"62","s":"Miscellaneous Pusan supplies would be priced against inventory and reconciled later with shipping documents, easing Yongdungpo congestion. April allocations included 4,555 bales raw cotton, 1,500 tons rubber and 78,980 bags cement."},{"t":"Petroleum","lv":3,"p":[299,301],"pp":"64","s":"April allocations were 6,414,345 liters gasoline, 3,815,507 kerosene, 2,924,821 diesel and 4,242,796 Navy Special fuel oil. An anti-illegal-sales ordinance was drafted and the Inchon pipeline to Wangemiri neared completion."},{"t":"Salt Distribution","lv":3,"p":[302,302],"pp":"67","s":"The Monopoly Bureau distributed 25,803 metric tons of salt in April, with 60,812,947 kg in stock for May. Soya-sauce rations took 21,169,282 kg and fisheries curing 3,268,020 kg."},{"t":"Allocation of Korean-Produced Commodities","lv":3,"p":[302,305],"pp":"67","s":"On 15 April the Board unanimously set a 40 percent share for government and vested-industry employees and 60 percent for the public, up from 30-70. April allocations totaled 766,082 thousand won across 446 allocations."},{"t":"Distribution of Korean-Produced Commodities","lv":3,"p":[306,309],"pp":"71","s":"The 1947 year-end bonus distribution bought 41,000,000 won of goods, 96 percent of the allocation. The FFA contract was completed 23 April: FFA receives 15 percent, pays FDA eight, retailers earn 13; 462,505 employees were eligible."},{"t":"Section 6: Imports and Exports","lv":2,"p":[310,322],"pp":"75","s":"April licenses show exports of 443.8 million won exceeding imports of 376.8 million by about 15 percent, against a 1,000 percent import excess a year earlier; Hong Kong took over 93 percent of exports."},{"t":"Private Trade Statistics","lv":3,"p":[310,314],"pp":"75","s":"April licenses (113 import, 94 export) indicate exports of 443.8 million won against imports of 376.8 million. March imports were 822.7 million won and exports 654.1 million; Hong Kong took over 93 percent of exports."},{"t":"Private Trade Activities","lv":3,"p":[315,315],"pp":"80","s":"April trade with Hong Kong was carried by vessels including the Suchow, which took 500 tons of exports. Arrivals at Pusan and Inchon included paraffin wax, caustic soda, rubber and 213 tons of wool."},{"t":"Government-to-Government Transactions","lv":3,"p":[315,316],"pp":"80","s":"Exports to Japan credited to the USAMGIK account were $351,976 for April and $4,223,237 for January-April. SCAP's offer of six cents per pound for hemp waste was accepted; 75 tons of lead bullion was offered to a New York firm."},{"t":"Korean Items for Americans in Korea","lv":3,"p":[317,317],"pp":"82","s":"Sales of Korean-made goods to the Army Exchange Service were $27,355 in April, bringing the total since August 1947 to $174,506. April deliveries were 17,434 pieces."},{"t":"Civilian Supply Imports","lv":3,"p":[318,322],"pp":"83","s":"April imports included 83,171 metric tons of coal, 47,815 tons of fertilizer with a record 15,551 tons of ammonium sulphate from Japan, rice (6,226 tons from the U.S.) and 19,515 tons of salt."},{"t":"Section 7: Labor","lv":2,"p":[323,331],"pp":"88","s":"A May Day permit dispute, a threatened Seoul Electric streetcar strike, labor law enforcement, labor education, industrial health and abandonment of the labor distribution project at refugee camps."},{"t":"Labor Union Activities","lv":3,"p":[323,324],"pp":"88","s":"Chung Pyung's May Day permit complaint went to General Dean, who warned against an illegal Seoul Electric strike and named an eight-member fact-finding committee. Tai Han won 366 of 413 votes."},{"t":"Labor Laws","lv":3,"p":[325,325],"pp":"90","s":"Samchok mines were found to have violated the Maximum Working Hours Law (Ordinance No. 121) until September 1947. Seoul ration stores were exempted from the 60-hour limit during the election; minors under 14 in government offices were dismissed."},{"t":"Labor Education","lv":3,"p":[326,326],"pp":"91","s":"The Bureau of Labor Relations showed an exhibit on American labor, planned educational tours to six towns in Kangwon-do and Chungchong-pukto, circulated 7,500 copies of Labor News and aired four Laborers' Hour broadcasts."},{"t":"Industrial Health","lv":3,"p":[327,330],"pp":"92","s":"Industrial health teams gave 6,615 immunizations at five Seoul firms and 43,969 at Kangwon-do mines, where they found poor hospitals, spoiled meat, silicosis and tuberculosis. A recreation program began for 200 girl workers."},{"t":"Labor Distribution","lv":3,"p":[331,331],"pp":"96","s":"Special labor officers were withdrawn from refugee camps at Uijongbu and Kaesong. Of 424 workers distributed during 1947 only 60 proved skilled, so the project was abandoned."},{"t":"Part III: Public Services","lv":1,"p":[332,365],"pp":"","s":"Transportation (record rail freight over 480,000 tons), communications (Cheju sabotage, new stamps), utilities (75.9 million KWH, power barges) and public works (volunteer labor, 144 fires)."},{"t":"Section 1: Transportation","lv":2,"p":[332,341],"pp":"97","s":"A 60-day shipping priority system was adopted; March Transportation revenue rose 119 percent; railroads carried over 480,000 tons of freight, the most since October 1945."},{"t":"General","lv":3,"p":[332,332],"pp":"97","s":"The Department of Transportation set a 60-day cargo priority system: 48 percent for Pusan allotments, 24 coal, 19 rice, six fertilizer from Yosu and three miscellaneous."},{"t":"Revenue and Expenditures","lv":3,"p":[332,332],"pp":"97","s":"March revenue rose 119 percent, or 283,000,000 won, the highest of F/Y 1947/48, including 161,000,000 won from the U.S. Army. Revenue was 520,065 thousand won against expenditures of 464,552 thousand."},{"t":"Railroads","lv":3,"p":[332,339],"pp":"97","s":"Anti-sabotage precautions kept April damage small. March rail receipts rose 269,000,000 won, passengers by 921,000, and freight passed 480,000 tons, the most since October 1945. Operable locomotives rose by 17."},{"t":"Marine Transportation","lv":3,"p":[340,340],"pp":"105","s":"Marine receipts for March were 147 percent above February. Employment rose to 2,540. Dredging let FS-type ships enter Pohang, continued at Kunsan and Mokpo, and wreckage removal began at Yosu."},{"t":"Highway and Air Transportation","lv":3,"p":[340,341],"pp":"105","s":"Highway expenditures fell to 356 thousand won in March while air outlays rose nine percent to 296 thousand. Neither reported income; employment was 72 on highways and 31 in air transport."},{"t":"Section 2: Communications","lv":2,"p":[342,350],"pp":"107","s":"Communist sabotage shifted to Cheju, where 16 poles and 26 wires were cut; March revenue, employment (13,113), postal savings, telephone, telegraph, radio fees, and two new stamps are reported."},{"t":"General","lv":3,"p":[342,342],"pp":"107","s":"Sabotage on the mainland waned but hit Cheju island: 16 telephone poles cut and 26 wires severed. In Kwangju, telephone and telegraph employees rioted, partly interrupting service for over 24 hours."},{"t":"Revenue and Expenditures","lv":3,"p":[342,342],"pp":"107","s":"March revenue rose markedly: telephone fees up 14,000,000 won, insurance 2,000,000 and stamps 1,000,000. Expenditures dropped over 50 percent to 39,282 thousand won for lack of year-end funds."},{"t":"Employment","lv":3,"p":[342,342],"pp":"107","s":"Department of Communications employment fell to 13,113 in March from 13,479, under Military Governor reduction orders. Postal staff numbered 3,292, insurance 871 and telephone 3,068."},{"t":"Special Functions","lv":3,"p":[342,344],"pp":"107","s":"Communications collected 726 million won for Finance in March, up over 39 percent and the highest since October 1945, with taxes at 704 million won and cigarette factories running the full month."},{"t":"Postal Deposits and Insurance","lv":3,"p":[345,346],"pp":"110","s":"Postal deposits held at 843 million won with 7,002 thousand depositors; transfer deposits were 154 million won. National Life Insurance in force rose 6,000,000 won to 1,776 million."},{"t":"Telephone and Telegraph","lv":3,"p":[347,348],"pp":"112","s":"March telephone messages were 182,000, up 17,000, with revenue of 11,464 thousand won. Domestic telegrams numbered 182,215, earning 6,691,343 won."},{"t":"Korean Broadcasting Corporation","lv":3,"p":[349,349],"pp":"114","s":"Effective 15 April, the radio license fee for Korean civilian owners of entertainment receivers rose from 40 won to 50 won per month."},{"t":"Communications Bureau","lv":3,"p":[349,349],"pp":"114","s":"The Korean Electric Power Company's Seoul-Pusan telephone circuit was re-aligned 20 April. Inspections of three Seoul plants surveyed capacity for telephone parts and 20 constabulary radio transmitters."},{"t":"Postal Bureau","lv":3,"p":[350,350],"pp":"115","s":"Two new stamps went on sale 10 April: a pink 20-won Independence Gate (one million printed) and a light brown 50-won Turtle Ship (500,000 printed)."},{"t":"Section 3: Public Utilities","lv":2,"p":[351,355],"pp":"116","s":"Electric power delivered in April was 75.9 million KWH, 33.7 million from southern sources; Emergency Power Board meetings, plant output, Seoul streetcars and the YFP-1 and Electra power barges."},{"t":"Electricity Distribution","lv":3,"p":[351,352],"pp":"116","s":"April deliveries totaled 75,900,000 KWH, slightly below March, with 42.2 million from north of 38 degrees and 33.7 from the south. Compiled data on all South Korean plants since August 1945 were shown for the first time."},{"t":"Emergency Power Board Transactions","lv":3,"p":[353,353],"pp":"118","s":"The Board met 7 and 21 April on allocation if North Korean power were cut. Unauthorized uses were estimated at 30 percent of supply. Adviser Roames outlined load dispatching and three 5,000 KVA transformers from Japan."},{"t":"Power Plants","lv":3,"p":[354,354],"pp":"119","s":"Chung Pyung hydro averaged 15,700 KW, Sumjingang 8,700 KW, Yong Wol steam 7,200 KW, and the Pusan floating plant about 6,500 KW. Tests showed Dong In Ri can generate 18,000 KW continuously."},{"t":"Street Railways","lv":3,"p":[354,354],"pp":"119","s":"Seoul streetcars ran about 135 cars. Fred Simons's study settled a dispute over 27,909,000 won in claimed taxes owed by Seoul Electric: vehicle and rail taxes due in installments, traffic tax invalid."},{"t":"Auxiliary Power Barges","lv":3,"p":[355,355],"pp":"120","s":"The 20,000 KW barge YFP-1 was berthed at Pusan and began producing at once, the first floating plant of its kind. The Navy barge Electra, with six 800 KW generators, reached Inchon."},{"t":"Section 4: Public Works","lv":2,"p":[356,365],"pp":"121","s":"No national road appropriation existed, so volunteer labor carried road, bridge, levee and fire-control work after a detailed spring inspection; April saw 144 fires and the Heavy Equipment School graduated 86."},{"t":"General","lv":3,"p":[356,356],"pp":"121","s":"April brought the most detailed inspection of Public Works sites yet, showing progress despite funding shortages. Volunteer labor on roads, bridges and irrigation increased and new volunteer fire companies were installed."},{"t":"Highways","lv":3,"p":[356,356],"pp":"121","s":"Highway employment and spending were nearly nil without a national appropriation. Surveys covered 54 km of the planned Seoul-east coast highway, the Kangnung-Samchok mine road and the Yongwol-Chungju-Chochiwon road."},{"t":"Bridges","lv":3,"p":[357,357],"pp":"122","s":"The Andong bridge was 20 percent complete at 4,478 thousand won, and a contract was let 22 April for the Waegwan bridge deck. Capt. Nelson's Namjong Bridge redesign saves 5,000 bags of cement and 3,500,500 won."},{"t":"Irrigation and Flood Control","lv":3,"p":[357,361],"pp":"122","s":"A Water Resources Committee under Choi Kyong-wol met 2 and 23 April and began reviewing the Sum Jin Gang dam. A flood report cited 311 rivers and 1,250,000 feet of needed levees."},{"t":"Urban Water Supply","lv":3,"p":[362,362],"pp":"127","s":"The City Planning Bureau drew up the 1948/49 water works program. Power failures were the main problem, and the Utilities Commission approved Seoul rate increases up to 80 times 1937 levels, about 25 won per seven cubic meters."},{"t":"Maps and Surveys","lv":3,"p":[362,364],"pp":"127","s":"The Bureau of Geodetic Survey studied the accuracy of the 1:50,000 maps and compiled all triangulation points. The pages also hold flood control programs (4,866 million won to complete, 69,500 hectares)."},{"t":"Fire Control","lv":3,"p":[365,365],"pp":"130","s":"April saw 144 fires with 76,018,490 won in losses, one-fifth of March's; January-April totaled 693 fires. All but five of Seoul's 32 fire lanes were passable, and a firm produced 1,200 yards of hose."},{"t":"Education","lv":3,"p":[365,365],"pp":"130","s":"On 3 April the Heavy Equipment School at Ascom City graduated its second class of 86 Korean engineers after a three-month course, 12 February to 3 April."},{"t":"Part IV: Finance","lv":1,"p":[366,385],"pp":"","s":"Government finance (note issue down 5,213 million won from December's 33,389 million, overdraft 22,005 million, budget 23,370 million) and non-government finance (deposits 55,305 million, loans 32,120 million)."},{"t":"Section 1: Government Finance","lv":2,"p":[366,375],"pp":"131","s":"Bank of Chosun note issue fell 5,213 million won from December's 33,389 million; cumulative overdraft hit 22,005 million; March revenue set a record of 2,135 million won."},{"t":"Note Issue of the Bank of Chosun","lv":3,"p":[366,367],"pp":"131","s":"Note issue peaked at 33,389 million won in December 1947 and fell 5,213 million by end of April, owing to completed rice collection, record tax collections and tobacco sales, and 1,132 million won of deposit transfers."},{"t":"Overdraft to South Korean Interim Government","lv":3,"p":[368,368],"pp":"133","s":"Cumulative excess withdrawals reached a new high of 22,005 million won at end of April. April withdrawals were 2,340 million against 1,555 million deposits; total overdrafts were 39,325 million."},{"t":"Expenditure Budget of the South Korean Interim Government","lv":3,"p":[369,370],"pp":"134","s":"The approved F/Y 1947/48 budget of 21,407 million won, with 787,000,000 added and 1,177 million pending, totals 23,370 million. The 1948/49 recommendation is 28,704,824 thousand won expenditure against 27,821,485 thousand revenue."},{"t":"Monthly Revenues and Expenditures","lv":3,"p":[371,371],"pp":"136","s":"March revenues of 2,135 million won set a monthly record, 507 million above February. Monopoly Bureau receipts were 1,004 million won, taxes 432 million; expenditures rose 601 million over February."},{"t":"Ratios Between Revenues and Expenditures","lv":3,"p":[372,373],"pp":"137","s":"Cumulative F/Y 1947/48 revenue of 15,336 million won, led by the Monopoly Bureau at 7,294 million, was 79 percent of expenditure payments of 19,319 million."},{"t":"Monopoly Bureau","lv":3,"p":[374,374],"pp":"139","s":"Monopoly Bureau March expenditures were 195.5 million won and fiscal year 3,539 million, mostly tobacco (2,698 million). Year revenue was 7,294 million; tobacco supplies 36 percent of estimated revenue."},{"t":"Civilian Supply Program","lv":3,"p":[374,375],"pp":"139","s":"The Civilian Supply Program sells goods bought from Japan and the United States through SKIG channels. The balance stood at 5,319 million won at end of April 1948, with 13,686 million in receivables."},{"t":"Section 2: Non-Government Finance","lv":2,"p":[376,385],"pp":"141","s":"Deposits in financial institutions rose to a record 55,305 million won, loans fell to 32,120 million, cash balances rose to 3,508.9 million, and insurance and bank activities are reported."},{"t":"Cash Balances in Financial Institutions","lv":3,"p":[376,376],"pp":"141","s":"Cash balances in financial institutions rose 1,088 million won in March to 3,508.9 million, partly a rebound from February's fall. South Korea has no securities markets and no legal liquid-reserve requirement."},{"t":"Deposits in Financial Institutions","lv":3,"p":[377,378],"pp":"142","s":"Deposit balances rose 6,279 million won, nearly 13 percent, to a record 55,305 million in March. Government balances grew nearly 1,900 million to 28,926 million; inter-bank balances reached 4,151 million."},{"t":"Deposit Balances in Each Financial Institution","lv":3,"p":[379,379],"pp":"144","s":"Public deposits rose 3,221 million won to a record 22,228 million, and every deposit type rose at every institution in March. Government deposits gained mainly at the Bank of Chosun; total balances were 55,305 million."},{"t":"Loans by Financial Institutions","lv":3,"p":[380,381],"pp":"145","s":"March was the third straight month retirements exceeded new loans; outstanding loans fell 1,591 million won to 32,120 million. The Bank of Chosun held 30 percent; loans equalled 58 percent of deposits."},{"t":"Life Insurance","lv":3,"p":[382,382],"pp":"147","s":"Insurance in force rose 74.4 million won, nine percent, to 814.6 million in March. Premiums collected were 68 percent above February; four companies reported."},{"t":"Damage Insurance","lv":3,"p":[382,382],"pp":"147","s":"Companies wrote 9,265 new policies in March. Liabilities assumed of nearly 4,400 million won set a record, 42 percent above February, and premiums written reached 51.6 million."},{"t":"Life and Damage Insurance Activities","lv":3,"p":[383,383],"pp":"148","s":"Damage insurers were told to limit investments to mortgages, real estate, bonds, stocks, deposits and Korean Government obligations. A new life insurance salary scale raised maximum salaries about 50 percent."},{"t":"Activities of Financial Institutions","lv":3,"p":[383,385],"pp":"148","s":"The Bank Examination Section examined its first Korean bank. Controller of Commodities proposed repaying the 12,502 million won rice loan by 31 October. Ordinance 182 replaced 100 won notes; 39 loans totaling 713 million won were approved."},{"t":"Part V: Government and Politics","lv":1,"p":[386,419],"pp":"","s":"Election preparations (7,730,000 registered, 942 candidates), KILA's dissolution, the Pyongyang conference, Executive Order 20 moving the election to 10 May, police reform, Cheju violence and weekly North-South mail."},{"t":"Section 1: Administration","lv":2,"p":[386,388],"pp":"151","s":"Registration closed 9 April with 7,730,000 voters and 942 candidates; the National Economic Board met seven times; Military Government personnel numbered 3,041."},{"t":"Preparation for National Election","lv":3,"p":[386,386],"pp":"151","s":"Registration closed 9 April with 7,730,000 voters, over 94 percent of those eligible; 200 districts and 13,407 polling places were set and 942 candidates registered. Public Information ran 100 film shorts and radio coverage."},{"t":"National Economic Board","lv":3,"p":[387,387],"pp":"152","s":"The NEB met seven times and reviewed F/Y 1948/49 budgets, authorised 400 million won credit for the Korean Irrigation Union, passed 250 million for the Sumjingang Dam, and approved summer grain collection of at least 20 percent."},{"t":"Military Government Personnel","lv":3,"p":[388,388],"pp":"153","s":"Military Government personnel numbered 3,041 in April 1948 (547 officers, 1,469 enlisted men, 1,025 civilians), up from 2,740 in March; the total was 3,721 in October 1946."},{"t":"Section 2: Korean Interim Legislative Assembly","lv":2,"p":[389,390],"pp":"154","s":"KILA met once, on 19 April, enacted nothing and adjourned for 30 days, tantamount to dissolution; its record was 11 Public Acts and four resolutions."},{"t":"April Session","lv":3,"p":[389,389],"pp":"154","s":"KILA met only on 19 April and enacted no legislation. It rejected three resignations to keep membership at 49, tabled pending bills until the National Assembly convenes, and adjourned 30 days."},{"t":"Summary of KILA Legislation","lv":3,"p":[389,390],"pp":"154","s":"KILA, convened 12 December 1946 with 45 elected and 45 appointed members, passed 11 Public Acts and four resolutions, and nearly dissolved three times: May 1947, November 1947 and January-February 1948."},{"t":"Section 3: Political Developments","lv":2,"p":[391,400],"pp":"156","s":"The Pyongyang North-South conference from 19 April, which Kim Koo and Kimm Kiu-sic attended, 939 candidates for 200 seats, and landlord and communist opposition to farmland sales."},{"t":"General","lv":3,"p":[391,391],"pp":"156","s":"The Pyongyang conference opening 19 April and the first general election absorbed parties. Over 90 percent registered and 939 candidates sought 200 seats when registration closed 16 April."},{"t":"North-South Conference","lv":3,"p":[391,396],"pp":"156","s":"Moderates and leftists backed the conference; the Hankook Democratic Party and Rhee opposed it. Kim Koo and Kimm Kiu-sic went to Pyongyang. A 30 April communique rejected the separate election."},{"t":"Election Preparations","lv":3,"p":[397,399],"pp":"162","s":"Rightists formed an acceleration committee under O Sei-chang; 939 candidates, 17 of them women, contested 200 seats, with Rhee's society fielding 239 and Dai Dong 89. Choi Nung-chin's challenge to Rhee was nullified 7 May."},{"t":"Land Reform","lv":3,"p":[400,400],"pp":"163","s":"Extreme rightists, backed largely by landlords, opposed sale of New Korea Company land to tenants. Communists accused U.S. authorities of keeping proceeds and wanted free distribution as in North Korea."},{"t":"Section 4: Legal Affairs","lv":2,"p":[401,412],"pp":"166","s":"Executive Order 20 moved the election to 10 May because of an eclipse; also Ordinances 182-191, Justice Department opinions, prison conditions, amnesty release, and Judge Lobingier's draft Civil Code."},{"t":"Ordinances and Orders","lv":3,"p":[401,402],"pp":"166","s":"Executive Order 20 moved the election to 10 May because of an eclipse; Ordinances 182-191 raised the income tax exemption to 25,000 won. Hodge proclaimed 11 rights of Koreans on 5 April."},{"t":"Legal Opinions","lv":3,"p":[403,404],"pp":"168","s":"Justice opinions held foreign firms such as Jardine, Matheson need no licence to trade, that English is official in the National Land Administration, and that the National Food Administration is independent of the NEB."},{"t":"Penal Institutions","lv":3,"p":[405,406],"pp":"170","s":"Prisons were guarded against election-time release attempts. Kunchon Prison became an adult institution taking about 200 overflow prisoners. The Kaesong Boys' Reformatory held 1,100 boys aged 14 to 20."},{"t":"Court Liaison","lv":3,"p":[407,407],"pp":"172","s":"Release of prisoners under the Military Governor's amnesty was completed in April except for some transferred prisoners being traced; many pardon-order copies could not be delivered for lack of addresses."},{"t":"Property Claims Commission","lv":3,"p":[407,407],"pp":"172","s":"The Justice adviser and Property Custodian recommended transferring claims with abundant proof of Korean ownership to the Custodian, and the Commission concurred, speeding administrative release of property."},{"t":"Codification Bureau","lv":3,"p":[407,412],"pp":"172","s":"Judge C. Sumner Lobingier's proposed Civil Code, Korea's first, was finished and being translated. It has four books: Persons, Obligations, Property and Succession."},{"t":"Section 5: National Police","lv":2,"p":[413,414],"pp":"178","s":"The court-warrant rule worked better than expected; communist violence aimed at registration, attacking about 52 of 13,455 booths; Cheju-do rioting killed 22 civilians and seven police by 19 April."},{"t":"Reform Measures","lv":3,"p":[413,413],"pp":"178","s":"The court-warrant requirement worked more smoothly than expected, though police felt they lost face when over 3,000 minor political offenders were released and resented limits on force."},{"t":"Violence","lv":3,"p":[413,413],"pp":"178","s":"About 52 of 13,455 registration booths were attacked, led by Kyongsang-pukto with 21. Cheju-do's outbreak from 3 April involved about 2,000 rioters; 22 civilians and seven police were killed to 19 April."},{"t":"Warning","lv":3,"p":[414,414],"pp":"179","s":"Communists were renewing efforts to win adherents among security forces, telling police the Americans were leaving and joining the winning side would be wise."},{"t":"Section 6: Interzone Activities","lv":2,"p":[415,419],"pp":"180","s":"Weekly North-South mail exchange handled 59,000 pieces, Radio Pyongyang attacked the southern election, and 38th parallel incidents reached a new high with 21 Koreans killed since 7 February."},{"t":"Mail Exchange","lv":3,"p":[415,415],"pp":"180","s":"Mail exchange became weekly from 15 April. Four exchanges handled 59,000 pieces versus 37,000 in March; the largest, on 3 April, carried 9,768 letters from South Korea."},{"t":"Radio Pyongyang Intercepts","lv":3,"p":[415,416],"pp":"180","s":"Radio Pyongyang used the Joint Conference to attack the southern election and denounce Rhee. Kim Il-sung's opening speech was the key broadcast; the People's Assembly adopted a constitution 28-30 April."},{"t":"Border Incidents","lv":3,"p":[417,419],"pp":"182","s":"Police Director Chough Pyong-ok said border incidents reached a new high, with Soviet soldiers accompanying raids. Since 7 February 21 Koreans were killed and about 150 wounded; marauders violated the border 74 times."},{"t":"Part VI: Social","lv":1,"p":[420,449],"pp":"","s":"Public health (cholera vaccine, Mukden repatriates), education (literacy from 21 to 71 percent), culture (Arbor Day, Olympic team), press, Korean National Youth (774,949 members) and women's activities."},{"t":"Section 1: Public Health and Welfare","lv":2,"p":[420,424],"pp":"185","s":"Welfare and medical teams sailed with the Mukden repatriate ship; cholera vaccine reached the provinces; nurse training began; 10,053 housing units were completed; lepers, narcotics and laboratories are covered."},{"t":"General","lv":3,"p":[420,420],"pp":"185","s":"Public Health outfitted the Mukden refugee ship with welfare and preventive medicine teams, began a graduate nurse program, and sent cholera vaccine to the provinces for three-phase vaccination."},{"t":"Preventive Medicine","lv":3,"p":[420,420],"pp":"185","s":"Typhus and typhoid declined. The National Health Center milk station served 120 infants daily. The Mukden refugee ship sailed 30 April with a 14-day quarantine planned."},{"t":"Medical Services","lv":3,"p":[421,421],"pp":"186","s":"The last UNRRA medical shipment, worth about $2,000, was divided among four leprosaria at Yosu, Sorakto, Taegu and Pusan. The tenth Lasan Sanitorium course finished, totalling 50 graduates."},{"t":"Nursing Affairs","lv":3,"p":[421,421],"pp":"186","s":"A three-month graduate nurse program began 1 April at three U.S. Army hospitals under the 80th Medical Group. Pusan Transportation Hospital was found poor, Pusan City Hospital much improved."},{"t":"Veterinary Affairs","lv":3,"p":[422,422],"pp":"187","s":"Cattle treated rose from 26,302 in 1946 to 34,591 in 1947, with 97 percent recoveries. The fifth veterinary bulletin on horse breeding was distributed, 560 copies in all."},{"t":"Pharmaceutical Affairs","lv":3,"p":[422,422],"pp":"187","s":"Military Government received 520,000 pounds of DDT, distributed through Army Malaria Control Units, and the first 100,000 bars of soap. Only two of 44 pharmacist candidates completed all three exam parts."},{"t":"Narcotics Section","lv":3,"p":[422,422],"pp":"187","s":"The Narcotics Section licensed 74 physicians and 31 pharmacists to handle narcotics, and inspected the narcotics ward at Sun Hwa Hospital, Seoul, interviewing six addicts."},{"t":"Laboratories","lv":3,"p":[423,423],"pp":"188","s":"The National Vaccine Laboratory in Seoul met requirements and increased cholera, typhoid and rabies vaccine output; smallpox vaccine was delayed by budget difficulties."},{"t":"Public Assistance","lv":3,"p":[423,424],"pp":"188","s":"A flood-season relief plan relies on local organizations. Welfare officers completed 10,053 low-cost housing units, five child welfare institutions were licensed, and the Kaesong camp processes 500 to 1,000 refugees."},{"t":"Section 2: Education","lv":2,"p":[425,430],"pp":"190","s":"Adult literacy rose from 21 to 71 percent, curriculum workshops grew, South Korea's first model hospital ward was arranged, and each bureau reports activities including 13,230,670 textbooks published."},{"t":"General","lv":3,"p":[425,425],"pp":"190","s":"Curriculum workshops in art, music, science, home economics and English drew many teachers. The Medical Section arranged South Korea's first model hospital ward, and adult education greatly reduced illiteracy."},{"t":"Bureau of Research and Special Subjects","lv":3,"p":[425,426],"pp":"190","s":"Literacy rose from 21 percent in August 1945 to 71 percent in August 1947. Higher education grew from 19 to 26 schools and 3,039 to 19,241 students; adult schools reached 15,502."},{"t":"Special Activities","lv":3,"p":[427,427],"pp":"192","s":"A sub-committee proposed reopening Korean schools in Japan with approved textbooks. A Personnel Ceiling Board cut the Department's table of organization from 177 to 143."},{"t":"Bureau of Administration","lv":3,"p":[427,427],"pp":"192","s":"A 3,200,000 won budget request was cut to 2,200,000 won. 2,041 new classrooms were built, yet classes average 95 children; per-classroom cost was estimated at 710,000 won."},{"t":"Bureau of Common Schools","lv":3,"p":[427,427],"pp":"192","s":"Curriculum workshops at Taegu and Seoul trained teachers to run provincial workshops. Plans included 10 English vacation institutes, and half of Seoul's middle schools had American volunteer English teachers."},{"t":"Bureau of Teachers' Training","lv":3,"p":[428,428],"pp":"193","s":"The Bureau prepared a booklet on teaching United Nations facts and sent a draft Educators' Guide to Washington. A Chunju meeting drew 600 against 200 expected; a summer session could train 10,000 teachers."},{"t":"Bureau of Higher Education","lv":3,"p":[428,428],"pp":"193","s":"Advisers helped develop Taegu Agriculture College's curriculum. A Seoul National University committee chose a model ward of about 30 beds, and 2,000 nursing ethics textbooks were printed."},{"t":"Bureau of Adult Education and Culture","lv":3,"p":[429,429],"pp":"194","s":"The Bureau prepared a manuscript on the schools' responsibility in the 10 May election and submitted it to the Department of Public Information for publication."},{"t":"Bureau of Textbooks","lv":3,"p":[429,429],"pp":"194","s":"13,230,670 textbooks had been published since 15 August 1945, including 678,500 Grade 6 science copies in April. The first 7,000 English-language textbooks arrived."},{"t":"American Language Institute","lv":3,"p":[429,429],"pp":"194","s":"The American Language Institute began a new term on 26 April; the middle school teachers' evening class will meet four times weekly. One thousand surplus library books from Inchon went to it and normal schools."},{"t":"Bureau of Vocational Education","lv":3,"p":[430,430],"pp":"195","s":"The adviser prepared a supplementary budget for a Korean Bureau of Vocational Education. A Korean Home Economics Association was organised; Taegu and Kaesong trips found equipment shortages."},{"t":"Section 3: Culture","lv":2,"p":[431,435],"pp":"196","s":"Arbor Day tree planting (100,000,000 trees), Hodge's gift of instruments to the Symphony Orchestra, music and dance events, a Night of Poets, broadcasting and the Olympic team."},{"t":"Arbor Day","lv":3,"p":[431,431],"pp":"196","s":"On 5 April, Arbor Day, Military Governor Dean planted the first of 100,000,000 trees nationwide, five per person; 26,000 trees covering 19 acres were planted on Nam San with about 10,000 participants."},{"t":"Korea Symphony Orchestra","lv":3,"p":[431,431],"pp":"196","s":"On 25 April Lt.-Gen. Hodge presented instruments from U.S. personnel to the Korea Symphony Orchestra, now 60 musicians. Lim Won-sik conducted the first Korean performance of Douglas Moore's Village Music."},{"t":"Music and Dances","lv":3,"p":[432,432],"pp":"197","s":"Events included a 28-29 April election publicity program by Seoul pupils, the 22d symphony concert under Kim In-soo, a rain dance by farmers on Nam San, and a 17 April court music program for UNTCOK."},{"t":"Other Cultural Activities","lv":3,"p":[433,433],"pp":"198","s":"About 10,000 viewed cherry blossoms at the Zoological Gardens on 11 April. A Night of Poets on 25 April supported Korean culture in Japan. Two stamps issued 10 April showed Independence Arch and the turtle ship."},{"t":"Korea Broadcasting System","lv":3,"p":[434,434],"pp":"199","s":"The Korea Broadcasting System publicised the election, ran a 52-student radio class at Ewha College, recorded Korean songs for pressing in the United States, and made two remote opera broadcasts."},{"t":"Sports","lv":3,"p":[435,435],"pp":"200","s":"Fifty-eight athletes were selected for the London Olympics. A 560,000-ticket lottery raised funds to $140,000, averting a 35-man cut. Suh Yun-bok won the Boston-memorial marathon on 18 April."},{"t":"Section 4: Press and Public Opinion","lv":2,"p":[436,438],"pp":"201","s":"Press focused on the election and the Pyongyang conference: riots, law enforcement, the amnesty of 3,140 offenders, and fear of Japan after the closure of Korean schools."},{"t":"General","lv":3,"p":[436,436],"pp":"201","s":"The 10 May election and the 19-24 April Pyongyang Conference dominated the press. Koreans read the closing of Korean schools in Japan as a plot to re-enslave Korea; American authorities saw a communist scheme."},{"t":"Pyongyang Conference","lv":3,"p":[436,436],"pp":"201","s":"Conference coverage filled most newspaper space early in April. Rightist reporters found liberties completely suppressed in Soviet-controlled North Korea, while leftist reporters found conditions perfect."},{"t":"Registration for Election","lv":3,"p":[436,436],"pp":"201","s":"Hodge's 2 April secret-ballot statement was printed, the civil rights ordinance took effect 1 April and an amnesty freed 3,140 offenders. 52 of 13,455 centers were attacked; Kyeng Hyang Sin Mun reported 91.7 percent registered."},{"t":"Political Campaign","lv":3,"p":[437,437],"pp":"202","s":"Rightist papers complained that independents made up 48 percent of 939 candidates for 200 seats. Dean's 8 April press conference denied charges about a High Commissioner, Bank of Chosun notes and land sales."},{"t":"Law Enforcement","lv":3,"p":[437,437],"pp":"202","s":"A Fatherland Protection Group enlisted men aged 18 to 55 against election terrorism. The Supreme Court rejected American advisers' 3 April petition on police warrants; Chang Duk-soo's assassins' sentences were commuted 23 April."},{"t":"Riots and Disorders","lv":3,"p":[438,438],"pp":"203","s":"Disturbances in the south continued, with violence on Cheju-do from 3-7 April. A police report cited 15 killed and 30 wounded; a 29 April report described 2,000 armed men in dugouts and martial law."},{"t":"Fear of Japan","lv":3,"p":[438,438],"pp":"203","s":"Papers protested the 31 March closing of Korean schools in Japan as an attack on Korean culture, while printing Dean's statement that they were communist training centers. The press feared Japanese travel permits would revive militarism."},{"t":"Section 5: Korean National Youth, Inc.","lv":2,"p":[439,441],"pp":"204","s":"Korean National Youth mobilized 67,475 members for Arbor Day, grew to 774,949 members, opened training centers, trained apprentices, and urged voting in the 10 May election."},{"t":"Community Service","lv":3,"p":[439,439],"pp":"204","s":"On Arbor Day, 5 April, 67,475 members in nine provinces planted 3,451,300 trees in 1,086,880 working hours; 1,246,480 trees were planted in early 1948 versus 175,000 in 1947. 7,000 members cleaned Inchon."},{"t":"Membership and Training","lv":3,"p":[439,440],"pp":"204","s":"KNY membership reached 774,949 on 30 April 1948, including 108,326 women, growing by over 3,000 daily; 1,000,000 was forecast by 30 June. Training centers rose to 62, and 36,599 leaders were trained."},{"t":"Vocational Training","lv":3,"p":[441,441],"pp":"206","s":"KNY emphasized recruiting employed workers and training apprentices, with on-the-job classes in about six communication and transportation industries. At Yongdongpo, apprentices assembled prefabricated homes."},{"t":"Election Preparations","lv":3,"p":[441,441],"pp":"206","s":"Thousands of KNY members urged neighbors to vote in the 10 May election without backing any candidate, held neighborhood sessions on suffrage, and distributed ballots and boxes where funds were lacking."},{"t":"Section 6: Women's Activities","lv":2,"p":[442,449],"pp":"207","s":"The Women's Bureau's election education, the Women's Press, sewing aids, a YWCA feeding program ($57,000), Junior Red Cross, KNY women and profiles of professional women; blank pages follow."},{"t":"The Women's Bureau","lv":3,"p":[442,442],"pp":"207","s":"The Bureau gave over 50 election speeches and toured provinces with the film The Peoples Choice. Its mothers' school averaged 80 attendees and 5,000 copies of New Homemaking were distributed."},{"t":"The Women's Press","lv":3,"p":[443,443],"pp":"208","s":"The Women's Press, Korea's only women's newspaper, founded 21 April 1947 with 27 staff and 8,000 daily circulation, urged women to vote in its 8 April editorial, citing 15,000,000 eligible women."},{"t":"Sewing Aids","lv":3,"p":[444,444],"pp":"209","s":"U.S. church women's groups sent several hundred dollars' worth of sewing aids, handed to Korean welfare agencies. A family newly arrived from Manchuria altered clothing supplied by LARA."},{"t":"Domestic Economy Science Association","lv":3,"p":[444,444],"pp":"209","s":"Korea's first home economics association was organized in April by adviser Mrs. Lyda Heffron at Shangshin Girls' Middle School, Seoul, with 30 charter members; only Seoul is active."},{"t":"The YWCA","lv":3,"p":[444,444],"pp":"209","s":"The National YWCA received $57,000 from American Overseas Aid for Adolescents and the UN Appeal for Children to feed 2,000 undernourished adolescents at three Seoul primary schools; Dr. Y.Y. Kim is examining physician."},{"t":"Junior Red Cross","lv":3,"p":[444,444],"pp":"209","s":"The first Korean Junior Red Cross members were initiated at a Seoul ceremony in April. Its first project is health education, supported by 50,000 boxes of toilet articles from the American Junior Red Cross."},{"t":"Women of Korean National Youth","lv":3,"p":[445,445],"pp":"210","s":"The KNY women's section under Kim Hyun Suk trains women for citizenship; 108,326 of 774,949 members were women. Training covers first aid, home nursing and child care; 121 graduated at Suwon."},{"t":"Professional Women","lv":3,"p":[445,449],"pp":"210","s":"Profiles of the Koh sisters: Evelyn (Doshisha law degree, Michigan M.A.), Gladys (piano diploma) and Dr. Nan (M.D.). They ran the Chamawon settlement; Evelyn now heads the Women's Bureau, Gladys the Red Cross women's volunteers."}],"by":"claude-sonnet (Claude Code subagent)"},{"id":"J-32","ia":"summation-non-military-activities-japan-no-32-1948-05","s":"J","no":32,"d":"1948-05","d2":"","t":"Summation of Non-Military Activities in Japan, No. 32 (May 1948)","v":"","pg":206,"o":"Summation No. 32 reports on Occupation non-military activities in Japan for May 1948, with most economic statistics running through March or April. Politically the month was dominated by the Ashida coalition's dispute over suspending war-bond interest, settled by a one-year deferral, and by Cabinet struggles over the 1948-49 budget and local finance. The report reprints MacArthur's message and other statements on the first anniversary of the Constitution, the Cabinet's Second Economic White Paper and the Economic Stabilization Board's five-year rehabilitation plan. It records party conventions, the Diet's investigation of political donations, the dissolution of the purge committees with 301,815 persons purged, and war crimes trial totals. Economic sections give detailed output, trade, price, finance and labor series, including postwar highs in steel, paper and textiles. The social sections cover health, nutrition, repatriation, education, religion and the press. Historians will find the primary-source texts and monthly statistical series especially useful.","h":["The Cabinet accepted Chief Cabinet Secretary Tomabechi's plan on 14 May, formally announced 22 May, deferring war-bond interest for one year from June, resolving a coalition split over the 460 million yen due.","MacArthur's 3 May message on the Constitution's first anniversary urged vigilance, local autonomy, free enterprise and a responsible press and labor, while Ashida and other leaders spoke before the Emperor at the Diet.","The Cabinet issued the Second Economic White Paper on 28 May, and the Economic Stabilization Board's 17 May draft five-year plan aims to restore the 1930-34 living standard by 1952, with coal at 44 million tons.","The purge committees were dissolved on 10 May under Cabinet Order No. 62, with 301,815 persons announced as purged; the Attorney-General's Investigation Bureau took over enforcement.","The Cabinet approved a 399.3 billion yen 1948-49 budget on 30 May after wrangling over rail and postal rates and wage base, while a 25.8 billion yen provisional budget passed 28 May.","April output set postwar records: pig iron 55,072 tons, steel ingots 118,557 tons, coke 233,940 tons, and paper, spun silk yarn, rayon and cotton and silk fabrics all reached postwar highs.","By 20 May, 666 war criminals had been tried and 617 convicted, including 95 sentenced to death; the Maritime Safety Board was activated 1 May and the first Impeachment Court president was elected.","Of 33,635 Japanese repatriated 30 April to 27 May, 97 percent came from Soviet areas, and the Ministry of Education's 7 May policy treated Korean schools separately."],"k":["Ashida cabinet","war bond interest","Constitution anniversary","Economic White Paper","Economic Rehabilitation Plan","purge","Illegal Transactions Investigation Committee","budget 1948-49","coal production","industrial production","labor relations","repatriation","Maritime Safety Board","war crimes trials","Korean schools"],"toc":[{"t":"Front matter","lv":1,"p":[1,5],"pp":"","s":"Cover, contents for Parts I to IV with 109 numbered charts, and lists of charts, maps and graphs by subject, ending with Men of Japan."},{"t":"Part I: General","lv":1,"p":[6,15],"pp":"1","s":"Digest of May 1948: no change in SCAP non-military organization, with paragraphs summarizing the political, economic and social developments detailed in Parts II to IV."},{"t":"Section 1: Organization Under SCAP","lv":2,"p":[7,7],"pp":"3","s":"States that the non-military organization of General Headquarters, SCAP, underwent no change during May 1948."},{"t":"Section 2: Political Activities","lv":2,"p":[7,8],"pp":"3","s":"Constitution anniversary on 3 May, war-bond interest dispute ending in a one-year deferral, 16 laws enacted, purge committees dissolved with 301,815 purged, Maritime Safety Board, and war crimes results (53 suspects, 44 convicted)."},{"t":"Section 3: Economic Activities","lv":2,"p":[9,13],"pp":"7","s":"April output rose in coke (233,940 tons), pig iron, steel, cement, paper and textiles. First-quarter trade deficit was $151.8 million; ration needs met from carryover stocks; labor calmer; tax receipts above estimate."},{"t":"Section 4: Social Activities","lv":2,"p":[14,15],"pp":"17","s":"April saw 3,237 communicable disease cases, 123,511 orphans counted and 33,635 repatriates, 97 percent from Soviet areas. Also the 7 May Korean school policy, a new National Christian Council and 379 Meiji sites delisted."},{"t":"Part II: Political","lv":1,"p":[16,80],"pp":"21","s":"Detailed political section: government and politics (Constitution anniversary, war-bond interest, White Paper, budget, parties, purge), public safety, and legal affairs and war crimes."},{"t":"Section 1: Government and Politics","lv":2,"p":[17,74],"pp":"23","s":"Constitution anniversary, war-bond interest deferral, Second Economic White Paper, five-year rehabilitation plan, budget wrangling, Diet, party conventions, and the purge dissolution."},{"t":"Anniversary of Promulgation of the Constitution","lv":3,"p":[17,21],"pp":"23","s":"3 May anniversary: MacArthur's message urged vigilance, local autonomy, free enterprise and responsible press and labor. Ashida and other leaders spoke before the Emperor; fourteen first-year reforms were listed, from popular sovereignty to land reform."},{"t":"War Bond Interest Payment Issue","lv":3,"p":[21,24],"pp":"30","s":"Coalition parties split over suspending war-bond interest (460 million yen due in June). Tomabechi proposed a one-year deferral 8 May; the Cabinet adopted it 14 May and announced it 22 May over financial-circle opposition."},{"t":"National Government","lv":3,"p":[24,25],"pp":"36","s":"On 22 May the Cabinet set ten principles for administrative readjustment, shifting authority to local governments. Local bodies rallied 24 May; remaining demobilization work passed to civilian ministries 31 May, with about 500,000 servicemen still in Soviet areas."},{"t":"Second Economic \"White Paper\"","lv":3,"p":[25,47],"pp":"38","s":"The Ashida cabinet's 28 May report finds living conditions hard but prospects brighter: coal reached 29.32 million tons (97.7 percent of target), rice quota met, note issue fell early 1948. It urges self-support."},{"t":"Foreword and Basic Conditions of Postwar Economy","lv":4,"p":[25,25],"pp":"38","s":"Hard living conditions persist but coal output, expected Allied aid and full rice delivery brighten the outlook. Handicaps: war losses of about 20 percent of prewar assets (45.7 billion yen), low trade, rapid population growth."},{"t":"General Survey of Present Economic Conditions","lv":4,"p":[26,27],"pp":"39","s":"Population reached 78.62 million through repatriation of 5.77 million. Industrial production averaged 43.1 percent of 1930-34 in fiscal 1947; rice crop 59.67 million koku; trade deficit 520 million dollars; transport remained the bottleneck."},{"t":"Coal Production Increase and Power Situation","lv":4,"p":[28,29],"pp":"44","s":"Coal-mine priorities and the Emergency Coal Production Increase Program produced a record 2.95 million tons in December. Drought and typhoon damage caused power shortages; 30.5 billion kWh was generated in 1947."},{"t":"Situation in Manufacturing Industry","lv":4,"p":[30,30],"pp":"48","s":"Output rose in all categories except food processing and crude oil, led by metals (71 percent) and chemicals (31 percent). Finished steel 569,727 tons; pig iron 411,462 tons; cement 1.28 million tons against a 1.67 million target."},{"t":"Production in Agriculture, Forestry and Aquatic Industries","lv":4,"p":[31,31],"pp":"50","s":"Fertilizer for rice rose to 4.3 kan per tan, about 60 percent of demand. Rice (59.67 million koku) stayed below 1930-34 levels; the typhoon cost 2.5 million koku; fish output was 62 percent of prewar."},{"t":"Transportation and Communication","lv":4,"p":[32,33],"pp":"52","s":"Government railways carried 112 million tons (85 percent of requirements); coastal shipping over 10.68 million tons, up 88 percent. Ports run at 40 to 60 percent of prewar capacity; Tokyo telephones fail in 54 of 100 calls."},{"t":"Foreign Trade Returns","lv":4,"p":[34,34],"pp":"56","s":"In 1947 imports were 526 million dollars and exports 174 million, a 352 million deficit covered by United States relief funds. Textiles supplied over half of exports; 68.2 percent of imports went to prevent hunger and disease."},{"t":"Prospects for Foreign Trade","lv":4,"p":[35,35],"pp":"58","s":"East Asia took 66.8 percent of exports while 88.3 percent of imports came from the United States. A 60 million dollar cotton loan helps, but dollar shortage and unsettled exchange rates remain obstacles."},{"t":"Trend of Inflation","lv":4,"p":[36,36],"pp":"60","s":"Tokyo indexes of official, black-market and effective prices are tabulated. Black-market retail prices rose only 16 percent from November to February, against 68 percent a year earlier, after the price revision."},{"t":"Currency Situation","lv":4,"p":[37,37],"pp":"62","s":"Bank of Japan note issue grew 103 billion yen in fiscal 1947, ending at 218.7 billion in March, with a 40 billion rise in December. January and February 1948 showed declines. Deposits fell short of the 232.3 billion yen needed."},{"t":"Balance of Government Finance","lv":4,"p":[37,38],"pp":"63","s":"General Account near balance as a pay-tax campaign lifted collections to 124.9 billion yen by March. Income-tax payers rose to 28.6 percent of the population; Special Account borrowing reached 66.8 billion yen, mostly from the Bank of Japan."},{"t":"Recent Condition of Industrial Enterprise Finance and Savings Accounts","lv":4,"p":[39,39],"pp":"66","s":"Industrial financing grew 167.5 billion yen, 32 percent through the Reconstruction Finance Bank; coal took 53 percent of its general-industry advances. The Bank of Japan absorbed 75 percent of RFB bonds; time deposits were 21 percent of savings."},{"t":"Recent Conditions of Enterprises","lv":4,"p":[40,40],"pp":"68","s":"Enterprises face money shortage and deficits from power shortages and wages averaging 1.6 times July 1947 levels. Labor productivity was only 28 to 33 percent of 1935-37; the report urges efficiency and support for small firms."},{"t":"Wage and Living Cost","lv":4,"p":[41,42],"pp":"70","s":"Average manufacturing wages rose from 964 yen (January 1947) to 3,224 yen (March 1948); government salaries reached 3,930 yen. Food took 67 percent of family spending; real wages stood near 30 percent of prewar."},{"t":"Demand and Supply of Living Necessities","lv":4,"p":[43,43],"pp":"74","s":"Staple rations were fulfilled since August 1947 and the rice quota fully delivered by mid-March. Fresh food rations reached about 70 percent of purchases by February; clothing supply is at most two pounds per capita."},{"t":"Labor Situation","lv":4,"p":[44,44],"pp":"76","s":"The 1 October 1947 census gave 33.88 million gainfully employed and 672,000 unemployed, probably underestimated. About 6.15 million essential workers received extra rations; unions numbered 36,471 with 5,993,613 members at end of November 1947."},{"t":"Construction Works","lv":4,"p":[45,46],"pp":"79","s":"Flood restoration cost far exceeded the 5 billion yen appropriated for fiscal 1947; river control needs about 150 billion yen. 1.68 million chobu await afforestation, only 145 kilometers of road were repaired, and urban housing losses equal a third of floor area."},{"t":"\"Japanese Economic Rehabilitation Plan\"","lv":3,"p":[47,59],"pp":"54","s":"The Economic Stabilization Board's first draft five-year plan, issued 17 May, aims to restore the 1930-34 living standard by 1952, with coal at 44,000,000 tons, exports of $1,646 million and 2,000,000 houses."},{"t":"Conclusion","lv":4,"p":[47,47],"pp":"82","s":"Calls 1948 a crossroads for real reconstruction toward a self-supporting economy. Stresses maximum production, curbing inflation, efficiency, a minimum living standard and mobilized domestic capital; outside aid depends on full use of Japan's own resources."},{"t":"Local Government","lv":3,"p":[59,59],"pp":"60","s":"A national conference of town and village heads opened in Tokyo 13 May, with Ashida pledging resignation if popular support was lost. A 27 May compromise settled local finance at 199,700,000,000 yen."},{"t":"The Diet","lv":3,"p":[59,60],"pp":"60","s":"Fourteen bills were introduced 25 April to 25 May and sixteen laws enacted, including Daylight Savings Time and Minor Offense. Oishi and Takahashi won the 21 May Miyagi by-election."},{"t":"Budget Action","lv":3,"p":[60,62],"pp":"62","s":"A June provisional budget of 25,800,000,000 yen passed 28 May while the Cabinet disputed rail and postal rates, a 3,731 yen wage base and local finance. It approved a 399,300,000,000 yen 1948-49 budget 30 May."},{"t":"Civil Service Institute","lv":3,"p":[62,63],"pp":"67","s":"On 14 May the Civil Service Institute graduated its first class of 354 from a 100-day course: 126 return to ministries, 226 join the National Personnel Commission. A second class runs 2 August to 1 October."},{"t":"Hoarded Goods and Illegal Transactions Investigation Committee","lv":3,"p":[63,66],"pp":"68","s":"The Diet's Illegal Transactions Investigation Committee, after 16 sessions and 45 witnesses on the Tsuji case, urged curbs on secret political funds. Four perjury indictments followed 10 and 12 May; Deputy Prime Minister Nishio was touched by construction-firm donations."},{"t":"Political Parties","lv":3,"p":[67,72],"pp":"72","s":"Democrats held their convention 4-5 May and accepted the war-bond compromise. Social Democrats moved to unify; Democratic Liberals attacked the Cabinet, with three small groups joining 25 May; Ashida proposed barring Communists from office."},{"t":"The Purge","lv":3,"p":[72,74],"pp":"85","s":"Purge committees were dissolved 10 May under Cabinet Order No. 62. Of 717,415 persons screened since 1946, 129 were reinstated 22 May. The Attorney-General's Investigation Bureau, with eight regional offices, took over enforcement."},{"t":"Section 2: Public Safety","lv":2,"p":[75,78],"pp":"139","s":"Hoarded goods recovered in April were worth 123,296,141 yen. March saw 180,850 offenses and 101,402 arrests; 36,000 new police were recruited; prisoners numbered 89,234. The Maritime Safety Board was activated 1 May."},{"t":"Section 3: Legal Affairs and War Crimes","lv":2,"p":[78,80],"pp":"145","s":"The first Impeachment Court president was elected; the Administrative Execution by Proxy Law passed 6 May. By 20 May 666 war criminals had been tried, 617 convicted including 95 sentenced to death; Okada was sentenced to hang."},{"t":"Part III: Economic","lv":1,"p":[81,183],"pp":"151","s":"Detailed economic section covering thirteen subjects: agriculture, forestry, heavy industries, manufacturing, textiles, transport, communications, labor, trade, rationing, finance, property control and science."},{"t":"Section 1: Agriculture and Fisheries","lv":2,"p":[82,85],"pp":"153","s":"Tenant land sales were 104,014 cho in April (531,334 cumulative); 11,774 agricultural cooperatives approved. March milk output was 10,185,000 liters, marine production 111,575 tons; Bonin whaling landed 243 whales."},{"t":"Section 2: Forestry and Mining","lv":2,"p":[86,92],"pp":"161","s":"April log output fell to 63,490,300 cubic feet. April coal production was 2,572,400 tons, nine percent under quota; fiscal 1947 coal was revised to 29,335,500 tons; crude oil 15,630 kiloliters."},{"t":"Section 3: Heavy Industries","lv":2,"p":[92,102],"pp":"173","s":"April coke reached 233,940 tons, a fifth consecutive postwar record; pig iron (55,072 tons) and steel ingots hit postwar highs. Cement was 169,050 tons; 10 tugs and 13 barges went to the USSR."},{"t":"Section 4: Manufacturing","lv":2,"p":[103,112],"pp":"195","s":"March flour 103,664 tons, sake 747,060 hectoliters; April paper reached a postwar high. Electrical goods were hurt by labor trouble; alarm clock and phonograph record output set records."},{"t":"Section 5: Textile Industries","lv":2,"p":[113,127],"pp":"215","s":"April set postwar highs in spun silk yarn, rayon and cotton and silk fabrics; raw silk was 10,310 bales, above the 10,000-bale goal. Raw cotton stocks fell 29,250,000 pounds."},{"t":"Section 6: Transportation and Public Utilities","lv":2,"p":[128,133],"pp":"245","s":"Registered commercial vehicles fell to 161,320, about 67 percent operable. Government railways carried 312,127,898 passengers and 9,787,700 metric tons of freight; power generation reached 3,878,000,000 kWh; gas curbs on industry extended to 1 August."},{"t":"Section 7: Communications","lv":2,"p":[134,140],"pp":"257","s":"The Cabinet approved a bill 22 May creating a Ministry of Communications. April brought 1,303,346 telephones and 6,535,531 radio households; ministry employees numbered 438,836, with a March profit of 355,966,254 yen."},{"t":"Section 8: Labor","lv":2,"p":[141,151],"pp":"271","s":"Labor relations were calmer than at any time since mid-1947; May Day rallies drew 300,000 in Tokyo. The Diet unanimously backed rejoining the ILO. Unions numbered 30,199 with 6,390,227 members."},{"t":"Section 9: Imports and Exports","lv":2,"p":[152,158],"pp":"293","s":"March imports were $40,160,265, about 77 percent from the United States; exports were $11,606,301, two thirds to Asia. Textiles were 69 percent of exports; first-quarter deficit with the United States was $133,121,288."},{"t":"Section 10: Rationing and Price Control","lv":2,"p":[158,163],"pp":"305","s":"Staple collections were negligible in May, the second month of rations from carryover stocks; 137,620 tons of imported food were authorized, including 97,620 tons of sugar. Incentive goods reached farmers and coal miners."},{"t":"Section 11: Finance","lv":2,"p":[164,170],"pp":"317","s":"Bank of Japan notes stood at 223.5 billion yen on 31 May. A 15 May financial reorganization budgeted about 18 billion yen for bank losses; RFB loans reached 64.3 billion yen; national debt was 358.3 billion yen."},{"t":"Section 12: Property Control and Reparations","lv":2,"p":[171,175],"pp":"331","s":"Looted property worth 278,781 yen was taken into custody and 737,044 yen restituted. The SCAP custody account reached 368.4 million yen; 80 control organizations were ordered liquidated; 9,967 machine tools had been shipped to reparations claimants."},{"t":"Section 13: Science and Technology","lv":2,"p":[176,183],"pp":"341","s":"Twenty-one observation groups studied the 9 May annular eclipse, mainly at Rebun Island. Japan Academy prizes went to Koga, Fujiwara, Kotani and Tomonaga; 2,353 standards were in effect and 346 patents granted in April."},{"t":"Part IV: Social","lv":1,"p":[183,206],"pp":"355","s":"Detailed social section: public health and welfare, and education, religion, cultural resources and media of expression."},{"t":"Section 1: Public Health and Welfare","lv":2,"p":[184,196],"pp":"357","s":"April recorded 3,237 communicable disease cases with no cholera; 123,511 orphans were counted. Unemployment insurance replaced allowances 1 May; population reached 79,690,953 and 33,635 persons were repatriated."},{"t":"Section 2: Education, Religion, Cultural Resources and Media of Expression","lv":2,"p":[197,206],"pp":"383","s":"Ministry of Education announced a Korean schools policy 7 May and 107 million authorized textbooks. The National Christian Council was organized 17 May; 379 Meiji historic sites were declassified; three new SCAP libraries opened."}],"by":"claude-sonnet (Claude Code subagent)"},{"id":"K-32","ia":"south-korean-interim-government-activities-no-32-1948-05","s":"K","no":32,"d":"1948-05","d2":"","t":"South Korean Interim Government Activities, No. 32 (May 1948)","v":"","pg":199,"o":"Report No. 32 covers May 1948, prepared by the National Economic Board. Its Summary calls the month historic: the 10 May general election (over 91 per cent of registrants voting despite communist terror) and the 31 May opening of the National Assembly, which elected Syngman Rhee Speaker. North Korea cut electricity to the South on 14 May, discrediting Kim Koo and Kimm Kiu-sic after the Pyongyang conference and cutting industrial output sharply. The text also covers Cheju-do guerrilla fighting, 38th Parallel incidents, Mukden repatriation, vested property sales, farmland transfers, drought, finance, public works, police, education, culture and youth/women's groups. Caution for researchers: the scan is misbound. PDF pages 47-113 and 134-137 are pages of a July 1947 report (Legislative Assembly debates, constitution draft, Joint Commission, police and prison tables), replacing May 1948 material on industry, mining, allocations, trade, labor, transportation, communications and loans.","h":["10 May 1948: first general election, with over 91 per cent of registrants voting despite communist terror; 198 Assembly members were elected, and Cheju-do's Puk Cheju A and B districts were postponed.","31 May: the National Assembly opened with all 198 members, and Syngman Rhee was elected Speaker with 188 votes.","14 May: North Korea cut the electric power that supplied about 60 per cent of South Korea's electricity; national distribution fell from 74.5 to 56.1 million KWH and overall production fell sharply.","Kim Koo and Kimm Kiu-sic lost political standing after their Pyongyang assurance that power would continue proved false.","By 31 May, 448,513 of 587,974 farms (76 per cent) of former Japanese farmland had been sold or transferred to Koreans through the National Land Administration.","The first Mukden repatriates reached Inchon by LST from Tientsin on 10-11 May; 3,831 had been repatriated by month's end and 2,651 were still awaiting passage.","Election violence left a press tally of 202 dead and 643 wounded, and the Cheju-do rebellion intensified, with 3,126 persons held; police reported 68 of 13,407 polling booths attacked.","Ordinance No. 192 separated the courts from the Department of Justice effective 1 June 1948; the scan is also misbound, with July 1947 pages replacing May 1948 industry, labor and transport sections."],"k":["May 1948 election","National Assembly","Syngman Rhee","power shut-off","Pyongyang conference","Kim Koo","Cheju-do","vested property","National Land Administration","Mukden repatriates","National Police","public works","Korean National Youth","fertilizer","National Economic Board"],"toc":[{"t":"Front matter","lv":1,"p":[1,7],"pp":"","s":"Cover (No. 32, May 1948, National Economic Board), blank page, list of Board members at 31 May 1948, and four-page table of contents."},{"t":"Summary","lv":1,"p":[8,11],"pp":"1","s":"Overview of May: the 10 May election, the 14 May power cutoff and falling output, drought, fertilizer, population growth, border and Cheju-do violence, fear of Japan, public works, vested property, trade and finance."},{"t":"Part I: Basic Aspects","lv":1,"p":[12,27],"pp":"5","s":"Population and migration (20.0 million by January 1948), Mukden repatriation, draft immigration ordinance, and Office of Property Custody preparations for Koreanization of vested property."},{"t":"Section 1: Population and Foreign Affairs","lv":2,"p":[12,21],"pp":"5","s":"South Korea's population rose one-third since 1940 to 20.0 million by January 1948; 2.4 million entered against 886,000 leaving. Covers Mukden repatriates, Korean schools in Japan and a draft immigration ordinance."},{"t":"Census Report","lv":3,"p":[12,13],"pp":"5","s":"Compares 1940, 1944, 1946 and 1948 counts; natural increase revised to 1.8 per cent; urban population up 72.7 per cent since 1940."},{"t":"Population Migration","lv":3,"p":[14,17],"pp":"7","s":"Table by province and cumulative movement tables; refugee arrivals from the North fell about 20 per cent from April to May (12,391)."},{"t":"Manchurian Repatriates","lv":3,"p":[18,18],"pp":"11","s":"First Mukden repatriates reached Inchon by LST from Tientsin on 11 May; 3,831 repatriated by month's end, 2,651 still awaiting; LARA aid."},{"t":"Korean Education in Japan","lv":3,"p":[19,19],"pp":"12","s":"Dispute between the Korean League and the Japanese government over Korean schools; CI&E proposals on textbooks and teachers; Osaka and Kobe disturbances halted talks."},{"t":"Repatriation of Korean Businessmen from Japan","lv":3,"p":[20,20],"pp":"13","s":"Korean businessmen in Japan increasingly applied to return; the 1,000-won allowance and property-shipment limits had slowed repatriation."},{"t":"Immigration and Emigration Regulations","lv":3,"p":[20,21],"pp":"13","s":"Digest of a new ordinance replacing Ordinance No. 49 (due about 1 August 1948): entry documents, Home Affairs Bureaus as agents, definition of Korean citizen, exempt aliens."},{"t":"Section 2: Vested Property","lv":2,"p":[22,27],"pp":"15","s":"Office of Property Custody prepared for Koreanization: a record 2,334 financial statements, reorganization studies, rising sales applications after the election, rent collection. Page 27 is garbled OCR."},{"t":"Operations","lv":3,"p":[22,23],"pp":"15","s":"Took over the Korean Residence Management Corporation on 6 May; investigated 1,841 companies for reorganization, 484 unlocated; loan guarantees status reviewed."},{"t":"Property Disposal","lv":3,"p":[24,24],"pp":"17","s":"Post-election rise in purchase applications (497 cases in May); tables of appraisals and sales, with May total 111 million won."},{"t":"Real Estate Section","lv":3,"p":[25,25],"pp":"18","s":"63 dependent houses and 11 Army parcels released; condition surveys arranged with Camp Seoul."},{"t":"Comptroller Division","lv":3,"p":[25,25],"pp":"18","s":"Training Korean auditors, handbook, bilingual reports, new tax forms, Budget Section and bank-deposit reconciliation."},{"t":"Rental Properties","lv":3,"p":[26,26],"pp":"19","s":"Full lease report as of 31 December 1947; table of new leases and rents collected January-April 1948 (2,252 new leases in April)."},{"t":"Property of Allied Nationals","lv":3,"p":[26,26],"pp":"19","s":"Japanese Custodian records checked for Allied-owned property; US-owned cases go to the State Department or to claims against Japan."},{"t":"Executive Division","lv":3,"p":[26,27],"pp":"19","s":"Collected synopses and narrative reports from all SKIG agencies toward a general inventory of vested property."},{"t":"Part II: Production and Supply","lv":1,"p":[28,46],"pp":"21","s":"Agriculture, forestry, fisheries and prices survive; drought, 448,513 farms transferred, reforestation collapse and May price rises. Later sections are missing from the scan."},{"t":"Section 1: Agriculture, Forestry and Fisheries","lv":2,"p":[28,38],"pp":"21","s":"Drought threatens summer grain on a forecast 1,956,227 acres; fertilizer receipts; reforestation collapse since 1944; fishery exports to Japan; 448,513 farms sold by NLA."},{"t":"General","lv":3,"p":[28,28],"pp":"21","s":"Insufficient spring rainfall likely to cut summer grain yields; final estimates redone in May."},{"t":"Bureau of Agricultural Economics","lv":3,"p":[28,28],"pp":"21","s":"5 May summer-grain forecast tables 1930-1948; 1,847 Crop Reporting Service staff appointed, 37 resignations over pay."},{"t":"Bureau of Agricultural Production","lv":3,"p":[29,30],"pp":"22","s":"Livestock, poultry feed, fertilizer allocations, cotton stocks of 7,578 tons, sericulture, power-pump irrigation (94,153 acres), sweet and Irish potatoes."},{"t":"Bureau of Forestry","lv":3,"p":[31,32],"pp":"24","s":"16 million acres of forestland; annual cut exceeds growth; reforestation fell from 358 million trees (1942) to 61.6 million (1947); charcoal, fuelwood and log tables."},{"t":"Bureau of Fisheries","lv":3,"p":[33,34],"pp":"26","s":"Exports to Japan Sept 1947-May 1948 worth $4.9 million (laver, anchovies, seaweed); landings expected near pre-war levels after fleet reorganization."},{"t":"Korea Agricultural Association","lv":3,"p":[35,36],"pp":"28","s":"Fertilizer receipts and sales (156,444 and 106,717 tons in 1948), US imports replacing Japan, 10,000 tons ammonium sulphate from the North by barter; straw bag and rope purchases."},{"t":"National Land Administration","lv":3,"p":[37,37],"pp":"30","s":"448,513 of 587,974 farms sold or transferred by 31 May (76 per cent), with table by regional office; surveys ordered for deeds."},{"t":"Agricultural Improvement Service","lv":3,"p":[38,38],"pp":"31","s":"Rice experiment projects seeded; Pusan Fisheries College placed under AIS for administration."},{"t":"Section 2: Prices","lv":2,"p":[39,46],"pp":"32","s":"Wholesale and retail price indexes; Seoul rice up nearly 10 per cent in May; controlled-price tables and price and production trend chart. Page 45 is garbled OCR."},{"t":"Wholesale and Retail Prices","lv":3,"p":[39,42],"pp":"32","s":"Indexes (August 1945 = 100) for 27 wholesale and 26 retail commodities to May 1948; grain shortage lifted food prices."},{"t":"Controlled Prices","lv":3,"p":[43,46],"pp":"37","s":"National Price Administration prices of controlled domestic goods; nine-city open-market retail prices; price and production trend charts."},{"t":"Part III: Public Services","lv":1,"p":[114,123],"pp":"91","s":"Public utilities and public works survive: power fell after the 14 May cutoff, streetcars and buses, bridges, levees and fires. Transportation and Communications are missing from the scan."},{"t":"Section 3: Public Utilities","lv":2,"p":[114,117],"pp":"107","s":"May power distribution fell to 56.1 million KWH from 74.5 million after the 14 May cutoff; emergency board, bulb substitution and 24-hour monitoring."},{"t":"Electricity Distribution","lv":3,"p":[114,114],"pp":"107","s":"South Korea produced 41.5 million KWH, plus 15.1 million from the North before the cutoff; production up 8.5 million over April."},{"t":"Emergency Power Board Transactions","lv":3,"p":[114,115],"pp":"107","s":"Board met 12, 19 and 24 May; emergency plans worked; 30-watt bulbs replaced 60-watt, saving 28,000 KW; new priorities set."},{"t":"Power Plants","lv":3,"p":[115,116],"pp":"108","s":"Table of supply by source; record output at Sum Jin Gang; Yongwol coal draw; power barges at Pusan and Inchon; national load dispatcher."},{"t":"Street Transit Systems","lv":3,"p":[117,117],"pp":"110","s":"Seoul streetcars stayed at 136, out only 45 minutes on 14 May; buses rose from 21 to 31."},{"t":"Section 4: Public Works","lv":2,"p":[118,123],"pp":"111","s":"Two-and-a-half-year stocktaking of roads, bridges, levees, water supply, surveys and fire service; bridge tables, flood-control subsidies and 181 fires in May."},{"t":"General","lv":3,"p":[118,119],"pp":"111","s":"Seoul-Pusan Highway rebuilt with 37 bridges; 162 km of levees; 27 water plants; 2,750 map plates recovered from Japan; fire service reorganized."},{"t":"Roads and Bridges","lv":3,"p":[120,121],"pp":"113","s":"Volunteer road-maintenance contest won by Cholla-namdo; map of roads and bridges May 1948; progress table of major bridges."},{"t":"Irrigation and Flood Control","lv":3,"p":[122,122],"pp":"115","s":"153 million won in flood-control subsidies; Ilsan levee and Kaesong road levee on the Han completed."},{"t":"Urban Water Supply","lv":3,"p":[122,122],"pp":"115","s":"Power cutoff and drought hurt water pumping at Inchon and elsewhere; emergency measures taken."},{"t":"Fire Control","lv":3,"p":[123,123],"pp":"","s":"181 fires in May, 37 by arson, with loss of 251.7 million won; industrial fire survey; extinguishing supplies received."},{"t":"Part IV: Finance","lv":1,"p":[124,139],"pp":"117","s":"Government finance (note issue, 20,232 million won overdraft, budgets) and non-government finance (deposits, insurance, loans), with four misbound July 1947 pages."},{"t":"Section 1: Government Finance","lv":2,"p":[124,129],"pp":"117","s":"Note issue rose 469 million won in May; SKIG overdraft 20,232 million won; F/Y 1948/49 budgets approaching 29 billion won; revenue-to-expenditure ratio 73 per cent."},{"t":"Note Issue of the Bank of Chosun","lv":3,"p":[124,124],"pp":"117","s":"Note issue reached 33,389 million won by end-December 1947, driven by the rice collection program."},{"t":"Overdraft to South Korean Interim Government","lv":3,"p":[124,125],"pp":"117","s":"Cumulative excess withdrawals 20,232 million won at end of May; tables of note issue and Bank of Chosun deposits and withdrawals."},{"t":"Expenditure Budget of the South Korean Interim Government","lv":3,"p":[126,126],"pp":"119","s":"F/Y 1947/48 authorizations 22,320 million won; table of F/Y 1948/49 agency budgets approved by 31 May."},{"t":"Monthly Revenues and Expenditures","lv":3,"p":[126,126],"pp":"119","s":"April revenue 1,930 million won, tobacco sales 702 million; expenditures 119 million above March."},{"t":"Ratios Between Revenues and Expenditures","lv":3,"p":[127,127],"pp":"120","s":"Revenue was 73 per cent of expenditure paid and 69 per cent of the expenditure budget in F/Y 1947/48; monthly table."},{"t":"Civilian Supply Program","lv":3,"p":[128,129],"pp":"121","s":"Assets summary of imported civilian supply goods; table of departmental expenditures for F/Y 1947/48."},{"t":"Section 2: Non-Government Finance","lv":2,"p":[130,139],"pp":"123","s":"April data: cash balances down 1,080 million won, deposit balances at a record 56,421 million, insurance and loans. Pages 134-137 are misbound July 1947 police and prison tables."},{"t":"Cash Balances in Financial Institutions","lv":3,"p":[130,130],"pp":"123","s":"Cash balances fell 1,080 million won in April to about the February level."},{"t":"Deposits in Financial Institutions","lv":3,"p":[131,132],"pp":"124","s":"Deposits up 11 per cent and balances up over 2 per cent; 55 per cent government accounts, 40 per cent public; deposit and withdrawal chart."},{"t":"Deposit Balance of Each Financial Institution","lv":3,"p":[133,133],"pp":"126","s":"Inter-bank and general public deposit tables; Bank of Chosun balances up from government deposits."},{"t":"Damage Insurance and Life and Damage Insurance Activities","lv":3,"p":[138,138],"pp":"131","s":"Life insurance table; 8,294 damage policies written in April; collateral insurance instructions and past-due agency balances."},{"t":"Activities of Financial Institutions","lv":3,"p":[139,139],"pp":"132","s":"Damage insurance table; Hyup Dong Life wins sales trophy; 95 loans totaling 1,839 million won approved, including 900 million for filatures; claims against Japan."},{"t":"Part V: Government and Politics","lv":1,"p":[140,171],"pp":"133","s":"Administration, the 10 May election and National Assembly, legal affairs, National Police (Cheju-do, election-day order), and interzone activities including 31 border incidents."},{"t":"Section 1: Administration","lv":2,"p":[140,143],"pp":"133","s":"Chief Adviser Johnson's Washington mission; NEB actions; civil service convention and 25-130 per cent pay rise from 1 June; Military Government personnel table."},{"t":"Chief Adviser's Mission","lv":3,"p":[140,140],"pp":"133","s":"Dr. Edgar Johnson in Washington 29 March-25 May: budget, supplies, post-transfer legislation, Foreign Liquidation Commission, power shortage."},{"t":"National Economic Board","lv":3,"p":[141,141],"pp":"134","s":"Four weekly meetings in May and a list of items acted on, including daylight saving time from 31 May and telephone rates."},{"t":"Korean Civil Service","lv":3,"p":[142,142],"pp":"135","s":"Four-day provincial officers' convention from 16 May; warning about abuse of eligible lists; general pay raise averaging 50 per cent."},{"t":"Military Government Personnel","lv":3,"p":[142,143],"pp":"135","s":"Table of personnel October 1946-May 1948; civilian turnover high due to one-year contracts."},{"t":"Section 2: Political Developments","lv":2,"p":[144,155],"pp":"137","s":"The 10 May election and 198-member Assembly (tables by province, party, occupation), Rhee's statements, 31 May opening, North-South Conference, the 14 May power shut-off and Koreans in Japan."},{"t":"General","lv":3,"p":[144,144],"pp":"137","s":"Over 91 per cent of registrants voted; nearly half the winners avowed rightists; the power cutoff angered the public."},{"t":"General Election","lv":3,"p":[144,150],"pp":"137","s":"Results by province with names, ages and parties of the 198 members; party and occupation tables; reactions of rightist and moderate parties and Kim Koo; average age 47."},{"t":"National Assembly","lv":3,"p":[151,152],"pp":"144","s":"Rhee's statements on the government; preliminary meeting 27 May; 31 May opening, Rhee elected Speaker with 188 votes, formal ceremony and his speech."},{"t":"North-South Conference","lv":3,"p":[153,153],"pp":"146","s":"Hodge and Dean speeches at the opening; Kimm and Kim Koo's return from Pyongyang on 5 May, their joint statement and rightist disapproval."},{"t":"Power Shut-off","lv":3,"p":[154,154],"pp":"147","s":"Cutoff of 14 May after Kim and Kimm said power would continue; Kim Koo denies knowing the contract; rightists call the promise false."},{"t":"Koreans in Japan","lv":3,"p":[155,155],"pp":"148","s":"South Korean parties protested for months over Japan's treatment of Koreans, claiming Japanese imperialism was being revived; closure of Korean schools in Japan was seen as an infringement of Korean rights."},{"t":"Section 3: Legal Affairs","lv":2,"p":[156,160],"pp":"149","s":"Ordinances Nos. 192-197 (court reorganization first), daylight saving proclamation, four legal opinions on vested property, prisons (22,141 inmates), courts and Cheju-do trial team."},{"t":"Ordinances and Orders","lv":3,"p":[156,156],"pp":"149","s":"Ordinance 192 Court Reorganization through Ordinance 197 (Coast Guard searches); daylight saving from 31 May; proclamation on the National Assembly; summer grain collection."},{"t":"Legal Opinions","lv":3,"p":[157,157],"pp":"150","s":"Opinions on SKIG claims for vested property damage, the Chosun Aircraft board election, Seoul city property powers, and the free silk market."},{"t":"Penal Institutions","lv":3,"p":[158,158],"pp":"151","s":"Guard target practice, prison labor for constabulary uniforms and raincoats, prison population 22,141 at 30 April, sports equipment."},{"t":"Korean Courts","lv":3,"p":[159,159],"pp":"152","s":"Court team sent to Cheju-do; NLA suits; courts separated from the Department of Justice effective 1 June."},{"t":"Codification Bureau","lv":3,"p":[160,160],"pp":"153","s":"Drafting of the Air Carriers section of the proposed Korean Civil Code was completed in May, with articles on definitions, rights, jurisdiction, liability and operation."},{"t":"Property Claims Commission","lv":3,"p":[160,160],"pp":"153","s":"Simplified procedure directive of 17 April 1948 for vested property claims."},{"t":"Section 4: National Police","lv":2,"p":[161,166],"pp":"154","s":"Police largely kept order on election day (68 of 13,407 booths attacked) but not on Cheju-do; also decentralization, police finances and Pusan police."},{"t":"General and National Election","lv":3,"p":[161,162],"pp":"154","s":"14 labor and nine school strikes, rail and power-line sabotage, four candidates killed, attacks on police and towns, plus propaganda."},{"t":"Cheju-do","lv":3,"p":[163,163],"pp":"156","s":"Fighting intensified despite appeals; 3,126 persons held; voting postponed in two districts; reasons for the election's success."},{"t":"Provincial Police","lv":3,"p":[164,164],"pp":"157","s":"Decentralization aims, provincial chiefs and governors, and unofficial finances through the Police Protective Association."},{"t":"Pusan Police","lv":3,"p":[165,166],"pp":"158","s":"Profile of Kyongsang-namdo police chief Pak Myun-jae and adviser Hackett; stations, property, cooperative stores, discipline."},{"t":"Section 5: Interzone Activities","lv":2,"p":[167,171],"pp":"160","s":"31 border incidents, over 43,000 pieces of mail exchanged, and Radio Pyongyang propaganda on the North constitution, election and power cutoff. Page 171 is garbled OCR."},{"t":"Border Incidents","lv":3,"p":[167,167],"pp":"160","s":"Nightly small-arms fire; dam fight near Oe-Dong; a Soviet Yak-9 landed near Yosu on 19 May."},{"t":"Mail Exchange","lv":3,"p":[167,167],"pp":"160","s":"South sent 28,810 pieces of mail to the North and received 14,707."},{"t":"Radio Pyongyang Intercepts","lv":3,"p":[168,171],"pp":"161","s":"North Korean constitution of 28 April, attacks on the South election, Soviet-line commentary and the radio campaign after the power cutoff."},{"t":"Part VI: Social","lv":1,"p":[172,199],"pp":"165","s":"Health and welfare, education (845 teachers in summer training), culture, press, Korean National Youth (859,208 members) and women's activities."},{"t":"Section 1: Public Health and Welfare","lv":2,"p":[172,175],"pp":"165","s":"Mukden refugees arriving by ship, smallpox cases, low-cost housing in Kyongsang-namdo, UNRRA grants to child welfare institutions, nursing, veterinary, pharmaceutical, narcotics and laboratory items."},{"t":"Preventive Medicine","lv":3,"p":[172,172],"pp":"165","s":"Smallpox among Cheju constabulary; first Mukden refugee ship (1,091) and later shipments with measles cases."},{"t":"Public Assistance","lv":3,"p":[173,173],"pp":"167","s":"Relief staff devoted to Mukden refugees; 2,106 housing units built in Kyongsang-namdo; UNRRA grants to orphanages and boys' schools."},{"t":"Medical Services","lv":3,"p":[174,174],"pp":"167","s":"38 doctors examined for Army hospital internships; a leper settlement of 100 living in caves visited."},{"t":"Nursing Affairs","lv":3,"p":[174,174],"pp":"168","s":"Qualification exams for nurses and midwives, three-month training course, Nurses' Association meeting."},{"t":"Section 2: Education","lv":2,"p":[176,182],"pp":"169","s":"Three ordinances drafted for school districts and Boards of Education financed by family income tax; teacher certification, summer training for 845 teachers, textbooks and Korean typewriter."},{"t":"Bureau of Administration","lv":3,"p":[176,176],"pp":"169","s":"F/Y 1948/49 budget 1.5 billion won; three ordinances on school districts, boards and school financing."},{"t":"Bureau of Common Schools","lv":3,"p":[177,177],"pp":"170","s":"Curriculum workshops, civics book for middle schools; special education clinics for handicapped children."},{"t":"Bureau of Teachers' Training","lv":3,"p":[178,178],"pp":"171","s":"Unified certification standards effective 15 June; summer sessions; Teacher Training Center committees."},{"t":"Bureau of Higher Education","lv":3,"p":[179,179],"pp":"171","s":"Nursing education, 23 Severance graduates, first postwar nursing textbook, Seoul National University Hospital rehabilitation board, veterinary hospital."},{"t":"American Language Institute","lv":3,"p":[180,180],"pp":"174","s":"26 English teachers and 15 interpreters; fees introduced after the budget was eliminated; 13 of 32 students qualified for US study."},{"t":"Bureau of Vocational and Technical Education","lv":3,"p":[180,180],"pp":"174","s":"New bureau budget 2,000,000 won; Korean typewriter demonstration, typist training and accounting course."},{"t":"Bureau of Adult and Social Education","lv":3,"p":[181,181],"pp":"173","s":"Plans for a national dramatic contest and an Adult Education Association conference."},{"t":"Bureau of Textbooks","lv":3,"p":[181,181],"pp":"173","s":"Nearly 14,000,000 textbooks published from August 1945 to May 1948."},{"t":"Bureau of Research","lv":3,"p":[181,181],"pp":"173","s":"Manual on objective tests; primary school achievement tests approved 14 May."},{"t":"Provincial Education","lv":3,"p":[182,182],"pp":"175","s":"Home economics association meeting; 200 Kyonggi-do teachers visit an American dependent school."},{"t":"Section 3: Culture","lv":2,"p":[183,186],"pp":"176","s":"Election campaign teams gave 803 performances to 1.33 million; music festival and orchestra scholarships; US information centers; Rhee portrait exhibition; election stamps; KBS election coverage."},{"t":"Election Campaign Teams","lv":3,"p":[183,183],"pp":"176","s":"Stage Arts Association teams toured from 7 April: 803 performances, 1,334,464 attendees, plays and songs on voting."},{"t":"Music","lv":3,"p":[184,184],"pp":"177","s":"Musical Festival 15-17 May; Seoul Philharmonic Beethoven concerts; Korea Symphony filmed; scholarships for three musicians."},{"t":"Information Centers and Art Exhibits","lv":3,"p":[185,185],"pp":"178","s":"US Information Center in Seoul opened 29 April, Kaesong center 6 May; about 100,000 viewed a Rhee Syngman exhibition."},{"t":"Other Cultural Activities","lv":3,"p":[186,186],"pp":"179","s":"Children's Day ceremony 5 May; election commemorative stamps issued 10 May."},{"t":"Korea Broadcasting System","lv":3,"p":[186,186],"pp":"179","s":"Election publicity campaign, shortwave relay on election day, BBC-style procedures, Olympic and Assembly coverage plans."},{"t":"Section 4: Press and Public Opinion","lv":2,"p":[187,191],"pp":"180","s":"Press fights over the Pyongyang conference, election disorder tallies (202 dead), Rhee's 24 May press conference, power cutoff blame and a resurgent fear of Japan."},{"t":"Pyongyang Conference","lv":3,"p":[187,187],"pp":"180","s":"Press battle over the conference; alleged promises to the two Kims that power would continue; one promise broken."},{"t":"Election","lv":3,"p":[188,189],"pp":"181","s":"Precautions, UN Commission trips, casualty tallies, congratulations, Country Guard Corps criticism, Rhee's press conference."},{"t":"Electric Power","lv":3,"p":[190,190],"pp":"183","s":"Press explanations of the North Korean power cutoff followed political lines: rightists blamed anger at the election, leftists blamed non-payment, and U.S. authorities refused to deal with the North Korean People's Committee."},{"t":"Fear of Japan","lv":3,"p":[190,191],"pp":"183","s":"Closure of Korean schools in Japan and rumors of Japanese officials returning provoke denunciations; press objects to UN report in Tokyo."},{"t":"Section 5: Korean National Youth, Inc.","lv":2,"p":[192,194],"pp":"185","s":"33 KNY members elected to the Assembly; membership 859,208 at end May; reserve leader training; F/Y 1948/49 budget 20.6 million won; community service projects."},{"t":"Election Results","lv":3,"p":[192,192],"pp":"185","s":"33 members elected as private citizens; KNY judged the strongest of ten dozen youth groups; two-month turnout drive."},{"t":"Membership and Training","lv":3,"p":[192,193],"pp":"185","s":"Membership 859,208, with screening to weed out extremists; tables by month and by province; 65 training centers."},{"t":"Operations","lv":3,"p":[194,194],"pp":"187","s":"Budget of 20,600,000 won; discussion groups, anti-illiteracy classes, road repair, scouting, clean-up, reforestation."},{"t":"Section 6: Women's Activities","lv":2,"p":[195,199],"pp":"188","s":"About 3,000,000 women voted; 19 women candidates, none elected; Women's Bureau, volunteer service, Junior Red Cross, YWCA fashion show and Ewha University's 62nd anniversary."},{"t":"General Election","lv":3,"p":[195,195],"pp":"188","s":"Women voted 3,000,000 strong; 19 of 594 candidates were women, none elected; table of registered women voters."},{"t":"Women's Bureau","lv":3,"p":[196,196],"pp":"189","s":"Two members on the National Election Committee; Mothers' School certificates; Korean group accepted by US women's clubs federation."},{"t":"Women's Special Volunteer Service","lv":3,"p":[196,196],"pp":"189","s":"Over 1,200 volunteer women daily on welfare and community projects as of 30 May."},{"t":"Junior Red Cross","lv":3,"p":[196,196],"pp":"189","s":"Gift boxes to schools and orphanages, 4,714 members, planned publications and training."},{"t":"The YWCA","lv":3,"p":[197,197],"pp":"190","s":"American-Korean fashion show on 15 May drew over 500 spectators and 33 models."},{"t":"Ewha Women's University","lv":3,"p":[197,199],"pp":"190","s":"62nd anniversary; history since 1886 under Dr. Helen Kim; dormitories; list of colleges and departments."}],"by":"claude-sonnet (Claude Code subagent)","emb":["K-22-in-K-32"]},{"id":"K-32-in-K-30","ia":"south-korean-interim-government-activities-no-30-1948-03","s":"K","no":32,"d":"1948-05","d2":"","t":"South Korean Interim Government Activities, No. 32 (May 1948)","v":"Bound in K-30","host":"K-30","rg":[[450,655]],"kind":"bound","found":"Title page '105.13:32' on PDF p. 450","pg":206,"o":"This issue of South Korean Interim Government Activities (No. 32, prepared by the National Economic Board) covers May 1948, a month dominated by the 10 May general election, the first in Korean history, with about 91 percent of registrants voting despite communist terror. Nearly half of the 198 elected Assemblymen were rightists, Rhee Syngman became Assembly chairman, and the Assembly convened on 31 May. North Korea's 14 May electricity cutoff discredited Kim Koo and Kimm Kiu-sic after the Pyongyang conference and cut industrial output about 75 percent, while drought threatened summer grain. The report also covers the Cheju rebellion, border incidents, sabotage, Mukden repatriates, vested property Koreanization, court reorganization, education ordinances and women's participation. Detailed tables on prices, coal, trade, petroleum, transport, power, finance and insurance give historians a month-by-month statistical baseline for the last weeks of the Interim Government.","h":["The 10 May 1948 election, the first in Korean history, drew more than 91 percent of registrants despite communist terror; of 198 Assemblymen, nearly half were rightists and none communist.","The National Assembly convened on 31 May with all 198 members present, and Rhee Syngman won the speakership with 188 votes; Hodge and Dean spoke at the opening.","North Korea cut electricity to the South on 14 May; power distribution fell to 56.1 million KWH from 74.5 million, and overall industrial production dropped about 75 percent by month end.","The power cutoff discredited Kim Koo and Kimm Kiu-sic, who had reported after the 19-25 April Pyongyang conference that the North promised no separate government and continued electricity.","Election violence killed four candidates, 15 election officials and 32 policemen; elections in two Cheju districts were voided as fighting on the island intensified, with 3,126 held.","Ordinance No. 192 separated the courts from the Department of Justice from 1 June, and three education ordinances were drafted for school districts and Boards of Education.","The first Mukden repatriates reached Inchon on 11 May, about 3,800 people in four ships; refugees from the north fell about 20 per cent from April.","Thirty-one border incidents with the North Korean Constabulary occurred, and communist saboteurs burned the Inchon telephone exchange and the Yongdungpo repeater station, killing two employees and a policeman."],"k":["South Korean Interim Government","National Economic Board","general election 1948","National Assembly","power shut-off","Pyongyang Conference","Cheju-do","Rhee Syngman","vested property","Mukden repatriates","private trade Hong Kong","coal production","Bank of Chosun","National Police","Korean National Youth"],"toc":[{"t":"Front matter","lv":1,"p":[450,457],"pp":"","s":"Cover of No. 32 (May 1948), prepared by the National Economic Board; Board members at 31 May 1948 (Chairman Wilhelm Anderson, Executive Secretary Otis T. Weaver) and a six-part table of contents."},{"t":"Summary","lv":1,"p":[458,461],"pp":"1","s":"May 1948 review: the 10 May election drew 91 per cent of registrants despite communist terror, and the Assembly sat 31 May. North Korea's 14 May power cutoff cut production about 75 per cent; drought, Cheju rebellion and a 469,000,000 won note issue also noted."},{"t":"Part I. Basic Aspects","lv":1,"p":[462,477],"pp":"5","s":"Population and foreign affairs (about 20,041,000 people, first Mukden repatriates at Inchon) and vested property, where the Office of Property Custody prepared Koreanization and recorded 2,334 financial statements."},{"t":"Section 1. Population and Foreign Affairs","lv":2,"p":[462,471],"pp":"5","s":"South Korea's population reached about 20,041,000 by January 1948, with net migration of roughly 1,534,000. The first Mukden repatriates reached Inchon on 11 May (3,831 by month end); a new immigration ordinance was expected about 1 August."},{"t":"Section 2. Vested Property","lv":2,"p":[472,477],"pp":"15","s":"The Office of Property Custody prepared Koreanization by decentralizing and training auditors. A record 2,334 financial statements arrived, the Korean Residence Management Corporation was taken over on 6 May, and sales totaled 111,225 thousand won."},{"t":"Part II. Production and Supply","lv":1,"p":[478,547],"pp":"21","s":"Production and supply: summer grain forecast of 673,982 tons, May wholesale price rises, a 75 per cent production fall after the 14 May power cutoff, anthracite output of 68,612 tons in April, trade licenses and Seoul and Pusan labor disputes."},{"t":"Section 1. Agriculture, Forestry and Fisheries","lv":2,"p":[478,488],"pp":"21","s":"Summer grain forecast of 673,982 metric tons on 1,956,227 acres (104.1 per cent of 1947) was threatened by drought; 94,153 acres of rice land depended on power pumps. Fisheries exports to Japan were worth $4,936,162."},{"t":"Section 2. Prices","lv":2,"p":[489,496],"pp":"32","s":"Seoul wholesale rice rose nearly 10 per cent to about the January level, with barley, red beans, metals and raw silk also higher. Sugar, salt and dried fish fell. Nine-city rice averaged 734 won by 25 May."},{"t":"Section 3. Industrial Production","lv":2,"p":[497,507],"pp":"40","s":"The 14 May North Korean power cutoff cut overall production about 75 per cent by month end, after a strong April (2,261,906 pairs of rubber shoes from 154 plants). Paper, cement and cotton goods fell; salt rose to 28,056 tons."},{"t":"Section 4. Mining and Minerals","lv":2,"p":[508,518],"pp":"51","s":"Anthracite output reached 68,612 metric tons in April, up 9.3 per cent, before the May power cutoff flooded the Ongjin and Haeju mines and Yong Wol fell to 6,639 tons. Bituminous imports from Japan dropped to 61,068 tons."},{"t":"Section 5. Allocations and Distribution","lv":2,"p":[519,528],"pp":"62","s":"The Consumer Goods Allocation Board dealt with delayed cotton cloth deliveries. May petroleum allocations included 6,936,921 liters of gasoline for elections, and salt distribution reached 27,434 tons."},{"t":"Section 6. Imports and Exports","lv":2,"p":[529,541],"pp":"72","s":"May licenses (95 import, 83 export) pointed to exports near 548 million won against imports near 323 million. April private trade: imports 746 million won, exports 391 million, 99 percent of exports to Hong Kong."},{"t":"Section 7. Labor","lv":2,"p":[542,547],"pp":"85","s":"At Seoul Electric, a communist-inspired strike was denied and a 29 May conference followed Maj.-Gen. Dean's fact-finding report. At Pusan, 450 stevedores stopped work on 11-12 May and a joint hiring hall replaced the honcho system."},{"t":"Part III. Public Services","lv":1,"p":[548,573],"pp":"91","s":"Transportation, communications, utilities and public works: election sabotage on rail and telephone lines, a power fall from 74.5 to 56.1 million KWH after the 14 May shut-off, and a stocktaking of Public Works achievements."},{"t":"Section 1. Transportation","lv":2,"p":[548,556],"pp":"91","s":"Communist sabotage around the 10 May election wrecked train No. 36 and damaged locomotives. April Transportation revenue fell 28 percent to 374.6 million won; coal received top marine shipping priority at 42.9 percent."},{"t":"Section 2. Communications","lv":2,"p":[557,563],"pp":"100","s":"Saboteurs burned the Inchon telephone exchange (about 7 million won) and arsonists destroyed the Yongdungpo repeater station on 23 May, killing two employees and a policeman. April revenue fell 30.6 percent; rates rose at month end."},{"t":"Section 3. Public Utilities","lv":2,"p":[564,567],"pp":"107","s":"May power distribution fell to 56.1 million KWH from 74.5 million in April; the North delivered 15.1 million before the 14 May shut-off. Daylight Saving Time began 31 May and US monitoring of dispatch 26 May."},{"t":"Section 4. Public Works","lv":2,"p":[568,573],"pp":"111","s":"Public Works was credited with rebuilding the Seoul-Pusan Highway (37 bridges, 497 km, about 236.8 million won), 162 km of levees and 515 pieces of heavy equipment. May brought 153 million won in flood subsidies and 181 fires."},{"t":"Part IV. Finance","lv":1,"p":[574,589],"pp":"117","s":"Government finance (note issue, overdraft, budgets and April revenue) and non-government finance (banks, loans, deposits and insurance), with the note issue up 469 million won in May."},{"t":"Section 1. Government Finance","lv":2,"p":[574,579],"pp":"117","s":"Bank of Chosun note issue peaked at 33,389 million won in December 1947, rising 469 million in May. The overdraft stood at 20,232 million won. April revenue was 1,930 million against expenditure of 2,010 million."},{"t":"Section 2. Non-Government Finance","lv":2,"p":[580,589],"pp":"123","s":"April data: cash balances fell 1,080 million won to 2,428.7 million; deposits reached a record 56,421 million. Loans outstanding fell 2,096 million to 30,025 million. May loans approved totaled 1,839 million."},{"t":"Part V. Government and Politics","lv":1,"p":[590,621],"pp":"133","s":"Administration, politics, legal affairs, police and interzone matters: the 10 May election, Assembly opening on 31 May, Ordinance No. 192 on courts, election violence and 31 border incidents."},{"t":"Section 1. Administration","lv":2,"p":[590,593],"pp":"133","s":"Chief Adviser Dr. Edgar Johnson returned on 25 May from Washington after 72 conferences. The National Economic Board met four times; pay rises of 25 to 130 percent were approved from 1 June. Military Government personnel numbered 3,003."},{"t":"Section 2. Political Developments","lv":2,"p":[594,605],"pp":"137","s":"The 10 May election (91% turnout, 198 seats), the National Assembly's opening, the Pyongyang conference outcome, the 14 May power shut-off's effect on Kim Koo and Kimm Kiu-sic, and protests over Koreans in Japan."},{"t":"General Election","lv":3,"p":[594,600],"pp":"137","s":"Over 91 percent of registrants voted on 10 May in Korea's first general election. Of 198 members, nearly half were rightists and none communist; two Cheju districts were voided. The average age was 47."},{"t":"National Assembly","lv":3,"p":[601,602],"pp":"144","s":"On 27 May 149 members elected Rhee Syngman temporary chairman. On 31 May all 198 attended; Rhee won the speakership with 188 votes, with Shin Ik-hi and Kim Tong-won as vice-speakers."},{"t":"North-South Conference","lv":3,"p":[603,603],"pp":"146","s":"Kimm Kiu-sic and Kim Koo returned from Pyongyang on 5 May after the 19-25 April conference, reporting a four-Kim agreement to oppose a separate southern government and demand withdrawal of US and Soviet forces."},{"t":"Power Shut-off","lv":3,"p":[604,604],"pp":"147","s":"North Korea cut electricity to the South on 14 May, citing unpaid bills and USAFIK's refusal to send a delegation. Rightists blamed the Soviets, communists blamed the United States, and Kim Koo disclaimed knowledge of the contract."},{"t":"Koreans in Japan","lv":3,"p":[605,605],"pp":"148","s":"Several South Korean parties protested treatment of Koreans in Japan as revived Japanese imperialism. Reports that Korean schools in Japan had been closed were read as an infringement of Korean rights."},{"t":"Section 3. Legal Affairs","lv":2,"p":[606,610],"pp":"149","s":"Ordinance No. 192 separated the courts from the Department of Justice from 1 June, alongside Ordinances 193-197. Prison population was 22,141; Cheju trials were expedited; 49 vested-property claims were screened."},{"t":"Section 4. National Police","lv":2,"p":[611,616],"pp":"154","s":"Election violence killed four candidates, 15 election officials and 32 policemen, with about 275 civilian deaths; only 68 of 13,407 booths were attacked. On Cheju-do, 3,126 were held and two districts could not vote."},{"t":"Section 5. Interzone Activities","lv":2,"p":[617,621],"pp":"160","s":"Thirty-one border incidents with the North Korean Constabulary occurred, mostly near the Oe-dong dam; an American civilian was wounded on 26 May. A Soviet Yak-9 landed near Yosu; mail fell to 43,000 pieces."},{"t":"Part VI. Social","lv":1,"p":[622,649],"pp":"165","s":"Health, education, culture, press opinion, youth and women: Mukden repatriation, three education ordinances, 803 election campaign performances, 33 Korean National Youth Assemblymen and 3,000,000 women voters."},{"t":"Section 1. Public Health and Welfare","lv":2,"p":[622,625],"pp":"165","s":"Repatriation of Mukden refugees began, with four ships carrying about 3,800 people through Inchon quarantine camps. Smallpox appeared on Cheju, and Kyongsang-namdo completed 2,106 low-cost housing units."},{"t":"Section 2. Education","lv":2,"p":[626,632],"pp":"169","s":"Three ordinances were drafted creating school districts, Boards of Education and a household-income-tax basis for school finance. The FY 1948/49 budget was 1.5 billion won; 13.9 million textbooks were printed."},{"t":"Section 3. Culture","lv":2,"p":[633,636],"pp":"176","s":"Thirty election campaign teams gave 803 performances to 1,334,464 people from 7 April. The Seoul U.S. Information Center opened 29 April, and election stamps were issued 10 May."},{"t":"Section 4. Press and Public Opinion","lv":2,"p":[637,641],"pp":"180","s":"The press debated the Pyongyang Conference and the broken power promise, then the election. Police counted 202 dead and 643 wounded from 31 March to 17 May; the Country Guard Corps was ordered disbanded on 22 May."},{"t":"Section 5. Korean National Youth, Inc.","lv":2,"p":[642,644],"pp":"185","s":"Thirty-three Korean National Youth members were elected to the National Assembly, against Dai Dong's 13. Audited membership reached 859,208; the FY 1948/49 allocation was 20,600,000 won."},{"t":"Section 6. Women's Activities","lv":2,"p":[645,649],"pp":"188","s":"About 3,000,000 women voted on 10 May, of 3,903,293 registered women; 19 of 594 candidates were women and none was elected. The Women's Bureau ran a Mothers' School."},{"t":"Back matter","lv":1,"p":[650,655],"pp":"","s":"Blank pages, a library date-due slip stamped June 2010, and a closing source note giving the UCSF Industry Documents Library URL; no report content."}],"by":"claude-sonnet (Claude Code subagent)"},{"id":"J-33","ia":"summation-non-military-activities-japan-no-33-1948-06","s":"J","no":33,"d":"1948-06","d2":"","t":"Summation of Non-Military Activities in Japan, No. 33 (June 1948)","v":"","pg":170,"o":"Summation No. 33 covers non-military Occupation activities in Japan for June 1948 (data mostly for April-May). Politically the month was dominated by the Nishio political-funds scandal, the Diet budget deadlock, and the Illegal Transactions Investigation Committee's probes of donations and hoarded military supplies, along with rifts in the Democratic Party. The 28 June Fukui earthquake devastated Fukui city and threatened textile rehabilitation. Economic sections report postwar peaks in coke, vacuum tubes, watches and silk fabric, a growing coal output, and new trade machinery: Boeki Cho's direct dealing authority, a $60 million raw-cotton credit and a sterling payment agreement. Labor reports union membership (6.55 million), left-right struggle and wage standards. Social sections cover new pharmaceutical and narcotics laws, repatriation, the Diet's rescinding of the Imperial Rescript on Education, and information programs. Historians will find detailed statistics, war crimes trial figures, land reform totals, and party and Diet legislative detail.","h":["The House of Representatives rejected nonconfidence in Deputy Prime Minister Nishio 209 to 178 on 24 June over 500,000 yen in unreported political funds, which split the Social Democrats and ended in a 30 June budget compromise.","The 28 June Fukui earthquake destroyed about 70 percent of Fukui city, killing 3,195 and injuring 9,858 there, and threatened rayon and textile rehabilitation.","The Diet enacted the National Property Law on 28 June, creating five classes of public property and a classification commission, and both houses reaffirmed the 1890 Imperial Rescript on Education invalid on 18-19 June.","Boeki Cho was authorized 28 May to deal directly with foreign firms; a $60 million raw-cotton credit, a 31 May sterling payment agreement and a 9 June China trade agreement followed, against a four-month deficit of $200,186,092.","The Cabinet approved a Broadcast Bill on 18 June creating an independent Broadcasting Commission and a nonprofit Broadcasting Corporation of Japan.","By 20 June 673 war crimes suspects had been tried, 623 convicted and 95 sentenced to death; 1,136 internees remained at Sugamo.","Unions numbered 31,075 with 6,553,816 members amid sharpening left-right struggle; the government wage base was set at ¥3,700 for price revisions and 3,791 yen in the budget compromise.","Postwar peaks were reached in coke (254,819 tons), watches and clocks (197,039), and silk fabric (10,631,000 square yards); tenants bought 101,498 cho of land in May."],"k":["Nishio scandal","budget deadlock","Illegal Transactions Investigation Committee","Fukui earthquake","National Property Law","war crimes trials","land reform","coal production","Broadcast Bill","labor unions","Boeki Cho","sterling payment agreement","Imperial Rescript on Education","Ashida cabinet","Pharmaceutical Law","repatriation"],"toc":[{"t":"Front matter","lv":1,"p":[1,6],"pp":"","s":"Cover, title pages and contents for Summation No. 33 (June 1948): four-part outline, a list of 80 charts, maps and graphs, and a topographic map of Japan."},{"t":"Part I: General","lv":1,"p":[7,17],"pp":"1","s":"Digest of the month: the Nishio funds charge, 209-178 nonconfidence vote and Fukui earthquake; coal and coke output, union membership of 6.55 million; new pharmaceutical laws and the Rescript on Education resolution."},{"t":"Organization Under SCAP","lv":2,"p":[8,8],"pp":"3","s":"No change occurred in the non-military organization of General Headquarters, SCAP, during June 1948."},{"t":"Political Activities","lv":2,"p":[8,9],"pp":"3","s":"Nishio's political-funds charge and 209 to 178 nonconfidence vote, a 30.1 billion yen budget revision, the National Property Law, Democratic Party defections, the 28 June Fukui earthquake (3,195 dead) and 1,136 internees at Sugamo."},{"t":"Economic Activities","lv":2,"p":[10,14],"pp":"7","s":"May and April data: tenants bought 101,498 cho of land, coal output rose to 2,788,500 tons, coke hit a postwar peak, union membership reached 6.55 million, and the four-month trade deficit was 200 million dollars."},{"t":"Social Activities","lv":2,"p":[15,17],"pp":"17","s":"First model health center at Morioka, new Pharmaceutical, Narcotics and Marihuana laws, 59,196 repatriates, the 18 June resolution nullifying the Imperial Rescript on Education, 124.9 million textbooks and Cardinal Spellman's visit."},{"t":"Part II: Political","lv":1,"p":[17,43],"pp":"21","s":"Politics and law: the Nishio scandal, budget compromise of 30 June, illegal-transactions probes, the National Property Law, Fukui earthquake relief, judges' pay laws and war crimes totals (673 tried by 20 June)."},{"t":"Government and Politics","lv":2,"p":[18,35],"pp":"23","s":"Nishio scandal, the budget deadlock ended 30 June, illegal-transactions probes, the National Property Law, local finance bills, Diet business and party splits among Democrats and others."},{"t":"Political Developments","lv":3,"p":[18,18],"pp":"23","s":"The antiadministration alliance resolved to overthrow the Ashida cabinet; government parties united at month's end to reject the nonconfidence vote against Nishio and moved toward budget agreement."},{"t":"Nishio Controversy","lv":3,"p":[18,19],"pp":"23","s":"Nishio failed to report 500,000 yen in political funds, splitting the Social Democrats. The 24 June nonconfidence motion lost 209 to 178; Attorney General Suzuki said Nishio would resign after the budget passed, then be prosecuted."},{"t":"Budget Controversy","lv":3,"p":[19,23],"pp":"25","s":"Budget deadlock ended in a 30 June compromise: fares set at 255 percent rather than a proposed 330 percent, a 30.1 billion yen change to the 399 billion yen General Account, and a 3,791 yen wage base."},{"t":"Zaibatsu Dissolution and Foreign Capital","lv":3,"p":[23,23],"pp":"","s":"Ashida told the Diet on 22 June that Zaibatsu dissolution was virtually complete. Official Horigoshi said little private foreign capital could be expected soon, citing taxes, labor unrest and undetermined exchange rates."},{"t":"Illegal Transactions","lv":3,"p":[23,28],"pp":"","s":"Procurators uncovered hoarded military goods (Nakajima, Tohoku Metals, Fuji Sanpro). The Diet committee probed Nishio's 500,000 yen, Democratic Party funds, the Gunma coffee case (governor resigned), Finance Minister Kitamura and Ono's perjury case."},{"t":"Government Activities","lv":3,"p":[28,30],"pp":"","s":"The National Property Law, passed 28 June, creates five classes of public property and a classification commission. Three local finance measures seek 199.4 billion yen; 34 prefectural conferences were held."},{"t":"The Diet","lv":3,"p":[30,32],"pp":"","s":"Diet bills introduced 26 May-25 June are tabulated; the political contributions bill was enacted 19 June. Both houses reaffirmed the 1890 Rescript on Education invalid (18-19 June); Takahashi and Fujieda won by-elections."},{"t":"Political Parties","lv":3,"p":[33,35],"pp":"","s":"Eight Democratic Diet members announced secession on 6 June over war bond interest deferment. Social Democrats approved nationalizing electric power; the People's Cooperative Party offered ten budget revisions; Democratic Liberals campaigned against Ashida."},{"t":"Public Safety","lv":2,"p":[36,39],"pp":"59","s":"The 28 June Fukui earthquake destroyed about 70 percent of Fukui city and killed 3,195 there. A blast at the Mitsubishi-Katsuda mine killed 62; April offenses numbered 196,951 and prisoners 92,607."},{"t":"Legal Affairs and War Crimes","lv":2,"p":[40,43],"pp":"67","s":"The Diet passed the administrative litigation law and judges' pay increases on 25 June. Sugamo held 1,136 internees; 673 suspects tried by 20 June, 623 convicted, 95 sentenced to death."},{"t":"Part III: Economic","lv":1,"p":[43,149],"pp":"73","s":"Economic reports: May land sales of 101,498 cho, postwar peaks in coke and watches, coal output, textiles, power, the Broadcast Bill, union membership of 6.55 million, trade credits, finance and science."},{"t":"Agriculture and Fisheries","lv":2,"p":[44,52],"pp":"75","s":"Tenants bought 101,498 cho in May (632,632 cho cumulative); reclamation lands 567,873 cho; cooperatives, crop insurance, April slaughter of 4,064 tons, fisheries and whaling."},{"t":"Agrarian Reform","lv":3,"p":[44,45],"pp":"","s":"Tenant farmers bought 101,498 cho in May, bringing agrarian-reform sales to 632,632 cho. Reclamation holdings reached 567,873 cho; 200,383 cho had passed to the Agriculture Ministry; 2,767 cho of demonstration farms were to be sold."},{"t":"Arable Land by Prefectures","lv":3,"p":[45,49],"pp":"76","s":"Arable land was 12.49 percent of Japan Proper's 36,048,047 hectares at 31 August 1947; Hokkaido 9.40 percent. The farm population of 36,509,458 averaged 7.345 persons per hectare. Pages 46-49 are charts only."},{"t":"Livestock, Fisheries and Whaling","lv":3,"p":[50,52],"pp":"88","s":"5,470 cooperatives elected 75,473 directors and auditors; April slaughter fell to 4,064 tons; marine output nearly doubled to 222,509 tons. Antarctic whaling was authorized 21 June and 104 whales were taken."},{"t":"Forestry and Mining","lv":2,"p":[53,58],"pp":"93","s":"May log output was 60,355,400 cubic feet and forests were overcut. Coal output reached 2,788,500 tons, below quota; crude oil 15,399 kiloliters; 13 of 33 minerals rose in April."},{"t":"Heavy Industries","lv":2,"p":[59,69],"pp":"105","s":"Coke reached a postwar peak of 254,819 tons in May and ammonium sulfate 104,765 tons. Rolled steel rose 5.6 percent, cement fell to 141,883 tons, and shipyards completed 21 steel vessels."},{"t":"Manufacturing","lv":2,"p":[70,78],"pp":"127","s":"Wheat flour fell sharply in April amid depleted grain stocks; beer rose 20 percent. Small trucks hit a postwar peak of 243, typewriters rose 361 percent, and watches and clocks reached 197,039."},{"t":"Textile Industries","lv":2,"p":[79,94],"pp":"145","s":"The Fukui earthquake threatened rayon rehabilitation. Raw silk output was 10,536 bales; cotton yarn was held down while fabrics rose; rayon, wool, bast fibers, garments and dyeing are reported."},{"t":"Cotton","lv":3,"p":[79,82],"pp":"146","s":"May raw cotton imports dropped 3,347,000 pounds; pure yarn output held at 23.9 million pounds. Independent weavers gained 4,678,000 square yards of fabric, and operating looms reached 105,277."},{"t":"Silk","lv":3,"p":[82,85],"pp":"151","s":"Raw silk output was 10,536 bales in May and 11,856 bales were tested for export. The spring cocoon crop was estimated at 77,379,000 pounds; silk fabric hit a postwar record of 10,631,000 square yards."},{"t":"Artificial Fibers","lv":3,"p":[85,87],"pp":"159","s":"Rayon staple and filament output set postwar records, up 589,000 and 380,000 pounds. Imports included 1,700 tons of rayon pulp and the first nylon waste; Kurashiki began commercial polyvinyl alcohol fiber."},{"t":"Wool","lv":3,"p":[87,89],"pp":"162","s":"May wool imports totaled 3,183 bales, but little of the 9,605 bales received since 1 April reached mills because of inspection and distribution delays. Woolen fabric reached 1,571,000 square yards."},{"t":"Hard and Bast Fibers, Garments and Finishing","lv":3,"p":[89,94],"pp":"","s":"The seventh postwar abaca shipment, 6,558 bales, arrived. Flax yarn rose 10 percent, but ramie, hemp and jute fell after a strike at the largest ramie and hemp mill; cotton printing set a postwar record."},{"t":"Transportation and Public Utilities","lv":2,"p":[95,101],"pp":"177","s":"Registered motor vehicles numbered 209,002 in May; railways carried 565,601,143 passengers. Coastal cargo rose 11.4 percent to 1,129,958 tons, and power generation was 2,974,000,000 kWh."},{"t":"Communications","lv":2,"p":[102,110],"pp":"191","s":"The Cabinet approved a Broadcast Bill on 18 June creating a Broadcasting Commission and a nonprofit Broadcasting Corporation. Telephones reached 1,322,017, radio licensees 6,650,046, and 988,505 vacuum tubes were produced."},{"t":"Labor","lv":2,"p":[111,119],"pp":"209","s":"Left-right struggle in unions, the Labor-Farmer Liaison Council, 31,075 unions with 6.55 million members, labor relations disputes, a 3,700 yen wage base, employment and labor education."},{"t":"Labor and Employer Organizations","lv":3,"p":[111,114],"pp":"","s":"The Labor-Farmer Liaison Council (9 June) and Tokyo Council of Independent Labor Unions (12 June, 125,000 members) were founded. Unions numbered 31,075 with 6,553,816 members; SCAP did not object to ILO observers."},{"t":"Labor Relations","lv":3,"p":[114,117],"pp":"","s":"Coal unions ratified grievance machinery; Tokyo-Yokohama rail locals struck for 24 hours. The CLRC set a ¥3,923 private railway base wage and settled the All-Japan Express dispute at ¥4,854; Toho Studio cut 1,019 jobs."},{"t":"Wages, Working Conditions and Employment","lv":3,"p":[118,119],"pp":"","s":"Wage stabilization set ¥3,700 as the base for 23 June price revisions; government workers sought ¥5,200. April manufacturing wages rose 14 percent; inspections numbered 9,955 with 19,019 violations."},{"t":"Imports and Exports","lv":2,"p":[120,126],"pp":"227","s":"Boeki Cho was authorized 28 May to deal directly with foreign firms. A $60 million U.S. cotton credit, a 31 May sterling agreement and a 9 June China trade agreement followed; the four-month deficit reached $200,186,092."},{"t":"Price and Distribution","lv":2,"p":[127,132],"pp":"241","s":"Full ration needs were met, with imported sugar substituted for five days. The Cabinet approved fish and vegetable control plans 1 June; milk, vegetable and grain prices rose, and 12,500 ration \"ghosts\" were caught."},{"t":"Finance","lv":2,"p":[133,140],"pp":"253","s":"Nine foreign banks' licenses were broadened 14 June. Bank of Japan notes stood at 230.6 billion yen, tax collections reached 147.2 billion yen (108.7 percent of estimate), and national debt totaled 365.6 billion yen."},{"t":"Property Control and Reparations","lv":2,"p":[140,144],"pp":"267","s":"Looted property custody centered on 39.5 million yen of cinchona bark; 246 vessels were deleted from looted lists. The SCAP Custody Account reached 284,957,133 yen; 14,122 machine tools had shipped to claimants by 30 June."},{"t":"Science and Technology","lv":2,"p":[144,149],"pp":"275","s":"The Cabinet approved the Science Council Bill 8 June. Professor Honda discovered a new comet 3 June; 1,327 Japanese Standards were in effect and patent registrations totaled 123."},{"t":"Part IV: Social","lv":1,"p":[149,169],"pp":"285","s":"Social reports: communicable diseases, new pharmaceutical and narcotics laws, public assistance, repatriation, the Rescript on Education invalidation, religion, public opinion surveys, press, radio, film and libraries."},{"t":"Public Health and Welfare","lv":2,"p":[150,159],"pp":"287","s":"Communicable diseases rose to 4,399 cases in May; the Pharmaceutical Affairs Law passed 30 June and narcotics laws 28 June. May public assistance reached 1,911,666 persons; repatriates numbered about 59,000."},{"t":"Preventive Medicine and Veterinary Affairs","lv":3,"p":[150,153],"pp":"","s":"A model health center opened at Morioka 15 June. Eleven principal communicable diseases caused 4,399 cases and 430 deaths in May; the Councillors passed the Preventive Vaccination and Venereal Disease Prevention Laws."},{"t":"Medical Administration and Supply","lv":3,"p":[153,156],"pp":"","s":"The Pharmaceutical Affairs Law passed 30 June, requiring accredited pharmacists. Japan had 3,451 hospitals with 310,562 beds; May medical supply output was 1.94 billion yen; narcotics laws passed 28 June, with 82,324 handlers registered."},{"t":"Public Assistance, Vital Statistics and Repatriation","lv":3,"p":[156,159],"pp":"","s":"May public assistance reached 1,911,666 persons, with payments of 377 million yen. April births were 324,393 and deaths 79,756; 59,198 persons were repatriated, mostly from Soviet areas."},{"t":"Education, Religion, Cultural Resources and Media of Expression","lv":2,"p":[160,169],"pp":"307","s":"The Rescript on Education invalidated, a 1948 education budget of 26.7 billion yen, boards of education, textbooks, Cardinal Spellman's visit, opinion surveys, press, radio, film, music and libraries."},{"t":"Education","lv":3,"p":[160,163],"pp":"307","s":"The Councillors reaffirmed on 18 June the 1890 Imperial Rescript on Education is invalid. The education budget carried 5,381,320,000 yen for school buildings; 687 textbook manuscripts were announced and 1,850 characters standardized."},{"t":"Religion and Cultural Resources","lv":3,"p":[163,164],"pp":"","s":"Cardinal Spellman and 14 Catholic officials visited Tokyo 7 June; 46 religious workers were cleared to enter. Applications for transfer of state precincts (72,000 shrines, 30,000 temples) closed."},{"t":"Media of Expression","lv":3,"p":[164,169],"pp":"","s":"A 19-newspaper Public Opinion League formed; 821 new book editions appeared in May and 98 foreign books were licensed. The first press-code prosecution (Nikkan Sports) occurred; new SCAP libraries opened at Kanazawa and Kobe."},{"t":"Map of Japan - Topography","lv":1,"p":[170,170],"pp":"326","s":"Final page: a topographic map of Japan, with the back cover and a printing-plant mark."}],"by":"claude-sonnet (Claude Code subagent)"},{"id":"K-33","ia":"south-korean-interim-government-activities-no-33-1948-06","s":"K","no":33,"d":"1948-06","d2":"","t":"South Korean Interim Government Activities, No. 33 (June 1948)","v":"","pg":591,"o":"This file is a bound Google-digitized volume holding three consecutive economic and political reports, not only No. 33. No. 33 (June 1948) covers the monsoon-quiet month after the Cheju and May disorders, the first full month without North Korean electricity (cut 14 May), collapsing industrial output, the new weighted wholesale price index (12-fold rise since 1945), summer grain and land sales, and the new National Assembly drafting the Constitution. No. 34 (July-August 1948) is the final SKIG report, covering passage of the Constitution, Rhee's election, the 15 August proclamation of the Republic, the end of USAMGIK, with three-year retrospectives on population, vested property, labor, utilities, public works, police, press, youth, women and culture. No. 35 (September-October 1948) is the first economic-only summation under the Republic, with transfer of offices to Korean ministries, rice collection and the ration-roll 'ghost population'. Historians get dense statistics on prices, trade, power, coal, finance and land reform.","h":["June 1948 was the first full month without North Korean electric power: only 41 million KWH reached consumers, 15 million less than May, and flour, paper and cement output fell sharply from April.","Arthur Peterson's first weighted index showed South Korean wholesale prices rose about twelve-fold from August 1945 to July 1948, reaching 171 on a 1947 base of 100.","The new National Assembly adopted its rules 153 to 1 on 10 June and began reading the draft Constitution on 23 June, disputing presidential powers and unicameralism.","On 8 June U.S. planes bombed Dok Do fishermen, provoking Korean protest from all factions; Cheju-do violence continued, with the constabulary commander murdered on 18 June.","The Constitution passed 12 July, Rhee Syngman was elected President by 180 votes on 20 July, and the Republic of Korea was proclaimed 15 August 1948, ending USAMGIK.","By end-July 85 percent of Japanese-owned farmland had been sold to tenants through the National Land Administration, and about 494,699 farms had been sold by June.","SKIG closed with an overdraft of about 22,500 million won, while the Office of Property Custody administered roughly 85,000 million won of vested property.","No. 35 reports summer grain collection of 107,766 tons and padded ration rolls neared 9,987,000, raising suspicion of ghost recipients, as economic offices passed to Korean ministries."],"k":["USAMGIK","South Korean Interim Government","National Economic Board","North Korean power cutoff","wholesale price index","vested property","land sales","National Assembly","Constitution","Republic of Korea","Cheju-do rebellion","civilian supply imports","Korean National Youth","rice collection","Dok Do bombing"],"toc":[{"t":"Front matter (Google Books notice)","lv":1,"p":[1,5],"pp":"","s":"Google Books digitization notice, a library-stamp image and pages marked only \"Digitized by Google\"; no report content."},{"t":"No. 33 (June 1948): Front matter","lv":1,"p":[6,15],"pp":"","s":"Cover of South Korean Interim Government Activities No. 33, June 1948, prepared by the National Economic Board; map of Korea, 1 July 1948 organization charts, 30 June Board roster (Acting Chairman C. H. Donnelly) and contents."},{"t":"No. 33: Summary","lv":1,"p":[16,19],"pp":"1","s":"Monsoon rains quieted Cheju and southern disorders, but the North Korean power cutoff cut output from April (flour down 33 percent, paper 57). Wholesale prices stood 12-fold above 1945; over 15,000 refugees crossed the parallel."},{"t":"No. 33, Part I: Basic Aspects","lv":1,"p":[20,31],"pp":"5","s":"Over 15,000 refugees crossed the 38th parallel in June; seven Kobe and Osaka school-closure rioters were jailed in Japan; the Office of Property Custody processed 69,223,437 won in capital requests and decided 141 title claims."},{"t":"Population and Foreign Affairs","lv":2,"p":[20,22],"pp":"5","s":"Over 15,000 refugees crossed the 38th parallel in June; cumulative movement was 886,385 outbound and 2,424,175 inbound. In Japan, a Korean trade association formed and seven Kobe and Osaka school-closure rioters were jailed by a Hyogo military court."},{"t":"Vested Property","lv":2,"p":[23,31],"pp":"8","s":"The Office of Property Custody collected 3,370 financial statements, processed 69,223,437 won in capital requests and surveyed 3,072 former Japanese corporations. It took over the Korean Residence Management Corporation and decided 141 title claims."},{"t":"No. 33, Part II: Production and Supply","lv":1,"p":[32,111],"pp":"17","s":"Summer grain planted on 1,889,606 acres; first weighted wholesale index shows prices twelve-fold since 1945; the power cutoff cut flour, cotton and cement output; imports of 368 million won against exports of 323 million; Pusan stevedore reforms."},{"t":"Agriculture, Forestry and Fisheries","lv":2,"p":[32,41],"pp":"17","s":"Summer grain was planted on 1,889,606 acres, about 15 percent lost to drought; rice transplanting ran two weeks early. NLA had sold farms to tenants (494,699 farms, 84 percent); silk cocoon credit was 1,170,368,400 won."},{"t":"Prices","lv":2,"p":[42,65],"pp":"27","s":"June wholesale and retail prices rose, rice about 25 percent above May (cleaned rice 1,850 won per mal against 1,460). A supplement introduces Arthur Peterson's first weighted wholesale index, showing a twelve-fold rise since August 1945."},{"t":"Monthly wholesale, retail and controlled prices","lv":3,"p":[42,45],"pp":"27","s":"Seoul retail prices rose in June, led by rice and red beans (cleaned rice 1,850 won per mal against 1,460 in May), while fish and vegetables eased. Tables cover wholesale, retail, open-market and controlled prices."},{"t":"Index Numbers of Wholesale Prices in South Korea, August 1945 to July 1948","lv":3,"p":[46,65],"pp":"30","s":"Arthur G. Peterson's first weighted wholesale index (1947=100, 34 commodities) shows prices twelve-fold since August 1945, reaching 171 in July 1948. It justifies the 1947 base and gives weights and monthly group tables."},{"t":"Industrial Production","lv":2,"p":[66,73],"pp":"51","s":"The northern power cutoff held down output all June. Flour rose to 1,653 tons from 1,023, but noodles fell 24.5 percent and cotton yarn 28 percent; cement idle, carbide stopped; cut tobacco hit a postwar high of 729.4 tons."},{"t":"Mining and Minerals","lv":2,"p":[74,82],"pp":"59","s":"Anthracite output fell to 54,954 tons in May from 68,612 in April; bituminous imports from Japan fell to 45,729 tons in June, 25 percent below May. About 4,600 tons of U.S. pitch arrived; silver output hit an occupation high."},{"t":"Allocations and Distribution","lv":2,"p":[83,92],"pp":"68","s":"The National Consumer Goods Office planned 500 million won of incentive goods for the summer grain collection. The Allocation Board approved 118.9 million won of civilian supplies and 458.2 million won of Korean goods; 6,360 tons of salt were distributed."},{"t":"Imports and Exports","lv":2,"p":[93,105],"pp":"78","s":"June licenses showed exports of about 323 million won against imports of 368 million. Oh Chung-soo and John Thorn attended the ECAFE session in India; the FLC credit expired 30 June with $2,139,775 committed."},{"t":"Labor","lv":2,"p":[106,111],"pp":"91","s":"Pusan stevedore dispute eased: contractors cut from 24 to eight, tonnage split moved from 80-20 to 70-30, and a hiring hall replaced the honcho system on 27 June. Child labor violations were found in 94 percent of Seoul firms inspected."},{"t":"No. 33, Part III: Public Services","lv":1,"p":[112,139],"pp":"97","s":"Railroads carried 3,069,000 passengers against 4,298,000 in May; only 41 million KWH of power reached consumers; 133 fires cost 353 million won; Waegwan Bridge deck began 19 June; communications staff fell for a fifth month."},{"t":"Transportation","lv":2,"p":[112,118],"pp":"97","s":"Revenue was about 14 percent below May; expenditures rose to 554,289,000 won. Railroads carried 3,069,000 passengers against 4,298,000 in May and about 440,564 tons of freight; operable locomotives stayed at 247 of 656."},{"t":"Communications","lv":2,"p":[119,124],"pp":"104","s":"Revenue rose nearly 20 million won in June, led by telephone income; staff fell for a fifth straight month under a reduction-in-force order. Postal savings deposits stood at 846 million won; insurance policies fell 38.5 percent."},{"t":"Public Utilities","lv":2,"p":[125,130],"pp":"110","s":"Only 41 million KWH reached consumers in June, down 15 million from May, the first full month without northern power. U.S. Army engineers began investigating on 28 June; 136 Seoul streetcars ran."},{"t":"Public Works","lv":2,"p":[131,139],"pp":"116","s":"First-quarter road subsidies of 50,222 thousand won (of 201,888 total) went to provinces; 11 national bridge projects stood at 30 June. The Masan-Chinhae tunnel opened; 133 fires cost 353 million won."},{"t":"No. 33, Part IV: Finance","lv":1,"p":[140,155],"pp":"125","s":"Bank of Chosun notes reached 30,000 million won; SKIG overdraft stood at 21,268 million. May deposits hit a record 58,280 million won, while loans outstanding fell 696 million to 29,328 million."},{"t":"Government Finance","lv":2,"p":[140,146],"pp":"125","s":"Bank of Chosun notes rose from 28,200 million won in April to 30,000 million at end-June. Cumulative SKIG overdraft stood at 21,268 million; May revenue was 1,736 million against expenditure of 1,644 million."},{"t":"Non-Government Finance","lv":2,"p":[147,155],"pp":"132","s":"May deposits reached a record 58,280 million won, with government balances at 34,315 million. Loans outstanding fell 696 million to 29,328 million; Yun Ho-byung became Bank of Chosun governor."},{"t":"No. 33, Part V: Government and Politics","lv":1,"p":[156,181],"pp":"141","s":"The Assembly adopted its rules 153 to 1 and began the Constitution; June police work was far quieter than May, with two policemen killed; Cheju constabulary commander murdered 18 June; 22,331 prisoners held in 19 prisons."},{"t":"Administration","lv":2,"p":[156,158],"pp":"141","s":"The Korea Civil Service processed 1,719 appointments and 1,979 resignations in June. The National Economic Board met five times, approving Transportation's 7,644 million won budget; USAMGIK personnel totaled 3,178."},{"t":"Political Developments","lv":2,"p":[159,167],"pp":"144","s":"The National Assembly adopted its rules 153 to 1, formed nine standing committees and a Constitution drafting committee. First reading of the Constitution ended 30 June amid dispute over presidential powers and unicameralism."},{"t":"Legal Affairs","lv":2,"p":[168,173],"pp":"153","s":"Executive Order 22 indefinitely postponed the Cheju-do by-election; Order 23 created an Election Review Board. The 19 prisons held 22,331 prisoners, and Chonju clerks were arrested for leftism."},{"t":"National Police","lv":2,"p":[174,176],"pp":"159","s":"June was far quieter than May: two policemen killed against over 30 in May. On Cheju-do the constabulary commander was murdered 18 June and about 3,000 rioters were reported arrested."},{"t":"Inter-Zone Activities","lv":2,"p":[177,181],"pp":"162","s":"Over 41,500 pieces of mail were exchanged at Tosong in four weekly exchanges, 65 percent southbound. Radio Pyongyang denounced the South Korean election, and North Korea announced price cuts on 280 items."},{"t":"No. 33, Part VI: Social (printed as Part IV)","lv":1,"p":[182,209],"pp":"167","s":"Public health nursing course began with 13 nurses; 38,816 homeless families; teacher summer sessions for about 7,520 teachers; Korean National Youth reached 873,310 members; women made up 300 of 3,250 registered doctors."},{"t":"Public Health and Welfare","lv":2,"p":[182,187],"pp":"167","s":"A public health nursing course began with 13 nurses and about 100 were recruited for the Constabulary Nurses Corps. About 6,100 Mukden refugees were processed at Inchon; 38,816 homeless families faced a 5,476-unit housing plan."},{"t":"Education","lv":2,"p":[188,192],"pp":"173","s":"Summer sessions (26 July to 21 August) were planned to train about 7,520 teachers in 18 localities with 3,384,000 won in subsidies. Textbook distribution since 1945 totaled 14,567,626."},{"t":"Press and Public Opinion","lv":2,"p":[193,197],"pp":"178","s":"Press focused on the National Assembly's opening and the 23 June draft constitution, the 8 June Dok Do bombing, the power cutoff and false reports of Japanese officials in Korea, which Hodge and Dean denied."},{"t":"Korean National Youth, Inc.","lv":2,"p":[198,201],"pp":"183","s":"Korean National Youth grew to 873,310 members, a June gain of only 14,087 from tighter screening. It trained 10,573 reserve leaders and ran 981 literacy classes and 159 speakers' bureaus."},{"t":"Women's Activities","lv":2,"p":[202,209],"pp":"187","s":"Women made up 300 of 3,250 registered doctors; Women's Medical University (404 students) was certified as a college in June. Covers the Nurses' Association (1,000 members), KNY women (114,370) and a home economics radio program."}],"by":"claude-sonnet (Claude Code subagent)","emb":["K-34-in-K-33","K-35-in-K-33"]},{"id":"J-34","ia":"summation-non-military-activities-japan-no-34-1948-07","s":"J","no":34,"d":"1948-07","d2":"","t":"Summation of Non-Military Activities in Japan, No. 34 (July 1948)","v":"","pg":183,"o":"Summation No. 34 covers July 1948 and the Ashida coalition's last weeks. The central political event is MacArthur's 22 July letter on National Public Service Law revision, followed by a 31 July cabinet order banning strikes and collective bargaining by government employees. The Diet's 209-day session ended 5 July after passing the budget and a wave of legal reforms: Habeas Corpus, criminal procedure, Reformatory and Juvenile laws, health and medical practice laws, boards of education, and new tax and local finance laws. Party politics show the Social Democratic split, the New Liberal Party, the Nishio indictment and graft inquiries. Economically the report tracks record coke output, the Fukui earthquake (28 June) damage to silk and rayon weaving, price stabilization, rate increases, financial liquidation, trade agreements, nutrition surveys and repatriation. Historians will find detailed statistical tables, legal chronology, labor and press reaction, and war crimes trial results.","h":["MacArthur's 22 July letter to Prime Minister Ashida held that government employees may not strike; a cabinet order of 31 July banned public-employee strikes and collective bargaining, and wage mediation was suspended.","The second Diet session closed 5 July after 209 days, the longest in Japanese history, enacting 230 of 273 bills including the Habeas Corpus, Reformatory and Juvenile laws and the 1948-49 budget of 414.5 billion yen.","Deputy Prime Minister Nishio resigned 6 July and was tried from 16 July for perjury and failing to report a 500,000-yen contractor donation, while graft inquiries led to arrests of Diet members.","The coalition weakened: a New Liberal Party formed 15 July with 12 members, six Social Democrats were expelled and 10 seceded over the budget, cutting the government margin to nine seats.","On 19 July the Supreme Court, 13 to one, used constitutional review for the first time to exclude a confession obtained after prolonged detention (Imai case).","The 28 June Fukui earthquake damaged 525 of 1,253 silk and rayon mills, and export fabric output was expected to fall by one third; a party of 97 seismologists investigated.","Eight war criminals were sentenced to death in 10 trials, including the Bataan Death March case, while the IMTFE held no session in July.","Land sold to tenants rose to 589,491 cho in June, coke output reached a postwar record of 265,707 tons, and postal, telephone and telegraph rates rose roughly fourfold."],"k":["National Public Service Law","Ashida cabinet","Supreme Commander's letter","Social Democratic Party","Nishio case","Illegal Transactions Investigation Committee","Fukui earthquake","land reform","price stabilization","financial reorganization","war crimes trials","Habeas Corpus Law","boards of education","public health laws","nutrition surveys"],"toc":[{"t":"Front matter","lv":1,"p":[1,5],"pp":"","s":"Cover, title pages, table of contents for four parts, and a list of 108 charts, maps and graphs, from industrial production indexes to textbooks, films and SCAP information libraries."},{"t":"Part I: General","lv":1,"p":[6,16],"pp":"1","s":"Overview of July 1948: MacArthur's 22 July letter and the 31 July strike ban, the Diet's close with 230 laws, the Fukui earthquake, 1948-49 budget of 414.5 billion yen, CARE, and 45,333 repatriates."},{"t":"Section 1. Organization Under SCAP","lv":2,"p":[7,7],"pp":"3","s":"No change occurred in the non-military organization of General Headquarters, SCAP, during July 1948. The page also opens Section 2 with its contents list."},{"t":"Section 2. Political Activities","lv":2,"p":[7,8],"pp":"3","s":"MacArthur's 22 July letter led to a 31 July cabinet order banning public-employee strikes. The Diet closed 5 July after enacting 230 measures; Nishio resigned and was indicted; a New Liberal Party formed 15 July."},{"t":"Section 3. Economic Activities","lv":2,"p":[9,14],"pp":"7","s":"Land sales to tenants rose to 589,491 cho in June; coke output hit a record 265,707 tons; the Fukui earthquake damaged 525 of 1,253 mills. Postal and telephone rates rose and the 414.5 billion yen budget passed."},{"t":"Section 4. Social Activities","lv":2,"p":[15,16],"pp":"19","s":"The Diet passed a package of health laws; CARE began 19 July with 10-dollar food packages; 1,841,429 people received welfare aid in June. Postcensorship was completed 25 July and 45,333 persons were repatriated."},{"t":"Part II: Political","lv":1,"p":[17,46],"pp":"23","s":"Government and politics, public safety, and legal affairs. Covers MacArthur's letter, the 209-day Diet session, the Nishio case, 38 killed in Kagoshima landslides, and eight war crimes death sentences."},{"t":"Section 1. Government and Politics","lv":2,"p":[18,38],"pp":"25","s":"Politics dominated by MacArthur's letter on public service labor, the Diet's close, the Nishio case, coalition strain, party realignments and graft investigations."},{"t":"Public Service Law Revision","lv":3,"p":[18,23],"pp":"25","s":"MacArthur's 22 July letter to Ashida said public employees may not strike and called for revising the National Public Service Law. The cabinet issued a 31 July order banning strikes and bargaining; a special Diet session was planned for mid-September."},{"t":"Governmental Activities (Diet, Wage Disputes, Laws, Party Strength)","lv":3,"p":[24,30],"pp":"","s":"The 209-day Diet session closed 5 July, enacting 230 of 273 bills. The wage base was 3,790 yen against union demands of 5,200 yen, and mediation halted after MacArthur's letter. The Ministry of Construction began 10 July."},{"t":"Political Developments (Nishio Case, Coalition Weakness)","lv":3,"p":[30,32],"pp":"","s":"Nishio resigned 6 July and was tried from 16 July for perjury and failing to report a 500,000-yen contractor donation. Social Democrats threatened to leave the coalition; a poll put Ashida's support at 16 percent."},{"t":"Political Parties","lv":3,"p":[32,36],"pp":"","s":"The New Liberal Party formed 15 July with 12 members. Six left-wing Social Democrats were expelled and 10 seceded, cutting the government margin to nine seats. Katayama sought dissolution 18 July; a bomb injured Tokuda 19 July."},{"t":"Illegal Transactions Investigation","lv":3,"p":[36,38],"pp":"","s":"The Illegal Transactions Investigation Committee pursued graft: Diet members Iimura and Nishimura were arrested or indicted, and Kono, Isazaki and Miura tried for perjury. A subcommittee probed black-market incomes above 5,000,000 yen."},{"t":"Revised Economic Policy","lv":3,"p":[38,38],"pp":"","s":"The Economic Rehabilitation Committee's revised program of 14 July moved the five-year plan to 1949-53, aiming to restore 1930-34 per capita real income. The Economic Stabilization Board issued a ten-point draft 17 July."},{"t":"Section 2. Public Safety","lv":2,"p":[39,41],"pp":"67","s":"Floods from 25 July in Fukui, Toyama, Ishikawa and Shiga killed 30 with 22 missing; Kagoshima landslides killed 38. June rail accidents numbered 622. Procurators uncovered about 1,950,000,000 yen of hoarded goods; prison population was 94,700."},{"t":"Section 3. Legal Affairs and War Crimes","lv":2,"p":[42,46],"pp":"73","s":"The Diet passed Habeas Corpus, Juvenile, Reformatory and Inquest of Prosecution laws. On 19 July the Supreme Court, 13 to one, first used constitutional review to bar a confession. War crimes trials produced eight death sentences."},{"t":"Part III: Economic","lv":1,"p":[47,154],"pp":"83","s":"Thirteen sections on the economy: 589,491 cho of land sold to tenants, 2,711,000 tons of coal, record coke output, earthquake damage to textiles, rate increases, trade agreements, finance and science."},{"t":"Section 1. Agriculture and Fisheries","lv":2,"p":[48,51],"pp":"85","s":"The Government sold 589,491 cho to tenants in June (1,222,323 cumulative) and bought 209,782 cho. Fertilizer allocations met 70, 59 and 1 percent of requirements; marine production fell to 200,630 tons."},{"t":"Section 2. Forestry and Mining","lv":2,"p":[52,57],"pp":"93","s":"June log production was 53,364,500 cubic feet. A five-year reforestation program began. July coal output was 2,711,000 tons against a 2,850,000 goal; crude oil was 16,632 kiloliters."},{"t":"Section 3. Heavy Industries","lv":2,"p":[58,68],"pp":"105","s":"Coke output hit a postwar record 265,707 tons in June. Pig iron rose 2.4 percent while power shortages cut electric-furnace steel 30 percent. Dyes reached a postwar peak; the Ministry of Construction began 10 July."},{"t":"Section 4. Manufacturing","lv":2,"p":[69,78],"pp":"127","s":"Canned food rose to 1,924 tons; wheat flour fell to 26,295 tons; cigarettes rose 11 percent. Pulp reached 39,612 tons and paper 40,555 tons. Watches and clocks set a record 206,226 units."},{"t":"Section 5. Textile Industries","lv":2,"p":[79,93],"pp":"147","s":"The Fukui-Ishikawa earthquake damaged 525 of 1,253 silk and rayon mills. The section also reports cotton, wool, bast fiber and knit goods output, stocks, machinery, and raw cotton and wool imports."},{"t":"Earthquake Damage","lv":3,"p":[79,79],"pp":"","s":"The 28 June earthquake destroyed or damaged over a third of weaving capacity, including 547 Fukui mills with 12,904 looms. About 28 percent of bleaching and dyeing capacity lies there; export fabric output expected down one third."},{"t":"Section 6. Transportation and Public Utilities","lv":2,"p":[94,100],"pp":"177","s":"The Diet passed the Port Regulations, Port Area (418 ports) and Pilot laws. Motor vehicles reached 211,092. Electric generation fell 11 percent to 2,642 million kWh amid drought; gas output was 63.7 million cubic meters."},{"t":"Section 7. Communications","lv":2,"p":[101,110],"pp":"191","s":"The Diet approved roughly fourfold postal, telephone and telegraph rate increases. Telephones in service reached 1,230,872; 6,762,022 radio households were counted. Postal savings restrictions were lifted 20 July; personnel numbered 434,516."},{"t":"Section 8. Labor","lv":2,"p":[111,120],"pp":"211","s":"The Mariners' Employment Security Law passed 3 July. Communications Workers demanded 5,200 yen against the Government's 3,791. Union membership reached 6,629,222 on 31 May; the Supreme Commander's letter halted wage mediation."},{"t":"Section 9. Imports and Exports","lv":2,"p":[121,127],"pp":"231","s":"Japan signed a trade agreement with the French Union on 6 July, including 25,000 tons of Indo-Chinese anthracite. May imports were $28.3 million, exports $17.8 million; the cumulative 1948 deficit was $219.7 million."},{"t":"Section 10. Price and Distribution","lv":2,"p":[128,134],"pp":"245","s":"The July staple ration was met in full, imported food being 28.6 percent of requirements. A new price stabilization program raised subsidized basic materials to 110 times 1934-36 levels and repriced four commodity groups."},{"t":"Section 11. Finance","lv":2,"p":[135,144],"pp":"259","s":"Bank of Japan notes outstanding reached 241.4 billion yen on 31 July. The 1948-49 General Account budget was 414.5 billion yen, national debt 367 billion; 20.3 million shares were sold and 47 control organizations closed."},{"t":"Section 12. Property Control and Reparations","lv":2,"p":[145,149],"pp":"279","s":"Looted property in custody stood at 65.9 million yen; the SCAP Custody Account reached $286.5 million. SCAP did not object to 766 transactions, 78 firms left the restricted list, and Japan Soda received the first reorganization order."},{"t":"Section 13. Science and Technology","lv":2,"p":[150,154],"pp":"289","s":"A party of 97 seismologists and engineers investigated the Fukui earthquake. The Science Council of Japan Law (210 members) and new Industrial Science and Technology and Smaller Enterprises Agencies were created; research appropriations were 2,297,500,000 yen."},{"t":"Part IV: Social","lv":1,"p":[155,182],"pp":"299","s":"Public health and welfare, and education, religion, culture and media: new health laws, CARE, nutrition surveys, boards of education, the textbook monopoly's end and postcensorship from 25 July."},{"t":"Section 1. Public Health and Welfare","lv":2,"p":[156,171],"pp":"301","s":"July 1948 laws: Preventive Vaccination, Venereal Disease Control, Medical Practitioner. June disease and drug output; CARE began 19 July; social security mission report; May nutrition survey (Tokyo 1,961 calories); 45,333 repatriated."},{"t":"Nutrition","lv":3,"p":[165,170],"pp":"320","s":"The May nutrition survey sampled 42,000 people. Per capita calories were 1,961 in Tokyo, 1,937 in 11 large cities and 2,001 in rural areas. Underweight fell (Tokyo 17.4 percent) but impaired lactation reached 27.8 percent."},{"t":"Section 2. Education, Religion, Cultural Resources and Media of Expression","lv":2,"p":[172,182],"pp":"333","s":"Covers the education budget, the boards of education law, the end of the textbook monopoly, religion, cultural resources, the move to postcensorship, and radio, film, theater and SCAP libraries."},{"t":"Education","lv":3,"p":[172,176],"pp":"333","s":"The Ministry of Education received 27,171,630,000 yen; elective boards of education are due in prefectures and major cities by 1 November. Strikes hit 94 of 658 higher schools over tuition increases."},{"t":"Religion","lv":3,"p":[176,176],"pp":"","s":"The first postwar Year Book of Religion appeared 15 July; the National Holiday Law cut holidays from twelve to nine. 1,297 foreign missionaries were in Japan; 946 shrines, temples and churches were damaged by the earthquake."},{"t":"Cultural Resources","lv":3,"p":[177,177],"pp":"","s":"The Ministry of Education asked governors to help preserve scenic and historic sites. The Fourth Annual Japanese Art Exhibition, set for October and November in Tokyo, passes to the Japan Art Academy."},{"t":"Media of Expression","lv":3,"p":[177,182],"pp":"","s":"Postcensorship was extended to all Japanese newspapers and news services by 25 July. Book output was 844 new editions in June. Radio, films, theater, music and SCAP libraries at Shizuoka and Nagasaki are reported."},{"t":"Map of Japan - Topography","lv":1,"p":[183,183],"pp":"354","s":"Fold-out topographic map of Japan at the end of the volume, with no printed text."}],"by":"claude-sonnet (Claude Code subagent)"},{"id":"K-34","ia":"south-korean-interim-government-activities-no-34-1948-07","s":"K","no":34,"d":"1948-07","d2":"1948-08","t":"South Korean Interim Government Activities, No. 34 (July-August 1948)","v":"","pg":271,"o":"Final issue of South Korean Interim Government Activities, covering July-August 1948 and prepared by the National Economic Board's Statistics Division. It records the 15 August 1948 establishment of the Republic of Korea, the end of USAMGIK (succeeded by the Civil Affairs Section, USAFIK) and the continuing SKIG wind-down. The Summary and the retrospective passages review three years of occupation: population movement, vested property, agriculture and fertilizer, industry under the loss of northern electric power (cut 14 May), trade, labor, transport, finance, law, police, health, education, press, youth and women. Political chapters cover the Constitution, Government Organization Law, National Assembly, Rhee's cabinet and party history. Historians will find extensive statistics (refugees, prices, production, trade, deposits, budgets), the vested-property handover, the Torture Case against the police, the floods, and candid official assessments of occupation achievements and limits.","h":["The Republic of Korea was proclaimed on 15 August 1948, ending USAMGIK, which was succeeded by the Civil Affairs Section, USAFIK; Rhee Syngman was elected President 180 votes of 196 and Lee Bum-suk approved Prime Minister 110-84.","After the Soviet cutoff of northern electric power on 14 May, anthracite output fell from 68,612 tons in April to 38,887 in July, though power deliveries held at about 56 million KWH in July and August.","Population movement since October 1945 saw 2,454,480 arrivals, including 1,115,550 Koreans from Japan and 622,044 from North Korea, against 886,618 departures, mostly Japanese; estimated population was 20.3 million on 1 July 1948.","Vested property had net worth of 85,849,975 thousand won; 35 of 5,358 investigated companies passed to elected boards from 10 July, and 481,957 acres of farmland were sold to farmers by 31 July.","The 1948 crop was estimated near 6,000,000 metric tons, the highest since 1941, helped by over 548,000 tons of fertilizer imports since October 1946, though July floods inundated 504,541 acres.","The SKIG overdraft reached 22,506 million won, note issue stood at 30,000 million won, and the urban retail price index (1947 = 100) rose from 86 in January 1947 to 179 in August 1948.","The Torture Case, a January death at Metropolitan Police Division M leaked in July, led Detective Bureau chiefs Lee Man-chong and Cho Byung-sul to resign on 12 August; Chief Chang Taik-sang survived a fourth attempt on his life on 24 July.","Korean National Youth reached 1,154,821 members and donated over 12,600,000 work days, and Korea competed under its own flag at the London Olympics, finishing 24th with 15 points."],"k":["Republic of Korea","end of USAMGIK","National Economic Board","refugees","repatriation","vested property","fertilizer","electric power shortage","foreign trade","inflation","Constitution","National Assembly","National Police","Korean National Youth","floods"],"toc":[{"t":"Front matter","lv":1,"p":[1,10],"pp":"","s":"Cover, National Economic Board members under Chairman Wilhelm Anderson, Statistics Division contributors, Arthur G. Peterson's foreword on the Republic's 15 August founding and the series since 1946, and the table of contents."},{"t":"Summary","lv":1,"p":[11,14],"pp":"1","s":"Chairman Anderson reviews three years of occupation: refugee growth, near food self-sufficiency from fertilizer imports, trade deficit cut from 400 to 25 per cent, the 14 May northern power cutoff, vested property, and an overdraft above 22,000 million won."},{"t":"Part I. Basic Aspects","lv":1,"p":[15,40],"pp":"5","s":"Covers population and refugee movement (886,618 outbound, 2,454,480 inbound since 1945) and vested property worth 85,849,975 thousand won net, with 35 companies transferred to elected boards."},{"t":"Section 1. Population and Foreign Affairs","lv":2,"p":[15,29],"pp":"5","s":"Refugee, repatriation and population statistics through August 1948, including 151 Turk-Tartars granted Turkish visas, an estimated 20.3 million population on 1 July 1948, and the Population and Census Committee."},{"t":"Refugees","lv":3,"p":[16,17],"pp":"6","s":"National Refugee Board created 12 May 1947. Table 1 counts 185,441 refugees in 1946, 163,235 in 1947 and 86,036 to mid-August 1948; Seoul entry was barred from 1 July 1947."},{"t":"Repatriation","lv":3,"p":[18,20],"pp":"8","s":"Since October 1945, 886,618 left (mostly Japanese) and 2,454,480 arrived, including 1,115,550 Koreans from Japan and 622,044 from North Korea; 596,780 Koreans remain in Japan."},{"t":"Emigration of Stateless Turk-Tartars","lv":3,"p":[20,22],"pp":"10","s":"Turkey issued visas in August 1948 to 151 stateless Turk-Tartars (97 adults, 54 children) from Seoul, Pusan and Inchon; the project passed to the Republic's Foreign Ministry, with sailing expected by end of October."},{"t":"Statistical Analysis of Population in South Korea","lv":3,"p":[22,28],"pp":"12","s":"George Deihl's analysis of 1947 registrations estimates about 887,000 births (44.35 per thousand), 260,000 deaths and 3.1 per cent natural increase, a 1 July 1948 population of 20.3 million, and life expectancy of 45 to 49."},{"t":"Population and Census Committee","lv":3,"p":[29,29],"pp":"19","s":"Committee on Population and Census Statistics, set up 4 March 1948, coordinated SKIG data and projected population from 20,100,000 on 31 March 1948 to 21,000,000 by 31 December 1949."},{"t":"Section 2. Vested Property","lv":2,"p":[30,40],"pp":"20","s":"Office of Property Custody prepared handover to Koreans: net vested worth 85,849,975 thousand won, July sales of 199 properties, 1,119 claims cases, loan guarantees, and 35 companies transferred to elected boards."},{"t":"Operations","lv":3,"p":[31,33],"pp":"21","s":"Office of Property Custody reorganized for handover: seven-section Administrative Division, Purchase and Contract Board for deals over 200,000 won, gasoline cut 50 per cent. Vested holdings were 256,019,630 thousand won gross, 85,849,975 thousand net."},{"t":"Property Disposal","lv":3,"p":[34,35],"pp":"24","s":"July sales of 199 properties brought 217,870 thousand won; 957 sales worth 1,918,888 thousand won since August 1947. Inspections covered Taegu and Pusan textile mills and 355 vessels at Mokpo and Kunsan."},{"t":"Real Estate Transfers","lv":3,"p":[35,35],"pp":"25","s":"Five USAFIK properties, including CIC Headquarters in Inchon and the Sugar Bowl Building, were regularized. The largest army property release yet included 149 dependent houses, 117 buildings at Camp Taejon and a 19-building Pusan Navy Compound."},{"t":"Policy Decisions and Claims Section","lv":3,"p":[36,36],"pp":"26","s":"OPC Memorandum No. 32 of 12 August treated Allied-owned companies organized in Japan as Allied, not vested. Claims Section at 31 August: 1,119 cases, 453 allowed, 233 disallowed, 531 pending."},{"t":"Comptroller Activities and Corporate Affairs","lv":3,"p":[37,40],"pp":"27","s":"Property Custodian guaranteed loans of 7,000,000 won to Chosun Tanning and 5,000,000 won to Korean Fisheries. Statements for 1,812 companies show 1,021,121 thousand won profit; 35 of 5,358 investigated companies went to elected boards, starting 10 July."},{"t":"Part II. Production and Supply","lv":1,"p":[41,142],"pp":"31","s":"Covers food, prices, industry, mining, distribution, trade and labor: 1948 crop near 6,000,000 tons, retail index 179, anthracite down to 38,887 tons in July, $328 million civilian supply imports, and Pusan wage disputes."},{"t":"Section 1. Food and Agriculture","lv":2,"p":[41,58],"pp":"31","s":"1948 crop near 6,000,000 tons, highest since 1941; floods affected 504,541 acres; fertilizer imports exceeded 548,000 tons; 481,957 acres of vested farmland sold to farmers by 31 July."},{"t":"Food","lv":3,"p":[41,46],"pp":"31","s":"The 1948 crop estimate of almost 6,000,000 metric tons is the highest since 1941, with self-sufficiency expected from January 1949. Summer grain collection reached 95.8 per cent of quota; stocks were 244,169 tons at end of August."},{"t":"Floods","lv":3,"p":[47,48],"pp":"37","s":"The 7 July typhoon broke 801 Cholla levees, damaged 28,219 acres and killed 75. Table 8 lists 504,541 acres flooded, mostly in Cholla-pukto; crops were replanted and forecasts stayed above 1947."},{"t":"Agricultural Bureaus and Forestry and Fisheries","lv":3,"p":[49,52],"pp":"39","s":"Rice transplanting finished two weeks early. Raw silk fell to 121.5 tons for lack of coal, power and cocoons; chick hatching reached 605,470 of a 2,000,000 goal; fishing landings were 67 per cent of 1947."},{"t":"Korea Agricultural Association","lv":3,"p":[53,54],"pp":"43","s":"Korea Agricultural Association shipped 5,200,000 straw grain bags for export to Japan despite floods. Directives of 10 and 28 July set ammonium sulphate at 370 won per bag; 424,970 tons were sold to farmers."},{"t":"Agricultural Improvement Service","lv":3,"p":[55,56],"pp":"45","s":"Fertilizer imports since October 1946 exceeded 548,000 metric tons by end of August 1948. Extension work trained over 1,000 myun agents and promoted disease-free potatoes and second-crop planting."},{"t":"National Land Administration","lv":3,"p":[57,58],"pp":"47","s":"Ordinance No. 215 of 10 August amended the Land Administration's ordinance. By 31 July 85 per cent of available farms were sold: 502,224 farms and 481,957 acres; rent collection reached 25 per cent."},{"t":"Section 2. Prices","lv":2,"p":[59,68],"pp":"49","s":"Prices resumed rising after five months of stability. A new weighted index of urban workers' retail prices (1947 = 100) rose from 86 in January 1947 to 179 in August 1948."},{"t":"Index Numbers of Retail Prices Paid by Urban Workers","lv":3,"p":[66,68],"pp":"56","s":"New weighted index (1947 = 100) replaced the unweighted Bank of Chosun series, weighting foods 57.5, utilities 21.9 and clothing 15.1. It rose from 86 in January 1947 to 179 in August 1948, using 32 series from nine cities."},{"t":"Section 3. Industrial Production","lv":2,"p":[69,81],"pp":"59","s":"Output fell under restricted power: flour 1,782 tons in August, cotton yarn up 22 per cent in July, nails up 46 per cent, no cement in July, insulators down to 4,800 pieces, salt 4,808 tons."},{"t":"Processed Food and Textiles","lv":3,"p":[72,73],"pp":"62","s":"One of two large vested flour mills had power; flour output was 707 tons in July and 1,782 in August versus 1,653 in June. Cotton yarn rose 22 per cent in July and cloth 34 per cent."},{"t":"Paper, Hardware and Chemicals","lv":3,"p":[74,78],"pp":"64","s":"Power shortage depressed paper, chemicals and building materials. The best paper mill idled for lack of sulphite pulp, nail output rose 46 per cent in July, and no cement was made in July."},{"t":"Miscellaneous and Monopoly Bureau","lv":3,"p":[79,81],"pp":"69","s":"Electric insulators fell to 4,800 pieces in July and 1,500 in early August; pencils rose 22 per cent to 4,400 gross. Monopoly Bureau salt fell to 4,808 tons and cigarettes to 108.3 tons."},{"t":"Section 4. Mining and Minerals","lv":2,"p":[82,90],"pp":"72","s":"After the Soviet power cut of 14 May, anthracite fell from 68,612 tons in April to 38,887 in July. August allocations: 28,844 tons to Commerce, 26,000 to Army. Coal imports from Japan hit 104,055 tons in July."},{"t":"Section 5. Allocations and Distribution","lv":2,"p":[91,106],"pp":"81","s":"National Consumer Goods Office processed 1,095 allocations worth 2,184,468,531 won by 31 August; petroleum, salt and North Korean barter reported, including 950,861,250 won of northern imports to 15 August."},{"t":"National Consumer Goods Office","lv":3,"p":[91,94],"pp":"81","s":"Created by Executive Order No. 12 of 12 January 1948 under Dr. Chyung Yil-heung, it had processed 1,095 allocations worth 2,184,468,531 won by 31 August, 88.7 per cent for special groups and 28.2 per cent to farmers."},{"t":"Distribution of Controlled Commodities","lv":3,"p":[95,99],"pp":"85","s":"The Federation of Financial Associations, over 600 branches and nearly 2,000,000 members, distributes goods on a 15 per cent commission. Eligible employees numbered 480,273, up 43,630 since June."},{"t":"Petroleum","lv":3,"p":[100,100],"pp":"90","s":"Gasoline deliveries were 4,901,333 liters in July and 6,079,399 in August against allocations above 7,000,000, short mainly because the Pusan terminal could not fill drums."},{"t":"Salt Distribution","lv":3,"p":[101,103],"pp":"91","s":"Government salterns made 65,323 tons in F/Y 1947. For F/Y 1948, 68,774 tons (58.6 per cent of the 134,631-ton goal) were made by 15 August; stocks of 184,170 tons allowed full rations."},{"t":"Barter Trade with North Korea","lv":3,"p":[104,106],"pp":"94","s":"Commerce Order No. 4 (2 August 1948) legalized overland barter with North Korea. Northern imports totalled 950,861,250 won January to 15 August, notably dried fish; contracts worth 382,499,000 won were pending."},{"t":"Section 6. Imports and Exports","lv":2,"p":[107,131],"pp":"97","s":"Private trade in seven months of 1948 was 3,600 million won imports and 2,700 million exports; government trade with Japan totalled $8,377,383 since 1945, and civilian supply imports reached $328,117,607."},{"t":"Private Trade Statistics","lv":3,"p":[111,116],"pp":"101","s":"June imports were 436,652,000 won and exports 286,673,000; July imports 431,060,000 and exports 345,916,000. Inchon handled over 59 per cent of January-July trade; Hong Kong took most exports."},{"t":"Private Trade Activities","lv":3,"p":[117,118],"pp":"107","s":"Six commercial shipping companies (three American, two British, one Dutch) ran regularly by August 1948; Hong Kong-Pusan freight was HK$65 per ton. Fifteen businessmen were cleared to investigate trade."},{"t":"Government-to-Government Transactions","lv":3,"p":[119,124],"pp":"109","s":"Exports to Japan totalled $8,377,383 since 1945, $828,457 of it in July-15 August, chiefly 3,329,070 straw bags and graphite. Pusan exported 15,141 tons; China allowed no exports."},{"t":"Korean Items for Americans in Korea","lv":3,"p":[125,125],"pp":"115","s":"Army Exchange Service bought 160,789 Korean units worth $223,063 through 15 August 1948, mainly jewelry, brassware, lacquerware and silk. July sales were $4,857, the lowest since August 1947."},{"t":"Civilian Supply Imports","lv":3,"p":[126,131],"pp":"116","s":"Imports since September 1945 totalled $328,117,607, including GARIOA foodstuffs of $110,446,030 by June. Coal from Japan reached a record 104,055 tons in July; July salt imports were 19,609 tons."},{"t":"Section 7. Labor","lv":2,"p":[132,142],"pp":"122","s":"Covers wage policy (Wage Stabilization Division, 25 March 1948), Pusan stevedore dispute over a 500-won daily wage, Yongwol Mines election won by Tai Han with 2,099 votes, and bank overtime violations."},{"t":"Wage Problems","lv":3,"p":[134,137],"pp":"124","s":"Traces wage policy from the 25 June 1946 hold-the-line stance to the Wage Stabilization Division of 25 March 1948. A 3 July 1948 directive restricted general raises; a critical appraisal found no enforcement."},{"t":"General Current Activities and Labor Relations","lv":3,"p":[138,139],"pp":"128","s":"Pusan stevedores threatened a strike over a 500-won daily wage demand; talks on 22 July set a 350-won minimum, but a coal pier stoppage followed on 7 August. Marine Bureau contract for about 1,500 workers was agreed except wages."},{"t":"Unions, Standards, Health and Education","lv":3,"p":[140,142],"pp":"130","s":"Tai Han won the Yongwol Mines election of 8 July with 2,099 votes; its executive committee resigned after the Chun Jin-han motion failed 44 to 22. Bank overtime violations left 15,654 won owing to 158 employees."},{"t":"Part III. Public Services","lv":1,"p":[143,177],"pp":"133","s":"Covers rail (2,145 operating miles, 268 operable engines), marine, highway and air transport; 37,183 telephones; power deliveries of 56.2 million KWH in July; and public works, including 517 bridges rebuilt."},{"t":"Section 1. Transportation","lv":2,"p":[143,155],"pp":"133","s":"Department of Transportation covers railroads (August receipts 578 million won against 737 million expenditures), the Marine Bureau's 12 LSTs and 11 Freight Supply vessels, and highway and air transport."},{"t":"Railroads","lv":3,"p":[145,151],"pp":"135","s":"The rail system taken over in September 1945 had 2,145 operating miles. Operable engines fell from 300 to 268 by August 1948; August receipts were 578 million won against 737 million expenditures, with 31,894 employed."},{"t":"Marine Transportation","lv":3,"p":[152,152],"pp":"142","s":"Marine Bureau history: merged into the Bureau of Transportation in 1942; sham ship sales to Koreans were voided after liberation; 12 LSTs and 11 Freight Supply vessels were bought; Rainbow service began in 1948."},{"t":"Highway and Air Transportation","lv":3,"p":[153,155],"pp":"143","s":"Highway transport ran from the 1944 Pan-Korean Trucking Union (80 per cent Japanese-owned) to the Korean Truck Co. of December 1945, disbanded July 1946. Highway employment had fallen to 46 and air to 27."},{"t":"Section 2. Communications","lv":2,"p":[156,165],"pp":"145","s":"July revenue of 88.9 million won was a post-liberation high. Reports 37,183 telephones with 10,000 applications unserved, 623 post offices, and 11 radio stations with a 100-won monthly fee from 1 August."},{"t":"Section 3. Public Utilities","lv":2,"p":[166,171],"pp":"155","s":"Power deliveries were 56.2 million KWH in July and 55.7 million in August after the 14 May northern cutoff. Yong Wol was separated from the Korea Electric Power Company on 26 July; the Giroux mission report of 23 July is quoted."},{"t":"Section 4. Public Works","lv":2,"p":[172,177],"pp":"161","s":"Public works rebuilt 517 bridges, 1,422 km of road and 162 km of levees. Floods caused over 2,000 million won damage; 800 million won was sought for repairs, and an Emergency Water Conservation Board was set up 26 July."},{"t":"Part IV. Finance","lv":1,"p":[178,193],"pp":"167","s":"Government finance shows note issue of 30,000 million won, overdraft of 22,506 million and appropriations of 35,264 million; non-government finance shows deposits of 43,335 million and loans of 30,038 million through July."},{"t":"Section 1. Government Finance","lv":2,"p":[178,184],"pp":"167","s":"Note issue was 30,000 million won at end-August against a 33,400 million peak; the overdraft was 22,506 million. August revenue was 2,157 million against 1,924 million expenditure; appropriations totaled 35,264 million."},{"t":"Section 2. Non-Government Finance","lv":2,"p":[185,193],"pp":"174","s":"Through July 1948, deposits reached 43,335 million won, 57 per cent from the public, with loans of 30,038 million (70 per cent of deposits). Life insurance in force was 1,067 million won; damage insurers wrote 9,243 policies."},{"t":"Part V. Government and Politics","lv":1,"p":[194,231],"pp":"183","s":"Records the Republic's proclamation on 15 August 1948, Rhee's inauguration on 24 July, the Constitution of 12 July, Lee Bum-suk's approval as Prime Minister 110-84, legal ordinances, the Torture Case, and party history."},{"t":"Section 1. Administration","lv":2,"p":[194,198],"pp":"183","s":"Proclamation of the Republic (Tai Han Min Kook) on 15 August 1948 ended Military Government. Covers the 10 May election and Rhee's 24 July inauguration; Military Government personnel fell to 2,870 by 15 August."},{"t":"Section 2. Political Developments","lv":2,"p":[199,213],"pp":"188","s":"Constitution and Government Organization Law, Rhee elected President 180 to 196, Lee Bum-suk approved Prime Minister 110-84, cabinet completed by 6 August, moderates' split, and party development since liberation."},{"t":"Constitution and Government Organization Law","lv":3,"p":[199,203],"pp":"188","s":"The Constitution passed unanimously on 12 July, with a strong presidency, four-year Assembly and farmland to tillers. The Government Organization Law of 16 July created eleven departments, an Economic Board and Civil Service and Inspection Committees."},{"t":"National Assembly","lv":3,"p":[204,206],"pp":"193","s":"Rhee was elected President 180 votes of 196 on 20 July; Lee Bum-suk was approved Prime Minister 110-84 after Lee Yun-yung was rejected 132-59. Kim Byung-no was confirmed Chief Justice 117-31."},{"t":"Republic of Korea","lv":3,"p":[207,208],"pp":"196","s":"Rhee's cabinet was completed by 6 August. The Republic was proclaimed on 15 August; General Hodge said Americans would only advise, and transfers began 16 August. The press called the cabinet mediocre and partisan."},{"t":"Moderate Activities","lv":3,"p":[209,210],"pp":"198","s":"Chough Pyung-ok became ambassador-at-large and Chang Myun chief UN delegate in Paris. Moderates split three ways; Kim Koo and Kimm Kiu-sic held to unified independence, Kimm wavering after meeting Lee Bum-suk on 10 August."},{"t":"Summary (political parties)","lv":3,"p":[211,213],"pp":"200","s":"Three blocs emerged: communists, extreme rightists and moderates under Kimm Kiu-sic. Ordinance No. 55 of February 1946 produced over 400 Seoul parties; Rhee gained power as Kim Koo defected."},{"t":"Section 3. Legal Affairs","lv":2,"p":[214,221],"pp":"203","s":"Ordinances Nos. 203-219 drafted 1 July-31 August, four Department of Justice opinions, court caseloads (90,112 cases in 1947), Cheju-do riot trials, and prison population of 22,321 in July."},{"t":"Ordinances and Orders","lv":3,"p":[214,216],"pp":"203","s":"Ordinances Nos. 203-219 drafted 1 July-31 August covered litigation fees, taxicabs, the bar, rice collection, National Land Administration suits and school districts. 26 American lawyers were admitted to the Korean bar."},{"t":"Legal Opinions","lv":3,"p":[217,217],"pp":"206","s":"Four Department of Justice opinions: Japanese repatriates' pension deposits (No. 1627), the Chosun Mining Development Corporation (No. 1612), narcotics informer awards (No. 1650), and the metric system as official but not exclusive (No. 1633)."},{"t":"Korean Courts","lv":3,"p":[218,218],"pp":"207","s":"Cheju-do riot trials were stalled, with about 250 held; the Military Governor allowed transfer to Mokpo or Kwangju. Courts heard 67,181 cases in 1946 and 90,112 in 1947; a Kunsan branch opened."},{"t":"Penal Institutions","lv":3,"p":[219,221],"pp":"208","s":"Prison population was 22,141 in April and 22,321 in July, about 45 per cent for theft or burglary. The juvenile house of correction held 541 in June and 664 in July; 174 guards graduated in June."},{"t":"Section 4. National Police","lv":2,"p":[222,227],"pp":"211","s":"Covers the Torture Case, the 24 July attack on Chief Chang Taik-sang, Detective Bureau resignations of 12 August, Seoul crime statistics, and the police's retrospective, including over 120 policemen killed by October 1946."},{"t":"Torture Case","lv":3,"p":[224,224],"pp":"213","s":"Suspect Lim Wha was tortured to death at Metropolitan Police Division M in January. The Detective Bureau leaked it in July; Chief Detective Ro Duk-sul reportedly confessed and escaped on 27 July. No prosecutor decision by August."},{"t":"Attack on Chief Chang","lv":3,"p":[225,225],"pp":"214","s":"Three men fired on Chang Taik-sang's car in Yongdungpo on 24 July, the fourth attempt on his life; Chang was unhurt. Detective Bureau chiefs Lee Man-chong and Cho Byung-sul resigned 12 August."},{"t":"Summary (National Police)","lv":3,"p":[226,227],"pp":"215","s":"Police separated from National Defense in April 1946 and endured riots from January 1946 to Taegu in October 1946, with over 120 policemen killed. Economic and Sanitation Bureaus were eliminated; a special Inspection Command serves Cheju-do."},{"t":"Section 5. Inter-Zone Activities","lv":2,"p":[228,231],"pp":"217","s":"Mail crossed the 38th parallel on 9 trips in July-August 1948. Commerce Order No. 4 of 2 August required permits and bonds for barter. An American soldier was killed on 19 July; Radio Pyongyang promoted the 25 August election."},{"t":"Part VI. Social","lv":1,"p":[232,271],"pp":"221","s":"Covers flood relief (254,664 persons needy), 14,989,286 textbooks, three education ordinances of 12 August, 58 dailies, Korean National Youth's 1,154,821 members, women's equality, and the 1948 London Olympics."},{"t":"Section 1. Public Health and Welfare","lv":2,"p":[232,243],"pp":"221","s":"Cholera deaths fell from over 15,000 in 1946 to 10 in 1947 through immunization. Flood relief reached 254,664 needy persons; UNRRA granted 2,700,000 won in July and 6,135,000 won in August."},{"t":"Public Assistance","lv":3,"p":[239,241],"pp":"228","s":"Flood damage: 3,604 houses washed away, 14,881 crushed, 254,664 persons made needy. 1,149 tents went to refugees; UNRRA granted 2,700,000 won in July and 6,135,000 won in August."},{"t":"Summary (Public Health and Welfare)","lv":3,"p":[242,243],"pp":"231","s":"Cholera deaths fell from over 15,000 in 1946 to 10 in 1947 through mass immunization. The Department also opened the first venereal disease clinic and moved the Chung Nyang Ni asylum to medical supervision on 1 July."},{"t":"Section 2. Education","lv":2,"p":[244,249],"pp":"233","s":"Three ordinances effective 12 August created school districts, nine-member boards and local school taxation. 7,500 teachers trained, including 500 at Kwanju; 14,989,286 textbooks published since liberation; Teacher Training Center of 400 with 20 American educators."},{"t":"Section 3. Press and Public Opinion","lv":2,"p":[250,257],"pp":"239","s":"In July Korea had 58 dailies, 67 weeklies and 125 monthlies. The press covered the new government, the North Korean election of 25 August, floods, Cheju-do riots and the Dok Do bombing."},{"t":"Formation of New Government","lv":3,"p":[251,252],"pp":"240","s":"Papers backed Assembly rejection of Lee Yun-yung and approval of Lee Bum-suk, and feared a separate Police Department, defeated 108 to 88. Seoul Shin Mun led circulation at 76,000 among 58 dailies."},{"t":"Floods, Cheju-do Riots and Dok Do Incident","lv":3,"p":[253,253],"pp":"242","s":"The press protested the North Korean election of 25 August. Floods and typhoon cost an estimated 2,000,000,000 won, with 169 killed in Cholla-namdo; papers demanded more than 2,480,000 won reparations for the Dok Do bombing."},{"t":"Foreign News and Editorials","lv":3,"p":[254,256],"pp":"243","s":"Foreign news covered the Berlin crisis and the Cominform's Yugoslav condemnation. Editorials blamed SKIG's failure on military officials and Korean corruption, praised Lee Bum-suk, and criticized the Constitution as too socialistic."},{"t":"Miscellany","lv":3,"p":[256,257],"pp":"245","s":"The torture row ended when Maj.-Gen. Dean limited police press statements to the police director. Papers exposed Seoul school entrance fees and exaggerated sentences for Koreans in the Kobe and Osaka riots."},{"t":"Section 4. Korean National Youth, Inc.","lv":2,"p":[258,262],"pp":"247","s":"Chartered 9 October 1945, it reached 1,154,821 audited members by 15 August 1948. Members donated over 12,600,000 work days, planted 6,770,397 trees and trained over 100,000 leaders; Lee Jun-sic succeeded Lee Bum-suk as president."},{"t":"Section 5. Women's Activities","lv":2,"p":[263,266],"pp":"252","s":"Women gained constitutional equality; the policewoman force numbers 500 and Louise Yim became Minister of Commerce and Industry. Ordinances Nos. 70 and 112 and Executive Order No. 16 outlaw prostitution; the Women's Bureau dates from Ordinance No. 107 of 1946."},{"t":"Section 6. Culture","lv":2,"p":[267,271],"pp":"256","s":"Hangul returned to schools from October 1945, though Chinese characters persist. The American Language Institute has about 500 students. Korea's 67-man team finished 24th with 15 points at the London Olympics under its own flag."}],"by":"claude-sonnet (Claude Code subagent)"},{"id":"K-34-in-K-33","ia":"south-korean-interim-government-activities-no-33-1948-06","s":"K","no":34,"d":"1948-07","d2":"1948-08","t":"South Korean Interim Government Activities, No. 34 (July-August 1948)","v":"Bound in K-33","host":"K-33","rg":[[210,485]],"kind":"bound","found":"Title page 'M105.13:34' on PDF p. 210","pg":276,"o":"This final issue of South Korean Interim Government Activities, prepared by the National Economic Board Statistics Division, covers July-August 1948 and the transition on 15 August 1948 from US Army Military Government to the Republic of Korea under President Rhee Syngman. It opens with a summation of three years of occupation, citing food self-sufficiency prospects, fertilizer imports, farmland sales, a narrowing trade deficit and vested property returned to Korean management. Regular sections report prices (wholesale index 184 percent of 1947), industrial output under reduced power after the 14 May North Korean cutoff, labor, transport, communications, finance with a 22,000 million won overdraft, politics, police, and social fields. Historians will find refugee and population statistics, trade figures, the Constitution and cabinet formation, and retrospective accounts of each department.","h":["The Republic of Korea was proclaimed on 15 August 1948 under President Rhee Syngman, and USAMGIK was succeeded by the Civil Affairs Section of USAFIK; MG personnel had fallen to 2,870.","Refugees from the north numbered 185,441 in 1946, 163,235 in 1947 and 86,036 by mid-August 1948; cumulative inbound movement was 2,454,480 against 886,618 outbound.","Wholesale prices rose to 184 percent of the 1947 average in August, up from 171 in July, ending five months of stability, while the SKIG overdraft exceeded 22,000 million won.","After the North Korean power cutoff of 14 May, local generation recovered to 55,600,000 KWH in August from 33,000,000 in April; anthracite output fell from 68,612 tons to 38,887.","Management of 35 vested companies passed to elected Boards of Directors, and the National Land Administration had sold 481,957 acres of farmland, about 85 percent of available farms.","The Assembly elected Rhee Syngman President with 180 votes and approved Lee Bum-suk as Prime Minister 110 to 84 after the Constitution of 12 July.","The 1948 crop was estimated near 6,000,000 metric tons, the highest since 1941, with food self-sufficiency expected by January 1949 and fertilizer imports above 548,000 tons.","Korea competed in the London Olympics under its own flag for the first time, sending 67 athletes and placing 24th with 15 points."],"k":["South Korean Interim Government","Republic of Korea","Rhee Syngman","National Economic Board","vested property","refugees","fertilizer","electric power shortage","prices","Bank of Chosun","Constitution","National Police","Korean National Youth","Hangul","Olympics 1948"],"toc":[{"t":"Front matter","lv":1,"p":[210,223],"pp":"","s":"Cover and front matter of the final SKIG Activities report (No. 34, July-August 1948). Lists Board members under Chairman Wilhelm Anderson, Arthur G. Peterson's Forward on the 15 August 1948 Republic, and the table of contents."},{"t":"Summary","lv":1,"p":[224,227],"pp":"1","s":"Chairman Wilhelm Anderson reviews three years of occupation: 500,000 tons of imported fertilizer, near food self-sufficiency, the 14 May power cutoff, 85,000 million won of vested property, 481,957 acres sold, and a 22,000 million won overdraft."},{"t":"Part I: Basic Aspects","lv":1,"p":[228,253],"pp":"5","s":"Covers refugees and population (86,036 refugees from the north to mid-August 1948) and vested property, including 1,119 title claims heard and 35 companies passed to elected boards."},{"t":"Section 1. Population and Foreign Affairs","lv":2,"p":[228,242],"pp":"5","s":"Refugees from the north totalled 86,036 in 1948 to mid-August, after 185,441 in 1946; inbound movement was 2,454,480 against 886,618 outbound. 151 Turk-Tartars awaited emigration; population estimated at 20.3 million."},{"t":"Section 2. Vested Property","lv":2,"p":[243,253],"pp":"20","s":"Office of Property Custody prepared handover to the Republic. Claims hearings covered 1,119 cases; Corporate Affairs investigated 5,358 companies and passed 35 to elected boards. Net worth of vested property was about 85,850 million won."},{"t":"Part II: Production and Supply","lv":1,"p":[254,355],"pp":"31","s":"Covers the 1948 economy: crop near 6,000,000 tons, wholesale prices at 184 percent of 1947, power-limited output, coal losses after 14 May, trade of 3,643 million won imports, and about 118,000 unionists."},{"t":"Section 1. Food and Agriculture","lv":2,"p":[254,271],"pp":"31","s":"The 1948 crop is estimated near 6,000,000 metric tons, highest since 1941, with self-sufficiency expected by January 1949. Summer grain collection reached 95.8 percent of quota; fertilizer imports passed 548,000 tons; 481,957 acres of farmland sold."},{"t":"Section 2. Prices","lv":2,"p":[272,281],"pp":"49","s":"Wholesale prices rose to 184 percent of the 1947 average in August, from 171 in July, led by grains, fish, raw cotton and salt. Controlled grain prices rose 1 August; a new urban worker index stood at 179."},{"t":"Section 3. Industrial Production","lv":2,"p":[282,294],"pp":"59","s":"July-August 1948 output suffered from the power shortage: flour 707 and 1,782 tons, paper mills short of pulp, cement halted, salt hit by rains. Explains why 1947 output looks far below 1940."},{"t":"Section 4. Mining and Minerals","lv":2,"p":[295,303],"pp":"72","s":"Coal output fell after the 14 May 1948 Soviet power cutoff: anthracite dropped from 68,612 tons in April to 38,887 in July. Bituminous imports from Japan hit a record 104,055 tons in July."},{"t":"Section 5. Allocations and Distribution","lv":2,"p":[304,319],"pp":"81","s":"National Consumer Goods Office (est. 12 January 1948) processed 1,095 allocations worth 2.18 billion won by 31 August. Salt production reached 68,774 tons by 15 August; barter with North Korea brought 950.9 million won inflow."},{"t":"Section 6. Imports and Exports","lv":2,"p":[320,344],"pp":"97","s":"Private trade for January-July 1948 was about 3,643 million won imports and 2,763 million exports. Government exports to Japan were $828,457; paper led imports, sea products and straw hats led exports."},{"t":"Section 7. Labor","lv":2,"p":[345,355],"pp":"122","s":"About 118,000 unionists in some 500 unions, mostly in rightist Tai Han. Wage Stabilization Division formed 25 March 1948; Pusan stevedores sought a 350 won minimum; Tai Han won the Yongwol election with 2,099 votes."},{"t":"Part III: Public Services","lv":1,"p":[356,389],"pp":"133","s":"Covers transportation, communications, public utilities and public works: record August rail revenue, 37,183 telephones, power deliveries of 55.6 million KWH in August, and flood damage over 2,000 million won."},{"t":"Section 1. Transportation","lv":2,"p":[356,367],"pp":"133","s":"August transport revenue was the highest since liberation. Rail had 268 operable engines in August with higher freight income; the marine fleet included LSTs and the Rainbow service. Expenditures exceeded income."},{"t":"Section 2. Communications","lv":2,"p":[368,377],"pp":"145","s":"July communications revenue was the highest since liberation after the June rate increase, while expenditures more than doubled to 196.9 million won. Noted 37,183 telephones, 623 post offices and 11 radio stations."},{"t":"Section 3. Public Utilities","lv":2,"p":[378,383],"pp":"155","s":"North Korea's 14 May 1948 power cutoff and the Chung Pyung transformer failure caused shortages; deliveries recovered to 56.2 million KWH in July and 55.6 million in August. Giroux Commission reported 23 July."},{"t":"Section 4. Public Works","lv":2,"p":[384,389],"pp":"161","s":"Summer 1948 brought preparation for transfer to the Korean Government and flood damage exceeding 2,000 million won. The Emergency Water Conservation Board was set up 28 July; road and bridge repairs and subsidy requests noted."},{"t":"Part IV: Finance","lv":1,"p":[390,405],"pp":"167","s":"Covers government finance (SKIG overdraft of 22,506 million won, F/Y 1948/49 appropriations of 35,264 million won) and private finance (deposits of 43,335 million won)."},{"t":"Section 1. Government Finance","lv":2,"p":[390,396],"pp":"167","s":"Bank of Chosun note issue was about 30,000 million won at end of August; SKIG overdraft 22,506 million. August revenue 2,157 million against expenditure 1,924 million; F/Y 1948/49 appropriations 35,264 million won."},{"t":"Section 2. Non-Government Finance","lv":2,"p":[397,405],"pp":"174","s":"Private finance through July: cash balances 2,244 million won, deposits 43,335 million (57 percent public), loans outstanding 30,038 million with record June and July lending. Life insurance in force passed 1,067 million won."},{"t":"Part V: Government and Politics","lv":1,"p":[406,443],"pp":"183","s":"Covers administration, the Constitution and election of Rhee Syngman, legal affairs, police, and inter-zone activities as USAMGIK gave way to the Republic on 15 August 1948."},{"t":"Section 1. Administration","lv":2,"p":[406,410],"pp":"183","s":"The new government (Tai Han Min Kook) was proclaimed 15 August 1948, ending USAMGIK; the 10 May election drew over 90 percent of 7,730,000 voters. NEB raised rice to 150 won per small mal; MG personnel fell to 2,870."},{"t":"Section 2. Political Developments","lv":2,"p":[411,425],"pp":"188","s":"Constitution adopted 12 July. The Assembly elected Rhee Syngman President (180 votes) and Lee Si-young Vice-President, and approved Lee Bum-suk as Prime Minister 110 to 84. Moderates split between Kim Koo, Kimm Kiu-sic and the North."},{"t":"Section 3. Legal Affairs","lv":2,"p":[426,433],"pp":"203","s":"Justice drafted Ordinances Nos. 203-219 and Executive Orders Nos. 24-28. Cheju-do riot trials were movable to Mokpo or Kwangju. Prisoners numbered 22,321 in July; 664 juveniles were in correction houses."},{"t":"Section 4. National Police","lv":2,"p":[434,439],"pp":"211","s":"Police focused on communist efforts to force signatures for the 25 August North Korean election, with 200 officials held in Taegu. A torture death in Metropolitan Police caused resignations; a fourth attempt on Chang Taik-sang failed 24 July."},{"t":"Section 5. Inter-Zone Activities","lv":2,"p":[440,443],"pp":"217","s":"North-South mail exchange continued at Tosong with 9 trips in July-August 1948. Commerce Order No. 4 on interzonal barter took effect 2 August; an American soldier was killed at the 38th parallel on 19 July."},{"t":"Part VI: Social","lv":1,"p":[444,485],"pp":"221","s":"Covers health, education, press, youth, women and culture: 254,664 flood-needy people, school districts created 12 August, Korean National Youth membership of 1,154,821, and Korea's first Olympic entry under its own flag."},{"t":"Section 1. Public Health and Welfare","lv":2,"p":[444,455],"pp":"221","s":"Floods and typhoons left 254,664 people needy; hog cholera hit five provinces (43,428 hogs). Two smallpox outbreaks were controlled, with about 420,000 vaccinated in Seoul; 28,698 kg of opium returned to Japan."},{"t":"Section 2. Education","lv":2,"p":[456,461],"pp":"233","s":"Three ordinances effective 12 August 1948 created school districts, nine-member school boards and local school taxation. Teacher workshops reached 7,500 teachers; textbook output was 421,660 copies in July."},{"t":"Section 3. Press and Public Opinion","lv":2,"p":[462,469],"pp":"239","s":"The press focused on forming the new government: the police-under-Internal Affairs vote (108 to 88), rejection of Lee Yun-yung, approval of Lee Bum-suk as Premier. Table lists 11 leading dailies among 58."},{"t":"Section 4. Korean National Youth, Inc.","lv":2,"p":[470,474],"pp":"247","s":"Korean National Youth, Inc., chartered 9 October 1945, reached 1,154,821 members by 15 August 1948 and gave over 12,600,000 work-days of labor. Lee Jun-sic succeeded Lee Bum-suk after he became Prime Minister."},{"t":"Section 5. Women's Activities","lv":2,"p":[475,478],"pp":"252","s":"The new Constitution and Military Government ordinances guarantee women equality. Louise Yim became Minister of Commerce and Industry; the Women's Bureau ran a magazine, classes and the Federation of Women's Clubs."},{"t":"Section 6. Culture","lv":2,"p":[479,485],"pp":"256","s":"Hangul was reintroduced in schools; the American Language Institute taught English to about 500 students. Korea's first Olympic entry under its own flag at London sent 67 athletes and placed 24th with 15 points."}],"by":"claude-sonnet (Claude Code subagent)"},{"id":"J-35","ia":"summation-non-military-activities-japan-no-35-1948-08","s":"J","no":35,"d":"1948-08","d2":"","t":"Summation of Non-Military Activities in Japan, No. 35 (August 1948)","v":"","pg":173,"o":"Summation No. 35, the final issue of the series, covers August 1948 (statistics mostly for June-July) and carries a Review of the Third Occupation Year (1 September 1947 - 31 August 1948). Politically the month is dominated by the Ashida cabinet's cabinet order and planned revision of the National Public Service Law following MacArthur's July letter, labor unrest on the railways, argument over dissolving the Diet and a proposed centrist league, the Illegal Transactions Investigation Committee hearings (Nishio acquitted, Nakasone sentenced), the Fukui-Ishikawa earthquake damage to textile mills, and MacArthur's address at the inauguration of the Republic of Korea. Economic parts cover SCAP's removal of export controls (15-18 August), a sterling-area trade plan, price stabilization, hoarded goods, the Reconstruction Finance Bank, holding-company dissolution and reparations shipments. Long statistical sections give production of coal, metals, chemicals, textiles, power, communications, labor, trade, prices, finance, science, patents, health and education, useful for quantifying recovery. The appendix surveys the third Occupation year.","h":["MacArthur addressed the 15 August inauguration of the Republic of Korea in Seoul before President Rhee, calling Korea's division an artificial barrier to be torn down.","A July cabinet order denied government employees strikes and collective bargaining; a Cabinet interpretation followed 11 August, and Shikoku absenteeism cancelled 118 trains on 16 August with 956 discharged.","Former deputy prime minister Nishio was acquitted 27 August, while Nakasone was sentenced to four years on 31 August in the Army Uniform Case.","SCAP authorized elimination of major Japanese Government export controls on 15-18 August, letting foreign buyers deal directly with sellers; a sterling-area trade plan was set at 242 million dollars.","The Holding Company Liquidation Commission issued its first final reorganization order on 22 August, splitting Japan Soda Company into six companies.","The Fukui-Ishikawa earthquake damaged 686 of 1,774 textile mills; the Reconstruction Finance Bank was to lend about 1.12 billion yen for repairs.","Japanese B encephalitis struck about 4,100 people with 219 deaths by 28 August, and equine encephalomyelitis killed 522 horses.","The review of the third Occupation year shows the industrial production index rising from 43.1 to 59.9, with Japan's cumulative trade deficit near 822.8 million dollars."],"k":["Ashida cabinet","National Public Service Law","SCAP","Republic of Korea inauguration","export controls","Illegal Transactions Investigation Committee","holding company dissolution","Fukui earthquake","coal production","price stabilization","war crimes trials","labor disputes","third Occupation year review","textile industry","Japanese B encephalitis"],"toc":[{"t":"Front matter","lv":1,"p":[1,5],"pp":"","s":"Archive cover, notice that this is the final issue of the Summation, title page, table of contents and list of 108 numbered charts and tables."},{"t":"Part I: General","lv":1,"p":[6,13],"pp":"1","s":"Digest of August 1948: no change in SCAP organization, then political, economic and social items including MacArthur at Seoul, trade controls lifted 15 August, and encephalitis epidemics."},{"t":"Section 1. Organization Under SCAP","lv":2,"p":[6,6],"pp":"3","s":"No change occurred in the non-military organization of GHQ SCAP during August."},{"t":"Section 2. Political Activities","lv":2,"p":[6,7],"pp":"3","s":"Digest of MacArthur's Seoul address, constitutional revision, the Public Service Law debate, Diet dissolution, centrist realignment, court decisions, dissolved ultranationalist groups, public safety figures and war crimes trials."},{"t":"Section 3. Economic Activities","lv":2,"p":[8,11],"pp":"7","s":"Digest of rail unrest, trade controls lifted 15 August, food rations, finance, property control and antitrust, alongside agriculture, mining, industrial output, power, communications, labor and science items."},{"t":"Section 4. Social Activities","lv":2,"p":[12,13],"pp":"15","s":"Digest of encephalitis epidemics, public assistance increases, repatriation of 44,400, the Board of Education Law, university reorganization, and religion and press items."},{"t":"Part II: Political","lv":1,"p":[14,31],"pp":"19","s":"Government and politics (Public Service Law order, Diet dissolution dispute, corruption hearings, purge), public safety, and legal affairs including war crimes trials."},{"t":"Section 1. Government and Politics","lv":2,"p":[15,26],"pp":"21","s":"MacArthur and Ashida messages, constitutional revision study, Public Service Law cabinet order, Diet dissolution dispute, party maneuvers, local elections, corruption hearings, purge cases and economic policy statements."},{"t":"Supreme Commander's Statement to Republic of Korea","lv":3,"p":[15,16],"pp":"","s":"MacArthur's 15 August Seoul address to President Rhee: friendship since 1882, Korea's artificial division, and defense of liberty."},{"t":"Prime Minister's Message on Anniversary of Defeat","lv":3,"p":[16,16],"pp":"","s":"Ashida's 15 August anniversary message cited improving living conditions and falling inflation, and urged a middle path and peace-loving reconstruction."},{"t":"Constitutional Revision Proposals","lv":3,"p":[16,17],"pp":"","s":"Diet leaders agreed 20 August on a joint committee to study revision; six topics listed, including the Emperor's ceremonial role and Diet conflicts."},{"t":"Political Developments: National Public Service Law Discussion","lv":3,"p":[17,19],"pp":"","s":"Cabinet order banning government employees' strikes and collective bargaining; labor and Communist reaction, Cabinet interpretation of 11 August, a social democratic seven-point plan and cabinet disagreements."},{"t":"Problem of Diet Dissolution","lv":3,"p":[19,20],"pp":"","s":"Government parties split on dissolving the Diet: Ashida wants delay to year end; Katayama and left-wing Social Democrats want a November election."},{"t":"Prime Minister's Address to Foreign Correspondents","lv":3,"p":[20,20],"pp":"","s":"Ashida's 27 August speech to foreign correspondents on food, clothing, slowing inflation, labor, political stability and middle-of-the-road policy."},{"t":"Political Parties","lv":3,"p":[21,22],"pp":"","s":"Debate over a Middle Course Political League; positions of the Democratic, Social Democratic, People's Cooperative and Democratic Liberal parties, plus minor parties including the Communists."},{"t":"Local Government","lv":3,"p":[22,23],"pp":"","s":"Recall of a Saitama village mayor upheld on a tied recount; an independent was elected governor in an August prefectural election."},{"t":"Illegal Transactions Investigations","lv":3,"p":[23,24],"pp":"","s":"Nishio acquitted; Nakasone sentenced in the Army Uniform Case; Kano case, Area Disposal and coal-mine bribery hearings; new charges against Miki and Kitamura."},{"t":"The Purge","lv":3,"p":[24,25],"pp":"","s":"Ultranationalist groups dissolved in August; 377,788 screened and 201,333 purged by month end; indictments for purge violations."},{"t":"Economic Plans and Policies","lv":3,"p":[25,26],"pp":"","s":"Government pledge to the ESB ten-point policy, financial reorganization plan, wage dispute over the National Personnel Authority, payroll cuts, and party anti-inflation and reconstruction programs."},{"t":"Section 2. Public Safety","lv":2,"p":[27,29],"pp":"45","s":"Hoarded goods (5.95 million yen recovered in July), black-market and bribery cases, June crime statistics, prison population, fires, and transfer of offshore policing to the Maritime Safety Board."},{"t":"Section 3. Legal Affairs and War Crimes","lv":2,"p":[30,31],"pp":"51","s":"Supreme Court caseload, domestic relations court, 70 suspects apprehended and 713 tried to date; seven trials in the period, two death sentences; IMTFE in recess."},{"t":"Part III: Business","lv":1,"p":[32,130],"pp":"50","s":"Fourteen economic sections of production, trade, price, finance and science statistics, from agriculture and coal to textiles, labor, exports, reparations and patents."},{"t":"Section 1. Agriculture and Fisheries","lv":2,"p":[33,36],"pp":"57","s":"Agricultural cooperative program and cabinet orders, Kanto Plain soil survey, June marine catch down to 150,018 tons, tuna export rejections, petroleum allocations and whaling."},{"t":"Agricultural cooperatives and administration","lv":3,"p":[33,34],"pp":"","s":"Dissolution of agricultural associations and three August cabinet orders on property transfers to cooperatives."},{"t":"Fisheries","lv":3,"p":[34,36],"pp":"","s":"Smaller sardine and mackerel catches, spoilage from lack of ice, a new purse seiner, August fuel allocations and 2,210 tons of whale products."},{"t":"Section 2. Forestry and Mining","lv":2,"p":[37,42],"pp":"","s":"August coal output 3,542,000 tons (down about 300,000), deliveries, stockpiles, lignite, crude oil drilling and strike effects, and June mineral output with mercury up."},{"t":"Coal, oil and mining industry","lv":3,"p":[39,42],"pp":"","s":"Coal deliveries of 2,669,000 tons in July, district production, stockpiles, lignite, crude oil and mine production."},{"t":"Section 3. Industrial Raw Materials","lv":2,"p":[43,50],"pp":"77","s":"July coke 264,112 tons; pig iron and rolled steel at postwar peaks; cement down 12 percent; light metals, copper, zinc, petroleum refining, chemicals, salt and paper figures."},{"t":"Coke and metal industries","lv":3,"p":[43,47],"pp":"","s":"Coke production, deliveries and stocks; iron, steel, aluminum, copper, zinc, tin and ferroalloys; petroleum products."},{"t":"Cement, chemicals, salt, pulp and paper","lv":3,"p":[47,50],"pp":"","s":"Cement down on coal and transport shortages; 21 of 28 chemicals up; caustic soda at postwar peak; salt output up 50.6 percent; paper at postwar peak."},{"t":"Section 4. Machinery, Equipment and Construction","lv":2,"p":[51,56],"pp":"93","s":"Machine tools, bearings, textile machinery, rolling stock, electrical and communications equipment, truck output of 1,388, coal miners' housing and building permits."},{"t":"Section 5. Manufactured Goods","lv":2,"p":[57,63],"pp":"105","s":"June cigarettes up 12.3 percent, plus food processing, brewing, glass, rubber, leather, watches, sewing machines, ship completions and the Soviet tug contract."},{"t":"Section 6. Textile Industries","lv":2,"p":[64,79],"pp":"119","s":"Fukui-Ishikawa earthquake damage to mills, cotton imports, raw silk at a postwar peak of 11,521 bales, rayon, wool, bast fibers and knit goods."},{"t":"Earthquake damage","lv":3,"p":[64,65],"pp":"","s":"Survey recommends 1.514 billion yen for repairs; 686 of 1,774 mills damaged; Reconstruction Finance Bank to lend 1.12 billion yen."},{"t":"Cotton","lv":3,"p":[65,67],"pp":"","s":"Cotton imports rose to 43.9 million pounds in July; spinners' supplies, yarn and fabric production and machinery."},{"t":"Silk","lv":3,"p":[67,70],"pp":"","s":"July raw silk output reached a postwar peak of 11,531 bales, mostly 20/22 denier; tested silk fell to 2,124 bales, and silk fabric output dropped 316,000 square yards amid yarn shortages and earthquake-area mill losses."},{"t":"Artificial fibers and wool","lv":3,"p":[71,73],"pp":"","s":"Rayon pulp imports and caustic soda bottleneck limit rayon yarn and fabric; raw wool imported from Australia and South America."},{"t":"Hard and bast fibers, spun and reprocessed materials, knit goods, sundries, dyeing","lv":3,"p":[74,79],"pp":"","s":"Hard and bast fiber stocks rose to 20,520,000 pounds with Indian jute arrivals; rope output rose 1,841,000 pounds, jute yarn jumped to 522,000 pounds, and knit underwear for export fell to 181,319 dozen."},{"t":"Section 7. Transportation and Public Utilities","lv":2,"p":[80,86],"pp":"151","s":"Shikoku railway unrest, 519.9 million passengers, 17 August directive for Japan to assume port operations, coastwise shipping, July electric power of 2.94 billion kWh and gas industry figures."},{"t":"Railways and water transportation","lv":3,"p":[80,83],"pp":"","s":"Communist-led absenteeism on Shikoku with 956 discharged; 17 August directive on port and terminal operations; coastal and foreign cargo."},{"t":"Electric power and gas","lv":3,"p":[84,86],"pp":"","s":"July electric power generation was about 2.94 billion kWh, 8.5 percent below July 1947; hydroelectric supplied 2,775 million kWh while thermal fell 65.5 percent to 71 million; stream flow ran 21 percent above June."},{"t":"Section 8. Communications","lv":2,"p":[87,91],"pp":"165","s":"1,386,084 telephones after the Telephone Bond Law, 6,858,814 licensed radio listeners, radio frequency changes, and postal volume drop after the 11 July rate rise."},{"t":"Section 9. Labor","lv":2,"p":[92,97],"pp":"176","s":"Labor rally against the Ashida cabinet, union statistics, Hokkaido and Imperial Oil disputes, wage increases for Occupation workers, labor standards inspections, and women and minors."},{"t":"Labor relations and disputes","lv":3,"p":[94,94],"pp":"","s":"Hokkaido railway walkout, coal wage agreement, electric power dispute, Imperial Oil mediation and Toho studio eviction."},{"t":"Employment and unemployment","lv":3,"p":[97,98],"pp":"","s":"Employment Security Advisory Committee, coal mine employment recheck, vocational training and unemployment insurance."},{"t":"Section 10. Imports and Exports","lv":2,"p":[98,104],"pp":"187","s":"Export controls eliminated, sterling-area trade plan for 242 million dollars, 10 million dollars of cotton contracts, Siam talks, and a first-half deficit of 271 million dollars."},{"t":"Section 11. Price and Distribution","lv":2,"p":[105,110],"pp":"201","s":"Record summer vegetable harvest, three more price stabilization groups, textile prices up about 90 percent, consumer price index 342.9, incentive goods and surplus Army cigarettes."},{"t":"Section 12. Finance","lv":2,"p":[111,117],"pp":"213","s":"Economic Investigation Board created 1 August; notes in circulation 264 billion yen; Reconstruction Finance Bank plan; 18 control associations closed; first prison terms for tax evasion."},{"t":"Section 13. Property Control and Reparations","lv":2,"p":[118,122],"pp":"227","s":"Attorney-General to liquidate dissolved organizations, looted property status, holding company orders, Fair Trade Commission filings and machine tool shipments to claimants."},{"t":"Section 14. Science and Technology","lv":2,"p":[123,130],"pp":"","s":"New Industrial Technique Management Council, Japanese physicists' travel to Princeton, FEC access to Japanese processes, patent statistics, and a background survey of Japan's climate."},{"t":"Patents and standards","lv":3,"p":[124,127],"pp":"","s":"Forty Japanese Standards issued in July, FEC technical access order of 6 August, and patent and utility-model statistics."},{"t":"Climate of Japan","lv":3,"p":[127,130],"pp":"","s":"Describes Japan's climate: eight storm types from China (South Mongolia 21.2 a year), typhoons peaking July–October, three forest zones, rice and mandarin limits near 27°N, sweet potato acreage 276,000 ha in 1947, and the late April 1947 frost near Tokyo."},{"t":"Part IV: Social","lv":1,"p":[131,151],"pp":"283","s":"Public health and welfare (encephalitis, repatriation, public assistance), and education, religion, cultural resources and media, including the Board of Education Law."},{"t":"Section 1. Public Health and Welfare","lv":2,"p":[132,144],"pp":"255","s":"Japanese B encephalitis and equine epidemics, Fukui earthquake aid, medical supplies, narcotics, public assistance, population 80,170,815 and repatriation of 44,625."},{"t":"Medical administration and supply","lv":3,"p":[135,138],"pp":"","s":"Nursing courses, production of penicillin, DDT and X-ray equipment, veterinary and inspection data, and distribution of medical supplies."},{"t":"Narcotics, public assistance and social security","lv":3,"p":[138,140],"pp":"","s":"Narcotics handler registrations, welfare payments and new allowance rates, LARA shipments, a 40 percent medical fee increase and insurance statistics."},{"t":"Vital statistics, population and repatriation","lv":3,"p":[141,144],"pp":"","s":"June birth rate lowest since 1929; population 80,170,815; census density maps; 6,024,896 repatriated by 2 September."},{"t":"Section 2. Education, Religion, Cultural Resources and Media of Expression","lv":2,"p":[145,151],"pp":"281","s":"Teachers' union cabinet ordinance, Board of Education Law and October elections, university reorganization, textbooks, religion, press comment, the Asahi Saitama case, radio, films and SCAP libraries."},{"t":"Education","lv":3,"p":[145,148],"pp":"","s":"Redefined relations with the teachers' union, Board of Education Law, 219 university applications, textbooks and libraries."},{"t":"Religion, cultural resources and media of expression","lv":3,"p":[148,151],"pp":"","s":"Buddhist reform, Christian missions, press and radio themes, motion pictures and two new SCAP libraries."},{"t":"Appendix: Review of the Third Occupation Year (1 September 1947 - 31 August 1948)","lv":1,"p":[152,173],"pp":"295","s":"Year-end review of 1 September 1947 to 31 August 1948: SCAP organization, economic recovery (production index 43.1 to 59.9), living conditions and democratization."},{"t":"Section 1. Organization Under SCAP","lv":2,"p":[153,153],"pp":"297","s":"Restitution Advisory Committee established 13 April; no other organizational change during the year; chart of GHQ organization follows."},{"t":"Section 2. Economic Recovery","lv":2,"p":[154,161],"pp":"299","s":"Industrial production index rose from 43.1 to 59.9; coal and power, textiles, trade and sterling arrangements, transport, communications, finance, property control and reparations."},{"t":"Section 3. Living Conditions","lv":2,"p":[162,164],"pp":"315","s":"Nutrition, ration distribution, housing and rents, textile distribution, wage and price inflation, disaster relief, disease campaigns, health and child welfare laws, welfare and repatriation."},{"t":"Section 4. Progress of Democratization","lv":2,"p":[165,173],"pp":"321","s":"Third-year reforms: Home Ministry abolished 21 December 1947; police decentralized; 301,510 purged; party realignment; IMTFE adjourned 15 April 1948; 1,378 atrocity cases; cooperative and antitrust laws; Labor Ministry; 13,000,000 students; press censorship ended 25 July."}],"by":"claude-sonnet (Claude Code subagent)"},{"id":"K-35-in-K-33","ia":"south-korean-interim-government-activities-no-33-1948-06","s":"K","no":35,"d":"1948-09","d2":"1948-10","t":"Republic of Korea Economic Summation, No. 35 (September-October 1948)","v":"Bound in K-33","host":"K-33","rg":[[486,591]],"kind":"bound","found":"Title page '105.13:35' on PDF p. 486; economic sections only","pg":106,"o":"Republic of Korea Economic Summation No. 35 covers September and October 1948, the first economic report after USAMGIK handed authority to the Republic of Korea, and continues the SKIG Activities series. It records transfer of American-run departments to Korean ministries and the end of the Office of Property Custody under US control on 12 October. Main topics are the grain collection programs, ration-roll inflation, falling electric power, coal imports, prices, trade, currency growth and finances. Part I covers refugees and vested property sales; Part II food, prices, industry, mining, allocations and trade; Part III transport, communications, utilities and public works; Part IV finance. Historians will find provincial quotas, ration estimates, trade statistics, power figures, and the barter suspension with North Korea, in statistical tables.","h":["Summer grain collection reached 107,766 metric tons, 100.4 percent of quota (Cheju only 4 percent), and the 1948 rice collection goal was set at 1,125,000 tons, split by province.","Ration rolls listed 9,987,597 persons in October, up nearly 1,000,000 in a year; officials suspected a 'ghost population', about 500,000 of it in Seoul.","The Office of Property Custody passed to a Korean custodian on 12 October, with 1,279 cumulative sales worth about 2,611 million won; immovable sales halted 18 September.","Electricity deliveries fell about 18 percent below August, to 45.6 million KWH in September; Yongwol's rehabilitated plant carried 80,400 KW in a 23 October test.","Currency in circulation rose 2,500 million won from 31 August to 31 October, over 1,500 million of it for Monopoly tobacco purchases; the overdraft reached 38,400 million won.","Bituminous coal imports from Japan reached 100,015 metric tons in September, 73 percent above the 1947 monthly average, while anthracite output fell a fifth month, 51 percent below April.","Commerce Minister Louise Yim suspended barter trade with North Korea pending Assembly approval; it resumed by end of October under modified rules, mainly manufactured goods.","Wholesale prices fell to 177 percent of the 1947 average in October from 184 in August, and September exports were 45 percent of imports, with Hong Kong taking over 93 percent."],"k":["Republic of Korea","economic summation","transfer of authority","grain collection","rice quota","ration rolls","vested property","Office of Property Custody","electric power","coal imports","prices","foreign trade","barter trade North Korea","refugees","government finance","public works"],"toc":[{"t":"Front matter","lv":1,"p":[486,493],"pp":"","s":"Cover, blank digitization pages, foreword and contents of Economic Summation No. 35 (September-October 1948), the first under the Republic, successor to SKIG Activities; No. 36 is planned through December."},{"t":"Summary","lv":1,"p":[494,495],"pp":"1","s":"Agencies shifted to Korean management. Summer grain collection reached 107,766 tons (100.4 percent of goal), a 1,125,000-ton rice goal was set, and ration rolls were suspected padded. Electricity fell 18 percent; currency rose 2,500 million won."},{"t":"Part I. Basic Aspects","lv":1,"p":[496,503],"pp":"","s":"Refugee arrivals across the 38th parallel and the 12 October transfer of the Office of Property Custody, with 1,279 cumulative vested-property sales."},{"t":"Section 1. Population","lv":2,"p":[496,498],"pp":"3","s":"Refugee entries across the 38th parallel were 11,718 in September and 7,764 in October (1948 total 106,463). Net movement since October 1945 added 1,588,030 residents."},{"t":"Refugee Migration","lv":3,"p":[496,497],"pp":"3","s":"Refugee entries were 11,718 in September (up 57 percent on August) and 7,764 in October (down 33 percent), attributed to colder weather. January-October totals fell 34 percent in 1948 against 1947."},{"t":"Population Movement","lv":3,"p":[496,497],"pp":"3","s":"From 1 October 1945 to 31 October 1948, 887,301 left South Korea (705,056 Japanese civilians) and 2,475,331 entered, including 1,117,819 Koreans from Japan and 646,034 from North Korea; net gain 1,588,030."},{"t":"Section 2. Vested Property","lv":2,"p":[499,503],"pp":"6","s":"Office of Property Custody passed to a Korean custodian on 12 October 1948, with 1,279 cumulative sales worth about 2,611 million won. Immovable sales halted 18 September; KRMC planned low-cost housing."},{"t":"Administration Division","lv":3,"p":[499,499],"pp":"6","s":"Office of Property Custody shrank before transfer: American staff fell from 96 to 58, and Korean employees from 2,948 to 2,635 (national office 468, provincial offices 2,167)."},{"t":"Property Disposal","lv":3,"p":[500,500],"pp":"7","s":"581 sales worth 1,358.4 million won since 1 July brought the total to 1,279. Sales of immovables and shares stopped 18 September under the financial agreement; OPC asked to ship 1,600 tons of steel billets to the US."},{"t":"Korean Residence Management Corporation","lv":3,"p":[501,501],"pp":"8","s":"KRMC planned 500 low-cost houses (types B, C, D) costing about 240 million won, financed by subsidy, loans, lot sales and 20 percent down payments. Police guarded 149,056 pyung of lots."},{"t":"Real Estate Transfers","lv":3,"p":[502,502],"pp":"9","s":"The department accepted US-released property, with Korean agencies given priority under Procedural Memorandums 21 and 24: 280 dependent houses and 625 other properties. The 30 September quarterly rent could not be paid."},{"t":"Policy Division","lv":3,"p":[502,502],"pp":"9","s":"Of 1,394 cases from the Property Claims Commission, 825 were allowed, 356 disallowed and 213 returned. Ordinance No. 210 claim forms went out 24 August; the Custodian held shares in 38 corporations."},{"t":"Comptroller Division","lv":3,"p":[502,502],"pp":"9","s":"Manuals on taxes, loans and auditing were translated to train Korean staff. Korean auditors, supervised by an American, made quarterly audits of all provincial offices and of custodian-controlled firms."},{"t":"Part II. Production and Supply","lv":1,"p":[504,553],"pp":"","s":"Food, prices, industry, mining, allocations and trade: summer grain at 100.4 percent of quota, wholesale prices down to 177, bituminous imports of 100,015 tons, 1,211 allocations, and exports at 45 percent of imports."},{"t":"Section 1. Food and Agriculture","lv":2,"p":[504,512],"pp":"11","s":"Ministry of Agriculture and Forestry took over 13 September. Summer grain reached 107,766 tons; the rice quota was 1,125,000 tons. Ration rolls were inflated; fertilizer imports, forestry and land payments reported."},{"t":"Administrative Changes and Rice Collection","lv":3,"p":[504,505],"pp":"11","s":"The Ministry of Agriculture and Forestry took over food agencies on 13 September and the National Land Administration on 11 October. A 1,125,000-ton rice quota was split by province; summer grain hit 100.4 percent, Cheju 4."},{"t":"Food","lv":3,"p":[506,509],"pp":"13","s":"Ration rolls rose to 9,987,597 in October, with padding suspected; grain was released to 9,285,000 recipients. Stocks fell to 170,551 tons. Imports included 18,328 tons of milo maize."},{"t":"Korea Agricultural Association","lv":3,"p":[510,510],"pp":"17","s":"Fertilizer imports from October 1946 to October 1948 totaled 630,777 tons. Of 521,776 tons received by provinces since July 1947, 461,344 (88 percent) were sold to farmers; 7,878 were apparent losses."},{"t":"Bureau of Forestry","lv":3,"p":[511,511],"pp":"18","s":"September and October output: plywood 10,000 and 12,000 pieces; logs 19,700 and 21,300 cubic meters; fuelwood 313,889 and 293,714; charcoal 3,660 and 3,960 tons. Fuelwood alone fell in October."},{"t":"National Land Administration","lv":3,"p":[512,512],"pp":"19","s":"Land purchasers pay 20 percent of annual crop output for 15 years. October installments were 10,801 tons (70.5 percent of 15,315 due), only 24.4 percent at Iri; rents 477 tons (58.8 percent)."},{"t":"Section 2. Prices","lv":2,"p":[513,521],"pp":"20","s":"Wholesale index fell to 177 percent of the 1947 average in October from 184 in August as grains declined after floods eased; cotton cloth and firewood rose. Retail prices followed."},{"t":"Wholesale Prices","lv":3,"p":[514,515],"pp":"21","s":"Wholesale indexes fell in October to 177 percent of the 1947 average (184 in August) on lower grains and protein foods. Cotton sheeting and sookso silk rose on power cuts; firewood jumped seasonally."},{"t":"Retail Prices","lv":3,"p":[514,519],"pp":"21","s":"Seoul retail prices and nine-city indexes followed wholesale prices. Cleaned rice fell from 2,480 won per mal in September to 1,800; firewood and kimchi vegetables rose. Table 6 put the October retail index at 175."},{"t":"Controlled Prices","lv":3,"p":[520,521],"pp":"27","s":"Controlled prices were set by the former National Price Administration, now the Bureau of Prices in the Office of Planning. No new prices were set after the Republic took over; cleaned rice was 300 won per large mal."},{"t":"Section 3. Industrial Production","lv":2,"p":[522,529],"pp":"29","s":"Output improved slightly despite the power shortage. Flour, noodles, coal briquettes (15,849 tons in October), insulators and oxygen set 1948 highs. Cement stopped in early November for lack of power; no carbide was made."},{"t":"Section 4. Mining and Minerals","lv":2,"p":[530,536],"pp":"37","s":"Anthracite output fell for a fifth month, 51 percent below April. Lignite rose to 5,496 tons, bituminous imports from Japan reached 100,015 tons in September, and gold, silver, copper, tungsten and graphite recovered."},{"t":"Indigenous Anthracite and Lignite Coal","lv":3,"p":[530,532],"pp":"37","s":"Anthracite output fell a fifth month, 51 percent below April, from power shortage. Lignite reached 5,496 tons; the 70,500-ton quota proved unrealistic, Samchuk made 21,650 tons, and Yongwol 19,636 short tons in October."},{"t":"Bituminous Coal and Shipments to Consumers","lv":3,"p":[533,533],"pp":"40","s":"Bituminous imports from Japan were 100,015 tons in September, 73 percent above the 1947 monthly average, and 90,297 tons in October. September supply was 18,474 tons of anthracite and 64,566 of bituminous."},{"t":"Other Minerals","lv":3,"p":[534,536],"pp":"41","s":"Non-coal minerals recovered in September: gold 15.9 kg, silver 129.6 kg, copper concentrate 276 tons, tungsten ore 119.4 tons. Zinc (31.8 tons) and lead (33.8) resumed after flood stoppage."},{"t":"Section 5. Allocations and Distribution","lv":2,"p":[537,546],"pp":"44","s":"Allocations Bureau processed 1,211 allocations worth 2,551.8 million won; 37,181 tons of salt distributed; petroleum deliveries fell short of allocations. Barter trade with North Korea was suspended, then resumed under restrictions."},{"t":"Allocation of Consumer Goods","lv":3,"p":[537,537],"pp":"44","s":"The Allocations Bureau replaced the National Consumer Goods Office on 16 August and had processed 1,211 allocations worth 2,551.8 million won by October, farmers receiving 37 percent. September-October allocations: 116, worth 367.4 million won."},{"t":"Distribution of Controlled Commodities","lv":3,"p":[538,538],"pp":"45","s":"Federation of Financial Associations purchases of controlled goods totaled 315.95 million won in September and 117.75 million in October, mainly cotton cloth and underwear, against 3,577.8 million since July 1946."},{"t":"Salt Distribution","lv":3,"p":[538,538],"pp":"45","s":"37,181 tons of salt were distributed in September and October, mostly kimchi rations. Stock on 31 October was 89,927 tons; government salterns made 88,637 tons since April, 66 percent of the F/Y 1948/49 estimate."},{"t":"Petroleum","lv":3,"p":[538,545],"pp":"45","s":"Petroleum deliveries fell short of allocations, such as 6.48 million liters of gasoline in October against 7.78 million allocated, owing to stock shortages and poor Pusan terminal facilities. Korean use fell from 409 million liters in 1937 to 48 million in 1944."},{"t":"Barter Trade with North Korea","lv":3,"p":[546,546],"pp":"53","s":"Minister Louise Yim ordered trade with North Korea suspended pending National Assembly approval and barred exports of food, rubber, cotton cloth and petroleum. By end of October the Assembly approved and barter resumed under modified rules."},{"t":"Section 6. Imports and Exports","lv":2,"p":[547,553],"pp":"54","s":"Private imports rose 22 percent and exports 80 percent in September, exports reaching 45 percent of imports. Government shipments went to the US, Hong Kong and Japan; souvenirs were sold to Americans."},{"t":"Private Trade Statistics","lv":3,"p":[547,550],"pp":"54","s":"September imports were 1,155 million won (newsprint, raw rubber, thread, plate glass); exports 516 million (shellfish, agar), 93 percent to Hong Kong. Imports rose 22 percent and exports over 80 percent; Inchon handled 96 percent of imports."},{"t":"Government-to-Government Transactions","lv":3,"p":[551,552],"pp":"58","s":"October government shipments to the US exceeded 3,600 tons, mostly 3,595.6 tons of scrap metal ($106,941). Over 5,800 tons went to Japan from 15 August, chiefly 4,108 tons of graphite."},{"t":"Korean Items for Americans in Korea","lv":3,"p":[553,553],"pp":"60","s":"Korean-made souvenirs such as brassware and lacquer sold at the Seoul Army PX: 10,414 pieces ($11,274) in August, 4,850 ($5,399) in September, 5,320 ($4,259) in October."},{"t":"Part III. Public Services","lv":1,"p":[554,572],"pp":"","s":"Railroads, communications, utilities and public works: transport and communications departments passed to Korean ministries, power deliveries fell 18 percent, and Public Works became a bureau under Home Affairs."},{"t":"Section 1. Transportation","lv":2,"p":[554,560],"pp":"61","s":"Transportation department turned over 15 August. September expenditures rose 61 percent to a postliberation high of 1,418.6 million won; passengers and freight rose, operable locomotives fell to 256, and coal use increased."},{"t":"Railroads","lv":3,"p":[554,559],"pp":"61","s":"Transportation passed to the Republic on 15 August; Min He-sik resigned 6 October, succeeded by Ho Chung. September spending peaked at 1,418.6 million won. Operable locomotives fell to 256; employment declined while wages rose."},{"t":"Marine, Highway and Air Transportation","lv":3,"p":[560,560],"pp":"67","s":"Railroad coal use rose to 45,919 tons in September and 49,923 in October. Marine income rose to 28.6 and 32.4 million won; September marine spending spiked to 206 million won, then fell to 28 million."},{"t":"Section 2. Communications","lv":2,"p":[561,566],"pp":"68","s":"Communications passed to the Republic on 13 September under Minister Yoon Suk-ku. September revenue nearly doubled on higher telephone rates; expenditure hit 201 million won with 12,570 employees. Seoul-Shanghai radio-telephone tests began."},{"t":"Section 3. Public Utilities","lv":2,"p":[567,569],"pp":"74","s":"Power deliveries were 45.6 million KWH in September and 46.1 million in October, about 18 percent below August. Yongwol's rehabilitated plant carried 80,400 KW on 23 October; a seven-man power mission under Loyal R. Millburn arrived 19 October."},{"t":"Section 4. Public Works","lv":2,"p":[570,572],"pp":"77","s":"Public Works became a Home Affairs bureau on 13 September. Highway projects used 82,677 man-days and 41.3 million won; Waegwon bridge finished. Water plants were repaired and river works planned at 82.4 million won."},{"t":"Part IV. Finance","lv":1,"p":[573,585],"pp":"","s":"Government finance and non-government finance: notes outstanding rose 2,500 million won, the overdraft reached 38,400 million, and deposits stood at 52,328 million won at end of September."},{"t":"Section 1. Government Finance","lv":2,"p":[573,578],"pp":"80","s":"Notes outstanding rose 2,500 million won from August to October, over 1,500 million for tobacco purchases. Spending exceeded revenue by 3,695 million in September and 878 million in October; appropriations reached 43,749 million won."},{"t":"Section 2. Non-Government Finance","lv":2,"p":[579,585],"pp":"86","s":"Deposits reached 52,328 million won at end of September (38 percent government, 51 public); Bank of Chosun held nearly 36 percent. Loans outstanding fell 960 million to 27,441 million won."},{"t":"Back matter","lv":1,"p":[586,591],"pp":"","s":"Blank pages marked 'Digitized by Google', a library due-date slip and a closing page; no report content."}],"by":"claude-sonnet (Claude Code subagent)"}];
